window.SUBJECT_DATA=window.SUBJECT_DATA||{};
window.SUBJECT_DATA["peds"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which branch of medicine deals with the different disease conditions and their treatment in children and also provides clinical care during growth and development?","options":["Geriatrics","Paediatrics","Obstetrics","Gynaecology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paediatrics is defined as the branch of medicine that deals with different disease conditions and their treatment in children and provides clinical care during growth and development.  Why A wrong: Geriatrics deals with the care of the elderly.  Why C wrong: Obstetrics deals with pregnancy and childbirth.  Why D wrong: Gynaecology deals with the female reproductive system."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which of the following is stated as the MOST common factor affecting child health?","options":["Available health facilities","Socioeconomic conditions and environment","Maternal health","Family health and social support"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists maternal health as the most common factor affecting child health.  Why A wrong: Available health facilities is listed as a factor but not the most common.  Why B wrong: Socioeconomic conditions/environment is a factor but not identified as most common.  Why D wrong: Family health and social support is a factor but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Select ALL the factors listed as affecting child health.","options":["Maternal health","Family health and social support","Socioeconomic conditions and environment","Available health facilities","Blood group of the father"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed factors affecting child health are maternal health, family health and social support, socioeconomic conditions and environment, and available health facilities.  Why E wrong: Father's blood group is not listed as a factor affecting child health."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Growth is BEST defined as the process of:","options":["Functional and physiological maturation of the individual","Physical maturation of the body resulting in an increase in size and shape of the body and various organs","Progressive increase in skill and capacity to function","Qualitative change that cannot be measured"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Growth is the process of physical maturation of the body resulting in an increase in size and shape of the body and various organs.  Why A wrong: This defines development, not growth.  Why C wrong: This describes development.  Why D wrong: Qualitative and unmeasurable describes development; growth is quantitative."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Growth is a QUANTITATIVE change that can be measured in which of the following units?","options":["Inches, centimetre and pound/kilogram","Intelligence quotient","Developmental milestones","Behavioural scores"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Growth is quantitative and can be measured in inches, centimetre and pound/kilogram.  Why B wrong: IQ measures cognitive development, not physical growth.  Why C wrong: Milestones assess development, not growth measurement units.  Why D wrong: Behavioural scores relate to development, not measurable growth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A nurse teaches that growth does NOT continue throughout life. When does growth stop?","options":["At birth","When maturity has been attained","During adolescence only","It never stops"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Growth does not continue throughout life; it stops when maturity has been attained.  Why A wrong: Growth is rapid, not stopped, at/after birth.  Why C wrong: Growth stops at maturity, which is reached after adolescence.  Why D wrong: Unlike development, growth does stop at maturity."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Development is BEST defined as the process of:","options":["Physical maturation with increase in body size","Functional and physiological maturation of the individual","Increase in weight measured in kilograms","Increase in height measured in centimetres"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Development is the process of functional and physiological maturation of the individual.  Why A wrong: Increase in body size defines growth.  Why C wrong: Weight in kg is a growth measurement.  Why D wrong: Height in cm is a growth measurement."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which statement about development is TRUE?","options":["It is a quantitative measurement that can be measured","It stops when maturity is attained","It is a progressive increase in skill and capacity to function and continues throughout life","It occurs only in the first year of life"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Development is a progressive increase in skill and capacity to function and continues throughout life.  Why A wrong: Development is qualitative and cannot be measured.  Why B wrong: Growth stops at maturity; development continues throughout life.  Why D wrong: Development is lifelong, not limited to the first year."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Development is described as which type of measurement?","options":["Quantitative and measurable","Qualitative, which cannot be measured","Objective and numerical","Metric and directional"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Development is a qualitative measurement which cannot be measured.  Why A wrong: Quantitative/measurable describes growth.  Why C wrong: Objective and numerical describes growth.  Why D wrong: Development is not directional/metric in this sense; growth is measured metrically."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the principles of growth and development, the process is described as:","options":["Random and unpredictable","Continuous and orderly with individual differences, unique to each child","Identical in every child","Discontinuous and reversible"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Growth and development is a continuous and orderly process with individual difference and is unique to each child.  Why A wrong: It is orderly, not random.  Why C wrong: It is unique to each child, not identical.  Why D wrong: It is continuous, not discontinuous."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A nurse states that different body tissues grow at different rates. This reflects which principle of growth and development?","options":["Growth is uniform in all tissues","Different body tissues grow at different rates","All tissues stop growing at the same time","Tissue growth is unrelated to development"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A stated principle is that different body tissues grow at different rates.  Why A wrong: Growth is not uniform across tissues.  Why C wrong: The book does not state all tissues stop at the same time; rates differ.  Why D wrong: Growth and development are interrelated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The pattern of development that progresses from the head to the tail is called:","options":["Proximodistal","Cephalocaudal","Distoproximal","Caudocephalic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cephalocaudal means development proceeds from head to the tail.  Why A wrong: Proximodistal is from centre/midline to periphery.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the terms used; development is cephalocaudal (head to tail) and proximodistal (midline to periphery)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Development that progresses from the centre or midline of the body toward the periphery is termed:","options":["Cephalocaudal","Proximodistal","Mediolateral only","Craniocaudal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Proximodistal development proceeds from the centre or midline to the periphery.  Why A wrong: Cephalocaudal is head to tail.  Why C wrong: Not the term used in the source.  Why D wrong: Craniocaudal is a synonym for cephalocaudal (head to tail), not midline to periphery."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the principles of growth and development, the rate of growth and development is rapid during which periods?","options":["Preschool and school age","Infancy and puberty","Only during infancy","Only during adulthood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rate of growth and development is interrelated and rapid in infancy and in puberty, but slow in preschool and school age children.  Why A wrong: These are the periods of slow growth.  Why C wrong: It is rapid in both infancy AND puberty.  Why D wrong: Growth stops at maturity in adulthood."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Growth and development depend on a combination of many interdependent factors related to:","options":["Heredity and environment","Diet and exercise only","Immunization alone","Socioeconomic status alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Growth and development depend on a combination of many interdependent factors of heredity and environment.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifically names heredity and environment as the combined determinants, not any single factor alone."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes growth from development?","options":["Growth is qualitative; development is quantitative","Growth can be objectively observed and measured; development cannot be measured","Growth continues throughout life; development stops at maturity","Growth is affected by learning; development is not"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Growth is quantitative, objectively observed and measured; development is qualitative and cannot be measured.  Why A wrong: Reversed — growth is quantitative, development is qualitative.  Why C wrong: Reversed — growth stops at maturity, development continues throughout life.  Why D wrong: Reversed — growth is not affected by learning, whereas learning and experience affect development."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"As per the difference table, which statement about growth is correct?","options":["It is progressive and sequential","It is not directional and not uniform in all parts","It is affected by learning","It is integrative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Growth is stated to be not directional and not uniform in all the parts.  Why A wrong: Progressive and sequential describes development.  Why C wrong: Growth is NOT affected by learning; development is.  Why D wrong: Being integrative describes development."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the difference table, which of the following is a characteristic of DEVELOPMENT?","options":["Stops after maturation","Occurs in different parts and is not directional","Is progressive and sequential and is integrative","Can be objectively measured in centimetres"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Development is progressive and sequential and is integrative.  Why A wrong: Stopping after maturation is a feature of growth.  Why B wrong: Occurring in different parts/not directional describes growth.  Why D wrong: Development cannot be measured; growth is measured in centimetres."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"During the prenatal period, the \"ovum or gamete\" stage extends over which time frame?","options":["From ovulation to fertilization (usually 6 to 8 days after ovulation)","0 to 14 days after fertilization","14 days to 8 weeks","8 weeks to 40 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ovum/gamete stage is from ovulation to fertilization, usually 6 to 8 days after ovulation.  Why B wrong: 0 to 14 days after fertilization is the zygote stage.  Why C wrong: 14 days to 8 weeks is the embryo stage.  Why D wrong: 8 weeks to 40 weeks is the fetus stage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The zygote stage of prenatal development is defined as:","options":["Ovulation to fertilization","0 to 14 days after fertilization/conception (0 to 2 weeks)","14 days to 8 weeks","8 weeks to birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The zygote stage is 0 to 14 days after fertilization/conception (0 to 2 weeks).  Why A wrong: That is the ovum/gamete stage.  Why C wrong: That is the embryo stage.  Why D wrong: That is the fetus stage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The embryonic period extends from:","options":["0 to 14 days after fertilization","14 days to 8 weeks","8 weeks to 40 weeks","Birth to 28 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The embryo stage extends from 14 days to 8 weeks.  Why A wrong: 0 to 14 days is the zygote stage.  Why C wrong: 8 weeks to 40 weeks is the fetus stage.  Why D wrong: Birth to 28 days is the neonatal period."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The fetal period of prenatal development is defined as:","options":["14 days to 8 weeks","8 weeks to 40 weeks or till birth","0 to 2 weeks","Birth to 4 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The fetus stage is 8 weeks to 40 weeks or till birth.  Why A wrong: 14 days to 8 weeks is the embryo stage.  Why C wrong: 0 to 2 weeks is the zygote stage.  Why D wrong: Birth to 4 weeks is the neonatal period."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The neonatal (newborn) period is defined as:","options":["From birth to 28 days or the first 4 weeks of life","29 days to 1 year","1 to 3 years","3 to 6 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The neonate/newborn period is from birth to 28 days or the first 4 weeks of life.  Why B wrong: 29 days to 1 year is infancy.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 years is the toddler stage.  Why D wrong: 3 to 6 years is preschool/early childhood."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The \"early neonate\" period is defined as:","options":["Birth to 7 days","8 days to 28 days","29 days to 1 year","Birth to 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The early neonate period is from birth to 7 days.  Why B wrong: 8 days to 28 days is the late neonate period.  Why C wrong: 29 days to 1 year is infancy.  Why D wrong: Birth to 24 hours is not the defined early neonate range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The \"late neonate\" period is defined as:","options":["Birth to 7 days","8 days to 28 days","1 to 3 years","28 days to 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The late neonate period is 8 days to 28 days.  Why A wrong: Birth to 7 days is the early neonate period.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 years is the toddler stage.  Why D wrong: This overlaps into infancy and is not the late neonate definition."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, infancy is defined as the period from:","options":["Birth to 28 days","29 days to 1 year of life","1 to 3 years","Birth to 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Infancy is defined as 29 days to 1 year of life.  Why A wrong: Birth to 28 days is the neonatal period.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 years is the toddler stage.  Why D wrong: Birth to 6 months is not the defined infancy range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The toddler stage is defined as:","options":["29 days to 1 year","1 to 3 years of life","3 to 6 years","6 to 12 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The toddler stage is 1 to 3 years of life.  Why A wrong: 29 days to 1 year is infancy.  Why C wrong: 3 to 6 years is preschool/early childhood.  Why D wrong: 6 to 12 years falls under school age."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The preschool child or early childhood stage is defined as:","options":["1 to 3 years","3 to 6 years","6 to 10 years","12 to 14 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preschool child/early childhood stage is 3 to 6 years.  Why A wrong: 1 to 3 years is the toddler stage.  Why C wrong: 6 to 10 years is school age for girls.  Why D wrong: 12 to 14 years is early adolescence."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"As per the book, the school age (middle childhood) period for GIRLS is:","options":["6 to 10 years","6 to 12 years","3 to 6 years","10 to 19 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: School age/middle childhood for girls is 6 to 10 years.  Why B wrong: 6 to 12 years is the school age range for boys.  Why C wrong: 3 to 6 years is preschool.  Why D wrong: 10 to 19 years is the WHO adolescent age."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"As per the book, the school age (middle childhood) period for BOYS is:","options":["6 to 10 years","6 to 12 years","12 to 16 years","1 to 3 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: School age/middle childhood for boys is 6 to 12 years.  Why A wrong: 6 to 10 years is the range for girls.  Why C wrong: This is not the defined school age range.  Why D wrong: 1 to 3 years is the toddler stage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The adolescent period is defined as extending from:","options":["Puberty to adulthood","Birth to puberty","1 to 3 years","Conception to birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Adolescence is defined as the period from puberty to adulthood.  Why B wrong: Birth to puberty covers earlier childhood stages.  Why C wrong: 1 to 3 years is the toddler stage.  Why D wrong: Conception to birth is the prenatal period."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The \"early adolescent\" stage is defined as:","options":["12 to 14 years","14 to 16 years","16 to 20 years","10 to 12 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Early adolescent is 12 to 14 years.  Why B wrong: 14 to 16 years is middle adolescence.  Why C wrong: 16 to 20 years is late adolescence.  Why D wrong: 10 to 12 years is not the defined early adolescent range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The \"middle adolescent\" stage is defined as:","options":["12 to 14 years","14 to 16 years","16 to 20 years","10 to 19 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Middle adolescent is 14 to 16 years.  Why A wrong: 12 to 14 years is early adolescence.  Why C wrong: 16 to 20 years is late adolescence.  Why D wrong: 10 to 19 years is the WHO adolescent age."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The \"late adolescent\" stage is defined as:","options":["12 to 14 years","14 to 16 years","16 to 20 years","20 to 25 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Late adolescent is 16 to 20 years.  Why A wrong: 12 to 14 years is early adolescence.  Why B wrong: 14 to 16 years is middle adolescence.  Why D wrong: 20 to 25 years is beyond the defined adolescent range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to WHO (as noted in MP CHO-2022), the adolescent age is defined as:","options":["12 to 20 years","10 to 19 years","13 to 18 years","10 to 24 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that according to WHO the adolescent age is 10 to 19 years (MP CHO-2022).  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the WHO definition of 10 to 19 years given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Select ALL of the following that are listed as factors affecting growth and development.","options":["Age, sex and genetic factors","Prenatal, natal and postnatal factors","Nutritional factors","Hormonal changes","Socioeconomic factors"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The listed factors affecting growth and development are age/sex/genetic factors; prenatal, natal and postnatal factors; environmental factors; nutritional factors; socioeconomic factors; and hormonal changes — all options are correct.  (No incorrect option present; environmental factors are also included in the source list.)"},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In the circulatory system, the heart rate of a child shows which pattern with aging?","options":["Increases with aging","Decreases with aging","Remains constant throughout life","Fluctuates randomly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The heart rate is decreasing with aging.  Why A wrong: Blood pressure increases with aging, but heart rate decreases.  Why C wrong: Heart rate changes (decreases) with age.  Why D wrong: The change is a consistent decrease, not random."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The normal heart rate for an infant from birth to 3 months is:","options":["80 to 150 beats/minute","110 to 160 beats/minute","60 to 100 beats/minute","100 to 110 beats/minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: From birth to 3 months the heart rate is 110 to 160 beats/minute.  Why A wrong: 80 to 150 beats/minute is for 3 months to 2 years.  Why C wrong: 60 to 100 is a normal adult range, not this infant range.  Why D wrong: 100 to 110 mm Hg is a systolic BP value, not a heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The normal heart rate for a child aged 3 months to 2 years is:","options":["110 to 160 beats/minute","80 to 150 beats/minute","60 to 100 beats/minute","16 to 18 beats/minute"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: From 3 months to 2 years the heart rate is 80 to 150 beats/minute.  Why A wrong: 110 to 160 is for birth to 3 months.  Why C wrong: 60 to 100 is a normal adult range.  Why D wrong: 16 to 18/min is the adolescent respiratory rate, not heart rate."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which site is described as the most accurate or reliable site for assessing the pulse in an infant?","options":["Radial pulse","Carotid pulse","Apical pulse","Popliteal pulse"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The apical pulse is the most accurate or reliable site for pulse assessment in an infant (other sites listed are femoral and brachial).  Why A wrong: Radial pulse is not listed and is less reliable in infants.  Why B wrong: Carotid pulse is not the listed reliable infant site.  Why D wrong: Popliteal is not among the listed assessment sites."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Blood pressure in children shows which pattern with aging?","options":["Decreases with aging","Increases with aging","Remains fixed","Same as heart rate pattern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood pressure is increasing with aging.  Why A wrong: Heart rate decreases; BP increases.  Why C wrong: BP changes (increases) with age.  Why D wrong: Heart rate decreases with aging, so BP does NOT follow the same pattern."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"For a child aged 0 to 7 years, the normal systolic blood pressure range is stated as:","options":["55 to 70 mm Hg","100 to 110 mm Hg","120 to 150 mm Hg","90 to 100 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 0 to 7 years the systolic BP is 100 to 110 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 55 to 70 mm Hg is the diastolic value for this age.  Why C wrong: 120 to 150 mm Hg is an adult suction pressure value, not BP.  Why D wrong: 90 to 100 ml is the total lung capacity in a newborn, not BP."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"For a child aged 0 to 7 years, the normal diastolic blood pressure range is stated as:","options":["100 to 110 mm Hg","55 to 70 mm Hg","80 to 100 mm Hg","60 to 80 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 0 to 7 years the diastolic BP is 55 to 70 mm Hg.  Why A wrong: 100 to 110 mm Hg is the systolic value.  Why C wrong: 80 to 100 mm Hg is a suction pressure for term newborn/infant.  Why D wrong: 60 to 80 mm Hg is the preterm suction pressure."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The appropriate blood pressure cuff size for a child should cover approximately what percentage of the arm width?","options":["25 to 40 % arm width","45 to 70 % arm width","80 to 100 % arm width","10 to 20 % arm width"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The appropriate cuff size is 45 to 70 % of arm width.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 45 to 70 % range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"For blood pressure assessment, which site is recommended for younger children?","options":["Upper extremity","Lower extremity","Neck","Abdomen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For BP assessment, the lower extremity is used for younger children and the upper extremity for older children.  Why A wrong: Upper extremity is for older children.  Why C wrong: The neck is not a listed BP assessment site.  Why D wrong: The abdomen is not a BP assessment site."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The normal haemoglobin level at birth in a newborn is:","options":["13 to 17 gm/100 ml","17 to 24 gm/100 ml","10 to 12 gm/100 ml","6 to 8 gm/100 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At birth the haemoglobin is 17 to 24 gm/100 ml Hb.  Why A wrong: 13 to 17 gm/100 ml is the value by 1 year.  Why C, D wrong: These are lower than the stated at-birth range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"By 1 year of age, the haemoglobin level is expected to be approximately:","options":["17 to 24 gm/100 ml","13 to 17 gm/100 ml","6 to 10 gm/100 ml","20 to 25 gm/100 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: By 1 year the haemoglobin is 13 to 17 gm/100 ml.  Why A wrong: 17 to 24 gm/100 ml is the at-birth value.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 1-year value."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) after birth is totally replaced by adult haemoglobin (as per AIIMS-2015):","options":["Within 6 weeks of life","Within 24 hours","Within 1 year","Within 2 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetal Hb after birth is totally replaced by adult Hb within 6 weeks of life (AIIMS-2015).  Why B wrong: 24 hours is the time within which a newborn first voids urine.  Why C wrong: 1 year is when Hb reaches 13 to 17 gm/100 ml, not when HbF is replaced.  Why D wrong: 2 years relates to brain development and GFR reaching adult value."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Body fluid constitutes approximately what percentage of body weight at birth in a newborn?","options":["60 %","55 %","80 %","45 %"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Body fluid is 80 % of body weight at birth in a newborn.  Why A wrong: 60 % is the value in an adult.  Why B wrong: 55 % is the value in an older adult.  Why D wrong: 45 % is the ECF proportion in an infant at birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Total body water/body fluid constitutes what percentage of body weight in an adult?","options":["80 %","60 %","55 %","45 %"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Body fluid is 60 % in an adult.  Why A wrong: 80 % is at birth in the newborn.  Why C wrong: 55 % is in the older adult.  Why D wrong: 45 % is the infant ECF proportion."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Body fluid constitutes what percentage of body weight in an older adult?","options":["80 %","60 %","55 %","30 %"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Body fluid is 55 % in an older adult.  Why A wrong: 80 % is at birth.  Why B wrong: 60 % is in an adult.  Why D wrong: 30 % is the adult ECF proportion, not total body fluid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In an infant at birth, the extracellular fluid (ECF) and intracellular fluid (ICF) proportions are respectively:","options":["ECF 30 % and ICF 70 %","ECF 45 % and ICF 25 %","ECF 25 % and ICF 45 %","ECF 70 % and ICF 30 %"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In an infant at birth, ECF is 45 % and ICF is 25 %.  Why A wrong: ECF 30 % and ICF 70 % is the adult distribution.  Why C wrong: Values are reversed.  Why D wrong: These are reversed/incorrect for the infant."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In an adult, the body fluid distribution of extracellular fluid (ECF) and intracellular fluid (ICF) is:","options":["ECF 45 % and ICF 25 %","ECF 30 % and ICF 70 %","ECF 70 % and ICF 30 %","ECF 25 % and ICF 45 %"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the adult, ECF is 30 % and ICF is 70 %.  Why A wrong: ECF 45 %/ICF 25 % is the infant distribution.  Why C wrong: Values are reversed.  Why D wrong: These match neither infant nor adult correctly."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The total lung capacity in a newborn is approximately:","options":["30 to 60 ml","90 to 100 ml","150 to 200 ml","250 to 300 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Total lung capacity in a newborn is 90 to 100 ml.  Why A wrong: 30 to 60 ml relates to stomach size ranges, not lung capacity.  Why C, D wrong: These exceed the stated newborn lung capacity."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"How does the respiratory rate change as a child ages?","options":["Increases as age increases","Decreases as age increases","Stays constant","Doubles at puberty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The respiratory rate decreases as age increases.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases, with age.  Why C wrong: It changes (decreases) with age.  Why D wrong: The source states a decrease, not doubling."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The normal respiratory rate during infancy is stated as:","options":["30 to 40 breaths/min","20 to 24 breaths/min","16 to 18 breaths/min","12 to 16 breaths/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The respiratory rate in infancy is 30 to 40 breaths/min.  Why B wrong: 20 to 24 breaths/min is for childhood.  Why C wrong: 16 to 18 breaths/min is for adolescence.  Why D wrong: This is not a range stated for these age groups."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The normal respiratory rate during childhood is stated as:","options":["30 to 40 breaths/min","20 to 24 breaths/min","16 to 18 breaths/min","40 to 60 breaths/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The respiratory rate in childhood is 20 to 24 breaths/min.  Why A wrong: 30 to 40 breaths/min is for infancy.  Why C wrong: 16 to 18 breaths/min is for adolescence.  Why D wrong: 40 to 60 is not a stated range here (30 to 60 is the newborn breathing rate at birth in Step-02)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The normal respiratory rate during adolescence is stated as:","options":["30 to 40 breaths/min","20 to 24 breaths/min","16 to 18 breaths/min","24 to 30 breaths/min"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The respiratory rate in adolescence is 16 to 18 breaths/min.  Why A wrong: 30 to 40 breaths/min is for infancy.  Why B wrong: 20 to 24 breaths/min is for childhood.  Why D wrong: This is not a stated range for adolescence."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, vital capacity in children shows which pattern?","options":["Gradual increase throughout childhood and adolescence","Rapid decrease with age","No change after birth","Decreases during puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vital capacity gradually increases throughout childhood and adolescence.  Why B, C, D wrong: The source specifies a gradual increase, not a decrease or no change."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Basal metabolism in the newborn compared with later life is described as:","options":["Lower in the newborn and increases with aging","Higher in the newborn and decreases with aging","Unchanged throughout life","Absent at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Basal metabolism is higher in the newborn, and the rate decreases with aging.  Why A wrong: It is higher, not lower, in the newborn.  Why C wrong: It changes (decreases) with aging.  Why D wrong: It is present and high at birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The urine specific gravity range in a newborn is stated as (NIA Jaipur-2023):","options":["1.015 to 1.030","1.001 to 1.020","1.030 to 1.040","1.000 to 1.005"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The newborn specific gravity is 1.001 to 1.020 (NIA Jaipur-2023).  Why A wrong: 1.015 to 1.030 is the adult specific gravity.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated newborn range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The urine specific gravity range in an adult is stated as:","options":["1.001 to 1.020","1.015 to 1.030","1.030 to 1.050","1.000 to 1.010"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The adult specific gravity is 1.015 to 1.030.  Why A wrong: 1.001 to 1.020 is the newborn value.  Why C, D wrong: These do not match the stated adult range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Within what time frame can a newborn void their first urine (PGI-2018)?","options":["Within 6 hours","Within 12 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A newborn can void the first urine within 24 hours (PGI-2018).  Why A wrong: 6 hours relates to when Vitamin K should be given (within 6 hours after 1 hour of birth).  Why B wrong: 12 hours relates to Hep-B vaccine timing in an HBsAg-positive mother.  Why D wrong: 24 to 48 h relates to anti-hepatitis B immunoglobulin administration."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) reaches adult value at what age?","options":["Between 1 to 2 years of age","At birth","By 6 months","By 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The GFR rapidly increases in the first 6 months and reaches adult value between 1 to 2 years of age.  Why B wrong: It is not at adult value at birth.  Why C wrong: It rapidly increases in the first 6 months but reaches adult value between 1 to 2 years.  Why D wrong: The source states 1 to 2 years, not 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, the GFR rapidly increases during which period after birth?","options":["First 6 months","First 6 weeks","First 2 years only","First 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The GFR rapidly increases in the first 6 months of life.  Why B wrong: 6 weeks is the timeframe for fetal Hb replacement.  Why C wrong: Adult value is reached between 1 to 2 years, but rapid increase is in the first 6 months.  Why D wrong: 24 hours relates to first urine voiding."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The stomach capacity of an early neonate is approximately:","options":["30 ml","60 to 90 ml","150 ml","250 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stomach size in an early neonate is 30 ml.  Why B wrong: 60 to 90 ml is the late neonate stomach size.  Why C wrong: 150 ml is the infant stomach size.  Why D wrong: 250 ml exceeds all stated values."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The stomach capacity of a late neonate is approximately:","options":["30 ml","60 to 90 ml","150 ml","200 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The stomach size in a late neonate is 60 to 90 ml.  Why A wrong: 30 ml is the early neonate stomach size.  Why C wrong: 150 ml is the infant stomach size.  Why D wrong: 200 ml exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The stomach capacity of an infant is approximately:","options":["30 ml","60 to 90 ml","150 ml","90 to 100 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The stomach size in an infant is 150 ml.  Why A wrong: 30 ml is the early neonate size.  Why B wrong: 60 to 90 ml is the late neonate size.  Why D wrong: 90 to 100 ml is the newborn total lung capacity, not stomach size."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In the digestive system, peristalsis activity changes in which way with aging?","options":["Increases with aging","Decreases with aging","Remains unchanged","Stops at adolescence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peristalsis activity decreases with aging.  Why A wrong: It decreases, not increases.  Why C wrong: It changes (decreases) with age.  Why D wrong: The source does not state it stops at adolescence."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, blood sugar level changes in which way with aging?","options":["Decreases with aging","Increases with aging","Remains fixed","Is highest at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blood sugar level increases with aging.  Why A wrong: It increases, not decreases.  Why C wrong: It changes (increases) with age.  Why D wrong: The source states it increases with age, not that it is highest at birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"HCl (hydrochloric acid) secretion in the stomach changes in which way with aging?","options":["Increases with aging till adolescence","Decreases with aging","Is absent until 2 years","Remains constant from birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HCl secretion increases with aging till adolescence.  Why B wrong: It increases, not decreases (peristalsis is what decreases).  Why C wrong: The source does not state absence until 2 years.  Why D wrong: It increases with age until adolescence, so it is not constant."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Regarding digestive enzymes at birth, which statement is TRUE?","options":["No enzymes are present at birth","All enzymes are sufficiently present at birth to digest protein, a moderate amount of fat, and simple carbohydrate","Only fat-digesting enzymes are present","Enzymes appear only after 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: All enzymes are sufficiently present at birth to digest protein, a moderate amount of fat, and simple carbohydrate.  Why A wrong: Enzymes are present at birth.  Why C wrong: Enzymes for protein and simple carbohydrate are also present, not just fat.  Why D wrong: They are present at birth, not delayed to 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"By what age is the brain approximately 90% developed?","options":["At birth","By 1 year","By 2 years","By 5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The brain is 90% developed at the age of 2 years.  Why A wrong: It is not 90% developed at birth.  Why B wrong: The source states 2 years, not 1 year.  Why D wrong: The source states 2 years, not 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In the routine care of the newborn, what is the FIRST step immediately after birth?","options":["Weigh the newborn","Dry and stimulate the baby","Apply the identity band","Give the BCG vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step-01 of routine newborn care is to dry and stimulate the baby (especially the head) with a warm dry towel.  Why A wrong: Weighing is Step-09, done after stabilization.  Why C wrong: Applying the identity band is Step-08.  Why D wrong: BCG is part of newborn immunization, not the first routine care step."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"After thorough drying, a newborn who does NOT breathe spontaneously should be stimulated by:","options":["Slapping the buttocks firmly","Rubbing the back 2-3 times before clamping the cord and initiating positive pressure ventilation","Immediately giving chest compressions","Immersing in warm water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Newborns who do not breathe spontaneously after thorough drying should be stimulated by rubbing the back 2-3 times before clamping the cord and initiating positive pressure ventilation.  Why A wrong: Slapping is not the recommended stimulation method.  Why C wrong: Chest compressions are not the initial stimulation step.  Why D wrong: Immersion in water is not recommended; the baby must be kept warm and dry."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"During the assessment of breathing, the normal respiratory rate for a newborn at birth is:","options":["12 to 20 breaths per minute","20 to 30 breaths per minute","30 to 60 breaths per minute","60 to 90 breaths per minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In Step-02, the normal newborn breathing rate is 30 to 60 breaths per minute.  Why A, B wrong: These are below the stated newborn range.  Why D wrong: This exceeds the stated newborn range."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A newborn is gasping and not breathing well. According to the book, the cord should be clamped and cut leaving a stump of at least:","options":["2 cm long","5 cm long","10 cm long","15 cm long"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If the baby is having trouble breathing/gasping/not breathing, quickly clamp and cut the cord leaving a stump at least 10 cm long, call for help and start resuscitation immediately.  Why A, B wrong: These are shorter than the stated 10 cm.  Why D wrong: The book specifies at least 10 cm, not 15 cm."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A blue colour of the tongue, lips, and trunk in a newborn indicates:","options":["A normal finding requiring no action","A sign of a lack of oxygen in the blood (central cyanosis)","Acrocyanosis, which is harmless","Hyperoxygenation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A blue colour of the tongue, lips and trunk is a sign of a lack of oxygen in the blood (central cyanosis).  Why A wrong: It is an abnormal finding indicating hypoxia.  Why C wrong: Acrocyanosis (hands and feet only) is the harmless finding, not central cyanosis of the trunk.  Why D wrong: It indicates lack of oxygen, not excess."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A newborn has a bluish colour of only the hands and feet after birth. This finding:","options":["Always indicates severe hypoxia","May be present after birth and usually does not indicate a lack of oxygen","Requires immediate intubation","Is a sign of central cyanosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A bluish colour of only the hands and feet (acrocyanosis) may be present after birth and usually does not indicate a lack of oxygen.  Why A wrong: It usually does NOT indicate hypoxia.  Why C wrong: It does not require immediate intubation.  Why D wrong: Central cyanosis involves the trunk/lips/tongue, not just the extremities."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The APGAR score should be continuously assessed at which time intervals?","options":["At 1 and 5 minutes, and if necessary again at 15 minutes","Only at 1 minute","At 10 and 20 minutes","Every hour for the first day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Continuously assess the APGAR score at 1 and 5 minutes, and if necessary it can be done again at 15 minutes.  Why B wrong: It is assessed at both 1 and 5 minutes (not only 1 minute).  Why C wrong: The stated intervals are 1, 5, and 15 minutes.  Why D wrong: Hourly assessment for a day is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to routine newborn care, the umbilical cord should be clamped and cut within what time frame after birth for all births?","options":["Immediately (within seconds)","Within 1 to 3 minutes","After 5 minutes","After 10 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cut the cord within 1 to 3 minutes after birth for all births while initiating simultaneous essential newborn care.  Why A wrong: Immediate/early cord clamping (<1 minute) is not recommended unless asphyxiated.  Why C, D wrong: The recommended window is 1 to 3 minutes, not 5 or 10 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Early cord clamping (less than 1 minute after birth) is recommended ONLY in which situation?","options":["All normal deliveries","When the neonate is asphyxiated and needs to be moved immediately for resuscitation","When the mother is tired","Whenever twins are born"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Early cord clamping (<1 minute after birth) is not recommended unless the neonate is asphyxiated and needs to be moved immediately for resuscitation.  Why A wrong: It is not recommended in normal deliveries.  Why C wrong: Maternal fatigue is not a stated indication.  Why D wrong: Twin birth alone is not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which cord care is recommended for the umbilical stump of a newborn?","options":["Apply antiseptic powder and cover tightly","Do not apply anything on the cord stump, leave it uncovered; clean, dry cord care is recommended","Apply spirit and bandage daily","Keep it moist with oil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Do not apply anything on the cord stump and leave it uncovered; clean, dry cord care is recommended for newborns in health facilities and at home.  Why A wrong: Nothing should be applied and it should not be covered tightly.  Why C wrong: Applying spirit/bandage is contrary to dry cord care.  Why D wrong: Keeping it moist with oil is not recommended; dry care is advised."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"To prevent hypothermia and promote breastfeeding, a newborn without complications should be kept in:","options":["An incubator away from the mother","Skin-to-skin contact with the mother during the first hour after birth","A radiant warmer for 24 hours","A separate warm room"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Newborns without complications should be kept in skin-to-skin contact with the mother during the first hour after birth to prevent hypothermia and promote breastfeeding.  Why A wrong: Routine separation in an incubator is not indicated for a well newborn.  Why C wrong: A radiant warmer for 24 hours is not the stated routine care.  Why D wrong: Separation into another room is not recommended for a well newborn."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, breastfeeding should be initiated within what time frame after birth?","options":["Within the first hour of birth","After 6 hours","After 24 hours","Only after weighing the baby"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Help the mother initiate breastfeeding within the first hour of birth.  Why B, C wrong: Breastfeeding should be within the first hour, not delayed.  Why D wrong: The completion of the first breastfeed should actually be given priority OVER weighing."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Exclusive breastfeeding should be promoted for the first:","options":["3 months","4 months","6 months","12 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Promote exclusive breastfeeding for the first six months.  Why A, B wrong: The recommended duration of exclusive breastfeeding is six months, not 3 or 4 months.  Why D wrong: Exclusive breastfeeding is for 6 months, after which complementary feeding begins."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Newborn eye antimicrobial medication (eye care) should be given within what time frame after birth to protect against serious eye infection?","options":["Within 1 hour after birth","Within 6 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Within 1 hour after birth, give the newborn eye antimicrobial medication to protect the baby from serious eye infection.  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated time frame for eye care is within 1 hour after birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"ARV (antiretroviral) prophylaxis should be given to which newborns, according to the PMTCT guidelines?","options":["All newborns","All HIV-exposed neonates according to national PMTCT guidelines","Only preterm neonates","Only low-birth-weight babies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Give ARV prophylaxis to all HIV-exposed neonates according to the national guidelines on prevention of mother to child transmission (PMTCT).  Why A wrong: It is for HIV-exposed neonates, not all newborns.  Why C, D wrong: The indication is HIV exposure, not prematurity or low birth weight."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The standard dose and route of Vitamin K given to a term neonate at birth to prevent haemorrhage is:","options":["0.5 mg orally","1 mg intramuscularly (IM) in the vastus lateralis muscle of the mid-thigh","2 mg subcutaneously","5 mg intravenously"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer 1 mg of Vitamin K intramuscularly (IM) in the vastus lateralis muscle of the mid-thigh to all neonates (including preterm) after birth to prevent haemorrhage or bleeding.  Why A wrong: 0.5 mg is the IM dose for a neonate less than 1 kg; the oral option is 2 mg.  Why C wrong: The route is IM, not subcutaneous.  Why D wrong: 5 mg IV is not the stated dose/route."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The IM dose of Vitamin K for a neonate weighing less than 1 kg (1000 gm) is:","options":["1 mg","0.5 mg","2 mg","0.05 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer 0.5 mg Vitamin K through the IM route to a neonate less than 1 kg (1000 gm).  Why A wrong: 1 mg is the standard dose for neonates (including preterm) at birth.  Why C wrong: 2 mg is the oral dose option.  Why D wrong: 0.05 ml is the BCG dose, not the Vitamin K dose."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the book, Vitamin K should be administered within what time window relative to birth?","options":["Immediately at birth only","Within 6 hours but after 1 hour following the birth","Within 24 hours only","Between day 2 and 7"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K should be administered within 6 hours but after 1 hour following the birth.  Why A wrong: It is given after 1 hour, not immediately.  Why C wrong: The window is within 6 hours (after 1 hour), not simply 24 hours.  Why D wrong: Day 2 to 7 refers to the second oral dose for parents refusing IM Vitamin K."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"If parents refuse IM Vitamin K and the neonate is formula-fed without risk of haemorrhage, the recommended oral regimen is:","options":["A single 1 mg oral dose at birth","A 2 mg oral dose at birth, followed by a second 2 mg oral dose between day 2 and 7","0.5 mg oral daily for a week","No Vitamin K is needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If parents refuse IM Vitamin K and for formula-fed neonates without risk of haemorrhage, a 2 mg oral dose at birth followed by a second 2 mg oral dose between day 2 and 7 is probably sufficient to prevent VKDB.  Why A wrong: The oral regimen is 2 mg with a repeat dose, not a single 1 mg dose.  Why C wrong: 0.5 mg is an IM dose for very low birth weight, not the oral regimen.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K prophylaxis is still needed to prevent VKDB."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Vitamin K helps in the formation of which clotting factors in the liver?","options":["Factors 1, 3, 5, 8","Factors 2, 7, 9, 10","Factors 4, 6, 11, 12","Factors 5, 8, 13"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K is administered because it helps in the formation of clotting factors 2, 7, 9, 10 by the liver, so it prevents bleeding in the newborn.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated Vitamin K-dependent factors (2, 7, 9, 10)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Why is a newborn especially at risk of Vitamin K deficiency bleeding?","options":["The newborn liver cannot process Vitamin K","Vitamin K normally is present in the GIT, but in the newborn the intestine is sterile","Newborns are not given breast milk","Vitamin K is destroyed by stomach acid in newborns"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vitamin K is normally present in the GIT (produced by gut bacteria), but in the newborn the intestine is sterile, so Vitamin K prophylaxis is needed.  Why A wrong: The issue is the sterile gut, not liver inability to process it.  Why C wrong: Lack of breast milk is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Destruction by stomach acid is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"If the umbilical cord stump is found to be bleeding, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Apply direct pressure with bare hands","Put an additional clamp between the abdomen and the existing clamp","Cut the cord shorter","Apply a tourniquet around the abdomen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Check if the cord stump is bleeding; if it is, put on an additional clamp between the abdomen and the existing clamp.  Why A wrong: Applying pressure with bare hands is not the stated intervention.  Why C wrong: Cutting the cord shorter is not recommended for bleeding.  Why D wrong: A tourniquet around the abdomen is not appropriate."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The newborn identification band should be placed on the wrist or ankle and must include:","options":["The baby's blood group and weight","The mother's name and the sex of the baby","The father's name and religion","The delivery time only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the infant's identification band on the wrist or ankle with the mother's name and sex of the baby on it.  Why A wrong: Blood group and weight are not the stated band contents.  Why C wrong: Father's name and religion are not required on the band.  Why D wrong: Delivery time is called out but is not the stated content of the identity band."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Regarding weighing the newborn, which statement reflects the book's recommendation?","options":["The baby must be weighed immediately at birth before anything else","It is not necessary to weigh immediately; all infants should be weighed after stabilization and warmth, and the first breastfeed given priority over weighing","Weighing should be delayed for 24 hours","Weight should not be recorded on the mother's chart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: It is not necessary to weigh immediately; infants should be weighed after stabilization and warmth, and completion of the first breastfeed should be given priority over weighing.  Why A wrong: Immediate weighing is not necessary.  Why C wrong: The source does not state a 24-hour delay; it says after stabilization/warmth.  Why D wrong: Weight should be recorded on the mother's chart, delivery book and other records."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the initial assessment of the newborn, which action is the FIRST priority?","options":["Assess the APGAR score","Observe or assist with the initiation of respiration","Obtain footprints","Assess for gross anomalies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Observing or assisting with the initiation of respiration is the first priority in the initial assessment of the newborn.  Why A wrong: Assessing APGAR follows, but respiration is the first priority.  Why C wrong: Footprints/finger prints are obtained but are not the first priority.  Why D wrong: Assessing for gross anomalies is part of the assessment but not the first priority."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Select ALL components included in the initial assessment of the newborn.","options":["Assess the APGAR score","Note characteristics of the cry","Assess for central cyanosis and acrocyanosis","Assess for signs of respiratory distress","Assess for gross anomalies"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The initial newborn assessment includes observing initiation of respiration, assessing APGAR, noting cry characteristics, assessing for respiratory distress, assessing for central cyanosis and acrocyanosis, obtaining vital signs and foot/hand prints, observing for hypothermia, and assessing for gross anomalies — all listed options are included.  (No incorrect option; all are part of the initial assessment list.)"},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Routine nasal, oral, and tracheal suctioning is NOT recommended in which neonates?","options":["Neonates born through liquor (with or without meconium) who start breathing on their own and are vigorous (active)","All meconium-stained neonates","All preterm neonates","Neonates who are not breathing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Routine suctioning is not recommended in neonates born through liquor with or without meconium who start breathing on their own and are vigorous (active).  Why B wrong: Meconium-stained neonates who do NOT start breathing on their own should be suctioned before PPV.  Why C wrong: Prematurity alone is not the stated criterion.  Why D wrong: A non-breathing neonate may need suctioning/PPV; the \"no suction\" rule applies to vigorous babies."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In a neonate born through MECONIUM-stained amniotic fluid who does NOT start breathing on their own, suctioning of the mouth and nose should be:","options":["Avoided entirely","Done before initiating positive pressure ventilation","Done only after 10 minutes","Done only if the baby cries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In neonates born through meconium-stained amniotic fluid who do not start breathing on their own, suctioning of the mouth and nose should be done before initiating positive pressure ventilation.  Why A wrong: In this specific scenario, suctioning is indicated (not avoided).  Why C wrong: It should be done before PPV, not after a 10-minute delay.  Why D wrong: These are non-breathing babies; suction is done before PPV."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In a neonate born through CLEAR amniotic fluid who does not breathe after drying and back rubbing, suctioning should be done only if:","options":["The baby is preterm","The mouth or nose is full of secretions","The APGAR is below 7","The mother requests it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In neonates born through clear amniotic fluid who do not start breathing after thorough drying/back rubbing, suctioning should not be done routinely before PPV, and should be done only if the mouth or nose is full of secretions.  Why A wrong: Prematurity is not the stated criterion.  Why C wrong: APGAR value is not the stated trigger for suctioning here.  Why D wrong: Maternal request is not the criterion."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Deep and aggressive pharyngeal stimulation with a suction catheter should be avoided because it may cause:","options":["Tachycardia and hypertension","Vagal stimulation leading to bradycardia and arrhythmia","Hyperthermia","Excessive salivation only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deep and aggressive pharyngeal stimulation with a suction catheter may cause vagal stimulation, which leads to bradycardia and arrhythmia, so it should be avoided.  Why A wrong: It causes bradycardia (slow heart rate), not tachycardia.  Why C wrong: Hyperthermia is not the stated effect.  Why D wrong: The concern is vagal bradycardia/arrhythmia, not salivation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Select ALL of the FOUR basic needs of a baby at birth.","options":["Warmth","Normal breathing","Mother's milk","Protection from infections","Immediate vaccination"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four basic needs of a baby at birth are warmth, normal breathing, mother's milk, and protection from infections.  Why E wrong: Immediate vaccination is important but is not one of the four listed basic needs at birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which vaccines are given as neonatal vaccination at birth (MP NHM-2023)?","options":["DPT, OPV, measles","BCG, OPV, and Hep-B","MMR, hepatitis A, rotavirus","Typhoid, BCG, measles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Neonatal vaccination at birth includes BCG, OPV, and Hep-B (MP NHM-2023).  Why A wrong: DPT and measles are given later, not at birth.  Why C wrong: MMR, hepatitis A, and rotavirus are not birth-dose vaccines here.  Why D wrong: Typhoid and measles are not given at birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"When obtaining a heel-stick blood specimen from a newborn, the puncture should be taken from which site to prevent lancet penetration of the bone (UP NHM-CHO-2018)?","options":["The centre of the heel","The outer aspect of the newborn's heel","The great toe","The medial arch of the foot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A heel-stick blood specimen should be taken from the outer aspect of the newborn's heel to prevent lancet penetration of the bone (UP NHM-CHO-2018).  Why A wrong: The centre of the heel risks bone penetration.  Why C wrong: The great toe is not the recommended site.  Why D wrong: The medial arch is not the recommended heel-stick site."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to CDC and WHO, an umbilical venous catheter (UVC) may be used for up to how long if managed aseptically?","options":["Up to 3 days","Up to 7 days","Up to 14 days","Up to 28 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The CDC and WHO recommend that UVCs be removed as soon as possible when no longer needed, but they can be used for up to 14 days if managed aseptically.  Why A, B wrong: These are shorter than the stated maximum of 14 days.  Why D wrong: 28 days exceeds the stated maximum of 14 days."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"If the mother is HBsAg positive, the first dose of anti-hepatitis B vaccine should be given to the baby within what time frame following delivery?","options":["Within the first 12 hours","Within 24 hours","Within 48 hours","Within 6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the mother is HBsAg positive, administer the first dose of anti-hepatitis B vaccine within the first 12 hours following delivery.  Why B wrong: 24 hours is the general preferred timing for Hep-B in non-exposed newborns.  Why C wrong: 48 hours relates to the outer limit for immunoglobulin (24 to 48 h), not the vaccine timing.  Why D wrong: 6 hours relates to Vitamin K timing."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"For a baby born to an HBsAg-positive mother, the anti-hepatitis B vaccine dose and route are:","options":["0.05 ml intradermally","0.5 ml IM in the quadriceps muscle","2 mg orally","1 ml subcutaneously in the deltoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For an HBsAg-positive mother, administer 0.5 ml IM in the quadriceps muscle to the baby.  Why A wrong: 0.05 ml ID is the BCG dose.  Why C wrong: 2 mg oral refers to Vitamin K.  Why D wrong: The route/site is IM into the quadriceps, not SC deltoid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"If anti-hepatitis B immunoglobulin is available for a baby of an HBsAg-positive mother, the recommended dose and timing are:","options":["100 IU IM within 12 hours","200 IU IM in the first 24 to 48 hours of life","500 IU IV within 6 hours","50 IU orally at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If anti-hepatitis B immunoglobulin is available, administer 200 IU IM in the first 24 to 48 hours of life.  Why A wrong: The dose is 200 IU, not 100 IU, and the window is 24 to 48 h.  Why C wrong: 500 IU IV within 6 hours is incorrect.  Why D wrong: It is given IM, not orally, and the dose is 200 IU."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In the WHO summary of immediate care of a normal newborn, what is the FIRST listed step?","options":["Clamp and cut the umbilical cord","Call out the time of birth","Dry the baby","Place an identity label"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first step in the WHO summary is to call out the time of birth.  Why A wrong: Clamping/cutting the cord is step 3.  Why C wrong: Drying the baby is step 4.  Why D wrong: Placing the identity label is step 8."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In the WHO immediate newborn care, after delivery the baby should be placed onto a warm, clean, dry towel and kept:","options":["In a separate cot","On the mother's chest and abdomen (between the breasts)","Under a radiant warmer immediately","In the father's arms"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deliver the baby onto a warm, clean, dry towel and keep on the mother's chest and abdomen (between the breasts).  Why A wrong: A separate cot is contrary to skin-to-skin care.  Why C wrong: The baby is placed on the mother, not immediately under a warmer.  Why D wrong: The baby is kept on the mother's chest/abdomen."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the WHO immediate newborn care steps, the eyes should be wiped:","options":["Together with a single swab","Both eyes (separately) with sterile gauze","With plain water","With antiseptic solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wipe both the eyes (separately) with sterile gauze.  Why A wrong: Each eye should be wiped separately, not with a single swab.  Why C wrong: Sterile gauze is used, not plain water.  Why D wrong: The step specifies sterile gauze, not antiseptic solution."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"In the WHO immediate newborn care, covering the baby's head with a cap and covering the mother and baby with a warm cloth helps to:","options":["Increase the APGAR score","Maintain warmth / prevent heat loss","Facilitate weighing","Promote the moro reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Covering the head with a cap and covering the mother and baby with a warm cloth (Step 9) is a warmth-maintenance/heat-loss prevention measure (the head is a major site of heat loss).  Why A wrong: It does not directly increase the APGAR score.  Why C wrong: It is not for facilitating weighing.  Why D wrong: It is unrelated to eliciting the moro reflex."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which of the following is a sign of respiratory distress in a newborn?","options":["Nasal flaring and intercostal retraction","Regular relaxed breathing","Pink tongue and lips","Sleeping quietly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nasal flaring (dilation of the nostril) and intercostal retraction (compression of the space between the ribs during respiration) are signs of respiratory distress.  Why B wrong: Regular relaxed breathing is normal.  Why C wrong: Pink tongue and lips indicate adequate oxygenation.  Why D wrong: Sleeping quietly is not a sign of distress."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Intercostal retraction as a sign of respiratory distress refers to:","options":["Bluish discolouration of the lips","Compression of the space between the ribs during respiration","Sudden cessation of respiration","Dilation of the nostrils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intercostal retraction is the compression of the space between the ribs during respiration.  Why A wrong: Bluish lips describe cyanosis.  Why C wrong: Sudden cessation of respiration is apnoea.  Why D wrong: Dilation of the nostrils is nasal flaring, a separate sign."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"\"Seesaw respiration\" (abdominal respiration) as a sign of respiratory distress means:","options":["The chest and abdomen rise and fall together smoothly","The rise and fall of the chest and abdomen do NOT occur together","Breathing stops for over 20 seconds","Rapid regular breathing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A seesaw respiration pattern (with grunting on expiration and abdominal respiration) means the rise and fall of the chest and abdomen do not occur together.  Why A wrong: Synchronous rise and fall is normal, not seesaw.  Why C wrong: A pause over 20 seconds describes apnoea, not seesaw breathing.  Why D wrong: Rapid regular breathing is tachypnoea, a separate sign."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Apnoea in the newborn is defined as:","options":["Rapid breathing","Sudden cessation of respiration","Bluish discolouration of the skin","Slow heart rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apnoea is the sudden cessation of respiration.  Why A wrong: Rapid breathing is tachypnoea.  Why C wrong: Bluish discolouration is cyanosis.  Why D wrong: A slow heart rate is bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Birth asphyxia in the newborn is described as:","options":["Rapid breathing at birth","Could not start respiration in the newborn","Bluish hands and feet only","High-pitched cry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Birth asphyxia is defined as could not start respiration in the newborn.  Why A wrong: Rapid breathing is tachypnoea, not asphyxia.  Why C wrong: Bluish hands/feet is acrocyanosis.  Why D wrong: A high-pitched cry is not the definition of birth asphyxia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"What is the FIRST sign of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in a newborn?","options":["Cyanosis","Dyspnoea","Apnoea","Bradycardia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first sign of RDS is dyspnoea.  Why A wrong: Cyanosis is described as the late sign of RDS.  Why C wrong: Apnoea is a general sign of respiratory distress, not named as the first RDS sign.  Why D wrong: Bradycardia is listed among distress signs but not as the first RDS sign."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"What is described as the LATE sign of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)?","options":["Dyspnoea","Cyanosis","Nasal flaring","Grunting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The late sign of RDS is cyanosis.  Why A wrong: Dyspnoea is the first (early) sign.  Why C wrong: Nasal flaring is an early distress sign, not the named late RDS sign.  Why D wrong: Grunting is an early distress sign, not the named late RDS sign."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The recommended suction pressure for a PRETERM newborn is (AIIMS Rishikesh-2017):","options":["60-80 mm of Hg","80-100 mm of Hg","100-120 mm of Hg","120-150 mm of Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The suction pressure for a preterm newborn is 60-80 mm of Hg (AIIMS Rishikesh-2017).  Why B wrong: 80-100 mm of Hg is for term newborns and infants.  Why C wrong: 100-120 mm of Hg is for older children.  Why D wrong: 120-150 mm of Hg is for adults."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The recommended suction pressure for a TERM newborn and infant is:","options":["60-80 mm of Hg","80-100 mm of Hg","100-120 mm of Hg","120-150 mm of Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The suction pressure for a term newborn and infant is 80-100 mm of Hg.  Why A wrong: 60-80 mm of Hg is for preterm newborns.  Why C wrong: 100-120 mm of Hg is for older children.  Why D wrong: 120-150 mm of Hg is for adults."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The recommended suction pressure for older children (toddler, pre-school, school, adolescent) is:","options":["60-80 mm of Hg","80-100 mm of Hg","100-120 mm of Hg","120-150 mm of Hg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The suction pressure for older children (toddler, pre-school, school, adolescent) is 100-120 mm of Hg.  Why A wrong: 60-80 mm of Hg is for preterm newborns.  Why B wrong: 80-100 mm of Hg is for term newborns/infants.  Why D wrong: 120-150 mm of Hg is for adults."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The recommended suction pressure for ADULTS is:","options":["60-80 mm of Hg","80-100 mm of Hg","100-120 mm of Hg","120-150 mm of Hg"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The suction pressure for adults is 120-150 mm of Hg.  Why A wrong: 60-80 mm of Hg is for preterm newborns.  Why B wrong: 80-100 mm of Hg is for term newborns/infants.  Why C wrong: 100-120 mm of Hg is for older children."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The recommended duration for a single suctioning attempt in a newborn is:","options":["1-2 seconds","5-10 seconds","15-20 seconds","30 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recommended time for suction is 5-10 seconds.  Why A wrong: 1-2 seconds is shorter than recommended.  Why C, D wrong: These exceed the recommended 5-10 seconds and risk hypoxia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Before and after suctioning a newborn, the nurse should hyper-oxygenate the baby in order to:","options":["Increase the heart rate","Prevent irreversible changes in the brain tissues","Warm the baby","Improve digestion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Before and after suctioning, hyper-oxygenate the newborn to prevent irreversible changes in the brain tissues.  Why A wrong: The purpose is preventing hypoxic brain injury, not raising heart rate.  Why C wrong: Hyper-oxygenation is not for warming.  Why D wrong: It is unrelated to digestion."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The dose and route of the Hepatitis-B vaccine given to a newborn at birth is:","options":["0.05 ml intradermal","0.5 ml IM in the vastus lateralis muscle of the mid-thigh","2 drops orally","1 mg IM in the deltoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hepatitis-B is given as 0.5 ml by IM route in the vastus lateralis muscle of the mid-thigh for protection against hepatitis B DNA viral disease.  Why A wrong: 0.05 ml ID is the BCG dose/route.  Why C wrong: 2 drops orally is the OPV route.  Why D wrong: 1 mg IM refers to Vitamin K, not Hep-B, and the site is the vastus lateralis, not deltoid."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The Hepatitis-B vaccine should be administered to a newborn preferably within:","options":["6 hours of birth","24 hours of birth","48 hours of birth","1 week of birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Hep-B vaccine should be administered as soon as possible following birth, preferably within 24 hours.  Why A wrong: 6 hours relates to Vitamin K timing.  Why C wrong: The preferred window is within 24 hours.  Why D wrong: A week is far beyond the preferred window."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"BCG (Bacillus Calmette Guerin) vaccine provides active artificial immunity primarily against:","options":["Poliomyelitis","Tuberculosis (and sometimes cross-immunity against leprosy)","Hepatitis B","Diphtheria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BCG provides active artificial immunity against tuberculosis and sometimes cross-immunity that also protects against leprosy.  Why A wrong: Poliomyelitis is prevented by OPV.  Why C wrong: Hepatitis B is prevented by the Hep-B vaccine.  Why D wrong: Diphtheria protection comes from DPT, not BCG."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The dose and route of BCG vaccine given at birth is:","options":["0.5 ml IM","0.05 ml through the intradermal (ID) route","2 drops orally","0.1 ml IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The BCG dose at birth is 0.05 ml through the ID route.  Why A wrong: 0.5 ml IM is the Hep-B dose.  Why C wrong: 2 drops orally is the OPV route.  Why D wrong: 0.1 ml ID is the catch-up dose if BCG is not given at birth (given up to 1 year), and it is ID, not IM."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"If BCG is NOT given at birth, the dose given as a catch-up (up to 1 year of age) is:","options":["0.05 ml ID","0.1 ml through the ID route","0.5 ml IM","2 drops orally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If BCG is not given at birth, then the dose should be 0.1 ml through the ID route up to 1 year of age.  Why A wrong: 0.05 ml ID is the dose when given at birth.  Why C wrong: 0.5 ml IM is the Hep-B dose.  Why D wrong: 2 drops orally is OPV."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Oral Polio Vaccine (OPV) is described as which type of vaccine?","options":["Killed (inactivated) Salk-type vaccine","Sabin-type, live vaccine","Toxoid vaccine","Subunit vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV is the Sabin-type, live vaccine.  Why A wrong: The killed/inactivated polio vaccine is the Salk type; OPV is the live Sabin type.  Why C wrong: OPV is not a toxoid.  Why D wrong: OPV is a live attenuated vaccine, not a subunit vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The dose and route of OPV given for prevention of poliomyelitis is:","options":["0.5 ml IM","2 drops by the oral route","0.05 ml ID","1 mg IM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OPV is given as 2 drops by the oral route for the prevention of poliomyelitis.  Why A wrong: 0.5 ml IM is the Hep-B dose.  Why C wrong: 0.05 ml ID is the BCG dose.  Why D wrong: 1 mg IM refers to Vitamin K."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"The birth dose of OPV given to a newborn is specifically known as the:","options":["Booster dose","Zero dose","Catch-up dose","First dose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The birth dose of OPV is known as the zero dose.  Why A wrong: A booster dose is given later, not at birth.  Why C wrong: A catch-up dose refers to delayed vaccination (e.g., BCG up to 1 year).  Why D wrong: The birth dose is specifically termed the zero dose, not the first dose."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Which vaccine is given FIRST at birth (NORCET-2021)?","options":["BCG","OPV","Hep-B","DPT"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The vaccine given firstly at birth is Hep-B (NORCET-2021).  Why A wrong: BCG is given at birth but the one given first is Hep-B.  Why B wrong: OPV is given at birth (zero dose) but Hep-B is given first.  Why D wrong: DPT is not a birth-dose vaccine."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the high-yield PYQ box, the FIRST intervention for a newborn after birth is:","options":["Assess the APGAR score","Suctioning or clearing the airway","Weigh the baby","Give the BCG vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first intervention of the newborn after birth is suctioning or clearing the airway.  Why A wrong: APGAR assessment follows airway management.  Why C wrong: Weighing is done later after stabilization.  Why D wrong: BCG immunization is not the first intervention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the high-yield PYQ box, the FIRST assessment of the newborn after birth is to:","options":["Observe the initiation of respiration","Check the weight","Assess the moro reflex","Record the head circumference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first assessment of the newborn after birth is to observe the initiation of respiration.  Why B wrong: Weight is checked later after stabilization.  Why C wrong: Assessing the moro reflex is not the first assessment.  Why D wrong: Head circumference is not the first newborn assessment."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"Select ALL of the vaccines given at birth (FAQs).","options":["BCG","OPV","Hep-B","Measles","DPT"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The vaccines given at birth are BCG, OPV, and Hep-B.  Why D wrong: Measles vaccine is given later in infancy, not at birth.  Why E wrong: DPT is given later (primary schedule), not at birth."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A nurse is caring for a vigorous term newborn who was born through clear amniotic fluid and is breathing well and pink. Which action is MOST appropriate regarding airway suctioning?","options":["Perform deep pharyngeal suctioning routinely","Avoid routine suctioning since the baby is vigorous and breathing on their own","Suction the trachea every 5 minutes","Apply suction at 150 mm Hg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Routine nasal, oral, and tracheal suctioning is not recommended in neonates who start breathing on their own and are vigorous (active); routine suctioning should be avoided.  Why A wrong: Deep pharyngeal suctioning can cause vagal bradycardia and should be avoided.  Why C wrong: Routine repeated tracheal suctioning is not indicated in a vigorous newborn.  Why D wrong: 150 mm Hg is at the adult upper limit and is inappropriate for a newborn (60-100 mm Hg range)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A preterm neonate weighing 900 gm requires Vitamin K prophylaxis. Which order is correct?","options":["Vitamin K 1 mg IM","Vitamin K 0.5 mg IM","Vitamin K 2 mg orally","Withhold Vitamin K because the baby is preterm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A neonate less than 1 kg (1000 gm) should receive 0.5 mg Vitamin K through the IM route; a 900 gm baby qualifies.  Why A wrong: 1 mg IM is the dose for neonates at/above 1 kg (including most preterms >1 kg).  Why C wrong: 2 mg oral is the alternative when parents refuse IM in a formula-fed baby without haemorrhage risk, not the routine order for a <1 kg preterm.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K should be given to all neonates, including preterm, to prevent haemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"A newborn born through meconium-stained amniotic fluid is limp and not breathing after drying. What is the appropriate airway action before positive pressure ventilation?","options":["No suctioning; proceed directly to feeding","Suction the mouth and nose before initiating positive pressure ventilation","Give chest compressions first","Administer OPV immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In neonates born through meconium-stained amniotic fluid who do not start breathing on their own, suctioning of the mouth and nose should be done before initiating positive pressure ventilation.  Why A wrong: A non-breathing meconium-stained baby needs suctioning, not feeding.  Why C wrong: Airway clearance/PPV precedes compressions in this context.  Why D wrong: Immunization is not an airway emergency intervention."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"According to the observational care column of routine newborn care, which of the following is a listed observational care activity?","options":["Provide warmth and observe breathing and temperature","Weigh the newborn immediately","Administer Vitamin K","Apply the identity band"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Observational care includes providing warmth, observing breathing and temperature, watching for complications (refer if so), and initiating breastfeeding.  Why B wrong: Weighing is a routine care step, not listed under observational care, and is done after stabilization.  Why C wrong: Administering Vitamin K is a routine care step (Step-07), not an observational-care item.  Why D wrong: Applying the identity band is a routine care step (Step-08)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Definition & scope of pediatrics, factors affecting child health…","question":"During newborn assessment, \"acrocyanosis\" specifically refers to a bluish discolouration of the:","options":["Tongue, lips, and trunk","Hands and feet","Entire body","Face and neck"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acrocyanosis is the bluish colour of only the hands and feet, which may be present after birth and usually does not indicate a lack of oxygen (contrast with central cyanosis of the trunk/lips/tongue).  Why A wrong: Blue tongue/lips/trunk is central cyanosis, indicating hypoxia.  Why C wrong: Whole-body cyanosis is not the definition of acrocyanosis.  Why D wrong: Acrocyanosis involves the extremities, not the face/neck."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The \"5 C's\" that should be followed while cutting the umbilical cord include clean hand, clean blade, clean cloths, clean surface, and which fifth item traditionally used in villages for clamping the cord?","options":["Clean cotton","Clean thread","Clean water","Clean gloves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The 5th \"C\" listed is clean thread, used in villages for clamping (tying) of the umbilical cord.  Why A wrong: Clean cotton is not one of the listed 5 C's.  Why C wrong: Clean water is not among the 5 C's for cord cutting.  Why D wrong: Clean gloves are not listed as the 5th C in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the source, how much blood does the umbilical cord contain?","options":["40–60 ml","60–80 ml","80–100 ml","100–120 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the umbilical cord contains 80–100 ml blood.  Why A wrong: 40–60 ml understates the stated volume.  Why B wrong: 60–80 ml is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 100–120 ml exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which agent is recommended for care of the umbilical cord stump after cutting?","options":["Normal saline (NS)","Povidone-iodine","70% alcohol","Sterile water"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Care of the umbilical cord stump after cutting is done with 70% alcohol because of its volatile nature.  Why A wrong: NS should not be used because of its moist nature (keeps stump wet).  Why B wrong: Povidone-iodine is not the agent named in the source.  Why D wrong: Sterile water is moist and not recommended for stump care here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Why does the source state that normal saline should NOT be used for umbilical cord stump care?","options":["Because it is expensive","Because of its moist nature","Because it causes chemical burns","Because it is not sterile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NS should not be used due to its moist nature; the stump must be kept dry.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: NS does not cause chemical burns and this is not stated.  Why D wrong: NS can be sterile; sterility is not the stated concern."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"After cutting the umbilical cord, the site should be kept dry and assessed regularly for which finding?","options":["Any drainage","Skin colour change","Pulsation","Temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the site should be kept dry and assessed regularly for any drainage.  Why B wrong: Skin colour change is not the assessment focus stated.  Why C wrong: Pulsation assessment is not mentioned for the cut stump.  Why D wrong: Temperature of the stump is not the specified assessment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which of the following are shown as cord-cutting equipment in the source figure?","options":["Umbilical scissor, Kocher forceps, cord clamp","Artery forceps, sponge holder, needle holder","Episiotomy scissor, Allis forceps, cord clamp","Umbilical scissor, Babcock forceps, hemostat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure \"Cord Cutting Equipments\" shows umbilical scissor, Kocher forceps, and cord clamp.  Why B wrong: These instruments are not the ones labelled in the figure.  Why C wrong: Episiotomy scissor and Allis forceps are not shown.  Why D wrong: Babcock forceps and hemostat are not the labelled instruments."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During cord cutting, at what distance from the abdomen of the newborn is the FIRST umbilical cord clamp applied?","options":["2 cm","3.5 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first umbilical cord clamp is applied at 2 cm from the abdomen of the newborn.  Why B wrong: 3.5 cm is the length remaining after cutting, not the first clamp position.  Why C wrong: 5 cm is the position of the second clamp.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is the length left in pre-term/high-risk babies for catheter insertion."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During cord cutting, the second umbilical clamp is applied at what distance from the abdomen of the newborn?","options":["2 cm","3.5 cm","5 cm","7 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The second umbilical clamp is applied at 5 cm from the abdomen, and the cord is cut in between the two clamps.  Why A wrong: 2 cm is the first clamp position.  Why B wrong: 3.5 cm is the remaining stump length after cutting.  Why D wrong: 7 cm is not a stated measurement."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"After cutting the umbilical cord in a normal newborn, what length of cord should remain?","options":["2 cm","3.5 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After cutting, the umbilical cord length should remain 3.5 cm.  Why A wrong: 2 cm is the first clamp distance, not the remaining stump.  Why C wrong: 5 cm is the second clamp distance.  Why D wrong: 10 cm is reserved for pre-term/high-risk babies for catheter insertion."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In pre-term and high-risk newborns, at least what cord length should be left to allow umbilical cord catheter insertion?","options":["3.5 cm","5 cm","10 cm","15 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In pre-term and other high-risk newborns, at least 10 cm of umbilical cord should be left for umbilical cord catheter insertion.  Why A wrong: 3.5 cm applies to normal newborns.  Why B wrong: 5 cm is the second clamp distance, not the retained pre-term length.  Why D wrong: 15 cm exceeds the stated minimum of 10 cm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which of the following is correct regarding care of the umbilical cord stump per the source?","options":["Bind the stump firmly with a bandage","Apply antiseptic powder daily","Do not apply any substance and leave the stump uncovered","Cover the stump with an occlusive dressing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs not to apply any substance to the stump and not to bind or bandage it—leave the stump uncovered.  Why A wrong: Binding/bandaging the stump is specifically advised against.  Why B wrong: No substance (including powder) should be applied.  Why D wrong: The stump should be left uncovered, not covered with an occlusive dressing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the note in the source, applying traditional remedies to the umbilical cord may cause which complication?","options":["Neonatal jaundice","Infections and neonatal tetanus","Umbilical hernia","Omphalocele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states applying traditional remedies to the cord may cause infections and neonatal tetanus.  Why A wrong: Jaundice is not the stated risk of traditional cord remedies.  Why C wrong: Umbilical hernia is not attributed to traditional remedies here.  Why D wrong: Omphalocele is a congenital defect, not caused by cord remedies."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The APGAR score is a physiological assessment scale developed by whom, and in which year?","options":["Dr. Jeanne Ballard, 1979","Dr. Virginia Apgar, 1952","Dr. Virginia Apgar, 1962","Dr. Jeanne Ballard, 1952"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The APGAR score was developed by Dr. Virginia Apgar in 1952.  Why A wrong: Dr. Jeanne Ballard (1979) developed the Ballard scale, not APGAR.  Why C wrong: The stated year is 1952, not 1962.  Why D wrong: Wrong person and wrong scale."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR score, what does the letter \"A\" for \"Appearance\" assess?","options":["Heart rate","Skin colour","Reflex response","Muscle response"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A = Appearance = Skin colour.  Why A wrong: Heart rate is assessed by \"P\" (Pulse).  Why C wrong: Reflex response is assessed by \"G\" (Grimace).  Why D wrong: Muscle response is assessed by \"A\" (Activity), the other \"A\"."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR mnemonic, \"P\" for \"Pulse\" corresponds to which parameter?","options":["Reflex response","Respiratory efforts","Heart rate","Muscle response"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: P = Pulse = Heart rate.  Why A wrong: Reflex response is \"G\" (Grimace).  Why B wrong: Respiratory efforts is \"R\" (Respiratory).  Why D wrong: Muscle response is \"A\" (Activity)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR score, \"G\" for \"Grimace\" assesses which of the following?","options":["Reflex response","Skin colour","Heart rate","Respiratory efforts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: G = Grimace = Reflex response.  Why B wrong: Skin colour is \"A\" (Appearance).  Why C wrong: Heart rate is \"P\" (Pulse).  Why D wrong: Respiratory efforts is \"R\" (Respiratory)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR score, the second \"A\" (Activity) evaluates which response?","options":["Skin colour","Muscle response","Reflex response","Respiratory effort"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A = Activity = Muscle response.  Why A wrong: Skin colour is \"Appearance\", the first \"A\".  Why C wrong: Reflex response is \"Grimace\".  Why D wrong: Respiratory effort is \"R\" (Respiratory)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR score, \"R\" for \"Respiratory\" evaluates which parameter?","options":["Reflex response","Heart rate","Respiratory efforts","Skin colour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: R = Respiratory = Respiratory efforts.  Why A wrong: Reflex response is \"Grimace\".  Why B wrong: Heart rate is \"Pulse\".  Why D wrong: Skin colour is \"Appearance\"."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"At what times following birth should the APGAR score routinely be evaluated?","options":["At 1 minute and 5 minutes of age","At 5 minutes and 10 minutes of age","At birth and at 30 minutes","At 2 minutes and 10 minutes of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The APGAR score should be evaluated at 1 min and 5 min of age following birth.  Why B wrong: The standard evaluation times are 1 and 5 minutes, not 5 and 10.  Why C wrong: It is not routinely scored \"at birth\" and 30 minutes.  Why D wrong: 2 and 10 minutes are not the stated routine times."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"If the APGAR score remains significantly depressed at 5 minutes, after what additional interval should it be re-evaluated?","options":["After 2 minutes","After 10 minutes","After 15 minutes","After 20 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If the score remains significantly depressed at 5 minutes, it should be evaluated again after 15 minutes.  Why A wrong: 2 minutes is not the stated re-evaluation interval.  Why B wrong: 10 minutes is not what the source states.  Why D wrong: 20 minutes is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"While taking the APGAR score, which parameter is assessed on FIRST priority, and why?","options":["Respiratory rate, because it is easiest to observe","Heart rate, because respiration was already assessed","Colour, because it is most visible","Muscle tone, because it needs no equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heart rate is assessed on first priority instead of respiratory rate because respiration was already assessed.  Why A wrong: Respiratory rate is not the first priority per the source.  Why C wrong: Colour is not stated as the first-priority assessment.  Why D wrong: Muscle tone is not prioritized first."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(AIIMS, CHO-2022) What are the maximum and minimum possible APGAR scores?","options":["Maximum 12, minimum 2","Maximum 10, minimum 0","Maximum 8, minimum 0","Maximum 10, minimum 2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maximum possible APGAR score is 10 whereas minimum possible score is 0 (AIIMS, CHO-2022).  Why A wrong: Maximum is 10, not 12; minimum is 0, not 2.  Why C wrong: Maximum is 10, not 8.  Why D wrong: Minimum is 0, not 2."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In APGAR scoring of breathing effort, what respiratory score is given if the infant is NOT breathing?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the infant is not breathing, the respiratory score is 0.  Why B wrong: A score of 1 is for slow, irregular, weak or gasping respirations.  Why C wrong: A score of 2 is for vigorous/lusty crying.  Why D wrong: The maximum for any single parameter is 2."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What APGAR respiratory (breathing effort) score is assigned when respirations are slow and irregular, weak or gasping?","options":["0","1","2","Not scored"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Slow and irregular, weak or gasping respirations score 1.  Why A wrong: 0 is for no breathing at all.  Why C wrong: 2 is for vigorous/lusty crying.  Why D wrong: Respiratory effort is always scored."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An infant who is crying vigorously or lustily is assigned what APGAR respiratory score?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If the infant is crying vigorously or lusty, the respiratory score is 2.  Why A wrong: 0 indicates no breathing.  Why B wrong: 1 indicates weak/gasping respirations.  Why D wrong: 4 is not a valid single-parameter score (max 2)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In APGAR scoring, the heart rate is best evaluated using which method, and it is described as the most critical part for determining what?","options":["Palpation of the radial pulse; need for feeding","Stethoscope (apical pulse); need for resuscitation","Pulse oximeter; need for oxygen","ECG; need for warming"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Heart rate is evaluated with a stethoscope (apical pulse) and is the most critical part of the score in determining the need for resuscitation.  Why A wrong: Apical (stethoscope) assessment is specified, not radial palpation; and it relates to resuscitation, not feeding.  Why C wrong: A pulse oximeter is used later for SpO2, not to grade the APGAR heart rate.  Why D wrong: ECG is not the method described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In APGAR heart-rate scoring, what score is given if there is NO heartbeat?","options":["0","1","2","Absent from scoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If there is no heartbeat, the heart rate score is 0.  Why B wrong: 1 is for a heart rate less than 100 beats per minute.  Why C wrong: 2 is for a heart rate more than 100 beats per minute.  Why D wrong: Heart rate is always scored (0, 1, or 2)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What APGAR heart-rate score is given when the heart rate is LESS than 100 beats per minute?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the heart rate is less than 100 beats per minute, the heart rate score is 1.  Why A wrong: 0 is for no heartbeat.  Why C wrong: 2 is for heart rate more than 100 bpm.  Why D wrong: Maximum single-parameter score is 2."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What APGAR heart-rate score is given when the heart rate is MORE than 100 beats per minute?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: If the heart rate is more than 100 beats per minute, the heart rate score is 2.  Why A wrong: 0 is for no heartbeat.  Why B wrong: 1 is for heart rate less than 100 bpm.  Why D wrong: 4 is not valid (max 2 per parameter)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In APGAR muscle tone scoring, an infant that is loose and floppy without activity receives what score?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the muscle tone is loose and floppy without activity, the score for muscle tone is 0.  Why B wrong: 1 is for some tone and flexion.  Why C wrong: 2 is for active motion with flexed tone resisting extension.  Why D wrong: 3 is not a valid muscle-tone score."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What APGAR muscle-tone score is given if the infant demonstrates some tone and flexion?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the infant demonstrates some tone and flexion, the muscle tone score is 1.  Why A wrong: 0 is loose/floppy.  Why C wrong: 2 is active motion resisting extension.  Why D wrong: 4 is invalid (max 2)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An infant in active motion with a flexed muscle tone that resists extension receives what APGAR muscle-tone score?","options":["0","1","2","Not scored"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Active motion with flexed muscle tone that resists extension scores 2.  Why A wrong: 0 is loose/floppy without activity.  Why B wrong: 1 is some tone and flexion.  Why D wrong: Muscle tone is always scored."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In grimace/reflex-irritability scoring, what score is given if there is NO response to stimulation?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If there is no response to stimulation, the reflex irritability response score is 0.  Why B wrong: 1 is for grimacing in response.  Why C wrong: 2 is for cry/cough/sneeze on stimulation.  Why D wrong: Max single-parameter score is 2."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What reflex-irritability (grimace) score is given if the infant only grimaces in response to stimulation?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grimacing in response to stimulation scores 1 (only facial expression).  Why A wrong: 0 is no response.  Why C wrong: 2 is cry/cough/sneeze.  Why D wrong: 4 is invalid."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"If the infant cries, coughs, or sneezes on stimulation, the reflex-irritability (grimace) score is:","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cry, cough, or sneeze on stimulation scores 2.  Why A wrong: 0 is no response.  Why B wrong: 1 is only grimacing.  Why D wrong: 3 is not valid."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Why do most normal infants score only 1 for the colour (Appearance) component of APGAR?","options":["Because central cyanosis is common","Because peripheral cyanosis (acrocyanosis) is common among normal infants","Because pallor is normal at birth","Because jaundice is common at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most infants score 1 for colour because peripheral cyanosis is common among normal infants (and colour can be misleading in non-white infants).  Why A wrong: Central cyanosis is abnormal, not the reason for a normal score of 1.  Why C wrong: Pallor scores 0, not 1.  Why D wrong: Jaundice is not the reason cited."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In APGAR colour scoring, an infant who is pale or blue receives what score?","options":["0","1","2","Not scored"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the infant is pale or blue, the score for colour is 0.  Why B wrong: 1 is for a pink body with blue extremities.  Why C wrong: 2 is for an entirely pink infant.  Why D wrong: Colour is always scored."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An infant whose body is pink but whose extremities are blue (acrocyanosis) receives what APGAR colour score?","options":["0","1","2","3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pink body with blue extremities (acrocyanosis) scores 1.  Why A wrong: 0 is pale or blue (whole body).  Why C wrong: 2 is entirely pink.  Why D wrong: 3 is not valid."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An infant who is entirely pink receives what APGAR colour score?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An entirely pink infant scores 2 for colour.  Why A wrong: 0 is pale/blue.  Why B wrong: 1 is pink body with blue extremities.  Why D wrong: 4 is invalid (max 2)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR table, the \"Activity (Muscle Tone)\" score of 1 point corresponds to which finding?","options":["Absent","Arms and legs flexed","Active movements","Flaccid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The APGAR table lists Activity = 1 point as \"Arms and legs flexed.\"  Why A wrong: \"Absent\" is the 0-point description.  Why C wrong: \"Active movements\" is the 2-point description.  Why D wrong: \"Flaccid\" is the 0-point term for Grimace, not Activity's 1-point value."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the APGAR table, the \"Pulse (Heart Rate)\" values for 1 point and 2 points are respectively:","options":["Absent and <100 bpm","<100 bpm and >100 bpm",">100 bpm and <100 bpm","<60 bpm and >60 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists Pulse: Absent (0), <100 pbm (1), >100 pbm (2).  Why A wrong: \"Absent\" is the 0-point value, not the 1-point value.  Why C wrong: The values are reversed here.  Why D wrong: The cut-off is 100 bpm, not 60."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the APGAR result-interpretation table, a score of 8 to 10 indicates which severity level?","options":["Severe distress","Moderate distress","Mild distress","No distress"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 8 to 10 = No distress.  Why A wrong: Severe distress is 0 to 2.  Why B wrong: Moderate distress is 3 to 4.  Why C wrong: Mild distress is 5 to 7."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An APGAR score of 5 to 7 is interpreted as which category?","options":["No distress","Mild distress","Moderate distress","Severe distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 5 to 7 = Mild distress.  Why A wrong: No distress is 8 to 10.  Why C wrong: Moderate distress is 3 to 4.  Why D wrong: Severe distress is 0 to 2."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An APGAR score of 3 to 4 corresponds to which severity?","options":["No distress","Mild distress","Moderate distress","Severe distress"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 3 to 4 = Moderate distress.  Why A wrong: 8 to 10 is No distress.  Why B wrong: 5 to 7 is Mild distress.  Why D wrong: 0 to 2 is Severe distress."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An APGAR score of 0 to 2 indicates which severity level?","options":["No distress","Mild distress","Moderate distress","Severe distress"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A score of 0 to 2 = Severe distress.  Why A wrong: 8 to 10 = No distress.  Why B wrong: 5 to 7 = Mild distress.  Why C wrong: 3 to 4 = Moderate distress."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"For a newborn with mild distress (APGAR 5 to 7), 100% oxygen is given by bag and mask at what flow rate and pressure?","options":["3 L/min at 20–30 cm H2O","5 L/min at 30–40 cm H2O","8 L/min at 40–50 cm H2O","10 L/min at 50–60 cm H2O"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For mild distress, 100% oxygen is given at a rate of 5 L/min by bag & mask by a pressure of 30–40 cm H2O.  Why A wrong: The stated flow is 5 L/min at 30–40 cm H2O, not 3 L/min at 20–30.  Why C wrong: 8 L/min applies to moderate-distress O2 therapy (5–8 L/m), and the pressure figure is wrong.  Why D wrong: 10 L/min at 50–60 cm H2O is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In managing an APGAR score of 5 to 7 (mild distress), the pulse oximeter is attached to which extremity, and how is the head positioned?","options":["Left hand; head flexed forward","Right hand; head in midline maintained in slight extension","Either foot; head turned to the side","Left foot; head hyperextended"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states attach pulse oximeter with the right hand and put the head in midline maintained in slight extension.  Why A wrong: The oximeter goes on the right hand, and the head is slightly extended, not flexed forward.  Why C wrong: Foot placement and side-turned head are not specified.  Why D wrong: Slight extension is specified, not hyperextension, and not the left foot."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"For an APGAR score of 3 to 4 (moderate distress), if stimulation is not effective and the baby is intubated, IPPV should be started at what breath rate, with reassessment in what interval?","options":["20–30 breaths/min; reassess in 60 seconds","30–40 breaths/min; reassess in the next 15–30 seconds","40–60 breaths/min; reassess in 5 minutes","60–80 breaths/min; reassess in 2 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For moderate distress, ventilation with IPPV should be started at 30–40 breath rate and the baby reassessed in the next 15–30 seconds.  Why A wrong: The stated rate is 30–40 and reassessment is 15–30 seconds, not 60.  Why C wrong: 40–60 breaths/min and 5-minute reassessment are not stated here.  Why D wrong: 60–80 breaths/min is not the stated IPPV rate."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In managing moderate distress (APGAR 3 to 4), oxygen therapy is given at what flow rate?","options":["2–4 L/m","5–8 L/m","8–10 L/m","10–12 L/m"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For moderate distress, O2 therapy is given at 5–8 L/m.  Why A wrong: 2–4 L/m is not the stated range.  Why C wrong: 8–10 L/m exceeds the stated 5–8 L/m.  Why D wrong: 10–12 L/m is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"For severe distress (APGAR 0 to 2), the drug of choice for resuscitation and its recommended dose is:","options":["Atropine, 0.02 mg/kg","Epinephrine, 0.1–0.3 ml/kg of 1:10000, given intra-tracheal","Sodium bicarbonate, 1 mEq/kg","Dopamine, 5 mcg/kg/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For severe distress, the drug of choice is epinephrine (adrenaline). [Note: per NRP, 0.1-0.3 mL/kg of 1:10,000 (0.01-0.03 mg/kg) is the IV/IO dose and IV/IO is the PREFERRED route; the endotracheal route is a fallback needing a higher dose, 0.5-1 mL/kg of 1:10,000.]  Why A wrong: Atropine is not the resuscitation drug of choice stated.  Why C wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is not the drug named.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is not the resuscitation drug named here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In severe distress (APGAR 0 to 2), ventilation is performed with CPPV and 100% oxygen, and the heart rate is rechecked in what interval?","options":["15 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds","5 minutes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In severe distress the HR is rechecked in 30 seconds after immediate resuscitation, intubation, and ventilation with CPPV and 100% oxygen.  Why A wrong: 15 seconds is not the stated recheck interval.  Why C wrong: 60 seconds is not stated.  Why D wrong: 5 minutes is the epinephrine repeat interval, not the HR recheck."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(Bhatinda AIIMS-2019) A newborn has heart rate 110/min, respiration slow and irregular, grimacing, acrocyanosis, and full body flexed. What is the APGAR score?","options":["5","6","7","8"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: HR 110 (>100) = 2; slow/irregular respiration = 1; grimacing = 1; acrocyanosis = 1; full body flexed (active flexion) = 2; total 2+1+1+1+2 = 7.  Why A wrong: 5 undercounts the components.  Why B wrong: 6 miscounts one parameter.  Why D wrong: 8 overcounts; grimace and colour each score only 1 here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(ESIC-2019) Which statement correctly distinguishes the APGAR score from the Ballard scale?","options":["Both assess gestational age","APGAR assesses gestational age; Ballard assesses physiological status","APGAR is a quick physiological assessment of the newborn after birth; Ballard is a gestational-age/maturational assessment of the fetus","Both are physiological assessments done at 1 and 5 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states APGAR is a quick physiological assessment scale of the newborn following birth, whereas the Ballard scale is a gestational-age/maturational assessment of the fetus.  Why A wrong: Only Ballard assesses gestational age.  Why B wrong: The roles are reversed in this option.  Why D wrong: The Ballard scale is not a 1-/5-minute physiological assessment."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"As defined in the source, \"acrocyanosis\" refers to:","options":["Central cyanosis of the whole body","Extremities cyanosis (body pink, extremities blue)","Cyanosis of the lips only","Generalized pallor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acrocyanosis is defined as extremities cyanosis (body pink, extremities blue).  Why A wrong: Central/whole-body cyanosis is not acrocyanosis.  Why C wrong: Lip cyanosis alone is not the definition given.  Why D wrong: Pallor is a different finding."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the source glossary, \"grimace\" is described as:","options":["Complete absence of muscle response","Hypoactive response with only facial expression","Vigorous crying","Whole-body flexion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grimace is defined as a hypoactive response—only facial expression.  Why A wrong: Absent muscle response describes flaccid.  Why C wrong: Vigorous crying is a strong (score 2) response, not grimace.  Why D wrong: Whole-body flexion refers to muscle tone/flexion position."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The term \"flaccid\" as used in the source means:","options":["Some tone and flexion","Active resistance to extension","No response of muscles / paresis","Rigid muscle spasticity"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Flaccid is defined as no response of muscles / paresis.  Why A wrong: Some tone/flexion is a score of 1, not flaccid.  Why B wrong: Active resistance is a score of 2.  Why D wrong: Flaccid is loss of tone, not rigid spasticity."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The Ballard score is best described as:","options":["A physiological assessment scale of the newborn after birth","A gestational age assessment / neuromuscular maturational assessment tool of the fetus","A pain assessment scale for neonates","A jaundice severity scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ballard score is a gestational age assessment scale or neuromuscular maturational assessment tool of the fetus.  Why A wrong: That describes the APGAR score.  Why C wrong: It is not a pain scale.  Why D wrong: It does not assess jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Who developed the Ballard scale and in which year?","options":["Dr. Virginia Apgar, 1952","Dr. Jeanne L Ballard, 1979","Dr. Jeanne L Ballard, 1969","Dr. Virginia Apgar, 1979"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ballard scale was developed by Dr. Jeanne L Ballard in 1979.  Why A wrong: Dr. Virginia Apgar (1952) developed the APGAR score.  Why C wrong: The year is 1979, not 1969.  Why D wrong: Wrong developer for the year given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The Ballard scale assesses how many criteria, and which are they?","options":["5 criteria: Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration","2 criteria: Physical and Neuromuscular","3 criteria: Physical, Neuromuscular, and Behavioural","4 criteria: Skin, Reflexes, Tone, Colour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ballard scale assesses 2 criteria—Physical & Neuromuscular.  Why A wrong: The 5 A-P-G-A-R criteria belong to the APGAR score.  Why C wrong: A behavioural criterion is not listed.  Why D wrong: Only two overarching criteria (physical/neuromuscular) are named."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Over what gestational-age range is the (original) Ballard scale assessed?","options":["20 to 40 weeks","26 to 44 weeks","28 to 42 weeks","24 to 40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ballard scale is assessed from 26 weeks to 44 weeks of gestational age.  Why A wrong: 20 weeks is the lower limit for the New Ballard score, not the original.  Why C wrong: 28–42 weeks is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 24–40 weeks is not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The New Ballard score can be used from as early as how many weeks, including extremely pre-term babies?","options":["24 weeks","22 weeks","20 weeks","26 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The New Ballard score is used up to (as early as) 20 weeks, which includes extremely pre-term babies.  Why A wrong: 24 weeks is not the stated lower limit for the New Ballard score.  Why B wrong: 22 weeks is not stated.  Why D wrong: 26 weeks is the lower limit of the original Ballard scale."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Up to how many days after birth can the Ballard score be used (though in practice usually within the first 24 hours)?","options":["2 days","4 days","7 days","10 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ballard score can be used up to 4 days after birth (in practice usually in the first 24 hours).  Why A wrong: The stated limit is 4 days, not 2.  Why C wrong: 7 days is not stated.  Why D wrong: 10 days is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which of the following are the NEUROMUSCULAR parameters of the Ballard score? (Select all that apply)","options":["Posture","Square window (wrist flexibility)","Lanugo hair","Popliteal angle","Scarf sign"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Neuromuscular parameters are posture, square window (wrist flexibility), arm recoil, popliteal angle, scarf sign, and heel to ear—so A, B, D, and E are neuromuscular.  Why C wrong: Lanugo hair is a physical parameter, not neuromuscular.  Why A/B/D/E correct: Each is explicitly listed as a neuromuscular parameter."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which of the following are PHYSICAL parameters of the Ballard score? (Select all that apply)","options":["Skin","Lanugo hair","Scarf sign","Breast bud","Genitals"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Physical parameters are skin, lanugo hair, eye/ear, plantar surface, breast bud, and genitals—so A, B, D, and E are physical.  Why C wrong: Scarf sign is a neuromuscular parameter, not physical.  Why A/B/D/E correct: Each is explicitly listed as a physical parameter."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What are the minimum and maximum possible scores of the Ballard scale?","options":["Minimum 0, maximum 50","Minimum -10, maximum 50","Minimum -5, maximum 45","Minimum -10, maximum 60"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The minimum possible Ballard score is -10 and the maximum is 50.  Why A wrong: The minimum is -10, not 0.  Why C wrong: Neither the min nor max matches the source.  Why D wrong: The maximum is 50, not 60."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The \"warm chain\" is best defined as:","options":["A set of interlinked procedures at birth and over the next few hours/days to minimize heat loss in all newborns","A method of active rewarming using warm IV fluids","A chain of incubators used in NICU","A schedule for feeding warm milk to neonates"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The warm chain is a set of interlinked procedures to be taken at birth and during the next few hours and days to minimize heat loss in all newborns.  Why B wrong: It is not specifically about warm IV fluids.  Why C wrong: It is not a chain of incubators.  Why D wrong: It is not a milk-feeding schedule."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Heat loss in the newborn occurs through which four mechanisms?","options":["Radiation, convection, conduction, evaporation","Radiation, diffusion, osmosis, evaporation","Convection, conduction, filtration, radiation","Evaporation, sublimation, radiation, conduction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Heat loss in the newborn occurs through four ways—radiation, convection, conduction and evaporation.  Why B wrong: Diffusion and osmosis are not heat-loss mechanisms listed.  Why C wrong: Filtration is not a heat-loss mechanism.  Why D wrong: Sublimation is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The warm chain is maintained mainly to prevent neonatal hypothermia, which is described as a major risk factor for what?","options":["Neonatal jaundice","Neonatal mortality","Neonatal seizures","Neonatal sepsis only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Warm chain prevents neonatal hypothermia, which is a major risk factor for neonatal mortality.  Why A wrong: Jaundice is not the stated outcome.  Why C wrong: Seizures are not the stated risk here.  Why D wrong: The text links hypothermia to mortality, not sepsis alone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which of the following are listed benefits of early and prolonged skin-to-skin contact for newborns? (Select all that apply)","options":["Promotes bonding","Maintains warmth","Lowers infection and reduces neonatal mortality (NMR)","Enables establishment of breastfeeding","Prevents physiological jaundice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed benefits are bonding, warmth, lowering infection/NMR, and enabling breastfeeding—A, B, C, and D.  Why E wrong: Prevention of physiological jaundice is not listed as a benefit.  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly enumerated as a benefit of skin-to-skin contact."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"To keep the newborn warm in the delivery room, the source recommends forming groups of 4 consisting of:","options":["Mother, doctor, nurse, and pediatrician","Mother, midwife, companion, and observer/helper","Mother, father, midwife, and doctor","Mother, midwife, doctor, and grandmother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The group of 4 is mother, midwife, companion, and observer/helper.  Why A wrong: This grouping is not stated.  Why C wrong: Father and doctor are not the listed members.  Why D wrong: Grandmother is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to WHO, how many components make up the warm chain?","options":["6","8","10","12"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: There are 10 components of the warm chain described by WHO.  Why A wrong: 6 is the number of WHO \"six cleans\", not warm chain components.  Why B wrong: 8 is not the stated number.  Why D wrong: 12 is not the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the warm chain, the delivery room temperature should be maintained at least at what value?","options":["20°C (20–23°C)","25°C (25–28°C)","30°C (30–33°C)","22°C (22–26°C)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Warm delivery room temperature should be maintained at least 25°C (25°C–28°C).  Why A wrong: 20°C is below the stated minimum.  Why C wrong: 30°C exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 22–26°C is the environmental temperature figure, not the warm delivery-room minimum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the warm-chain steps, immediate thorough drying of the newborn should be done within what time of birth?","options":["Within 5 seconds","Within 30 seconds","Within 1 minute","Within 5 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate thorough drying should be done within 5 seconds of birth.  Why B wrong: 30 seconds is not the stated time.  Why C wrong: 1 minute is not stated.  Why D wrong: 5 minutes is far beyond the stated time."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the warm chain, prolonged skin-to-skin contact should last how long?","options":["30–45 minutes","45–60 minutes","60–90 minutes","90–120 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prolonged skin-to-skin contact should be 60–90 minutes.  Why A wrong: 30–45 minutes is below the stated duration.  Why B wrong: 45–60 minutes is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 90–120 minutes exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the warm-chain protocol, bathing and weighing of the newborn should be delayed for how long?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The warm chain calls for delayed bathing (24h) and weighing.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is not the stated delay.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not stated.  Why D wrong: 48 hours is longer than the stated 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which warm-chain component emphasizes keeping the mother and baby together at all times?","options":["Warm transportation","Zero separation","Warm resuscitation","Appropriate clothing and bedding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Zero separation: keep the mother and baby together\" is the relevant warm-chain component.  Why A wrong: Warm transportation refers to skin-to-skin during transport.  Why C wrong: Warm resuscitation is a separate component (if required).  Why D wrong: Appropriate clothing/bedding is a distinct component."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which warm-chain components involve maintaining warmth during movement and during resuscitation, respectively?","options":["Warm transportation (skin-to-skin); Warm resuscitation (if required)","Delayed bathing; Zero separation","Warm delivery room; Immediate drying","Appropriate clothing; Raise awareness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two components are \"Warm transportation (skin-to-skin)\" and \"Warm resuscitation (if required)\".  Why B wrong: These describe bathing and separation, not transport/resuscitation.  Why C wrong: These are the room and drying steps.  Why D wrong: These are the clothing and awareness/training steps."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the WHO classification, mild hypothermia in the newborn corresponds to which temperature range?","options":["36.0°C to 36.4°C (96.8°F to 97.5°F)","32.0°C to 35.9°C (89.6°F to 96.6°F)","<32.0°C (<89.6°F)","36.5°C to 37.4°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild hypothermia is 36.0°C to 36.4°C (96.8°F to 97.5°F), signifying cold stress/cause for concern.  Why B wrong: 32.0–35.9°C is moderate hypothermia.  Why C wrong: <32.0°C is severe hypothermia.  Why D wrong: 36.5–37.4°C is the normal newborn temperature, not hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the WHO classification, moderate hypothermia is defined as which temperature range, requiring what action?","options":["36.0–36.4°C; routine care","32.0°C to 35.9°C (89.6°F to 96.6°F); dangerous, requiring immediate warming of the newborn","<32.0°C; grave outlook","35.0–36.0°C; no action needed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate hypothermia is 32.0°C to 35.9°C (89.6°F to 96.6°F) and is dangerous, requiring immediate warming of the newborn.  Why A wrong: 36.0–36.4°C is mild hypothermia.  Why C wrong: <32.0°C is severe hypothermia.  Why D wrong: This range/action pair is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(NORCET 2021 context) Severe hypothermia in the newborn is classified as a temperature below what value, with what outlook?","options":["<35°C; mild concern","<34°C; dangerous","<32.0°C (<89.6°F); grave outlook, urgently needing intensive and skilled care","<30°C; routine warming only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe hypothermia is <32.0°C (<89.6°F); outlook is grave and intensive, skilled care is urgently needed.  Why A wrong: <35°C is within the moderate range, not severe.  Why B wrong: <34°C is not the severe cut-off.  Why D wrong: <30°C with routine warming misstates both the value and the action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which of the following are among the WHO \"six cleans\" for clean delivery? (Select all that apply)","options":["Clean attendant's hands (washed with soap)","Clean delivery surface","Clean cord-cutting instrument (razor/blade)","Clean string to tie the cord","Clean drinking water for the mother"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The WHO six cleans are clean hands, clean delivery surface, clean cord-cutting instrument, clean string to tie cord, clean cloth to wrap the baby, and clean cloth to wrap the mother—so A, B, C, and D are included.  Why E wrong: Clean drinking water is not one of the six cleans.  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly listed among the six cleans."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Which two items complete the WHO \"six cleans\" alongside clean hands, clean surface, clean cord-cutting instrument, and clean string?","options":["Clean cloth to wrap the baby and clean cloth to wrap the mother","Clean gloves and clean apron","Clean water and clean towel","Clean bed and clean floor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The remaining two of the six cleans are clean cloth to wrap the baby and clean cloth to wrap the mother.  Why B wrong: Gloves and apron are not among the listed six cleans.  Why C wrong: Water and towel are not the two items.  Why D wrong: Bed and floor are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"After delivery, the source's clean-chain steps advise all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["All caregivers should wash hands before handling the baby","Feed only breast milk","Apply antiseptic to the cord daily","Keep the cord clean and dry; do not apply anything"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The after-delivery steps say keep the cord clean and dry and do NOT apply anything—so applying antiseptic daily is the exception (incorrect action).  Why A wrong: Hand washing before handling the baby is a listed step.  Why B wrong: Feeding only breast milk is a listed step.  Why D wrong: Keeping the cord clean/dry without applying anything is a listed step."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the note, the most commonly recommended first-line treatment for both early and late onset neonatal sepsis is:","options":["A macrolide alone (azithromycin)","A beta-lactam antibiotic (e.g., ampicillin) combined with an aminoglycoside (e.g., gentamicin)","A fluoroquinolone (ciprofloxacin) alone","A cephalosporin combined with metronidazole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The first-line treatment is a beta-lactam antibiotic (commonly ampicillin, flucloxacillin, penicillin) combined with an aminoglycoside (commonly gentamicin).  Why A wrong: A macrolide alone is not the stated regimen.  Why C wrong: A fluoroquinolone alone is not recommended here.  Why D wrong: Cephalosporin plus metronidazole is not the stated first-line therapy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The \"transition period\" of the newborn, during which the baby stabilizes between intrauterine and extrauterine life, lasts approximately how long following birth?","options":["First 2–4 hours","First 6–8 hours","First 12 hours","First 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A newborn stabilizes in the first 6–8 hours following birth—the transition period.  Why A wrong: 2–4 hours understates the period.  Why C wrong: 12 hours is longer than stated.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"At birth, newborns make three major physiological adaptations necessary for survival. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","options":["Emptying fluids from their lungs and beginning ventilation","Changing their circulatory pattern","Maintaining body temperature","Beginning renal filtration for the first time"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The three adaptations are emptying lung fluids/ventilation, changing circulatory pattern, and maintaining body temperature; beginning renal filtration is NOT listed.  Why A wrong: Emptying lung fluid/ventilation is a listed adaptation.  Why B wrong: Changing circulatory pattern is listed.  Why C wrong: Maintaining body temperature is listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(AIIMS, ESIC, DSSSB, PGI, RPMER) The transition period of the newborn is divided into how many phases?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transition period is divided into three phases.  Why A wrong: It is not two phases.  Why C wrong: It is not four.  Why D wrong: It is not five."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The first phase of the transition period is also known as the \"first period of reactivity\" and lasts for what duration?","options":["0 to 30 minutes","30 minutes to 2 hours","2 to 8 hours","6 to 8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first phase (first period of reactivity / active and alert stage) lasts 0 to 30 minutes.  Why B wrong: 30 minutes to 2 hours is the second phase.  Why C wrong: 2 to 8 hours is the third phase.  Why D wrong: 6 to 8 hours is the total transition period."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During the first period of reactivity, what is the newborn's heart rate for the initial 15 minutes before it decreases to normal?","options":["100–120 b/m","120–140 b/m","160–180 b/m","180–200 b/m"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Heart rate is 160–180 b/m for the initial 15 minutes, then decreases to normal 100–120 b/m.  Why A wrong: 100–120 b/m is the \"normal\" value it decreases to, not the initial rate.  Why B wrong: 120–140 b/m is not the stated initial rate.  Why D wrong: 180–200 b/m exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In the first period of reactivity, the newborn's respiration is characterized as:","options":["20–30 b/m, regular and thoracic","40–60 b/m, abdominal and irregular, with flaring of nostrils","30–40 b/m, deep and slow","60–80 b/m, synchronous and shallow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Respiration is 40–60 b/m, abdominal and irregular, with flaring of nostrils and possible grunting/intermittent chest retractions.  Why A wrong: The rate is 40–60 b/m, not 20–30, and it is abdominal/irregular.  Why C wrong: 30–40 b/m deep/slow is not the first-phase description.  Why D wrong: 60–80 b/m synchronous/shallow describes neither this phase accurately."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The second phase of the transition period is also known as the \"period of decreased responsiveness\" and lasts for what interval?","options":["0 to 30 minutes","Next 30 minutes to 2 hours","2 to 8 hours","8 to 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The second phase (period of decreased responsiveness / relaxation and rest stage) lasts the next 30 minutes to 2 hours.  Why A wrong: 0 to 30 minutes is the first phase.  Why C wrong: 2 to 8 hours is the third phase.  Why D wrong: 8 to 12 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During the second phase of transition, the newborn's heart rate is between which values?","options":["100–140 b/m","120–160 b/m","160–180 b/m","80–100 b/m"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the second phase, heart rate is between 100–140 b/m.  Why B wrong: 120–160 b/m is the third-phase HR.  Why C wrong: 160–180 b/m is the first-phase initial HR.  Why D wrong: 80–100 b/m is not stated for this phase."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The third phase of the transition period is also known as the \"second period of reactivity\" and lasts for what duration?","options":["0 to 30 minutes","30 minutes to 2 hours","2 to 8 hours","8 to 24 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The third phase (second period of reactivity) lasts 2 to 8 hours.  Why A wrong: 0 to 30 minutes is the first phase.  Why B wrong: 30 minutes to 2 hours is the second phase.  Why D wrong: 8 to 24 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During the third phase of transition (second period of reactivity), which findings are expected? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased responsiveness to stimuli","Heart rate near normal (120–160 b/m)","Respiratory rate may increase (30–40 b/m)","Bowel sounds more frequent; may pass meconium","Heart rate 160–180 b/m sustained"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The third phase shows increased responsiveness, HR near normal (120–160 b/m), RR may increase (30–40 b/m), colour/tone change, and more frequent bowel sounds with possible meconium passage.  Why E wrong: A sustained HR of 160–180 b/m describes the first-phase initial rate, not the third phase.  Why A/B/C/D correct: Each is explicitly listed for the third phase."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Based on the first period of reactivity lasting 0 to 30 minutes, breastfeeding should ideally be initiated within what time, and why?","options":["Within 2 hours, to allow maternal rest","Within 30 minutes (or ASAP), because after 30 minutes the baby enters the period of decreased responsiveness","Within 6 hours, once vital signs stabilize","After the first bath, to keep the baby clean"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because the active/alert stage lasts 0–30 minutes, breastfeeding should start within 30 minutes or ASAP, since after 30 minutes the baby enters the second stage (decreased responsiveness).  Why A wrong: 2 hours is too late per the rationale given.  Why C wrong: 6 hours misses the optimal alert window.  Why D wrong: Bathing is delayed 24 hours and is unrelated to feeding timing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"After birth, an ABG (arterial blood gas) sample may be taken from which vessel, whereas the umbilical catheter is inserted into which vessel?","options":["ABG from umbilical vein; catheter into umbilical artery","ABG from umbilical artery; catheter into umbilical vein","Both from the umbilical vein","Both from the umbilical artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After birth, an ABG sample may be taken from the umbilical artery, and the umbilical catheter should be inserted into the umbilical vein.  Why A wrong: The vessels are reversed in this option.  Why C wrong: ABG is from the artery, not the vein.  Why D wrong: The catheter goes into the vein, not the artery."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the source, the \"silent killer disease\" of the newborn is:","options":["Hypertension","Hypothermia","Glaucoma","Hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The silent killer disease of the newborn is hypothermia, so keep the baby dry and clean and use a radiant warmer if necessary.  Why A wrong: Hypertension is the silent killer of the adult.  Why C wrong: Glaucoma is the silent killer of sight.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Match the \"silent killer\" diseases as stated in the source: adult and sight, respectively.","options":["Adult—hypothermia; Sight—cataract","Adult—hypertension; Sight—glaucoma","Adult—diabetes; Sight—glaucoma","Adult—hypertension; Sight—cataract"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Silent killer of the adult is hypertension; silent killer of sight is glaucoma.  Why A wrong: Hypothermia is the newborn's silent killer, and cataract is not stated for sight.  Why C wrong: Diabetes is not stated as the adult silent killer.  Why D wrong: Cataract is not the sight silent killer named."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The source states the environmental temperature is 22° to 26°C while amniotic fluid temperature is approximately 37°C. Because of this difference, what should be done at delivery?","options":["Bathe the baby immediately in warm water","Wrap the baby in a pre-warmed towel and, if necessary, use a radiant warmer to prevent hypothermia","Place the baby in a cold cot to stabilize temperature","Expose the baby to room air for adaptation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because amniotic fluid (37°C) is warmer than the environment (22–26°C), wrap the baby in a pre-warmed towel and use a radiant warmer if needed to prevent hypothermia.  Why A wrong: Immediate bathing is contraindicated (bathing is delayed).  Why C wrong: A cold cot would worsen heat loss.  Why D wrong: Exposing to room air promotes heat loss."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the source, the best time for the first bath is generally when, and until then which type of bath is used?","options":["Within 24 hours; tub bath","Usually after the umbilical cord falls (about 5 to 7 days); sponge bath until then","Immediately after birth; shower","At 2 weeks; tub bath"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best time for bathing is usually when the umbilical cord falls off, about 5 to 7 days, and until then only a sponge bath is used.  Why A wrong: The cord typically falls at 5–7 days, not necessarily within 24 hours, and a sponge bath is used first.  Why C wrong: Immediate bathing is not recommended.  Why D wrong: 2 weeks with a tub bath is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In pre-term babies, why is the umbilical cord clamped quickly?","options":["To reduce blood loss from the mother","To prevent hypervolaemia and development of hyperbilirubinaemia","To speed up placental delivery","To prevent maternal infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In pre-term babies the cord is clamped quickly to prevent hypervolaemia and development of hyperbilirubinaemia.  Why A wrong: The concern is neonatal hypervolaemia, not maternal blood loss.  Why C wrong: Placental delivery speed is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Maternal infection prevention is not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In pre-term babies where exchange blood transfusion (EBT) may be required, the umbilical cord length should be kept at approximately:","options":["3.5 cm","5 cm","10–12 cm","15–18 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In pre-term babies, cord length should be kept long, about 10–12 cm, in case EBT is required.  Why A wrong: 3.5 cm is the normal newborn remaining length.  Why B wrong: 5 cm is the second clamp distance.  Why D wrong: 15–18 cm exceeds the stated 10–12 cm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What is the normal body temperature range of the newborn per the source?","options":["35.5°C to 36.5°C","36.5°C to 37.4°C","37.0°C to 38.0°C","36.0°C to 36.4°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal temperature of the newborn is 36.5°C to 37.4°C.  Why A wrong: 35.5–36.5°C is below normal (borderline hypothermic).  Why C wrong: 37.0–38.0°C is not the stated normal range.  Why D wrong: 36.0–36.4°C is the mild-hypothermia range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the source, the normal temperature of the labour room should be:","options":["20°C to 22°C","22°C to 26°C","25°C to 28°C","28°C to 30°C"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal temperature of the labour room is 25°C to 28°C.  Why A wrong: 20–22°C is below the stated range.  Why B wrong: 22–26°C is the environmental temperature figure, not the labour-room norm.  Why D wrong: 28–30°C exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During newborn care on the first day, the baby should be assessed at what frequency and for at least how long until stable, warm, and pink?","options":["Every 15 minutes for at least 2 hours","Every 30 minutes to 1 hour for at least 6 hours","Every 2 hours for at least 12 hours","Every 4 hours for at least 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assess the baby every 30 minutes to 1 hour for at least 6 hours or until the newborn is stable and stays warm and pink.  Why A wrong: The interval is 30 min–1 hour and duration at least 6 hours.  Why C wrong: Every 2 hours for 12 hours is not stated.  Why D wrong: Every 4 hours for 24 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During first-day newborn care, a normal baby breathes at what rate, and without which signs?","options":["20–40 times/min, without crying","30–60 times/min, without gasping, grunting, or in-drawing of the chest","40–80 times/min, without hiccups","60–80 times/min, without sneezing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal baby breathes 30–60 times a minute with no gasping, grunting, or in-drawing of the chest.  Why A wrong: 20–40 times/min understates the rate and misstates the signs.  Why C wrong: 40–80 times/min is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 60–80 times/min exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During first-day newborn care, the \"warmth\" check involves taking which temperature, which must fall in what range?","options":["Rectal temperature; 37.0–38.0°C","Axillary temperature; 36.5°C to 37.4°C","Oral temperature; 36.0–37.0°C","Tympanic temperature; 35.5–36.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use a thermometer to take an axillary temperature, which must be between 36.5°C to 37.4°C.  Why A wrong: Axillary (not rectal) is specified, and the range differs.  Why C wrong: Oral temperature is not used for newborns here.  Why D wrong: Tympanic route and range are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During first-day newborn care, the \"colour\" check verifies that which structures are pink?","options":["Palms and soles only","Tongue, lips, and mucous membranes (inside the mouth)","Fingernails only","Sclera and conjunctiva"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The colour check verifies that the tongue, lips, and mucous membranes (inside the mouth) are pink.  Why A wrong: Palms and soles are not the stated structures.  Why C wrong: Fingernails are not the stated structures.  Why D wrong: Sclera/conjunctiva are not mentioned for the colour check."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During first-day newborn care, if the cord is found to be bleeding, the nurse should:","options":["Apply pressure with gauze for 10 minutes","Add another cord clamp","Suture the cord","Apply a tourniquet above the cord"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states: check the cord for bleeding; if present, add another cord clamp.  Why A wrong: Prolonged gauze pressure is not the stated action.  Why C wrong: Suturing the cord is not indicated.  Why D wrong: A tourniquet is not the recommended action."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Why should vital signs in the neonate be assessed in the sequence \"RPT\" (respiration, pulse, temperature) rather than the traditional \"TPR\"?","options":["Because respiration is the most important sign","Because assessment should go from least intrusive to more intrusive—taking temperature first may awaken and make the baby cry, falsely raising heart and respiration rates","Because the thermometer is unreliable in neonates","Because pulse cannot be assessed while the baby is awake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Assessment should be least intrusive to more intrusive; taking temperature first may awaken and make the baby cry, falsely raising heart and respiration rates, so the sequence is RPT instead of TPR.  Why A wrong: It is about intrusiveness/order, not respiration being \"most important.\"  Why C wrong: Thermometer reliability is not the reason.  Why D wrong: Pulse can be assessed while awake; that is not the reason."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"How should the respiration rate of a neonate be measured?","options":["By auscultation for 30 seconds and doubling","By inspection/observation methods for a full 1 minute","By palpation of the chest for 15 seconds","By pulse oximeter waveform"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Respiration rate should be measured by inspection/observation methods for a full 1 minute.  Why A wrong: Doubling a 30-second count is not the stated method.  Why C wrong: Palpation for 15 seconds is not stated.  Why D wrong: Oximeter waveform is not the method described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(ESIC-2024) What is the normal respiration rate range for a newborn per the Newborn Vital Signs table?","options":["20–40 bpm","30–60 bpm","40–80 bpm","12–20 bpm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Newborn Vital Signs table lists respiration rate as 30–60 bpm (ESIC-2024).  Why A wrong: 20–40 bpm is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 40–80 bpm exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 12–20 bpm is an adult range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the Newborn Vital Signs table, the normal apical heart rate of a newborn is:","options":["60–100 bpm","100–120 bpm","110 to 160 bpm","120–180 bpm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists heart rate (apical) as 110 to 160 bpm.  Why A wrong: 60–100 bpm is an adult range.  Why B wrong: 100–120 bpm is the \"normal\" value in the first phase, not the table's stated newborn range.  Why D wrong: 120–180 bpm is not the stated table value."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Per the Newborn Vital Signs table, the normal blood pressure of a newborn is:","options":["60/40 to 80/50 mm of Hg","90/60 to 120/80 mm of Hg","40/20 to 60/40 mm of Hg","80/50 to 100/70 mm of Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists blood pressure as 60/40 to 80/50 mm of Hg.  Why B wrong: 90/60 to 120/80 is an older child/adult range.  Why C wrong: 40/20 to 60/40 is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 80/50 to 100/70 is not the stated table value."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the Newborn Vital Signs table, the normal oxygen saturation range for a newborn is:","options":["95–100%","90–95%","88–94%","85–90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book's Newborn Vital Signs table lists oxygen saturation as 88-94% (this is the source-stated value). [Clinical note: a stable term newborn after transition normally maintains SpO2 95-100%; 88-94% denotes borderline/mild hypoxaemia. In CCHD screening, <90% fails and 90-94% is borderline. Answer keyed to the source table as printed.]  Why A wrong: 95-100% is the true clinical norm after transition but is not the value printed in this source table.  Why B wrong: 90-95% is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 85-90% is below the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Per the Newborn Vital Signs table, the normal axillary temperature range of a newborn is:","options":["35.5°C to 36.5°C","36.5°C to 37.4°C","37.0°C to 38.0°C","36.0°C to 36.9°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists temperature (axillary) as 36.5°C to 37.4°C.  Why A wrong: 35.5–36.5°C is below the stated normal range.  Why C wrong: 37.0–38.0°C exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 36.0–36.9°C is not the stated table value."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What is the normal average length/height of a newborn at birth?","options":["45 cm","50 cm","55 cm","60 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal average length/height at birth is 50 cm.  Why A wrong: 45 cm is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 55 cm exceeds the stated birth length.  Why D wrong: 60 cm is not the stated birth length."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the source, the average height at 1 year and at 4 years, respectively, is:","options":["70 cm and 90 cm","75 cm and 100 cm","80 cm and 110 cm","75 cm and 110 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Height at 1 year is 75 cm and at 4 years is 100 cm.  Why A wrong: The stated values are 75 cm and 100 cm.  Why C wrong: 80 cm and 110 cm are not the stated values.  Why D wrong: The 4-year value is 100 cm, not 110 cm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During the first 6 months, a baby's height increases by approximately how much per month, and by 1 year the height has increased by what proportion of birth height?","options":["1 cm/month; 25% of birth height","1 inch/month; 50% of birth height","2 inches/month; 100% of birth height","0.5 inch/month; 30% of birth height"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Height increases by 1 inch per month in the first 6 months, and by 1 year has increased by 50% of birth height (e.g., 50 cm at birth reaches 75 cm at 1 year).  Why A wrong: The increase is 1 inch/month and 50% (not 1 cm/month, 25%).  Why C wrong: 2 inches/month and 100% are not stated.  Why D wrong: 0.5 inch/month and 30% are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"After 4 years of age, a child gains approximately how much height per year, and until what age?","options":["4 cm/year until 10 years","5 cm/year until 15 years","6 cm/year until 12 years","7 cm/year until 14 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After 4 years, a child gains approximately 6 cm height every year until 12 years of age.  Why A wrong: The gain is 6 cm/year, not 4, and until 12 years.  Why B wrong: 5 cm/year until 15 years is not stated.  Why D wrong: 7 cm/year until 14 years is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In a newborn and infant, height/length is measured using which instrument?","options":["Stadiometer","Infantometer","Measuring tape","Calipers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In newborns and infants, height is measured by an infantometer.  Why A wrong: A stadiometer is used for children >2 years in standing position.  Why C wrong: A measuring tape is not the specified instrument.  Why D wrong: Calipers are not used for length here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(NORCET-4 Prelims-2023) Up to what age is a child's height measured in the lying-down position?","options":["Up to 1 year (12 months)","Up to 2 years (24 months)","Up to 3 years (36 months)","Up to 5 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Up to 2 years or 24 months, height is measured in the lying-down position (NORCET-4 Prelims-2023).  Why A wrong: The limit is 2 years/24 months, not 1 year.  Why C wrong: 3 years is beyond the stated lying-down cut-off.  Why D wrong: 5 years is well beyond the stated limit."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(AIIMS Mangalagiri-2018) A child older than 2 years has height measured in the standing position using which device?","options":["Infantometer","Stadiometer","Length board","Height caliper"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For children >2 years, height is measured in the standing position by a stadiometer (for toddler and older child) (AIIMS Mangalagiri-2018).  Why A wrong: An infantometer is for newborns/infants in lying position.  Why C wrong: A length board is used in lying position, not standing.  Why D wrong: \"Height caliper\" is not the instrument named."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What is the normal weight range of a baby at birth per the source?","options":["2000 to 2500 gm (2.0 to 2.5 kg)","2500 to 3000 gm (2.5 to 3 kg)","3000 to 3500 gm (3 to 3.5 kg)","3500 to 4000 gm (3.5 to 4 kg)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal weight at birth is 2500 to 3000 gm (2.5 to 3 kg).  Why A wrong: 2000–2500 gm overlaps the low-birth-weight range.  Why C wrong: 3000–3500 gm is above the stated birth range.  Why D wrong: 3500–4000 gm is not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"By what age is the birth weight typically DOUBLED, and by what age is it TRIPLED?","options":["Doubled at 3 months; tripled at 9 months","Doubled at 5–6 months; tripled at 1 year (12 months)","Doubled at 4 months; tripled at 10 months","Doubled at 6 months; tripled at 2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weight is doubled at 5–6 months and tripled at 1 year (12 months).  Why A wrong: The stated ages are 5–6 months and 12 months.  Why C wrong: 4 and 10 months are not stated.  Why D wrong: Tripling occurs at 12 months, not 2 years."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"According to the source, birth weight becomes quadruple and five times at which ages, respectively?","options":["Quadruple at 2 years; five times at 3 years","Quadruple at 3 years; five times at 5 years","Quadruple at 1 year; five times at 2 years","Quadruple at 2 years; five times at 4 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight is quadruple at 2 years and five times at 3 years.  Why B wrong: These ages are not stated.  Why C wrong: At 1 year it is tripled (not quadruple).  Why D wrong: Five times occurs at 3 years, not 4."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Match the birth-weight categories with their correct weight ranges. Which pairing is correct?","options":["LBW: 1500–2500 gm","VLBW: <1000 gm","ELBW: 1000–1500 gm","Normal average weight: 2000 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low Birth Weight (LBW) baby = 1500–2500 gm.  Why B wrong: VLBW is 1000–1500 gm, not <1000 gm.  Why C wrong: ELBW is <1000 gm, not 1000–1500 gm.  Why D wrong: Normal average weight is 3000 gm (3 kg), not 2000 gm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A neonate weighs 1200 gm at birth. According to the source's weight parameters, this baby is classified as:","options":["Low Birth Weight (LBW)","Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW)","Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW)","Normal average weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: VLBW is 1000–1500 gm; 1200 gm falls in this range.  Why A wrong: LBW is 1500–2500 gm, above 1200 gm.  Why C wrong: ELBW is <1000 gm; 1200 gm is above that.  Why D wrong: Normal average weight is ~3000 gm."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A baby classified as Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW) weighs:","options":["1500–2500 gm","1000–1500 gm","<1000 gm","<2000 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ELBW baby weighs <1000 gm.  Why A wrong: 1500–2500 gm is the LBW range.  Why B wrong: 1000–1500 gm is the VLBW range.  Why D wrong: <2000 gm is broader than the ELBW cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A newborn weighing more than 4 kg (4000 gm) is termed macrosomic. This condition is commonly seen in babies of mothers with which condition?","options":["Anaemia","Diabetes","Hypertension","Hypothyroidism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: More than 4 kg (4000 gm) weight is known as macrosomia, commonly seen in a diabetic mother.  Why A wrong: Anaemia is associated with low birth weight, not macrosomia.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not the cause named for macrosomia here.  Why D wrong: Hypothyroidism is not the stated association."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"In Indian children, the normal weight of a neonate is stated as:","options":["2.0–2.5 kg","2.5–3.5 kg","3.0–4.0 kg","3.5–4.5 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Indian children, normal weight of a neonate is 2.5–3.5 kg.  Why A wrong: 2.0–2.5 kg falls in the LBW zone.  Why C wrong: 3.0–4.0 kg is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 3.5–4.5 kg approaches/exceeds macrosomia."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A newborn loses about what percentage of birth weight during the first week (7 days) of life, and regains it within what time?","options":["5% birth weight; regains within 3–5 days","10% birth weight; regains within 7–10 days","15% birth weight; regains within 14 days","20% birth weight; regains within 21 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The newborn loses 10% of birth weight during the first week/7 days (due to environmental adaptation, dilute urine from immature kidneys, and passage of meconium) and regains the lost weight in the next 7 to 10 days.  Why A wrong: The loss is 10%, and regain is 7–10 days.  Why C wrong: 15% loss/14 days is not stated.  Why D wrong: 20% loss/21 days is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A newborn weighs 3 kg at birth. According to the 10% weight-loss rule, what is the expected weight at 7 days of life?","options":["2900 gm (2.9 kg)","2700 gm (2.7 kg)","2500 gm (2.5 kg)","3300 gm (3.3 kg)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A 3 kg baby losing 10% loses 300 gm, so at 7 days the weight is 2700 gm (2.7 kg), per the worked example.  Why A wrong: 2900 gm reflects only ~3% loss.  Why C wrong: 2500 gm reflects a larger loss than 10%.  Why D wrong: 3300 gm is a gain, not a loss."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"The average daily weight gain of a newborn is 30 gm/day during which period, and 15 gm/day during which period?","options":["30 gm/day for the first 3 months; 15 gm/day for the next 3 months","30 gm/day for the first 6 months; 15 gm/day for the next 6 months","15 gm/day for the first 6 months; 30 gm/day for the next 6 months","30 gm/day for the first year; 15 gm/day for the second year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weight increases 30 gm/day during the first 6 months and 15 gm/day for the next 6 months.  Why A wrong: The periods are 6 months each, not 3 months.  Why C wrong: The rates are reversed in this option.  Why D wrong: The periods are 6-month halves of the first year, not first/second year."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What is the normal head circumference of a newborn at birth?","options":["30–32 cm","33–35 cm (13.2 to 14 inch)","36–38 cm","31–33 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At birth, normal head circumference is 33–35 cm (13.2 to 14 inch).  Why A wrong: 30–32 cm is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 36–38 cm exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 31–33 cm is the chest circumference range, not head circumference."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"At birth, the head circumference is approximately how much more than the chest circumference?","options":["1 cm","2 to 3 cm","5 cm","Equal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Head circumference is approximately 2 to 3 cm more than chest circumference at birth.  Why A wrong: 1 cm understates the stated difference.  Why C wrong: 5 cm exceeds the stated difference.  Why D wrong: They are equal only at 1 year of age, not at birth."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"How is head circumference correctly measured in a newborn?","options":["Around the forehead only","Placing tape over the occipital prominence at the back and just over the supra-orbital ridge","From ear to ear over the vertex","Around the neck at the level of the chin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Head circumference is measured by placing the tape over the occipital prominence at the back and just over the supra-orbital ridge.  Why A wrong: Around the forehead only is incorrect placement.  Why C wrong: Ear-to-ear over the vertex measures a different dimension.  Why D wrong: The neck at the chin is unrelated to head circumference."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An abnormally INCREASED head circumference in a newborn is associated with which conditions?","options":["Microcephaly","Hydrocephalus and macrocephaly","Anencephaly","Craniosynostosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased HC is associated with hydrocephalus and macrocephaly.  Why A wrong: Microcephaly is associated with decreased HC.  Why C wrong: Anencephaly is not the stated association.  Why D wrong: Craniosynostosis is not listed here."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"An abnormally DECREASED head circumference is associated with which condition?","options":["Hydrocephalus","Macrocephaly","Microcephaly","Moulding"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased HC is associated with microcephaly.  Why A wrong: Hydrocephalus causes increased HC.  Why B wrong: Macrocephaly is increased HC.  Why D wrong: Moulding is a temporary skull-bone overlap, not decreased HC."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"What is the normal chest circumference of a newborn at birth?","options":["33–35 cm","31–33 cm (12.5–13 inches)","28–30 cm","36–38 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: At birth, normal chest circumference is 31–33 cm (12.5–13 inches), or CC is about 3 cm less than HC.  Why A wrong: 33–35 cm is the head circumference range.  Why C wrong: 28–30 cm is below the stated CC range.  Why D wrong: 36–38 cm exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"At what anatomical level, and during what respiratory point, is the chest circumference of a newborn measured?","options":["At the xiphisternum, during full inspiration","At the level of the nipples, in between inspiration and expiration","At the axilla, during full expiration","At the lowest rib, during breath-holding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chest circumference is measured at the level of the nipples, in between inspiration and expiration.  Why A wrong: The xiphisternum during full inspiration is not stated.  Why C wrong: The axilla during full expiration is incorrect.  Why D wrong: The lowest rib during breath-holding is not the stated method."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"(AIIMS, PGI, ESIC) At what age do the head circumference and chest circumference become equal?","options":["At birth","At 6 months","At 1 year","At 2 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that at 1 year of age head circumference and chest circumference will become equal (AIIMS, PGI, ESIC).  Why A wrong: At birth, HC is 2–3 cm greater than CC.  Why B wrong: 6 months is not the stated age.  Why D wrong: 2 years is not the stated age."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"During head examination, the length of the newborn's head is normally what proportion of the total body length?","options":["1/2 of body length (average 25 cm)","1/4th of body length (average 12.5 cm)","1/6th of body length (average 8 cm)","1/8th of body length (average 6 cm)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The head length is normally 1/4th part of the total body length (average 12.5 cm).  Why A wrong: 1/2 (25 cm) overstates the head proportion.  Why C wrong: 1/6th (8 cm) is not the stated proportion.  Why D wrong: 1/8th (6 cm) is the adult proportion, not the newborn value stated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"\"Moulding\" of the newborn head is best described as:","options":["A pathological swelling of the scalp requiring surgery","A normal condition in which the skull bones overlap during passage of the fetus through the maternal pelvis, resolving automatically within 72 hours","Fluid collection under the periosteum that crosses suture lines","Permanent deformity of the skull from birth trauma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moulding is a normal condition in which skull bones overlap during passage through the maternal pelvis and it resolves automatically within 72 hours.  Why A wrong: It is normal and does not require surgery.  Why C wrong: That describes caput/cephalhematoma-type findings, not moulding.  Why D wrong: Moulding is temporary, not a permanent deformity."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A nurse is preparing to assess a stable neonate's vital signs. To obtain the most accurate readings, which action should the nurse take FIRST?","options":["Take the axillary temperature","Count the respiratory rate by observation","Measure the blood pressure","Palpate the femoral pulse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sequence is least intrusive to more intrusive (RPT), so respiration is assessed first by observation before disturbing the baby.  Why A wrong: Temperature is the most intrusive and is done last; taking it first may make the baby cry and falsely raise HR/RR.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure is more intrusive than observing respiration.  Why D wrong: Pulse is assessed after respiration in the RPT sequence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A newborn's temperature is recorded as 34°C (93.2°F). According to the WHO classification, this represents which category of hypothermia and requires what action?","options":["Mild hypothermia; routine care","Moderate hypothermia; immediate warming of the newborn","Severe hypothermia; intensive skilled care","Normal temperature; no action"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 34°C falls within 32.0–35.9°C = moderate hypothermia, which is dangerous and requires immediate warming of the newborn.  Why A wrong: Mild hypothermia is 36.0–36.4°C, higher than 34°C.  Why C wrong: Severe hypothermia is <32.0°C, lower than 34°C.  Why D wrong: 34°C is well below the normal range of 36.5–37.4°C."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"A term newborn is assessed at 1 minute: completely pink, heart rate 130/min, active movements resisting extension, crying vigorously, and sneezing on suction. What is the APGAR score?","options":["8","9","10","7"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Completely pink = 2 (colour); HR >100 = 2 (pulse); active motion resisting extension = 2 (activity); vigorous cry = 2 (respiratory); sneeze on stimulation = 2 (grimace); total = 10.  Why A wrong: 8 would require two parameters scoring 1; all score 2 here.  Why B wrong: 9 would require one parameter scoring 1; none do.  Why D wrong: 7 undercounts the maximal responses described."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Umbilical cord cutting, APGAR score, Ballard scale, Warm chain…","question":"Regarding the timing of breastfeeding initiation, which single reason from the source best explains why it should begin within 30 minutes of birth?","options":["Colostrum is only available in the first 30 minutes","The first period of reactivity (active/alert stage) lasts only 0–30 minutes; afterward the baby enters decreased responsiveness","Maternal milk supply stops after 30 minutes","The rooting reflex disappears after 30 minutes permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The active and alert first period of reactivity lasts 0–30 minutes, after which the baby enters the period of decreased responsiveness, so feeding should start within 30 minutes.  Why A wrong: Colostrum availability limited to 30 minutes is not stated.  Why C wrong: Milk supply does not stop after 30 minutes; not stated.  Why D wrong: Permanent loss of the rooting reflex is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The skull suture, which is the joint between skull bones in the newborn, is an example of which type of joint?","options":["Synovial joint (diarthrosis)","Cartilaginous joint (amphiarthrosis)","Fibrous joint (synarthrosis)","Ball and socket joint"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the skull suture is the joint between skull bones and is an example of a fibrous joint or synarthrosis; sutures are widely separated in the newborn.  Why A wrong: Synovial (diarthrosis) joints are freely movable, unlike immovable sutures.  Why B wrong: Cartilaginous (amphiarthrosis) joints are slightly movable, not the sutural type described.  Why D wrong: A ball and socket joint (e.g., hip) is highly mobile and unrelated to skull sutures."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"How many sutures are present in the newborn skull?","options":["6","8","10","12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Total 8 sutures are present in new born skull.\"  Why A wrong: 6 is the total number of fontanels, not sutures.  Why C wrong: 10 overstates the number of sutures given.  Why D wrong: 12 is not the value stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Why are the sutures widely separated in a newborn's head?","options":["To allow rapid ossification of the skull","To provide space for development of the brain","To reduce intracranial pressure permanently","To allow the fontanels to close early"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source notes the suture is separated in the newborn head because it provides the space for development of the brain.  Why A wrong: Wide separation delays, not promotes, ossification.  Why C wrong: Suture separation is for brain growth, not permanent ICP reduction.  Why D wrong: Separation does not cause early fontanel closure; it accommodates growth."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A fontanel in the newborn skull is best defined as:","options":["The joint between two skull bones","The wide gaps between the suture lines","A bony ridge on the occipital bone","The point where the spinal cord exits the skull"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines a fontanel as the wide gaps between the suture lines present in the newborn skull.  Why A wrong: The joint between skull bones is the suture, not the fontanel.  Why C wrong: A fontanel is a soft gap, not a bony ridge.  Why D wrong: That describes the foramen magnum, not a fontanel."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"How many fontanels are present in the newborn in total?","options":["4","5","6","8"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Total 6 fontanel are present in the new born.\"  Why A wrong: 4 is fewer than the stated total.  Why B wrong: 5 is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 8 is the number of sutures, not fontanels."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the source, how many mastoid and sphenoid fontanels are present in the newborn respectively?","options":["1 mastoid and 1 sphenoid","2 mastoid and 2 sphenoid","2 mastoid and 1 sphenoid","1 mastoid and 2 sphenoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists mastoid fontanel - 2 and sphenoid fontanel - 2.  Why A wrong: There is 1 each only for the anterior and posterior fontanels.  Why C wrong: Both mastoid and sphenoid are 2, not 2 and 1.  Why D wrong: Both are 2, not 1 and 2."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The anterior fontanel is also known as:","options":["Lambda","Bregma","Glabella","Pterion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the anterior fontanel is also known as Bregma.  Why A wrong: Lambda is the name for the posterior fontanel.  Why C wrong: Glabella is a facial landmark between the eyebrows, not the anterior fontanel.  Why D wrong: Pterion is a lateral skull junction, not the anterior fontanel."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the shape of the anterior fontanel?","options":["Triangular","Diamond shaped","Oval","Circular"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the anterior fontanel is diamond shaped.  Why A wrong: Triangular is the shape of the posterior fontanel.  Why C wrong: Oval is not the described shape.  Why D wrong: Circular is not the described shape."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The anterior fontanel is formed by how many suture lines?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is formed by 4 suture lines: frontal-1, Coronal-2, Sagittal-1.  Why A wrong: 2 understates the number of contributing suture lines.  Why B wrong: 3 suture lines form the posterior fontanel, not the anterior.  Why D wrong: 5 exceeds the stated number."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the anteroposterior and transverse diameter of the anterior fontanel?","options":["AP 1.2 cm and transverse 1.2 cm","AP 3-4 cm and transverse 2 to 3 cm","AP 2-3 cm and transverse 3-4 cm","AP 5-6 cm and transverse 4-5 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the anterior fontanel diameter as anteroposterior 3-4 cm and transverse 2 to 3 cm.  Why A wrong: 1.2 cm each is the posterior fontanel's diameter.  Why C wrong: The values are reversed/incorrect for the anterior fontanel.  Why D wrong: These larger values are not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"At what age does the anterior fontanel normally close?","options":["2 to 3 months","6 to 8 months","12 to 18 months","24 to 36 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the anterior fontanel is closed at 12 to 18 months of age.  Why A wrong: 2-3 months is too early per the source.  Why B wrong: 6-8 months is earlier than the stated closure window.  Why D wrong: If not ossified after 24 months it is pathological, so 24-36 months is abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"If the anterior fontanel is not ossified after which age is it considered pathological and requiring clinical intervention?","options":["12 months","18 months","24 months","36 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if not ossified after 24 months then it is pathological and needs clinical intervention.  Why A wrong: 12 months is within the normal closure window.  Why B wrong: 18 months is the upper end of normal closure, not the pathological threshold.  Why D wrong: 36 months is beyond the threshold; the pathological cutoff is 24 months."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The posterior fontanel is also known as:","options":["Bregma","Lambda","Asterion","Vertex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the posterior fontanel is also known as lambda.  Why A wrong: Bregma is the anterior fontanel.  Why C wrong: Asterion is a lateral skull point, not the posterior fontanel.  Why D wrong: Vertex is the topmost point of the skull, not the posterior fontanel."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the shape of the posterior fontanel?","options":["Diamond shaped","Triangular shaped","Square","Oval"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the posterior fontanel is triangular shaped.  Why A wrong: Diamond shape belongs to the anterior fontanel.  Why C wrong: Square is not the described shape.  Why D wrong: Oval is not the described shape."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The posterior fontanel is formed by 3 suture lines. Which sutures are these?","options":["Frontal-1 and Coronal-2","Sagittal-1 and Lambdoid-2","Coronal-2 and Squamous-1","Frontal-2 and Sagittal-1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the posterior fontanel is formed by 3 suture lines: Sagittal-1, Lambdoid-2.  Why A wrong: Frontal and coronal contribute to the anterior fontanel.  Why C wrong: Squamous suture is not listed as forming the posterior fontanel.  Why D wrong: Frontal suture is not part of the posterior fontanel formation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the anteroposterior diameter of the posterior fontanel?","options":["1.2 cm","2 cm","3-4 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the posterior fontanel diameter as anteroposterior 1.2 cm and transverse 1.2 cm.  Why B wrong: 2 cm is not the stated posterior value.  Why C wrong: 3-4 cm is the anterior fontanel's AP diameter.  Why D wrong: 5 cm is not given for any fontanel here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn presents with a depressed or sunken fontanel. This finding most likely indicates:","options":["Increased intracranial pressure","Dehydration","Meningitis","Normal hydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (RRB-2019) states a depressed or sunken fontanel is seen in dehydration.  Why A wrong: Increased ICP causes a bulging/tense fontanel, the opposite finding.  Why C wrong: Meningitis typically bulges the fontanel, not sinks it.  Why D wrong: A sunken fontanel is abnormal, indicating fluid deficit, not normal hydration."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A bulging or tense fontanel in a newborn is seen in:","options":["Dehydration","Increased intracranial pressure (ICP)","Hypovolemia","Malnutrition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (DSSSB-2019) states a bulging or tense fontanel is seen in increased ICP.  Why A wrong: Dehydration causes a sunken fontanel, not a bulging one.  Why C wrong: Hypovolemia (fluid loss) leads to a sunken fontanel.  Why D wrong: Malnutrition is not the cause given for a tense/bulging fontanel."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Regarding pain assessment, the source states that pain is:","options":["A subjective assessment in adults and an objective assessment in newborn & infant","An objective assessment in both adults and infants","A subjective assessment in both adults and infants","An objective assessment in adults and subjective in infants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pain is a subjective assessment in adults whereas an objective assessment in newborn & infant.  Why B wrong: In adults it is subjective, not objective.  Why C wrong: In newborns/infants it is objective (observed), not subjective.  Why D wrong: The relationship is reversed; adults report subjectively, infants are assessed objectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) is largely replaced by adult haemoglobin (HbA) by approximately what age?","options":["2 weeks","6 weeks","3 months","6 months"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: HbF is ~70-80% at birth and declines gradually (~3-4%/week); it is largely replaced by HbA by ~6 months of age (falling to adult trace levels <2-3% by 6-12 months). [Corrected: the source's \"within 6 weeks\" is physiologically incorrect.]  Why A wrong: 2 weeks is far too early; HbF is still the majority fraction.  Why B wrong: At 6 weeks HbF is still roughly 55-65% — not yet replaced (this is the erroneous source/PYQ value).  Why C wrong: At 3 months HbF is still substantial; replacement is not complete."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The source gives cardiac output in the newborn (SV × HR) as approximately:","options":["100 to 200 ml/minute","300 to 400 ml/minute","500 to 600 ml/minute","800 to 1000 ml/minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source calculates cardiac output in newborn = SV×HR = 5×100/120 = 500 to 600 ml/minute.  Why A wrong: 100-200 ml/min is below the calculated value.  Why B wrong: 300-400 ml/min is below the stated 500-600 range.  Why D wrong: 800-1000 ml/min exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the \"Sutures in Newborn Skull\" table, the coronal suture lies between which bones?","options":["Between two frontal bones","Between frontal and parietal bone","Between two parietal bones","Between parietal and occipital bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the coronal suture is between the frontal and parietal bone (and there are two).  Why A wrong: Between two frontal bones is the frontal suture.  Why C wrong: Between two parietal bones is the sagittal suture.  Why D wrong: Between parietal and occipital bone is the lambdoid suture."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the table, the sagittal suture lies between which bones and how many are there?","options":["Between two parietal bones; One","Between two frontal bones; Two","Between parietal and temporal bone; Two","Between frontal and parietal bone; One"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the sagittal suture is between two parietal bones and there is One.  Why B wrong: Between two frontal bones is the frontal suture (One).  Why C wrong: Between parietal and temporal is the squamous suture (Two).  Why D wrong: Between frontal and parietal is the coronal suture (Two)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The lambdoid suture and the squamous suture are located, respectively, between:","options":["Two frontal bones; two parietal bones","Parietal and occipital bone; parietal and temporal bone","Frontal and parietal bone; two parietal bones","Parietal and temporal bone; parietal and occipital bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states lambdoid suture is between parietal and occipital bone (Two) and squamous suture is between parietal and temporal bone (Two).  Why A wrong: These describe frontal and sagittal sutures, not lambdoid/squamous.  Why C wrong: These describe coronal and sagittal sutures.  Why D wrong: The two are reversed."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the normal blood volume of a newborn?","options":["40 ml/kg","60 ml/kg","80 ml/kg","100 ml/kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists blood volume as 80 ml/kg.  Why A wrong: 40 ml/kg is below the stated value.  Why B wrong: 60 ml/kg is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 100 ml/kg exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The normal RBC count of the newborn is:","options":["4-5 million/mm3","6-8 million/mm3","9-11 million/mm3","2-4 million/mm3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists RBC as 6-8 million/mm3.  Why A wrong: 4-5 million/mm3 is closer to adult values, not the newborn value given.  Why C wrong: 9-11 million/mm3 is above the stated range.  Why D wrong: 2-4 million/mm3 is below the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The normal WBC count of the newborn per the source is:","options":["4000 to 11000/mm3","11000 to 17000/mm3","18000 to 25000/mm3","5000 to 9000/mm3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists WBC as 11000 : 17000 mm3 (11000-17000).  Why A wrong: 4000-11000/mm3 is the typical adult range, not the newborn value given.  Why C wrong: 18000-25000/mm3 exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 5000-9000/mm3 is below the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The normal platelet count of the newborn is:","options":["1-2 lakh/mm3","3-5 lakh/mm3","6-8 lakh/mm3","8-10 lakh/mm3"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists platelets as 3-5 lakh/mm3.  Why A wrong: 1-2 lakh/mm3 is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 6-8 lakh/mm3 exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 8-10 lakh/mm3 exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The normal haemoglobin (Hb) level of the newborn is:","options":["11-14 gm/dl","14-16 gm/dl","17-20 gm/dl","21-24 gm/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Hb as 17-20 gm/dl.  Why A wrong: 11-14 gm/dl is below the stated newborn range.  Why B wrong: 14-16 gm/dl is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 21-24 gm/dl exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The life span of RBCs in the newborn is:","options":["40 days","60 days","80 days","120 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists life span of RBCs as 80 days.  Why A wrong: 40 days is below the stated value.  Why B wrong: 60 days is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 120 days is the adult RBC lifespan, not the newborn value given here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The stroke volume of the newborn per the source is:","options":["1 to 2 ml","3 to 5 ml","6 to 8 ml","10 to 12 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists stroke volume as 3 to 5 ml.  Why A wrong: 1-2 ml is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 6-8 ml exceeds the stated range.  Why D wrong: 10-12 ml exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The cardiac output of the newborn is listed in the blood values as:","options":["100 ml/kg/minute or 250 ml/min","200 ml/kg/minute or 500 ml/min","300 ml/kg/minute or 600 ml/min","50 ml/kg/minute or 150 ml/min"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists cardiac output as 200 ml/kg/minute or 500 ml/min.  Why A wrong: These values are lower than those stated.  Why C wrong: These values are higher than those stated.  Why D wrong: These values are far below those stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"In caput succedaneum, the fluid (oedema) accumulates in which location?","options":["Between the bone/cranium and its periosteum","Between the scalp and the periosteum of the bone","Within the ventricles of the brain","Beneath the dura mater"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states caput succedaneum is an oedema/fluid accumulation occurring between the scalp and periosteum of the bone.  Why A wrong: Accumulation between bone/cranium and periosteum describes cephalohematoma.  Why C wrong: Ventricular fluid relates to hydrocephalus, not caput.  Why D wrong: Subdural collection is not the site described for caput."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Caput succedaneum occurs due to which mechanism?","options":["Rupture of minor emissary veins","Subperiosteal haemorrhage","Improper venous drainage causing fluid accumulation","Arterial bleeding into the skull"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states accumulation of fluid in caput occurs due to improper venous drainage.  Why A wrong: Rupture of minor emissary veins causes cephalohematoma.  Why B wrong: Subperiosteal haemorrhage describes cephalohematoma.  Why D wrong: Caput is a fluid/oedema collection, not arterial bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What causes caput succedaneum during labour?","options":["Pressure of the fetal head against the cervix, which decreases blood flow and results in edema","Forceps injury to the emissary veins","Vitamin K deficiency","Congenital skull fracture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states caput is caused by pressure of the fetal head against the cervix during labour, decreasing blood flow to the area and resulting in edema.  Why B wrong: Emissary vein rupture is the mechanism of cephalohematoma.  Why C wrong: Vitamin K deficiency relates to haemorrhagic disease, not caput.  Why D wrong: Skull fracture is a complication of cephalohematoma, not the cause of caput."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which cardinal sign distinguishes caput succedaneum from cephalohematoma?","options":["The swelling is not compressible","The swelling crosses the suture lines","The swelling increases in size after birth","The swelling develops only after 2-3 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies \"swelling crossed the suture lines\" as the cardinal sign of caput succedaneum.  Why A wrong: Caput is compressible; non-compressibility is a feature of cephalohematoma.  Why C wrong: Caput is present at birth and does not increase in size; cephalohematoma increases.  Why D wrong: Cephalohematoma develops within a few hours and increases over 2-3 days, unlike caput."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which of the following is TRUE of caput succedaneum? (Select all that apply)","options":["The swelling is compressible","It is always present at the time of birth and does not increase in size","It usually disappears after birth within 72 hours","Complications are rare","It does not cross the suture line"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source states caput swelling is compressible (A), is always present at birth and does not increase in size (B), usually disappears within 72 hours (C), and complications are rare (D).  Why E wrong: Caput crosses the suture line; not crossing the suture line is a feature of cephalohematoma."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"In cephalohematoma, the blood accumulation is due to:","options":["Improper venous drainage","Rupture of minor emissary veins (subperiosteal haemorrhage)","Pressure of the head against the cervix","Rupture of a cerebral artery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cephalohematoma occurs due to rupture of minor emissary veins and is caused by subperiosteal haemorrhage.  Why A wrong: Improper venous drainage causes caput, not cephalohematoma.  Why C wrong: Cervical pressure describes caput succedaneum.  Why D wrong: The source attributes it to emissary vein rupture, not a cerebral artery."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"In cephalohematoma, blood accumulates between which structures?","options":["Between the scalp and periosteum","Between the bone (cranium) and its periosteum","Within the subdural space","Between the dura and the arachnoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states blood accumulation in cephalohematoma occurs between the bone or cranium and its periosteum.  Why A wrong: Between scalp and periosteum is the site of caput succedaneum.  Why C wrong: Subdural space is not the described location.  Why D wrong: Subarachnoid location is not the described site."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which statement is TRUE regarding cephalohematoma?","options":["The swelling is compressible and crosses the suture line","The swelling does not cross the suture line and is not compressible","It is always present at birth and disappears within 72 hours","Complications are rare"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cephalohematoma swelling does not cross the suture line and is not compressible.  Why A wrong: Being compressible and crossing the suture line describes caput.  Why C wrong: Cephalohematoma develops within hours after birth and increases in size over 2-3 days; it is not present at birth like caput.  Why D wrong: Cephalohematoma has notable complications (defective clotting, skull fracture, intracranial bleeding, jaundice), unlike caput."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the typical time course of cephalohematoma?","options":["Present at birth and disappears within 72 hours","Develops after birth within a few hours and increases in size over 2-3 days","Develops only after 6 weeks","Present at birth but decreases immediately"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cephalohematoma develops after birth within a few hours and increases in size over 2-3 days.  Why A wrong: Being present at birth and resolving in 72 hours describes caput succedaneum.  Why C wrong: It develops within hours, not after 6 weeks (6 weeks is when a minor thrombus may dissolve).  Why D wrong: It increases rather than immediately decreases."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"In cephalohematoma, if the bleeding is minor, the thrombus may dissolve within:","options":["72 hours","One week","Six weeks","Six months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if bleeding is minor the thrombus may dissolve within six weeks; if severe, surgical intervention may be required.  Why A wrong: 72 hours is the resolution time of caput, not cephalohematoma.  Why B wrong: One week is not the stated period.  Why D wrong: Six months is longer than the stated six weeks."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Complications of cephalohematoma include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Defective blood clotting","Underlying skull fracture","Intracranial bleeding","Jaundice","Congenital cataract"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists complications of cephalohematoma as defective blood clotting, underlying skull fracture, intracranial bleeding, and jaundice.  Why E wrong: Congenital cataract is associated with rubella syndrome/absent red reflex, not cephalohematoma."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Absence of the eyeball in a newborn is termed:","options":["Microphthalmia","Anophthalmia","Anotia","Arrhinia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states absence of the eyeball is known as anophthalmia.  Why A wrong: Microphthalmia means a small eye, not absence, and is not the term given.  Why C wrong: Anotia is absence of the external ear.  Why D wrong: Arrhinia is absence of the nose."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Redness of the eyes or excessive tear production in a newborn indicates:","options":["Normal adaptation","Infection in the eyes","Dehydration","Congenital cataract"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states redness of the eyes or excessive tear production indicates infection in the eyes.  Why A wrong: These are abnormal findings, not normal adaptation (eyes should be clear after birth).  Why C wrong: Dehydration is indicated by a sunken fontanel/dry gums, not eye redness.  Why D wrong: Congenital cataract is indicated by an absent red reflex, not eye redness/tearing."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Ophthalmia neonatorum, described as a whole eye infection in the newborn, is caused by:","options":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Neisseria gonococci","Candida albicans","Corynebacterium diphtheriae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ophthalmia neonatorum is a whole eye infection caused by Neisseria gonococci.  Why A wrong: Chlamydia trachomatis causes neonatal conjunctivitis per the source.  Why C wrong: Candida albicans causes oral thrush, not ophthalmia neonatorum.  Why D wrong: Corynebacterium diphtheriae causes diphtheria, not this eye infection."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Neonatal conjunctivitis in the newborn is caused by:","options":["Neisseria gonococci","Chlamydia trachomatis","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus aureus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states neonatal conjunctivitis is caused by chlamydia trachomatis.  Why A wrong: Neisseria gonococci causes ophthalmia neonatorum (whole eye infection).  Why C wrong: E. coli is not the causative organism given.  Why D wrong: Staphylococcus aureus is not the causative organism given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The mnemonic PERRLA used in pupil examination stands for Pupil, Equal, Round, Reactive to, Light, and:","options":["Adaptation","Accommodation","Acuity","Alignment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source expands PERRLA as Pupil, Equal, Round, Reactive to, Light, Accommodation.  Why A wrong: \"Adaptation\" is not the term given; the A stands for Accommodation.  Why C wrong: Acuity is not part of the PERRLA mnemonic here.  Why D wrong: Alignment is not part of the PERRLA mnemonic here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"During newborn eye assessment, an absent red reflex most likely indicates:","options":["Normal lens of the eye","Possible congenital cataract requiring HCP intervention","Dehydration","Normal newborn adaptation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if the red reflex is absent then a congenital cataract may be present, requiring HCP intervention (a present red reflex means a normal lens).  Why A wrong: A normal lens corresponds to a present red reflex, not an absent one.  Why C wrong: Dehydration is not signalled by the red reflex.  Why D wrong: An absent red reflex is abnormal, not a normal finding."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which ophthalmic eye drops are administered to the newborn for prevention of eye infection?","options":["Azithromycin 0.5% or erythromycin 0.5%","Gentamicin 1% or ciprofloxacin 0.3%","Silver nitrate 5% only","Tetracycline 2% or neomycin 1%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer ophthalmic eye drops azithromycin 0.5% or erythromycin 0.5% for prevention of infection.  Why B wrong: Gentamicin/ciprofloxacin are not the agents named in the source.  Why C wrong: Silver nitrate is not listed; the source specifies azithromycin/erythromycin 0.5%.  Why D wrong: Tetracycline/neomycin are not the agents/strengths given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Rubella syndrome in the mother during pregnancy leads to which triad in the newborn?","options":["Cleft lip, club foot, and cryptorchidism","Congenital cataract, deafness, and Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)","Anophthalmia, anotia, and arrhinia","Caput, cephalohematoma, and jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states rubella syndrome in the mother during pregnancy leads to congenital cataract, deafness, and Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA).  Why A wrong: These are unrelated congenital anomalies not linked to rubella here.  Why C wrong: These are absence anomalies, not the rubella triad.  Why D wrong: These are birth traumas, not features of rubella syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Match the terms: absence of external ear and small external ear are respectively called:","options":["Microtia and anotia","Anotia and microtia","Anophthalmia and arrhinia","Athelia and anorchia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states absence of the external ear is called Anotia and a small external ear is called microtia.  Why A wrong: The terms are reversed.  Why C wrong: Anophthalmia (no eyeball) and arrhinia (no nose) relate to eye/nose, not ear.  Why D wrong: Athelia (absent nipple) and anorchia (absent testes) are unrelated to the ear."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A soft (rather than firm) ear cartilage on newborn examination indicates:","options":["A term newborn","A pre-term newborn","A post-term newborn","Down syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the ear cartilage should be firm; soft ear cartilage is seen in pre-term newborn babies.  Why A wrong: Term newborns have firm cartilage.  Why C wrong: Soft cartilage indicates prematurity, not post-term status.  Why D wrong: Down syndrome is associated with low-set ears, not cartilage softness here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"In assessing the location of the external ear, the top of the pinna should normally be:","options":["Below the line drawn from the outer canthus of the eye","At the line or above the line drawn from the outer canthus of the eye","At the level of the mouth","Below the level of the nostrils"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the top of the pinna should be at the line or above the line drawn from the outer canthus of the eye.  Why A wrong: An ear below that line is \"low set,\" an abnormal finding.  Why C wrong: Mouth-level ear placement is abnormally low.  Why D wrong: Nostril-level placement is abnormally low."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A low-set ear in a newborn is characteristically seen in:","options":["Rubella syndrome","Down syndrome (trisomy of the 21st chromosome)","Turner syndrome","Caput succedaneum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states low-set ears are seen in Down syndrome (trisomy of the 21st chromosome).  Why A wrong: Rubella syndrome causes cataract, deafness, and PDA, not specifically low-set ears here.  Why C wrong: Turner syndrome is not the condition named in the source.  Why D wrong: Caput succedaneum is a birth trauma unrelated to ear position."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"During assessment of ear discharge, a CSF discharge indicates head trauma and should be confirmed by:","options":["Culture and sensitivity only","Glucose assessment and the halo ring test","Ultrasound of the ear","Complete blood count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CSF discharge indicates head trauma and should be confirmed by glucose assessment and the halo ring test.  Why A wrong: Culture/sensitivity is not the confirmatory method given.  Why C wrong: Ultrasound is not the stated confirmation test.  Why D wrong: A CBC is not the stated confirmation for CSF discharge."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"During ear discharge assessment, a bloody discharge most likely indicates:","options":["Infection","Injury during birth","Head trauma with CSF leak","Normal cerumen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bloody discharge indicates injury during birth.  Why A wrong: A purulent discharge indicates infection, not a bloody one.  Why C wrong: CSF discharge (not bloody) indicates head trauma.  Why D wrong: Bloody discharge is abnormal, not normal cerumen."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Absence of the nose in a newborn is termed:","options":["Arrhinia","Anophthalmia","Anotia","Athelia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states absence of the nose is called Arrhinia.  Why B wrong: Anophthalmia is absence of the eyeball.  Why C wrong: Anotia is absence of the external ear.  Why D wrong: Athelia is congenital absence of the breast nipple."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Nasal flaring in a newborn is a sign of:","options":["Normal breathing","Respiratory distress","Dehydration","Hunger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nasal flaring indicates respiratory distress.  Why A wrong: Nasal flaring is abnormal, not a normal breathing pattern.  Why C wrong: Dehydration is shown by a sunken fontanel/dry gums, not nasal flaring.  Why D wrong: Hunger is not signalled by nasal flaring."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The presence of teeth at birth is called:","options":["Deciduous teeth","Natal teeth","Wisdom teeth","Epstein pearl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states presence of teeth at birth is called 'natal teeth' (teeth are normally absent at birth).  Why A wrong: Deciduous teeth are the primary/temporary teeth that erupt later.  Why C wrong: Wisdom teeth are the third molars appearing at 17-22 years.  Why D wrong: Epstein pearl is a normal white oral finding, not teeth."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"During mouth examination, blue gums and dry gums respectively indicate:","options":["Dehydration and cyanosis","Cyanosis and dehydration","Infection and injury","Anaemia and jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states blue gums indicate cyanosis and dry gums indicate dehydration.  Why A wrong: The associations are reversed.  Why C wrong: Infection/injury relate to ear discharge, not gum colour.  Why D wrong: Anaemia/jaundice are not the gum findings described."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Oral thrush in a newborn is a fungal infection caused by:","options":["Candida albicans","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Neisseria gonococci","Chlamydia trachomatis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oral thrush is a fungal infection caused by candida albicans, in which patches are not removed easily, with bleeding and excessive crying.  Why B wrong: Corynebacterium diphtheriae causes diphtheria.  Why C wrong: Neisseria gonococci causes ophthalmia neonatorum.  Why D wrong: Chlamydia trachomatis causes neonatal conjunctivitis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn has white patches in the oral cavity. On rubbing with a gauze-wrapped finger, the patches are removed easily with no bleeding and the baby is not crying. This finding is most consistent with:","options":["Oral thrush","Epstein pearl (a normal condition)","Diphtheria","Natal teeth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Epstein pearl is a normal condition in which patches are removed easily, with no bleeding and the baby not crying.  Why A wrong: In oral thrush the patches are NOT removed easily, and there is bleeding with excessive crying.  Why C wrong: Diphtheria produces an adherent pseudomembrane, not easily removed patches.  Why D wrong: Natal teeth are teeth, not removable white patches."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Diphtheria is a bacterial infection caused by Corynebacterium diphtheriae and is characterized by which finding in the throat?","options":["Easily removable white pearls","A pseudomembrane — a thick gray and white membrane in the throat","Cheesy white body covering","Bluish-green discolouration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diphtheria produces a pseudomembrane, a thick gray and white membrane in the throat.  Why A wrong: Easily removable pearls describe Epstein pearl, not diphtheria.  Why C wrong: A cheesy white body covering describes vernix caseosa.  Why D wrong: Bluish-green discolouration describes a Mongolian spot."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The primary/temporary teeth are also known as:","options":["Permanent teeth","Deciduous teeth or teeth of milk","Wisdom teeth","Natal teeth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states primary/temporary teeth are also known as deciduous teeth or teeth of milk.  Why A wrong: Permanent teeth are the secondary teeth.  Why C wrong: Wisdom teeth are the third molars of the permanent set.  Why D wrong: Natal teeth are teeth present at birth, not the primary set as a whole."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the total number of primary (deciduous) teeth?","options":["20","28","30","32"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of primary teeth is 20 (formula 212/212 × 2 = 20).  Why B wrong: 28 is not the stated number.  Why C wrong: 30 is not the stated number.  Why D wrong: 32 is the total number of permanent teeth, not primary."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which teeth are absent in the primary/temporary dentition?","options":["Incisors","Canines","Premolar teeth","Molars"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states premolar teeth are absent in temporary teeth (the primary teeth table shows 0 first and second premolars).  Why A wrong: Primary dentition includes central and lateral incisors.  Why B wrong: Primary dentition includes canines (cuspids).  Why D wrong: Primary dentition includes first and second molars."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The dental formula of the primary teeth is:","options":["2123/2123 × 2 = 32","212/212 × 2 = 20","123/123 × 2 = 12","221/221 × 2 = 20"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the formula of primary teeth as 212/212 × 2 = 20.  Why A wrong: 2123/2123 × 2 = 32 is the permanent teeth formula.  Why C wrong: 123/123 × 2 = 12 is not a formula given.  Why D wrong: 221/221 × 2 is not the formula given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"At what age does the first eruption of primary teeth typically occur?","options":["At birth","At 6 months","At 12 months","At 6 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states first eruption of primary teeth is at 6 months.  Why A wrong: Teeth at birth are abnormal (natal teeth), not the normal first eruption.  Why C wrong: 12 months is later than the stated first eruption.  Why D wrong: 6 years is when the first permanent molar erupts, not the primary teeth."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The temporary (primary) teeth completely appear between which age range?","options":["12-18 months","24-30 months","18-25 years","17-22 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states temporary teeth completely appear between 24-30 months.  Why A wrong: 12-18 months is the anterior fontanel closure window, not full primary dentition.  Why C wrong: 18-25 years is when permanent teeth completely appear.  Why D wrong: 17-22 years is when wisdom teeth (third molars) appear."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The first eruption of permanent teeth occurs at 6 years and is which tooth?","options":["Central incisor","First molar","Canine","Third molar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states first eruption of permanent teeth is at 6 years: the First Molar.  Why A wrong: The central incisor is not the tooth named for the 6-year first permanent eruption here.  Why C wrong: The canine is not the first permanent tooth to erupt per the source.  Why D wrong: The third molar (wisdom tooth) is the last to appear, at 17-22 years."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"What is the total number of permanent (secondary) teeth, and what is their dental formula?","options":["20 teeth; 212/212 × 2 = 20","32 teeth; 2123/2123 × 2 = 32","28 teeth; 2122/2122 × 2 = 28","30 teeth; 2124/2124 × 2 = 30"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total number of permanent teeth is 32, with formula 2123/2123 × 2 = 32.  Why A wrong: 20 teeth (212/212 × 2) is the primary dentition.  Why C wrong: 28 with that formula is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 30 with that formula is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The permanent teeth completely appear between which age range?","options":["6 to 12 years","18 to 25 years","24 to 30 months","12 to 18 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states permanent teeth completely appear between 18 to 25 years of age.  Why A wrong: 6-12 years is the mixed-dentition period, not full permanent appearance.  Why C wrong: 24-30 months is when temporary teeth complete.  Why D wrong: 12-18 months is the anterior fontanel closure window."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The last permanent teeth to appear are the third molars, known as \"wisdom teeth,\" which erupt at:","options":["6 years","12 years","17-22 years","24-30 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the last appearance of permanent teeth is the third molar teeth at 17-22 years, known as 'wisdom teeth'.  Why A wrong: 6 years is when the first permanent molar erupts.  Why B wrong: 12 years is not the stated eruption age for third molars.  Why D wrong: 24-30 months is when primary teeth complete."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the primary vs secondary teeth table, how many first premolars (bicuspids) are present in the primary and permanent dentitions respectively?","options":["4 in primary and 4 in permanent","0 in primary and 4 in permanent","4 in primary and 0 in permanent","0 in primary and 0 in permanent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table shows first premolars = 0 in primary (decidual) and 4 in secondary (permanent) teeth.  Why A wrong: Primary dentition has 0 premolars, not 4.  Why C wrong: The values are reversed; permanent has 4 premolars.  Why D wrong: Permanent dentition has 4 first premolars, not 0."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the teeth function table, which teeth serve \"to grasp and tear food\"?","options":["Incisors","Canine (cuspid)","Premolar","Molar"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns the function \"to grasp and tear food\" to the canine (cuspid).  Why A wrong: Incisors are used to bite off pieces of food.  Why C wrong: Premolars are used to grind food particles.  Why D wrong: Molars are used to grind and chew food particles."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the teeth function table, the incisors function is:","options":["To grasp and tear food","To bite off pieces of food","To grind food particles","To grind and chew food particles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table assigns \"to bite off pieces of food\" to the incisors (central and lateral).  Why A wrong: Grasping and tearing is the canine's function.  Why C wrong: Grinding food particles is the premolar's function.  Why D wrong: Grinding and chewing is the molar's function."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Torticollis (wry neck / loxia) in a newborn is due to damage of which cranial nerve?","options":["7th cranial nerve","9th cranial nerve","11th cranial nerve","12th cranial nerve"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states torticollis is twisting of the neck to one side due to damage in the 11th cranial nerve, also known as loxia/wry neck.  Why A wrong: The 7th cranial nerve (facial) is not the nerve implicated in the source.  Why B wrong: The 9th cranial nerve (glossopharyngeal) is not implicated.  Why D wrong: The 12th cranial nerve (hypoglossal) is not implicated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which is described as the most common fracture during normal delivery?","options":["Skull fracture","Clavicle fracture","Femur fracture","Humerus fracture"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess for clavicle fracture, as it is the most common fracture during normal delivery.  Why A wrong: Skull fracture is a possible complication of cephalohematoma, not the commonest delivery fracture.  Why C wrong: Femur fracture is not identified as the most common.  Why D wrong: Humerus fracture is not identified as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"On assessing nipple bud size in a newborn, a size of less than 5 mm indicates:","options":["A term newborn","A pre-term newborn","A post-term newborn","Witch milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the nipple bud size should be more than 5 mm, and less than 5 mm indicates a pre-term newborn.  Why A wrong: A term newborn has buds more than 5 mm.  Why C wrong: Small buds indicate prematurity, not post-term status.  Why D wrong: Witch milk is a secretion, unrelated to bud size measurement."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"\"Witch milk,\" a milky white secretion on the chest of a newborn, is secreted due to:","options":["Infection","Maternal hormones","Dehydration","Fetal haemoglobin breakdown"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (CHO-UP-2019) states witch milk is a milky white secretion on the chest of the newborn secreted due to maternal hormones.  Why A wrong: It is a hormonal effect, not an infection.  Why C wrong: Dehydration does not cause witch milk.  Why D wrong: Fetal Hb breakdown relates to jaundice, not witch milk."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Congenital absence of the breast nipple is called:","options":["Athelia","Anorchia","Anotia","Arrhinia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states congenital absence of the breast nipple is called Athelia.  Why B wrong: Anorchia is absence of the testes.  Why C wrong: Anotia is absence of the external ear.  Why D wrong: Arrhinia is absence of the nose."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Match the normal breathing sounds to their auscultation site: the bronchial sound is best heard over:","options":["The alveoli","The trachea","The main stem bronchi","The lower posterior chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the bronchial sound is listened over the trachea.  Why A wrong: The vesicular sound is heard over the alveoli.  Why C wrong: The broncho-vesicular sound is heard over the main stem bronchi.  Why D wrong: The lower posterior chest is a general monitoring site, not the specific bronchial location."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The broncho-vesicular breath sound is best auscultated over:","options":["The trachea","The main stem bronchi","The alveoli","The apex of the heart"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the broncho-vesical(ar) sound is listened over the main stem bronchi.  Why A wrong: The bronchial sound is heard over the trachea.  Why C wrong: The vesicular sound is heard over the alveoli.  Why D wrong: The cardiac apex is not a breath-sound auscultation site."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The vesicular breath sound is normally heard over:","options":["The trachea","The main stem bronchi","The alveoli","The larynx"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the vesicular sound is listened over the alveoli.  Why A wrong: The trachea corresponds to the bronchial sound.  Why B wrong: The main stem bronchi correspond to the broncho-vesicular sound.  Why D wrong: The larynx is not the site given for vesicular sounds."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Abnormal breathing sounds are also known as:","options":["Vesicular sounds","Adventitious sounds","Bronchial sounds","Physiological sounds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states abnormal breathing sounds are also known as adventitious sounds.  Why A wrong: Vesicular sounds are normal, not abnormal.  Why C wrong: Bronchial sounds are normal breath sounds.  Why D wrong: \"Physiological\" implies normal, not abnormal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Match the adventitious sound to its commonly associated condition: Stridor is commonly seen in:","options":["Asthma","Pulmonary oedema","Laryngeal obstruction","Respiratory distress"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states stridor is commonly seen in laryngeal obstruction.  Why A wrong: Wheezing is commonly seen in asthma.  Why B wrong: Crackle is commonly seen in pulmonary oedema.  Why D wrong: Grunting is commonly seen in respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which adventitious breath sound is commonly seen in asthma?","options":["Wheezing","Crackle","Stridor","Grunting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states wheezing is commonly seen in asthma.  Why B wrong: Crackle is associated with pulmonary oedema.  Why C wrong: Stridor is associated with laryngeal obstruction.  Why D wrong: Grunting is associated with respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A crackle sound on auscultation is commonly seen in:","options":["Asthma","Pulmonary oedema","Laryngeal obstruction","Respiratory distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states crackle is commonly seen in pulmonary oedema.  Why A wrong: Wheezing, not crackle, is linked to asthma.  Why C wrong: Stridor is linked to laryngeal obstruction.  Why D wrong: Grunting is linked to respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Grunting as an abnormal breath sound is commonly seen in:","options":["Laryngeal obstruction","Asthma","Respiratory distress","Pulmonary oedema"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states grunting is commonly seen in respiratory distress.  Why A wrong: Stridor, not grunting, is linked to laryngeal obstruction.  Why B wrong: Wheezing is linked to asthma.  Why D wrong: Crackle is linked to pulmonary oedema."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Inter-costal retraction on examination of a newborn is a sign of:","options":["Normal chest expansion","Respiratory distress or blocked airway","Dehydration","Cardiac failure only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inter-costal retraction is a sign of respiratory distress or blocked airway.  Why A wrong: Retractions are abnormal, not a normal expansion pattern.  Why C wrong: Dehydration is assessed by skin turgor/fontanel, not retractions.  Why D wrong: The source attributes retractions to respiratory distress/blocked airway, not cardiac failure specifically."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A normal umbilical cord usually contains how many and which blood vessels?","options":["2 veins and 1 artery","2 arteries and 1 vein (3 vessels total)","1 artery and 1 vein (2 vessels total)","3 arteries and 1 vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the umbilical cord usually has 3 blood vessels: 2 umbilical arteries and 1 umbilical vein.  Why A wrong: The count of arteries and veins is reversed.  Why C wrong: A two-vessel cord (single artery) is abnormal, not the usual finding.  Why D wrong: There are 2 arteries, not 3."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the source, one umbilical vein disappears (obliterates) at what age?","options":["Immediately at birth","4 months of age","6 weeks of age","1 year of age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states 1 umbilical vein disappeared at 4 months of age.  Why A wrong: The source specifies 4 months, not immediately at birth.  Why C wrong: 6 weeks is the timeframe for fetal Hb replacement, not vein disappearance.  Why D wrong: 1 year is not the age stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The umbilical cord is also known by which name?","options":["Funis","Lambda","Bregma","Athelia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (DSSSB PHN-2015) states the umbilical cord is also known as Funis.  Why B wrong: Lambda is the posterior fontanel.  Why C wrong: Bregma is the anterior fontanel.  Why D wrong: Athelia is congenital absence of the nipple."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Infection of the umbilical cord is known as:","options":["Omphalocele","Omphalitis","Gastroschisis","Orchitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states infection of the umbilical cord is known as omphalitis.  Why A wrong: Omphalocele is a covered abdominal wall protrusion, not an infection.  Why C wrong: Gastroschisis is an uncovered abdominal wall defect.  Why D wrong: Orchitis is inflammation of the testes."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"On auscultation, the bowel sound in a newborn should be present at what time after birth, and it is present due to which activity?","options":["Immediately at birth, due to swallowing","1 hour after birth, due to peristaltic movement","24 hours after birth, due to feeding","72 hours after birth, due to meconium passage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the bowel sound should be present 1 hour after birth, present due to peristaltic movement.  Why A wrong: The source specifies 1 hour after birth, not immediately.  Why C wrong: 24 hours is later than the stated 1 hour.  Why D wrong: 72 hours is not the stated time; the mechanism is peristalsis, not meconium."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn is born with abdominal organs protruding outside the body, and the protruding internal organs are covered by a thin layer. This defect is:","options":["Gastroschisis","Omphalocele (umbilical hernia)","Omphalitis","Simian crease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines omphalocele/umbilical hernia as protrusion of abdominal organs outside the body in which the internal organ is covered by a thin layer.  Why A wrong: In gastroschisis the protrusion is without covering.  Why C wrong: Omphalitis is infection of the umbilical cord, not organ herniation.  Why D wrong: A simian crease is a single palmar crease, unrelated to abdominal defects."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"How does gastroschisis differ from omphalocele?","options":["In gastroschisis the protruding organs are covered by a thin layer","In gastroschisis the abdominal organs protrude through the abdominal wall without any covering","Gastroschisis is an infection of the umbilical cord","Gastroschisis only involves the umbilical vein"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines gastroschisis as protrusion of abdominal organs outside the abdominal wall without covering.  Why A wrong: The covered protrusion describes omphalocele, not gastroschisis.  Why C wrong: Infection of the umbilical cord is omphalitis.  Why D wrong: Gastroschisis is a wall defect with organ protrusion, not limited to the umbilical vein."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Auscultation of the newborn abdomen should start from which quadrant?","options":["Left upper quadrant","Right lower quadrant, then auscultate all six quadrants","Right upper quadrant","The periumbilical region only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (JIPMER-2016) states auscultation of the abdomen should start from the right lower quadrant then auscultate all six quadrants.  Why A wrong: The starting point given is the right lower quadrant, not the left upper.  Why C wrong: The right upper quadrant is not the specified starting point.  Why D wrong: All six quadrants are auscultated, not the periumbilical region alone."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"On skin examination, the presence of vernix caseosa only within the body folds indicates:","options":["A term newborn","A pre-term newborn","A post-term newborn","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if vernix is on the body folds then it indicates a term newborn.  Why B wrong: Vernix covering the entire body indicates a pre-term newborn.  Why C wrong: Absent vernix indicates a post-term newborn.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is assessed by skin turgor, not vernix distribution."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"If vernix caseosa covers the entire body of the newborn, it indicates:","options":["A term newborn","A pre-term newborn","A post-term newborn","Cyanosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if vernix covers the entire body then it indicates a pre-term newborn.  Why A wrong: Vernix limited to body folds indicates a term newborn.  Why C wrong: Absent vernix indicates a post-term newborn.  Why D wrong: Cyanosis is a blue skin colour, unrelated to vernix distribution."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Absent vernix caseosa on skin examination indicates:","options":["A term newborn","A pre-term newborn","A post-term newborn","A normal full-term newborn"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if vernix is absent then it indicates a post-term newborn.  Why A wrong: A term newborn has vernix in the body folds.  Why B wrong: A pre-term newborn is covered entirely by vernix.  Why D wrong: Absent vernix specifically indicates post-term, not normal full-term status."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Blue skin colour in a newborn indicates:","options":["Cyanosis","Jaundice","Normal pink colour","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal skin colour is pink, and blue skin then indicates cyanosis.  Why B wrong: Jaundice produces yellow, not blue, skin.  Why C wrong: Pink is the normal colour; blue is abnormal.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is assessed by turgor, not blue colouration."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Dark red / plethoric skin in a newborn indicates:","options":["A pre-term newborn","A post-term newborn","Cyanosis","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dark red/plethoric skin indicates a pre-term newborn.  Why B wrong: Dry peeling (desquamation) skin indicates a post-term newborn.  Why C wrong: Cyanosis is blue skin, not dark red.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is assessed by turgor, not plethora."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Dry, peeling skin (desquamation) in a newborn indicates:","options":["A pre-term newborn","A term newborn","A post-term newborn","Cyanosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dry peeling skin/desquamation indicates a post-term newborn.  Why A wrong: Dark red/plethoric skin (and full vernix) indicate a pre-term newborn.  Why B wrong: Term status is indicated by vernix in body folds, not desquamation.  Why D wrong: Cyanosis is a blue colour change, not desquamation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn is noted to have a single transverse palmar crease. This \"simian crease\" is associated with:","options":["Cyanosis","Down syndrome","Post-term birth","Dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a single transverse crease indicates Down syndrome and is also known as a simian crease.  Why A wrong: Cyanosis is a colour change, not a palmar crease pattern.  Why C wrong: Post-term birth is indicated by desquamation/long nails, not simian creases.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is not associated with the simian crease."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Regarding nail growth in the newborn, short nails and long nails respectively indicate:","options":["Post-term and pre-term newborn","Pre-term and post-term newborn","Term and pre-term newborn","Post-term and term newborn"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states short nails indicate pre-term and long nails indicate post-term newborn (nails reach the finger tips in term babies).  Why A wrong: The associations are reversed.  Why C wrong: Short nails indicate pre-term, not term.  Why D wrong: Long nails indicate post-term, and short nails indicate pre-term, not term."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Milia are best described as:","options":["Small whitish, pinpoint spots appearing on the forehead, nose, and chin","Bluish-green discolouration over the lumbosacral area","A cheesy white covering over the entire body","Fine hair covering the newborn's body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source note states milia are small whitish, pinpoint spots that appear on the forehead, nose, and chin.  Why B wrong: Bluish-green lumbosacral discolouration describes a Mongolian spot.  Why C wrong: A cheesy white body covering describes vernix caseosa.  Why D wrong: Fine hair covering describes lanugo."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Vernix caseosa, the cheesy white covering of the newborn, covers the entire body of the fetus at what gestational age?","options":["18-20 weeks","22-24 weeks","28-30 weeks","34-36 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source caption states vernix caseosa covers the entire body of the fetus at 22-24 weeks of gestation.  Why A wrong: 18-20 weeks is when lanugo develops.  Why C wrong: 28-30 weeks is not the value given.  Why D wrong: 34-36 weeks is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Lanugo, the fine hair covering the newborn, develops at what gestational age?","options":["12-14 weeks","18-20 weeks","22-24 weeks","30-32 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source caption states lanugo (fine hair covering) develops at 18-20 weeks of gestation.  Why A wrong: 12-14 weeks is not the value given.  Why C wrong: 22-24 weeks is when vernix covers the entire body.  Why D wrong: 30-32 weeks is not the value given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn's body shows deep pink/red colour on one side while the other side remains pale. This finding is called:","options":["Acrocyanosis","Harlequin sign","Mongolian spot","Nevus flammeus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source caption describes the Harlequin sign as deep pink or red colour developing over one side of the newborn's body while the other side remains pale or normal in colour.  Why A wrong: Acrocyanosis is pink body with blue extremities, not a one-sided colour split.  Why C wrong: A Mongolian spot is a bluish lumbosacral discolouration.  Why D wrong: Nevus flammeus (port wine stain) is a flat red facial mark, not a bilateral colour difference."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Acrocyanosis in a newborn is described as body pink with blue extremities. According to the source, it is:","options":["Always pathological and needs immediate surgery","Normal in the first few hours after birth and may be noted intermittently for the next 7 to 10 days","A sign of congenital heart disease requiring transfer","Permanent and never resolves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source caption states acrocyanosis is body pink and extremities blue, normal in the first few hours after birth and may be noted intermittently for the next 7 to 10 days.  Why A wrong: The source describes it as normal, not requiring surgery.  Why C wrong: The source frames acrocyanosis as normal, not a sign requiring transfer.  Why D wrong: It is transient (up to 7-10 days), not permanent."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"In a female newborn, slightly enlarged labia majora & minora and an enlarged clitoris are considered:","options":["Abnormal, requiring surgery","Normal findings","Signs of infection","Signs of dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in a female child, slightly enlarged labia majora & minora is a normal condition, and the clitoris may be enlarged normally.  Why A wrong: These are normal, not surgical findings.  Why C wrong: They are not signs of infection.  Why D wrong: They are not signs of dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Pseudo menstruation in a female newborn occurs due to which maternal hormone?","options":["Progesterone only","Oestrogen (specially)","Testosterone","Prolactin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (IGIMS-2018) states pseudo menstruation occurs due to the maternal hormone, specially oestrogen.  Why A wrong: The source specifies oestrogen specially, though the Also Remember note mentions both; the featured hormone is oestrogen.  Why C wrong: Testosterone is not the causative maternal hormone.  Why D wrong: Prolactin is not the causative hormone named."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Failure of the testes to descend into the scrotum in a male newborn is known as:","options":["Cryptorchidism","Orchitis","Phimosis","Hypospadias"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the undescended testes condition is known as cryptorchidism.  Why B wrong: Orchitis is inflammation of the testes, not undescent.  Why C wrong: Phimosis is failure to retract the foreskin.  Why D wrong: Hypospadias is a urethral opening on the ventral surface of the penis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Epispadias is defined as the opening of the urethra on which surface of the penis?","options":["Ventral surface","Dorsal surface","Lateral surface","Tip of the glans (normal)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states epispadias is the opening of the urethra on the dorsal surface of the penis.  Why A wrong: The ventral surface opening is hypospadias.  Why C wrong: A lateral surface opening is not described.  Why D wrong: An opening at the tip of the glans is the normal position, not epispadias."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which of the following is a recognized subtype of epispadias per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Penopubic epispadias — urethra found near the pubic bone","Glandular epispadias — urethra found on the head of the penis","Penile epispadias — urethra found along the shaft of the penis","Ventral epispadias — urethra on the underside","Scrotal epispadias — urethra within the scrotum"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists three subtypes of epispadias: penopubic (near the pubic bone), glandular (on the head of the penis), and penile (along the shaft).  Why D wrong: \"Ventral epispadias\" is not listed; a ventral opening would be hypospadias.  Why E wrong: \"Scrotal epispadias\" is not one of the three subtypes given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Hypospadias is defined as the urethral opening on which surface of the penis?","options":["Dorsal surface","Ventral surface","The head of the penis","Near the pubic bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hypospadias is an opening on the ventral surface of the penis.  Why A wrong: A dorsal opening is epispadias.  Why C wrong: The head-of-penis opening is glandular epispadias.  Why D wrong: An opening near the pubic bone is penopubic epispadias."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Phimosis is best defined as:","options":["Retracted foreskin of an uncircumcised male that cannot be returned to its normal position","Failure to retract the foreskin back over the penis","Inflammation of the glans penis","Absence of the testes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states phimosis is an abnormal condition of the penis in which there is failure to retract the foreskin over the penis (i.e., foreskin cannot be pulled back to expose the glans).  Why A wrong: A retracted foreskin that cannot be returned describes paraphimosis.  Why C wrong: Inflammation of the glans is balanitis.  Why D wrong: Absence of testes is anorchia/anorchidism."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Paraphimosis is defined as:","options":["Failure to retract the foreskin back over the penis","Retracted foreskin of an uncircumcised male that cannot be returned to its normal anatomical position","Opening of urethra on the dorsal surface","Milky white secretion from the chest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states paraphimosis is a retracted foreskin of an uncircumcised male that cannot be returned to its normal anatomical position (foreskin cannot be pulled forward to cover the glans).  Why A wrong: Failure to retract the foreskin is phimosis.  Why C wrong: A dorsal urethral opening is epispadias.  Why D wrong: Milky white chest secretion is witch milk."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Match the inflammatory conditions: inflammation of the foreskin and inflammation of both the glans and foreskin are respectively called:","options":["Balanitis and orchitis","Posthitis and balanoposthitis","Balanoposthitis and posthitis","Orchitis and balanitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inflammation of the foreskin is Posthitis and inflammation of both the glans and foreskin is Balanoposthitis.  Why A wrong: Balanitis is inflammation of the glans; orchitis is inflammation of the testes.  Why C wrong: The two terms are reversed.  Why D wrong: Orchitis and balanitis describe testes and glans inflammation, not the foreskin/both."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Inflammation of the glans penis and inflammation of the testes are respectively termed:","options":["Orchitis and balanitis","Balanitis and orchitis","Posthitis and balanoposthitis","Phimosis and paraphimosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states inflammation of the glans penis is Balanitis and inflammation of the testes is Orchitis.  Why A wrong: The terms are reversed.  Why C wrong: Posthitis/balanoposthitis relate to the foreskin and glans+foreskin, not glans/testes alone.  Why D wrong: Phimosis/paraphimosis are foreskin retraction disorders, not inflammations."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Absence of the testes as a congenital abnormality is known as:","options":["Cryptorchidism","Anorchia (anorchidism)","Orchitis","Athelia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess for congenital abnormalities like anorchia or anorchidism, meaning absence of the testes.  Why A wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended (but present) testes, not absence.  Why C wrong: Orchitis is inflammation of the testes.  Why D wrong: Athelia is congenital absence of the nipple."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The Tanner staging orchidometer is used to:","options":["Measure head circumference","Measure testicle size and track sexual development in boys (testicle volume in ml)","Assess breath sounds","Grade cephalohematoma severity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the orchidometer is used to measure testicle size and track sexual development in boys, with testicle volume measured in ml.  Why A wrong: It measures testicular volume, not head circumference.  Why C wrong: It is unrelated to breath sound assessment.  Why D wrong: It does not grade cephalohematoma."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"On the orchidometer scale shown, the adulthood testicular volumes are represented by which bead values (in mL)?","options":["1, 2, 3","4, 6","8, 10","18, 25"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The source figure labels the adulthood beads as 18 and 25 mL.  Why A wrong: 1, 2, 3 mL represent childhood.  Why B wrong: 4, 6 mL represent early puberty.  Why C wrong: 8, 10 mL represent mid puberty."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"On the orchidometer, childhood testicular volume is represented by which values (in mL)?","options":["1, 2, 3","4, 6","14, 16","18, 25"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source figure labels childhood as 1, 2, 3 mL.  Why B wrong: 4, 6 mL represent early puberty.  Why C wrong: 14, 16 mL represent late puberty.  Why D wrong: 18, 25 mL represent adulthood."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Match the extremity finding: \"combined finger\" (fusion of fingers) is called:","options":["Polydactyly","Syndactyly","Dactyly","Koilonychia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states syndactyly means combined finger.  Why A wrong: Polydactyly means more fingers than normal.  Why C wrong: Dactyly refers to the normal 5 fingers.  Why D wrong: Koilonychia is spoon-shaped nails."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The presence of more fingers than the normal number is termed:","options":["Syndactyly","Polydactyly","Dactyly","Drumstick nail"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (Safdarjung-2018) states polydactyly means more fingers instead of the normal finger number.  Why A wrong: Syndactyly is combined/fused fingers.  Why C wrong: Dactyly is the normal 5 fingers.  Why D wrong: Drumstick nail refers to clubbing."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A drumstick nail (clubbing finger) on newborn extremity examination indicates:","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Chronic hypoxia","Dehydration","Normal nail growth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a drumstick nail (clubbing finger) indicates chronic hypoxia.  Why A wrong: Koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails), not clubbing, indicates iron deficiency anaemia.  Why C wrong: Dehydration is not indicated by clubbing.  Why D wrong: Clubbing is abnormal, not normal nail growth."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails) is a sign of:","options":["Chronic hypoxia","Hypochromic anaemia, such as iron deficiency anaemia","Cyanosis","Down syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states koilonychia (spoon-shaped nails) indicates hypochromic anaemia like iron deficiency anaemia.  Why A wrong: Chronic hypoxia is indicated by clubbing (drumstick nail).  Why C wrong: Cyanosis is a bluish colour, not a nail shape change.  Why D wrong: Down syndrome is associated with the simian crease/low-set ears, not koilonychia here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"During extremity examination, developmental dysplasia of the hip is abbreviated and assessed as:","options":["DDH (development dysplasia of the Hip)","PDA","DDT","HDN"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess for abnormality of the hip joint like development dysplasia of the Hip (DDH).  Why B wrong: PDA is patent ductus arteriosus, a cardiac finding of rubella syndrome.  Why C wrong: DDT is not a term used in the source.  Why D wrong: HDN is not the abbreviation given for hip dysplasia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A birth mark also known as \"stork bite and salmon patch,\" consisting of dilated capillaries on the face, nose tip and eyelids, pink/red and flat, is:","options":["Nevus flammeus","Telangiectasia nevi","Nevus vasculosus","Mongolian spot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states telangiectasia nevi is also known as stork bite and salmon patch, consisting of dilated capillaries on the face, nose tip, and eyelids, pink or red and flat, disappearing at 2 years of age.  Why A wrong: Nevus flammeus is the port wine stain that does not disappear.  Why C wrong: Nevus vasculosus is the strawberry mark (elevated).  Why D wrong: A Mongolian spot is a bluish lumbosacral discolouration."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"By what age does telangiectasia nevi (stork bite/salmon patch) disappear, and does it require cosmetic surgery?","options":["2 years of age; no cosmetic surgery needed","7 to 9 years of age; cosmetic surgery required","It does not disappear; cosmetic surgery may be required","1 to 2 years of age; surgery required"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states telangiectasia nevi disappears at 2 years of age and needs no cosmetic surgery.  Why B wrong: 7-9 years is when nevus vasculosus (strawberry mark) disappears.  Why C wrong: Not disappearing describes nevus flammeus (port wine stain).  Why D wrong: 1-2 years is when a Mongolian spot disappears."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The \"Port Wine Stain\" birth mark, a tuft of capillaries (capillary angioma) occurring on the face, red and flat, is:","options":["Telangiectasia nevi","Nevus flammeus","Nevus vasculosus","Milia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nevus flammeus is also known as Port Wine Stain, a tuft of capillaries due to capillary angioma, occurring on the face, red coloured and flat.  Why A wrong: Telangiectasia nevi is the stork bite/salmon patch.  Why C wrong: Nevus vasculosus is the strawberry mark (elevated, haemangioma).  Why D wrong: Milia are whitish pinpoint spots, not a capillary lesion."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which statement about nevus flammeus (port wine stain) is TRUE?","options":["It disappears at 2 years of age","It does not disappear with time and may require cosmetic surgery in future","It is elevated and palpable","It appears over the lumbosacral area"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nevus flammeus does not disappear with time and may require cosmetic surgery in future.  Why A wrong: Telangiectasia nevi disappears at 2 years, not nevus flammeus.  Why C wrong: Nevus flammeus is flat; the elevated palpable mark is nevus vasculosus.  Why D wrong: The lumbosacral location describes the Mongolian spot."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"The \"strawberry mark,\" a tuft of capillaries due to capillary haemangioma, commonly appearing on the head region, red and elevated/palpable, is:","options":["Nevus flammeus","Nevus vasculosus","Telangiectasia nevi","Mongolian spot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nevus vasculosus is also known as strawberry mark, a tuft of capillaries due to capillary haemangioma, commonly on the head region, red and elevated/palpable.  Why A wrong: Nevus flammeus (port wine stain) is flat, not elevated.  Why C wrong: Telangiectasia nevi is flat and on the face/nose/eyelids.  Why D wrong: The Mongolian spot is a flat bluish lumbosacral mark."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"By what age does nevus vasculosus (strawberry mark) typically disappear?","options":["1 to 2 years of age","2 years of age","7 to 9 years of age","It does not disappear"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states nevus vasculosus disappears at 7 to 9 years of age and does not require cosmetic surgery in the future.  Why A wrong: 1-2 years is when a Mongolian spot disappears.  Why B wrong: 2 years is when telangiectasia nevi disappears.  Why D wrong: Not disappearing describes nevus flammeus, not nevus vasculosus."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A Mongolian spot, commonly appearing in dark-skinned people, is described as:","options":["A red elevated mark on the head","Greenish and bluish discolouration of the skin over the lumbosacral area","Whitish pinpoint spots on the nose and chin","A flat pink patch on the eyelids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Mongolian spot commonly appears in dark-skinned people (like West Indies) and is a greenish and bluish discolouration of the skin over the lumbosacral area.  Why A wrong: A red elevated head mark describes nevus vasculosus.  Why C wrong: Whitish pinpoint spots describe milia.  Why D wrong: A flat pink eyelid patch describes telangiectasia nevi."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"By what age does a Mongolian spot typically disappear?","options":["Within a few months","1 to 2 years of age","7 to 9 years of age","It does not disappear"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a Mongolian spot disappears at 1 to 2 years of age.  Why A wrong: Disappearing within a few months describes milia.  Why C wrong: 7-9 years is when nevus vasculosus disappears.  Why D wrong: Not disappearing describes nevus flammeus."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"According to the birth marks section, milia (white patch on the face due to obstruction of the sebaceous gland secretion) disappear:","options":["At 2 years of age","Within a few months","At 7 to 9 years of age","Never"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states milia is a white patch on the face due to obstruction in the secretion of the sebaceous gland and disappears within a few months.  Why A wrong: 2 years is when telangiectasia nevi disappears.  Why C wrong: 7-9 years is when nevus vasculosus disappears.  Why D wrong: Milia do disappear (within a few months); it is nevus flammeus that persists."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn shows swelling on the head that is soft/compressible and crosses the suture line, present at birth without increasing in size. Which is the priority understanding for the nurse?","options":["This is cephalohematoma and needs urgent surgery","This is caput succedaneum, which is benign, usually resolves within 72 hours, and complications are rare","This indicates a skull fracture requiring immediate imaging","This is a Mongolian spot"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A compressible swelling that crosses the suture line, present at birth and not increasing, is caput succedaneum, which usually disappears within 72 hours with rare complications.  Why A wrong: Cephalohematoma does NOT cross the suture line and is non-compressible; it is not this presentation.  Why C wrong: Crossing the suture line and being compressible argues against a fracture; caput is benign.  Why D wrong: A Mongolian spot is a bluish lumbosacral skin discolouration, not a head swelling."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"Which of the following are correctly matched normal newborn blood values? (Select all that apply)","options":["Blood volume — 80 ml/kg","Hb — 17-20 gm/dl","RBC lifespan — 80 days","Platelets — 3-5 lakh/mm3","WBC — 4000-11000/mm3"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists blood volume 80 ml/kg (A), Hb 17-20 gm/dl (B), RBC lifespan 80 days (C), and platelets 3-5 lakh/mm3 (D).  Why E wrong: The source gives WBC as 11000-17000/mm3, not 4000-11000/mm3 (which is the adult range)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn examination reveals nasal flaring, grunting, and inter-costal retraction. These findings together most strongly indicate:","options":["Normal newborn adaptation","Respiratory distress","Dehydration","Physiological jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, nasal flaring indicates respiratory distress, grunting is commonly seen in respiratory distress, and inter-costal retraction is a sign of respiratory distress or blocked airway; together they point to respiratory distress.  Why A wrong: These are abnormal signs, not normal adaptation.  Why C wrong: Dehydration is assessed by skin turgor and fontanel, not these respiratory signs.  Why D wrong: Jaundice is a yellow discolouration, unrelated to these breathing signs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"A newborn has white oral patches that bleed on rubbing and cannot be removed easily, and the baby cries excessively. What is the most likely condition and its cause?","options":["Epstein pearl, a normal condition","Oral thrush caused by Candida albicans","Diphtheria caused by Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Natal teeth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oral thrush (candida albicans) presents with patches that are not removed easily, bleeding, and excessive crying.  Why A wrong: Epstein pearl patches are removed easily with no bleeding and no crying.  Why C wrong: Diphtheria forms an adherent gray-white pseudomembrane in the throat, a different presentation.  Why D wrong: Natal teeth are teeth present at birth, not white removable/bleeding patches."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Newborn skull sutures & fontanels…","question":"During newborn skin assessment, the nurse assesses skin turgor over which area to determine hydration status?","options":["The forehead","The abdomen","The sole of the foot","The back"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to assess skin turgor over the abdomen to determine hydration status.  Why A wrong: The forehead is not the site specified for turgor assessment.  Why C wrong: The sole of the foot is not the specified turgor site.  Why D wrong: The back is not the site given; the abdomen is specified."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A nurse strokes the cheek of a neonate and the baby turns his face toward the stimulated side. Which reflex is being elicited?","options":["Sucking reflex","Rooting reflex","Moro reflex","Tonic neck reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rooting reflex is elicited when the neonate is stimulated (on the cheek/mouth) and responds by turning the face toward the stimulated site.  Why A wrong: Sucking is elicited by an object entering the oral cavity, not by cheek stimulation.  Why C wrong: Moro is elicited by suddenly lowering the head, producing extension then flexion of extremities.  Why D wrong: Tonic neck reflex involves turning the face and limb extension/flexion, not turning toward a stimulated cheek."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"By what age does the rooting reflex normally disappear, though it may persist up to 1 year?","options":["3 to 4 months","4 to 6 months","8 months","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The rooting reflex disappears at 3 to 4 months but may persist up to 1 year of age.  Why B wrong: 4–6 months is the disappearance age for the startle and palmar grasp reflexes.  Why C wrong: 8 months is the plantar reflex disappearance age.  Why D wrong: 1 year is only the maximum persistence, not the normal disappearance age."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The sucking reflex is initiated when:","options":["A loud noise is produced near the neonate","A foreign object enters the oral cavity of the neonate","The sole of the foot is stroked","The head is suddenly lowered"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sucking begins when any foreign object enters the oral cavity of the neonate.  Why A wrong: A loud noise elicits the startle reflex.  Why C wrong: Stroking the sole elicits plantar/Babinski responses.  Why D wrong: Suddenly lowering the head elicits the Moro reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The sucking reflex normally disappears by 1 year but may persist up to:","options":["4 months","6 months","2 years","5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The sucking reflex disappears at 1 year but may persist up to 2 years.  Why A/B wrong: These are too early; the reflex persists beyond them.  Why D wrong: 5 years is not stated; maximum persistence is 2 years."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"To elicit the Moro reflex, the nurse should:","options":["Place a finger in the neonate's palm","Hold the neonate in semi-sitting position then suddenly lower the head end by 30 degrees","Turn the neonate's face to one side","Stroke the sole from heel to toe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Moro stimulant is to hold the neonate in a semi-sitting position and then suddenly lower the head end 30 degrees.  Why A wrong: A palm stimulus elicits the palmar grasp reflex.  Why C wrong: Turning the face to one side elicits the tonic neck reflex.  Why D wrong: Stroking the sole elicits the Babinski sign."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The classic Moro reflex response consists of sharp extension with abduction of the extremities, immediately followed by:","options":["Sharp flexion with adduction and the thumb/finger in a 'C' position","Continuous crying only","Dorsiflexion of the big toe","Rapid stepping movements"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Moro response is fast extension with abduction, then sharp flexion with adduction and four fingers and thumb in a 'C' position.  Why B wrong: Crying is not the defining motor component described.  Why C wrong: Dorsiflexion of the big toe is the Babinski response.  Why D wrong: Rapid stepping is the dancing/stepping reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"At what age does the Moro reflex normally disappear?","options":["3 to 4 months","4 to 6 months","8 months","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Moro reflex disappears at 3 to 4 months.  Why B wrong: 4–6 months applies to the startle and palmar grasp reflexes.  Why C wrong: 8 months is the plantar reflex.  Why D wrong: 1 year applies to the sucking and Babinski reflexes."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A neonate shows a sharp flexion of the body with adduction when a loud voice is produced in the environment. This is the:","options":["Moro reflex","Startle reflex","Rooting reflex","Galant reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The startle reflex occurs when any loud voice is produced in the environment, and the response is sharp flexion with adduction.  Why A wrong: Moro is triggered by sudden lowering of the head, not sound alone, and includes an extension phase.  Why C wrong: Rooting is triggered by cheek stimulation.  Why D wrong: Galant is elicited by stroking the spine in ventral suspension."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The startle reflex disappears by which age?","options":["3 to 4 months","4 to 6 months","8 months","2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The startle reflex disappears at 4–6 months.  Why A wrong: 3–4 months is Moro, rooting, tonic neck and stepping.  Why C wrong: 8 months is the plantar reflex.  Why D wrong: 2 years is a maximum persistence figure for sucking/Babinski, not startle."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The (asymmetric) tonic neck reflex is also known by which other name?","options":["Fencing reflex","Moro reflex","Landau reflex","Perez reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The asymmetric tonic neck reflex (ATNR) is the \"fencing reflex\" — when the head turns to one side, the arm/leg on that side extend and the opposite limbs flex (fencer's posture). [Corrected: the source's \"fanning reflex\" is an error — toe fanning belongs to the Babinski response.]  Why B wrong: The Moro is the startle/embrace reflex, a different reflex.  Why C wrong: Landau is a separate ventral-suspension reflex.  Why D wrong: Perez is a separate spinal-stimulation reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When a neonate's face is turned toward one side to elicit the tonic neck reflex, the expected response is:","options":["Extension of extremities on the same side and flexion on the opposite side","Flexion of extremities on the same side and extension on the opposite side","Bilateral extension of all extremities","Bilateral flexion of all extremities"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: With the face toward one side, the same side shows extension and the opposite side shows flexion of the extremities.  Why B wrong: This reverses the described same-side/opposite-side responses.  Why C/D wrong: The response is asymmetric (one side extension, other flexion), not bilaterally uniform."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The tonic neck (Fanning) reflex disappears at approximately:","options":["3 to 4 months","6 months","8 months","12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The tonic neck reflex disappears at 3 to 4 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3–4 month disappearance age for this reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Holding a neonate in a vertical position and allowing the feet to touch a firm surface produces rapid flexion and extension of the lower extremities, appearing as if the baby is walking. This reflex is called:","options":["Palmar grasp reflex","Dancing or stepping reflex","Plantar reflex","Parachute reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The dancing or stepping reflex is elicited by holding the neonate vertical and letting the lower extremities touch a firm surface, producing rapid flexion/extension resembling dancing/stepping.  Why A wrong: Palmar grasp involves the palm, not the feet on a surface.  Why C wrong: The plantar reflex causes toe curling, not stepping.  Why D wrong: Parachute involves extension/abduction when the baby is moved through air."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The dancing/stepping reflex disappears at:","options":["3 to 4 months","4 to 6 months","7 months","8 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stepping/dancing reflex disappears at 3 to 4 months.  Why B wrong: 4–6 months applies to palmar grasp/startle.  Why C wrong: 7 months is the appearance age of the parachute reflex.  Why D wrong: 8 months is the plantar reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When a foreign particle is placed in the palm of a neonate, the baby grasps the object. This is the:","options":["Plantar reflex","Palmar grasp reflex","Perez reflex","Tonic neck reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The palmar grasp reflex is grasping of an object placed in the palm of the neonate.  Why A wrong: The plantar reflex involves the sole/toes, not the palm.  Why C wrong: Perez involves spinal column stimulation in prone position.  Why D wrong: Tonic neck involves turning the face to one side."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The palmar grasp reflex normally disappears at:","options":["3 to 4 months","4 to 6 months","8 months","1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The palmar grasp reflex disappears at 4–6 months.  Why A wrong: 3–4 months applies to rooting, Moro, tonic neck and stepping.  Why C wrong: 8 months is the plantar reflex.  Why D wrong: 1 year is the sucking/Babinski disappearance age."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When the sole of the foot is stimulated at the base of the toes, the toes curl downward. This response is the:","options":["Babinski sign","Plantar reflex","Dancing reflex","Doll's eye reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The plantar reflex is elicited by stimulating the sole at the base of the toes, causing the toes to curl downward.  Why A wrong: Babinski produces dorsiflexion/upward movement and fanning of the toes, the opposite direction.  Why C wrong: The dancing reflex involves the whole lower limb stepping motion.  Why D wrong: The doll's eye reflex involves eye movement with head turning."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The plantar reflex disappears at approximately:","options":["4 months","6 months","8 months","12 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The plantar reflex disappears at 8 months.  Why A/B/D wrong: These do not match the stated 8-month disappearance age."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"To elicit the Perez reflex, the newborn is placed prone on a firm surface and the vertebral column is stimulated:","options":["From the cervical vertebra moving toward the sacral vertebra","From the sacral vertebra moving toward the cervical vertebra","Only over the lumbar region","By turning the head to one side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the Perez reflex, stimulation starts from the sacral vertebra and moves toward the cervical vertebra.  Why A wrong: The direction is sacral-to-cervical, not cervical-to-sacral.  Why C wrong: The entire vertebral column is stimulated, not only the lumbar area.  Why D wrong: Head turning describes the tonic neck reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The response to the Perez reflex includes crying, flexion of extremities, and:","options":["Lordosis of the spine","Kyphosis of the spine","Scoliosis of the spine","No spinal movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Perez response is crying, flexion of extremities, and lordosis of the spine.  Why B/C wrong: Kyphosis and scoliosis are not the described response.  Why D wrong: There is a definite spinal response (lordosis)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Perez reflex disappears at:","options":["3 to 4 months","4 to 6 months","7 months","8 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Perez reflex disappears at 4 to 6 months.  Why A wrong: 3–4 months applies to tonic neck/stepping/Moro.  Why C wrong: 7 months is parachute appearance.  Why D wrong: 8 months is the plantar reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Galant reflex is elicited by:","options":["Placing an object in the palm","Holding the newborn in ventral suspension (face down) and stroking along one side of the spine","Lowering the head suddenly by 30 degrees","Turning the face to one side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Galant reflex is elicited by holding the newborn in ventral suspension (face down) and stroking along one side of the spine.  Why A wrong: A palm stimulus elicits the palmar grasp.  Why C wrong: Lowering the head elicits the Moro reflex.  Why D wrong: Turning the face elicits the tonic neck reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The normal reaction of the Galant reflex is:","options":["Extension of both legs","Lateral flexion (swimming of spine) toward the stimulated side","Curling of the toes","Grasping with the hand"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The normal Galant reaction is lateral flexion toward the stimulated side (swimming of spine toward the stimulated side).  Why A wrong: Leg extension is not the Galant response.  Why C wrong: Toe curling is the plantar reflex.  Why D wrong: Grasping is the palmar grasp reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Regarding the Galant reflex, which is correct?","options":["It emerges at 7 months and never disappears","It is present at birth and disappears at or before 4 months","It appears at 3 months and disappears at 24 months","It is a lifelong reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Galant reflex is present at birth and disappears at or before 4 months of age.  Why A wrong: 7-month emergence and never disappearing describe the parachute reflex.  Why C wrong: Appearing at 3 months and disappearing at 12–24 months describes the Landau reflex.  Why D wrong: Galant does disappear (by about 4 months); it is not lifelong."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Landau reflex refers to the position of infants when the head is held horizontally in the air in the prone position. If the head is flexed, then:","options":["The hips, knees and elbows also flex","All extremities extend","Only the arms extend","The baby cries and arches the back"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Landau reflex, if the head is flexed, the hip, knees and elbows are also flexed.  Why B wrong: Extension of all extremities is not the described response to head flexion.  Why C wrong: Not only the arms; hips, knees and elbows are involved.  Why D wrong: Crying and arching describe the Perez reflex, not Landau."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Landau reflex emerges and disappears at which ages, respectively?","options":["Emerges at birth; disappears at 4 months","Emerges 3 months after birth; disappears at 12–24 months","Emerges at 7 months; never disappears","Emerges at 6 months; disappears at 8 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Landau reflex emerges 3 months after birth and disappears at 12–24 months of age.  Why A wrong: Birth emergence with 4-month disappearance describes the Galant reflex.  Why C wrong: 7-month emergence with never disappearing describes the parachute reflex.  Why D wrong: These ages do not match the Landau reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The clinical significance of an absent Landau reflex is that it can occur in:","options":["Hypertonia, hypotonia and mental abnormality","Only normal healthy infants","Dehydration only","Physiological jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Absence of the Landau reflex occurs in hypertonia, hypotonia and mental abnormality.  Why B wrong: Its absence is abnormal, not a normal finding.  Why C/D wrong: The source links its absence to tone/mental abnormality, not dehydration or jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"To elicit the Babinski sign, a blunt object is used to stroke the sole starting from the:","options":["Heel, moving toward the ball of the foot through the lateral aspect of the sole","Ball of the foot, moving toward the heel","Base of the toes only","Medial arch toward the great toe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Babinski technique starts from the heel and moves toward the ball of the foot through the lateral aspect of the sole using a blunt object.  Why B wrong: The direction is heel-to-ball, not ball-to-heel.  Why C wrong: Stimulating only the base of the toes describes the plantar reflex.  Why D wrong: The stroke follows the lateral aspect of the sole, not the medial arch."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A positive Babinski sign produces:","options":["Downward curling of the toes","Dorsiflexion/upward movement of the big toe and hyperextension (fanning) of the toes","No toe movement","Grasping of the object"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Babinski response is dorsiflexion or upward movement of the big toe and hyperextension/fanning of the toes.  Why A wrong: Downward curling is the normal plantar (negative Babinski) response.  Why C wrong: There is a definite response (dorsiflexion + fanning).  Why D wrong: Grasping is the palmar grasp reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Babinski sign normally disappears at 1 year but may persist up to 2 years in some children. After what age does a persistent Babinski response always indicate neurological abnormality?","options":["After 6 months","After 1 year","After 2 years","After 5 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After the age of 2 years, a Babinski response always indicates neurological abnormalities.  Why A/B wrong: Up to 1 year (and persisting to 2 years) it can still be normal.  Why D wrong: The threshold stated is 2 years, not 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A nurse turns a newborn's face toward the left and right and observes that the eyes do not move with the movement of the face. Which reflex does this describe?","options":["Parachute reflex","Doll's eye reflex","Galant reflex","Landau reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The doll's eye reflex is elicited by turning the newborn's face left and right; the eyes do not move with the movement of the face.  Why A wrong: Parachute involves limb extension/abduction when moved through air.  Why C wrong: Galant involves spinal stroking.  Why D wrong: Landau involves head/limb positioning in ventral suspension."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The doll's eye reflex disappears at:","options":["3 to 4 months","6 months","7 months","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The doll's eye reflex disappears at 3 to 4 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated 3–4 month disappearance age."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The parachute reflex is elicited when the newborn is moved through the air and, during returning, the response is:","options":["Flexion of all limbs","Extension with abduction of the extremities","Grasping movement of hands","Toe curling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The parachute response is extension with abduction of the extremities as if to break a fall.  Why A wrong: The response is extension/abduction, not flexion.  Why C wrong: Grasping is the palmar grasp reflex.  Why D wrong: Toe curling is the plantar reflex."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which statement about the parachute reflex is correct? (AIIMS BBSR-2018)","options":["It is present at birth and disappears at 4 months","It appears at 7 months, is not present at birth, and never disappears (lifelong)","It appears at 3 months and disappears at 24 months","It disappears at 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The parachute reflex appears at 7 months, is not present at birth, and never disappears (lifelong).  Why A wrong: Present-at-birth/4-month disappearance describes the Galant reflex.  Why C wrong: 3–24 months describes the Landau reflex.  Why D wrong: The parachute reflex never disappears; it does not disappear at 1 year."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When the oral cavity fills with food particles, a systematic movement of the pharynx and epiglottis moves the food from the oral cavity to the stomach. This reflex is the:","options":["Sucking reflex","Swallowing reflex","Gag reflex","Rooting reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The swallowing reflex moves food from the oral cavity to the stomach via coordinated pharyngeal/epiglottic movement.  Why A wrong: Sucking is initiated by an object in the mouth, not moving food to the stomach.  Why C wrong: The gag reflex produces vomiting from posterior pharyngeal stimulation.  Why D wrong: Rooting is turning toward a cheek stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following is a lifelong (never disappearing) reflex? Select all that apply.","options":["Sneezing reflex","Swallowing reflex","Coughing reflex","Moro reflex","Gag reflex"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Sneezing, swallowing, coughing and gag reflexes are the lifelong reflexes of the newborn (never disappear).  Why A correct: Sneezing is protective for the upper respiratory tract, lifelong.  Why B correct: Swallowing moves food to the stomach, lifelong.  Why C correct: Coughing protects the lower respiratory tract, lifelong.  Why D wrong: The Moro reflex disappears at 3–4 months, so it is not lifelong.  Why E correct: The gag reflex protects against foreign particles at the posterior pharynx, lifelong."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Stimulation of the upper respiratory tract by foreign particles produces which protective reflex?","options":["Coughing reflex","Sneezing reflex","Gag reflex","Swallowing reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sneezing is the protective response when the upper respiratory tract is stimulated by foreign particles.  Why A wrong: Coughing is triggered by lower respiratory tract stimulation.  Why C wrong: The gag reflex is triggered at the posterior pharynx.  Why D wrong: Swallowing moves food to the stomach and is not a protective response to airway irritation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Stimulation of the lower respiratory tract by foreign objects produces which protective reflex?","options":["Sneezing reflex","Coughing reflex","Gag reflex","Doll's eye reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coughing is the protective response to lower respiratory tract stimulation by foreign objects.  Why A wrong: Sneezing corresponds to the upper respiratory tract.  Why C wrong: The gag reflex is a posterior pharyngeal response.  Why D wrong: The doll's eye reflex involves eye movement."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When a foreign particle stimulates the posterior pharyngeal region, the protective response is vomiting. This reflex is the:","options":["Gag reflex","Sneezing reflex","Swallowing reflex","Sucking reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The gag reflex is the vomiting response when a foreign particle stimulates the posterior pharyngeal part.  Why B wrong: Sneezing is an upper respiratory response.  Why C wrong: Swallowing moves food to the stomach.  Why D wrong: Sucking begins when an object enters the mouth."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Regarding the Babinski reflex, which statement is correct?","options":["A positive Babinski reflex is normal in infants but pathological in adults","A positive Babinski reflex is normal in adults","A negative Babinski reflex is abnormal in adults","A positive Babinski reflex is always abnormal at any age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A positive Babinski reflex is normal in infants but pathological in adults.  Why B wrong: In adults a positive Babinski is pathological, not normal.  Why C wrong: A negative Babinski is normal in adults; it is abnormal in infants.  Why D wrong: It is normal in infants, so it is not always abnormal at any age."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which statement correctly describes dorsiflexion and plantar flexion of the newborn foot?","options":["Dorsiflexion (movement toward the body) is normal in neonates and abnormal in adults","Dorsiflexion is abnormal in neonates","Plantar flexion is normal in neonates and abnormal in adults","Both movements are abnormal at all ages"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dorsiflexion (movement toward the body) is normal in neonates and abnormal in adults.  Why B wrong: Dorsiflexion is normal, not abnormal, in neonates.  Why C wrong: Plantar flexion (movement toward the plantar surface) is normal in adults and abnormal in neonates — the reverse of this option.  Why D wrong: Each movement is normal in one group, not abnormal at all ages."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Passive immunity in utero is transferred to the fetus by which immunoglobulin, and through which route?","options":["IgA through colostrum","IgG through the placenta, beginning from 12 weeks","IgM produced by the fetus","IgE through amniotic fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive immunity in utero is IgG passing from mother to fetus through the placenta, beginning from 12 weeks.  Why A wrong: IgA through colostrum is passive immunity after birth, not in utero.  Why C wrong: Fetal IgM production is active immunity, not passive.  Why D wrong: IgE via amniotic fluid is not described."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Passive immunity after birth is provided to the newborn mainly through which immunoglobulin in colostrum?","options":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passive immunity after birth is IgA passing from mother to newborn through colostrum, the precursor of breast milk.  Why A wrong: IgG is the placental (in utero) passive immunity.  Why C wrong: IgM is fetal-produced active immunity.  Why D wrong: IgE is not the immunoglobulin described for colostrum."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Active immunity in utero is characterized by the fetus producing which immunoglobulin, and by when?","options":["IgG by 12 weeks","IgA by birth","IgM by the end of the first trimester","IgD by term"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Active immunity in utero means the fetus produces IgM by the end of the first trimester.  Why A wrong: IgG passing at 12 weeks is passive (maternal) immunity, not fetal active production.  Why B wrong: IgA via colostrum is passive immunity after birth.  Why D wrong: IgD by term is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The daily fluid requirement for term babies is:","options":["130 to 200 ml/kg or 2 to 3 oz/lb of body weight","150 ml/kg","100 ml/kg","50 to 80 ml/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Term babies require 130 to 200 ml/kg or 2 to 3 oz/lb of body weight.  Why B wrong: 150 ml/kg is the fluid requirement for preterm babies.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not stated for term babies."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The daily fluid requirement for preterm babies is:","options":["130 to 200 ml/kg","150 ml/kg","200 ml/kg","100 ml/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preterm babies require 150 ml/kg of fluid per day.  Why A wrong: 130–200 ml/kg is the term baby range.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated preterm value."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The caloric requirement for a preterm neonate is:","options":["120–150 kcal/kg/day","110–130 kcal/kg/day","90–100 kcal/kg/day","60–80 kcal/kg/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Preterm neonates require 120–150 kcal/kg/day.  Why B wrong: 110–130 kcal/kg/day is the term neonate requirement.  Why C/D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The caloric requirement for a term neonate is:","options":["120–150 kcal/kg/day","110–130 kcal/kg/day","150–180 kcal/kg/day","80–100 kcal/kg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Term neonates require 110–130 kcal/kg/day.  Why A wrong: 120–150 kcal/kg/day applies to preterm neonates.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated term neonate values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The protein requirement for a preterm newborn is:","options":["2.5–3 gm/kg","2.0 to 2.2 gm/kg","1.5 to 1.8 gm/day","1.0 gm/kg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Preterm newborns require 2.5–3 gm/kg of protein.  Why B wrong: 2.0–2.2 gm/kg is for term newborns.  Why C wrong: 1.5–1.8 gm/day is for infants and toddlers.  Why D wrong: 1.0 gm/kg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The protein requirement for a term newborn is:","options":["2.5–3 gm/kg","2.0 to 2.2 gm/kg","1.5 to 1.8 gm/day","3.5 gm/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Term newborns require 2.0 to 2.2 gm/kg of protein.  Why A wrong: 2.5–3 gm/kg is for preterm newborns.  Why C wrong: 1.5–1.8 gm/day is for infants and toddlers.  Why D wrong: 3.5 gm/kg is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The protein requirement for an infant and toddler is:","options":["2.5–3 gm/kg","2.0 to 2.2 gm/kg","1.5 to 1.8 gm/day","4 gm/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Infants and toddlers require 1.5 to 1.8 gm/day of protein.  Why A wrong: 2.5–3 gm/kg is for preterm newborns.  Why B wrong: 2.0–2.2 gm/kg is for term newborns.  Why D wrong: 4 gm/day is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The recommended dose of Vitamin D for preterm and term babies to prophylaxis rickets is:","options":["200 IU/daily for up to 3 months","400 IU/daily for up to 6 months","800 IU/daily for 1 year","100 IU/daily for 12 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The recommended dose is 400 IU/daily for up to 6 months to prophylaxis rickets.  Why A/C/D wrong: These dose/duration combinations do not match the stated 400 IU/daily for up to 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"According to the text, the primary cause of rickets in premature infants is:","options":["Vitamin D deficiency alone","Deficiency of calcium and phosphorus, not vitamin D","Excess iron intake","Deficiency of vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The primary cause of rickets in premature infants is a deficiency of calcium and phosphorus, not vitamin D; vitamin D is necessary for active absorption of calcium and skeletal health.  Why A wrong: The source specifically states it is NOT primarily vitamin D deficiency in premature infants.  Why C wrong: Excess iron is unrelated to rickets here.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K deficiency causes bleeding, not rickets."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"For extremely low birth weight (ELBW) babies weighing <1000 g, the recommended dose of Vitamin K IM at birth is:","options":["0.1 mg","0.5 mg","1 mg","2 mg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ELBW babies <1000 g should receive 0.5 mg IM at birth.  Why A wrong: 0.1 mg is the dose for all other (non-ELBW) babies.  Why C/D wrong: These doses are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"For a normal/term newborn (birth weight >=1500 g), the recommended prophylactic Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione) dose at birth is:","options":["0.1 mg","0.5 mg","1 mg","5 mg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The universally recommended single IM dose of Vitamin K1 for a normal/term newborn is 1 mg (0.5 mg is used for LBW <1500 g / ELBW). [Corrected: the source's \"0.1 mg\" is a 10-fold decimal error; the guideline dose is 1 mg IM.]  Why A wrong: 0.1 mg is a ~10-fold underdose and is not a recognised prophylactic dose (decimal error in source).  Why B wrong: 0.5 mg is reserved for low-birth-weight (<1500 g) / ELBW babies.  Why D wrong: 5 mg is not the neonatal prophylactic dose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"In addition to standard nutrition, which supplementation is specifically needed for preterm babies? Select all that apply.","options":["Iron","Calcium and phosphorus","Vitamin A","Vitamin C only","Sodium bicarbonate"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Iron, calcium and phosphorus, and vitamin A supplementation are also needed for preterm babies.  Why A correct: Iron supplementation is specifically listed.  Why B correct: Calcium and phosphorus are listed together.  Why C correct: Vitamin A is listed.  Why D wrong: Vitamin C alone is not listed in this supplementation set.  Why E wrong: Sodium bicarbonate is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"According to WHO/the text, exclusive breastfeeding is recommended for the first:","options":["3 months","6 months","1 year","2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exclusive breastfeeding should be done for the first 6 months of the infant's age.  Why A wrong: 3 months is too short per the guidance.  Why C wrong: 1 year is not the exclusive breastfeeding period.  Why D wrong: 2 years is the recommended total breastfeeding duration, not the exclusive period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Milk secreted for a few days after childbirth, characterized by high protein and antibody content, is known as:","options":["Mature milk","Colostrum","Transitional milk","Foremilk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum is the milk secreted for a few days after childbirth, high in protein and antibody content.  Why A wrong: Mature milk is secreted from about 14 days and is watery.  Why C wrong: Transitional milk follows colostrum (days 4–14).  Why D wrong: Foremilk is the watery milk at the start of a feed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Colostrum is also known as:","options":["Liquid gold or precursor of breast milk","Hind milk","Transitional milk","Weaning milk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum is also known as \"liquid gold\" or the precursor of breast milk.  Why B wrong: Hind milk is the fat-rich milk at the end of a feed.  Why C wrong: Transitional milk is a distinct later stage.  Why D wrong: \"Weaning milk\" is not a term used here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The total secretion of breast milk per day is approximately:","options":["200 to 300 ml","400 to 500 ml","600 to 700 ml","900 to 1000 ml"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The total secretion of breast milk is about 600 to 700 ml per day, sufficient for the baby.  Why A/B/D wrong: These volumes do not match the stated 600–700 ml/day."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A Paladai is best described as:","options":["A breast pump for expressing milk","A small bowl with a long pointed lip traditionally used for feeding LBW infants","A nasogastric feeding tube","A type of pacifier"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A Paladai is a small bowl with a long pointed lip traditionally used for feeding low-birth-weight (LBW) infants in some cultures.  Why A wrong: It is a feeding bowl, not a pump.  Why C wrong: It is not an NG tube.  Why D wrong: It is a feeding device, not a pacifier."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The suckling action of an infant produces prolactin, leading to milk production. This is an example of a:","options":["Negative feedback system","Positive feedback system","Neutral feedback system","Closed-loop inhibitory system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suckling produces prolactin and more milk, and the product (milk) produces more suckling and more hormone — a positive feedback system.  Why A wrong: Milk production increases rather than being inhibited, so it is not negative feedback.  Why C/D wrong: The described self-amplifying loop is positive feedback, not neutral or inhibitory."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"According to WHO, breastfeeding after a normal vaginal delivery should be initiated:","options":["Within 30 minutes to 1 hour (or as soon as possible)","Within 4 hours","After 24 hours","After the first bath"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In normal vaginal delivery, initiation should be within 30 minutes to 1 hour or as soon as possible (WHO).  Why B wrong: Within 4 hours applies to caesarean delivery.  Why C wrong: 24 hours is too late.  Why D wrong: Initiation timing is tied to delivery, not bathing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"After a caesarean delivery, breastfeeding should be initiated:","options":["Within 30 minutes","Within 1 hour","Within 4 hours or when anaesthetic medicine effects have subsided in the mother","After 48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In caesarean delivery, initiation is within 4 hours or when anaesthetic medicine effects have subsided in the mother.  Why A/B wrong: 30 minutes to 1 hour applies to normal vaginal delivery.  Why D wrong: 48 hours is far later than recommended."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The most common position for breastfeeding is the:","options":["Laid back position","Cradle hold position","Football hold position","Side-lying position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common breastfeeding position is the cradle hold position.  Why A wrong: The laid back position (biological nursing position) is a different position and not the most common one described.  Why C/D wrong: Football and side-lying positions are not identified as the most common here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The laid back position for breastfeeding is also known as the: (Jharkhand CHO-2019)","options":["Biological nursing position","Cradle hold position","Cross-cradle position","Clutch position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The laid back position is also known as the biological nursing position (Jharkhand CHO-2019).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate positions and are not the alternate name for the laid back position."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Breast milk contains lactose and galactose that are essential for:","options":["Bone mineralization","Brain growth","Iron absorption","Muscle contraction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast milk has lactose and galactose that are essential for brain growth.  Why A wrong: Bone mineralization relates to calcium/phosphorus/vitamin D, not these sugars per the text.  Why C wrong: Iron absorption is not the described function of these sugars.  Why D wrong: Muscle contraction is not the stated role."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which amino acids in breast milk are important for neurotransmitters?","options":["Glycine and alanine","Taurine and cysteine","Leucine and lysine","Glutamine and proline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast milk contains amino acids taurine and cysteine, which are important for neurotransmitters.  Why A/C/D wrong: These amino acids are not the ones identified for neurotransmitter function in the text."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which maternal benefit of breastfeeding is correctly described?","options":["It increases the risk of postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)","It reduces PPH and improves uterine involution","It causes early return of menstruation","It increases the risk of ovarian cancer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breastfeeding reduces PPH and improves uterine involution.  Why A wrong: It reduces, not increases, PPH.  Why C wrong: It causes lactational amenorrhoea, delaying menstruation.  Why D wrong: It reduces the risk of breast and ovarian cancer."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Lactational amenorrhoea (LAM) provides contraceptive benefit for how long if exclusive breastfeeding is carried out?","options":["3 months","6 months","1 year","2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: LAM provides amenorrhoea for 6 months if exclusive breastfeeding is carried out.  Why A wrong: 3 months is shorter than the stated period.  Why C/D wrong: These exceed the stated 6-month LAM period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Breastfeeding reduces the maternal risk of which cancers?","options":["Breast and ovarian cancer","Cervical and lung cancer","Colon and skin cancer","Thyroid and gastric cancer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Breastfeeding reduces the risk of breast and ovarian cancer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These cancers are not listed among the reduced risks in the text."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The MOST common indicator of adequacy of breastfeeding is:","options":["Audible swallowing during the feed","Average weight gain of 20 to 30 gm/day","Passing urine 6–8 times in 24 hours","Frequent soft bowel movements 3–8 times in 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An average weight gain of 20 to 30 gm/day is the most common indicator of adequate breastfeeding.  Why A wrong: Audible swallowing is an indicator but not identified as the most common.  Why C wrong: Passing urine 6–8 times/24 h is the second most common indicator.  Why D wrong: Soft bowel movements 3–8 times/24 h is an indicator but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The SECOND most common indicator of adequacy of breastfeeding is that the baby:","options":["Gains 20–30 gm/day","Passes urine 6–8 times in 24 hours","Has soft bowel movements 3–8 times in 24 hours","Sleeps well and does not cry frequently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Passing urine 6–8 times in 24 hours is the second most common indicator.  Why A wrong: Weight gain 20–30 gm/day is the most common indicator.  Why C wrong: Bowel movements 3–8 times/24 h is a general indicator, not the second most common.  Why D wrong: Good sleep is a supportive sign but not the second most common indicator."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A breastfed baby with adequate intake typically passes frequent soft bowel movements how many times in 24 hours?","options":["1–2 times","3–8 times","9–12 times","Once every 2 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frequent soft bowel movements occur 3–8 times in 24 hours as an indicator of adequate breastfeeding.  Why A/C/D wrong: These frequencies do not match the stated 3–8 times/24 h."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"How many calories are contained in 100 ml of breast milk (with cow's milk containing 67)?","options":["60 calories","66 calories","70 calories","88 calories"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 100 ml of breast milk contains 66 calories (cow's milk contains 67 calories).  Why A/C wrong: These do not match the stated 66 calories.  Why D wrong: 88 is the water percentage, not calories."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"In 100 ml of breast milk, the total protein is 1.2 gm. The correct breakdown is:","options":["Casein 0.4, lactoalbumin 0.3, lactoferrin 0.2","Casein 0.2, lactoalbumin 0.4, lactoferrin 0.6","Casein 1.0, lactoalbumin 0.1, lactoferrin 0.1","Casein 0.6, lactoalbumin 0.6, lactoferrin 0.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The protein (1.2 gm) breaks down into casein 0.4, lactoalbumin 0.3, and lactoferrin 0.2.  Why B/C/D wrong: These breakdowns do not match the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The fat content in 100 ml of breast milk is:","options":["1.2 gm","3.8 gm (polyunsaturated)","7 gm","66 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 100 ml of breast milk contains 3.8 gm of fat (polyunsaturated).  Why A wrong: 1.2 gm is the protein content.  Why C wrong: 7 gm is the lactose content.  Why D wrong: 66 is the calorie count, not fat in grams."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The lactose content in 100 ml of breast milk is:","options":["1.2 gm","3.8 gm","7 gm","8.6 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 100 ml of breast milk contains 7 gm of lactose.  Why A wrong: 1.2 gm is the protein content.  Why B wrong: 3.8 gm is the fat content.  Why D wrong: 8.6 is the colostrum protein percentage, not lactose."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The water content of breast milk is approximately:","options":["78%","88%","66%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast milk is 88% water, along with IgA immunoglobulin and other nutrients.  Why A/C/D wrong: These percentages do not match the stated 88% water content."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Since human milk does not contain iron, supplementary iron should be initiated from what age along with breastfeeding?","options":["Birth","2 months","4 months","6 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Human milk does not contain iron, so supplementary iron should be initiated from 4 months of age along with breastfeeding.  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier than the stated 4 months.  Why D wrong: 6 months is the exclusive breastfeeding endpoint/complementary feeding, but iron is stated to start at 4 months."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"In the percentage composition table, the protein content of colostrum versus breast milk is respectively:","options":["8.6 and 1.2","1.2 and 8.6","2.3 and 3.2","3.2 and 7.5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum has 8.6 protein and breast milk has 1.2 protein (percentage composition), showing colostrum is much higher in protein.  Why B wrong: This reverses the values.  Why C wrong: 2.3 and 3.2 are fat values.  Why D wrong: 3.2 and 7.5 are carbohydrate values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"According to the percentage composition table, the carbohydrate content of colostrum and breast milk is respectively:","options":["8.6 and 1.2","2.3 and 3.2","3.2 and 7.5","86 and 87"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Carbohydrate is 3.2 in colostrum and 7.5 in breast milk.  Why A wrong: 8.6 and 1.2 are protein values.  Why B wrong: 2.3 and 3.2 are fat values.  Why D wrong: 86 and 87 are water values."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The colour of colostrum is described as:","options":["Watery and bluish","Thick yellow and golden","Cream white","Clear and colourless"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum colour is thick yellow and golden.  Why A wrong: Watery/bluish describes mature or foremilk, not colostrum.  Why C wrong: Cream-like describes transitional milk.  Why D wrong: Colostrum is not colourless."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Colostrum is secreted for what duration following delivery?","options":["First 3–4 days","4–14 days","From 14 days onward","Only on day 1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum is secreted for the first 3–4 days following delivery.  Why B wrong: 4–14 days is transitional milk.  Why C wrong: From 14 days onward is mature milk.  Why D wrong: It lasts 3–4 days, not only day 1."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The volume of colostrum secreted is:","options":["30 ml on the first day, then 60 ml/day for the next 2 days","500 ml/day","600–700 ml/day","150 ml/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum volume is 30 ml the first day, then about 60 ml/day for the next 2 days.  Why B wrong: 500 ml/day is transitional milk.  Why C wrong: 600–700 ml/day is mature milk.  Why D wrong: 150 ml/kg is the preterm fluid requirement, not colostrum volume."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Colostrum is called the \"First Immunization\" of the baby because it is rich in:","options":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum is called the First Immunization (IgA) of the baby.  Why A wrong: IgG is transferred via the placenta in utero.  Why C wrong: IgM is fetal-produced active immunity.  Why D wrong: IgE is not the immunoglobulin the text credits for colostrum's \"first immunization.\""},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Colostrum helps the newborn pass the first stool (meconium) easily due to its:","options":["High fat content","Laxative effect","Antibiotic effect","Diuretic effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum's laxative effect makes it easy to pass the first stool (meconium).  Why A wrong: Colostrum actually contains less fat than mature milk.  Why C/D wrong: The stated mechanism is a laxative effect, not antibiotic or diuretic."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Transitional milk is secreted during which duration after delivery?","options":["First 3–4 days","After 4 days to 14 days","From 14 days onward","After 1 month"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transitional milk is secreted after 4 days to 14 days, following colostrum.  Why A wrong: The first 3–4 days is colostrum.  Why C wrong: From 14 days onward is mature milk.  Why D wrong: After 1 month is not the stated transitional milk period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Compared with colostrum, transitional milk has:","options":["Increased fat and carbohydrate, and decreased protein and IgA","Decreased fat and increased protein","No change in composition","Increased protein and IgA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transitional milk has increased fat and carbohydrate content and decreased protein and IgA content.  Why B wrong: Fat increases (not decreases) and protein decreases (not increases).  Why C wrong: The composition changes from colostrum.  Why D wrong: Protein and IgA decrease, not increase."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The average daily volume of transitional milk secreted is:","options":["60 ml/day","150 ml/day","500 ml/day","700 ml/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Transitional milk is secreted at an average of 500 ml/day.  Why A wrong: 60 ml/day is colostrum on days 2–3.  Why B wrong: 150 ml/kg is a fluid requirement figure.  Why D wrong: 600–700 ml/day is mature milk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Mature milk is usually secreted starting from:","options":["Day 1","4 days after delivery","14 days after delivery","1 month after delivery"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mature milk is secreted usually from 14 days after delivery.  Why A wrong: Day 1 is colostrum.  Why B wrong: 4 days marks the start of transitional milk.  Why D wrong: The stated onset is 14 days, not 1 month."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Mature milk is described as watery but containing all nutrients, and is secreted at approximately:","options":["30 ml/day","500 ml/day","600–700 ml/day","1000 ml/day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Mature milk is secreted at 600–700 ml/day and contains all nutrients for optimal growth.  Why A wrong: 30 ml is colostrum's first-day volume.  Why B wrong: 500 ml/day is transitional milk.  Why D wrong: 1000 ml/day exceeds the stated 600–700 ml/day."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which statement about foremilk is correct? (DSSSB-2019)","options":["It is secreted at the end of a feed and is rich in fat","It is secreted at the start of the feed, is more watery, and contains more proteins, sugar, vitamins and minerals","It is the same as colostrum","It is secreted only after 14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Foremilk is secreted at the start of regular breastfeeding, is more watery to satisfy the baby's thirst, and contains more proteins, sugar, vitamins and minerals.  Why A wrong: Fat-rich milk at the end of a feed is hind milk.  Why C wrong: Foremilk is not the same as colostrum.  Why D wrong: Foremilk relates to the timing within a feed, not a 14-day threshold."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Hind milk, secreted at the end of a regular breastfeed, is characterized by:","options":["More fat and energy","More water content","More antibodies than colostrum","Higher lactose only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hind milk is secreted at the end of regular breastfeeding and contains more fat and energy.  Why B wrong: Higher water content describes foremilk.  Why C wrong: Colostrum is the highest in antibodies; that is not hind milk's defining feature.  Why D wrong: Its defining feature is fat and energy, not lactose alone."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"To provide both foremilk and hind milk, a mother should:","options":["Switch breasts every 2 minutes","Allow one breast to empty completely before offering the other breast","Offer only foremilk","Feed only from one breast permanently"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mother should feed allowing one breast to empty (providing both fore and hind milk) before offering the other breast.  Why A wrong: Frequent switching prevents the baby from reaching hind milk.  Why C wrong: Providing only foremilk deprives the baby of the fat-rich hind milk.  Why D wrong: One breast alone permanently is not advised; alternate after emptying."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following are maternal contraindications to breastfeeding? Select all that apply.","options":["Active tuberculosis (TB)","HIV positive status","Radiotherapy or lithium therapy","Mild iron-deficiency anaemia","Opioid addiction"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Maternal contraindications include TB, acute contagious diseases, HIV positive, Hepatitis-B/HBsAg, cancer, radiotherapy, ergot therapy, lithium therapy and opioid addiction.  Why A correct: TB is listed.  Why B correct: HIV positive is listed.  Why C correct: Radiotherapy and lithium therapy are listed.  Why D wrong: Mild iron-deficiency anaemia is not listed as a contraindication.  Why E correct: Opioid addiction is specifically listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which infant conditions are contraindications to breastfeeding? Select all that apply.","options":["Cleft lip or palate that prevents grasping the nipple","Inadequate sucking force (prematurity, cardiac anomalies)","Phenylketonuria (PKU)","Physiological jaundice","Lactose intolerance"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Infant contraindications include conditions interfering with grasping the nipple (cleft lip/palate), inadequate sucking force (prematurity, cardiac anomalies), and inborn errors of metabolism (PKU, lactose intolerance).  Why A correct: Cleft lip/palate prevents nipple grasping.  Why B correct: Prematurity and cardiac anomalies cause inadequate sucking force.  Why C correct: PKU is an inborn error of metabolism listed.  Why D wrong: Physiological jaundice is not a contraindication in this list.  Why E correct: Lactose intolerance is specifically listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"For breast hygiene during breastfeeding, the nurse should teach the mother to:","options":["Clean the breast with soap and alcohol twice daily","Clean the breast with plain water once daily and avoid soap and alcohol","Never clean the breast","Use antiseptic scrub before each feed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clean the breast with plain water once daily; soap and alcohol should be avoided because they cause irritation and dryness.  Why A wrong: Soap and alcohol are specifically discouraged.  Why C wrong: The breast should be cleaned (with plain water), not left uncleaned.  Why D wrong: Antiseptic scrub is not recommended and would cause irritation/dryness."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Why should plastic bra liners be avoided by a breastfeeding mother?","options":["They increase heat and perspiration","They reduce milk supply","They cause allergy to the baby","They lower breast temperature too much"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Plastic bra liners should be avoided because they increase heat and perspiration.  Why B wrong: Reduced milk supply is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Baby allergy is not the reason given.  Why D wrong: They increase (not lower) heat."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"For a mother with breast engorgement, which measure is recommended? (JIPMER-2022)","options":["Stop breastfeeding until the swelling resolves","Take warm showers, apply cold packs between feedings, and put the infant to breast more frequently","Apply heat continuously and reduce feeding","Bind the breasts tightly and avoid feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For engorgement, take warm showers, apply cold packs between feedings, and put the infant to breast more frequently (JIPMER-2022).  Why A wrong: Stopping breastfeeding worsens engorgement; more frequent feeding is advised.  Why C wrong: Continuous heat with reduced feeding is not recommended; cold packs are used between feeds.  Why D wrong: Binding breasts and avoiding feeds is discouraged."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Expressed breast milk (EBM) stored at room temperature (25°C or 77°F) can be kept for:","options":["6 to 8 hours","Up to 24 hours","Up to 2 weeks","6 to 12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: EBM in a room at 25°C or 77°F can be stored for 6 to 8 hours.  Why B wrong: Up to 24 hours applies to a refrigerator (4°C).  Why C wrong: Up to 2 weeks applies to a freezer compartment inside the refrigerator (-15°C).  Why D wrong: 6–12 months applies to a defrost deep freezer (-20°C)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Expressed breast milk stored in a refrigerator (4°C or 39°F) can be kept for:","options":["6 to 8 hours","Up to 24 hours","Up to 2 weeks","4 to 6 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: EBM in a refrigerator at 4°C or 39°F can be stored up to 24 hours.  Why A wrong: 6–8 hours applies to room temperature.  Why C wrong: 2 weeks applies to the freezer compartment (-15°C).  Why D wrong: 4–6 months applies to a combined refrigerator/freezer with separate doors (-18°C)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Expressed breast milk stored in the freezer compartment inside the refrigerator (-15°C or 5°F) can be kept for:","options":["6 to 8 hours","Up to 24 hours","Up to 2 weeks","6 to 12 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: EBM in a freezer compartment inside the refrigerator at -15°C or 5°F can be stored up to 2 weeks.  Why A/B wrong: These are the room and refrigerator times, respectively.  Why D wrong: 6–12 months applies to a defrost deep freezer (-20°C)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Expressed breast milk stored in a combined refrigerator and freezer with separate doors (-18°C or 0°F) can be kept for:","options":["Up to 24 hours","Up to 2 weeks","Up to 4 to 6 months","6 to 12 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: EBM in a combined refrigerator/freezer with separate doors at -18°C or 0°F can be stored up to 4 to 6 months.  Why A wrong: 24 hours applies to a refrigerator (4°C).  Why B wrong: 2 weeks applies to the freezer compartment (-15°C).  Why D wrong: 6–12 months applies to a defrost deep freezer (-20°C)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Expressed breast milk stored in a defrost deep freezer (-20°C or -4°F) can be kept for:","options":["Up to 2 weeks","Up to 4 to 6 months","6 to 12 months","Indefinitely"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: EBM in a defrost deep freezer at -20°C or -4°F can be stored 6 to 12 months.  Why A wrong: 2 weeks applies to the freezer compartment (-15°C).  Why B wrong: 4–6 months applies to the combined fridge/freezer (-18°C).  Why D wrong: The stated maximum is 6–12 months, not indefinite."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which is one of the four key points in proper positioning for breastfeeding?","options":["The baby's head and body should be straight","The baby should face away from the breast","The baby's body should be kept far from the mother","Only the head should be supported"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four key points include: the baby's head and body should be straight; face should face the mother's breast; body should be close to her body; and she should support the baby's whole body.  Why B wrong: The baby's face should face the mother's breast, not away.  Why C wrong: The baby's body should be close to, not far from, the mother.  Why D wrong: The mother should support the baby's whole body, not only the head."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following are signs of good attachment during breastfeeding? Select all that apply. (ESIC-2024)","options":["More areola visible above the baby's mouth","The baby's mouth is wide open","The baby's lower lip is turned outwards","The baby's lower lip is turned inwards","The baby's chin is touching the breast"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The four key signs of good attachment are more areola visible above the mouth, mouth wide open, lower lip turned outwards, and chin touching the breast (ESIC-2024).  Why A correct: More areola above the mouth is a key sign.  Why B correct: A wide-open mouth is a key sign.  Why C correct: The lower lip turned outwards is a key sign.  Why D wrong: A lower lip turned inwards is a sign of poor latch-on, not good attachment.  Why E correct: The chin touching the breast is a key sign."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which set of findings indicates a GOOD latch-on rather than a poor latch-on?","options":["Lips turned outward, mouth wide, chin touching breast","Lips turned inward, mouth around nipple, chin not touching breast","Lips turned inward, mouth wide, chin not touching breast","Lips turned outward, mouth around nipple, chin touching breast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Good latch-on shows lips turned outward, mouth wide, and chin touching the breast.  Why B wrong: Lips turned inward, mouth around the nipple, and chin not touching describe poor latch-on.  Why C/D wrong: These mix good and poor latch-on features; the correct good latch-on set is in option A."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"During feeding, burping is done primarily to:","options":["Increase milk supply","Push out swallowed air and prevent regurgitation, vomiting, abdominal discomfort and colic","Stimulate the let-down reflex","Warm the milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Burping pushes out swallowed air and prevents regurgitation, vomiting, abdominal discomfort and colic.  Why A wrong: Burping does not increase milk supply.  Why C wrong: Let-down reflex is stimulated by oxytocin/suckling, not burping.  Why D wrong: Burping does not warm the milk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Complementary (supplementary) foods can be started at what age?","options":["Birth","2 months","4 to 6 months","1 year"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Complementary foods can be started at 4 to 6 months (supplementary iron can start at 4 months because breast milk lacks adequate iron).  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier than the stated range.  Why D wrong: 1 year is later than the stated 4–6 months."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The lactoalbumin-to-casein ratio of human milk is:","options":["60:40","20:80","40:60","80:20"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The lactoalbumin-to-casein ratio of human milk is 60:40 (whey dominant).  Why B wrong: 20:80 is the ratio for cow's milk.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated human milk ratio."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The lactoalbumin-to-casein ratio of cow's milk is:","options":["60:40","20:80","50:50","70:30"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The lactoalbumin-to-casein ratio of cow's milk is 20:80 (casein dominant).  Why A wrong: 60:40 is the human milk ratio.  Why C/D wrong: These do not match the stated cow's milk ratio."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The principal protein of human milk is lactoalbumin, which is also known as:","options":["Casein","Whey protein","Lactoferrin","Globulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principal protein of human milk is lactoalbumin, also known as whey protein.  Why A wrong: Casein is the principal protein of cow's milk.  Why C wrong: Lactoferrin is a separate protein component.  Why D wrong: Globulin is not the stated alternate name."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The principal protein of cow's milk is:","options":["Lactoalbumin","Casein","Whey protein","Lactoferrin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principal protein of cow's milk is casein.  Why A/C wrong: Lactoalbumin (whey) is the principal protein of human milk.  Why D wrong: Lactoferrin is not the principal protein of cow's milk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The principal carbohydrate of human milk is:","options":["Sucrose","Lactose","Glucose","Maltose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The principal carbohydrate of human milk is lactose.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sucrose, glucose and maltose are not the principal carbohydrate of human milk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The principal fat/fatty acids of human milk are:","options":["Palmitic and oleic acid","Stearic and linoleic acid","Butyric and caproic acid","Arachidonic acid only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The principal fat/fatty acids of human milk are palmitic and oleic acid.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the principal fatty acids identified for human milk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following are the principal electrolytes of human milk? Select all that apply.","options":["Sodium","Calcium","Phosphorus","Potassium","Magnesium"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The principal electrolytes of human milk are sodium, calcium, phosphorus, potassium and magnesium.  Why A correct: Sodium is listed.  Why B correct: Calcium is listed.  Why C correct: Phosphorus is listed.  Why D correct: Potassium is listed.  Why E correct: Magnesium is listed."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A galactagogue is best defined as:","options":["A food or drug that promotes or increases the flow of a mother's milk","A drug that suppresses lactation","A vitamin supplement for infants","A type of infant formula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A galactagogue is a food or drug that promotes or increases the flow of a mother's milk.  Why B wrong: It increases, not suppresses, lactation.  Why C/D wrong: It is not an infant vitamin or a formula."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following are examples of galactagogues? Select all that apply.","options":["Domperidone","Metoclopramide","Oxytocin","Furosemide","Warfarin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Synthetic galactagogues include domperidone and metoclopramide, and a natural galactagogue is oxytocin (which interacts with the dopamine system to increase prolactin production).  Why A correct: Domperidone is a synthetic galactagogue.  Why B correct: Metoclopramide is a synthetic galactagogue.  Why C correct: Oxytocin is the natural galactagogue listed.  Why D wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic, not a galactagogue.  Why E wrong: Warfarin is an anticoagulant, not a galactagogue."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Weaning is best defined as:","options":["Abrupt cessation of all feeding","The process of gradual and progressive transfer of the baby from breastfeeding to the usual family diet","Exclusive formula feeding from birth","Feeding only cow's milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Weaning is the gradual and progressive transfer of the baby from breastfeeding to the usual family diet.  Why A wrong: It is gradual, not abrupt.  Why C/D wrong: Weaning is a transition to the family diet, not exclusive formula or cow's milk."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Weaning can be started at what age, with breastfeeding continued up to 2 years?","options":["3 months","4 months","6 months","12 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Weaning can be started at 6 months of age, while breastfeeding continues up to 2 years.  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier than the stated weaning start of 6 months.  Why D wrong: 12 months is later than the stated 6-month start."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following are recognized major feeding problems in infants? Select all that apply.","options":["Regurgitation and vomiting","Suckling and swallowing difficulties","Dehydration fever and excessive crying","Underfeeding and overfeeding","Improved weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Major feeding problems are regurgitation/vomiting, suckling/swallowing difficulties, dehydration fever with excessive crying, and underfeeding/overfeeding.  Why A correct: Regurgitation and vomiting is listed.  Why B correct: Suckling and swallowing difficulties are listed.  Why C correct: Dehydration fever and excessive crying are listed.  Why D correct: Underfeeding and overfeeding are listed.  Why E wrong: Improved weight gain is a positive outcome, not a feeding problem."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Artificial feeding means to feed the child other than with breast milk. The best substitute for human milk is: (JKD CHO-2019)","options":["Goat's milk","Whole cow's milk","Soy formula","Rice water"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The best substitute for human milk is whole cow's milk (JKD CHO-2019).  Why A/C/D wrong: Goat's milk, soy formula and rice water are not identified as the best substitute in the text."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When giving cow's milk to a young infant, it should be diluted up to what age to reduce the load on the kidneys?","options":["1 week","1 month","2 months","6 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cow's milk should be diluted up to 2 months of age to reduce the kidney load.  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier than the stated 2 months.  Why D wrong: 6 months is later than the stated 2-month dilution period."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The AFASS criteria used to determine whether a mother can safely use replacement/artificial feeding stand for: (UK CHO-2022)","options":["Acceptable, Feasible, Affordable, Sustainable and Safe","Available, Fresh, Adequate, Standard and Sterile","Accessible, Fast, Affordable, Safe and Simple","Acceptable, Fresh, Affordable, Sterile and Safe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AFASS stands for Acceptable, Feasible, Affordable, Sustainable and Safe (UK CHO-2022).  Why B/C/D wrong: These expansions do not match the correct AFASS acronym."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Baby Friendly Hospital Initiative (BFHI) was launched by WHO and UNICEF in which year, in response to which declaration?","options":["1991, in response to the Innocenti Declaration-1990","1978, in response to the Alma-Ata Declaration","1992, in response to the WHO Code","2000, in response to the Millennium Declaration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: BFHI was launched by WHO and UNICEF in 1991 in response to the Innocenti Declaration-1990.  Why B wrong: 1978 refers to KMC's introduction (and Alma-Ata), not BFHI.  Why C wrong: 1992 is when India adopted BFHI, not the global launch year.  Why D wrong: 2000/Millennium Declaration is unrelated to BFHI's launch."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"In which year was BFHI adopted in India?","options":["1990","1991","1992","1994"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In India, BFHI was adopted in 1992.  Why A wrong: 1990 is the Innocenti Declaration year.  Why B wrong: 1991 is the global launch year.  Why D wrong: 1994 is not the stated adoption year."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The Innocenti Declaration-1990 is a joint global initiative of WHO and UNICEF based on:","options":["The protection, promotion and support of breastfeeding","Eradication of polio","Universal immunization","Maternal mortality reduction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Innocenti Declaration-1990 is based on the protection, promotion and support of breastfeeding.  Why B/C/D wrong: Polio eradication, immunization and maternal mortality are not the stated focus of this declaration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"According to the \"Ten steps to successful breastfeeding,\" breastfeeding should be initiated within: (CRE SNO AIIMS-2023)","options":["30 minutes of birth","An hour of birth","4 hours of birth","24 hours of birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Step 4 of the Ten steps states to initiate breastfeeding within an hour of birth.  Why A wrong: The Ten steps specify within an hour, not 30 minutes.  Why C wrong: 4 hours is the caesarean-delivery initiation window, not this step.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is too late."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Which of the following are included in the \"Ten steps to successful breastfeeding\"? Select all that apply.","options":["Practice \"rooming in\"","Encourage breastfeeding on demand","Give no artificial teats or pacifiers","Give infants glucose water routinely","Establish breastfeeding support groups"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Ten steps include rooming in, breastfeeding on demand, no artificial teats or pacifiers, giving infants nothing but breast milk, and establishing breastfeeding support groups.  Why A correct: \"Rooming in\" is step 7.  Why B correct: Breastfeeding on demand is step 8.  Why C correct: No artificial teats or pacifiers is step 9.  Why D wrong: Giving glucose water contradicts \"give infants nothing but breast milk.\"  Why E correct: Establishing support groups is step 10."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"In the comparison of breast milk and cow's milk, the protein content and dominance are:","options":["Human milk 1–1.5% (whey dominant); cow milk 3.3% (casein dominant)","Human milk 3.3% (casein dominant); cow milk 1–1.5% (whey dominant)","Both 3.3% and casein dominant","Both 1–1.5% and whey dominant"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Human milk protein is 1–1.5% (whey dominant); cow milk protein is 3.3% (casein dominant).  Why B wrong: This reverses the values and dominance.  Why C/D wrong: The two milks differ in both amount and dominant protein."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"In the comparison of breast milk and cow's milk, the carbohydrate (lactose) content is:","options":["Human 6.5–7% lactose; cow 4.5% lactose","Human 4.5% lactose; cow 6.5–7% lactose","Both 4.5% lactose","Both 7% lactose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Human milk contains 6.5–7% lactose; cow milk contains 4.5% lactose.  Why B wrong: This reverses the values.  Why C/D wrong: The lactose content differs between the two milks."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Regarding fat, the comparison of breast milk and cow's milk states:","options":["Human milk is high in LCFs; cow milk is high in MCFAs","Human milk is high in MCFAs; cow milk is high in LCFs","Both are high in MCFAs","Neither contains fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Human milk is high in long-chain fatty acids (LCFs) while cow milk is high in medium-chain fatty acids (MCFAs).  Why B wrong: This reverses the fatty acid types.  Why C wrong: They differ; only cow milk is high in MCFAs.  Why D wrong: Both contain fat."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Regarding minerals, the comparison of human and cow milk indicates that:","options":["Iron is better absorbed from human milk; cow milk is low in iron and copper","Iron is better absorbed from cow milk","Both have equally absorbed iron","Human milk is low in iron and copper"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iron is better absorbed from human milk, whereas cow milk is low in iron and copper.  Why B wrong: Human milk, not cow milk, has better iron absorption.  Why C wrong: The absorption differs between the two.  Why D wrong: It is cow milk that is low in iron and copper."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"According to the human vs cow milk comparison, human milk is low in which vitamin, while cow milk is low in vitamins C and D?","options":["Vitamin K","Vitamin A","Vitamin B12","Vitamin E"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Human milk is diet dependent and low in vitamin K, while cow milk is low in vitamin C and vitamin D.  Why B/C/D wrong: The vitamin the comparison flags as low in human milk is vitamin K, not A, B12 or E."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) is best described as:","options":["A special way of caring for low birth weight (LBW) babies by skin-to-skin contact","A method of tube feeding preterm babies","Incubator-based thermal care","A vaccination schedule for newborns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: KMC is a special way of caring for low birth weight (LBW) babies by skin-to-skin contact.  Why B wrong: KMC is skin-to-skin care, not tube feeding.  Why C wrong: It is an alternative/adjunct to incubator care, using the mother's body for warmth.  Why D wrong: KMC is not a vaccination schedule."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"KMC promotes newborn health and well-being through which mechanisms? Select all that apply.","options":["Effective thermal control","Breastfeeding","Bonding","Routine antibiotic administration","Continuous incubator isolation"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: KMC promotes health and well-being by effective thermal control, breastfeeding and bonding.  Why A correct: Thermal control is a core benefit.  Why B correct: Breastfeeding (exclusive) is part of KMC.  Why C correct: Bonding between mother and baby is promoted.  Why D wrong: Routine antibiotics are not part of KMC's described mechanisms.  Why E wrong: KMC uses skin-to-skin contact, the opposite of incubator isolation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"The KMC method was introduced by whom and in which year?","options":["Edgar Rey Sanabria in 1978","WHO and UNICEF in 1991","Innocenti in 1990","UNICEF in 1992"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: KMC was introduced by Edgar Rey Sanabria in 1978.  Why B wrong: WHO and UNICEF in 1991 refers to BFHI.  Why C wrong: 1990 refers to the Innocenti Declaration.  Why D wrong: 1992 is the year BFHI was adopted in India."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"During the initial stage of KMC, the baby should be monitored for which parameters? Select all that apply.","options":["Airway","Breathing","Colour","Temperature","Blood glucose only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: During the initial stage of KMC, the baby should be monitored for airway, breathing, colour and temperature.  Why A correct: Airway is monitored.  Why B correct: Breathing is monitored.  Why C correct: Colour is monitored.  Why D correct: Temperature is monitored.  Why E wrong: Blood glucose alone is not the monitoring set specified for KMC's initial stage."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"When can KMC be initiated?","options":["Only after 1 month of age","As soon as the baby is stable in the neonatal care unit","Only after discharge home","Only when the baby reaches 2.5 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: KMC can be started as soon as the baby is stable in the neonatal care unit; it is initiated at hospital and continued at home.  Why A wrong: It is not delayed to 1 month; it starts once the baby is stable.  Why C wrong: It starts in hospital, then continues at home.  Why D wrong: A specific 2.5 kg weight threshold is not the stated criterion; stability is."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A nurse notes that a healthy 2-month-old still has a strong positive Babinski response. How should the nurse interpret this finding?","options":["It is abnormal and requires urgent neurology referral","It is a normal finding for this age, as it may persist up to 2 years","It indicates cerebral palsy","It confirms a spinal cord injury"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A positive Babinski is normal in infants and the reflex may persist up to 2 years; only after 2 years does it always indicate neurological abnormality.  Why A wrong: At 2 months it is normal, so urgent referral is not warranted on this basis.  Why C/D wrong: In infancy a positive Babinski is a normal finding, not diagnostic of cerebral palsy or spinal cord injury."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A mother asks why colostrum should not be discarded. The nurse's BEST response is that colostrum:","options":["Is low in nutrients and only symbolic","Is the baby's first immunization (IgA), protective, and aids passage of meconium, so it should be given","Should be replaced with formula for the first 3 days","Is only useful after 14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colostrum is the First Immunization (IgA), is sufficient and protective, and its laxative effect aids meconium passage; it should not be discarded.  Why A wrong: Colostrum is rich in antibodies and protein, not merely symbolic.  Why C wrong: Colostrum should be given, not replaced by formula.  Why D wrong: Colostrum is most valuable in the first 3–4 days, not after 14 days."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A preterm neonate weighs 1.5 kg. Using the stated preterm fluid requirement, the approximate total daily fluid requirement is:","options":["150 ml","225 ml","300 ml","75 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Preterm babies require 150 ml/kg/day; 150 ml/kg x 1.5 kg = 225 ml/day.  Why A wrong: 150 ml would be the requirement for a 1 kg baby.  Why C wrong: 300 ml would require 2 kg at 150 ml/kg.  Why D wrong: 75 ml is half the per-kg rate and does not fit 1.5 kg."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Reflexes of the newborn with stimulus/response and age of disappearance…","question":"A breastfeeding mother is diagnosed HIV positive and is also on lithium therapy. Based on the contraindications, the nurse recognizes that:","options":["Breastfeeding is fully safe and encouraged","Both HIV positive status and lithium therapy are maternal contraindications to breastfeeding","Only HIV is a contraindication; lithium is safe","Only lithium is a contraindication; HIV is safe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Both HIV positive status and lithium therapy are listed maternal contraindications to breastfeeding.  Why A wrong: With these conditions, breastfeeding is contraindicated per the text.  Why C/D wrong: Both, not just one, are contraindications in the source list."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are the three components of Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC)?","options":["Skin-to-skin contact, exclusive breastfeeding, early discharge & follow-up","Radiant warming, formula feeding, prolonged hospitalization","Incubator care, mixed feeding, delayed discharge","Phototherapy, IV fluids, isolation nursing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists the three components of KMC as (1) skin-to-skin contact, (2) exclusive breastfeeding, and (3) early discharge & follow-up.  Why B wrong: Radiant warming and formula feeding contradict the skin-to-skin, breastfeeding basis of KMC.  Why C wrong: KMC promotes early discharge, not delayed discharge, and uses breastfeeding.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy and isolation are not KMC components."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"What are the two prerequisites of KMC?","options":["Support to mother in hospital and at home; post-discharge follow-up","Neonatal ICU admission; parenteral nutrition","Maternal sedation; continuous oxygen","Formula supplementation; incubator availability"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The two prerequisites listed are support to the mother in hospital and at home, and post-discharge follow-up.  Why B wrong: KMC does not require NICU admission or parenteral nutrition as a prerequisite.  Why C wrong: Maternal sedation is not a prerequisite for KMC.  Why D wrong: Formula and incubators are not prerequisites of KMC."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are benefits of Kangaroo Mother Care? (Select all that apply)","options":["Improved thermal control and reduced risk of hypothermia","Improved duration of breastfeeding","Reduced neonatal mortality rate","Increased morbidities such as apnoea and infection","Better mother-infant bonding and improved daily weight gain"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): KMC provides thermal control/less hypothermia, improves breastfeeding duration, reduces neonatal mortality, and improves bonding and daily weight gain.  Why D wrong: KMC produces LESS morbidity such as apnoea and infection, not increased morbidity."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Besides thermal control and reduced mortality, KMC also results in which of the following?","options":["Less stress for both baby and mother and early discharge from the hospital","Prolonged hospital stay","Reduced maternal satisfaction","Decreased weight gain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Benefits include less stress for baby and mother, and early discharge from hospital.  Why B wrong: KMC facilitates early, not prolonged, discharge.  Why C wrong: KMC improves maternal satisfaction.  Why D wrong: KMC improves daily weight gain."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the eligibility criteria for KMC, all stable low birth weight (LBW) babies with what weight are eligible?","options":["Weight < 2000 gm","Weight < 1000 gm","Weight < 3000 gm","Weight > 2500 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states all stable LBW babies with weight < 2000 gm are eligible for KMC.  Why B wrong: < 1000 gm is not the stated cut-off.  Why C wrong: < 3000 gm is too high a threshold.  Why D wrong: > 2500 gm babies are typically term/normal weight, not the LBW eligibility criterion given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"For a stable newborn, when can KMC be initiated?","options":["As soon as possible after birth","Only after 7 days of life","Only after reaching 2500 gm","Only after discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states for all stable newborns, KMC can be initiated as soon as possible after birth.  Why B wrong: There is no 7-day waiting rule for stable newborns.  Why C wrong: 2500 gm relates to discontinuation, not initiation timing.  Why D wrong: KMC is initiated before discharge, in the hospital."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A baby is receiving IV therapy, tube feeding, and O2 therapy. Regarding KMC, which statement is correct?","options":["KMC can still be initiated in these babies","KMC is absolutely contraindicated","KMC must be delayed until 6 months of age","KMC can only be done by a nurse, never a parent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states that if babies are on IV therapy, tube feeding, and O2 therapy, KMC can be initiated.  Why B wrong: It is not contraindicated; it can be initiated.  Why C wrong: No 6-month delay is stated.  Why D wrong: KMC is normally provided by the mother/family."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the eligibility criteria, which mothers are eligible to provide KMC?","options":["Mothers who are willing, free from any infection, and maintain good hygiene","Only mothers who delivered by cesarean section","Only first-time mothers","Only mothers of term babies"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: All mothers are eligible provided they are willing to provide KMC, free from any infection, and maintain good hygiene.  Why B wrong: Mode of delivery is not the eligibility criterion.  Why C wrong: Parity is not a stated criterion.  Why D wrong: KMC is especially for LBW/preterm babies, not restricted to term babies."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the duration classification of KMC, \"Start\" refers to how many hours daily?","options":["4 hrs daily","9–12 hrs daily","5–8 hrs daily","> 12 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book defines \"Start: 4 hrs daily.\"  Why B wrong: 9–12 hrs daily is \"Long.\"  Why C wrong: 5–8 hrs daily is \"Extended.\"  Why D wrong: > 12 hrs is \"Continuous.\""},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the KMC duration classification, \"Long\" duration is defined as:","options":["9–12 hrs daily","4 hrs daily","5–8 hrs daily","> 12 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Long: 9–12 hrs daily\" is the stated definition.  Why B wrong: 4 hrs daily is \"Start.\"  Why C wrong: 5–8 hrs daily is \"Extended.\"  Why D wrong: > 12 hrs is \"Continuous.\""},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the KMC duration classification, \"Extended\" and \"Continuous\" durations respectively are:","options":["5–8 hrs daily; > 12 hrs","4 hrs daily; 9–12 hrs","> 12 hrs; 5–8 hrs","9–12 hrs; 4 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Extended: 5 to 8 hrs daily\" and \"Continuous: > 12 hrs.\"  Why B wrong: 4 hrs is \"Start\" and 9–12 hrs is \"Long.\"  Why C wrong: The values are reversed.  Why D wrong: These correspond to Long and Start, not Extended/Continuous."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"To avoid frequent handling that may stress the baby, a single KMC session should not be less than:","options":["1 hour","15 minutes","30 minutes","4 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states KMC should not be less than 1 hour in a session to avoid frequent handling that may be stressful to the baby.  Why B wrong: 15 minutes is below the stated minimum.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes is below the stated minimum session length.  Why D wrong: 4 hours is the \"Start\" daily total, not the per-session minimum."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Gradually, the length of a KMC session should be increased up to:","options":["24 hours a day","6 hours a day","12 hours a day","2 hours a day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the length of KMC should gradually be increased up to 24 hours a day, with interruption only for changing diapers.  Why B wrong: 6 hours is the minimum initiation, not the gradual maximum.  Why C wrong: 12 hours is not the stated maximum target.  Why D wrong: 2 hours is far below the stated target."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the book (AIIMS, Raj, CHO 2020), a minimum of how many hours of KMC care should be initiated in a day?","options":["5 to 6 hours","1 to 2 hours","9 to 12 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states a minimum of 5 to 6 hours of KMC care should be initiated in a day (AIIMS, Raj, CHO 2020).  Why B wrong: 1–2 hours is below the stated minimum.  Why C wrong: 9–12 hours describes the \"Long\" duration, not the daily minimum.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is the gradual maximum, not the minimum."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"One discharge criterion for a KMC baby is a weight gain of:","options":["15 to 20 g/kg/day for at least 3 consecutive days","5 to 10 g/kg/day for 1 day","30 to 40 g/kg/day for 7 days","50 g/kg/day for 2 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The discharging criteria require gaining weight 15 to 20 g/kg/day for at least 3 consecutive days.  Why B wrong: These values and duration are below the stated criterion.  Why C wrong: 30–40 g/kg/day for 7 days is not the stated criterion.  Why D wrong: 50 g/kg/day for 2 days is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are discharge criteria for the KMC baby? (Select all that apply)","options":["Baby is stable and not on parenteral medication","General health good with no evidence of infection and apnoea","Accepting feeding exclusively with breast milk by spoon or paladai","Baby still requires IV antibiotics","Mother and family are able to care for the baby at home"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): These are all listed discharge criteria — stable/off parenteral meds, good health without infection/apnoea, exclusive breast-milk feeding by spoon/paladai, and family able to care at home.  Why D wrong: A baby still needing IV (parenteral) antibiotics is NOT stable enough; being off parenteral medication is a criterion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"KMC can be discontinued when the baby gains weight up to approximately what weight, or reaches what post-conception age?","options":["2500 g or 40 weeks of post-conception age","2000 g or 32 weeks","1500 g or 28 weeks","3500 g or 44 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states KMC can be continued while the baby gains weight up to 2500 g or 40 weeks of post-conception age (gestation reaching term).  Why B wrong: 2000 g/32 weeks is below the discontinuation target.  Why C wrong: 1500 g/28 weeks is well below term.  Why D wrong: 3500 g/44 weeks exceeds the stated criterion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"When the mother is not available to provide KMC, who else can provide it?","options":["Other family members such as father, grandfather, or aunty","Only trained NICU nurses","No one; KMC must stop","Only the treating physician"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states when the mother is not available, other family members such as father, grandfather, or aunty can provide KMC.  Why B wrong: KMC is not restricted to NICU nurses.  Why C wrong: KMC can continue via family members.  Why D wrong: The physician is not the designated provider of KMC."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the correct KMC position, the baby is placed:","options":["Between the mother's breasts in an upright position","Flat on the mother's abdomen facing down","In a supine position on the bed beside the mother","Over the mother's shoulder facing backward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: KMC positioning places the baby between the mother's breasts in an upright position.  Why B wrong: The baby is upright between the breasts, not prone on the abdomen.  Why C wrong: KMC requires skin-to-skin upright contact, not lying beside on the bed.  Why D wrong: The baby is placed on the chest between the breasts, not over the shoulder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In KMC positioning, the baby's head is turned to the side in a slightly extended position for which two reasons?","options":["To keep the airway open and to allow eye-to-eye contact between mother and baby","To prevent breastfeeding and reduce stimulation","To promote forward flexion of the neck","To help the baby sleep prone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Turning the head to the side in slight extension keeps the airway open and allows eye-to-eye contact between mother and baby.  Why B wrong: KMC promotes breastfeeding, not prevents it.  Why C wrong: Forward flexion of the neck is specifically avoided.  Why D wrong: KMC is upright, not prone sleeping."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"During KMC positioning, which neck positions must be AVOIDED?","options":["Forward flexion and hyperextension of the neck","Slight extension to the side","Midline head holding","Turning the head to one side"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states to avoid forward flexion and hyperextension of the neck.  Why B wrong: A slightly extended, side-turned position is the recommended position.  Why C/D wrong: Turning the head to the side is part of correct positioning, not something to avoid."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In KMC, the infant's limbs should be positioned and the binder placed such that:","options":["Legs and arms are in a flexed (frog-leg) position and the top of the binder is at the baby's ear","Limbs are fully extended and the binder covers the head","Arms are restrained straight at the sides and the binder is at the waist","The baby is left unwrapped for free movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The infant should be in a flexed position (legs & arms), secured with a binder/wrap, with the top of the binder at the baby's ear (frog-leg position).  Why B wrong: Limbs are flexed, not extended, and the binder should not cover the head.  Why C wrong: The flexed frog-leg posture is used, and the binder top is at the ear, not the waist.  Why D wrong: The baby is secured with a binder/wrap, not left unwrapped."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Phenylketonuria (PKU) testing in a newborn should be done:","options":["24–48 hours after the first feeding","Immediately at birth before any feeding","At 1 week of age regardless of feeding","Only after 1 month of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states PKU testing should be done 24–48 hours after first feeding.  Why B wrong: Testing is done after feeding, not before, since it depends on protein intake.  Why C wrong: The stated window is 24–48 hours after first feeding, not 1 week.  Why D wrong: 1 month is too late for the stated screening."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"If PKU testing is positive (excessive phenylketonuria), the newborn requires a special formula with which diet to prevent mental retardation?","options":["Low phenylalanine diet","High phenylalanine diet","High protein diet","Low carbohydrate diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A positive PKU test requires a special formula with a low phenylalanine diet to prevent mental retardation.  Why B wrong: A high phenylalanine diet would worsen PKU.  Why C wrong: Phenylalanine (an amino acid) is restricted; a generic high-protein diet increases phenylalanine.  Why D wrong: Carbohydrate restriction is not the intervention for PKU."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Formula-fed infants are typically fed on demand or about every:","options":["4 hours","1 hour","6 hours","8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states formula-fed infants are fed on demand or about every 4 hours.  Why B wrong: Every 1 hour is more frequent than stated.  Why C wrong: 6 hours is longer than the stated interval.  Why D wrong: 8 hours is far longer than stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Breastfed infants are fed on demand, approximately every:","options":["2 to 3 hours","4 to 5 hours","6 to 7 hours","8 to 9 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states breastfed infants are fed on demand, approximately every 2 to 3 hours.  Why B wrong: 4–5 hours is the longer formula-feeding interval region, not breastfeeding.  Why C/D wrong: These intervals are too long for breastfeeding."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which bone is most commonly fractured during birth and should be assessed during physical examination?","options":["Clavicle","Femur","Humerus","Skull"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the clavicle bone is most commonly fractured during birth and should be assessed during physical examination.  Why B wrong: Femur fracture is not the most common birth fracture.  Why C wrong: Humerus fractures can occur but the clavicle is the most common.  Why D wrong: The skull is not the most commonly fractured bone at birth per the book."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"When measuring heart rate in a newborn, which site and duration is best?","options":["Apical, for a full one minute","Radial, for 15 seconds","Carotid, for 30 seconds","Femoral, for 10 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the apical site for a full one minute is best for measuring heart rate in a newborn.  Why B wrong: Radial pulse for 15 seconds is not recommended for newborns.  Why C wrong: Carotid is used in adult CPR, not routine newborn HR.  Why D wrong: Femoral for 10 seconds is not the stated best method."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the book (AIIMS BBSR-2018), a period of apnoea in a newborn is considered when it lasts longer than:","options":["20 seconds","5 seconds","10 seconds","60 seconds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apnoea in a newborn is defined as a pause longer than 20 seconds (AIIMS BBSR-2018).  Why B wrong: 5 seconds is too brief to qualify.  Why C wrong: 10 seconds is below the stated threshold.  Why D wrong: 60 seconds exceeds the stated definition threshold of >20 seconds."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Normal (physiological) jaundice appears after the first ____ in a full-term newborn and after the first ____ in a preterm newborn.","options":["24 hours; 48 hours","48 hours; 24 hours","12 hours; 24 hours","72 hours; 96 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological jaundice appears after the first 24 hours in full-term newborns and after the first 48 hours in preterm newborns.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: 12/24 hours does not match the stated timing.  Why D wrong: 72/96 hours is later than stated."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are signs of dehydration to assess in a newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dry mucous membranes","Sunken eyeballs","Poor skin turgor","Bulging fontanel","Sunken fontanel"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The book lists dry mucous membranes, sunken eyeballs, poor skin turgor, and sunken fontanel as signs of dehydration.  Why D wrong: A BULGING fontanel suggests raised intracranial pressure, not dehydration; dehydration causes a SUNKEN fontanel."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Eye medications should be administered within how long after birth to prevent ophthalmia neonatorum?","options":["Within 1 hour after birth","Within 24 hours after birth","Within 12 hours after birth","Within 48 hours after birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states eye medications should be given within 1 hour after birth to prevent ophthalmia neonatorum.  Why B, C, D wrong: These delays exceed the stated 1-hour window for eye prophylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"After feeding, in which position should the newborn be placed, and why is the prone position contraindicated for sleep?","options":["Right side after feeding; prone position increases the chance of Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS)","Prone after feeding; it prevents aspiration","Left side after feeding; it improves digestion only","Supine with head lowered; it reduces reflux"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Position the newborn on the right side after feeding; side-lying is not recommended for sleep because it lets the baby roll to prone, and prone position is contraindicated as it increases the chance of SIDS.  Why B wrong: Prone is contraindicated because of SIDS risk, not recommended.  Why C wrong: The book specifies the right side, not left, after feeding.  Why D wrong: The book does not recommend a head-lowered supine position here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Cold stress in a newborn, if not prevented, can lead to:","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Hypercalcemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states that cold stress must be prevented, otherwise the baby will go into metabolic acidosis.  Why B wrong: Cold stress causes acidosis, not respiratory alkalosis.  Why C wrong: It causes acidosis, not alkalosis.  Why D wrong: Hypercalcemia is not the stated consequence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"An infant radiant warmer is also known as:","options":["Open care system","Closed incubator","Phototherapy unit","Bilibed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the radiant warmer is also called the open care system.  Why B wrong: A closed incubator is a separate device (insulated enclosure).  Why C wrong: Phototherapy treats jaundice, not thermal support.  Why D wrong: A bilibed is a phototherapy device."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In an infant radiant warmer, heat is produced by which element and reflected onto the baby by what?","options":["Overhead quartz heating element; parabolic reflector","Underneath coil; convex lens","Halogen lamp; flat mirror","Water bath; radiator fins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The warmer's overhead quartz heating element produces heat reflected by the parabolic reflector onto the baby on the bassinet.  Why B wrong: It is an overhead quartz element, not an underneath coil, and no convex lens is used.  Why C wrong: It uses a quartz element and parabolic reflector, not a halogen lamp/flat mirror.  Why D wrong: A water bath/radiator is not the mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"After a baby is discharged or every 7 days, the infant radiant warmer should be disinfected with which antiseptic solution?","options":["2% glutaraldehyde","10% povidone-iodine","70% ethanol only","0.5% chlorhexidine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the warmer should be disinfected after discharge or every 7 days with an antiseptic solution, e.g. 2% glutaraldehyde.  Why B, C, D wrong: The specified example agent is 2% glutaraldehyde, not povidone-iodine, ethanol, or chlorhexidine."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the servo mode of a radiant warmer, at what temperature is it typically set so that heater output adjusts automatically to keep the baby at the set temperature?","options":["36.5°C","34°C","38°C","40°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In servo mode, the temperature is set at 36.5°C and heater output adjusts automatically.  Why B, C wrong: 34°C and 38°C fall in the incubator ambient-air range, not the servo skin set-point.  Why D wrong: 40°C would be unsafe and is not the set temperature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In servo mode, when does the heater output become zero?","options":["When the baby is at the set temperature or higher","When the baby is below the set temperature","When the room is cold","When the alarm sounds due to low temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In servo mode, if the baby is at the set temperature or higher, the heater output becomes zero; if below, output increases.  Why B wrong: When below set temperature, output increases (not zero).  Why C wrong: A cold room does not by itself zero the output; it responds to the baby's temperature.  Why D wrong: The alarm relates to overheating, but the zeroing rule is based on the baby being at/above set temperature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"How often should the nurse look for probe displacement when the baby is in servo mode?","options":["Every hour","Every 4 hours","Every 12 hours","Once per shift"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states to look for probe displacement every hour when the baby is in servo mode.  Why B, C, D wrong: These intervals are longer than the stated hourly check."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which method is the preferred way of running the open care system (radiant warmer)?","options":["Servo mode","Manual mode","Alternating manual and off","Continuous 100% output"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the servo system is the preferred method of running the open care system.  Why B wrong: Manual mode is used only for specific situations (prewarming, resuscitation, initial stabilization).  Why C wrong: Alternating manual/off is not the preferred method.  Why D wrong: Continuous 100% output is the manual mode setting, not the preferred routine method."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In servo mode, a visual/audible alarm is activated whenever the baby's temperature rises by more than how much above the set temperature?","options":["0.5°C","1°C","2°C","0.1°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The alarm activates when the baby's temperature rises more than 0.5°C above the set temperature.  Why B, C wrong: 1°C and 2°C exceed the stated 0.5°C threshold.  Why D wrong: 0.1°C is below the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The servo-mode temperature alarm most often occurs because:","options":["The temperature probe comes off the baby's skin","The baby is deeply hypothermic","The room temperature is too high","The quartz rod has burned out"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the alarm often occurs when the temperature probe comes off the baby's skin, and the caregiver must sort out the fault.  Why B wrong: The alarm here is triggered by apparent overheating from a displaced probe, not hypothermia.  Why C wrong: Room temperature is not the stated cause of this specific alarm.  Why D wrong: A burned-out rod is not the stated common cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the manual mode of a radiant warmer, the heater output is set at:","options":["100% heater output","50% heater output","25% heater output","Automatic adjustment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In manual mode, the warmer runs with 100% heater output so that all items likely to contact the baby are warm.  Why B, C wrong: These partial outputs are not the manual-mode setting described.  Why D wrong: Automatic adjustment describes servo mode, not manual mode."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Manual mode of the radiant warmer should be used only for:","options":["Prewarming, during resuscitation, and initial stabilization","Long-term routine thermal care","Overnight monitoring of a stable baby","Phototherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states manual mode is used only for prewarming, during resuscitation, and initial stabilization.  Why B wrong: Long-term routine care should use the safer servo mode.  Why C wrong: A stable baby overnight would use servo mode.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy is unrelated to the warmer's manual mode."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A radiant warmer works best when the environmental (room) temperature is above:","options":["20°C","10°C","30°C","15°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the warmer works best when the environmental temperature is above 20°C, and should not be used in a cold room.  Why B, D wrong: 10°C and 15°C are below the stated minimum.  Why C wrong: 30°C is not the stated threshold value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"How long before the arrival of the baby should the radiant warmer be pre-warmed (or until the set temperature is reached with < 50% of total heater output)?","options":["About 20 minutes","About 5 minutes","About 60 minutes","About 2 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The warmer must be pre-warmed around 20 minutes before the baby's arrival or until the set temperature is reached with < 50% of total heater output.  Why B, D wrong: 5 and 2 minutes are too short.  Why C wrong: 60 minutes is longer than the stated pre-warm time."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"How frequently should the baby's temperature be assessed while using a radiant warmer?","options":["Preferably 2–4 hourly","Every 30 minutes","Every 8 hours","Once daily"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states to assess the baby's temperature regularly, preferably 2–4 hourly.  Why B wrong: Every 30 minutes is more frequent than stated (though hourly probe checks apply in servo mode).  Why C, D wrong: 8-hourly or daily is far too infrequent."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"An infant incubator is best described as:","options":["An insulated enclosure in which light, temperature, humidity, and oxygen level can be regulated for preterm/sick babies","An open bassinet with an overhead heater only","A phototherapy chamber","A resuscitation trolley"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The incubator is an insulated enclosure regulating light, temperature, humidity, and oxygen for preterm/sick (and sometimes term) babies.  Why B wrong: An open bassinet with an overhead heater describes a radiant warmer/open care system.  Why C wrong: A phototherapy chamber treats jaundice, not the incubator's function.  Why D wrong: A resuscitation trolley is not an insulated thermal enclosure."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"To maintain the baby's body temperature (36–37.2°C), the incubator must create an ambient air temperature of what, with what humidity?","options":["34–38°C ambient air with 40–80% humidity","30–32°C ambient air with 20–30% humidity","40–42°C ambient air with 90–100% humidity","25–28°C ambient air with 50–60% humidity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the incubator maintains baby body temperature 36–37.2°C by creating ambient air of 34–38°C with humidity of 40–80%.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ambient/humidity ranges do not match the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following functions does the infant incubator perform? (Select all that apply)","options":["Controls temperature and humidity","Can deliver additional oxygen","Protects against infections, diseases, and noise","Provides phototherapy for jaundice","Delivers chest compressions automatically"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The book lists that the incubator controls temperature/humidity, can deliver additional oxygen, and protects against infections, diseases, and noise.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy is a separate treatment, not an incubator function listed here.  Why E wrong: Incubators do not deliver chest compressions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"After preheating an incubator, how long should you wait until the temperature and humidity are stabilised?","options":["Half an hour","5 minutes","2 hours","10 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states to always preheat the incubator and wait for half an hour until temperature and humidity stabilise.  Why B, D wrong: 5 and 10 minutes are too short.  Why C wrong: 2 hours is longer than the stated half hour."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"How should a baby be dressed while inside an incubator, and how often should the temperature be recorded?","options":["Kept undressed apart from a nappy; temperature checked and recorded every hour","Fully clothed with a cap; temperature checked every 8 hours","Wrapped in multiple blankets; temperature checked once daily","Swaddled tightly; temperature not monitored"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states babies are kept in the incubator undressed apart from a nappy, and the temperature is checked and recorded every hour.  Why B wrong: Babies are undressed (except nappy), and temperature is hourly, not 8-hourly.  Why C, D wrong: Heavy wrapping/swaddling defeats incubator monitoring; temperature must still be monitored hourly."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"What is the default air temperature setting in the incubator?","options":["35°C","30°C","37°C","40°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the default air temperature in the incubator is 35°C.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated default of 35°C."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"For small babies, the incubator humidity setting is set at what value initially, and later reduced to what?","options":["70–80% initially, later 40%","40% initially, later 80%","20% initially, later 60%","90% initially, later 70%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The setting for humidity for small babies is 70–80% in the beginning, later 40%.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C, D wrong: These starting/ending humidity values do not match the book."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which type of water should be used in the incubator humidifier, and how often should it be changed?","options":["Distilled water only, drained and renewed every day","Tap water, changed weekly","Normal saline, changed every 3 days","Sterile dextrose, changed monthly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states to use distilled water only, drained and renewed every day; the humidifier reservoir must be cleaned and water changed daily.  Why B wrong: Tap water is not recommended, and daily (not weekly) change is required.  Why C, D wrong: Saline/dextrose are not used, and the change interval is daily."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The developmental milestone period termed \"infancy\" in the book covers which age group?","options":["First 12 months","First 3 months","First 24 months","First 6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states developmental milestones during infancy cover the age group of the first 12 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated first-12-months infancy period."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which reflex is noted at 1 month of age, appearing when the baby is fed?","options":["Extrusion (tongue thrust) reflex","Moro reflex","Babinski reflex","Rooting reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists the extrusion or tongue-thrust reflex at 1 month, which appears when feeding the baby.  Why B, C, D wrong: While these are newborn reflexes, the book specifically lists the extrusion/tongue-thrust reflex at 1 month here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following milestones occur at 2 to 3 months of infancy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Social smiles","Turns head side to side and follows objects","Neck control (head control)","Pincer grasp","Posterior fontanel closed"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): At 2–3 months the infant shows social smiles, turns head side to side, follows objects, achieves neck control, holds head in midline, and the posterior fontanel closes.  Why D wrong: Pincer grasp develops later, at 8–9 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the book, neck (head) control is achieved at what age?","options":["12 weeks or 3 months","6 months","1 month","9 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states neck control is achieved at 12 weeks or 3 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated 12 weeks/3 months for neck control."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Regarding neck control development, at 6 weeks the head is held up to horizontal, and at 12 weeks it is held:","options":["Above horizontal","Below horizontal","Fully upright at 90°","In hyperextension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states at 6 weeks the head is held up to horizontal and at 12 weeks above horizontal.  Why B wrong: At 12 weeks the head is above (not below) horizontal.  Why C wrong: The book uses \"above horizontal,\" not a specific 90° upright.  Why D wrong: Hyperextension is not described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what infancy age does the baby roll over for the first time (from abdomen to back)?","options":["4 to 5 months","1 month","8 to 9 months","10 to 12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states at 4 to 5 months the baby rolls over for the first time from abdomen to back.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 4–5 months for the first roll over."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are milestones at 4 to 5 months? (Select all that apply)","options":["Grasps objects and switches objects from hand to hand","Enjoys social interaction and begins to show memory","Recognises familiar objects and people","Rides a tricycle","Uses fingers to eat (at 5 to 6 months)"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): At 4–5 months the infant grasps and switches objects, enjoys social interaction with emerging memory, recognises familiar objects/people, and uses fingers to eat at 5–6 months.  Why D wrong: Riding a tricycle is a toddler milestone (end of 3 years), not 4–5 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Iron supplementation should be started from what age?","options":["4 to 6 months","Birth","12 months","2 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states iron supplementation should start from 4 to 6 months.  Why B wrong: Iron supplementation is not started at birth in this text.  Why C, D wrong: 12 months and 2 years are later than the stated start."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"By what age does an infant double its birth weight?","options":["5 to 6 months","2 to 3 months","10 to 12 months","4 to 5 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the infant doubles the birth weight by 5 to 6 months.  Why B wrong: 2–3 months is too early to double weight.  Why C wrong: The birth weight triples (not doubles) at about 10–12 months.  Why D wrong: The stated doubling age is 5–6 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"\"Creeps,\" a milestone at 6 to 7 months, is best defined as:","options":["Moving slowly with the abdomen closely to the ground","Walking with support","Standing without support","Rolling from back to abdomen only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book defines creeps as moving slowly with the abdomen closely to the ground.  Why B, C wrong: These are later locomotor milestones, not creeping.  Why D wrong: Rolling over is a separate milestone; creeping specifically involves abdominal movement along the ground."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Teething typically begins with which teeth?","options":["2 lower central incisors","2 upper central incisors","First molars","Canines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states teething begins with the first teeth: 2 lower central incisors.  Why B wrong: The lower (not upper) central incisors erupt first per the book.  Why C, D wrong: Molars and canines erupt later than the central incisors."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the book (BBSR AIIMS-2018), an infant begins to exhibit stranger anxiety around which period, and it usually appears at what age (from the Also Remember note)?","options":["During later infancy; usually at 6 months of age","At birth; at 1 month of age","At 2 years; at 24 months of age","Never in infancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the infant exhibits/begins stranger anxiety (BBSR AIIMS-2018) and, in the Also Remember note, that stranger anxiety usually appears at 6 months of age.  Why B wrong: Stranger anxiety is not present at birth/1 month.  Why C wrong: It appears around 6 months, not 24 months.  Why D wrong: It clearly occurs in infancy per the book."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the book, the first solid food is introduced at about what age, and finger foods at what age?","options":["First solid food at about 6 months; finger foods at 6 to 7 months","First solid food at 3 months; finger foods at 4 months","First solid food at 12 months; finger foods at 15 months","First solid food at birth; finger foods at 2 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: First solid food is introduced at about 6 months and finger foods at 6 to 7 months of age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated introduction times."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are milestones seen at 8 to 9 months? (Select all that apply)","options":["Sits steadily (unsupported)","Crawls and may go backward at first","Develops pincer grasp","Holds the bottle (at 9 months)","Walks independently without any support"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): At 8–9 months the infant sits steadily unsupported, crawls (may go backward at first, AMU 2016), develops pincer grasp, and holds the bottle at 9 months.  Why E wrong: Independent walking without support occurs later (about 10–12 months / 13–15 months), not at 8–9 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what age does the infant hold the bottle, according to the book?","options":["9 months","3 months","12 months","6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the infant holds the bottle at 9 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated 9 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which vocalisation is listed at 8 to 9 months?","options":["'mama, nana, dada, papa'","Tells a full story","Uses simple sentences","Vocabulary of 300 words"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At 8–9 months the infant vocalises 'mama, nana, dada, papa' and responds to own name.  Why B wrong: Telling a story is a 36-month milestone.  Why C wrong: Simple sentences appear at 24 months.  Why D wrong: A 300-word vocabulary is reached by 2 years (toddler)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"By 10 to 12 months, an infant's weight and height change how, relative to birth values?","options":["Weight triples birth weight; height increases by 50%","Weight doubles; height increases by 25%","Weight quadruples; height doubles","Weight is unchanged; height increases by 10%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At 10–12 months the birth weight triples and birth height increases by 50%.  Why B wrong: Weight doubles by 5–6 months, but triples by ~1 year.  Why C wrong: Height does not double in the first year.  Why D wrong: Both weight and height increase substantially."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At approximately 10 to 12 months, what happens to head and chest circumference?","options":["Head and chest circumference become equal","Head circumference greatly exceeds chest","Chest circumference greatly exceeds head","Both stop growing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book (FAQ) states head and chest circumference become equal around this age.  Why B wrong: Head exceeds chest earlier in infancy, but they equalize by ~1 year.  Why C wrong: Chest exceeds head later in childhood, not at this point of equalization.  Why D wrong: Growth continues; they simply become equal."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"By 10 to 12 months, the infant typically has how many teeth?","options":["6 to 8 teeth","20 teeth","2 teeth","12 teeth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states a total of 6 to 8 teeth by 10–12 months.  Why B wrong: 20 temporary teeth are complete at 36 months.  Why C wrong: 2 teeth is fewer than the stated 6–8.  Why D wrong: 12 teeth is not the stated count at this age."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which locomotor and self-help milestones appear at 10 to 12 months? (Select all that apply)","options":["Can change position from prone to sitting","Walks with help or while holding onto furniture","Sits down from a standing position without help","Eats from a spoon and drinks from a cup with help","Says simple sentences of 5–6 words"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): At 10–12 months the infant changes prone-to-sitting, walks with help/furniture, sits down from standing without help, and eats from spoon/drinks from cup with help.  Why E wrong: Simple sentences appear at 24 months, not in infancy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In toddlers, which develops first — bowel or bladder control — and by what age is fairly good control of both achieved?","options":["Bowel control develops before bladder control; both by age 3 years","Bladder control develops before bowel control; both by age 1 year","Both develop simultaneously by 6 months","Bladder control develops before bowel control; both by age 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states bowel control develops before bladder control, and by age 3 years the toddler achieves fairly good bowel and bladder control.  Why B wrong: Bowel control comes first, and full control is not by 1 year.  Why C wrong: They do not develop simultaneously at 6 months.  Why D wrong: Bowel comes first (not bladder), and the age is 3 years."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The characteristic type of play in a toddler (AIIMS-2017) is:","options":["Parallel play (or therapeutic play)","Cooperative/associative play","Competitive play","Solitary play only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists parallel play (or therapeutic play) as the toddler's play activity (AIIMS-2017).  Why B wrong: Cooperative/associative play is characteristic of pre-schoolers.  Why C wrong: Competitive play is characteristic of school-age children.  Why D wrong: Solitary play is characteristic of infants, not the toddler play type given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which safety instructions are given for toddlers? (Select all that apply)","options":["Never leave the toddler alone near a bathtub, pail of water, swimming pool, or any body of water","Keep all toxic products and medicines in a high area, locked","Allow the toddler to play unsupervised near water to build independence","Store medicines within the toddler's reach for convenience"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct (A, B): The book instructs never to leave the toddler alone near water and to keep toxic products/medicines in a high, locked area.  Why C wrong: Toddlers must never be left unsupervised near water — drowning risk.  Why D wrong: Medicines must be kept out of reach (high and locked), not within reach."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what age does a toddler typically say 'No' even while complying with a request?","options":["15 months","18 months","24 months","36 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the toddler says 'No' even while complying with a request at 15 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 15 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the toddler milestones section, the anterior fontanel closes at:","options":["18 months of age","6 months of age","12 months of age","36 months of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The toddler milestones list anterior fontanel closed at 18 months of age (the physical section notes 12–18 months; Also Remember notes ~1.5 years).  Why B, C wrong: 6 and 12 months are earlier than the milestone value given.  Why D wrong: 36 months is later than the stated 18 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what age can a toddler drink from a cup without help and begin to have temper tantrums?","options":["18 months","12 months","24 months","36 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists drinking from a cup without help at 18 months and beginning temper tantrums at 18 months of age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated 18 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A toddler can walk up and down stairs at what age?","options":["24 months (2 years)","12 months","15 months","36 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the toddler walks up and down stairs at 24 months or 2 years.  Why B, C wrong: 12 and 15 months are too early for this milestone.  Why D wrong: 36 months is later than the stated 24 months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what age does the child have a full set of 20 temporary teeth, and when should dental care begin?","options":["Full set of 20 teeth at 36 months; dental care begins between 1 and 2 years of age","Full set of 20 teeth at 12 months; dental care begins at 5 years","Full set of 20 teeth at 24 months; dental care begins at 4 years","Full set of 20 teeth at 6 years; dental care begins at 6 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states a full set of 20 temporary teeth at 36 months, with dental care beginning between 1 and 2 years of age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These teeth counts/ages or dental-care start ages do not match the book."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At 36 months, a toddler can do which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Stand on one foot alternatively","Copy horizontal or vertical lines","Say 'No' while complying","Drink from a cup without help","Ride a tricycle (at end of 3 years)"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, E): At 36 months the toddler stands on one foot alternatively, copies horizontal/vertical lines, and (at the end of 3 years) may ride a tricycle.  Why C wrong: Saying 'No' while complying is a 15-month milestone.  Why D wrong: Drinking from a cup without help is an 18-month milestone."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which is considered the most important integrative task for toddlers?","options":["Toilet training","Riding a tricycle","Learning to read","Competitive sports"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states toilet training is the most important integrative task for toddlers.  Why B wrong: Tricycle riding is a motor milestone, not the key integrative task.  Why C wrong: Reading is not a toddler integrative task described.  Why D wrong: Competitive sports pertain to school-age children."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Daytime bowel and bladder control usually occurs in the toddler after what age?","options":["After 2 years of age","After 6 months","After 4 years","After 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states daytime bowel and bladder control occurs usually after 2 years of age.  Why B wrong: 6 months is far too early.  Why C, D wrong: 4 and 5 years are later than the stated after-2-years point."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The toddler age group is defined as:","options":["1 to 3 years","Birth to 12 months","3 to 6 years","6 to 12 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book defines the toddler age group as 1 to 3 years.  Why B wrong: Birth to 12 months is infancy.  Why C wrong: 3 to 6 years is the pre-school age group.  Why D wrong: 6 to 12 years is the school-age group."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In toddlers, head circumference increases by how much per year, until what age?","options":["½ inch per year till 5 years of age","2 inches per year till 12 years","1 inch per month till 2 years","3 inches per year till 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states head circumference increases ½ inch per year till 5 years of age.  Why B wrong: 2 inches/year describes height growth in school-age children.  Why C wrong: 1 inch per month is not stated.  Why D wrong: 3 inches/year is the toddler height increase, not head circumference."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the toddler physical section, the average toddler weight is about ____ and the height increases by ____ per year (average height 34 inches at 2 years).","options":["22 to 27 pounds; 3 inches","40 pounds; 5 inches","10 to 15 pounds; 1 inch","30 to 35 pounds; 2 inches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the average toddler weight is about 22 to 27 pounds and height increases 3 inches per year (average 34 inches at age 2).  Why B wrong: 40 pounds is the pre-schooler weight at 5 years.  Why C, D wrong: These weights/height increases do not match the toddler values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The normal apical heart rate of a toddler is:","options":["80 to 120 beats/min","70 to 110 beats/min","120 to 160 beats/min","60 to 100 beats/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the toddler apical heart rate is 80 to 120 b/m.  Why B wrong: 70–110 b/m is the pre-schooler heart rate.  Why C wrong: 120–160 is more like a newborn/infant range.  Why D wrong: 60–100 is an adult-type range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The normal respiratory rate of a toddler is:","options":["20 to 30 breaths/min","16 to 22 breaths/min","40 to 60 breaths/min","12 to 16 breaths/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the toddler respiration rate is 20 to 30 b/m.  Why B wrong: 16–22 b/m is the pre-schooler respiratory rate.  Why C wrong: 40–60 is a newborn range.  Why D wrong: 12–16 is an adult range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The average blood pressure of a toddler is stated as:","options":["92/55 mm Hg","95/57 mm Hg","120/80 mm Hg","80/40 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the toddler blood pressure is average 92/55 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 95/57 mm Hg is the pre-schooler blood pressure.  Why C wrong: 120/80 is an adult value.  Why D wrong: 80/40 is not the stated toddler value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The normal axillary temperature range for a toddler (and pre-schooler) is:","options":["97.5°F to 98.6°F","96.0°F to 97.0°F","99.0°F to 100.0°F","100.4°F to 101.0°F"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states axillary temperature 97.5°F to 98.6°F for the toddler (and the same range for the pre-schooler).  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated axillary values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A nurse counseling a parent about feeding a toddler should teach which principle?","options":["Avoid using food as a reward or punishment","Use candy as a reward for good behaviour","Withhold meals as punishment","Force-feed to ensure adequate intake"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book instructs to avoid using food as a reward or punishment.  Why B wrong: Using food as a reward is specifically discouraged.  Why C wrong: Withholding meals as punishment is discouraged.  Why D wrong: Force-feeding is not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"By age 2 years, a toddler begins to use short sentences and has a vocabulary of about:","options":["300 words","50 words","1000 words","100 words"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the toddler uses short sentences and has a vocabulary of about 300 words by age 2 years.  Why B, D wrong: 50 and 100 words are below the stated 300.  Why C wrong: 1000 words exceeds the stated 300."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The first stage of separation anxiety in a hospitalized toddler is known as \"Protest.\" Which behaviour characterises it?","options":["Prolonged loud crying, consoled by no one but parent/usual caregiver, and continually asking to go home","Cheerful, undiscriminating friendliness","Sleep disturbance, decreased appetite, and weight loss","Complete indifference to parents"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Protest stage is characterised by prolonged loud crying, being consoled only by parent/usual caregivers, and continually asking to go home.  Why B wrong: Cheerful undiscriminating friendliness is the Detachment/Denial (third) stage.  Why C wrong: Sleep/appetite changes and weight loss describe the Despair (second) stage.  Why D wrong: Indifference is more consistent with detachment, not protest."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The second stage of separation anxiety, known as \"Despair,\" is characterised by which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Alteration in sleep pattern","Decreased appetite and weight loss","Sad facial expression and unresponsiveness to stimuli","Cheerful, undiscriminating friendliness","No interest in play activities"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): Despair features altered sleep, decreased appetite/weight loss, sad facial expression, unresponsiveness to stimuli, and no interest in play.  Why D wrong: Cheerful, undiscriminating friendliness characterises the third stage (Detachment/Denial), not Despair."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The third stage of separation anxiety is known as Detachment or Denial and is characterised by:","options":["Cheerful and undiscriminating friendliness","Prolonged loud crying","Weight loss and sad facial expression","Continually asking to go home"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Detachment/Denial stage is characterised by cheerful and undiscriminating friendliness.  Why B, D wrong: These describe the Protest (first) stage.  Why C wrong: Weight loss/sad expression describes the Despair (second) stage."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which nursing interventions help minimize separation anxiety in a hospitalized toddler? (Select all that apply)","options":["Promote parent-child relationship by limiting separation","Ask parents to bring the child's favourite items from home","Encourage parents to stay with the child and be involved in care","Prohibit parental visits to enforce routine","Provide psychological support"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The book lists limiting separation, bringing favourite items, encouraging parents to stay/participate, and providing psychological support.  Why D wrong: Prohibiting parental visits worsens separation anxiety; the book encourages parental presence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The pre-school age group is defined as:","options":["3 to 6 years","1 to 3 years","6 to 12 years","Birth to 1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book defines pre-school children as 3 to 6 years of age.  Why B wrong: 1–3 years is the toddler group.  Why C wrong: 6–12 years is the school-age group.  Why D wrong: Birth to 1 year is infancy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A pre-school child grows approximately how much in height per year, and gains approximately how much weight per year (average weight 40 pounds at age 5)?","options":["2½ to 3 inches per year; about 5 pounds per year","5 inches per year; 10 pounds per year","1 inch per year; 2 pounds per year","3 inches per year; 22 pounds per year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pre-school child grows 2½ to 3 inches per year and gains about 5 pounds per year (average 40 pounds at 5 years).  Why B, C, D wrong: These growth/weight-gain figures do not match the stated pre-school values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The average height of a child at age 5 years is stated as:","options":["45 inches","34 inches","59 inches","40 inches"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states average height at age 5 years is 45 inches.  Why B wrong: 34 inches is the toddler height at age 2.  Why C wrong: 59 inches is the height at 12 years (school age).  Why D wrong: 40 inches is the pre-schooler weight in pounds, not the height."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A pre-schooler requires approximately how many hours of sleep per day?","options":["About 12 hours","About 8 hours","About 16 hours","About 6 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the pre-schooler requires about 12 hours of sleep per day.  Why B, D wrong: 8 and 6 hours are less than the stated 12.  Why C wrong: 16 hours exceeds the stated amount."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The apical heart rate of a pre-schooler child is:","options":["70 to 110 beats/min","80 to 120 beats/min","60 to 100 beats/min","120 to 160 beats/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the pre-schooler apical heart rate is 70 to 110 b/m.  Why B wrong: 80–120 b/m is the toddler heart rate.  Why C wrong: 60–100 is an adult-type range.  Why D wrong: 120–160 is an infant range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The respiratory rate and average blood pressure of a pre-schooler child are:","options":["16 to 22 breaths/min; 95/57 mm Hg","20 to 30 breaths/min; 92/55 mm Hg","12 to 16 breaths/min; 120/80 mm Hg","40 to 60 breaths/min; 80/40 mm Hg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states pre-schooler respirations 16 to 22 b/m and average BP 95/57 mm Hg.  Why B wrong: 20–30 b/m and 92/55 are toddler values.  Why C wrong: 12–16 b/m and 120/80 are adult values.  Why D wrong: 40–60 b/m is a newborn rate."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what age does the pre-school child achieve complete bowel and bladder control?","options":["At age 5 years","At age 2 years","At age 3 years","At age 6 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states at age 5 years the child has complete bowel and bladder control.  Why B wrong: Daytime control occurs after 2 years, but complete control is stated at 5 years.  Why C wrong: Fairly good control is by 3 years, but complete control is at 5 years.  Why D wrong: 6 years is later than the stated 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The characteristic play activity of a pre-schooler and their view of death are:","options":["Cooperative/associative play; 'death is temporary'","Parallel play; death is permanent","Competitive play; realistic concept of death","Solitary play; no concept of death"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pre-schooler's play is cooperative/associative, and they view death as temporary.  Why B wrong: Parallel play is the toddler's play type.  Why C wrong: Competitive play and realistic concept of death describe school-age children.  Why D wrong: Solitary play is not the pre-schooler play type described."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the important milestones list, height doubles at what age?","options":["4 years of age","2 years of age","1 year of age","5 years of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states height doubles at 4 years of age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated 4 years for height doubling."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"At what age does a child begin to talk continuously and ask many 'Why' questions, and at what age is colour recognition well established?","options":["'Why' questions at 4 years; colour recognition at 5 years","'Why' questions at 2 years; colour recognition at 3 years","'Why' questions at 5 years; colour recognition at 6 years","'Why' questions at 3 years; colour recognition at 4 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the child asks many 'Why' questions at 4 years and colour recognition is well established at 5 years of age.  Why B, C, D wrong: These pairings do not match the stated ages."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the Also Remember note, the total number of bones in a newborn is:","options":["280 to 300","206","650","350"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the total number of bones in a newborn is 280 to 300.  Why B wrong: 206 is the adult number of bones.  Why C wrong: 650 is the number of muscles in a newborn, not bones.  Why D wrong: 350 is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The number of muscles in a newborn is:","options":["650","206","300","500"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the number of muscles in a newborn is 650.  Why B wrong: 206 is the number of adult bones.  Why C wrong: 300 is near the newborn bone count, not muscles.  Why D wrong: 500 is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the \"timelines for closing of fontanel\" note, the anterior and posterior fontanels close at:","options":["Anterior at 1.5 years; posterior at 1.5 months","Anterior at 1.5 months; posterior at 1.5 years","Both at 6 months","Anterior at 3 years; posterior at 12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the anterior fontanel closes at 1.5 years and the posterior at 1.5 months.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: They do not both close at 6 months.  Why D wrong: These times do not match the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The most common cause of neonatal death is:","options":["Preterm birth/prematurity","Pneumonia","Diarrhoea","Birth trauma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the most common cause of neonatal death is preterm/prematurity.  Why B wrong: Pneumonia is the most common cause of under-5 mortality (U5MR), not neonatal death.  Why C, D wrong: Diarrhoea and birth trauma are not the stated most common cause of neonatal death."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The most common cause of under-5 mortality rate (U5MR) is:","options":["Pneumonia","Prematurity","Malaria","Congenital anomaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the most common cause of U5MR is pneumonia.  Why B wrong: Prematurity is the most common cause of neonatal death, not overall U5MR.  Why C, D wrong: Malaria and congenital anomalies are not the stated leading U5MR cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The Ballard scale (ESIC:2019) is used for estimating:","options":["Newborn gestational age / neuromuscular maturation","Level of consciousness","Pain intensity","Nutritional status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the Ballard scale is for estimating newborn gestational age/neuromuscular maturation (ESIC:2019).  Why B wrong: Consciousness is assessed by GCS-type scales, not Ballard.  Why C wrong: Pain scales are FLACC/Wong-Baker/NRS, not Ballard.  Why D wrong: Ballard estimates gestational age/maturity, not nutrition."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The temperature-regulatory (thermoregulation) centre in the newborn is located in the:","options":["Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata","Pituitary gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the thermoregulation centre in the newborn is located in the hypothalamus.  Why B wrong: The cerebellum controls coordination/balance.  Why C wrong: The medulla controls respiration/cardiac centres.  Why D wrong: The pituitary is an endocrine gland, not the thermoregulation centre."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In a newborn, maximum heat loss occurs through the:","options":["Skin","Lungs","Urine","Stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states maximum heat loss is done through the skin.  Why B wrong: The lungs cause some evaporative loss, but the skin is stated as the maximum route.  Why C, D wrong: Urine and stool are not the stated primary route of heat loss."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In neonatal resuscitation, the correct sequence of steps and the highest-priority step during neonatal CPR are:","options":["T-A-B-C (Temperature, Airway, Breathing, Circulation); Temperature is the higher priority","A-B-C (Airway, Breathing, Circulation); Airway is highest priority","C-A-B (Circulation, Airway, Breathing); Circulation is highest priority","T-P-R (Temperature, Pulse, Respiration); Pulse is highest priority"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book gives the neonatal resuscitation sequence as T (Temperature) - A (Airway) - B (Breathing) - C (Circulation), with Temperature being the higher priority during neonatal CPR.  Why B wrong: The neonatal sequence begins with Temperature (T), which precedes Airway.  Why C wrong: C-A-B is the adult BLS sequence, not the neonatal sequence described.  Why D wrong: TPR here refers to the assessment sequence in adults, not resuscitation steps."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The sequence of assessment of vital signs in a newborn (least intrusive to more intrusive) versus in an adult are respectively:","options":["RPT in newborn; TPR in adult","TPR in newborn; RPT in adult","PTR in newborn; RTP in adult","TPR in both"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the assessment sequence in a newborn is RPT (respiration, pulse, temperature — least to more intrusive) and in an adult is TPR.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C wrong: PTR/RTP are not the stated orders.  Why D wrong: The newborn uses RPT, not TPR."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Care of the umbilical cord stump after cutting can be done with 70% alcohol; why is normal saline (NS) avoided?","options":["70% alcohol is used for its volatile (drying) nature, while NS is avoided because of its moist nature","NS is toxic to the cord","70% alcohol keeps the cord moist","NS is more expensive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states 70% alcohol is used because of its volatile nature (promotes drying), and NS should be avoided because it is moist in nature (delays drying).  Why B wrong: NS is avoided for its moist nature, not toxicity.  Why C wrong: Alcohol is drying/volatile, not moisture-retaining.  Why D wrong: Cost is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Central cyanosis is seen in which condition, and peripheral cyanosis (acrocyanosis) is seen in which condition?","options":["Central cyanosis in Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF); peripheral cyanosis in hypothermia","Central cyanosis in hypothermia; peripheral cyanosis in TOF","Both in TOF only","Both in hypothermia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states central cyanosis is seen in TOF, and acrocyanosis/peripheral cyanosis is seen in hypothermia.  Why B wrong: The associations are reversed.  Why C, D wrong: The two types are linked to different conditions, not the same one."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The school-age child is defined as which age group?","options":["6 to 12 years","3 to 6 years","1 to 3 years","12 to 18 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states 6 to 12 years is considered the school-age child.  Why B wrong: 3–6 years is pre-school.  Why C wrong: 1–3 years is toddler.  Why D wrong: 12–18 years is adolescence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"During school age (6 to 12 years), growth in height is about how much per year, with height ranging from ____ at 6 years to ____ at 12 years?","options":["About 2 inches per year; 45 inches at 6 years to 59 inches at 12 years","About 5 inches per year; 34 inches at 6 years to 45 inches at 12 years","About ½ inch per year; 40 inches at 6 years to 50 inches at 12 years","About 3 inches per year; 30 inches at 6 years to 55 inches at 12 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states growth is about 2 inches per year, with height 45 inches at 6 years and 59 inches at 12 years.  Why B, C, D wrong: These growth rates/height ranges do not match the stated school-age values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the school-age child, which statements are correct? (Select all that apply)","options":["Girls usually grow faster than boys","The first permanent (secondary) teeth erupt at about age 6 years","Play activities are competitive (skilled and intellectual play)","They view death as temporary","They have a realistic concept of death as an adult"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The book states girls grow faster than boys, first permanent teeth erupt at 6 years, play is competitive, and the death concept is realistic (as in an adult).  Why D wrong: Viewing death as temporary is the pre-schooler's concept, not the school-age child's."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Adolescence is considered to be which age group?","options":["12 to 18 years","6 to 12 years","10 to 14 years","18 to 21 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the age group 12 to 18 years is considered adolescence.  Why B wrong: 6–12 years is the school-age group.  Why C wrong: 10–14 years is when female puberty growth spurts occur, not the full adolescence range.  Why D wrong: 18–21 years exceeds the stated adolescence range."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Puberty growth spurts in females occur at what age?","options":["10 to 14 years","6 to 10 years","14 to 18 years","8 to 9 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states puberty growth spurts in females occur at 10 to 14 years.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ranges do not match the stated 10–14 years."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In females, the first sign of puberty (IGNOU-2020) is:","options":["Thelarche (breast bud development)","Menarche (onset of menses)","Adrenarche (pubic hair)","Pubarche (genital skin pigmentation)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the first puberty sign in females is thelarche — breast buds, then breast development (IGNOU-2020).  Why B wrong: Menarche occurs about 2 years AFTER the first sign, so it is not first.  Why C wrong: Adrenarche (hair at pubic area, then axillary) follows thelarche.  Why D wrong: Pubarche (pigmentation of genital skin) is not the first sign."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Match the puberty term to its correct description. Which pairing is correct?","options":["Menarche = onset of menses, about 2 years after the first sign","Thelarche = pigmentation of genital skin","Adrenarche = breast bud development","Pubarche = onset of menses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states menarche is the onset of menses, occurring about 2 years after the first sign (thelarche).  Why B wrong: Thelarche = breast bud development, not genital pigmentation.  Why C wrong: Adrenarche = pubic then axillary hair, not breast buds.  Why D wrong: Pubarche = pigmentation of genital skin, not onset of menses."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In males, the first sign of puberty is:","options":["Enlargement of the testes (testicular enlargement)","Voice deepening","Growth of facial hair","Appearance of acne"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states in males the first puberty sign is enlargement of the testes/testicular enlargement.  Why B, C, D wrong: Voice change, facial hair, and acne occur later, not as the first sign."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the consolidated milestone timeline, at what age does a child hold the head (head holding) and reach out to and hold an object?","options":["Head holding at 3 months; reaches out and holds an object at 5 to 6 months","Head holding at 6 months; reaches object at 9 months","Head holding at 1 month; reaches object at 3 months","Head holding at 9 months; reaches object at 12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Also Remember timeline lists head holding at 3 months and reaching out to hold an object at 5–6 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated timeline values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the milestone timeline, transferring an object from one hand to another occurs at ____, and holding a small object between the index finger and thumb (pincer grasp) at ____.","options":["6 to 7 months; 9 months","3 months; 6 months","9 months; 12 months","12 months; 15 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists transferring objects hand-to-hand at 6–7 months and holding a small object between index finger and thumb at 9 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated timeline."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the milestone timeline, standing with support occurs at ____ and standing without support at ____.","options":["9 months; 10 to 12 months","3 months; 6 months","13 to 15 months; 18 months","6 months; 9 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists standing with support at 9 months and standing without support at 10–12 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated standing milestones."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the timeline, walking with support occurs at ____ and feeding self with a spoon at ____.","options":["13 to 15 months; 12 to 15 months","9 months; 6 months","18 months; 24 months","24 months; 36 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists walking with support at 13–15 months and feeding self with a spoon at 12–15 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated timeline values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the timeline, running begins at ____, riding tricycles at ____, and climbing upstairs at ____.","options":["18 months; 3 years; 20 to 24 months","12 months; 2 years; 15 months","24 months; 4 years; 30 months","9 months; 18 months; 12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists running at 18 months, riding tricycles at 3 years, and climbing upstairs at 20–24 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated timeline values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the timeline, a child says 'ba-ba, ma-ma, da-da' (bisyllable words) at what age, and says two words with meaning (mama, nana, papa) at what age?","options":["Bisyllable words at 9 months; two words with meaning at 12 months","Bisyllable words at 3 months; two words at 6 months","Bisyllable words at 12 months; two words at 24 months","Bisyllable words at 6 months; two words at 9 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists 'ba-ba, ma-ma, da-da' bisyllables at 9 months and mama/nana/papa (two words with meaning) at 12 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated language milestones."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the timeline, a child says simple sentences at ____ and tells a story at ____.","options":["24 months; 36 months","12 months; 18 months","36 months; 48 months","9 months; 12 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists simple sentences at 24 months and telling a story at 36 months.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated language milestones."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"In the dressing-skills timeline, taking shoes and socks off occurs at ____, putting shoes and socks on at ____, and dressing self fully at ____.","options":["15 to 18 months; 24 months; 3 to 4 years","24 months; 36 months; 5 years","12 months; 18 months; 2 years","3 years; 4 years; 5 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists taking shoes/socks off at 15–18 months, putting them on at 24 months, and dressing self fully at 3–4 years.  Why B, C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated dressing-skill milestones."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"According to the timeline, control of bowel and bladder in the daytime is achieved at ____, and at night at ____.","options":["2 years (daytime); 3 years (night time)","3 years (daytime); 2 years (night time)","1 year (daytime); 2 years (night time)","4 years (daytime); 5 years (night time)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The timeline lists control of bowel and bladder at daytime by 2 years and at night by 3 years.  Why B wrong: The values are reversed — daytime control comes first (2 years).  Why C, D wrong: These ages do not match the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"How does an infant typically respond to pain? (Select all that apply)","options":["Total body response — arms and legs may tremor","Facial expressions such as grimaces, surprise, frowns, and facial flinching","A tense and rigid cry","Increased blood pressure, heart rate, and respiration with decreased oxygen saturation","Verbally rating pain on a numerical scale"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The book describes the infant's pain response as total body response with limb tremor, grimaces/frowns/flinching, tense rigid cry, and increased BP/HR/RR with decreased O2 saturation.  Why E wrong: Infants cannot self-report; a numerical rating scale is used for older children/adolescents/adults, not infants."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which pain assessment scale is used in infants (and non-verbal small children who cannot self-report)?","options":["FLACC scale","Numerical rating scale (NRS)","Wong-Baker Faces scale (self-report)","Visual analogue slider operated by the child"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states pain is assessed in infants using the FLACC scale, a behavioral scale for nonverbal/small children unable to self-report.  Why B wrong: NRS is recommended for older children, adolescents, and adults.  Why C wrong: Wong-Baker is recommended for children age 3 or older who can self-report by choosing faces.  Why D wrong: An infant cannot operate a self-report slider."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The FLACC scale has a score range of ____, and the total number of criteria is ____ (Safdarjung:2019).","options":["0 to 10; 5 criteria (Face, Legs, Activity, Cry, Consolability)","0 to 5; 3 criteria","1 to 10; 4 criteria","0 to 20; 6 criteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FLACC scores range 0–10 (0 = no pain) and it has 5 criteria — Face, Legs, Activity, Cry, Consolability — each scored 0, 1, or 2 (Safdarjung:2019).  Why B wrong: The range is 0–10 with 5 criteria, not 0–5/3.  Why C wrong: FLACC has 5 (not 4) criteria and the range starts at 0.  Why D wrong: The maximum is 10 (5 criteria x 2), not 20, and there are 5 criteria."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"On the FLACC scale, which \"Face\" finding scores 2 (the highest)?","options":["Frequent to constant frown, clenched jaw, quivering chin","No particular expression or smile","Occasional grimace or frown, withdrawn, disinterested","Content and relaxed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Face, a score of 2 is a frequent-to-constant frown, clenched jaw, quivering chin.  Why B wrong: No particular expression/smile scores 0.  Why C wrong: Occasional grimace/frown, withdrawn scores 1.  Why D wrong: \"Content, relaxed\" is the Consolability score of 0, not the Face-2 descriptor."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"On the FLACC scale, the \"Legs\" parameter scores 1 when the legs are:","options":["Uneasy, restless, tense","Normal position or relaxed","Kicking or drawn up","Lying quietly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Legs, a score of 1 is \"uneasy, restless, tense.\"  Why B wrong: Normal position/relaxed legs score 0.  Why C wrong: Kicking or legs drawn up scores 2.  Why D wrong: \"Lying quietly\" is an Activity-0 descriptor, not Legs."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"On the FLACC scale, \"Activity\" is scored 2 when the child is:","options":["Arched, rigid, or jerking","Lying quietly, moves easily","Squirming, shifting back and forth","Content and relaxed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Activity, a score of 2 is \"arched, rigid, or jerking.\"  Why B wrong: Lying quietly/moving easily scores 0.  Why C wrong: Squirming/shifting scores 1.  Why D wrong: \"Content, relaxed\" belongs to Consolability-0."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"On the FLACC scale, the \"Cry\" parameter scores 2 when the child is:","options":["Crying steadily, screaming or sobbing, with frequent complaints","Not crying (awake or asleep)","Moaning or whimpering, occasional complaint","Content"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Cry, a score of 2 is crying steadily, screams or sobs, frequent complaints.  Why B wrong: No crying scores 0.  Why C wrong: Moans/whimpers, occasional complaint scores 1.  Why D wrong: \"Content\" refers to Consolability, not the Cry-2 descriptor."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"On the FLACC scale, the \"Consolability\" parameter scores 2 when the child is:","options":["Difficult to console or comfort","Content and relaxed","Reassured by occasional touching or hugging, distractible","Lying quietly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For Consolability, a score of 2 is \"difficult to console or comfort.\"  Why B wrong: Content/relaxed scores 0.  Why C wrong: Reassured by touching/hugging, distractible scores 1.  Why D wrong: \"Lying quietly\" is an Activity descriptor, not Consolability."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which behaviours indicate pain in a toddler? (Select all that apply)","options":["Generalised restlessness; guards or rubs the painful area","Loud crying, grunting, or breath-holding; uses words to describe pain","Facial expressions such as a furrowed brow, wrinkled forehead, closed eyes, or angry appearance","Sleep changes such as waking often or sleeping more/less than usual","Precisely rating pain 0–10 on a numerical scale"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Toddler pain behaviours include restlessness/guarding, loud crying/grunting/breath-holding with some words, furrowed brow/angry face, and sleep changes.  Why E wrong: A toddler cannot reliably self-rate on a 0–10 numerical scale; the behavioural (FLACC) scale is used."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Pain in a toddler is assessed using which scale?","options":["Behavioural scale such as the FLACC scale","Numerical rating scale","Adult verbal descriptor scale","Visual analogue scale only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states pain is assessed in toddlers using a behavioural scale such as FLACC.  Why B wrong: Numerical scales are for older children/adolescents/adults.  Why C, D wrong: Adult verbal or visual analogue scales are not used for toddlers per the book."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which behaviours are characteristic of pain in the pre-school-age child? (Select all that apply)","options":["Intense crying and ability to describe or locate pain","Regression to an earlier developmental stage, with withdrawal","May believe pain is punishment for bad behaviour","May hit or kick the caregiver when receiving care","Understands cause and effect of pain fully like an adult"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Pre-schoolers show intense crying with ability to locate pain, regression/withdrawal, belief that pain is punishment, and may hit/kick the caregiver.  Why E wrong: Understanding cause and effect of pain is an adolescent characteristic, not pre-school."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Pain in a pre-schooler is assessed using which scale(s)?","options":["The Face scale or Wong-Baker faces rating scale","FLACC only","Numerical rating scale only","Ballard scale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states pain in pre-schoolers is assessed using the Face scale or Wong-Baker faces rating scale.  Why B wrong: FLACC is for infants/toddlers/nonverbal children.  Why C wrong: The numerical rating scale is for older children/adolescents/adults.  Why D wrong: Ballard is a gestational-age scale, not a pain scale."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"On the Wong-Baker Face scale, a score of 0 and a score of 10 correspond to which descriptors?","options":["0 = No Hurt; 10 = Hurts Worst","0 = Hurts Little Bit; 10 = Hurts Even More","0 = Mild; 10 = Moderate","0 = Hurts Whole Lot; 10 = No Hurt"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Face scale figure labels 0 as \"No Hurt\" and 10 as \"Hurts Worst\" (worst pain imaginable).  Why B wrong: \"Hurts Little Bit\" is 2 and \"Hurts Even More\" is 6.  Why C wrong: Mild/Moderate are severity bands, not the 0/10 face labels.  Why D wrong: The scale runs from No Hurt (0) to Hurts Worst (10), not reversed."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"How is pain assessed in the school-age child?","options":["Numerical pain rating scale and Face scale","FLACC scale only","Ballard scale","Behavioural observation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states school-age children's pain is assessed by numerical pain rating scale and Face scale (they can describe pain).  Why B wrong: FLACC is for infants/toddlers/nonverbal children.  Why C wrong: Ballard is a gestational-age tool, not a pain scale.  Why D wrong: School-age children can self-report, so more than behavioural observation is used."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which behaviours characterise pain in the adolescent, and which scale is used to assess it? (Select all that apply for behaviours)","options":["Describes location and intensity of pain","Increased muscle tension, decreased activity, and withdrawal","Understands cause and effect and perceives pain at physical, emotional, and mental levels","Pain assessed by the numerical rating scale","Pain assessed by the FLACC behavioural scale"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Adolescents describe location/intensity, show increased muscle tension/decreased activity/withdrawal, understand cause-effect and perceive pain physically/emotionally/mentally, and are assessed with the numerical rating scale.  Why E wrong: FLACC is for infants/toddlers/nonverbal children, not adolescents."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Never shake an infant, because it risks causing a closed head injury known as (RRB:2020):","options":["Shaken Baby Syndrome","Sudden Infant Death Syndrome","Caput succedaneum","Cephalohematoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states never shake an infant due to the risk of a closed head injury known as Shaken Baby Syndrome (RRB:2020).  Why B wrong: SIDS is sudden unexplained death, not a shaking-induced head injury.  Why C, D wrong: Caput succedaneum and cephalohematoma are birth-related scalp/skull swellings, not shaking injuries."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Regarding toy safety, infants should NOT be given toys with strings/cords of what length or longer (or furry toys), because they can cause choking/airway blockage?","options":["18 cm or longer","5 cm or longer","50 cm or longer","2 cm or longer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states infants should not be given toys with strings/cords 18 cm or longer, or furry toys, because if the string/hair is pulled/falls out it can enter the lungs, causing choking and airway blockage.  Why B, C, D wrong: These lengths do not match the stated 18 cm threshold."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"For infants, toddlers, and children who put objects in their mouths, toys with small parts should be avoided, and toys should not be smaller than:","options":["The infant's fist (closed palm)","A grain of rice","An adult's thumbnail","A coin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states toys should not be smaller than the infant's fist (closed palm) to avoid choking hazards.  Why B, C, D wrong: The stated reference size is the infant's closed fist, not a grain of rice, thumbnail, or coin."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Match each pain scale to the recommended age group. Which pairing is correct?","options":["FLACC = infants, toddlers, and non-verbal children; Wong-Baker = children age 3 or older; Numerical rating scale = older children, adolescents, and adults","FLACC = adults; Wong-Baker = infants; Numerical = toddlers","Wong-Baker = infants only; FLACC = adolescents; Numerical = toddlers","Numerical rating scale = infants; FLACC = adults; Wong-Baker = newborns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book recommends FLACC for infants/toddlers/non-verbal children, Wong-Baker Faces for children age 3 or older, and the numerical rating scale for older children, adolescents, and adults.  Why B, C, D wrong: These pairings contradict the recommended age groups for each scale."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"A child restraint is best defined as:","options":["A device which limits or prevents freedom of movement","A drug that sedates the child","A monitoring device for vital signs","A feeding aid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book defines a child restraint as a device that limits or prevents freedom of movement.  Why B wrong: A restraint is a physical device, not a sedating drug.  Why C wrong: It is not a vital-signs monitor.  Why D wrong: It is not a feeding aid."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of a child restraint? (Select all that apply)","options":["To limit the child's movements during a procedure that may harm/injure the baby","To provide constant immobilization","To facilitate examination of the child or a procedure","To ensure the child's safety and comfort","To punish the child for non-cooperation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The book lists limiting movements during procedures, providing constant immobilization (and maximum human contact via hand restraints), facilitating examination/procedures, and ensuring the child's safety and comfort.  Why E wrong: Restraints are never used for punishment; they are used only when necessary for safety/procedures."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"Which principles govern the use of child restraints? (Select all that apply)","options":["Used only when necessary and never as a substitute for observation","Cannot be used on a continuous basis","The reason should be specified and explained to the child and parents","Any required knot should be tied to permit quick release","Restraints may be left indefinitely without checking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): Principles include using restraints only when necessary (never substituting for observation), not on a continuous basis, explaining the reason to child/parents, and tying knots that permit quick release. They must be checked frequently.  Why E wrong: Restraints must be checked frequently for side effects and are not left indefinitely."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Kangaroo Mother Care — components, prerequisites, benefits, eligibility…","question":"The \"mummy restraint\" is used for what purpose?","options":["As a temporary restriction of the child during examination of the head, neck, and face","As permanent immobilization of the whole body","To restrain only one limb for IV insertion","For long-term behavioural control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the mummy restraint is used as a temporary restriction of the child during examination of the head, neck, and face.  Why B wrong: It is temporary, not permanent, and restraints cannot be continuous.  Why C wrong: It restrains the body/limbs to expose the head/neck/face, not a single limb for IV access.  Why D wrong: Restraints are not for long-term behavioural control."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which type of pediatric restraint is specifically used as chest support and after any chest surgery?","options":["Elbow restraint","Jacket restraint","Abdominal restraint","Extremity restraint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The jacket restraint is used as chest support and after any chest surgery.  Why A wrong: Elbow restraint controls flexion of the elbow during IV fluid/infusion, not chest support.  Why C wrong: Abdominal restraint holds the child supine after abdominal surgery.  Why D wrong: Extremity restraint immobilizes a limb for dressing/cannulation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"A child is receiving IV fluids and keeps flexing the elbow, dislodging the cannula. Which restraint is most appropriate to control the flexion movement of the elbow during IV infusion?","options":["Mitten restraint","Crib with dome restraint","Elbow restraint","Jacket restraint"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The elbow restraint is used for control of the flexion movement of the elbow during IV fluid or infusion.  Why A wrong: Mitten/finger restraint is for nail biting/thumb sucking and to prevent manipulating equipment.  Why B wrong: Crib with dome restraint prevents climbing/falling from side rails of the cot.  Why D wrong: Jacket restraint provides chest support after chest surgery."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which restraint is used for immobilization of an extremity, for example during dressing and cannulation?","options":["Extremity restraint","Abdominal restraint","Jacket restraint","Mitten restraint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extremity restraint is used for immobilization of an extremity, e.g. dressing and cannulation.  Why B wrong: Abdominal restraint holds the child supine after abdominal surgery.  Why C wrong: Jacket restraint is chest support after chest surgery.  Why D wrong: Mitten restraint prevents nail biting/thumb sucking and equipment manipulation."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"After abdominal surgery, which restraint is used to hold the child in a supine position on the bed?","options":["Elbow restraint","Abdominal restraint","Extremity restraint","Crib with dome restraint"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abdominal restraint is used to hold the child in supine position on the bed and is used after abdominal surgery.  Why A wrong: Elbow restraint controls elbow flexion during IV/infusion.  Why C wrong: Extremity restraint immobilizes a limb for dressing/cannulation.  Why D wrong: Crib with dome restraint prevents climbing and falling from cot side rails."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"A crib with dome restraint is primarily used to:","options":["Provide chest support after surgery","Control elbow flexion during infusion","Prevent the child from climbing and falling from the side rails of the cot","Prevent thumb sucking"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Crib with dome restraint prevents the child from climbing and falling from the side rails of the cot.  Why A wrong: That is the jacket restraint's function.  Why B wrong: That is the elbow restraint's function.  Why D wrong: That is the mitten/finger restraint's function."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Mitten/finger restraints are indicated for children who are nail biting and thumb sucking and to prevent the child from doing which of the following?","options":["Rolling out of bed","Manipulating equipment or accessing areas that should be restricted","Flexing the elbow during IV infusion","Aspirating during feeds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mitten/finger restraints are used for nail-biting and thumb-sucking children and to prevent the child from manipulating equipment or accessing areas that should be restricted.  Why A wrong: Falling from the cot relates to crib-with-dome restraints.  Why C wrong: Elbow flexion control is the elbow restraint.  Why D wrong: Aspiration prevention is not the stated purpose of a mitten restraint."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Neonatal jaundice is also known by which of the following names?","options":["Icterus neonatorum / hyperbilirubinemia","Kernicterus","Erythroblastosis fetalis","Icterus gravis neonatorum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neonatal jaundice is also called hyperbilirubinemia or icterus neonatorum.  Why B wrong: Kernicterus is bilirubin-induced encephalopathy from very high bilirubin, not the general name for jaundice.  Why C wrong: Erythroblastosis fetalis is another name for Rh incompatibility haemolytic disease.  Why D wrong: Icterus gravis neonatorum is fetal/neonatal death from severe haemolytic jaundice or Rh incompatibility."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The enzyme uridine diphosphate glucuronosyltransferase (UGT), present in the liver, normally converts:","options":["Conjugated (water-soluble) bilirubin into unconjugated (fat-soluble) bilirubin","Unconjugated (fat-soluble) bilirubin into conjugated (water-soluble) bilirubin","Bilirubin into biliverdin","Haemoglobin directly into bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: UGT converts unconjugated bilirubin (fat soluble) into conjugated bilirubin (water soluble), which is then excreted via urine and faeces.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse of the enzyme's true action; the source text on p-0801 mistakenly states \"conjugated bilirubin is not converted into unconjugated,\" but the factually correct action (as the same page and p-0803 confirm) is unconjugated → conjugated.  Why C wrong: Conversion of biliverdin to bilirubin is by biliverdin reductase, not UGT.  Why D wrong: UGT does not act directly on haemoglobin."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In neonates, jaundice occurs primarily because of lack or inappropriate levels of the UGT enzyme, which results in accumulation of which type of bilirubin?","options":["Conjugated (water-soluble) bilirubin","Unconjugated (fat-soluble) bilirubin","Biliverdin","Urobilinogen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deficiency of UGT means unconjugated (fat-soluble) bilirubin is not converted to the conjugated form, so unconjugated bilirubin accumulates and causes jaundice.  Why A wrong: Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and is excreted; it does not accumulate from UGT deficiency.  Why C wrong: Biliverdin is an upstream pigment, not the bilirubin fraction that accumulates.  Why D wrong: Urobilinogen is a gut breakdown product, not the accumulating fraction in UGT deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Jaundice is best defined as:","options":["Blue discolouration of skin due to lack of oxygen","Yellowish discolouration of the skin and tissues due to increased bilirubin level","Pallor of skin due to anaemia","Grey discolouration due to chloramphenicol toxicity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Jaundice is yellowish discolouration of the skin and tissues due to increased bilirubin level.  Why A wrong: Blue discolouration from hypoxia is cyanosis / blue baby syndrome.  Why C wrong: Pallor from anaemia is not jaundice.  Why D wrong: Grey/ashen discolouration from chloramphenicol is grey baby syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"According to the risk factor data, what percentage of term newborns and preterm newborns respectively develop neonatal jaundice?","options":["40% term and 60% preterm","50% term and 70% preterm","60% term and 80% preterm","80% term and 60% preterm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The stated risk factor is 60% in term newborns and 80% in preterm newborns.  Why A wrong: 40%/60% are not the stated values.  Why B wrong: 50%/70% are not the stated values.  Why D wrong: The values are reversed; term is 60% and preterm is 80%."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The normal bilirubin level is:","options":["0.3–1.3 mg/dl","5–15 mg/dl","12–14 mg/dl",">20 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal bilirubin level is 0.3–1.3 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 5–15 mg/dl is the range in physiological jaundice.  Why C wrong: 12–14 mg/dl is a Kramer's zone value (up to ankle & wrist).  Why D wrong: >20 mg/dl indicates pathological jaundice / kernicterus risk."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"(AIIMS Nagpur 2020) In an adult, a bilirubin level above which value is considered jaundice?","options":["More than 1 mg/dl","More than 2 mg/dl","More than 5 mg/dl","More than 15 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: More than 2 mg/dl is considered jaundice in an adult (AIIMS Nagpur 2020).  Why A wrong: 1 mg/dl is within the normal range.  Why C wrong: More than 5 mg/dl is the threshold for jaundice in neonates.  Why D wrong: 15 mg/dl is far above the adult threshold."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In neonates, a bilirubin level above which value is considered jaundice?","options":["More than 2 mg/dl","More than 5 mg/dl","More than 10 mg/dl","More than 20 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: More than 5 mg/dl is considered jaundice in neonates.  Why A wrong: More than 2 mg/dl is the adult threshold.  Why C wrong: 10 mg/dl is the level at which phototherapy is discontinued, not the jaundice threshold.  Why D wrong: >20 mg/dl indicates pathological jaundice/kernicterus risk, well above the threshold."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The confirmatory diagnosis of jaundice is established by:","options":["Kramer's index","Ingram icterometer","Serum bilirubin level","Transcutaneous bilirubinometer"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The confirmatory diagnosis of jaundice is serum bilirubin level.  Why A wrong: Kramer's index is a visual estimate and is explicitly not confirmatory.  Why B wrong: Ingram icterometer only estimates the bilirubin value non-invasively.  Why D wrong: The transcutaneous bilirubinometer is a non-invasive estimation method, not confirmatory."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In the adult classification of jaundice, pre-hepatic jaundice occurs due to:","options":["Damage in the liver","Excessive RBC destruction with no liver response","Obstruction in the common bile duct","Defective uptake of bilirubin by the liver"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-hepatic jaundice occurs due to excessive RBC destruction, with no liver response.  Why A wrong: Damage in the liver defines hepatic jaundice.  Why C wrong: CBD obstruction defines post-hepatic/obstructive jaundice.  Why D wrong: Defective hepatic uptake describes Gilbert syndrome, not the adult prehepatic category."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Post-hepatic (obstructive) jaundice in the adult classification is caused by:","options":["Excessive RBC destruction","Damage in the liver","Obstruction in the common bile duct (CBD)","Immature liver enzymes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Post-hepatic/obstructive jaundice occurs due to obstruction in the common bile ducts (CBD).  Why A wrong: Excessive RBC destruction is pre-hepatic.  Why B wrong: Liver damage is hepatic jaundice.  Why D wrong: Immature liver enzymes relate to neonatal physiological jaundice, not the adult obstructive type."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The paediatric classification of jaundice divides it into which two types?","options":["Pre-hepatic and post-hepatic","Physiological and pathological","Hepatic and obstructive","Conjugated and unconjugated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The paediatric classification is physiological jaundice and pathological jaundice.  Why A wrong: Pre-hepatic/post-hepatic belong to the adult classification.  Why C wrong: Hepatic/obstructive belong to the adult classification.  Why D wrong: Conjugated/unconjugated is a biochemical grouping, not the stated paediatric classification."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a term newborn, physiological jaundice characteristically appears:","options":["Within the first 24 hours after birth","After 24 hours after birth","After 48 hours after birth","Only after the 7th day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological jaundice occurs after 24 hours in the term newborn.  Why A wrong: Appearance within the first 24 hours is the cardinal sign of pathological jaundice.  Why C wrong: After 48 hours is the timing for the preterm newborn.  Why D wrong: By day 7–10 physiological jaundice is resolving, not first appearing."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a preterm newborn, physiological jaundice appears after:","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","5 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological jaundice occurs after 48 hours in the preterm newborn.  Why A wrong: 12 hours would be within the first 24 hours (pathological).  Why B wrong: 24 hours is the term-newborn onset.  Why D wrong: Day 5 is the peak level, not the onset."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Physiological jaundice reaches its peak level at approximately which day of life?","options":["1st day","3rd day","5th day","10th day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological jaundice peak level is reached at about the 5th day.  Why A wrong: Day 1 (within 24 hours) suggests pathological jaundice.  Why B wrong: 3rd day is not the stated peak.  Why D wrong: By day 10 term physiological jaundice is resolving."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are listed as causes of physiological jaundice? (Select all that apply)","options":["Decrease in life span of RBCs","Polycythemia","Immature liver with improper secretion of UDP-GT enzyme","Rh incompatibility","G6PD deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The listed causes of physiological jaundice are decreased RBC life span, polycythemia, and immature liver (improper secretion of Uridine Di-Phosphate Glucoronyl transferase, UDP-GT).  Why A/B/C correct: These are the three stated physiological causes.  Why D wrong: Rh incompatibility is a cause of pathological jaundice (haemolytic disease).  Why E wrong: G6PD deficiency is a cause of pathological jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In physiological jaundice the bilirubin level typically ranges between:","options":["0.3–1.3 mg/dl","5 to 15 mg/dl","12 to 14 mg/dl",">20 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In physiological jaundice the bilirubin level ranges between 5 to 15 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: 0.3–1.3 mg/dl is the normal (non-jaundiced) level.  Why C wrong: 12–14 mg/dl is a specific Kramer's zone value.  Why D wrong: >20 mg/dl indicates pathological jaundice/kernicterus risk."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Regarding treatment of physiological jaundice, which statement is correct?","options":["Immediate exchange transfusion is required","No treatment is required","Lifelong phenobarbitone therapy is needed","Surgery is required"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In physiological jaundice, no treatment is required.  Why A wrong: Exchange transfusion is reserved for severe/pathological hyperbilirubinemia.  Why C wrong: Phenobarbitone is a drug therapy used for jaundice needing treatment, not routine physiological jaundice.  Why D wrong: Surgery is not indicated for physiological jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Physiological jaundice self-resolves within what time frame in term and preterm newborns respectively?","options":["3–5 days (term) and 7–10 days (preterm)","7–10 days (term) and 10–14 days (preterm)","10–14 days (term) and 21 days (preterm)","24 hours (term) and 48 hours (preterm)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological jaundice self-resolves within 7 to 10 days in the term newborn and 10 to 14 days in the preterm neonate.  Why A wrong: These are not the stated resolution times.  Why C wrong: 21 days is not stated for preterm.  Why D wrong: 24/48 hours are onset times, not resolution times."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with physiological jaundice? (Select all that apply)","options":["Irritability","Lethargy","Yellow sclera","Poor feeding","Clay/white coloured stool"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological jaundice signs and symptoms are irritability, lethargy, yellow sclera, and poor feeding.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are listed under physiological jaundice signs and symptoms.  Why E wrong: Clay/white coloured stool is listed under the clinical features of pathological jaundice, not physiological."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Pathological jaundice is characteristically defined by jaundice occurring:","options":["After the 5th day","Within the first 24 hours after birth","After 48 hours in preterm","Only after 7–10 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pathological jaundice occurs (appears) within the first 24 hours after birth — this is the cardinal sign.  Why A wrong: Day 5 relates to the peak of physiological jaundice.  Why C wrong: After 48 hours is physiological onset in preterm.  Why D wrong: 7–10 days relates to resolution of physiological jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Approximately what percentage of neonates develop pathological jaundice?","options":["About 5%","About 20%","About 60%","About 80%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: About 5% of neonates develop pathological jaundice.  Why B wrong: 20% is not the stated value.  Why C wrong: 60% is the risk of jaundice in term newborns overall.  Why D wrong: 80% is the risk of jaundice in preterm newborns overall."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"What is the most common cause of pathological jaundice in the neonate?","options":["ABO incompatibility","Rh incompatibility","Thalassemia","G6PD deficiency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of pathological jaundice is Rh incompatibility.  Why A wrong: ABO incompatibility is common but usually milder/less severe than Rh.  Why C wrong: Thalassemia is a listed cause but not the most common.  Why D wrong: G6PD deficiency is a listed cause but not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In hereditary spherocytosis, RBCs become spherical due to absence of which proteins in the cell wall of the RBC?","options":["Spectrin and ankyrin","Actin and myosin","Globin and haem","Albumin and globulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In hereditary spherocytosis the RBC shape becomes spherical due to absence of spectrin and ankyrin protein in the cell wall of the RBC.  Why B wrong: Actin and myosin are muscle contractile proteins.  Why C wrong: Globin and haem form haemoglobin, not the cytoskeleton.  Why D wrong: Albumin and globulin are plasma proteins."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of pathological jaundice (haemolytic disease of the newborn)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Rh incompatibility and ABO incompatibility","Thalassemia and sickle cell anaemia","Hereditary spherocytosis","Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency","Polycythemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of pathological jaundice include Rh and ABO incompatibility, thalassemia and sickle cell anaemia, hereditary spherocytosis, and G6PD deficiency.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are listed under causes (haemolytic diseases of the newborn).  Why E wrong: Polycythemia is listed as a cause of physiological jaundice, not pathological."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In pathological jaundice, the direct (conjugated) bilirubin level is characteristically:","options":["<0.3 mg/dl","Greater than 2 mg/dl","5–15 mg/dl","Always normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In pathological jaundice the direct bilirubin level is >2 mg/dl (and total bilirubin may be >20 mg/dl).  Why A wrong: <0.3 mg/dl is below normal.  Why C wrong: 5–15 mg/dl is the total range in physiological jaundice.  Why D wrong: The direct fraction is elevated (>2 mg/dl), not normal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In dark-skinned people, initial jaundice is best assessed at which site, since the sclera is used in normal (lighter-skinned) individuals?","options":["Palms","Hard palate","Abdomen","Nail beds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In normal individuals the sclera is assessed for initial jaundice; in dark-skinned people the hard palate is assessed for initial jaundice.  Why A wrong: Palms and soles are late (Kramer zone 5) sites, not the initial dark-skin assessment site.  Why C wrong: The abdomen is not the stated initial site.  Why D wrong: Nail beds are used to assess cyanosis, not the stated initial jaundice site."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"(Daman & Diu 2018) Which stool colour is a clinical feature of pathological jaundice?","options":["Green meconium","Clay or white coloured stool","Black tarry stool","Yellow seedy stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Clay or white coloured stool is a clinical feature of pathological jaundice (Daman & Diu 2018).  Why A wrong: Green meconium is normal newborn stool.  Why C wrong: Black tarry stool (melena) suggests upper GI bleeding, not this feature.  Why D wrong: Yellow seedy stool is normal breastfed-infant stool."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"When the bilirubin level reaches greater than 20 mg/dl, it can cross the blood-brain barrier and cause yellow staining of the brain by unconjugated bilirubin. This condition is known as:","options":["Icterus neonatorum","Kernicterus (bilirubin-induced encephalopathy)","Erythroblastosis fetalis","Bronze baby syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When bilirubin exceeds 20 mg/dl it can cross the blood-brain barrier and cause yellow staining of the brain from unconjugated bilirubin, known as kernicterus or bilirubin-induced encephalopathy.  Why A wrong: Icterus neonatorum is the general term for neonatal jaundice.  Why C wrong: Erythroblastosis fetalis is Rh-incompatibility haemolytic disease.  Why D wrong: Bronze baby syndrome is skin/urine pigmentation following phototherapy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During physical examination for neonatal jaundice, the newborn's skin and sclera should be examined in:","options":["Dim room light","Natural light","Blue phototherapy light","Ultraviolet light"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The newborn's skin and sclera should be examined in natural light.  Why A wrong: Dim light impairs colour assessment.  Why C wrong: Under (blue) phototherapy light, visual assessment of jaundice is unreliable.  Why D wrong: UV light is not used for clinical skin colour examination."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During examination for jaundice, the nurse is taught to press a finger over which area to blanch the skin?","options":["Over a soft, fatty area only","Over a bony prominence or the tip of the newborn's nose","Over the abdomen only","Over the palms only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Press a finger over a bony prominence or the tip of the newborn's nose to assess jaundice.  Why A wrong: A soft fatty area does not blanch reliably; a bony prominence is specified.  Why C wrong: The abdomen alone is not the stated pressing site.  Why D wrong: Palms are a late site (Kramer zone 5), not the stated blanching technique."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In neonatal jaundice, the progression of visible jaundice on the body classically follows which pattern?","options":["Starts at the feet and moves up to the head","Starts at the head first, and the hands and feet are the last to be jaundiced","Appears only on the palms and soles","Appears uniformly all over at once"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Jaundice starts at the head first and the hands and feet are the last to be jaundiced.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse of the true cephalocaudal progression.  Why C wrong: Palms/soles are involved last (highest bilirubin), not exclusively.  Why D wrong: Progression is cephalocaudal, not simultaneous."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Why is early and frequent feeding encouraged in a jaundiced newborn?","options":["To increase the bilirubin level","To facilitate passage of meconium and encourage excretion of jaundice","To prevent hyperglycaemia","To reduce the head circumference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Offer early or frequent feeding to facilitate passage of meconium and encourage excretion of jaundice.  Why A wrong: The aim is to reduce, not increase, bilirubin.  Why C wrong: Feeding relates to bilirubin excretion here, not glucose control.  Why D wrong: Feeding does not affect head circumference."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which non-invasive device is described as a more accurate method for assessing the estimated value of bilirubin?","options":["Ingram icterometer","Serum bilirubin assay","Sphygmomanometer","Pulse oximeter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Ingram icterometer is used to assess the estimated bilirubin value and is described as a more accurate non-invasive method of assessing jaundice.  Why B wrong: Serum bilirubin assay is invasive (blood test) and is the confirmatory test, not the non-invasive icterometer.  Why C wrong: A sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure.  Why D wrong: A pulse oximeter measures oxygen saturation, not bilirubin."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The transcutaneous bilirubinometer is best described as:","options":["An invasive blood test","A non-invasive method used to assess the bilirubin level in the newborn","The confirmatory diagnostic test for jaundice","A device to measure blood glucose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The transcutaneous bilirubinometer is a non-invasive method used to assess the bilirubin level in the newborn.  Why A wrong: It is non-invasive, not a blood test.  Why C wrong: Serum bilirubin remains the confirmatory test.  Why D wrong: It measures bilirubin, not glucose."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Kramer's index is also known as:","options":["Visual Assessment of Neonatal Jaundice","Serum bilirubin estimation","Direct Coombs test","APGAR scoring"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kramer's index is also known as Visual Assessment of Neonatal Jaundice.  Why B wrong: Serum bilirubin is the laboratory confirmatory test, not Kramer's index.  Why C wrong: The direct Coombs test detects antibody-coated RBCs in haemolytic disease.  Why D wrong: APGAR scoring assesses newborn adaptation at birth, not jaundice zones."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which statement about Kramer's index is correct?","options":["It is the confirmatory diagnosis of jaundice","It is used only in preterm babies","It is used to assess the estimated value of bilirubin but is not confirmatory","It measures direct bilirubin only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Kramer's index is used to assess the estimated value of bilirubin and is explicitly not a confirmatory diagnosis of jaundice.  Why A wrong: It is not confirmatory; serum bilirubin is.  Why B wrong: It is a general visual assessment, not restricted to preterm babies.  Why D wrong: It estimates total visible bilirubin distribution, not a direct-only value."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"According to Kramer's index, jaundice involving the \"Head, Face & Neck\" (Zone 1) corresponds to an estimated serum bilirubin level of:","options":["4–6 mg/dl","6–8 mg/dl","8–12 mg/dl",">15 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kramer Zone 1 (Head, Face & Neck) corresponds to an estimated serum bilirubin of 4–6 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: 6–8 mg/dl is Zone 2 (upper trunk/above umbilicus).  Why C wrong: 8–12 mg/dl is Zone 3 (lower trunk and thighs/below umbilicus).  Why D wrong: >15 mg/dl is Zone 5 (palms & soles)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"According to Kramer's index, involvement of the palms and soles (Zone 5) corresponds to an estimated serum bilirubin of:","options":["4–6 mg/dl","8–12 mg/dl","12–14 mg/dl",">15 mg/dl"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Kramer Zone 5 (palms & soles) corresponds to an estimated serum bilirubin of >15 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: 4–6 mg/dl is Zone 1 (head, face & neck).  Why B wrong: 8–12 mg/dl is Zone 3.  Why C wrong: 12–14 mg/dl is Zone 4 (up to ankle & wrist)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Match Kramer's Zone 4 (\"Up to ankle & wrist\") with its estimated serum bilirubin level:","options":["6–8 mg/dl","8–12 mg/dl","12–14 mg/dl",">15 mg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Zone 4 (up to ankle & wrist) corresponds to 12–14 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: 6–8 mg/dl is Zone 2.  Why B wrong: 8–12 mg/dl is Zone 3.  Why D wrong: >15 mg/dl is Zone 5."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In Kramer's index, an estimated serum bilirubin of 8–12 mg/dl corresponds to which zone/region?","options":["Head, face & neck (Zone 1)","Upper trunk/above umbilicus (Zone 2)","Lower trunk and thighs/below umbilicus (Zone 3)","Palms & soles (Zone 5)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 8–12 mg/dl corresponds to Zone 3 — lower trunk and thighs or below umbilicus.  Why A wrong: Zone 1 is 4–6 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: Zone 2 is 6–8 mg/dl.  Why D wrong: Zone 5 (palms & soles) is >15 mg/dl."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The three types of treatment available for neonatal jaundice are: (Select all that apply)","options":["Phototherapy","Exchange blood transfusion (EBT)","Drug therapy","Radiotherapy","Dialysis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Three types of treatment are phototherapy, exchange blood transfusion (EBT), and drug therapy.  Why A/B/C correct: All three are the listed treatment modalities.  Why D wrong: Radiotherapy is not a treatment for neonatal jaundice.  Why E wrong: Dialysis is not listed as a treatment for neonatal jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During phototherapy, ultraviolet/light therapy acts by converting:","options":["Conjugated (direct) bilirubin into unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin","Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin into conjugated (direct) bilirubin","Bilirubin into haemoglobin","Albumin into bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phototherapy converts unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin into water-soluble products that can be excreted. [Note: phototherapy uses BLUE VISIBLE light (~420-470 nm), NOT ultraviolet; it works mainly by photoisomerization to water-soluble photoisomers/lumirubin excreted in bile and urine largely without hepatic conjugation.]  Why A wrong: This is the reverse of phototherapy's action.  Why C wrong: Phototherapy does not create haemoglobin.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy does not convert albumin to bilirubin."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The goal of phototherapy is to decrease the level of unconjugated/indirect bilirubin in order to prevent all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Kernicterus","Acute bilirubin-induced encephalopathy","Hearing loss","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The stated goals are preventing kernicterus, acute bilirubin-induced encephalopathy, and hearing loss; hyperglycaemia is not among them.  Why A wrong: Preventing kernicterus is a stated goal.  Why B wrong: Preventing acute bilirubin-induced encephalopathy is a stated goal.  Why C wrong: Preventing hearing loss is a stated goal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The recommended distance between the light source and the neonate during phototherapy is:","options":["20 cm or 8 inches","45 cm or 18 inches","60 cm or 24 inches","100 cm or 40 inches"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The distance between light source and neonate should be maintained at 45 cm or 18 inches.  Why A wrong: 20 cm/8 inches is not the stated distance.  Why C wrong: 60 cm/24 inches is not stated.  Why D wrong: 100 cm is far too great; \"not more than 40 cm\" is also specified as a limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During phototherapy, the distance between the light source and the baby should be:","options":["Not more than 40 cm","Exactly 100 cm","At least 80 cm","Not less than 60 cm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The distance between the light source and the baby should be not more than 40 cm.  Why B wrong: 100 cm exceeds the limit.  Why C wrong: 80 cm exceeds the stated limit.  Why D wrong: 60 cm exceeds the stated maximum of 40 cm."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which colour of light is most commonly used in phototherapy, but has the disadvantage of interfering with the early detection of cyanosis?","options":["White light","Green light","Blue light","Red light"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Blue and white fluorescent light is used; blue light is most commonly used but it produces disturbance in the early detection of cyanosis.  Why A wrong: White is used along with blue but the cyanosis-detection issue is attributed to blue light.  Why B wrong: Green light is not the stated commonly used light.  Why D wrong: Red light is not used for phototherapy here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The required light source wavelength for phototherapy is:","options":["220–320 nm","320–400 nm","420–460 nm","500–600 nm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The required light source wavelength is 420–460 nm.  Why A wrong: 220–320 nm is UV-C/UV-B range, not the stated therapeutic band.  Why B wrong: 320–400 nm is not the stated band.  Why D wrong: 500–600 nm is outside the stated therapeutic range."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a term newborn, phototherapy is indicated at a serum bilirubin level of:","options":[">5 mg/dl",">10 mg/dl",">15 mg/dl",">2 mg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In a term newborn phototherapy is indicated at serum bilirubin >15 mg/dl.  Why A wrong: >5 mg/dl is the indication in preterm newborns.  Why B wrong: 10 mg/dl is the discontinuation threshold, not the term indication.  Why D wrong: >2 mg/dl is the adult jaundice threshold."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a preterm newborn, phototherapy is indicated at a serum bilirubin level of, to prevent the need for blood transfusion:","options":[">5 mg/dl",">15 mg/dl",">20 mg/dl",">45 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the preterm newborn phototherapy is indicated at >5 mg/dl serum bilirubin, to prevent the need for blood transfusion.  Why B wrong: >15 mg/dl is the term-newborn indication.  Why C wrong: >20 mg/dl indicates pathological jaundice/EBT territory.  Why D wrong: 45 mg/dl relates to a glucose value, not bilirubin."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In \"double phototherapy,\" the neonate is placed on a transparent mirror so that:","options":["Only the anterior surface is treated","Only the posterior surface is treated","Both anterior and posterior surfaces are treated at one time","The eyes receive additional light"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In double phototherapy the neonate is placed on a transparent mirror and phototherapy is provided to both anterior and posterior surfaces at one time.  Why A wrong: Treating only the anterior surface describes single phototherapy.  Why B wrong: Treating only the posterior surface describes single phototherapy.  Why D wrong: The eyes are covered to prevent damage, not additionally exposed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Single phototherapy provides light to:","options":["Both anterior and posterior surfaces simultaneously","Only a single body surface (anterior or posterior)","The head only","The extremities only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Single phototherapy provides light to only a single body surface, which may be anterior or posterior.  Why A wrong: Treating both surfaces at once is double phototherapy.  Why C wrong: It is not restricted to the head.  Why D wrong: It is not restricted to the extremities."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which is described as the most common mechanism of action of phototherapy?","options":["Photo isomerisation","Photo oxidation","Photo synthesis","Photo coagulation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Photoisomerization is the predominant mechanism of phototherapy — configurational isomerization (fastest) plus structural isomerization to lumirubin (the principal route of irreversible bilirubin clearance). [Corrected: photoisomerization, not photo-oxidation, is the most common mechanism.]  Why B wrong: Photo-oxidation is only a minor, slow mechanism contributing little to bilirubin elimination.  Why C wrong: Photosynthesis is a plant process, not a phototherapy mechanism.  Why D wrong: Photocoagulation is a laser technique, not a phototherapy mechanism here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"(KGMU 2023) During phototherapy, the nurse must cover which structures to prevent damage?","options":["The eyes and genitals","The hands and feet","The mouth and nose","The abdomen and chest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cover the eyes and genitals to prevent damage (KGMU 2023); the eyes are covered with gauze pads and the infant is placed naked (nappy untied) under the lights.  Why B wrong: Hands and feet are exposed to maximize skin surface, not covered.  Why C wrong: Mouth and nose are not covered.  Why D wrong: Abdomen and chest are exposed to the light, not covered."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"How frequently should the baby's position be turned during phototherapy?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 2–3 hours","Every 12 hours","Once a day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Turn the baby every 2–3 hours during phototherapy.  Why A wrong: Every 30 minutes is the frequency for glucose monitoring in hypoglycaemia, not turning.  Why C wrong: Every 12 hours is the minimum serum bilirubin check interval, not turning.  Why D wrong: Once a day is the frequency for body-weight checks."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"(NORCET 2022) During phototherapy, temperature must be checked at what interval to identify hypothermia or hyperthermia?","options":["Every 30 minutes","Every 2 hours","Every 12 hours","Once daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Temperature must be checked every 2 hours to identify hypothermia or hyperthermia (NORCET 2022).  Why A wrong: Every 30 minutes applies to glucose monitoring.  Why C wrong: Every 12 hours is the serum bilirubin check interval.  Why D wrong: Once daily is the body-weight check interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During phototherapy, how often should the body weight be checked?","options":["Every 2 hours","Every 12 hours","Once a day","Every 30 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Body weight should be checked once a day during phototherapy.  Why A wrong: Every 2 hours is for temperature checks.  Why B wrong: Every 12 hours is for serum bilirubin checks.  Why D wrong: Every 30 minutes is for glucose monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During phototherapy, serum bilirubin level must be checked at least every ____; in cases of severe jaundice, total serum bilirubin (TSB) is checked ____.","options":["2 hours; 6 hourly","12 hours; 3 hourly","30 minutes; hourly","24 hours; 12 hourly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serum bilirubin must be checked at least every 12 hours; in severe jaundice, check the TSB 3 hourly.  Why A wrong: 2 hours is the temperature-check interval.  Why C wrong: 30 minutes applies to glucose monitoring.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"To prevent dehydration during phototherapy, additional fluid should be provided at:","options":["10 ml/kg/day","20 to 40 ml/kg/day","80 ml/kg/day","160 ml/kg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Provide additional fluid of 20 to 40 ml/kg/day to prevent dehydration.  Why A wrong: 10 ml/kg/day is below the stated range.  Why C wrong: 80 ml/kg is the normal total blood volume value, not the extra fluid.  Why D wrong: 160 ml/kg is the term double-exchange transfusion volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Phototherapy should be discontinued when the serum bilirubin level returns to:","options":["Less than 10 mg/dl for 2 times","Less than 15 mg/dl for 1 time","Less than 5 mg/dl for 3 times","Less than 20 mg/dl once"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phototherapy should be discontinued when the serum bilirubin returns to less than 10 mg/dl for 2 times.  Why B wrong: The threshold is <10 mg/dl (measured twice), not <15 mg/dl once.  Why C wrong: <5 mg/dl for 3 times is not the stated criterion.  Why D wrong: 20 mg/dl is a high pathological level, not the discontinuation criterion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Once an infant is under phototherapy, visual assessment of jaundice becomes:","options":["More accurate","Unreliable","Confirmatory","The preferred method"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Visual assessment of jaundice is unreliable once the infant is under phototherapy.  Why A wrong: It becomes unreliable, not more accurate.  Why C wrong: Serum bilirubin, not visual assessment, is confirmatory.  Why D wrong: It is explicitly not preferred under phototherapy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Successful phototherapy should produce a decline in total serum bilirubin (TSB) of approximately how much within 4–6 hours?","options":["1–2 mg/dl (17–34 micromol/L)","5–10 mg/dl","0.1 mg/dl","20 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Successful phototherapy should produce a decline in TSB of 1–2 mg/dl (17–34 micromol/L) within 4–6 hours, and the TSB should continue to fall.  Why B wrong: 5–10 mg/dl is far greater than the stated expected decline.  Why C wrong: 0.1 mg/dl is too small.  Why D wrong: 20 mg/dl is a total bilirubin level, not a 4–6 hour decline."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Failure of intensive phototherapy is said to occur if TSB levels do NOT decrease by:","options":["1 to 2 mg/dL (17–34 micromol/L) within 4 to 6 hours of initiation","5 mg/dL within 1 hour","10 mg/dL within 12 hours","20 mg/dL within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Failure of intensive phototherapy occurs if TSB does not decrease by 1 to 2 mg/dL (17–34 micromol/L) within four to six hours of initiation.  Why B wrong: 5 mg/dL within 1 hour is not stated.  Why C wrong: 10 mg/dL within 12 hours is not the stated failure criterion.  Why D wrong: 20 mg/dL within 24 hours is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Why is aluminium foil sometimes placed around the sides of the warmer or cot during phototherapy?","options":["To reduce the temperature of the baby","To maximize the amount of skin exposure to phototherapy","To block the light from the eyes","To sterilize the equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aluminium foil is placed around the sides of the warmer or cot to maximize the amount of skin exposure to phototherapy (by reflecting light).  Why A wrong: Its purpose is light reflection, not cooling.  Why C wrong: Eye protection is achieved with gauze eye pads.  Why D wrong: Foil is not for sterilization."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of phototherapy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Loose stools and dehydration","Electric shock","Bronze baby syndrome","Separation from mother","Hyperglycaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The listed complications of phototherapy are loose stools and dehydration, electric shock, bronze baby syndrome, and separation from mother.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed.  Why E wrong: Hyperglycaemia is not listed as a phototherapy complication."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Bronze baby syndrome is characterized by dark grey-brown pigmentation of skin, mucous membrane and urine following which treatment?","options":["Exchange blood transfusion","Phototherapy","Chloramphenicol therapy","Vancomycin therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bronze baby syndrome is a dark grey-brown pigmentation of skin, mucous membrane and urine following phototherapy.  Why A wrong: It follows phototherapy, not exchange transfusion.  Why C wrong: Chloramphenicol causes grey baby syndrome, a distinct condition.  Why D wrong: Vancomycin causes red man syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Grey baby syndrome, characterized by abdominal distension, hemodynamic collapse, and ashen-gray skin discoloration in neonates, is an adverse reaction caused by which drug?","options":["Vancomycin","Phenobarbitone","Chloramphenicol","Oxytocin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Grey baby syndrome is an adverse reaction to chloramphenicol, with abdominal distension, hemodynamic collapse, and ashen-gray skin discoloration.  Why A wrong: Vancomycin causes red man syndrome.  Why B wrong: Phenobarbitone is the drug of choice for neonatal jaundice, not the cause of grey baby syndrome.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin augmentation is a risk factor for birth asphyxia, not grey baby syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Red man syndrome (vancomycin flushing syndrome, VFS) is an anaphylactic reaction caused by which class of antibiotics?","options":["Aminoglycosides","Glycopeptides (e.g., vancomycin)","Cephalosporins","Penicillins"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Red man syndrome is an anaphylactic reaction caused by glycopeptide antibiotics, e.g., vancomycin, and is also known as vancomycin flushing syndrome (VFS).  Why A wrong: Aminoglycosides are not the stated cause.  Why C wrong: Cephalosporins are not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Penicillins are not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Blue baby syndrome (infant methemoglobinemia), in which the baby's skin turns blue due to lack of oxygen in the blood, is usually seen in:","options":["Nitrate poisoning","Chloramphenicol toxicity","Rh incompatibility","Vancomycin infusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blue baby syndrome, also known as infant methemoglobinemia, is usually seen in nitrate poisoning.  Why B wrong: Chloramphenicol causes grey baby syndrome.  Why C wrong: Rh incompatibility causes haemolytic disease/jaundice, not methemoglobinemia.  Why D wrong: Vancomycin causes red man syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Carbon baby syndrome is another name for which rare condition characterized by progressive hyperpigmentation of the skin since infancy?","options":["Acquired universal melanosis (AUM)","Extramedullary haematopoiesis","Methemoglobinemia","Erythroblastosis fetalis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Carbon baby syndrome is another name for acquired universal melanosis (AUM), characterized by progressive hyperpigmentation since infancy with heavy melanization of the entire epidermis.  Why B wrong: Extramedullary haematopoiesis is blueberry muffin baby syndrome.  Why C wrong: Methemoglobinemia is blue baby syndrome.  Why D wrong: Erythroblastosis fetalis is Rh haemolytic disease."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Blueberry muffin baby syndrome (extramedullary haematopoiesis) describes a newborn with multiple purpura following intrauterine infection with which agents? (Select all that apply)","options":["Rubella","Cytomegalovirus","Toxoplasmosis","Parvovirus B19 / herpes simplex / coxsackie virus","Vancomycin exposure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Blueberry muffin baby syndrome follows infection of the unborn baby with rubella, cytomegalovirus, toxoplasmosis, coxsackie virus, parvovirus B19, and herpes simplex.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are the listed infectious causes.  Why E wrong: Vancomycin is a drug (red man syndrome), not an intrauterine infection."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Exchange blood transfusion (EBT) is described as the most effective and reliable method for reduction of bilirubin in severe cases. What is done during EBT?","options":["Fresh blood is removed and stored","The neonate's blood containing the higher bilirubin level is removed and fresh blood is administered","Only plasma is exchanged","Only platelets are transfused"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In EBT, the neonate's blood that contains the higher bilirubin level is removed and fresh blood is administered; it is the most effective and reliable method for reducing bilirubin in severe hyperbilirubinemia to prevent kernicterus/anaemia.  Why A wrong: The high-bilirubin blood is removed and fresh blood added, not fresh blood removed/stored.  Why C wrong: Whole blood, not plasma alone, is exchanged.  Why D wrong: Whole blood, not just platelets, is used."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The normal amount of blood in a neonate is:","options":["40 ml/kg","60 ml/kg","80 ml/kg","160 ml/kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The normal amount of blood in a neonate is 80 ml/kg.  Why A wrong: 40 ml/kg is not the stated value.  Why B wrong: 60 ml/kg is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 160 ml/kg is the term double-exchange transfusion volume, not normal blood volume."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Using the double exchange method, the required EBT whole-blood volume for a term neonate and a preterm neonate respectively is:","options":["80 ml/kg and 120 ml/kg","160 ml/kg (term) and 180 ml/kg (preterm)","100 ml/kg and 150 ml/kg","200 ml/kg and 240 ml/kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Required EBT by the double exchange method is 160 ml/kg (term) or 180 ml/kg (preterm) whole blood.  Why A wrong: These values are not stated.  Why C wrong: These values are not stated.  Why D wrong: These values are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are indications for exchange blood transfusion (EBT)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cord blood haemoglobin level of 10 g/dl or less","Cord blood bilirubin level of 5 mg/dl or more","Unconjugated serum bilirubin rising at 0.5 mg/dl per hour despite phototherapy","Serum bilirubin of infant 12 mg/dl in first 24 hours and >20 mg/dl in neonatal period","Progressive anaemia of the neonate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are indications for EBT: cord Hb <=10 g/dl, cord bilirubin >=5 mg/dl, unconjugated bilirubin rising 0.5 mg/dl/hour despite phototherapy, serum bilirubin 12 mg/dl in first 24 hours and >20 mg/dl in neonatal period, and progressive anaemia (also when phototherapy fails to prevent bilirubin rise).  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: Each is explicitly listed as an indication of EBT.  Why none excluded: All five options match the source's indication list."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During the EBT procedure, the neonate's blood is removed and fresh blood administered using a syringe of what size, in aliquots?","options":["5 ml","10 ml","20 ml","50 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The neonate's blood is removed with a 10 ml syringe, discarded, and fresh 10 ml blood is administered; the procedure repeats until the recommended volume is replaced.  Why A wrong: 5 ml is not the stated aliquot.  Why C wrong: 20 ml is not the stated aliquot.  Why D wrong: 50 ml is the interval for calcium gluconate dosing, not the exchange aliquot."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During EBT, an IV line and three-way cannula are typically connected through which vessels?","options":["Femoral vein and artery","Umbilical vein and artery (peripheral vessels may also be used)","Jugular vein only","Radial artery only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An IV line and three-way cannula are connected through the umbilical vein and artery; peripheral blood vessels may also be used.  Why A wrong: Femoral vessels are not the stated route.  Why C wrong: Jugular vein alone is not stated.  Why D wrong: Radial artery alone is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During EBT, an important nursing responsibility regarding blood volumes is to:","options":["Record only the administered blood amount","Record only the discarded blood amount","Record both the discarded blood amount and the administered blood amount","Not record volumes at all"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A nurse should record both the discarded blood amount and the administered blood amount.  Why A wrong: Only recording administered volume is incomplete.  Why B wrong: Only recording discarded volume is incomplete.  Why D wrong: Documentation of both volumes is required for accurate balance."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of exchange blood transfusion? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypervolemia leading to cardiac failure","Hypovolemia and air embolism","Infection (hepatitis-C, hepatitis-B, HIV, malaria)","Hyperkalemia and hypocalcaemia","Lactic acidosis or alkalosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items are complications of EBT: hypervolemia (cardiac failure), hypovolemia and air embolism, infection (hep-C, hep-B, HIV, malaria), hyperkalemia and hypocalcaemia, and lactic acidosis or alkalosis.  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: Each is explicitly listed as a complication.  Why none excluded: All five options are from the source's complications list."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"To prevent hypocalcaemia during EBT, calcium gluconate is administered in what amount and how often?","options":["0.5 ml after every 50 ml of transfusion","1 ml after every 10 ml of transfusion","5 ml after every 100 ml of transfusion","10 ml after every 80 ml of transfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 0.5 ml calcium gluconate injection should be administered after every 50 ml of transfusion to prevent hypocalcaemia.  Why B wrong: These figures are not stated.  Why C wrong: These figures are not stated.  Why D wrong: These figures are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"(AIIMS 2019) The drug of choice for neonatal jaundice is:","options":["Vancomycin","Chloramphenicol","Phenobarbitone","Oxytocin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for neonatal jaundice is phenobarbitone (AIIMS 2019); it stimulates the liver to produce more UDP-GT enzyme, which aids bilirubin conjugation.  Why A wrong: Vancomycin causes red man syndrome and is not indicated for jaundice.  Why B wrong: Chloramphenicol causes grey baby syndrome.  Why D wrong: Oxytocin is a uterotonic, not a jaundice treatment."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Phenobarbitone helps reduce neonatal jaundice because it stimulates the liver to produce a higher amount of which enzyme?","options":["UDP-GT (uridine diphospho glucuronyl transferase)","G6PD","Alkaline phosphatase","Lactate dehydrogenase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phenobarbitone stimulates the liver to produce a higher amount of UDP-GT enzyme, which is helpful for conjugation of bilirubin.  Why B wrong: G6PD deficiency is a cause of haemolysis; phenobarbitone does not act via G6PD.  Why C wrong: Alkaline phosphatase is not the stated enzyme.  Why D wrong: Lactate dehydrogenase is not the stated enzyme."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The full form of UDP-GT is:","options":["Uridine Diphospho Glucuronyl Transferase","Universal Direct Protein-Glucose Transferase","Uridine Dehydrogenase Phosphate Glutamate Transaminase","Unconjugated Direct Protein Glucuronidase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The full form of UDP-GT is Uridine DiphosPho Glucuronyl Transferase.  Why B wrong: Incorrect expansion.  Why C wrong: Incorrect expansion.  Why D wrong: Incorrect expansion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Crigler-Najjar syndrome Type-I is caused by:","options":["Partial deficiency of UDP-GT","Total deficiency of UDP-GT","Defective uptake of bilirubin by the liver","Defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crigler-Najjar syndrome Type-I is due to total deficiency of UDP-GT.  Why A wrong: Partial deficiency of UDP-GT is Crigler-Najjar Type-II.  Why C wrong: Defective bilirubin uptake describes Gilbert syndrome.  Why D wrong: Defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin describes Dubin-Johnson syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Crigler-Najjar syndrome Type-II is due to:","options":["Total deficiency of UDP-GT","Partial deficiency of UDP-GT","Abnormal uncontrolled excretion of bilirubin","Defective uptake of bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crigler-Najjar syndrome Type-II is due to partial deficiency of UDP-GT.  Why A wrong: Total deficiency of UDP-GT is Type-I.  Why C wrong: Abnormal uncontrolled excretion describes Rotor syndrome.  Why D wrong: Defective uptake describes Gilbert syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Gilbert syndrome results from:","options":["Total deficiency of UDP-GT","Defective uptake of bilirubin by the liver","Obstruction of conjugated bilirubin excretion into the CBD","Abnormal excretion of bilirubin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gilbert syndrome is due to defective uptake of bilirubin by the liver.  Why A wrong: Total UDP-GT deficiency is Crigler-Najjar Type-I.  Why C wrong: Obstruction/defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin describes Dubin-Johnson syndrome.  Why D wrong: Abnormal/uncontrolled excretion describes Rotor syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which congenital hyperbilirubinaemia is due to obstruction/defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin into the common bile duct and is also known as \"Black Liver Jaundice\"?","options":["Gilbert syndrome","Rotor syndrome","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Crigler-Najjar syndrome"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dubin-Johnson syndrome is due to obstruction/defective excretion of conjugated bilirubin into the CBD and is also known as Black Liver Jaundice.  Why A wrong: Gilbert syndrome is defective uptake.  Why B wrong: Rotor syndrome is abnormal/uncontrolled excretion (not \"black liver\").  Why D wrong: Crigler-Najjar is UDP-GT deficiency (unconjugated)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Rotor syndrome is characterized by:","options":["Total deficiency of UDP-GT","Abnormal/uncontrolled excretion of bilirubin","Defective uptake of bilirubin","RBC maturation defect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rotor syndrome is due to abnormal/uncontrolled excretion of bilirubin.  Why A wrong: Total UDP-GT deficiency is Crigler-Najjar Type-I.  Why C wrong: Defective uptake describes Gilbert syndrome.  Why D wrong: RBC maturation relates to G6PD."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"According to the text, G6PD is responsible for maturation of RBCs and is responsible for which type of jaundice?","options":["Physiological jaundice","Pathological jaundice","Obstructive jaundice only","No jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: G6PD is responsible for maturation of RBC and is responsible for pathological jaundice.  Why A wrong: G6PD deficiency causes haemolysis (pathological), not physiological jaundice.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to obstructive jaundice.  Why D wrong: G6PD deficiency does cause jaundice (pathological)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following liver conditions are caused by UNCONJUGATED bilirubin? (Select all that apply)","options":["Haemolytic anaemias","Neonatal physiological jaundice","Crigler-Najjar syndromes type I and II","Gilbert syndrome","Obstruction of the biliary tree"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Unconjugated-bilirubin causes listed are haemolytic anaemias, neonatal physiological jaundice, Crigler-Najjar types I and II, Gilbert syndrome, and toxic hyperbilirubinemia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are listed as unconjugated causes.  Why E wrong: Obstruction of the biliary tree is a conjugated-bilirubin cause (along with Dubin-Johnson, Rotor, and hepatitis)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Birth asphyxia is also known by which other names? (Select all that apply)","options":["Perinatal asphyxia","Neonatal asphyxia","Erythroblastosis fetalis","Icterus neonatorum","Kernicterus"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Birth asphyxia is also known as perinatal asphyxia and neonatal asphyxia.  Why A/B correct: Both are the stated alternative names.  Why C wrong: Erythroblastosis fetalis is Rh haemolytic disease.  Why D wrong: Icterus neonatorum is neonatal jaundice.  Why E wrong: Kernicterus is bilirubin-induced encephalopathy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Birth asphyxia is defined as failure to initiate and maintain proper respiration, characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Hypoventilation","Hypoxia","Anaerobic glycolysis and lactic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Birth asphyxia is characterized by hypoventilation, hypoxia, anaerobic glycolysis, and lactic acidosis — not respiratory alkalosis.  Why A wrong: Hypoventilation is a listed feature.  Why B wrong: Hypoxia is a listed feature.  Why C wrong: Anaerobic glycolysis and lactic acidosis are listed features."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"According to WHO, perinatal asphyxia is characterised by profound metabolic acidosis with a pH less than ____ on umbilical cord arterial blood, and persistence of an Apgar score of 3 at the ____ minute.","options":["pH < 7.20; 5th minute","pH < 7.40; 1st minute","pH < 7.00; 10th minute","pH < 7.35; 5th minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: WHO characterises perinatal asphyxia by profound metabolic acidosis with pH less than 7.20 on umbilical cord arterial blood and persistence of an Apgar score of 3 at the 5th minute.  Why B wrong: pH 7.40 and 1st minute are not the WHO criteria stated.  Why C wrong: pH 7.00 at 10th minute is not stated.  Why D wrong: pH 7.35 is within normal range, not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"A baby with birth asphyxia often develops hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy (HIE), a brain injury that can lead to permanent disability such as:","options":["Cerebral palsy and mental retardation (MR)","Diabetes mellitus","Congenital heart disease","Cleft palate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: HIE is a type of brain injury that often leads to permanent brain damage and disability, e.g., cerebral palsy and mental retardation (MR).  Why B wrong: Diabetes mellitus is not a stated HIE outcome.  Why C wrong: Congenital heart disease is not a stated HIE outcome.  Why D wrong: Cleft palate is a structural anomaly, not an HIE outcome."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are listed risk factors for birth asphyxia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Prolonged rupture of membranes","Multiple births and malpresentation","Augmentation of labour with oxytocin","Severe eclampsia and pre-eclampsia","Advanced maternal antenatal care"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed risk factors include elderly/young mothers, prolonged rupture of membranes, multiple births/malpresentation, lack of antenatal care, low birth weight, oxytocin augmentation, antepartum hemorrhage, severe eclampsia/pre-eclampsia, and antepartum/intrapartum anemia.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed risk factors.  Why E wrong: LACK of antenatal care is a risk factor; advanced antenatal care is protective, not a risk."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Under the causes of birth asphyxia, \"knotting of the umbilical cord around the neck of the newborn, cord prolapse, prolonged labour and preterm labour\" are classified as which factor?","options":["Anti-partum factor","Intra-partum factor","Post-partum factor","Genetic factor"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These are intra-partum factors (occurring during labour/delivery).  Why A wrong: Anti-partum factors include malnutrition, infection, substance abuse, hypertensive disorder, placental insufficiency.  Why C wrong: Post-partum factors include congenital anomalies leading to pulmonary/cardiovascular/CNS deprivation.  Why D wrong: \"Genetic factor\" is not a category used here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following is an anti-partum factor causing birth asphyxia?","options":["Cord prolapse","Placental insufficiency and hypertensive disorder of pregnancy","Congenital anomalies of the newborn","Prolonged labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-partum factors include malnutrition and infection, substance abuse, hypertensive disorder of pregnancy, and placental insufficiency.  Why A wrong: Cord prolapse is an intra-partum factor.  Why C wrong: Congenital anomalies are a post-partum factor.  Why D wrong: Prolonged labour is an intra-partum factor."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Asphyxia livida (also known as the \"stage of cyanosis\") corresponds to which APGAR score range?","options":["APGAR 0 to 3","APGAR 4 to 6","APGAR 7 to 8","APGAR 9 to 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asphyxia livida (stage of cyanosis) is when the APGAR score is 4 to 6.  Why A wrong: APGAR 0 to 3 is asphyxia pallida (stage of shock).  Why C wrong: 7 to 8 is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 9 to 10 indicates a well newborn."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Asphyxia pallida, also known as the \"stage of shock\" and requiring immediate resuscitation, corresponds to which APGAR score range?","options":["APGAR 0 to 3","APGAR 4 to 6","APGAR 5 to 7","APGAR 8 to 10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asphyxia pallida (stage of shock) is when the APGAR score is 0 to 3 and requires immediate resuscitation.  Why B wrong: 4 to 6 is asphyxia livida (stage of cyanosis).  Why C wrong: 5 to 7 is not the stated range.  Why D wrong: 8 to 10 indicates a healthy newborn."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In the immediate nursing management of a newborn with birth asphyxia, which action is the FIRST priority?","options":["Assess skin colour","Suctioning","Umbilical cord cutting","Assess heart rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suctioning is explicitly listed as the first priority in immediate actions for birth asphyxia (clearing the airway).  Why A wrong: Assessing skin colour comes later in the sequence.  Why C wrong: Umbilical cord cutting follows suctioning.  Why D wrong: Assessing heart rate is important but not the stated first priority."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During resuscitation of an asphyxiated newborn, the baby is placed in which position?","options":["Prone with neck fully flexed","Supine and \"rose\" position with the neck slightly extended","Left lateral with hips flexed","Trendelenburg with head down"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Provide supine position and \"rose\" position with the neck slightly extended to open the airway.  Why A wrong: Full neck flexion obstructs the airway.  Why C wrong: Left lateral with hip flexion is not the stated resuscitation position.  Why D wrong: Head-down Trendelenburg is not stated for airway opening here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Haemolytic disease of the newborn due to Rh incompatibility is also known as:","options":["Icterus neonatorum","Erythroblastosis fetalis","Kernicterus","Physiological jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rh incompatibility haemolytic disease is also known as erythroblastosis fetalis.  Why A wrong: Icterus neonatorum is the general term for neonatal jaundice.  Why C wrong: Kernicterus is bilirubin-induced brain injury.  Why D wrong: Physiological jaundice is benign and non-haemolytic."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Rh incompatibility (destruction of fetal RBCs from an antigen-antibody reaction) occurs when:","options":["An Rh-positive mother conceives an Rh-negative fetus","An Rh-negative mother conceives an Rh-positive fetus","Both parents are Rh-negative","Both parents are Rh-positive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rh incompatibility happens when an Rh-negative mother conceives an Rh-positive fetus, leading to destruction of fetal RBCs.  Why A wrong: This is the reverse and does not cause classic Rh disease.  Why C wrong: An Rh-negative fetus of an Rh-negative mother poses no Rh conflict.  Why D wrong: Two Rh-positive parents do not produce Rh incompatibility of this type."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which is described as the most common cause of hyperbilirubinemia or jaundice?","options":["ABO incompatibility","Rh incompatibility","G6PD deficiency","Physiological jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rh incompatibility is stated to be the most common cause of hyperbilirubinemia or jaundice.  Why A wrong: ABO incompatibility is common but milder/less severe than Rh.  Why C wrong: G6PD deficiency is a listed cause but not the most common.  Why D wrong: Physiological jaundice is common overall, but Rh incompatibility is specifically named as the most common cause of hyperbilirubinemia in this context."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In the antenatal period, Rh incompatibility can be assessed by blood group testing and estimation of anti-D antibody titre using which test?","options":["Direct Coombs test","Indirect Coombs test","p24 antigen test","ELISA test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The disease can be assessed in the antenatal period by blood group test and estimation of antibody titre (anti-D antibodies) by the indirect Coombs test.  Why A wrong: The direct Coombs test is used to diagnose ABO incompatibility (antibody-coated newborn RBCs), not stated here for antenatal anti-D titre.  Why C wrong: p24 antigen test diagnoses HIV.  Why D wrong: ELISA is used for HIV screening (may be falsely positive up to 18 months)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"For prevention of Rh incompatibility, Rh-D immunoglobulin is administered intramuscularly to the mother at what dose and timing?","options":["300 mcg or 1500 IU IM at 28 weeks of gestation and again within the first 72 hours after delivering an Rh-positive fetus","150 mcg IM at 12 weeks only","500 mcg IV at delivery only","1000 mcg orally daily throughout pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administer Rh-D immunoglobulin 300 mcg or 1500 IU intramuscular to the mother at 28 weeks of gestation, and a second dose during the first 72 hours after delivering an Rh-positive fetus.  Why B wrong: 150 mcg at 12 weeks is not the stated regimen.  Why C wrong: It is given IM (not IV) and requires two doses, not just at delivery.  Why D wrong: It is not given orally or daily throughout pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In exchange transfusion for Rh incompatibility, indications include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Cord haemoglobin (Hb) level less than 10 gm/dl","Cord serum bilirubin level more than 5 mg/dl","Positive Coombs test","Cord haemoglobin level of 15 gm/dl"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Indications are cord Hb <10 gm/dl, cord serum bilirubin >5 mg/dl, and positive Coombs test; a cord Hb of 15 gm/dl (normal/high) is not an indication.  Why A wrong: Cord Hb <10 gm/dl is a listed indication.  Why B wrong: Cord bilirubin >5 mg/dl is a listed indication.  Why C wrong: A positive Coombs test is a listed indication."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During exchange transfusion for Rh incompatibility, blood is withdrawn from the umbilical vein 10 ml at a time and replaced with the same amount of:","options":["Rh-positive group AB blood","Rh-negative group O blood","Rh-positive group O blood","Rh-negative group AB blood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 10 ml of blood is withdrawn from the umbilical vein at a time and replaced by the same amount of Rh-negative group O blood.  Why A wrong: Rh-positive blood would perpetuate the reaction; group AB is not used.  Why C wrong: Rh-positive blood would be attacked by maternal anti-D.  Why D wrong: Group AB is not the stated replacement."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In exchange transfusion, the process is continued until what proportion of the fetal blood is exchanged?","options":["20 to 30%","40 to 50%","80 to 90%","100%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The process is continued until 80 to 90% of the fetal blood is exchanged.  Why A wrong: 20–30% is too low per the source.  Why B wrong: 40–50% is not the stated endpoint.  Why D wrong: 100% is not achievable/stated; 80–90% is the target."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are stated purposes of exchange transfusion in Rh incompatibility? (Select all that apply)","options":["Removal of bilirubin","Removal of formed antibodies","Correction of anaemia","Replacement of Rh-positive by Rh-negative RBCs","Increasing maternal antibody titre"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The purposes are removal of bilirubin, removal of formed antibodies, correction of anaemia, and replacement of Rh-positive by Rh-negative RBCs.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly listed purposes.  Why E wrong: The aim is to remove antibodies, not increase them."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Icterus gravis neonatorum is a condition in which the fetus dies inside the uterus or within 30 minutes due to:","options":["Birth asphyxia only","Haemolytic jaundice or Rh incompatibility","Congenital heart disease","Hypoglycaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Icterus gravis neonatorum is a condition in which the fetus dies inside the uterus or within 30 minutes due to haemolytic jaundice or Rh incompatibility.  Why A wrong: It is attributed to haemolytic jaundice/Rh incompatibility, not asphyxia alone.  Why C wrong: Congenital heart disease is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycaemia is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Regarding Rh incompatibility across pregnancies, the first newborn (RhD+) of an RhD- mother is generally safe because:","options":["The mother is already sensitized before the first pregnancy","Sensitization to the RhD antigen occurs from fetal-maternal blood transfer during labour, so the first baby is usually unaffected","The fetus is RhD-negative","Maternal IgM cannot cross the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the first pregnancy, fetal-maternal blood transfer during labour sensitizes the RhD-negative mother to the RhD antigen; the first newborn is safe, but the mother is now sensitized.  Why A wrong: Prior sensitization would actually affect the first fetus (per the note), which is the exception, not the usual case.  Why C wrong: The fetus is RhD-positive in this scenario.  Why D wrong: While IgM does not cross the placenta, the stated reason the first baby is safe is that sensitization occurs at labour."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a subsequent pregnancy of a sensitized RhD-negative mother carrying an RhD-positive fetus, fetal/newborn haemolytic anaemia occurs because:","options":["Maternal IgM anti-D crosses the placenta","Rapid production of IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and attaches to fetal RBCs, marking them for destruction","The fetus produces anti-D against itself","The father's antigen directly damages fetal RBCs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: On repeat encounter with fetal RhD antigen, the mother rapidly produces IgG anti-D, which crosses the placenta, attaches to fetal RBCs, and marks them for destruction, causing haemolytic anaemia.  Why A wrong: The pathogenic antibody is IgG (which crosses the placenta), not IgM.  Why C wrong: The fetus does not produce anti-D against itself.  Why D wrong: The father's antigen is not directly involved; maternal antibodies mediate the damage."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In severe Rh incompatibility of a subsequent pregnancy, complications include increased bilirubin, CNS damage (kernicterus), and death. In contrast, the \"mild case\" is characterized by:","options":["Mild anaemia and jaundice","Immediate fetal death","Kernicterus","Macrosomia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mild case is characterized by mild anaemia and jaundice.  Why B wrong: Fetal death is a feature of the severe case.  Why C wrong: Kernicterus (CNS damage) is a severe-case feature.  Why D wrong: Macrosomia relates to the newborn of a diabetic mother, not Rh disease severity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"According to the note on Rh incompatibility, if the mother is sensitized to the RhD antigen prior to the first pregnancy, then:","options":["No fetus will ever be affected","The first fetus will be affected","Only the third pregnancy is affected","Sensitization is reversed"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The note states that if the mother is sensitized to the RhD antigen prior to the first pregnancy, then the first fetus will be affected.  Why A wrong: Prior sensitization means the first fetus can be affected.  Why C wrong: The first fetus, not only the third pregnancy, is affected.  Why D wrong: Prior sensitization is not reversed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"ABO incompatibility most commonly occurs in which maternal-fetal blood group combination?","options":["O-positive mothers with an A or B group fetus","AB mother with an O group fetus","A mother with an A group fetus","B mother with a B group fetus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ABO incompatibility commonly occurs in O-positive mothers with an A or B group fetus; the mother's anti-A or anti-B antibodies transfer to the fetus through the placenta, causing haemolysis.  Why B wrong: An AB mother has no anti-A/anti-B antibodies to cause this.  Why C wrong: An A mother with an A fetus is compatible.  Why D wrong: A B mother with a B fetus is compatible."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes ABO incompatibility from Rh incompatibility?","options":["ABO incompatibility is always more severe and occurs only in later pregnancies","ABO incompatibility can occur in the first-born baby but is usually milder/less severe","ABO incompatibility never causes jaundice","ABO incompatibility requires an Rh-negative mother"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ABO incompatibility occurs in the first-born baby but is usually milder or less severe (compared with Rh disease).  Why A wrong: It is milder, not more severe, and can affect the first-born.  Why C wrong: It does cause jaundice (pathologic, within 24 hours).  Why D wrong: It relates to A/B/O groups, not the Rh-negative status of the mother."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"ABO incompatibility is diagnosed by examining cord blood for elevation of serum bilirubin and presence of maternal IgG, and is also diagnosed by which test?","options":["Indirect Coombs test","Direct Coombs blood test","p24 antigen test","Kramer's index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ABO incompatibility is diagnosed by examining cord blood for elevated serum bilirubin and presence of maternal IgG, and also by the direct Coombs blood test.  Why A wrong: The indirect Coombs test is used antenatally for Rh anti-D titre.  Why C wrong: p24 antigen diagnoses HIV.  Why D wrong: Kramer's index is a visual jaundice estimate, not a diagnostic test for ABO incompatibility."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a newborn of a diabetic mother, a birth weight greater than 4 kg is known as:","options":["Macrosomia","Microsomia","Polycythemia","Macrocephaly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Weight >4 kg in the newborn of a diabetic mother is known as macrosomia.  Why B wrong: Microsomia means abnormally small body size.  Why C wrong: Polycythemia is increased RBC mass, not a weight term.  Why D wrong: Macrocephaly refers to an enlarged head, not overall large body weight."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of the newborn of a diabetic mother? (Select all that apply)","options":["Large for gestational age with large body surface area","Puffy face due to excessive subcutaneous fat deposition","Hypoglycaemia (twitching, convulsion, coma, death)","Hypocalcaemia and hyperbilirubinemia","Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed are signs/symptoms: large for gestational age with large body surface area, puffy face from excess subcutaneous fat, hypoglycaemia (twitching/convulsion/coma/death), hypocalcaemia and hyperbilirubinemia, and RDS (from increased insulin suppressing surfactant).  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: Each is explicitly listed.  Why none excluded: All five are drawn from the source's signs and symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In the newborn of a diabetic mother, respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) may occur because increased insulin:","options":["Increases surfactant production","May suppress the production of surfactant","Causes airway obstruction","Directly damages the lungs by acidosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: RDS may occur because the increased level of insulin may suppress production of surfactant.  Why A wrong: Insulin suppresses (not increases) surfactant in this context.  Why C wrong: The mechanism is surfactant suppression, not mechanical obstruction.  Why D wrong: The stated mechanism is decreased surfactant, not acidosis-induced lung damage."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"After initial routine nursing care of the newborn of a diabetic mother, the priority assessment is to:","options":["Assess the blood sugar level","Weigh the baby","Assess head circumference","Start phototherapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After initial routine nursing care, the nurse should assess the blood sugar level (because hypoglycaemia is a key, life-threatening risk).  Why B wrong: Weighing is part of routine assessment but not the stated priority here.  Why C wrong: Head circumference is not the stated priority.  Why D wrong: Phototherapy is used for jaundice, not the immediate priority for a diabetic mother's newborn."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"If the newborn of a diabetic mother is hypoglycaemic and unable to suck, the nurse administers a bolus of:","options":["2–3 ml/kg of 10% IV dextrose","5 ml/kg of 5% IV dextrose","10 ml/kg of 25% IV dextrose","1 ml/kg of 50% IV dextrose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If the neonate is unable to suck, administer a bolus of 2–3 ml/kg 10% IV dextrose, plus other symptomatic management.  Why B wrong: 5% dextrose at 5 ml/kg is not the stated bolus.  Why C wrong: 25% dextrose is not the stated concentration.  Why D wrong: 50% dextrose is not used in neonates here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In the newborn of a diabetic mother, hypocalcaemia is managed by administering:","options":["Calcium gluconate","Potassium chloride","Sodium bicarbonate","Magnesium sulfate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Administer calcium gluconate for hypocalcaemia.  Why B wrong: Potassium chloride treats hypokalaemia, not hypocalcaemia.  Why C wrong: Sodium bicarbonate treats acidosis.  Why D wrong: Magnesium sulfate is not the stated treatment for this hypocalcaemia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The normal blood sugar level of a term newborn in the first 72 hours, and after 72 hours, respectively, is:","options":["40–60 mg/dl (first 72 hours) and 50–90 mg/dl (after 72 hours)","20–40 mg/dl and 40–60 mg/dl","60–90 mg/dl and 90–120 mg/dl","45–55 mg/dl and 55–70 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normal blood sugar of a term newborn is 40–60 mg/dl in the first 72 hours, then 50–90 mg/dl after 72 hours.  Why B wrong: 20–40 mg/dl overlaps the hypoglycaemic range.  Why C wrong: 60–90/90–120 mg/dl are not the stated ranges.  Why D wrong: 45–55/55–70 mg/dl are not the stated ranges."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Neonatal hypoglycaemia is defined as a blood sugar level of:","options":["<40 mg/dl in the first 72 hours and <45 mg/dl after 72 hours","<60 mg/dl at all times","<20 mg/dl only","<90 mg/dl in the first 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoglycaemia is considered when blood sugar is <40 mg/dl in the first 72 hours and <45 mg/dl after 72 hours.  Why B wrong: <60 mg/dl at all times is not the stated definition.  Why C wrong: <20 mg/dl only is too restrictive.  Why D wrong: <90 mg/dl in the first 24 hours is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The mnemonic \"TIRED-O\" for signs and symptoms of neonatal hypoglycaemia includes which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tachycardia","Irritability","Restlessness","Extreme hunger","Diaphoresis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: TIRED-O stands for Tachycardia, Irritability, Restlessness, Extreme hunger, Diaphoresis, and Other (tremors, lethargy, confusion, seizures, coma).  Why A/B/C/D/E correct: Each letter corresponds to a listed sign/symptom.  Why none excluded: All five options map exactly to T-I-R-E-D of the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In the \"TIRED-O\" mnemonic, the \"E\" stands for:","options":["Edema","Extreme hunger","Erythema","Excitement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In TIRED-O, \"E\" stands for Extreme Hunger.  Why A wrong: Edema is not part of the mnemonic.  Why C wrong: Erythema is not part of the mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Excitement is not the stated meaning."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Neonatal hypoglycaemia is best prevented by:","options":["Delaying feeding until 24 hours of age","Early initiation of breastfeeding within 30 minutes after birth","Immediate IV dextrose in all newborns","Administering insulin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoglycaemia is prevented by early initiation of breastfeeding within 30 minutes after birth.  Why A wrong: Delaying feeding increases the risk of hypoglycaemia.  Why C wrong: Routine IV dextrose in all newborns is not the prevention strategy; breastfeeding is.  Why D wrong: Insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"In a symptomatic hypoglycaemic infant WITH convulsions, the recommended treatment is:","options":["10% dextrose 2–3 ml/kg IV as a bolus","50% dextrose 5 ml/kg IV","Oral glucose gel only","Subcutaneous insulin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a symptomatic infant with convulsions, give 10% dextrose 2–3 ml/kg IV as a bolus.  Why B wrong: 50% dextrose is not used; 10% dextrose is specified.  Why C wrong: Oral gel is inadequate for a convulsing infant.  Why D wrong: Insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"If convulsions are NOT present, hypoglycaemia is treated with 10% dextrose 2 ml/kg IV bolus over one minute, followed by a continuous dextrose infusion at a rate of:","options":["2 to 4 mg/kg/minute","6 to 8 mg/kg/minute","10 to 12 mg/kg/minute","20 to 40 ml/kg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If convulsions are absent, give 10% dextrose 2 ml/kg IV bolus over one minute, followed by continuous dextrose infusion at 6 to 8 mg/kg/minute.  Why A wrong: 2–4 mg/kg/minute is below the stated rate.  Why C wrong: 10–12 mg/kg/minute is above the stated rate.  Why D wrong: 20–40 ml/kg/day is the extra fluid for phototherapy, not the dextrose infusion rate."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During management of neonatal hypoglycaemia, glucose monitoring should be done every:","options":["30 minutes","2 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glucose monitoring should be done every 30 minutes.  Why B wrong: Every 2 hours is the temperature-check interval during phototherapy.  Why C wrong: Every 6 hours is not the stated interval.  Why D wrong: Every 12 hours is the serum bilirubin monitoring interval."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During dextrose infusion for hypoglycaemia, the infusion rate is gradually reduced if the last 2 glucose estimations are:","options":["More than 45 mg/dl","Less than 40 mg/dl","More than 90 mg/dl","Less than 20 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The infusion rate is gradually reduced if the last 2 glucose estimations are more than 45 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: <40 mg/dl is hypoglycaemic and would require more, not less, dextrose.  Why C wrong: >90 mg/dl is not the stated threshold for tapering.  Why D wrong: <20 mg/dl indicates severe hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"If neonatal hypoglycaemia is not treated with dextrose, which additional therapies are initiated? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hydrocortisone","Epinephrine","Glucagon","Insulin","Furosemide"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: If not treated, hydrocortisone, epinephrine, and glucagon therapy are initiated (all raise blood glucose).  Why A/B/C correct: All three are listed rescue therapies.  Why D wrong: Insulin lowers blood glucose and would worsen hypoglycaemia.  Why E wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic, not a glucose-raising therapy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Why may calcium levels decrease in the newborn of a diabetic mother, warranting symptomatic treatment of hypocalcaemia?","options":["Excessive vitamin D intake","Parathyroid glands do not develop properly","Renal failure","Overactive thyroid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the newborn of a diabetic mother, the parathyroid glands do not develop properly, so calcium level may decrease, warranting symptomatic hypocalcaemia treatment.  Why A wrong: Excess vitamin D is not the stated cause.  Why C wrong: Renal failure is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: An overactive thyroid is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which statement about insulin and the placenta is correct, per the text?","options":["Insulin crosses the placenta freely","Insulin is not crossed (does not cross) the placenta","Insulin is produced by the placenta","Insulin destroys the placenta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states insulin is not crossed the placenta (i.e., maternal insulin does not cross to the fetus).  Why A wrong: The text explicitly says it does not cross.  Why C wrong: The placenta does not produce insulin here.  Why D wrong: Insulin does not destroy the placenta."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Which statement regarding hypoglycaemia in the diabetic mother's baby is emphasized in the text?","options":["It is a minor, self-limiting problem","It is a life-threatening condition","It never causes seizures","It occurs only after 72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states hypoglycaemia is a life-threatening condition in the diabetic mother's baby.  Why A wrong: It is life-threatening, not minor.  Why C wrong: Seizures/coma are listed among \"other\" symptoms.  Why D wrong: It can occur within the first 72 hours (<40 mg/dl threshold)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"An infant born to an HIV-infected mother has approximately what chance of infection?","options":["10%","25%","40%","60%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An infant born from an HIV-infected mother has a 40% chance of infection.  Why A wrong: 10% is not the stated overall chance.  Why B wrong: 25% is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 60% is not the stated overall chance (though 60–65% is the intrapartum transmission fraction)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Transmission of HIV from an infected mother's placenta to the fetus is called:","options":["Horizontal transmission","Vertical transmission","Vector-borne transmission","Nosocomial transmission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HIV transmission from mother's placenta to fetus is called vertical transmission.  Why A wrong: Horizontal transmission is person-to-person spread not from parent to offspring.  Why C wrong: Vector-borne transmission involves insects/vectors.  Why D wrong: Nosocomial transmission is hospital-acquired."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"(JIPMER & PGI 2016) Regarding the chance of HIV infection in the newborn, which route is the MOST COMMON?","options":["In utero (30 to 35%)","During delivery (60 to 65%)","Breastfeeding (10 to 15%)","Blood transfusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The chance of infection is in utero 30–35%, during delivery 60–65% (most common), and breastfeeding 10–15%.  Why A wrong: In utero is 30–35%, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Breastfeeding is 10–15%, the lowest of the three.  Why D wrong: Blood transfusion is not the stated vertical route here."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Match the HIV transmission routes with their approximate percentages. In utero transmission accounts for:","options":["10 to 15%","30 to 35%","40 to 45%","60 to 65%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In utero transmission accounts for 30 to 35%.  Why A wrong: 10–15% is the breastfeeding route.  Why C wrong: 40–45% is not a stated value.  Why D wrong: 60–65% is the during-delivery route."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"Why may the ELISA test give false results for HIV in a newborn up to the 18th month of age?","options":["Maternal antibody IgG is present in the newborn","The newborn cannot produce any antibodies","ELISA detects only viral RNA","The test is species-specific"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Until the 18th month of age, maternal antibody IgG is present in the newborn, so the ELISA (antibody) test result may be falsely positive.  Why B wrong: The issue is transferred maternal IgG, not an inability to produce antibodies.  Why C wrong: ELISA here detects antibodies, not viral RNA.  Why D wrong: The false result is due to maternal IgG, not species specificity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"The most reliable or definitive diagnosis of HIV infection in the newborn is the:","options":["ELISA test","p24 antigen test","Indirect Coombs test","Kramer's index"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most reliable/definitive diagnosis of HIV infection in the newborn is the p24 antigen test.  Why A wrong: ELISA (antibody) may give false results up to 18 months due to maternal IgG.  Why C wrong: The indirect Coombs test is for Rh anti-D titre.  Why D wrong: Kramer's index estimates jaundice, not HIV."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"A term newborn on day 2 of life develops visible jaundice of the face and chest. Serum bilirubin is 7 mg/dl and the baby is otherwise feeding and active. Based on the source, this most likely represents:","options":["Physiological jaundice","Pathological jaundice","Kernicterus","Icterus gravis neonatorum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Jaundice appearing after 24 hours in a term newborn with bilirubin in the 5–15 mg/dl range and no illness is consistent with physiological jaundice.  Why B wrong: Pathological jaundice appears within the first 24 hours with bilirubin often >20 mg/dl and direct >2 mg/dl.  Why C wrong: Kernicterus occurs with bilirubin >20 mg/dl crossing the blood-brain barrier.  Why D wrong: Icterus gravis neonatorum is fetal/early death from severe haemolytic disease."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"A neonate under phototherapy has gauze eye pads in place. During feeding time, the appropriate nursing action regarding the eye pads is to:","options":["Keep them on at all times, including feeds","Remove the eye pads during feeds and observe for conjunctivitis","Remove them permanently after 1 hour","Replace them with genital shields"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse should remove eye pads during feeds and observe for conjunctivitis (while covering eyes and genitals otherwise to prevent damage).  Why A wrong: Eye pads are removed during feeds to allow bonding/eye assessment.  Why C wrong: They are removed only during feeds, not permanently at 1 hour.  Why D wrong: Genitals are covered in addition to the eyes, not as a substitute for eye pads."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Pediatric restraints, High Risk Neonates — Neonatal Jaundice, Diagnosis…","question":"During phototherapy, how frequently is breastfeeding recommended to maintain hydration and aid bilirubin excretion?","options":["Once every 8 hours","Frequently, or may be every 2 hours","Only once a day","Every 12 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Provide breastfeeding frequently, or may every 2 hours, during phototherapy (and maintain hydration with IV fluids as needed).  Why A wrong: Every 8 hours is too infrequent.  Why C wrong: Once a day is insufficient and matches weight-check frequency, not feeding.  Why D wrong: Every 12 hours matches serum bilirubin checks, not feeding."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the care of an HIV-positive newborn, which practice regarding feeding is recommended per the source?","options":["Exclusive breastfeeding is encouraged","Breastfeeding is contraindicated","Mixed feeding is preferred","Breastfeeding only after 6 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly states that in HIV-positive newborns, breastfeeding is contraindicated (to reduce vertical transmission risk).  Why A wrong: Breastfeeding is not encouraged because HIV can transmit through breast milk.  Why C wrong: Mixed feeding is not mentioned and increases transmission risk.  Why D wrong: No delayed breastfeeding schedule is given; it is contraindicated outright."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the source, which procedure should be avoided in an HIV-positive male newborn?","options":["Vitamin K injection","Circumcision","Cord clamping","Immunization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Circumcisions should be avoided\" in HIV-positive newborns.  Why A wrong: Vitamin K administration is not listed as contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Cord clamping is routine and not restricted.  Why D wrong: Immunization is given (with specific exceptions), not avoided entirely."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"For an HIV-positive mother whose CD4+ cell count is <200 per ml, from what gestational age should Zidovudine be started until delivery?","options":["From 8 weeks gestation","From 14 weeks gestation","From 20 weeks gestation","From 28 weeks gestation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if the CD4+ count is <200 per ml, Zidovudine is given from 14 weeks gestation till delivery.  Why A/C/D wrong: 8, 20, and 28 weeks are not the values stated; the source specifies 14 weeks gestation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"For how long is Zidovudine given to the infant born to an HIV-positive mother?","options":["2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"For infant: Zidovudine for 6 weeks.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations are not stated; the correct duration is 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a SYMPTOMATIC HIV-positive newborn, which vaccines should be AVOIDED?","options":["OPV only","BCG only","OPV and BCG","All vaccines"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if the newborn is symptomatic, avoid OPV & BCG and may give all other vaccines.  Why A wrong: Both OPV and BCG are avoided in symptomatic infants, not OPV alone.  Why B wrong: BCG is avoided along with OPV, not BCG alone.  Why D wrong: Only OPV and BCG are avoided; all other vaccines may be given."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In an ASYMPTOMATIC HIV-positive newborn, which is the correct vaccination approach?","options":["Avoid OPV; give BCG and all other vaccines","Avoid BCG; give OPV and all other vaccines","Avoid both OPV and BCG","Avoid all live vaccines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that an asymptomatic newborn should avoid OPV and should receive BCG and all other vaccines.  Why B wrong: It is OPV that is avoided, not BCG, in the asymptomatic infant.  Why C wrong: Only OPV is avoided; BCG is given in asymptomatic infants.  Why D wrong: Only OPV is withheld; BCG (a live vaccine) is given."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS) is fundamentally caused by which deficiency?","options":["Deficiency of pulmonary surfactant (a lipoprotein)","Deficiency of albumin","Deficiency of hemoglobin","Deficiency of clotting factors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines RDS as a deficiency or lack of surfactant (lipoprotein) in the lungs resulting in inadequate lung inflation, ventilation hypoxia and acidosis.  Why B wrong: Albumin deficiency causes edema, not RDS.  Why C wrong: Hemoglobin deficiency is anaemia, unrelated to surfactant.  Why D wrong: Clotting factor deficiency causes bleeding disorders, not RDS."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the absence of surfactant, what happens to the newborn's lungs within a few hours after birth?","options":["The lungs hyperinflate","The lungs may collapse","The lungs fill with pus","No change occurs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that without surfactant, the lungs are unable to maintain airway patency, so within a few hours after birth the lungs may collapse.  Why A wrong: The lungs collapse (atelectasis), not hyperinflate, without surfactant.  Why C wrong: Pus filling suggests infection/pneumonia, not surfactant deficiency.  Why D wrong: A significant change (collapse) occurs."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Surfactant is a phospholipid glycoprotein formed by which two substances?","options":["Albumin and globulin","Lecithin and sphingomyelin","Collagen and elastin","Insulin and glucagon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states surfactant is the phospholipid glycoprotein formed by lecithin and sphingomyelin.  Why A wrong: Albumin/globulin are plasma proteins, not surfactant components.  Why C wrong: Collagen and elastin are structural connective tissue proteins.  Why D wrong: Insulin and glucagon are pancreatic hormones."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The normal lecithin:sphingomyelin (L:S) ratio at 32 weeks of gestation is:","options":["1.2 : 1","2 : 1","1 : 1","3 : 1"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states L:S = 1.2 : 1 at 32 weeks.  Why B wrong: 2:1 is the ratio at 36 weeks, not 32 weeks.  Why C/D wrong: 1:1 and 3:1 are not stated for 32 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The normal lecithin:sphingomyelin (L:S) ratio at 36 weeks of gestation is:","options":["1.2 : 1","1.5 : 1","2 : 1","0.5 : 1"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states L:S = 2 : 1 at 36 weeks.  Why A wrong: 1.2:1 is the ratio at 32 weeks.  Why B/D wrong: 1.5:1 and 0.5:1 are not the stated 36-week values."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Surfactant is produced by which cells (a frequently asked fact)?","options":["Pneumocyte type 1 / alveolar type 1 cells","Pneumocyte type 2 / alveolar type 2 cells","Goblet cells","Kupffer cells"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states surfactant is formed by pneumocyte type 2 cells / alveolar type 2 cells.  Why A wrong: Type 1 pneumocytes are for gas exchange, not surfactant production.  Why C wrong: Goblet cells secrete mucus in the airways.  Why D wrong: Kupffer cells are liver macrophages."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Between which gestational weeks is surfactant formed, according to the source?","options":["10 to 20 weeks","20 to 35 weeks","35 to 40 weeks","15 to 25 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the surfactant is formed between 20 to 35 weeks.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges are not stated; the correct range is 20 to 35 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Pneumocyte type 2 cells are stimulated by which substance for the formation of surfactant?","options":["Insulin","Steroids","Antibiotics","Antihistamines"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pneumocyte type 2 cells are stimulated by steroids for the formation of surfactant.  Why A wrong: Insulin (as in infants of diabetic mothers) depresses surfactant function.  Why C/D wrong: Antibiotics and antihistamines do not stimulate surfactant formation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"An L/S ratio of which value or more indicates fetal lung maturity and a relatively low risk of RDS?","options":["1 : 1 or more","1.2 : 1 or more","2 : 1 or more","0.5 : 1 or more"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an L:S ratio of 2:1 or more indicates fetal lung maturity and a relatively low risk of infant RDS.  Why A/B/D wrong: These lower ratios do not indicate lung maturity; 2:1 is the maturity threshold."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"An L/S ratio of less than which value is associated with a high risk of infant respiratory distress syndrome?","options":["Less than 2:1","Less than 1.5:1","Less than 3:1","Less than 2.5:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an L/S ratio of less than 1.5:1 is associated with a high risk of infant RDS.  Why A wrong: Less than 2:1 is not the stated high-risk cutoff; the specific value is 1.5:1.  Why C/D wrong: 3:1 and 2.5:1 are not the stated high-risk thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which drug is considered the drug of choice in the management of RDS (given antenatally)?","options":["Betamethasone","Ampicillin","Furosemide","Digoxin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states betamethasone is considered the drug of choice in RDS.  Why B wrong: Ampicillin is an antibiotic, not for lung maturity.  Why C wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic.  Why D wrong: Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are risk conditions for RDS according to the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pre-term newborn","Newborn of a diabetic mother","Post-term newborn as the primary risk","Hyperinsulinemia depressing surfactant function","Maternal hypothyroidism"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists pre-term newborn and newborn of a diabetic mother (in whom fetal hyperinsulinemia depresses surfactant function) as risk conditions for RDS.  Why C wrong: Post-term newborns are associated with MAS, not primarily RDS.  Why E wrong: Maternal hypothyroidism is not listed as an RDS risk condition on the page."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Within what time frame do the symptoms of RDS typically appear after birth?","options":["Within 1 hour","Within 5 to 6 hours","After 24 hours","After 48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the symptoms of RDS may appear within 5 to 6 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These time frames are not stated; the correct window is 5 to 6 hours."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of RDS as listed by the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Nasal flaring","Intercostal retractions","Grunting sound","Tachypnoea and cyanosis","Bradycardia with warm extremities"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists nasal flaring, intercostal retractions, grunting sound, tachypnoea, dyspnoea, cyanosis, and metabolic acidosis as RDS symptoms.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia with warm extremities is not listed; RDS features tachypnoea and cyanosis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the steroid (glucocorticoid) therapy for RDS, betamethasone is given at what dose and schedule?","options":["6 mg IM every 12 h, 4 doses","12 mg IM every 24 h, 2 doses","12 mg IM every 12 h, 2 doses","6 mg IM every 24 h, 4 doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states betamethasone: 12 mg, through IM route, every 24 h, 2 doses.  Why A wrong: That is the dexamethasone regimen (6 mg every 12 h, 4 doses).  Why C/D wrong: These mix up the dose/interval/number of doses for betamethasone."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the steroid therapy for RDS, dexamethasone is given at what dose and schedule?","options":["12 mg IM every 24 h, 2 doses","6 mg IM every 12 h, 4 doses","6 mg IM every 24 h, 2 doses","12 mg IM every 12 h, 4 doses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states dexamethasone: 6 mg, through IM route, every 12 h, 4 doses.  Why A wrong: That is the betamethasone regimen.  Why C/D wrong: These mix up the dose, interval, or number of doses for dexamethasone."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"By which route are surfactants replaced in the newborn with RDS?","options":["Intravenous route","Intra tracheal route","Oral route","Subcutaneous route"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states surfactants are replaced by the intra tracheal route.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the intra tracheal route, not IV, oral, or SC."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"What is the purpose of CPAP (continuous positive airway pressure) in the management of RDS?","options":["To prevent collapsing of the lung alveoli","To feed the newborn","To warm the newborn","To sterilize the airway"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CPAP may be used for prevention of collapsing of lung alveoli.  Why B/C/D wrong: CPAP is a respiratory support measure, not for feeding, warming, or sterilizing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The antenatal steroid dose given to the mother is 12 mg OD by IM route for 2 days at what gestational age (because steroids cross the placenta and stimulate type 2 cells of lung alveoli)?","options":["24 to 28 weeks","28 to 32 weeks","32 to 36 weeks","36 to 40 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states drug dose 12 mg OD for 2 days by IM route in the mother at 28 to 32 weeks, because steroids cross the placenta and stimulate the type 2 cells of lung alveoli.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 28 to 32 weeks, not these ranges."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"If betamethasone is not available for RDS prophylaxis, which drug can be used as an alternative?","options":["Hydrocortisone","Dexamethasone","Prednisolone","Methylprednisolone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if betamethasone is not available then dexamethasone can also be used as an alternative option.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies dexamethasone as the alternative, not these other steroids."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"What is the minimum interval that should elapse between antenatal steroid administration to the mother and delivery for better results?","options":["12 hours","24 hours","48 hours","72 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the minimum interval between the drug administration to the mother and delivery should be 48 hours for better results.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 48 hours, not these intervals."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A pre-term newborn is defined as a neonate born before how many weeks of gestation?","options":["40 weeks","38 weeks","37 weeks","34 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a neonate born before 37 weeks of gestation is called pre-term newborn.  Why A/B/D wrong: The cutoff for pre-term is before 37 weeks, not 40, 38, or 34 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the classification of pre-term newborns, \"Extremely Pre-Term\" corresponds to which gestational age?","options":["<28 weeks","28 to 32 weeks","32 to 37 weeks","<24 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table states Extremely Pre-Term = <28 weeks.  Why B wrong: 28 to 32 weeks is \"Very Pre-Term.\"  Why C wrong: 32-37 weeks is \"Moderate to late Pre-term.\"  Why D wrong: <24 weeks is not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"\"Very Pre-Term\" is defined as which gestational age range?","options":["<28 weeks","28 to 32 weeks","32 to 37 weeks","34 to 37 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table states Very Pre-Term = 28 to 32 weeks.  Why A wrong: <28 weeks is Extremely Pre-Term.  Why C wrong: 32-37 weeks is Moderate to late Pre-term.  Why D wrong: 34 to 37 weeks is not the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"\"Moderate to late Pre-term\" is defined as which gestational age range?","options":["<28 weeks","28 to 32 weeks","32 to 37 weeks","37 to 40 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's table states Moderate to late Pre-term = 32-37 weeks.  Why A wrong: <28 weeks is Extremely Pre-Term.  Why B wrong: 28 to 32 weeks is Very Pre-Term.  Why D wrong: 37-40 weeks would be term, not pre-term."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The birth weight of a pre-term newborn is typically:","options":["More than 4 kg","2.5 kg or less than 2.5 kg","Exactly 3 kg","3.5 kg or more"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the birth weight of a pre-term newborn is 2.5 kg or less than 2.5 kg.  Why A/C/D wrong: These higher weights are not characteristic of pre-term newborns."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a pre-term newborn, the difference between head circumference (H.C.) and chest circumference (C.C.) becomes more than how many cm?","options":["1 cm","2 cm","3 cm","5 cm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states H.C. is decreased and C.C. is also decreased, but the difference between H.C. and C.C. becomes more than 3 cm.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies more than 3 cm, not 1, 2, or 5 cm."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a pre-term newborn, the crown-heel length is typically less than:","options":["40 cm","47 cm","50 cm","55 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the crown-heel length is less than 47 cm in the pre-term newborn.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies less than 47 cm, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Regarding breast nodules in a pre-term newborn, the source states they are:","options":["Well-developed, >10 mm","Absent or <5 mm","Exactly 5 cm","Enlarged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In a pre-term newborn the breast nodule (breast bud) is absent or <5 mm; a term newborn has a ~5-10 mm nodule. [Unit corrected: breast bud is measured in millimetres, not centimetres.]  Why A wrong: A well-developed nodule >10 mm indicates a term/post-term infant.  Why C wrong: \"5 cm\" is an implausible size (unit error); the relevant threshold is ~5 mm.  Why D wrong: An enlarged nodule is not characteristic of prematurity."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are assessment features of a pre-term newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Vernix caseosa covers the entire body","Soft ear cartilage, immature palm and creases","Short nails, poor sucking, weak neonatal reflexes, undescended testes","Scarf sign is positive","Deep sole creases over the entire foot"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists vernix caseosa covering the entire body, soft ear cartilage/immature palm & creases, short nails/poor sucking/weak reflexes/undescended testes, and positive scarf sign as pre-term features.  Why E wrong: Deep sole creases are a term-newborn feature; pre-term feet have few creases."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Why is a pre-term newborn prone to hypothermia?","options":["Excess brown fat","Improper thermoregulation","Overactive thyroid","High metabolic rate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-term newborn is prone to hypothermia due to improper thermoregulation.  Why A wrong: Pre-term infants have a lack of subcutaneous/brown tissue, not excess.  Why C/D wrong: Thyroid overactivity and high metabolic rate are not the stated reasons."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A pre-term newborn is prone to develop atelectasis or Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS) due to:","options":["Improper production of surfactant","Excess surfactant","Meconium aspiration","Congenital heart disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pre-term newborns are prone to atelectasis or RDS due to improper production of surfactant.  Why B wrong: Excess surfactant does not cause RDS; deficiency does.  Why C wrong: Meconium aspiration is a post-term concern.  Why D wrong: Congenital heart disease is not the stated cause of RDS here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The Scarf Sign is used as which type of maturity scale in the newborn?","options":["Biochemical maturity scale","Neuromuscular maturity scale","Cardiovascular maturity scale","Renal maturity scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the scarf sign is a neuromuscular maturity scale of the newborn.  Why A/C/D wrong: The scarf sign assesses neuromuscular tone, not biochemical, cardiovascular, or renal maturity."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"During the scarf sign test, the newborn is placed in which position before bringing the elbow to the opposite side by slightly stretching?","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Lateral position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the newborn is placed in supine position and then the elbow is brought to the opposite side by slightly stretching.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies supine position, not prone, lateral, or Trendelenburg."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"During the scarf sign, if the elbow CROSSES the midline of the body, the infant is considered:","options":["Term baby","Pre-term infant","Post-term baby","Large for gestational age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the elbow crosses the midline of the body, this is considered a pre-term infant.  Why A wrong: If the elbow does NOT cross the midline, the baby is considered term.  Why C/D wrong: Post-term and LGA are not determined by the scarf sign in this manner."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"During the scarf sign, if the elbow DOES NOT cross the midline of the body, the infant is considered:","options":["Pre-term","Term baby","Extremely pre-term","Post-mature with MAS"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the elbow does not cross the midline, the baby is considered a term baby.  Why A/C wrong: Elbow crossing midline indicates pre-term/extremely pre-term.  Why D wrong: The scarf sign result of not crossing midline indicates term, not post-mature with MAS."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A pre-term infant has poor thermoregulation primarily due to lack of which tissues, making it prone to hypothermia?","options":["Muscle and bone tissue","Subcutaneous tissue and brown tissue (brown fat)","Cartilage and connective tissue","Nervous tissue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-term infant has poor thermoregulation due to lack of subcutaneous tissue and brown tissue, so it is prone to hypothermia; maintain body temperature using a radiant warmer.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies subcutaneous and brown tissue, not muscle/bone, cartilage, or nervous tissue."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which device is recommended to maintain body temperature in a pre-term infant with poor thermoregulation?","options":["Ice packs","Radiant warmer","Cooling blanket","Fan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to maintain body temperature by using a radiant warmer.  Why A/C/D wrong: Ice packs, cooling blankets, and fans would worsen hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are nursing care measures for the pre-term newborn per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Frequently monitor vital signs, colour, and I/O chart","Suctioning of mucus to maintain airway after birth","Intubation and tracheal suctioning to initiate respiration","Suctioning of stomach content to facilitate respiration","Withhold all nutrition until 1 week of age"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists frequent monitoring of vitals/colour/I-O, suctioning of mucus, intubation/tracheal suctioning, and suctioning of stomach content as nursing care measures.  Why E wrong: The source says to maintain nutrition, not withhold it for a week."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A post-term newborn is defined as a neonate born at how many days (weeks) or more from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP)?","options":["280 days (40 weeks)","294 days (42 weeks)","259 days (37 weeks)","266 days (38 weeks)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines post-term as 294 days (42 weeks) or more from the first day of LMP, or >13 days from the EDD.  Why A wrong: 280 days (40 weeks) is full term, not post-term.  Why C/D wrong: 259 and 266 days are pre-term/early term ranges."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Post-term is also defined as how many days more than the estimated due date (EDD)?","options":["> 7 days","> 10 days","> 13 days","> 20 days"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states post-term is >13 days from the estimated due date (EDD).  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies >13 days, not 7, 10, or 20 days."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The term \"desquamation or parchment-like skin\" in a post-term newborn refers to:","options":["Skin covered with thick vernix","Dry and cracked skin without lanugo","Bluish cold skin","Edematous shiny skin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states desquamation or parchment-like skin means dry and cracked skin without lanugo.  Why A wrong: Thick vernix is a feature of term/pre-term, not post-term desquamation.  Why C/D wrong: Bluish cold or edematous skin describe hypothermia, not parchment-like post-term skin."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are assessment features of a post-term newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Long thin body with muscle wasting and hypoglycaemia","Absence of vernix caseosa with long nails","Oligohydramnios and macrosomia","Meconium aspiration syndrome (MAS) and placental calcification","Abundant vernix covering the entire body"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists long thin body/muscle wasting/hypoglycaemia, absence of vernix caseosa, long nails, oligohydramnios, macrosomia, MAS, and placental calcification as post-term features.  Why E wrong: Abundant vernix covering the entire body is a pre-term feature; post-term has absence of vernix."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The nursing care for a post-term newborn specifically emphasizes monitoring for which two conditions?","options":["Hypoglycaemia and temperature","Hyperglycemia and hypertension","Jaundice and anaemia","Seizures and apnea only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to provide normal newborn care and monitor hypoglycaemia and temperature.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically emphasizes hypoglycaemia and temperature monitoring for post-term newborns."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, what is the best sleeping position for a baby admitted to the NICU under observation?","options":["Supine position","Prone position","Left lateral position","Sitting position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the best position during sleep for a baby under NICU observation is the prone position, as it makes the neonate more comfortable, less cry, and reduces the chance of aspiration.  Why A wrong: Supine is the normal position for normal newborns, but the box specifies prone for NICU-observed babies.  Why C/D wrong: Left lateral and sitting are not the stated best positions."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Why should the prone position NOT be given to a baby who is unsupervised or in the absence of the mother?","options":["It causes reflux","It may cause SIDS (Sudden Infant Death Syndrome)","It causes hip dislocation","It causes torticollis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this position should never be given when unsupervised or in the absence of the mother because it may cause SIDS.  Why A/C/D wrong: Reflux, hip dislocation, and torticollis are not the stated risks of unsupervised prone positioning here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which scoring system is used for estimating the gestational age of babies?","options":["APGAR score","Dubowitz scoring system","Glasgow Coma Scale","Silverman Anderson score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Dubowitz scoring system is used for estimating the gestational age of babies.  Why A wrong: APGAR assesses physiological condition after birth, not gestational age.  Why C wrong: Glasgow Coma Scale assesses level of consciousness.  Why D wrong: Silverman Anderson score assesses respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The Ballard score is used as which type of scale for the neonate?","options":["Pain assessment scale","Gestational maturation scale","Nutritional assessment scale","Jaundice assessment scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Ballard score is used as a gestational maturation scale for the neonate.  Why A/C/D wrong: Ballard score assesses gestational maturity, not pain, nutrition, or jaundice."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The APGAR score is used for which purpose?","options":["Estimating gestational age","Assessment of physiological conditions of the newborn after labour","Measuring blood glucose","Assessing hearing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the APGAR score is used for assessment of physiological conditions of the newborn after labour.  Why A wrong: Gestational age is estimated by Dubowitz/Ballard scores.  Why C/D wrong: APGAR does not measure blood glucose or hearing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following physical characteristics belong to a TERM newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Head and body are proportionate in size","Fully flexed in position (normal position)","Deep sole creases are present","Well-developed scrotum with descended testis in male","Superficial veins visible on abdomen and scalp"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists proportionate head/body, full flexion, deep sole creases, and well-developed descended-testis scrotum as term-newborn characteristics.  Why E wrong: Superficial veins visible on abdomen and scalp is a pre-term characteristic."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a TERM newborn, during the elbow (scarf) maneuver, the elbow may be brought to the midline of the chest with:","options":["No resistance at all","Resistance beyond midline","Easy crossing well past midline","Complete flaccidity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for a term newborn, the elbow may be brought to the midline of the chest with resistance beyond midline.  Why A/C/D wrong: Easy crossing/no resistance/flaccidity are pre-term features (sluggish reflexes, scarf sign positive)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are physical characteristics of a POST-TERM newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dry, loose, peeling skin","Long nails and hairs with large amount of hair on the head","Little vernix and lanugo","More alert and \"wide-eyed\"","Abundant vernix caseosa with lanugo covering forehead and shoulders"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists dry/loose/peeling skin, long nails/hairs, large amount of scalp hair, little vernix/lanugo, and \"more alert and wide-eyed\" as post-term characteristics.  Why E wrong: Abundant vernix with lanugo covering forehead/shoulders is a pre-term characteristic."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a post-term newborn, green, brown, or yellow coloring of the skin (meconium staining) indicates that the baby:","options":["Has jaundice from liver disease","Passed stool (meconium) in the womb","Has a skin infection","Is dehydrated"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the green, brown, or yellow coloring of skin is from the baby passing stool in the womb (meconium stained).  Why A wrong: This coloring is from meconium staining, not liver-related jaundice.  Why C/D wrong: Skin infection and dehydration are not the stated cause of meconium staining."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the source note (AIIMS-2019), which condition is each newborn type more likely to develop?","options":["Preterm → MAS; Post-term → RDS","Preterm → RDS due to surfactant deficiency; Post-term → MAS","Both develop RDS equally","Both develop MAS equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source note states preterm babies are more likely to develop RDS (due to surfactant deficiency) whereas post-term babies are more likely to develop MAS.  Why A wrong: This reverses the correct associations.  Why C/D wrong: The two types have different predominant complications, not equal."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Per the World Health Organization (WHO), Low Birth Weight (LBW) is defined as a birth weight of less than:","options":["3000 g (up to 2999 g)","2500 g (up to and including 2499 g)","2000 g","1500 g"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LBW is defined as a birth weight of less than 2500 g (up to and including 2499 g) per WHO.  Why A wrong: 3000 g is not the LBW cutoff.  Why C wrong: 2000 g is not the LBW cutoff.  Why D wrong: <1500 g defines Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A \"Very Low Birth Weight Baby (VLBW)\" is defined by which weight?","options":["<2.5 kg or <2500 gm","<1.5 kg or <1500 gm","<1 kg or <1000 gm","<2 kg or <2000 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's terminology table states VLBW = <1.5 kg or <1500 gm.  Why A wrong: <2.5 kg/<2500 gm defines LBW.  Why C wrong: <1 kg/<1000 gm defines ELBW.  Why D wrong: <2 kg is not a stated category."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"An \"Extremely Low Birth Weight Baby (ELBW)\" is defined by which weight?","options":["<2.5 kg or <2500 gm","<1.5 kg or <1500 gm","<1 kg or <1000 gm","<0.5 kg or <500 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's terminology table states ELBW = <1 kg or <1000 gm.  Why A wrong: <2.5 kg defines LBW.  Why B wrong: <1.5 kg defines VLBW.  Why D wrong: <0.5 kg is not a stated category."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are MATERNAL causes of Low Birth Weight (LBW)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Malnutrition and anaemia","Hypertensive disorders (gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, eclampsia)","TORCH infections","Smoking and drug abuse","Chromosomal abnormalities of the fetus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists malnutrition/anaemia, hypertensive disorders, TORCH infections, and smoking/drug abuse (Raj CHO-2020) as maternal causes of LBW.  Why E wrong: Chromosomal abnormalities are a FETAL cause, not a maternal cause."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a FETAL cause of Low Birth Weight?","options":["Placental insufficiency","IUGR, chromosomal abnormalities, multiple pregnancy","Maternal smoking","Gestational hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists IUGR, chromosomal abnormalities, and multiple pregnancy as fetal causes of LBW.  Why A wrong: Placental insufficiency is a placental cause.  Why C/D wrong: Smoking and gestational hypertension are maternal causes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which is identified as the PLACENTAL cause of Low Birth Weight?","options":["Placental insufficiency","Multiple pregnancy","Maternal anaemia","IUGR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists placental insufficiency as the placental cause of LBW.  Why B/D wrong: Multiple pregnancy and IUGR are fetal causes.  Why C wrong: Maternal anaemia is a maternal cause."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Newborns are classified based on maturity and intrauterine growth as SGA and LGA. \"SGA\" stands for:","options":["Standard Gestational Age","Small for Gestational Age","Severe Growth Abnormality","Slow Growing Adaptation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source classifies newborns as SGA (Small for Gestational Age) and LGA based on maturity and intrauterine growth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the correct expansions of SGA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Small for Gestational Age (SGA) is defined as a weight that is:","options":["2 SD above the mean weight for GA / above the 90th percentile","2 SD below the mean weight for GA / below the 10th percentile","Exactly at the 50th percentile","Between the 10th and 90th percentiles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines SGA as 2 standard deviations below the mean weight for GA or weight below the 10th percentile of gestational age.  Why A wrong: That defines LGA.  Why C wrong: 50th percentile is average, not SGA.  Why D wrong: 10th-90th percentile defines AGA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are newborn complications associated with SGA? (Select all that apply)","options":["Signs of failure to thrive","Hypoglycaemia and hypothermia","Polycythemia and birth asphyxia","Intrauterine death (IUD), infections, congenital malformations","Macrosomia with birth weight >4000 gm"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists failure to thrive, hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, polycythemia, birth asphyxia, IUD, infections, and congenital malformations as SGA newborn complications.  Why E wrong: Macrosomia with weight >4000 gm is an LGA feature, not SGA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Using the source's example, at 28 weeks the normal fetal weight is 1.5 kg. If at 28 weeks the fetus weighs 1 kg, it is considered:","options":["Large for Gestational Age","Small for Gestational Age","Appropriate for Gestational Age","Normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's example states that if at 28 weeks the fetus weighs 1 kg (below the normal 1.5 kg), it is considered Small for Gestational Age.  Why A wrong: LGA would be if weight is higher than normal (e.g., 2 kg at 28 weeks).  Why C/D wrong: A weight below normal is not appropriate/normal for gestational age."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Large for Gestational Age (LGA) is defined as a weight that is:","options":["2 SD below the mean / below the 10th percentile","2 SD above the mean weight for GA / above the 90th percentile","Below the 50th percentile","Exactly at the 25th percentile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines LGA as 2 standard deviations above the mean weight for GA or weight above the 90th percentile of gestational age.  Why A wrong: That defines SGA.  Why C/D wrong: These lower percentiles do not define LGA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"LGA newborns are born with an abnormally high weight of:","options":[">2500 gm",">3000 gm",">4000 gm",">5000 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LGA newborns are born with abnormally high weight (>4000 gm).  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies >4000 gm, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are NEWBORN complications of LGA? (Select all that apply)","options":["Shoulder dystocia","Hypoglycemia","Brachial plexus injuries","Hip subluxation and talipes calcaneovalgus","Failure to thrive due to being underweight"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists shoulder dystocia, hypoglycemia, brachial plexus injuries, hip subluxation, and talipes calcaneovalgus (due to intrauterine deformation) as LGA newborn complications.  Why E wrong: Failure to thrive from being underweight is an SGA complication, not LGA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are MATERNAL complications associated with delivering an LGA newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Emergency caesarean section","Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)","Obstetric anal sphincter injury","Placental insufficiency causing IUGR","Reduced amniotic fluid only"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists emergency caesarean section, PPH, and obstetric anal sphincter injury as maternal complications of LGA.  Why D wrong: Placental insufficiency/IUGR is associated with LBW/SGA, not LGA maternal complications.  Why E wrong: Reduced amniotic fluid is not listed as an LGA maternal complication."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are risk factors for LGA per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mother with diabetes","Maternal obesity","Postterm pregnancy","Previous history and genetics","Maternal malnutrition and anaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists mother with diabetes, obesity, postterm pregnancy, previous history, and genetics as LGA risk factors.  Why E wrong: Maternal malnutrition and anaemia are causes of LBW/SGA, not LGA."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Using the source's example, at 28 weeks the normal fetal weight is 1.5 kg. If at 28 weeks the fetus weighs 2 kg, it is considered:","options":["Small for Gestational Age","Large for Gestational Age","Appropriate for Gestational Age","Normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's example states that if at 28 weeks the fetus weighs 2 kg (above the normal 1.5 kg), it is considered Large for Gestational Age.  Why A wrong: SGA would be a weight below normal (e.g., 1 kg).  Why C/D wrong: A weight above normal is not appropriate/normal for gestational age."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Appropriate for Gestational Age (AGA) is defined as a newborn between which percentiles of gestational age?","options":["Between the 5th and 50th percentiles","Between the 10th and 90th percentiles","Below the 10th percentile","Above the 90th percentile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source note defines AGA as a newborn between the 10th to 90th percentiles of gestational age.  Why C wrong: Below the 10th percentile is SGA.  Why D wrong: Above the 90th percentile is LGA.  Why A wrong: The stated range is 10th-90th, not 5th-50th."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Why should all procedures in the NICU be carried out under proper aseptic technique?","options":["Because newborns need extra warmth","Because there is a higher chance of infection due to improper (immature) immune power of the newborn","Because it saves time","Because it reduces feeding needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states all NICU procedures should be under aseptic technique because there is a higher chance of infection in these newborns due to improper immune power.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated reason is infection risk from immature immunity, not warmth, time, or feeding."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are measures for \"Maintenance of Breathing\" in high-risk neonate management? (Select all that apply)","options":["Suctioning","Provide supine position with slightly neck extended","Provide stimulation and administer oxygen therapy","Provide positive pressure ventilation (bag and mask or mechanical ventilation if required)","Place the neonate prone and withhold oxygen"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists suctioning, supine position with slightly neck extended, stimulation, oxygen therapy, and positive pressure ventilation (bag/mask or mechanical) under Maintenance of Breathing.  Why E wrong: The source specifies supine with slightly neck extended and giving oxygen, not prone with withheld oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"For maintenance of temperature in a high-risk neonate, which devices may be used?","options":["Radiant warmer and incubator","Ice bath and fan","Cold compress","Nebulizer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states radiant warmer and incubator may be used for maintenance of temperature.  Why B/C wrong: Ice bath, fan, and cold compress would cause hypothermia.  Why D wrong: A nebulizer delivers respiratory medication, not temperature control."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the sample cow's milk preparation for a baby, what is the correct recipe for 24 hours?","options":["250 ml cow's milk + 250 ml water + 4 tsp sugar","500 ml cow's milk + 100 ml water + 2 tsp sugar","250 ml cow's milk + 100 ml water + 1 tsp sugar","100 ml cow's milk + 250 ml water + 4 tsp sugar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the example: 250 ml cow's milk + 250 ml water + 4 tsp sugar for 24 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These alter the proportions; the source specifies equal parts milk and water (250 ml each) plus 4 tsp sugar."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"When cow's milk is used for a high-risk neonate, how should it be prepared?","options":["Given undiluted","Diluted with an equal amount of water, then add sugar","Diluted with an equal amount of glucose saline","Mixed with formula only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if cow's milk is used, dilute it with an equal amount of water and then add sugar.  Why A wrong: Undiluted cow's milk is not recommended.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies dilution with water and adding sugar, not glucose saline or formula."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A high-risk neonate with weak swallowing reflexes should be started on which type of feeding?","options":["Oral bottle feeding","Gavage feeding","Intravenous nutrition only","Spoon feeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states weak swallowing reflexes so start gavage feeding.  Why A/D wrong: Oral bottle and spoon feeding are unsafe with weak swallowing (aspiration risk).  Why C wrong: The source recommends gavage (tube) feeding, not IV nutrition only."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The normal caloric requirement for a TERM newborn is:","options":["60-80 calories/kg or 30-40 calorie/lb","110-130 calories/kg or 50-60 calorie/lb","150-200 calories/kg or 100 calorie/lb","90-100 calories/kg or 45 calorie/lb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states normally calories for a term newborn is 110-130 calories/kg or 50-60 calorie/lb.  Why A/C/D wrong: These values are not stated for term newborns; the correct range is 110-130 cal/kg or 50-60 cal/lb."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"For a PRE-TERM baby, the caloric requirement should be increased to (AIIMS-2020):","options":["110-130 calories/kg or 50-60 calorie/lb","120-150 calories/kg or 100 calorie/lb","60-80 calories/kg or 40 calorie/lb","200-250 calories/kg or 150 calorie/lb"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for a pre-term baby, calories should be increased to 120-150 calories/kg or 100 calorie/lb for adequate growth and metabolism (AIIMS-2020).  Why A wrong: 110-130 cal/kg is the term newborn requirement.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated pre-term values."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"What is the daily fluid requirement for all newborns per the source?","options":["100 ml/kg day","150 ml/kg day","200 ml/kg day","60 ml/kg day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for all newborns, required fluids: 150 ml/kg day.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 150 ml/kg/day for all newborns; the other values apply to specific preterm weight categories or are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"For a preterm baby weighing MORE than 1500 gm, the fluid requirement is:","options":["60 ml/kg/day","80 ml/kg/day","150 ml/kg/day","120 ml/kg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the preterm baby's weight is >1500 gm, fluids = 80 ml/kg/day.  Why A wrong: 60 ml/kg/day is for babies <1500 gm.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated values for >1500 gm."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"For a preterm baby weighing LESS than 1500 gm, the starting fluid requirement is 60 ml/kg/day, gradually increased by 15 ml/day until which day, after which 150 ml/kg/day should be started?","options":["3rd day","5th day","7th day","10th day"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for a preterm baby <1500 gm, 60 ml/kg/day gradually increased 15 ml/day till the 7th day, after which 150 ml/kg/day should start for all neonates.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies the 7th day, not the 3rd, 5th, or 10th day."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"How much ADDITIONAL fluid is required if a baby is on phototherapy or a radiant warmer?","options":["5-10 ml/kg/day","20-30 ml/kg/day","50-60 ml/kg/day","100 ml/kg/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states additional fluid of 20-30 ml/kg/day is also required if the baby is on phototherapy or a radiant warmer.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 20-30 ml/kg/day of additional fluid, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Regarding vaccination of high-risk neonates, which statement is correct per the source?","options":["All live vaccines are contraindicated","There are no contraindications for any type of vaccines; all vaccines are given per the national immunization schedule","Only killed vaccines can be given","Vaccination should be delayed until 1 year"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there are no contraindications for any type of vaccines, and all vaccines are administered according to the national immunization schedule.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states no contraindications, so live vaccines are not banned, vaccination is not limited to killed vaccines, and it is not delayed to 1 year."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"During transportation of a newborn, which is described as a major complication?","options":["Hyperthermia","Hypothermia","Dehydration","Hemorrhage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states during transportation of a newborn, hypothermia is a major complication that occurs during transportation.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically identifies hypothermia, not hyperthermia, dehydration, or hemorrhage."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which is described as the best option for temperature regulation during transportation of a newborn?","options":["Incubator","Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC)","Radiant warmer","Warm water bottle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states KMC is the best option for temperature regulation during transportation.  Why A/C wrong: Incubators and radiant warmers are used in the NICU, but KMC is specified as best for transportation.  Why D wrong: Warm water bottles are not recommended (burn risk)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the Key Points, the choice of position for a neonate to maintain a patent airway is:","options":["Prone","Supine with slightly neck extended","Left lateral","Full flexion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Key Points state the choice of position for neonates for patent airway is supine with slightly neck extended.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated airway position is supine with slightly neck extended, not prone, lateral, or full flexion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The normal position for a normal newborn baby is:","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Sitting position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Key Points state the normal position for normal newborn babies is supine position.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated normal position is supine, not prone, sitting, or Trendelenburg."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The position used in tracheostomy in newborns, children, and adults is:","options":["Prone","Supine with slightly neck extended","Left lateral","High Fowler's"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Key Points state the position used in tracheostomy in newborns, children, and adults is supine with slightly neck extended.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stated tracheostomy position is supine with slightly neck extended."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the Key Points, a tonsillectomy should be done through which route?","options":["Direct oral cavity","Nasal cavity","Endoscopic transnasal route","External neck incision"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's Key Points state tonsillectomy should be done through the direct oral cavity.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the direct oral cavity route, not nasal, endoscopic transnasal, or external neck incision."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The normal temperature of a newborn should be maintained between which range?","options":["35.0°C - 36.0°C","36.5°C - 37.4°C","37.5°C - 38.5°C","34.0°C - 35.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states normal temperature of the newborn is to be maintained between 36.5°C-37.4°C.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 36.5-37.4°C, not these ranges."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Hypothermia in a newborn is defined as a temperature less than (AIIMS Rai Barely-2018):","options":["37.4°C","36.5°C","35.0°C","34.5°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the temperature of the newborn is less than 36.5°C, it is called hypothermia (AIIMS Rai Barely-2018).  Why A wrong: 37.4°C is the upper normal limit.  Why C/D wrong: These are within the moderate/severe hypothermia ranges, not the defining cutoff of <36.5°C."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"When measuring axillary temperature in a newborn, the thermometer should be read after how many minutes?","options":["1 minute","3 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in axillary temperature, the temperature should be read after 5 minutes.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 5 minutes, not 1, 3, or 10 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"When a newborn's feet are cold but the abdomen is warm, this indicates:","options":["Normal (normothermic) baby","Cold stress","Severe hypothermia","Hyperthermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states when feet are cold and abdomen is warm, it indicates that the baby is in cold stress.  Why A wrong: A normothermic baby has both abdomen and feet warm.  Why C wrong: In hypothermia, both feet and abdomen are cold.  Why D wrong: These findings indicate cold stress, not hyperthermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a newborn with hypothermia, the touch findings are:","options":["Warm feet and warm abdomen","Both feet and abdomen cold to touch","Warm feet and cold abdomen","Warm abdomen and cold feet only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in hypothermia, both feet and abdomen are cold to touch.  Why A wrong: Both warm indicates a normothermic baby.  Why D wrong: Cold feet with warm abdomen indicates cold stress, not established hypothermia.  Why C wrong: This pattern is not described for hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A baby's surface temperature is assessed by touch with which part of the examiner's hand?","options":["Palmar surface of the hand","Dorsal surface of the hand","Fingertips","Back of the wrist"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the baby's surface temperature is assessed by touch with the dorsal surface of the hand on the abdomen, hands, and feet.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the dorsal surface of the hand, not palmar, fingertips, or wrist."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the WHO Classification of hypothermia, the \"Normal\" category corresponds to which temperature range?","options":["36.5 to 37.4°C","36 to 36.4°C","32 to 35.9°C","Less than 32°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's WHO Classification table lists Normal = 36.5 to 37.4°C (warm trunk, warm extremities).  Why B wrong: 36 to 36.4°C is mild hypothermia (cold stress).  Why C wrong: 32 to 35.9°C is moderate hypothermia.  Why D wrong: Less than 32°C is severe hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the WHO Classification, \"Mild hypothermia (Cold stress)\" (NORCET-2021) corresponds to which temperature range and touch finding?","options":["36.5 to 37.4°C; warm trunk and extremities","36 to 36.4°C; warm trunk, cold extremities","32 to 35.9°C; cold trunk and extremities","Less than 32°C; cold trunk and extremities"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Mild hypothermia (Cold stress) = 36 to 36.4°C with warm trunk and cold extremities (NORCET-2021).  Why A wrong: 34.5-37.4°C with warm trunk/extremities is Normal.  Why C wrong: 32-35.9°C is moderate hypothermia.  Why D wrong: <32°C is severe hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The clinical features of MILD hypothermia (cold stress) include:","options":["Poor sucking, lethargy, weak cry","Extremities bluish and cold, poor weight gain if chronic cold stress","Hardening of skin with redness and edema","Slow heart rate and bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists extremities bluish and cold, and poor weight gain if chronic cold stress, as clinical features of mild hypothermia.  Why A wrong: Poor sucking, lethargy, weak cry are features of moderate hypothermia.  Why C/D wrong: Hardening of skin, redness, edema, slow heart rate, and bleeding are features of severe hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the WHO Classification, \"Moderate hypothermia\" corresponds to which temperature range?","options":["36 to 36.4°C","32 to 35.9°C","36.5 to 37.4°C","Less than 32°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Moderate hypothermia = 32 to 35.9°C (cold trunk, cold extremities).  Why A wrong: 36-36.4°C is mild hypothermia.  Why C wrong: 34.5-37.4°C is Normal.  Why D wrong: <32°C is severe hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are clinical features of MODERATE hypothermia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Poor sucking","Lethargy","Weak cry","Fast breathing","Warm and pink extremities"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists poor sucking, lethargy, weak cry, and fast breathing as clinical features of moderate hypothermia.  Why E wrong: In moderate hypothermia the trunk and extremities are cold, not warm and pink."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the WHO Classification, \"Severe hypothermia\" corresponds to which temperature?","options":["Less than 32°C","32 to 35.9°C","36 to 36.4°C","36.5 to 37.4°C"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Severe hypothermia = less than 32°C (cold trunk, cold extremities).  Why B wrong: 32-35.9°C is moderate hypothermia.  Why C wrong: 36-36.4°C is mild hypothermia.  Why D wrong: 34.5-37.4°C is Normal."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are clinical features of SEVERE hypothermia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lethargic and poor perfusion","Fast or slow breathing, slow heart rate","Hardening of skin with redness and edema","Bleeding and low blood sugar","Vigorous cry with strong sucking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists lethargy/poor perfusion, fast or slow breathing, slow heart rate, hardening of skin with redness/edema, bleeding, and low blood sugar as severe hypothermia features.  Why E wrong: Vigorous cry and strong sucking indicate a well/normothermic baby, not severe hypothermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the management of SEVERE hypothermia, rapid re-warming should be done until the baby reaches what temperature, after which slow re-warming is done?","options":["36.5°C","34°C","32°C","37°C"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for severe hypothermia: rapid re-warming till the baby is 34°C and then slow re-warming.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 34°C as the transition point, not 36.5, 32, or 37°C."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the management of severe hypothermia, the IV fluid glucose bolus recommended is:","options":["Bolus of 3 ml/kg of 10% glucose (warm dextrose)","Bolus of 10 ml/kg of 5% glucose","Bolus of 20 ml/kg of normal saline","Bolus of 5 ml/kg of 25% glucose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states for severe hypothermia give IV fluids-dextrose (warm), a bolus of 3 ml/kg of 10% glucose.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 3 ml/kg of 10% glucose, not these other volumes/concentrations."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In severe hypothermia management and in moderate hypothermia, how often should the baby be reassessed?","options":["Every 5 minutes","Every 15 minutes","Every 30 minutes","Every hour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to reassess every 15 minutes; if temperature doesn't improve, provide additional heat.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies reassessment every 15 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which injection is included in the action/management column for SEVERE hypothermia?","options":["Injection Vitamin K","Injection Insulin","Injection Adrenaline","Injection Calcium gluconate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Inj. Vitamin-K in the action column for severe hypothermia (along with oxygen and warm IV dextrose).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies Vitamin K, not insulin, adrenaline, or calcium gluconate."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Heat is lost in a newborn by \"conduction\" through which mechanism (and how is it prevented)?","options":["Loss by direct contact; prevented using pre-warm linen","Loss by circulating air; prevented by closing doors/windows","Loss by evaporation of moisture; prevented by drying the baby","Loss by indirect radiation; prevented by removing cold equipment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states conduction is heat lost by direct contact; use pre-warm linen to prevent heat loss.  Why B wrong: Loss by circulating air is convection.  Why C wrong: Loss by moisture evaporation is evaporation.  Why D wrong: Indirect radiation is radiation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Heat lost by \"convection\" in a newborn refers to loss through circulating air; how is it prevented?","options":["By drying the baby immediately","By closing doors and windows and shutting off fans and AC of the labour room","By using pre-warm linen","By removing cold equipment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states convection is heat lost by circulating air, so doors and windows should be closed and the fans and AC of the labour room shut off.  Why A wrong: Drying prevents evaporative loss.  Why C wrong: Pre-warm linen prevents conductive loss.  Why D wrong: Removing cold equipment prevents radiant loss."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"To prevent heat loss by \"evaporation\" in a newborn, the nurse should:","options":["Close all windows","Dry the newborn as early as possible","Remove cold equipment","Use pre-warm linen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states evaporation is prevented by drying the newborn as early as possible.  Why A wrong: Closing windows addresses convection.  Why C wrong: Removing cold equipment addresses radiation.  Why D wrong: Pre-warm linen addresses conduction."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the source, what percentage of heat loss occurs through \"radiation,\" and how is it prevented?","options":["30% heat loss; keep baby covered","60% heat loss; all cold equipment should be removed from near the neonate's unit","40% heat loss; close windows","20% heat loss; dry the baby"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states radiation is heat lost by indirect means, and 60% of heat loss occurs by this; all cold equipment should be removed from near the neonate's unit.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 60% for radiation and removal of cold equipment as prevention."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are INITIAL symptoms of hypothermia in a newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hand and feet are cold to touch","Poor suckling ability","Peripheral vasoconstriction","Metabolic acidosis and death","Bradycardia and cyanosis"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists cold hands/feet, poor suckling ability, and peripheral vasoconstriction as initial symptoms of hypothermia.  Why D wrong: Metabolic acidosis and death are later symptoms.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia (bradypnea/bradycardia) and cyanosis are later symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are LATER symptoms of hypothermia in a newborn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bradypnea and bradycardia","Lethargy and cyanosis","Metabolic acidosis and death","Cold hands and feet as the only sign","Peripheral vasoconstriction as the earliest change"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bradypnea, bradycardia, lethargy, cyanosis, metabolic acidosis, and death as later symptoms of hypothermia.  Why D/E wrong: Cold hands/feet and peripheral vasoconstriction are initial symptoms, not later ones."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the nursing care for hypothermia, if the abdomen and chest are cold to touch, this indicates:","options":["Cold stress only","Moderate and severe hypothermia","Normal temperature","Hyperthermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hand and feet cold to touch indicate cold stress, and if the abdomen and chest are cold to touch, that indicates moderate and severe hypothermia.  Why A wrong: Cold stress is indicated by cold hands/feet with a warm trunk.  Why C/D wrong: Cold abdomen/chest indicates significant hypothermia, not normal temperature or hyperthermia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the nursing care for hypothermia, cold stress can be managed by:","options":["Ice packs","KMC (Kangaroo Mother Care)","Cold sponging","Antipyretics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cold stress can be managed by KMC.  Why A/C wrong: Ice packs and cold sponging would worsen cold stress.  Why D wrong: Antipyretics are used for fever, not cold stress."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS) occurs when the mother does what during pregnancy?","options":["Takes alcohol during the gestational period","Takes iron supplements","Consumes excess protein","Takes prenatal vitamins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states FAS occurs in a newborn whose mother is taking alcohol during the gestational period or pregnancy.  Why B/C/D wrong: Iron supplements, protein, and prenatal vitamins do not cause FAS; alcohol does."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Regarding alcohol and pregnancy, which statement from the source is correct?","options":["Alcohol cannot cross the placenta","Alcohol is a teratogenic substance and it can cross the placenta","Alcohol only affects the mother","Alcohol is protective for the fetus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states alcohol is a teratogenic substance and it can cross the placenta.  Why A wrong: Alcohol does cross the placenta.  Why C/D wrong: Alcohol harms the fetus (teratogenic), it is not confined to the mother nor protective."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are facial structure abnormalities seen in Fetal Alcohol Syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Thin upper lip","Skin folds at the corner of the eye or small eye opening","Short nose, low nasal bridge","Small head circumference and small mid-face; hypoplastic philtrum","Cleft palate with macroglossia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists thin upper lip, skin folds at corner of eye/small eye opening, short nose/low nasal bridge, small head circumference/small mid-face, and hypoplastic philtrum as FAS facial abnormalities.  Why E wrong: Cleft palate with macroglossia is not among the listed FAS facial abnormalities."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The \"hypoplastic philtrum\" seen in FAS refers to the groove located where?","options":["Between the nasal septum and the upper lip","Between the eyebrows","On the chin","Behind the ear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hypoplastic philtrum is the groove between the nasal septum and the upper lip.  Why B/C/D wrong: The philtrum is located between the nose and upper lip, not between the eyebrows, on the chin, or behind the ear."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In addition to facial abnormalities, FAS may present with which cardiac finding?","options":["Congenital heart abnormalities","Cardiac hypertrophy from exercise","Complete heart block only","No cardiac involvement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists congenital heart abnormalities as a feature of FAS.  Why B/C wrong: Exercise-related hypertrophy and isolated complete heart block are not the listed FAS features.  Why D wrong: FAS does involve congenital heart abnormalities."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In a newborn with FAS, alcohol withdrawal symptoms may appear as which of the following (due to no alcohol supply)?","options":["Excessive crying, irritability, and seizures","Excessive sleeping and hypotonia only","Constipation and weight gain","Hyperthermia and flushing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states alcohol withdrawal symptoms may appear like excessive crying, irritability, and seizures due to no alcohol supply.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies crying, irritability, and seizures, not the other patterns."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In FAS nursing care, if seizures occur, the nurse should immediately provide which position, followed by suction, to prevent aspiration?","options":["Supine position","Side-lying position","Prone position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if seizures occur, immediately provide a side-lying position and then suction to prevent aspiration.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies side-lying position for seizure/aspiration prevention, not supine, prone, or Trendelenburg."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"To prevent seizures in a newborn with FAS, the nurse should:","options":["Increase environmental stimuli","Decrease environmental stimuli","Keep bright lights on","Encourage loud noises"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to decrease environmental stimuli to prevent seizures.  Why A/C/D wrong: Increasing stimuli, bright lights, or loud noises could trigger seizures."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The drug of choice for management of alcohol withdrawal syndrome is:","options":["Chlordiazepoxide (Librium)","Disulfiram","Naloxone","Phenytoin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for management of alcohol withdrawal syndrome is chlordiazepoxide or Librium.  Why B wrong: Disulfiram is used for aversion/de-addiction therapy, not acute withdrawal.  Why C wrong: Naloxone reverses opioid overdose.  Why D wrong: Phenytoin is an anticonvulsant, not the DOC for alcohol withdrawal here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which drug is used for aversion therapy or de-addiction therapy for alcoholic clients?","options":["Chlordiazepoxide","Disulfiram","Diazepam","Lorazepam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states disulfiram is used for aversion therapy or de-addiction therapy for alcoholic clients.  Why A/C/D wrong: These benzodiazepines are used in withdrawal management, not aversion therapy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Meconium Aspiration Syndrome (MAS) occurs due to the release of meconium into which fluid, triggered by stressful events?","options":["Cerebrospinal fluid","Amniotic fluid","Pleural fluid","Synovial fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MAS occurs due to the release of meconium into the amniotic fluid due to any stressful events.  Why A/C/D wrong: Meconium is released into the amniotic fluid, not CSF, pleural, or synovial fluid."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In MAS, the aspiration of meconium occurs at which point?","options":["Only after 1 week of life","In utero or with the first breath","Only during breastfeeding","Only during vaccination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states aspiration occurs in utero or with the first breath.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies in utero or with the first breath, not later feeding or vaccination."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Meconium Aspiration Syndrome (MAS) is most commonly seen in which type of babies?","options":["Pre-term babies","Post-term babies","Term babies only","Extremely low birth weight babies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MAS is commonly seen in post-term babies.  Why A wrong: Pre-term babies are more prone to RDS.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies post-term babies as most commonly affected by MAS."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In MAS, the newborn's nails, skin, and umbilical cord may be stained which colour?","options":["Blue","Yellow green","Red","White"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the newborn's nails, skin, and umbilical cord may be stained a yellow-green colour in MAS.  Why A/C/D wrong: The staining is yellow-green (from meconium), not blue, red, or white."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the nursing care of MAS, when must suctioning be done?","options":["After the first breath is taken","Immediately after the head is delivered and before the first breath is taken","Only after 24 hours","Only if the baby cries"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This reflects the source's (older) teaching that suctioning is done as the head is delivered, before the first breath. [IMPORTANT UPDATE: per 2015 NRP/ILCOR and ACOG (2017), routine intrapartum suctioning \"on the perineum\" and routine endotracheal suctioning of non-vigorous meconium-stained infants are NO LONGER recommended — a non-vigorous meconium-stained newborn should receive positive-pressure ventilation (PPV). Answer keyed to the source as printed.]  Why A wrong: Within the source's dated framework, suctioning after the first breath risks meconium aspiration.  Why C/D wrong: Not consistent with the source's stated timing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the RDS overview on the later page, RDS is also known by which name and was previously called what?","options":["Surfactant deficiency disorder (SDD); previously hyaline membrane disease (HMD)","Meconium aspiration syndrome; previously RDS","Transient tachypnea; previously wet lung","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia; previously HMD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RDS is also known as surfactant deficiency disorder (SDD) and was previously called hyaline membrane disease (HMD).  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies SDD/HMD, not these other names."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"RDS affects about what percentage of newborns and is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in which group?","options":["About 1% of newborns; leading cause in preterm infants","About 10% of newborns; leading cause in term infants","About 50% of newborns; leading cause in post-term infants","About 25% of newborns; leading cause in LGA infants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RDS affects about 1% of newborns and is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in preterm infants.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies about 1% and preterm infants."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which score is used for assessing respiratory distress in the newborn (NIMHANS:2018)?","options":["Silverman Anderson score (SAS)","APGAR score","Ballard score","Dubowitz score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Silverman Anderson score (SAS) is used for assessing respiratory distress in the newborn (NIMHANS:2018).  Why B wrong: APGAR assesses overall physiological condition after birth.  Why C/D wrong: Ballard and Dubowitz assess gestational maturity/age."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"According to the source, which scoring system is used for TERM neonates and which is often used for PRETERM neonates in respiratory distress?","options":["Downes scoring for term; SAS for preterm","SAS for term; Downes for preterm","APGAR for term; Ballard for preterm","Silverman for term; Downes for preterm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Downes scoring system is used for term neonates whereas SAS is often used in preterm neonates.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct assignment.  Why C/D wrong: These mix up the correct scoring systems."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The diagnostic criterion of tachypnoea in RDS is defined as a respiratory rate of:","options":["≥40 breaths/minute","≥50 breaths/minute","≥60 breaths/minute","≥80 breaths/minute"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source's diagnostic criteria list tachypnoea as ≥60 breaths/minute.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies ≥60 breaths/minute, not these values."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are diagnostic criteria for RDS per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tachypnoea (≥60 breaths/minute)","Expiratory grunting","Intercostal and sternal retractions (recession)","Central cyanosis while breathing room air","Bradypnoea with warm pink skin"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source's diagnostic criteria list tachypnoea ≥60/min, expiratory grunting, intercostal and sternal retractions, and central cyanosis while breathing room air.  Why E wrong: Bradypnoea with warm pink skin contradicts the tachypnoea and cyanosis of RDS."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of RDS listed on the later page? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inspiratory stridor, nasal flaring, and expiratory grunting","Tachypnoea and tachycardia","Blue discoloration of the skin (cyanosis)","Chest wall retractions, crackles, apnoea, dyspnoea, and poor feeding","Bradycardia with vigorous feeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists inspiratory stridor/nasal flaring/expiratory grunting, tachypnoea/tachycardia, cyanosis, chest wall retractions/crackles, apnoea/dyspnoea, and poor feeding as RDS signs.  Why E wrong: Bradycardia with vigorous feeding contradicts the tachycardia and poor feeding of RDS."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The \"INSURE\" technique used to treat preterm infants with RDS stands for:","options":["INtubation-SURfactant-Extubation","INhaled-SURfactant-Emergency","INjection-SURfactant-Extraction","INternal-SURgical-Extraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states INSURE = INtubation-SURfactant-Extubation, a widely used surfactant administration method.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the INSURE acronym."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The \"LISA\" technique used in preterm RDS management stands for:","options":["Less Invasive Surfactant Administration","Low Intensity Suction Application","Lung Inflation Support Apparatus","Late Intravenous Surfactant Administration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LISA = Less Invasive Surfactant Administration, a widely used surfactant administration method to treat preterm infants with RDS.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are incorrect expansions of the LISA acronym."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The Silverman Anderson Respiratory Severity Score (RSS) includes how many parameters?","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the RSS score includes five parameters: upper chest retraction, lower chest retraction, xiphoid retraction, nasal flares, and expiratory grunting sound.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies five parameters."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are the five parameters of the Silverman Anderson Respiratory Severity Score (RSS)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Upper chest retraction","Lower chest retraction","Xiphoid retraction","Nasal flares and expiratory grunting sound","Heart rate and blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists upper chest retraction, lower chest retraction, xiphoid retraction, nasal flares, and expiratory grunting sound as the five RSS parameters.  Why E wrong: Heart rate and blood pressure are not among the five RSS parameters."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the RSS interpretation, a score of 0 indicates:","options":["No respiratory distress (RD)","Mild respiratory distress","Moderate respiratory distress","Severe respiratory distress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states score 0 = no respiratory distress (RD).  Why B wrong: 1-3 = mild RD.  Why C wrong: 4-6 = moderate RD.  Why D wrong: Score 10 indicates severe respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the RSS interpretation, a score of 1-3 indicates which level of respiratory distress?","options":["No respiratory distress","Mild respiratory distress","Moderate respiratory distress","Impending respiratory distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states 1-3 = mild RD.  Why A wrong: Score 0 = no RD.  Why C wrong: 4-6 = moderate RD.  Why D wrong: >6 = impending respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the RSS interpretation, a score of 4-6 indicates which level of respiratory distress?","options":["Mild respiratory distress","Moderate respiratory distress","Impending respiratory distress","Severe respiratory distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states 4-6 = moderate RD.  Why A wrong: 1-3 = mild RD.  Why C wrong: >6 = impending respiratory distress.  Why D wrong: Score 10 = severe respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the RSS interpretation, a score of greater than 6 (>6) indicates:","options":["No respiratory distress","Mild respiratory distress","Impending respiratory distress","Normal breathing"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states >6 = impending respiratory distress.  Why A/B/D wrong: Score 0 = no RD, 1-3 = mild RD, and >6 is not normal breathing."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In the RSS interpretation, which score indicates SEVERE respiratory distress?","options":["Score 6","Score 8","Score 10","Score 4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states score 10 indicates severe respiratory distress.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies a score of 10 for severe respiratory distress."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Facial palsy as a birth injury is also known as which condition, and results from injury to which nerve during delivery?","options":["Bell's palsy; facial nerve","Erb's palsy; brachial nerve","Klumpke's palsy; median nerve","Cerebral palsy; spinal nerve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Birth-related facial palsy results from injury/compression of the facial (VII) nerve during delivery. [Note: strictly, \"Bell's palsy\" denotes IDIOPATHIC facial paralysis; traumatic birth facial palsy is a facial-nerve compression injury. The \"facial nerve\" part is what makes A correct.]  Why B/C wrong: Erb's and Klumpke's palsy are brachial plexus injuries.  Why D wrong: Cerebral palsy is not a peripheral birth-nerve injury."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Brachial palsy is an injury to the brachial nerve occurring due to difficult vaginal deliveries. Which type is due to injury at the C5-C6 level and is the most common?","options":["Klumpke's palsy","Erb's palsy","Bell's palsy","Facial palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Erb's palsy is due to injury at C5-C6 level and is the most common type of brachial palsy.  Why A wrong: Klumpke's palsy is due to injury at C7, C8, and T1.  Why C/D wrong: Bell's/facial palsy involve the facial nerve, not the brachial plexus."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Klumpke's palsy results from injury at which spinal levels?","options":["C3, C4, C5","C5, C6","C7, C8, and T1","T2, T3, T4"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Klumpke's palsy is due to injury at C7, C8, and T1 level.  Why A wrong: These are not the stated levels for Klumpke's palsy.  Why B wrong: C5-C6 is Erb's palsy.  Why D wrong: T2-T4 are not the stated Klumpke's levels."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are listed as birth injuries in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Facial palsy (Bell's palsy)","Brachial palsy (Erb's and Klumpke's palsy)","Cephalohematoma","Caput succedaneum","Congenital heart disease"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists facial palsy, brachial palsy (Erb's and Klumpke's), cephalohematoma, and caput succedaneum as birth injuries.  Why E wrong: Congenital heart disease is a congenital abnormality, not a birth (delivery) injury."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Neonatal convulsions are described as a common life-threatening emergency in the newborn due to what?","options":["Cerebral or biochemical abnormalities","Excess feeding","Normal reflexes","Physiological jaundice"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states neonatal convulsions are a common life-threatening emergency in the newborn due to cerebral or biochemical abnormalities.  Why B/C/D wrong: Excess feeding, normal reflexes, and physiological jaundice are not the stated causes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Neonatal convulsions typically occur within which time frame after birth?","options":["First 24-48 hours of life","First 1 hour of life","After 1 week of life","After 1 month of life"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states neonatal convulsions typically occur within the first 24-48 hours of life.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the first 24-48 hours."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The most common type of newborn seizure is which type (accounting for 50%)?","options":["Tonic-clonic type","Subtle type (50%)","Myoclonic type","Absence type"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of newborn seizure is the subtle type (50%).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies subtle type as the most common (50%); the second most common is febrile seizures."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"After subtle seizures (the most common, ~50%), which is the next most common type of neonatal seizure?","options":["Clonic seizures","Subtle seizures","Myoclonic seizures","Febrile seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Volpe classification of neonatal seizures (subtle, clonic, tonic, myoclonic), clonic seizures are the next most common after subtle seizures. [Corrected: \"febrile seizures\" are NOT a neonatal seizure type.]  Why B wrong: Subtle seizures are the MOST common (~50%), not the second.  Why C wrong: Myoclonic seizures are less common than clonic.  Why D wrong: Febrile seizures by definition occur in children 6 months-5 years and do NOT occur in neonates."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"The most common cause of TERM newborn seizures is:","options":["Hypoxia","Intracranial haemorrhage","Hypocalcaemia","Pyridoxine deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of term newborn seizure is hypoxia.  Why B wrong: Intracranial haemorrhage is the most common cause in preterm babies.  Why C/D wrong: Hypocalcaemia and pyridoxine deficiency are listed among \"other\" causes, not the most common in term newborns."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"other\" causes of term newborn seizures (besides hypoxia)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Intracranial haemorrhage and perinatal asphyxia","Hypoglycaemia and hypocalcaemia","Pyridoxine (Vitamin-B6) deficiency","CNS infections","Physiological weight loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists intracranial haemorrhage, perinatal asphyxia, hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, pyridoxine (Vitamin-B6) deficiency, and CNS infections as other causes.  Why E wrong: Physiological weight loss is not a listed cause of seizures."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Pyridoxine deficiency, a cause of neonatal seizures, refers to a deficiency of which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin B1","Vitamin B6","Vitamin B12","Vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies pyridoxine as Vitamin-B6 deficiency among the causes of newborn seizures.  Why A/C/D wrong: Pyridoxine is Vitamin B6, not B1, B12, or K."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In PRETERM babies, which is the most common cause of neonatal convulsions?","options":["Hypoxia","Intracranial haemorrhage","Hypoglycaemia","CNS infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in preterm babies, intracranial haemorrhage is the most common cause of neonatal convulsions.  Why A wrong: Hypoxia is the most common cause in term newborns.  Why C/D wrong: Hypoglycaemia and CNS infections are \"other\" causes, not the most common in preterm babies."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Neonatal convulsions are said to commonly occur in which infants?","options":["Post-term or macrosomic babies","Preterm or low birth weight babies","Only in term AGA babies","Only in breastfed babies"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states neonatal convulsions commonly occur in preterm or low birth weight babies.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies preterm or LBW babies, not post-term/macrosomic, term AGA, or breastfed babies specifically."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"In FAS, \"abnormal palmar crease\" and \"sign of respiratory distress (apnoea and cyanosis)\" are noted. What should the mother do to prevent FAS?","options":["Avoid alcohol during pregnancy","Increase alcohol slowly","Take alcohol only in the third trimester","Substitute alcohol with tobacco"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the mother should avoid alcohol during pregnancy to prevent FAS.  Why B/C/D wrong: Any alcohol use is teratogenic; substituting tobacco is also harmful. Prevention is complete avoidance of alcohol."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A nurse assessing a newborn finds warm extremities and a warm abdomen on touch with the dorsal hand. This indicates:","options":["Cold stress","A normothermic (normal temperature) baby","Moderate hypothermia","Severe hypothermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states in a normothermic baby (baby with normal temperature), both abdomen and feet are warm to touch.  Why A wrong: Cold stress shows cold feet with a warm abdomen.  Why C/D wrong: Moderate/severe hypothermia shows cold trunk and extremities."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"A post-term newborn is characterized by which amniotic fluid finding along with a large body?","options":["Polyhydramnios and microsomia","Oligohydramnios and macrosomia","Normal fluid and low birth weight","Polyhydramnios and macrosomia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists oligohydramnios and macrosomia as assessment features of the post-term newborn.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies oligohydramnios (reduced fluid) with macrosomia (large body), not the other combinations."},{"section":"Batch 007 · HIV-positive newborn care & vaccination…","question":"Which of the following are physical characteristics of a PRE-TERM newborn per the source's comparison table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Skin transparent or loose","Lack of subcutaneous fat","Lanugo covering forehead, shoulders, and arms","Sluggish reflexes; elbow easily brought across the chest (scarf sign)","Deep sole creases with well-developed scrotum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists transparent/loose skin, lack of subcutaneous fat, lanugo covering forehead/shoulders/arms, sluggish reflexes, and easily crossed elbow (scarf sign) as pre-term characteristics.  Why E wrong: Deep sole creases and well-developed descended-testis scrotum are term-newborn characteristics."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the drug of choice for neonatal seizures?","options":["Midazolam","Phenobarbitone","Phenytoin","Diazepam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for neonatal seizure is phenobarbitone.  Why A wrong: Midazolam (nasal spray) is the drug of choice for febrile seizures, not neonatal seizures.  Why C wrong: Phenytoin is not listed as the DOC for neonatal seizures on the page.  Why D wrong: Diazepam is not the stated drug of choice for neonatal seizures."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the correct dosing regimen of phenobarbitone for neonatal seizures as stated in the text?","options":["5 mg/kg IV bolus, then 20 mg/kg daily for 3 days","20 mg/kg IV bolus, then 5 mg/kg daily for 3 days","10 mg/kg IV bolus, then 10 mg/kg daily for 5 days","20 mg/kg IV bolus, then 5 mg/kg daily for 7 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text specifies phenobarbitone 20 mg/kg IV bolus, then 5 mg/kg daily for 3 days.  Why A wrong: The loading (bolus) and maintenance doses are reversed.  Why C wrong: Neither the doses (10 mg/kg) nor the duration (5 days) match.  Why D wrong: The duration is 3 days, not 7 days."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice for febrile seizures according to the text?","options":["Phenobarbitone IV bolus","Rectal diazepam","Midazolam nasal spray","Oral phenytoin"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states midazolam nasal spray is the drug of choice for febrile seizures.  Why A wrong: Phenobarbitone is the DOC for neonatal seizures, not febrile seizures.  Why B wrong: Rectal diazepam is not the choice named in the text.  Why D wrong: Oral phenytoin is not mentioned as the DOC for febrile seizures."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the components of nursing care listed for neonatal seizures.","options":["Airway clearance","Oxygen and IV-line","Thermal protection","Prevention of infection and injury","Immediate craniotomy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists airway clearance, oxygen, IV-line, thermal protection, and prevention of infection & injury as nursing care.  Why E wrong: Craniotomy is not part of the listed nursing care for neonatal seizures."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Ophthalmia neonatorum is also known by which name?","options":["Neonatal conjunctivitis","Retinopathy of prematurity","Congenital cataract","Neonatal glaucoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states ophthalmia neonatorum is also known as neonatal conjunctivitis.  Why B wrong: Retinopathy of prematurity is a distinct retinal condition, not this diagnosis.  Why C wrong: Congenital cataract is a lens opacity, unrelated.  Why D wrong: Neonatal glaucoma is a pressure disorder, not the given synonym."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Inflammation of the conjunctiva during the first ____ of life is termed ophthalmia neonatorum.","options":["1 week","2 weeks","3 weeks","4 weeks"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines it as inflammation of the conjunctiva during the first 3 weeks of life.  Why A wrong: 1 week is shorter than the stated period.  Why B wrong: 2 weeks is not the period given.  Why D wrong: 4 weeks exceeds the stated 3-week window."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Ophthalmia neonatorum most commonly occurs due to which organisms?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus or E. coli","Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis","Streptococcus pneumoniae or Haemophilus influenzae","Pseudomonas or Klebsiella"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it mostly occurs due to Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis.  Why A wrong: Staphylococcus aureus and E. coli are not the named causative organisms.  Why C wrong: Streptococcus pneumoniae and H. influenzae are not listed.  Why D wrong: Pseudomonas and Klebsiella are not the stated causes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Gonococcal (Neisseria gonorrhoeae) ophthalmia neonatorum typically presents as which onset type?","options":["Early onset — within 5 days after birth","Late onset — after 2 weeks","Delayed onset — after 3 weeks","Onset only after 1 month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Neisseria gonorrhoeae occurs within 5 days after birth (early onset).  Why B wrong: Late onset (5 days to 2 weeks) describes Chlamydia, not gonococcal infection.  Why C wrong: 3 weeks is not the timing given for gonococcal onset.  Why D wrong: 1 month is far beyond the stated 5-day early-onset window."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Chlamydia trachomatis ophthalmia neonatorum characteristically occurs during which period, and why is its onset later?","options":["Within 2 days; short incubation period","5 days after birth to 2 weeks; C. trachomatis has a longer incubation period","After 1 month; slow bacterial growth","At birth; immediate transmission"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Chlamydia occurs 5 days after birth to 2 weeks (late onset) because C. trachomatis has a longer incubation period.  Why A wrong: 2 days with a short incubation describes early (gonococcal) onset instead.  Why C wrong: 1 month is not the stated window.  Why D wrong: Onset at birth contradicts the late-onset description."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"According to the text, how is Chlamydia trachomatis transmitted to the neonate causing ophthalmia neonatorum?","options":["Through breast milk only","Through infected hands during caring and from the genital tract of the infected mother","Only via airborne droplets","Through contaminated formula"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is transmitted through infected hands during caring and from the genital tract of the infected mother.  Why A wrong: Breast milk transmission is not stated for this organism.  Why C wrong: Airborne droplet spread is not the described route.  Why D wrong: Contaminated formula is not mentioned as a transmission route."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which ophthalmic antibiotic is listed as the drug of choice in the nursing care of ophthalmia neonatorum?","options":["1% silver nitrate","0.5% erythromycin","2% tetracycline","0.3% gentamicin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists ophthalmic antibiotics as 0.5% erythromycin (drug of choice) and 1% silver nitrate.  Why A wrong: 1% silver nitrate is listed but not designated as the drug of choice.  Why C wrong: 2% tetracycline is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: 0.3% gentamicin is not listed for this condition."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the nursing care measures listed for ophthalmia neonatorum.","options":["Treatment of maternal infection before delivery","Maintain aseptic technique","Ophthalmic antibiotic 0.5% erythromycin","1% silver nitrate","Enucleation of the eye"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists treatment of maternal infection before delivery, aseptic technique, 0.5% erythromycin, and 1% silver nitrate.  Why E wrong: Enucleation is not part of the described care and would be inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Oral thrush in the newborn is a fungal infection caused by which organism?","options":["Aspergillus fumigatus","Candida albicans","Cryptococcus neoformans","Histoplasma capsulatum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states oral thrush is a fungal infection of the newborn's oral cavity and tongue caused by Candida albicans.  Why A wrong: Aspergillus is a mould, not the cause of oral thrush here.  Why C wrong: Cryptococcus causes meningitis, not neonatal oral thrush.  Why D wrong: Histoplasma is not the causative organism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Oral thrush is a fungal infection of the newborn's oral cavity and tongue occurring within what period of life?","options":["First 2 weeks of life","First 24 hours","First 3 months","First 6 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it occurs within the first 2 weeks of life.  Why B wrong: 24 hours is not the stated window.  Why C wrong: 3 months exceeds the stated 2-week period.  Why D wrong: 6 months exceeds the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of oral thrush (KGMU-2023)?","options":["Bleeding gums","Milky white patches on the buccal mucosa, lips, tongue and gums that cannot be easily removed by gauze","Blue discoloration of the tongue","Yellow crusts on the lips easily wiped off"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text names milky white patches (cardinal sign) on buccal mucosa, lips, tongue and gums that cannot be easily removed by gauze.  Why A wrong: Bleeding gums is not the cardinal sign described.  Why C wrong: Blue discoloration describes cyanosis, not thrush.  Why D wrong: Thrush patches cannot be easily removed; easily wiped crusts do not fit."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the drug of choice (DOC) for managing oral thrush per the text?","options":["1% silver nitrate","0.5% gentian violet applied orally","0.5% erythromycin","Oral phenobarbitone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states oral thrush is managed by oral application of 0.5% gentian violet (DOC) and other antifungal medicines.  Why A wrong: 1% silver nitrate is used for eye prophylaxis, not thrush.  Why C wrong: 0.5% erythromycin is the DOC for ophthalmia neonatorum, not thrush.  Why D wrong: Phenobarbitone is an anticonvulsant, not an antifungal."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC) is best described as an acute or severe inflammatory infection and damage of the intestinal mucosa resulting from which underlying mechanism?","options":["Ischemia resulting from asphyxia or prolonged hypoxia","Autoimmune destruction of villi","Bacterial overgrowth from gluten ingestion","Congenital absence of ganglion cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states NEC results from ischemia resulting from asphyxia or prolonged hypoxia.  Why B wrong: Autoimmune villous destruction describes celiac disease, not NEC.  Why C wrong: Gluten-related bacterial overgrowth is not the NEC mechanism.  Why D wrong: Absence of ganglion cells describes Hirschsprung disease."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Necrotising enterocolitis is MORE common in which group of infants?","options":["Post-term and breastfed infants","Preterm and formula-fed infants","Full-term exclusively breastfed infants","Large-for-gestational-age infants"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states NEC is more common in preterm and formula-fed infants.  Why A wrong: Post-term/breastfed is the opposite of the stated risk group.  Why C wrong: Exclusively breastfed full-term infants are lower risk.  Why D wrong: Large-for-gestational-age is not the described risk group."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the chief clinical features of necrotising enterocolitis listed in the text.","options":["Lethargy","Abdominal distension","Vomiting and blood in stool","Metabolic acidosis and peritonitis","Projectile non-bilious vomiting only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists lethargy, abdominal distension, vomiting, blood in stool, metabolic acidosis and peritonitis as chief features.  Why E wrong: Projectile non-bilious vomiting is the cardinal sign of pyloric stenosis, not NEC."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Meconium is best defined as which of the following?","options":["The first stool of the newborn","The first urine of the newborn","Amniotic fluid in the stomach","Digested breast milk"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states meconium is the first stool of the newborn.  Why B wrong: It is stool, not urine.  Why C wrong: It is not swallowed amniotic fluid in the stomach.  Why D wrong: Meconium precedes milk digestion and is not digested breast milk."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the characteristics of meconium described in the text.","options":["Odourless","Tenacious (sticky)","Contains epidermal cells, lanugo and hair","Contains bile pigment","Foul-smelling and watery"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists meconium as odourless, tenacious, containing epidermal cells, lanugo & hair and bile pigment.  Why E wrong: Meconium is described as odourless (not foul-smelling) and tenacious (not watery)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Meconium is usually passed within what time after birth?","options":["Within first 6 hours","Within first 24 hours","Within first 72 hours","Within first week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states meconium is usually passed within the first 24 hours after birth.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is shorter than the stated typical passage window.  Why C wrong: 72 hours is beyond the normal window (24–48 hours is already concerning).  Why D wrong: A week is far beyond the normal window."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"If meconium is NOT passed within the first 24–48 hours, it may be a sign of which conditions (select all that apply)?","options":["Intestinal obstruction","Hirschsprung's disease","Normal newborn variation requiring no action","Other obstructive causes needing prompt physician notification","Physiological jaundice"],"correct":"A,B,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states failure to pass meconium within 24–48 hours may signal intestinal obstruction, Hirschsprung's disease and others, requiring the doctor to be informed shortly.  Why C wrong: It is NOT a normal variation; it requires prompt notification.  Why E wrong: Physiological jaundice is a bilirubin issue, not linked here to delayed meconium."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The grades of neonatal care three-tier system is based primarily on which two parameters?","options":["Birth weight and gestational age","Apgar score and head circumference","Blood group and gender","Maternal age and parity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the three-tier system is based on birth weight and gestational age.  Why B wrong: Apgar and head circumference are not the basis of the tier system.  Why C wrong: Blood group and gender do not define the levels.  Why D wrong: Maternal age/parity are not the classification basis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What are the birth weight and gestational age criteria for Level-1 neonatal care?","options":["Birth weight >2000 gm or gestational age >37 weeks","Birth weight 1500–2000 gm or gestational age 32–36 weeks","Birth weight <1500 gm or gestational age <32 weeks","Birth weight >2500 gm or gestational age >40 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Level-1 criteria are birth weight >2000 gm or gestational age >37 weeks.  Why B wrong: 1500–2000 gm / 32–36 weeks is the Level-2 criteria.  Why C wrong: <1500 gm / <32 weeks is the Level-3 criteria.  Why D wrong: >2500 gm / >40 weeks is not the stated Level-1 cut-off."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Where is Level-1 neonatal care provided according to the text?","options":["Apex hospitals and ICUs","Well-equipped district & teaching hospitals","At home, sub-centre, and PHC","Only in NICU"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Level-1 care is provided at home, sub-centre, and PHC.  Why A wrong: Apex hospitals/ICUs are the Level-3 setting.  Why B wrong: District & teaching hospitals are the Level-2 setting.  Why D wrong: NICU corresponds to Level-3 care."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Approximately what percentage of newborns should receive essential perinatal care as basic care at birth under Level-1 care?","options":["About 50%","About 80%","About 10–15%","About 3–5%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states essential perinatal care should be provided to about 80% of newborns as basic care at birth.  Why A wrong: 50% is not the stated figure.  Why C wrong: 10–15% is the proportion needing Level-2 care.  Why D wrong: 3–5% is the proportion needing Level-3 care."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the weight and gestational age criteria for Level-2 neonatal care?","options":[">2000 gm or >37 weeks","Neonate weight 1500 to 2000 gm or gestation age 32–36 weeks","<1500 gm or gestation age before 32 weeks","1000–1500 gm or 28–30 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Level-2 criteria are neonate weight 1500 to 2000 gm or gestation age 32–36 weeks.  Why A wrong: >2000 gm / >37 weeks is Level-1.  Why C wrong: <1500 gm / <32 weeks is Level-3.  Why D wrong: 1000–1500 gm / 28–30 weeks is not the stated Level-2 range."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the facilities/procedures the text lists as arrangements for Level-2 (intermediate) neonatal care.","options":["Resuscitation procedure","Thermoregulation","IV infusion and gavage feeding","Phototherapy and EBT (exchange blood transfusion) therapy","Centralized oxygen with ventilators in an ICU setting"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists resuscitation, thermoregulation, IV infusion, gavage feeding, and phototherapy and EBT therapy for Level-2 care.  Why E wrong: Centralized oxygen with ventilators in a full ICU describes Level-3 care."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What percentage of neonates require Level-2 care according to the text?","options":["About 80%","10 to 15%","3 to 5%","50%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states 10 to 15% neonates require Level-2 care.  Why A wrong: 80% is the proportion getting basic Level-1 care.  Why C wrong: 3–5% is the proportion needing Level-3 care.  Why D wrong: 50% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the criteria for Level-3 neonatal care?","options":["Neonates weight <1500 gm or gestation age before 32 weeks","Weight 1500–2000 gm or 32–36 weeks","Weight >2000 gm or >37 weeks","Weight >2500 gm at term"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Level-3 criteria are neonates weight <1500 gm or gestation age before 32 weeks.  Why B wrong: 1500–2000 gm / 32–36 weeks is Level-2.  Why C wrong: >2000 gm / >37 weeks is Level-1.  Why D wrong: >2500 gm at term is not the stated criteria."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Level-3 neonatal care is provided at apex hospitals/ICUs. Which institutions does the text cite as examples?","options":["Only sub-centres","AIIMS, JIPMER, PGIMER, etc.","Home and PHC","Nursing homes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text names AIIMS, JIPMER, PGIMER etc. as Level-3 apex hospital examples with full ICU facilities.  Why A wrong: Sub-centres provide Level-1 care.  Why C wrong: Home and PHC are Level-1 settings.  Why D wrong: Nursing homes are cited under Level-2 intermediate care."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What percentage of neonates require Level-3 care?","options":["3-5%","10-15%","80%","25%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states 3-5% neonates require Level-3 care.  Why B wrong: 10-15% require Level-2 care.  Why C wrong: 80% receive basic Level-1 care.  Why D wrong: 25% is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the grades-of-neonatal-care diagram, Level-III (Referral hospital/MCH) corresponds to which unit?","options":["Normal neonatal care","Special neonatal care unit","Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU)","Health centre"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The diagram labels Level-III (Referral hospital/MCH) as the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU).  Why A wrong: Normal neonatal care corresponds to Level-I.  Why B wrong: Special neonatal care unit corresponds to Level-II.  Why D wrong: Health centre is Level-I."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Lactose intolerance is the inability to digest lactose carbohydrate due to deficiency or absence of which enzyme?","options":["Lipase","Lactase enzyme","Amylase","Sucrase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is due to deficiency/absence of lactase enzyme secreted in the small intestine.  Why A wrong: Lipase digests fats, not lactose.  Why C wrong: Amylase digests starch, not lactose.  Why D wrong: Sucrase digests sucrose, not lactose."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Lactose is a disaccharide found in which of the following (FAQs)?","options":["Egg yolk","Breast milk","Wheat","Meat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states lactose is a disaccharide and found in breast milk.  Why A wrong: Egg yolk is not the stated source of lactose.  Why C wrong: Wheat contains gluten, not lactose.  Why D wrong: Meat is not a lactose source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the small intestine, lactose is broken down by lactase enzyme into which two monosaccharides (one of which is called \"brain sugar\")?","options":["Glucose + Fructose","Glucose + Galactose","Fructose + Galactose","Glucose + Maltose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text shows lactose → glucose + galactose, with galactose noted as \"Brain Sugar.\"  Why A wrong: Fructose is not a product of lactose digestion.  Why C wrong: Lactose does not yield fructose.  Why D wrong: Maltose is a disaccharide, not a lactose breakdown product."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In lactose intolerance, undigested lactose reaching the large intestine leads to which process, causing symptoms?","options":["Fermentation by normal flora causing excessive flatus and severe watery diarrhoea","Immediate absorption into the bloodstream","Conversion into bile pigment","Formation of gallstones"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states normal flora ferment the lactose in the large intestine, causing excessive flatus formation and severe watery diarrhoea.  Why B wrong: Undigested lactose is not absorbed; that is the problem.  Why C wrong: It is not converted to bile pigment.  Why D wrong: Gallstone formation is unrelated to this mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the signs and symptoms of lactose intolerance listed in the text.","options":["Abdominal distension and abdominal pain","Severe watery diarrhoea and dehydration","Electrolyte imbalance","Projectile bilious vomiting","Milky white oral patches"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists abdominal distension/pain, severe watery diarrhoea with dehydration, and electrolyte imbalance.  Why D wrong: Projectile vomiting relates to pyloric stenosis, not lactose intolerance.  Why E wrong: Milky white oral patches are a sign of oral thrush."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which test is specifically listed for the diagnosis of lactose intolerance?","options":["Lactose tolerance test","Rectal biopsy","MSAFP level","Chest X-ray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists physical examination and the lactose tolerance test for diagnosis.  Why B wrong: Rectal biopsy is the confirmatory test for Hirschsprung disease.  Why C wrong: MSAFP is used for abdominal wall defects.  Why D wrong: Chest X-ray is used in oesophageal atresia/TEF, not lactose intolerance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A key nursing care instruction for a child with lactose intolerance is to:","options":["Increase human breast milk intake","Avoid human breast milk and milk products","Give only whole cow's milk","Provide gluten-free diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states nursing care is to avoid human breast milk and milk products.  Why A wrong: Increasing breast milk would worsen symptoms.  Why C wrong: Cow's milk also contains lactose and should be avoided.  Why D wrong: A gluten-free diet is for celiac disease, not lactose intolerance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A child with lactose intolerance is at risk of deficiency of which nutrients due to abstention from breast milk, requiring supplements?","options":["Iron and folate","Calcium and vitamin-D","Vitamin B12 and copper","Sodium and potassium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the child can develop calcium and vitamin-D deficiency due to abstention of breast milk, so supplements should be initiated.  Why A wrong: Iron and folate are not the deficiencies named here.  Why C wrong: B12 and copper are not stated.  Why D wrong: Sodium/potassium are electrolytes but not the deficiency named for supplementation here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Cleft lip is also known by which term?","options":["Palatoschisis","Cheiloschisis","Cheiloplasty","Exomphalos"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states cleft lip is also known as cheiloschisis.  Why A wrong: Palatoschisis is the term for cleft palate.  Why C wrong: Cheiloplasty is the surgical repair of cleft lip, not the condition name.  Why D wrong: Exomphalos is another name for omphalocele."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Normal lip formation occurs by fusing of the vermillion border between which weeks of gestation?","options":["5 to 12 weeks","9 to 12 weeks","3 to 5 weeks","12 to 16 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states normal lip formation occurs by fusing of the vermillion border between 5 to 12 weeks of gestation.  Why B wrong: 9 to 12 weeks is the timing for normal palate formation.  Why C wrong: 3 to 5 weeks is not the stated lip fusion window.  Why D wrong: 12 to 16 weeks is beyond the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Cleft palate is also known as which of the following?","options":["Cheiloschisis","Palatoschisis","Gastroschisis","Palatoplasty"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states cleft palate is also known as palatoschisis.  Why A wrong: Cheiloschisis is cleft lip.  Why C wrong: Gastroschisis is an abdominal wall defect lateral to the umbilicus.  Why D wrong: Palatoplasty is the surgical repair of cleft palate, not the condition name."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Normal palate formation occurs at which weeks of gestation?","options":["5 to 12 weeks","9 to 12 weeks","3 to 8 weeks","16 to 20 weeks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states normal palate formation occurs at 9 to 12 weeks of gestation.  Why A wrong: 5 to 12 weeks is the lip fusion window.  Why C wrong: 3 to 8 weeks is not stated for palate formation.  Why D wrong: 16 to 20 weeks is beyond the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the formation of the hard palate, which process fuses medially?","options":["The vermillion border","The lateral palatine process","The nasal septum only","The maxillary sinus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the lateral palatine process fuses medially to form the hard palate.  Why A wrong: The vermillion border relates to lip, not hard palate.  Why C wrong: The text specifies the lateral palatine process, not the nasal septum alone.  Why D wrong: The maxillary sinus is not involved in this fusion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the causes of cleft lip and cleft palate listed in the text.","options":["TORCH infection, radiation exposure","Drug abuse, alcohol use and smoking during pregnancy","Maternal diabetes, malnutrition and folic acid deficiency","Use of valproic acid, phenytoin","Excessive maternal calcium intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists TORCH infection, radiation, drug abuse, alcohol, smoking, maternal diabetes, malnutrition, folic acid deficiency, and medicines like valproic acid and phenytoin.  Why E wrong: Excessive maternal calcium intake is not listed as a cause."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the IMMEDIATE complications of cleft lip/palate as listed in the text.","options":["Poor sucking and poor feeding","Aspiration resulting in RDS","Growth retardation","Repeated otitis media","Hyper nasal speech"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists immediate complications as poor sucking/poor feeding and aspiration resulting in RDS.  Why C wrong: Growth retardation is listed as a later complication.  Why D wrong: Repeated otitis media is a later complication.  Why E wrong: Speech difficulty/hyper nasal speech is a later complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Repeated otitis media occurs as a later complication of cleft lip/palate. What anatomical reason does the text give?","options":["The ear's Eustachian tube is connected to the middle ear, increasing chance of frequent middle ear infection or hearing loss","Direct spread of oral candida to the ear","Congenital absence of the tympanic membrane","Blockage of the nasolacrimal duct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states repeated otitis media occurs because the ear's Eustachian tube is connected to the middle ear, increasing the chance of frequent middle ear infection or hearing loss.  Why B wrong: Candida spread is not the stated mechanism.  Why C wrong: Absence of the tympanic membrane is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Nasolacrimal duct blockage relates to the eye, not this complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"According to the classification in the text, if a cleft lip is unilateral, it is more commonly seen on which side?","options":["Right side","Left side","Midline","Both sides equally"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if unilateral, it is more common on the left side.  Why A wrong: Right side is not the stated more common side.  Why C wrong: A midline cleft is not the unilateral classification described.  Why D wrong: Equal bilateral distribution contradicts the unilateral left-side predominance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Because a baby with cleft lip cannot form a vacuum with the mouth to suck, which special feeding devices are recommended (select all that apply)?","options":["Paladai","Medela Haberman feeder","Pigeon bottle","NUK nipple and Anti Colic-Air System","Wide-bore IV cannula"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists Paladai, Medela Haberman feeders, Pigeon Bottles, NUK nipple and Anti Colic-Air System as special feeding devices.  Why E wrong: A wide-bore IV cannula is not a feeding device and is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A child with cleft palate is especially prone to which infection due to the anatomical connection with the middle ear?","options":["Otitis media","Meningitis","Pneumonia","Osteomyelitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the child is prone to infection, especially otitis media, with hearing problems from recurrent otitis media.  Why B wrong: Meningitis is not the stated predisposition.  Why C wrong: Pneumonia is not named as the special proneness here.  Why D wrong: Osteomyelitis is unrelated to the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the immediate nursing care of cleft lip/palate, which device can be used to place food particles in the back of the mouth or near the soft palate?","options":["Ryle's tube only","Palladai spoon","Tongue depressor","Oral thermometer"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states a Palladai spoon can be used to place food particles in the back of the mouth or near the soft palate.  Why A wrong: Ryle's tube feeding is mentioned separately, but the \"back of mouth/near soft palate\" placement device is the Palladai spoon.  Why C wrong: A tongue depressor is not a feeding device and is contraindicated post-palatoplasty.  Why D wrong: An oral thermometer is not for feeding and is contraindicated at the surgical site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The modified feeding technique used during bottle feeding of a cleft infant is called the ESSR technique. What does ESSR stand for?","options":["Enlarge the nipple, Stimulate the sucking, Swallow, Rest","Elevate, Suction, Support, Reposition","Educate, Support, Stabilize, Rehydrate","Extend, Stimulate, Suture, Repair"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines ESSR as Enlarge the nipple, Stimulate the sucking, Swallow, Rest.  Why B wrong: These words do not match the ESSR expansion given.  Why C wrong: This expansion is not the one stated.  Why D wrong: These surgical terms do not match ESSR."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After feeding a cleft infant, the nurse should position the infant how?","options":["Flat and prone","Elevate the head end of the bed","Trendelenburg position","Left lateral flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs to elevate the head end of the bed after feeding.  Why A wrong: Flat prone position is not advised after feeding here.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg (head-down) would increase aspiration/reflux risk.  Why D wrong: Flat left lateral is not the stated post-feeding position."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The surgical procedure for cleft lip repair is known as:","options":["Palatoplasty","Cheiloplasty","Pyloromyotomy","Colostomy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the surgical procedure for cleft lip is known as cheiloplasty.  Why A wrong: Palatoplasty is the repair of cleft palate.  Why C wrong: Pyloromyotomy is for pyloric stenosis.  Why D wrong: Colostomy is a surgical management for Hirschsprung disease."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"According to the text, the time of surgery for cleft lip (cheiloplasty) is:","options":["Within 6 months of age, from the day of birth","Between 6 to 24 months","At 2 years of age","Immediately after birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states cleft lip surgery time is within 6 months of age from the day of birthing.  Why B wrong: 6 to 24 months is the timing for cleft palate surgery.  Why C wrong: 2 years is beyond the stated cleft lip window.  Why D wrong: Immediate post-birth surgery is not stated; Kilner's rule of 10 must be met first."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Kilner's rule of 10 must be met before cleft lip surgery. Select ALL its four components.","options":["10 weeks of age","10 lb body weight","Haemoglobin 10 g","White blood cell count of less than 10000","10 mmHg systolic blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists Kilner's rule of 10 as 10 weeks of age, 10 lb body weight, haemoglobin 10 g, and WBC count less than 10000.  Why E wrong: A blood pressure of 10 mmHg is not part of the rule and is physiologically implausible."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In pre-operative nursing care of cleft lip, in which position should the infant be fed to prevent aspiration?","options":["Supine flat","Upright position","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states feed in upright position to prevent aspiration.  Why A wrong: Supine flat increases aspiration risk during feeding.  Why C wrong: Prone feeding is not advised for aspiration prevention here.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg (head-down) would worsen aspiration risk."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the POSToperative care after cleft LIP repair (cheiloplasty), which position is CONTRAINDICATED?","options":["Supine (back upright)","Side lying toward the un-operated side","Prone position","Elevated head end"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states prone position is contraindicated after cleft lip repair because it can produce damage at the surgical site.  Why A wrong: Supine (back upright) with arm/elbow restraint is advised.  Why B wrong: Side lying toward the un-operated side is permitted.  Why D wrong: Elevated head end is acceptable and not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which suturing technique is used for prevention of stretching at the cleft lip surgical site?","options":["Purse-string suture","Z technique of suturing","Mattress-only closure","Subcuticular running only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Z technique of suturing is used for prevention of stretching.  Why A wrong: Purse-string suture is not mentioned for this.  Why C wrong: Mattress-only closure is not the stated technique.  Why D wrong: Subcuticular running suture is not named here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which assistive device is used to prevent stretching at the cleft lip surgical site?","options":["Logan bow","Logan clamp","Barton bandage","Kocher's forceps"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text names the 'LOGAN BOW' as the assistive device used to prevent stretching at the surgical site.  Why B wrong: \"Logan clamp\" is not the device named.  Why C wrong: Barton bandage is used for jaw/mandible, not cleft lip.  Why D wrong: Kocher's forceps is a surgical instrument, not a stretch-prevention device."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"For how long should elbow restraints be used after cleft lip repair to prevent the infant from injuring the surgical site?","options":["At least 2 weeks","At least 2 days","At least 6 months","Only 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states elbow restraint should be used at least 2 weeks to prevent the infant from injuring or traumatizing the surgical site.  Why B wrong: 2 days is shorter than the stated period.  Why C wrong: 6 months is far longer than stated.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is insufficient per the text."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After cleft lip repair, oral feeding is allowed but the surgical site should be cleaned with what after each feeding?","options":["Hydrogen peroxide","Normal saline","Povidone iodine","Gentian violet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states oral feeding is allowed but the surgical site should be clean with normal saline after each feeding.  Why A wrong: Hydrogen peroxide is not the cleaning agent stated.  Why C wrong: Povidone iodine is not stated for after-feeding cleaning here.  Why D wrong: Gentian violet is the DOC for oral thrush, not for cleaning this site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The surgical procedure for cleft palate repair is known as:","options":["Cheiloplasty","Palatoplasty","Pyloromyotomy","Ramstedt's procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the surgical procedure for cleft palate is known as palatoplasty.  Why A wrong: Cheiloplasty is the cleft lip repair.  Why C wrong: Pyloromyotomy is for pyloric stenosis.  Why D wrong: Ramstedt's procedure is another name for pyloromyotomy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the time of surgery for cleft palate (palatoplasty)?","options":["Within 6 months from birth","Between 6 months to 24 months or before speech development","At 5 years of age","Immediately at birth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states cleft palate surgery time is between 6 months to 24 months or before speech development.  Why A wrong: Within 6 months is the cleft lip timing.  Why C wrong: 5 years is beyond the stated window and after speech development.  Why D wrong: Immediate birth surgery is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the POSToperative care after cleft PALATE repair (palatoplasty), which position is CONTRAINDICATED, and which is preferred?","options":["Prone/side-lying preferred; supine contraindicated","Supine preferred; prone contraindicated","Trendelenburg preferred; supine contraindicated","Sitting only; side-lying contraindicated"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states after palatoplasty provide prone & side lying position to prevent aspiration, and supine position is contraindicated.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct positioning (supine is contraindicated after palate repair).  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not the recommended post-op position.  Why D wrong: Side-lying is actually recommended, not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"For how many days is oral packing present at the surgical site after cleft palate repair?","options":["2 to 3 days","7 to 8 days","1 day","14 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states oral packing is present at the surgical site for 2 to 3 days.  Why B wrong: 7 to 8 days is the duration of Ryle's tube feeding, not oral packing.  Why C wrong: 1 day is shorter than stated.  Why D wrong: 14 days is longer than stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After cleft palate repair, feeding should be provided by Ryle's tube for how many days before oral feeding is started per doctor's order?","options":["First 2 to 3 days","First 7 to 8 days","First 24 hours","First 30 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states feeding should be provided by Ryle's tube for the first 7 to 8 days, then oral will be started per doctor order.  Why A wrong: 2 to 3 days is the oral packing duration, not the Ryle's tube feeding period.  Why C wrong: 24 hours is shorter than stated.  Why D wrong: 30 days is far longer than stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After cleft palate repair, which items must be AVOIDED because they can damage the surgical site (select all that apply)?","options":["Oral suction","Tongue depressor","Oral thermometer","Spoon and straw","Ryle's tube feeding"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to avoid oral suction, tongue depressor, oral thermometer, spoon and straw because they can produce damage at the surgical site.  Why E wrong: Ryle's tube feeding is actually recommended for the first 7 to 8 days, not avoided."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After cleft palate repair, parents should be instructed to avoid which of the following?","options":["Hard food items and toothbrush","Soft foods and water","Ryle's tube feeding","Elevating the head end"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text instructs parents to avoid hard food items and toothbrush after palatoplasty.  Why B wrong: Soft foods/water are not the items to be avoided.  Why C wrong: Ryle's tube feeding is recommended, not avoided.  Why D wrong: Elevating the head is not prohibited."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"From the \"Should Remember\" rules box, the \"Rule of 10\" is used for which condition?","options":["Dengue","Cleft lip","Burn clients (adults)","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The box states Rule 10 is used for cleft lip (Kilner's rule of 10).  Why A wrong: Rule of 20 is used for Dengue.  Why C wrong: Rule 9 is used for burn clients (adults).  Why D wrong: Rule of Havel is used for hypertension."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"According to the rules box, the \"Rule of 9\" and \"Rule of 5\" are used for which clients respectively?","options":["Rule 9 → burn clients (adults); Rule 5 → burn clients (childs)","Rule 9 → dengue; Rule 5 → cleft lip","Rule 9 → children; Rule 5 → adults","Rule 9 → PPH; Rule 5 → hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states Rule 9 is used for burn clients (adults) and Rule 5 for burn clients (childs).  Why B wrong: Dengue uses Rule of 20; cleft lip uses Rule 10.  Why C wrong: The adult/child assignment is reversed (9 = adults, 5 = children).  Why D wrong: PPH uses Rule of 30; hypertension uses Rule of Havel."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Match the rule to its use per the rules box: Rule of 15 and Rule of 20.","options":["Rule 15 → suspected hypoglycaemic reaction; Rule 20 → Dengue","Rule 15 → Dengue; Rule 20 → hypoglycaemia","Rule 15 → PPH; Rule 20 → diverticulitis","Rule 15 → cleft lip; Rule 20 → burns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states Rule 15 is used for suspected hypoglycaemic reaction and Rule of 20 for Dengue.  Why B wrong: The two assignments are reversed.  Why C wrong: PPH uses Rule of 30; diverticulitis uses Rule of 2.  Why D wrong: Cleft lip uses Rule 10; burns use Rule 9/5."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"According to the rules box, the \"Rule of 30\" and \"Rule of 2\" are used for which conditions?","options":["Rule 30 → PPH/haemorrhage management; Rule 2 → diverticulitis","Rule 30 → diverticulitis; Rule 2 → PPH","Rule 30 → dengue; Rule 2 → hypertension","Rule 30 → cleft lip; Rule 2 → burns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states Rule of 30 is used for PPH or haemorrhage management and Rule of 2 for diverticulitis.  Why B wrong: The two are reversed.  Why C wrong: Dengue uses Rule of 20; hypertension uses Rule of Havel.  Why D wrong: Cleft lip uses Rule 10; burns use Rule 9/5."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Per the rules box, the \"Rule 6\" for contraception after delivery states which timing for lactating vs non-lactating mothers?","options":["Lactating: 6 months (24 weeks); Non-lactating: 6 weeks (1.5 months)","Lactating: 6 weeks; Non-lactating: 6 months","Both at 6 months","Both at 6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The box states Rule 6 — contraceptive after delivery in a lactating mother at 6 months (24 weeks) and in a non-lactating mother at 6 weeks (1.5 months).  Why B wrong: The timings are reversed.  Why C wrong: Non-lactating is 6 weeks, not 6 months.  Why D wrong: Lactating is 6 months, not 6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Oesophageal atresia (EA) is defined as:","options":["Failure of the oesophagus to form a continuous pathway from the pharynx to the stomach","An abnormal connection between the trachea and oesophagus","Absence of an opening between the nasal passage and nasopharynx","Narrowing of the pyloric sphincter"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines EA as failure of the oesophagus to form a continuous pathway from the pharynx to the stomach.  Why B wrong: An abnormal trachea–oesophagus connection defines TEF, not EA.  Why C wrong: Absent nasal-to-nasopharynx opening defines choanal atresia.  Why D wrong: Pyloric narrowing describes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Tracheo-oesophageal fistula (TEF) is defined as:","options":["Failure of the oesophagus to form a continuous pathway","An abnormal connection between the trachea and oesophagus","No opening between the nasal passage and nasopharynx","Aganglionic segment of the colon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines TEF as an abnormal connection between the trachea and oesophagus.  Why A wrong: Failure to form a continuous pathway describes oesophageal atresia.  Why C wrong: Absent nasal opening describes choanal atresia.  Why D wrong: Aganglionic colon describes Hirschsprung disease."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Choanal atresia (AIIMS, DSSSB, ESIC) is defined as:","options":["No opening between one or both nasal passages and the nasopharynx","Abnormal connection between the trachea and oesophagus","Failure of oesophageal continuity","Herniation of abdominal organs at the umbilicus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines choanal atresia as no opening between one or both nasal passages and the nasopharynx.  Why B wrong: That describes TEF.  Why C wrong: That describes oesophageal atresia.  Why D wrong: Herniation at the umbilicus describes omphalocele."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON type of oesophageal atresia with TEF, and what is its approximate chance of occurrence?","options":["Esophageal atresia with upper TEF; 50%","Esophageal atresia with lower TEF; 80 to 90%","TEF without EA; 30%","Esophageal atresia without TEF; 10%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states esophageal atresia with lower TEF is the most common type with an occurrence of 80 to 90%, where the lower part of the esophagus connects to the trachea.  Why A wrong: Upper TEF is not the most common type, and 50% is not stated.  Why C wrong: TEF without EA is a rarer variant, not 30% or most common.  Why D wrong: EA without TEF is not the most common type."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the 5 types of oesophageal atresia/TEF listed in the text.","options":["Esophageal atresia without TEF","Esophageal atresia with upper TEF","Esophageal atresia with lower TEF","Esophageal atresia with both upper & lower TEF","TEF without EA"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists all five: EA without TEF, EA with upper TEF, EA with lower TEF, EA with both upper & lower TEF, and TEF without EA.  Why (all correct): Each option is explicitly one of the five enumerated types."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The cardinal \"3C\" sign of oesophageal atresia/TEF consists of which three features?","options":["Cough, Chest pain, Constipation","Coughing, Chocking (choking), Cyanosis","Colic, Cyanosis, Convulsion","Cyanosis, Constipation, Crying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text names the cardinal '3C' sign as Coughing, Chocking (choking), and Cyanosis.  Why A wrong: Chest pain and constipation are not part of the 3C sign.  Why C wrong: Colic and convulsion are not the 3Cs listed.  Why D wrong: Constipation and crying are not part of the 3C sign."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the signs and symptoms of oesophageal atresia/TEF listed in the text.","options":["Frothy saliva or drooling from the mouth","Choking, sneezing, and coughing during feeding with regurgitation through mouth and nose","Abdominal distension","Aspiration & respiratory distress; catheter cannot be passed into the stomach","Olive-shaped mass in the epigastrium"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists frothy saliva/drooling, choking/sneezing/coughing during feeding with regurgitation, abdominal distension, aspiration/respiratory distress, and inability to pass a catheter into the stomach.  Why E wrong: An olive-shaped mass is a sign of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, not EA/TEF."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which diagnostic method is used for oesophageal atresia/TEF per the text?","options":["USG and Chest X-ray with radio-opaque catheter","Lactose tolerance test","Rectal biopsy","MSAFP level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists physical examination and USG and Chest X-ray with radio-opaque catheter for diagnosis.  Why B wrong: The lactose tolerance test is for lactose intolerance.  Why C wrong: Rectal biopsy is confirmatory for Hirschsprung disease.  Why D wrong: MSAFP level is for abdominal wall defects."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In pre-operative care of oesophageal atresia/TEF, the infant should be maintained in what position to facilitate drainage and prevent aspiration of gastric secretion?","options":["Supine flat","Upright position at least 30 degrees","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states maintain in upright position at least 30 degrees upright to facilitate drainage and prevent aspiration of gastric secretion.  Why A wrong: Supine flat does not facilitate drainage and increases aspiration risk.  Why C wrong: Prone is not the stated position.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg (head-down) would worsen gastric secretion aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the pre-operative nursing care measures for oesophageal atresia/TEF listed in the text.","options":["Observe for signs of respiratory distress; suction if needed","Keep NPO and monitor I/O chart","Change position to prevent pneumonia","Maintain upright position at least 30 degrees","Feed orally in upright position every 2 hours"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists observing for respiratory distress with suction, keeping NPO with I/O monitoring, position changes to prevent pneumonia, and upright ≥30° positioning.  Why E wrong: The infant is kept NPO; oral feeding is not given pre-operatively."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In postoperative care of EA/TEF with a gastrostomy, how should the gastrostomy tube be managed?","options":["Kept closed at all times","Left open for prevention of abdominal distension, and closed for 1 hour after feeding","Left open for 1 hour after feeding","Removed immediately after surgery"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the gastrostomy tube should be left open for prevention of abdominal distension and should be closed for 1 hour after feeding.  Why A wrong: Keeping it closed at all times contradicts leaving it open to prevent distension.  Why C wrong: The tube is closed (not left open) for 1 hour after feeding.  Why D wrong: Immediate removal is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After EA/TEF surgery, feeding will usually be started after how many postoperative days?","options":["1 to 2 days","5 to 7 days","10 to 14 days","30 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states usually after postoperative 5 to 7 days feeding will start.  Why A wrong: 1 to 2 days is earlier than stated.  Why C wrong: 10 to 14 days is later than stated.  Why D wrong: 30 days is far beyond the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the signs of surgical site infection to monitor after EA/TEF surgery, as listed in the text.","options":["Redness and purulent discharge","Increased body temperature (hyperthermia)","Increased WBC count and warmth of the area","Decreased WBC count and cool, pale skin","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists redness/purulent discharge, hyperthermia, and increased WBC count with warmth as signs of surgical site infection.  Why D wrong: Decreased WBC and cool pale skin are not the infection signs listed.  Why E wrong: Hyperthermia (not hypothermia) is the stated sign."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is best described as:","options":["Abnormal thickening or narrowing of the pylorus sphincter in the lower portion of the stomach leading to gastric outlet obstruction","Absence of ganglion cells in the colon","Herniation of abdominal organs","Inflammation of intestinal mucosa from ischemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines it as abnormal thickening/narrowing of the pylorus sphincter in the lower portion of the stomach causing gastric outlet obstruction.  Why B wrong: Absent colonic ganglion cells describes Hirschsprung disease.  Why C wrong: Herniation of abdominal organs describes abdominal wall defects.  Why D wrong: Ischemic mucosal inflammation describes NEC."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"According to the text, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is classified as which type of condition?","options":["A congenital condition present at birth","An acquired condition because it develops after a few days of birth","An autoimmune condition","An infectious condition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it is an acquired condition because it is developed after a few days of birth.  Why A wrong: The text explicitly calls it acquired (developing after birth), not congenital.  Why C wrong: It is not described as autoimmune.  Why D wrong: It is not described as infectious."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the drug of choice for hypertrophic pyloric stenosis per the text?","options":["Atropine","Phenobarbitone","Gentian violet","Erythromycin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the drug of choice is atropine.  Why B wrong: Phenobarbitone is the DOC for neonatal seizures.  Why C wrong: Gentian violet is the DOC for oral thrush.  Why D wrong: Erythromycin is the ophthalmic DOC for ophthalmia neonatorum."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (FAQs)?","options":["Bilious projectile vomiting","Projectile vomiting that is non-bilious","Frothy drooling saliva","Ribbon-like foul-smelling stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states projectile vomiting (non-bilious) is the cardinal sign.  Why A wrong: The vomiting is specifically non-bilious, not bilious.  Why C wrong: Frothy drooling is a sign of EA/TEF.  Why D wrong: Ribbon-like foul-smelling stool is a Hirschsprung feature."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Persistent vomiting in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis leads to which acid-base disturbance?","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists metabolic alkalosis as a feature of pyloric stenosis (from loss of gastric acid via vomiting).  Why A wrong: Metabolic acidosis is seen in NEC and celiac crisis, not pyloric stenosis.  Why C wrong: Respiratory acidosis is not the stated disturbance.  Why D wrong: Respiratory alkalosis is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The classic palpable abdominal finding in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis (FAQs) is described as:","options":["A rigid board-like abdomen","An olive-shaped mass over the abdomen or mid-epigastric region","A pulsatile mass in the right iliac fossa","A fluctuant umbilical swelling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes an olive-shaped mass palpated over the abdomen or mid-epigastric region.  Why A wrong: A board-like abdomen suggests peritonitis, not this classic mass.  Why C wrong: A pulsatile RIF mass is not the pyloric finding.  Why D wrong: An umbilical swelling describes an abdominal wall defect/hernia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In pyloric stenosis, visible peristalsis over the abdomen is characteristically felt moving in which direction?","options":["From right to left","From left to right","Top to bottom","In a circular pattern"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states visible peristalsis is felt over the abdomen from left to right (along with constipation and distended epigastrium).  Why A wrong: Right to left is the reverse of the stated direction.  Why C wrong: Top to bottom is not stated.  Why D wrong: A circular pattern is not described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The surgical procedure for hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, creating an opening in the muscle layer of the pylorus, is called pyloromyotomy. It is also known by which eponym (DSSSB-2019)?","options":["Ramstedt's procedure","Kasai procedure","Ladd's procedure","Whipple procedure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states pyloromyotomy is also known as Ramstedt's procedure.  Why B wrong: The Kasai procedure is for biliary atresia, not mentioned here.  Why C wrong: Ladd's procedure is for malrotation, not stated here.  Why D wrong: The Whipple procedure is a pancreaticoduodenectomy, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the nursing care measures for hypertrophic pyloric stenosis listed in the text.","options":["Monitor strictly I/O chart and daily weight","Correct dehydration and electrolyte imbalances","Prepare for pyloromyotomy (Ramstedt's procedure)","Provide a gluten-free diet","Daily bowel irrigation with NS"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists strict I/O and daily weight monitoring, correcting dehydration/electrolyte imbalances, and pyloromyotomy.  Why D wrong: A gluten-free diet is nursing care for celiac disease.  Why E wrong: Daily bowel irrigation with NS is nursing care for Hirschsprung disease."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"An abnormal opening in the abdominal wall is called:","options":["Abdominal wall defect","Hiatal hernia","Diaphragmatic hernia","Inguinal hernia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states an abnormal opening in the abdominal wall is called an abdominal wall defect.  Why B wrong: A hiatal hernia involves the diaphragm/stomach, not the abdominal wall opening described.  Why C wrong: A diaphragmatic hernia involves the diaphragm.  Why D wrong: An inguinal hernia is in the groin, not the described anterior abdominal wall defect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Omphalocele is also known as which of the following?","options":["Gastroschisis","Exomphalos","Palatoschisis","Cheiloschisis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states omphalocele is also known as exomphalos.  Why A wrong: Gastroschisis is a distinct defect lateral to the umbilicus.  Why C wrong: Palatoschisis is cleft palate.  Why D wrong: Cheiloschisis is cleft lip."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In omphalocele, at what level is the abnormal opening located, and what covers the protruding organs?","options":["At the level of the umbilicus; organs covered by a transparent membrane or sac","Lateral to the umbilicus; no covering membrane","In the groin; covered by peritoneum","Over the diaphragm; covered by pleura"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states omphalocele has an abnormal opening at the level of the umbilicus, and the protruding organs are covered by a transparent membrane or sac.  Why B wrong: Lateral opening with no membrane describes gastroschisis.  Why C wrong: A groin location is not the described defect.  Why D wrong: A diaphragmatic/pleural location is not described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which statement correctly describes gastroschisis?","options":["Abnormal opening at the umbilicus with a covering sac","Abnormal opening lateral to the umbilicus, with protruding organs directly exposed to the environment and NO transparent membrane","A cleft in the hard palate","Narrowing of the pylorus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states gastroschisis has an abnormal opening lateral to the umbilicus, with organs protruding and directly exposed to the environment (transparent membrane absent).  Why A wrong: An umbilical opening with a covering sac describes omphalocele.  Why C wrong: A hard palate cleft describes cleft palate.  Why D wrong: Pyloric narrowing describes pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which is the KEY differentiating feature between omphalocele and gastroschisis according to the text?","options":["Presence (omphalocele) versus absence (gastroschisis) of a covering transparent membrane/sac","Omphalocele is lateral; gastroschisis is at the umbilicus","Only gastroschisis has a sac","Both always have a sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states omphalocele organs are covered by a transparent membrane/sac, whereas in gastroschisis the transparent membrane is absent and organs are directly exposed.  Why B wrong: This reverses the locations (omphalocele = umbilicus, gastroschisis = lateral).  Why C wrong: Gastroschisis lacks a sac; omphalocele has one.  Why D wrong: Gastroschisis has no sac, so \"both always have a sac\" is false."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the causes of abdominal wall defects listed in the text.","options":["Malnutrition with folic acid deficiency","Radiation exposure","Infection and drug abuse","Other unknown causes","Excess maternal iron intake"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists malnutrition with folic acid deficiency, radiation exposure, infection and drug abuse, and other unknown causes.  Why E wrong: Excess maternal iron intake is not a listed cause."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Maternal Serum Alpha-Feto Protein (MSAFP) is assessed antenatally for abdominal wall defects. An INCREASED MSAFP value indicates which of the following?","options":["Down syndrome","Abdominal wall defects and neural tube defect","Normal pregnancy","Gestational diabetes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if MSAFP value is increased it indicates abdominal wall defects and neural tube defect.  Why A wrong: A decreased MSAFP value indicates Down syndrome, not an increased value.  Why C wrong: An elevated MSAFP is abnormal, not normal.  Why D wrong: Gestational diabetes is not linked to MSAFP here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A DECREASED MSAFP value indicates which condition?","options":["Neural tube defect","Down syndrome","Abdominal wall defect","Anencephaly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if the MSAFP value is decreased it indicates Down syndrome.  Why A wrong: Neural tube defects are associated with increased MSAFP.  Why C wrong: Abdominal wall defects are associated with increased MSAFP.  Why D wrong: Anencephaly (a neural tube defect) would raise, not lower, MSAFP."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In the pre-operative care of an abdominal wall defect (AIIMS, PGI, GMCH), the FIRST action to protect the defect is to:","options":["Cover the defect immediately with a sterile gauze piece moistened with normal saline","Apply direct plastic wrap onto the sac","Push the organs back into the abdomen","Leave the defect open to air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to cover the defect immediately with a sterile gauze piece with normal saline.  Why B wrong: Plastic drape should NOT be in direct contact with the sac (it can cause necrosis); it goes over the gauze.  Why C wrong: Manually reducing the organs is not advised and can cause damage.  Why D wrong: Leaving it open risks dehydration and heat/fluid loss."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Why should a plastic drape NOT be placed in direct contact with the sac in an abdominal wall defect (PGI-2018)?","options":["Because it can cause necrosis","Because it causes hypothermia","Because it dissolves the sac","Because it increases MSAFP"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the plastic drape goes over the gauze for protection against dehydration, but must not directly contact the sac because it can cause necrosis.  Why B wrong: The stated reason is necrosis, not hypothermia.  Why C wrong: The drape does not dissolve the sac.  Why D wrong: MSAFP is an antenatal test, unrelated to drape placement."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In an abdominal wall defect, which position is CONTRAINDICATED because it can cause further damage to the sac (ESIC, AIIMS)?","options":["Supine","Prone","Side-lying","Elevated head"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to provide the supine position and that the prone position is contraindicated because it can cause further damage to the sac.  Why A wrong: Supine is the recommended position.  Why C wrong: Side-lying is not the specifically contraindicated position named.  Why D wrong: Elevated head is not contraindicated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In an abdominal wall defect, vital signs (especially temperature) should be monitored how frequently, and why (JIPMER-2017)?","options":["Every 2 hours; because the infant can lose heat through the sac","Every 8 hours; because of infection risk","Once daily; to track weight","Every 30 minutes; to detect vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states to monitor vital signs frequently or every 2 hours, particularly temperature, because the infant can lose heat through the sac.  Why B wrong: Every 8 hours is not the stated frequency.  Why C wrong: Once daily is insufficient for the stated heat-loss risk.  Why D wrong: Every 30 minutes and vomiting detection are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the confirmatory treatment for an abdominal wall defect, and when should it be done?","options":["Surgery, performed as early as possible","Antibiotics only","Observation for 1 year","Colostomy at 2 years"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the confirmatory treatment is surgery and it should be performed as early as possible.  Why B wrong: Antibiotics alone are not the confirmatory treatment.  Why C wrong: Prolonged observation delays needed early surgery.  Why D wrong: A delayed colostomy is not the stated treatment for abdominal wall defects."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Celiac disease is described in the text as which type of condition?","options":["An infectious condition","An allergic condition with allergy to gluten protein","A congenital enzyme deficiency of lactase","An autoimmune vascular disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This matches the source's description (celiac disease as a gluten-related condition). [Important: celiac disease is actually an AUTOIMMUNE (T-cell/HLA-DQ2-DQ8-mediated) gluten-sensitive enteropathy, NOT a true IgE-mediated allergy; wheat allergy is a separate entity. Answer keyed to the source's wording.]  Why A wrong: It is described as allergic, not infectious.  Why C wrong: Lactase deficiency describes lactose intolerance, not celiac disease.  Why D wrong: It is described here as an allergic (gluten) condition, not a vascular disorder."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Gluten protein is present in which food particles, remembered by the trick \"BROW\"?","options":["Beans, Rice, Oats, Wheat","Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat","Bread, Rice, Onion, Wheat","Barley, Rice, Oats, Walnut"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states gluten is present in Barley, Rye, Oats and Wheats (trick: BROW).  Why A wrong: Beans and rice are not gluten sources; rice is actually allowed.  Why C wrong: Onion is not in the BROW list, and rice is allowed.  Why D wrong: Rice and walnut are not part of the BROW gluten list."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In celiac disease, without digestion of gluten protein, the toxicity of which amino acid can occur in affected newborns?","options":["Glutamine amino acid","Glycine","Leucine","Tryptophan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states without digestion of gluten protein, toxicity of glutamine amino acid can occur in the affected newborns.  Why B wrong: Glycine is not the amino acid named.  Why C wrong: Leucine is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: Tryptophan is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Regarding the progression of celiac disease, which absorption is affected in the EARLY stage versus the LATER stage?","options":["Early: fat absorption affected; Later: protein, carbohydrate, mineral and electrolyte absorption affected with growth failure and muscle wasting","Early: protein absorption; Later: only fat absorption","Early: carbohydrate only; Later: no absorption issues","Early: mineral absorption; Later: fat only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in the early stage fat absorption is affected, and in the later stage protein, carbohydrate, mineral and electrolyte absorption are affected with growth failure and muscle wasting.  Why B wrong: This reverses the stages (fat is early, not later-only).  Why C wrong: The later stage does have absorption problems.  Why D wrong: Fat is affected early, not in the later stage only."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Due to the allergic condition in celiac disease, which structure of the small intestine is damaged?","options":["The villi of the small intestine","The pyloric sphincter","The ganglion cells","The gastric mucosa only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the villi of the small intestine are damaged due to the allergic condition.  Why B wrong: Pyloric sphincter thickening describes pyloric stenosis.  Why C wrong: Ganglion cell absence describes Hirschsprung disease.  Why D wrong: The text specifies villous (small intestine) damage, not just gastric mucosa."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"\"Steatorrhea,\" a sign of celiac disease, is defined as:","options":["Blood in the stool","Presence of undigested fat in stool","Ribbon-like stool","Watery stool without fat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines steatorrhea as the presence of undigested fat in stool.  Why A wrong: Blood in stool is a feature of NEC, not the definition of steatorrhea.  Why C wrong: Ribbon-like stool is a Hirschsprung feature.  Why D wrong: Steatorrhea specifically involves fat in the stool."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Celiac disease causes deficiency of which group of vitamins due to malabsorption of fat?","options":["Water-soluble vitamins (B and C)","Fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K","Vitamin B12 only","Folic acid only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K occurs due to malabsorption of fat.  Why A wrong: Water-soluble vitamins are not the fat-malabsorption-related group named.  Why C wrong: B12 is water-soluble and not the group specified.  Why D wrong: Folic acid is water-soluble and not the group specified here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which alternative (allowed) food particles are recommended in the nursing care of celiac disease?","options":["Rice, millets, corn, potatoes","Barley, rye, oats, wheat","Bread, biscuits, noodles","Cookies and toast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states alternative food particles can be used like rice, millets, corn, potatoes etc.  Why B wrong: Barley, rye, oats, wheat contain gluten and are restricted (BROW).  Why C wrong: Bread, biscuits, and noodles are among the restricted foods.  Why D wrong: Cookies and toast are among the restricted foods."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Celiac crisis is best defined as:","options":["Sudden appearance of severe symptoms of celiac disease or severe episodes of dehydration and metabolic acidosis from diarrhoea, usually triggered by infection and ingestion of excessive gluten protein","Sudden pyloric obstruction","Herniation of abdominal contents","Acute otitis media"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines celiac crisis as sudden severe symptoms/dehydration and metabolic acidosis from diarrhoea, triggered by infection and ingestion of excessive gluten protein.  Why B wrong: Pyloric obstruction relates to pyloric stenosis.  Why C wrong: Herniation of abdominal contents describes abdominal wall defects.  Why D wrong: Otitis media is a cleft complication, not celiac crisis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Foods ALLOWED in celiac disease are remembered by the trick \"M-PCR\" (PGI, ILBS-2021). What does it represent?","options":["Milk, Potato, Corn, Rye","Millets, Potatoes, Corn, Rice","Meat, Peas, Corn, Rice","Millets, Peanuts, Cheese, Rice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states foods allowed in celiac disease (trick M-PCR) are Millets, Potatoes, Corn and Rice.  Why A wrong: Rye is a restricted (gluten) food, not allowed.  Why C wrong: Meat and peas are not the M-PCR expansion (though meat is allowed as an \"other\").  Why D wrong: Peanuts and cheese are not the M-PCR expansion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Besides the M-PCR staples, select ALL the OTHER foods that are ALLOWED in celiac disease per the text.","options":["Milk & milk products","Eggs","Meats","All fruits and vegetables","Biscuits and breads"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists milk & milk products, eggs, meats, and all fruits and vegetables as other allowed foods.  Why E wrong: Biscuits and breads are listed among the restricted foods (contain gluten)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the foods that are RESTRICTED in celiac disease (GMCH, AIIMS-2019) per the text.","options":["Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat (BROW)","Biscuits, Breads","Cookies, Toast, Noodles","Rice and potatoes","Corn and millets"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text restricts Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat (BROW) and others like biscuits, breads, cookies, toast, noodles.  Why D wrong: Rice and potatoes are allowed foods (M-PCR), not restricted.  Why E wrong: Corn and millets are allowed foods, not restricted."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Hirschsprung disease is also called:","options":["Congenital aganglionic megacolon","Necrotising enterocolitis","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","Exomphalos"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states Hirschsprung disease is also called congenital aganglionic megacolon.  Why B wrong: NEC is a separate ischemic mucosal disease.  Why C wrong: Pyloric stenosis is a gastric outlet obstruction.  Why D wrong: Exomphalos is another name for omphalocele."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the underlying pathophysiology of Hirschsprung disease as described in the text?","options":["Absence of ganglionic nerve fibres in a portion of the GIT, so nerve stimulation and peristalsis do not occur in that aganglionic portion","Thickening of the pyloric sphincter","Herniation of organs through the umbilicus","Ischemic damage to intestinal mucosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states that in the absence of ganglionic nerve fibres, nerve stimulation is not received and peristalsis does not occur in that aganglionic portion.  Why B wrong: Pyloric thickening describes pyloric stenosis.  Why C wrong: Umbilical herniation describes omphalocele.  Why D wrong: Ischemic mucosal damage describes NEC."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In Hirschsprung disease, due to absence of peristalsis, what happens proximal to the aganglionic portion?","options":["Food particles accumulate and the proximal portion becomes enlarged (megacolon)","The proximal portion becomes narrowed","The distal portion enlarges only","The colon becomes ischemic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states food particles accumulate just proximal to the aganglionic portion and the proximal portion becomes enlarged.  Why B wrong: The proximal portion enlarges, not narrows.  Why C wrong: It is the proximal (not distal) portion that enlarges.  Why D wrong: Ischemia describes NEC, not this mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The exact cause of Hirschsprung disease is unknown but may occur in a baby with a defect in which chromosome?","options":["Chromosome no. 10","Chromosome no. 21","Chromosome no. 13","Chromosome no. 18"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states it may occur in a baby with a defect in chromosome no. 10.  Why B wrong: Chromosome 21 is associated with Down syndrome, not stated here.  Why C wrong: Chromosome 13 (Patau) is not stated.  Why D wrong: Chromosome 18 (Edwards) is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"What is the confirmatory diagnostic test for Hirschsprung disease?","options":["Rectal biopsy","Lactose tolerance test","Chest X-ray with radio-opaque catheter","MSAFP level"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states diagnosis is made by physical examination, USG and rectal biopsy (confirmatory diagnostic test).  Why B wrong: The lactose tolerance test is for lactose intolerance.  Why C wrong: Chest X-ray with radio-opaque catheter is for EA/TEF.  Why D wrong: MSAFP is for abdominal wall defects."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Which portion of the bowel is MOST COMMONLY affected in Hirschsprung disease (FAQs)?","options":["Recto-sigmoid colon","Ascending colon","Ileum","Duodenum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the most commonly affected portion is the recto-sigmoid colon.  Why B wrong: The ascending colon is not the most commonly affected site.  Why C wrong: The ileum is not the stated common site.  Why D wrong: The duodenum is not the stated common site."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the signs and symptoms of Hirschsprung disease seen IN THE NEWBORN.","options":["Failure to pass meconium","Abdominal distension","Poor sucking & poor feeding","Projectile non-bilious vomiting","Olive-shaped abdominal mass"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists failure to pass meconium, abdominal distension, and poor sucking & poor feeding in the newborn.  Why D wrong: Projectile non-bilious vomiting is the cardinal sign of pyloric stenosis.  Why E wrong: An olive-shaped mass is a pyloric stenosis finding."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"In older children with Hirschsprung disease, the characteristic stool is described as (SJH, AIIMS-2018):","options":["Bilious vomiting with ribbon-like, foul-smelling, hard, pellet-like stool","Watery, odourless stool","Fatty, bulky steatorrhea","Blood-streaked mucoid stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text describes older children with bilious vomiting and ribbon-like, foul-smelling (hard) & pellet-like stool.  Why B wrong: Watery, odourless stool is not the described Hirschsprung stool.  Why C wrong: Fatty, bulky steatorrhea is a celiac disease feature.  Why D wrong: Blood-streaked stool suggests NEC, not the described ribbon-like stool."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"The \"Blast sign\" in Hirschsprung disease (JIPMER-2017) refers to:","options":["Sudden passing of stool like a blast during rectal examination","A blast of gas from the stomach on auscultation","An explosive projectile vomiting","A loud bowel sound on the left side"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines the blast sign as sudden passing of stool like a blast during rectal examination.  Why B wrong: It refers to stool during rectal exam, not gastric gas on auscultation.  Why C wrong: Explosive vomiting is not the blast sign; it involves stool.  Why D wrong: A bowel sound is not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"Select ALL the nursing care measures for Hirschsprung disease listed in the text.","options":["Monitor all vitals and bowel sounds frequently","Give stool softener as per order","Daily bowel irrigation with normal saline (NS)","Surgical management is colostomy","Provide a gluten-free diet"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists monitoring vitals/bowel sounds, stool softener per order, daily bowel irrigation with NS, and colostomy as surgical management.  Why E wrong: A gluten-free diet is nursing care for celiac disease, not Hirschsprung disease."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"After colostomy for Hirschsprung disease, a normal, healthy stoma should appear how (GMCH-2019)?","options":["Pale and dusky","Slightly red or pink and moist","Black and dry","Blue and cold"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the normal stoma will appear slightly red or pink and moist.  Why A wrong: A pale/dusky stoma suggests poor perfusion, not a normal stoma.  Why C wrong: A black, dry stoma indicates necrosis.  Why D wrong: A blue, cold stoma indicates ischemia/compromised circulation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A preterm, formula-fed neonate develops lethargy, abdominal distension, vomiting, blood in stool, and metabolic acidosis. Which condition is MOST likely?","options":["Necrotising enterocolitis","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","Oral thrush","Lactose intolerance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These are the chief clinical features of NEC, which is more common in preterm and formula-fed infants.  Why B wrong: Pyloric stenosis presents with non-bilious projectile vomiting and metabolic alkalosis, not blood in stool/acidosis.  Why C wrong: Oral thrush presents with milky white oral patches, not this systemic GI picture.  Why D wrong: Lactose intolerance causes watery diarrhoea/flatus, not bloody stool with acidosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A 3-week-old formula-fed infant has non-bilious projectile vomiting, a palpable olive-shaped epigastric mass, and metabolic alkalosis. Which is the MOST likely diagnosis?","options":["Hirschsprung disease","Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","Celiac disease","Omphalocele"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Non-bilious projectile vomiting, an olive-shaped mass, and metabolic alkalosis are the classic features of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis.  Why A wrong: Hirschsprung presents with failure to pass meconium and ribbon-like stool, not an olive mass with alkalosis.  Why C wrong: Celiac disease causes steatorrhea and malabsorption, not projectile vomiting with an olive mass.  Why D wrong: Omphalocele is a visible umbilical wall defect, not vomiting with a palpable pyloric mass."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A newborn drools frothy saliva and develops coughing, choking, and cyanosis during the first feeding; a catheter cannot be passed into the stomach. Which is the priority action?","options":["Keep NPO and maintain the infant in an upright position (at least 30 degrees)","Start oral feeding in the supine position","Begin gluten-free formula","Apply an eye ointment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These features indicate oesophageal atresia/TEF; pre-operative care is to keep NPO, observe for respiratory distress, and maintain upright (≥30°) positioning to prevent aspiration.  Why B wrong: Oral feeding is contraindicated (NPO); supine positioning increases aspiration risk.  Why C wrong: Gluten-free formula is for celiac disease, irrelevant here.  Why D wrong: Eye ointment addresses ophthalmia neonatorum, not this airway/feeding emergency."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A neonate is born with abdominal organs protruding through a defect lateral to the umbilicus with NO covering membrane. What is the immediate priority nursing action?","options":["Cover the exposed organs with sterile gauze moistened with normal saline and prevent heat loss","Place the infant prone","Apply plastic wrap directly onto the organs","Begin oral feeds immediately"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This is gastroschisis (no membrane); the immediate care is to cover the defect with sterile normal-saline gauze, provide the supine position, and monitor temperature every 2 hours because heat is lost through the exposed sac.  Why B wrong: The prone position is contraindicated because it can cause further damage to the sac.  Why C wrong: Plastic drape must not directly contact the sac (necrosis risk); it goes over the gauze.  Why D wrong: The priority is protecting the defect and thermoregulation, not oral feeding."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Neonatal seizure & febrile seizure drug of choice, Ophthalmia neonatorum…","question":"A newborn fails to pass meconium in the first 48 hours, has abdominal distension, and on rectal examination suddenly passes stool \"like a blast.\" Which condition and confirmatory test are indicated?","options":["Hirschsprung disease; confirmed by rectal biopsy","Pyloric stenosis; confirmed by lactose tolerance test","Celiac disease; confirmed by MSAFP","NEC; confirmed by chest X-ray"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Failure to pass meconium, distension, and the blast sign point to Hirschsprung disease, confirmed by rectal biopsy.  Why B wrong: Pyloric stenosis features projectile vomiting; the lactose tolerance test is for lactose intolerance.  Why C wrong: Celiac disease is diagnosed by endoscopy/biopsy; MSAFP is for abdominal wall defects.  Why D wrong: NEC is not confirmed by chest X-ray, and the blast sign is specific to Hirschsprung disease."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A dark red colour of a child's stoma most likely indicates which of the following?","options":["Dehydration","Active bleeding","Cyanosis","Infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per the source, a dark red colour of the stoma indicates active bleeding.  Why A wrong: Dehydration is indicated by a dry stoma.  Why C wrong: Cyanosis is indicated by a blue/dusky blue stoma.  Why D wrong: Infection is indicated by purulent discharge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A dry stoma in a paediatric patient is an indicator of:","options":["Active bleeding","Cyanosis","Dehydration","Infection"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A dry stoma indicates dehydration (AIIMS-2018).  Why A wrong: Active bleeding is shown by a dark red colour.  Why B wrong: Cyanosis is shown by a blue/dusky blue stoma.  Why D wrong: Infection is shown by purulent discharge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A blue/dusky blue colour of the stoma indicates:","options":["Dehydration","Cyanosis","Active bleeding","Infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A blue/dusky blue stoma indicates cyanosis (poor perfusion/oxygenation).  Why A wrong: Dehydration is indicated by a dry stoma.  Why C wrong: Active bleeding is indicated by a dark red stoma.  Why D wrong: Infection is indicated by purulent discharge."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Purulent discharge from a stoma is an indicator of:","options":["Cyanosis","Dehydration","Infection","Active bleeding"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Purulent discharge indicates infection (DSSSB-2019).  Why A wrong: Cyanosis is shown by a blue/dusky blue stoma.  Why B wrong: Dehydration is shown by a dry stoma.  Why D wrong: Active bleeding is shown by a dark red stoma."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are recognised surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon (Hirschsprung's disease)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Duhamel procedure","Soave procedure","Swenson procedure","Whipple procedure","Kasai procedure"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Duhamel, Soave (Soaves), and Swenson procedures for aganglionic megacolon.  Why D wrong: The Whipple procedure is a pancreaticoduodenectomy, not a megacolon surgery.  Why E wrong: The Kasai procedure (hepatoportoenterostomy) is for biliary atresia, not aganglionic megacolon."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Telescoping of one portion of the bowel into another adjacent portion is called:","options":["Volvulus","Intussusception","Hirschsprung's disease","Malrotation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Intussusception is defined as telescoping of bowel into another portion (NPCIL-2018).  Why A wrong: Volvulus is a twisting of the bowel, not telescoping.  Why C wrong: Hirschsprung's disease is aganglionic megacolon (absence of ganglion cells).  Why D wrong: Malrotation is an abnormal rotation of the intestine during development."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"What is the most common site of intussusception, and what percentage of cases does it account for?","options":["Ileocecal junction, 90%","Duodenojejunal junction, 50%","Rectosigmoid junction, 70%","Gastroesophageal junction, 90%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of intussusception is the ileocecal junction, accounting for 90% of cases (ILBS-Delhi).  Why B wrong: The duodenojejunal junction is not the common site cited.  Why C wrong: The rectosigmoid junction is not the site or percentage given.  Why D wrong: The gastroesophageal junction is in the upper GI tract, not the site of intussusception."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"An infant with intussusception classically presents with severe colicky/paroxysmal abdominal pain evidenced by which characteristic behaviour?","options":["Arching the back and extending the legs","Kicking and drawing the legs up to the abdomen","Lying completely still and rigid","Rolling repeatedly from side to side"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states severe colicky/paroxysmal abdominal pain is evidenced by kicking and drawing legs up to the abdomen.  Why A wrong: Back arching with leg extension (opisthotonus) is not the described behaviour.  Why C wrong: The child is not still; the pain is paroxysmal and causes drawing up of legs.  Why D wrong: Rolling side to side is not the described sign."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The classic \"current-jelly\" like stool seen in intussusception is composed of:","options":["Undigested fat and mucus","Mucus and blood","Pure fresh blood only","Rice-water fluid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Current-jelly like stool consists of mucus and blood (FAQs).  Why A wrong: Fat with mucus (steatorrhoea) is not the current-jelly description.  Why C wrong: It is not pure fresh blood but a mix of mucus and blood.  Why D wrong: Rice-water stool is a feature of cholera, not intussusception."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"On abdominal examination in intussusception, a sausage-shaped mass is characteristically palpated in which region (in the ileo-cecal type)?","options":["Left lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Epigastric region","Suprapubic region"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sausage-shaped mass is found in the right upper quadrant in the ileo-cecal type (RRB-2019).  Why A wrong: Left lower quadrant is not the described site.  Why C wrong: Epigastric region is not where the mass is palpated in ileo-cecal intussusception.  Why D wrong: Suprapubic region is not the described location."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following radiological signs are seen in intussusception? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Target sign (doughnut sign)","Claw sign","Pitchfork sign","Sail sign","Double-bubble sign"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists target sign (doughnut sign), claw sign, and pitchfork sign as seen in intussusception.  Why D wrong: The sail sign is associated with a normal thymus on chest X-ray, not intussusception.  Why E wrong: The double-bubble sign is characteristic of duodenal atresia, not intussusception."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In intussusception, Rigler's sign on X-ray may be present if which complication has occurred?","options":["Dehydration","Perforation","Spontaneous reduction","Rectal prolapse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Rigler's sign may be present if perforation has occurred.  Why A wrong: Rigler's sign relates to free air (perforation), not dehydration.  Why C wrong: Spontaneous reduction is indicated by passage of brown (normal) stool, not Rigler's sign.  Why D wrong: Rectal prolapse is a separate condition unrelated to Rigler's sign."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which investigations are used to diagnose intussusception? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Physical examination","Ultrasonography (USG)","Bone marrow biopsy","Sweat chloride test"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists physical examination and USG for the diagnosis of intussusception.  Why C wrong: Bone marrow biopsy is used for haematological disorders, not intussusception.  Why D wrong: The sweat chloride test is used to diagnose cystic fibrosis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"During the nursing care of a child with intussusception, passage of brown (normal) stool is significant because it indicates:","options":["Onset of perforation","Automatic (spontaneous) resolving of the condition","Worsening obstruction","Development of dehydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Monitoring for brown stool (normal stool) indicates automatic resolving of the condition.  Why A wrong: Perforation is suggested by sudden severe pain, hypotension, and shock, not by normal brown stool.  Why C wrong: Normal brown stool suggests resolution, not worsening obstruction.  Why D wrong: Normal stool passage is a reassuring sign, not a marker of dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are signs of intestinal rupture (perforation) that a nurse must monitor for in a child with intussusception? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Sudden relief of pain followed by more severe generalized pain","Hypotension and tachycardia","Oliguria and anuria","Cold extremities and shock","Passage of formed brown stool"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sudden relief then more severe pain (generalized peritonitis), hypotension/tachycardia (bleeding or fluid loss), oliguria/anuria, and cold extremities/shock are all listed signs of intestinal rupture.  Why E wrong: Passage of formed brown stool indicates spontaneous resolution, not rupture."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"What is the primary non-surgical treatment used to reduce an intussusception?","options":["Barium enema alone for diagnosis only","Hydrostatic reduction pressure of water","Nasogastric suction only","High-dose antibiotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrostatic reduction pressure of water is used to treat intussusception.  Why A wrong: Diagnosis alone does not treat the condition; hydrostatic reduction is therapeutic.  Why C wrong: NG suction is supportive and does not reduce the intussusception.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics do not reduce the telescoped bowel."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"If hydrostatic pressure reduction is not successful in intussusception, the next step in management is:","options":["Repeat hydrostatic reduction indefinitely","Surgery","Long-term observation at home","Oral rehydration only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if hydrostatic pressure is not useful, then surgery can be performed.  Why A wrong: Indefinite repetition is not advised; surgery is indicated when hydrostatic reduction fails.  Why C wrong: Home observation is inappropriate for a failed reduction.  Why D wrong: Oral rehydration does not correct the mechanical intussusception."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"After surgery for intussusception, if the condition repeatedly recurs, the definitive surgical management involves:","options":["Cutting and removing the decreased muscle-tone portion, then end-to-end anastomosis","Permanent ileostomy without resection","Gastric bypass","Splenectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that for repeated recurrence, the decreased-muscle-tone portion is cut and removed, then end-to-end anastomosis is done.  Why B wrong: A permanent ileostomy without resection is not the described procedure.  Why C wrong: Gastric bypass is a bariatric procedure, unrelated.  Why D wrong: Splenectomy is removal of the spleen, unrelated to recurrent intussusception."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The classic triad of intussusception consists of vomiting, abdominal pain, and:","options":["Fever","Bloody (currant-jelly) stool","Constipation","Jaundice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The classic triad is vomiting (may be bilious), abdominal pain (intermittent, colicky/crampy), and bloody stool (currant jelly).  Why A wrong: Fever is not part of the stated classic triad.  Why C wrong: Constipation is not one of the three triad components.  Why D wrong: Jaundice is not part of the intussusception triad."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the classic triad of intussusception, the abdominal pain is described as:","options":["Constant and dull","Intermittent, colicky or crampy","Burning and epigastric","Sharp and localized to McBurney's point"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the classic triad, abdominal pain is intermittent, colicky or crampy.  Why A wrong: The pain is intermittent/colicky, not constant and dull.  Why C wrong: Burning epigastric pain suggests gastritis/ulcer, not intussusception.  Why D wrong: McBurney's point localization is typical of appendicitis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which type of vomiting is characteristically seen in intussusception, along with abdominal distension?","options":["Projectile non-bilious vomiting","Bilious vomiting","Coffee-ground vomiting","Feculent vomiting only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists bilious vomiting and abdominal distension as signs of intussusception (vomiting in the triad may be bilious).  Why A wrong: Projectile non-bilious vomiting is classic for pyloric stenosis.  Why C wrong: Coffee-ground vomiting suggests upper GI bleeding, not the described feature.  Why D wrong: Feculent vomiting is not the specified feature."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Diarrhoea is defined as passage of loose and watery stool:","options":["More than 3 times in a day","More than 5 times in a day","Once a day for 2 weeks","More than 3 times in a week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines diarrhoea as passage of loose and watery stool more than 3 times in a day.  Why B wrong: The threshold is more than 3, not 5, times per day.  Why C wrong: Once a day is not the definition of diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: The frequency is per day, not per week."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"What is the most common cause of infantile diarrhoea?","options":["Rotavirus","Salmonella typhi","Vibrio cholerae","Giardia lamblia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rotavirus is stated as the most common cause of infantile diarrhoea.  Why B wrong: Salmonella typhi is a bacterial cause but not the most common cause of infantile diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Vibrio cholerae is a bacterial cause, not the most common infantile cause.  Why D wrong: Giardia lamblia is a parasitic cause, not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which viruses are listed as viral infectious causes of diarrhoea? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Rotavirus","Measles virus","Hepatitis A virus","Rabies virus"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists rotavirus and measles virus as viral causes of diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Hepatitis A virus is not listed as a diarrhoeal cause on this page.  Why D wrong: Rabies virus is not a cause of diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which organisms are listed as bacterial infectious causes of diarrhoea? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Salmonella typhi","Salmonella paratyphi","Vibrio cholerae","Candida albicans"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Salmonella typhi, Salmonella paratyphi, and Vibrio cholerae as bacterial causes.  Why D wrong: Candida albicans is listed as a fungal cause, not bacterial."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are parasitic causes of diarrhoea per the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Entamoeba histolytica","Giardia lamblia","Candida albicans","Rotavirus"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Entamoeba histolytica and Giardia lamblia are listed as parasitic causes.  Why C wrong: Candida albicans is a fungal cause.  Why D wrong: Rotavirus is a viral cause."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which organism is listed as the fungal cause of diarrhoea?","options":["Candida albicans","Aspergillus fumigatus","Cryptococcus neoformans","Histoplasma capsulatum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Candida albicans is listed as the fungal cause of diarrhoea.  Why B wrong: Aspergillus fumigatus is not listed as a diarrhoeal cause here.  Why C wrong: Cryptococcus neoformans is not listed.  Why D wrong: Histoplasma capsulatum is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are listed as non-infectious causes of diarrhoea? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Lactose intolerance","Celiac disease","Malabsorption syndrome","Hirschsprung's disease","Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists lactose intolerance, celiac disease, malabsorption syndrome, Hirschsprung's disease, and IBD as non-infectious causes.  (All options are correct as listed on the page.)"},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to duration, acute diarrhoea is defined as lasting:","options":["Less than 2 weeks, mostly infectious","More than 4 weeks, mostly non-infectious","2 to 4 weeks with infectious cause","More than 6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute diarrhoea is < 2 weeks and is mostly infectious.  Why B wrong: > 4 weeks (mostly non-infectious) describes chronic diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: 2–4 weeks with infectious cause describes persistent diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: > 6 weeks is not the definition given for acute diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Chronic diarrhoea, classified by duration, is defined as diarrhoea lasting:","options":["Less than 2 weeks","More than 4 weeks, mostly non-infectious","Exactly 14 days","Less than 1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic diarrhoea is > 4 weeks and mostly non-infectious.  Why A wrong: < 2 weeks describes acute diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: 14 days/2–4 weeks with infectious cause is persistent diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: < 1 week is not the definition of chronic diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Persistent diarrhoea is defined as diarrhoea lasting:","options":["< 2 weeks","> 14 days or 2–4 weeks with an infectious cause","> 6 months","Exactly 3 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Persistent diarrhoea is > 14 days or 2–4 weeks with an infectious cause.  Why A wrong: < 2 weeks describes acute diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: > 6 months is not the definition given.  Why D wrong: 3 days is not the definition of persistent diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"\"Rice water stool\" is characteristically associated with which condition?","options":["Typhoid","Cholera","Dysentery","Celiac disease"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rice water stool is associated with cholera.  Why A wrong: Pea soup water stool is associated with typhoid.  Why C wrong: Blood in the stool is associated with dysentery.  Why D wrong: Celiac disease is a non-infectious cause, not characterized by rice-water stool."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"\"Pea soup water stool\" is characteristically seen in which condition?","options":["Cholera","Typhoid","Dysentery","Rotavirus infection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pea soup water stool is associated with typhoid.  Why A wrong: Rice water stool is associated with cholera.  Why C wrong: Blood in the stool is associated with dysentery.  Why D wrong: Rotavirus is not associated with pea-soup stool in the source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The presence of blood in the stool is characteristically associated with:","options":["Cholera","Typhoid","Dysentery","Lactose intolerance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Blood in the stool is associated with dysentery.  Why A wrong: Rice water stool is cholera.  Why B wrong: Pea soup water stool is typhoid.  Why D wrong: Lactose intolerance is a non-infectious cause, not classically bloody."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which electrolyte imbalances are commonly seen as signs of diarrhoea? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypokalaemia","Hyponatraemia","Hypocalcaemia","Hyperkalaemia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, and hypocalcaemia as electrolyte imbalances in diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: Hyperkalaemia is not listed; potassium is typically lost (hypokalaemia)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A child with prolonged diarrhoea is at risk of developing which acid-base disturbance?","options":["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists metabolic acidosis as a sign/symptom of diarrhoea (loss of bicarbonate in stool).  Why A wrong: Metabolic alkalosis is typical of vomiting/loss of gastric acid, not diarrhoea.  Why C wrong: Respiratory acidosis is due to CO2 retention, not diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: Respiratory alkalosis results from hyperventilation, not diarrhoea."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Dehydration is best defined as:","options":["Excessive loss of body fluids caused by illness, sweating, or inadequate intake / more water loss compared to intake","Excess accumulation of interstitial fluid","Loss of electrolytes only, with normal water balance","An increase in total body water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dehydration is excessive loss of body fluids from illness, sweating, or inadequate intake / more water loss than intake.  Why B wrong: Excess interstitial fluid is oedema, the opposite of dehydration.  Why C wrong: Dehydration involves fluid loss, not electrolyte loss with normal water.  Why D wrong: Increased total body water is fluid overload, not dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are listed clinical signs/symptoms of dehydration in a child? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Depressed/sunken fontanels","Decreased skin turgor","Decreased tear production","Oliguria and anuria (in severe dehydration)","Bulging fontanelle"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Sunken fontanels, decreased skin turgor, decreased tear production, and oliguria/anuria (severe) are all listed dehydration signs.  Why E wrong: A bulging fontanelle indicates raised intracranial pressure/overhydration, not dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Tachycardia and hypotension in a dehydrated child reflect:","options":["Fluid overload","Circulatory compromise from fluid volume loss","Improved perfusion","Normal newborn findings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tachycardia and hypotension are listed dehydration signs, reflecting circulatory compromise from volume loss.  Why A wrong: Fluid overload typically causes hypertension/oedema, not this pattern.  Why C wrong: These are signs of worsening perfusion, not improvement.  Why D wrong: They are pathological signs of dehydration, not normal findings."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to severity, mild dehydration corresponds to what percentage of body fluid loss?","options":["2 to 5%","6 to 9%","Greater than 10%","15 to 20%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild dehydration is 2 to 5% of body fluid loss.  Why B wrong: 6 to 9% is moderate dehydration.  Why C wrong: > 10% is severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: 15–20% is not a category listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Moderate dehydration corresponds to what percentage of body fluid loss?","options":["2 to 5%","6 to 9%","Greater than 10%","Less than 1%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate dehydration is 6 to 9% of body fluid loss.  Why A wrong: 2–5% is mild dehydration.  Why C wrong: > 10% is severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: < 1% is not a category listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Severe dehydration is defined as body fluid loss of:","options":["2 to 5%","6 to 9%","Greater than 10%","Exactly 8%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe dehydration is > 10% of body fluid loss.  Why A wrong: 2–5% is mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: 6–9% is moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: 8% falls within the moderate range."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In isotonic dehydration, which statements are true? (Select all that apply.)","options":["There is an equal loss of water and electrolytes","No fluid shifting from ICF to ECF occurs","Cell swelling occurs","The remaining body fluid becomes hypertonic"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Isotonic dehydration has equal loss of water and electrolytes, with no fluid shifting from ICF to ECF.  Why C wrong: Cell swelling occurs in hypotonic dehydration, not isotonic.  Why D wrong: Hypertonic remaining fluid occurs in hypertonic dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In hypotonic dehydration, the loss of electrolytes is higher than fluid loss. Which of the following are correct features? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Remaining body fluid is hypotonic","Cell swelling occurs","Fluid shifts from ECF to ICF","Hypertonic fluids may be used for management","Cell shrinkage occurs"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In hypotonic dehydration, electrolyte loss > fluid loss so remaining fluid is hypotonic; cell swelling occurs; fluid shifts from ECF to ICF; hypertonic fluids may be used for management.  Why E wrong: Cell shrinkage occurs in hypertonic dehydration, not hypotonic (swelling occurs instead)."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In hypertonic dehydration, which of the following occur? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Fluid loss is higher than electrolyte loss","Remaining body fluid is hypertonic","Fluid shifts from ICF to ECF","Cell shrinkage occurs","Hypotonic fluids may be used for management"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: In hypertonic dehydration, fluid loss > electrolyte loss, remaining fluid is hypertonic, fluid shifts from ICF to ECF, cell shrinkage occurs, and hypotonic fluids may be used for management — all as listed.  (All options are correct per the source.)"},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In hypotonic dehydration, in which direction does the fluid shift occur?","options":["ICF to ECF","ECF to ICF","No shifting occurs","Plasma to lymph only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hypotonic dehydration, fluid shifting from ECF to ICF occurs (causing cell swelling).  Why A wrong: ICF-to-ECF shift occurs in hypertonic dehydration.  Why C wrong: No shift occurs in isotonic dehydration.  Why D wrong: Plasma-to-lymph shift is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the degree-of-dehydration table, the general condition of a child with moderate dehydration is described as:","options":["Well, alert","Restless, thirsty, irritable","Drowsy, cold extremities, lethargic","Comatose"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In moderate dehydration, general condition is restless, thirsty, irritable.  Why A wrong: \"Well, alert\" describes mild dehydration.  Why C wrong: \"Drowsy, cold extremities, lethargic\" describes severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: \"Comatose\" is not the term used in the table."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In severe dehydration, the eyes are described in the degree-of-dehydration table as:","options":["Normal","Sunken","Very sunken, dry","Bulging"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In severe dehydration, eyes are very sunken and dry.  Why A wrong: Normal eyes correspond to mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: Sunken (not \"very sunken, dry\") corresponds to moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: Bulging eyes are not listed for any dehydration category."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the degree-of-dehydration table, the anterior fontanelle in moderate dehydration is:","options":["Normal","Depressed","Very depressed","Bulging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In moderate dehydration, the anterior fontanelle is depressed.  Why A wrong: Normal fontanelle corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why C wrong: Very depressed corresponds to severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: A bulging fontanelle is not listed for dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the degree-of-dehydration table, the \"Tears\" finding in mild dehydration is:","options":["Present","Absent","Excessive","Bloody"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tears are present in mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: Tears are absent in both moderate and severe dehydration.  Why C wrong: Excessive tears are not a dehydration finding.  Why D wrong: Bloody tears are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the degree-of-dehydration table, the mouth and tongue finding in moderate dehydration is:","options":["Moist","Sticky","Dry","Bleeding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The mouth + tongue are sticky in moderate dehydration.  Why A wrong: Moist corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why C wrong: Dry corresponds to severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: Bleeding is not a listed finding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the degree-of-dehydration table, skin turgor in severe dehydration is described as:","options":["Slightly decreased","Decreased (1–2 sec)","Very decreased (>2 sec)","Increased"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In severe dehydration, skin turgor is very decreased (>2 sec).  Why A wrong: Slightly decreased corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: Decreased (1–2 sec) corresponds to moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: Increased skin turgor is not a dehydration finding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the degree-of-dehydration table, thirst in severe dehydration is described as:","options":["Normal","Drinks eagerly, thirsty","Unable to drink or drinks poorly","Absent thirst with excessive drinking"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In severe dehydration, the child is unable to drink or drinks poorly.  Why A wrong: Normal thirst corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: \"Drinks eagerly, thirsty\" corresponds to moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: This combination is not listed in the table."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the degree-of-dehydration table, the pulse in moderate dehydration is described as:","options":["Slightly increased","Rapid, weak","Rapid, sometimes impalpable","Slow and bounding"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In moderate dehydration, pulse is rapid and weak.  Why A wrong: Slightly increased pulse corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why C wrong: Rapid, sometimes impalpable corresponds to severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: Slow, bounding pulse is not a listed dehydration finding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the degree-of-dehydration table, blood pressure in severe dehydration is:","options":["Normal","Depressed","Decreased, may be unrecordable","Elevated"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In severe dehydration, BP is decreased and may be unrecordable.  Why A wrong: Normal BP corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: Depressed BP corresponds to moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: Elevated BP is not a dehydration finding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the degree-of-dehydration table, the respiratory rate in severe dehydration is described as:","options":["Slightly increased","Increased","Deep, rapid","Slow, shallow"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In severe dehydration, respiration is deep and rapid (compensating for metabolic acidosis).  Why A wrong: Slightly increased corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: \"Increased\" corresponds to moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: Slow shallow breathing is not the listed finding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the degree-of-dehydration table, urine output in severe dehydration is:","options":["Normal","Reduced","Markedly reduced","Increased"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In severe dehydration, urine output is markedly reduced.  Why A wrong: Normal urine output corresponds to mild dehydration.  Why B wrong: Reduced corresponds to moderate dehydration.  Why D wrong: Increased urine output is not a dehydration finding."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Match the treatment plan to dehydration severity per the degree-of-dehydration table: mild, moderate, and severe dehydration are treated respectively with:","options":["Plan A, Plan B, Plan C","Plan C, Plan B, Plan A","Plan B, Plan A, Plan C","Plan A, Plan C, Plan B"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mild = Plan A, moderate = Plan B, severe = Plan C.  Why B wrong: The order is reversed.  Why C wrong: Plans are mismatched to severity.  Why D wrong: Moderate and severe plans are swapped."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are listed diagnostic methods for dehydration? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Physical examination","Stool culture","Capillary refill test (CRT)","Stool hanging drop test","Skin pinch test"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Physical examination, stool culture, CRT, stool hanging drop test, and skin pinch test are all listed diagnostic methods.  (All options are correct per the source.)"},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The stool hanging drop test is used for the diagnosis of which condition, and what does it demonstrate?","options":["Typhoid; visualizing Salmonella spores","Cholera; visualizing the darting movement of Vibrio cholerae","Amoebiasis; visualizing trophozoites","Giardiasis; visualizing cysts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The hanging drop test is used to diagnose cholera, in which the darting movement of Vibrio cholerae can be seen.  Why A wrong: The test is for cholera (Vibrio), not typhoid.  Why C wrong: Amoebiasis is not diagnosed by the hanging drop test in the source.  Why D wrong: Giardiasis is not the stated indication."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"For the skin pinch test used to assess dehydration, which is the best site, and how are the results interpreted?","options":["Forearm; slow recoil = no dehydration","Abdomen; if skin goes back quickly = no dehydration, if it does not go back quickly (decreased turgor) = dehydration","Forehead; immediate recoil = severe dehydration","Thigh; slow recoil = normal hydration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The abdomen is the best site; quick recoil = no dehydration, and failure to return quickly (decreased skin turgor) = dehydration (FAQs).  Why A wrong: The best site is the abdomen, and slow recoil indicates dehydration, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: The forehead is not the best site and the interpretation is wrong.  Why D wrong: The thigh is not the stated best site and slow recoil indicates dehydration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The Capillary Refilling Test (CRT) is also known by which other name?","options":["Nail blanch test","Tourniquet test","Rumpel-Leede test","Allen's test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CRT is also known as the nail blanch test, used to monitor dehydration and tissue blood flow.  Why B wrong: The tourniquet test is a capillary fragility test, not CRT.  Why C wrong: The Rumpel-Leede test assesses capillary fragility, not CRT.  Why D wrong: Allen's test evaluates hand arterial patency, not capillary refill."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"During the Capillary Refilling Test (CRT), the applied pressing time is:","options":["2 seconds","3 seconds","5 seconds","10 seconds"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the applied pressing time is 5 seconds, measured with a manual stopwatch.  Why A wrong: 2 seconds is the normal neonatal CRT value, not the pressing time.  Why B wrong: 3 seconds is the normal CRT value for children/adults, not the pressing time.  Why D wrong: 10 seconds is not the stated pressing time."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In children and adults, the most reliable site for CRT testing is the:","options":["Finger pulp (not at the fingernail)","Fingernail bed","Midpoint of the sternum","Earlobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In children and adults, the most reliable CRT site is the finger pulp (not at the fingernail).  Why B wrong: The source specifically states NOT at the fingernail.  Why C wrong: The midpoint of the sternum is the reliable site in neonates.  Why D wrong: The earlobe is not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In children and adults, the normal capillary refill time (CRT) is:","options":["≤ 2 seconds","≤ 3 seconds","≤ 5 seconds","≤ 10 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In children and adults, normal CRT is ≤ 3 seconds (abnormal is > 3 seconds; note the page's printed \"abnormal is ≤ 3 seconds\" is a typographical error — abnormal should be > 3 seconds).  Why A wrong: ≤ 2 seconds is the normal value in neonates.  Why C wrong: 5 seconds is the pressing time, not the normal CRT.  Why D wrong: ≤ 10 seconds is not a stated normal value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In neonates, the most reliable site for CRT testing and the normal value are:","options":["Finger pulp; ≤ 3 seconds","Midpoint of the sternum; ≤ 2 seconds (abnormal > 2 seconds)","Fingernail; ≤ 5 seconds","Earlobe; ≤ 2 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In neonates, the most reliable CRT site is the midpoint of the sternum, with normal ≤ 2 seconds and abnormal > 2 seconds.  Why A wrong: The finger pulp with ≤ 3 seconds applies to children/adults, not neonates.  Why C wrong: The fingernail is not the reliable site, and 5 seconds is not the value.  Why D wrong: The earlobe is not the stated neonatal site."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Per the \"Also Remember\" note, the normal capillary refilling time in children is:","options":["< 2 seconds","< 3 seconds","< 5 seconds","< 1 second"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source's \"Also Remember\" note states normal capillary refilling time in children is < 3 seconds (Raj, CHO-2020).  Why A wrong: < 2 seconds is the neonatal value.  Why C wrong: 5 seconds is the pressing time, not the normal CRT.  Why D wrong: < 1 second is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are components of the management of dehydration listed in the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Plan A, B, or C for fluids based on assessed level of hydration","Giving ORS and zinc supplementation","Antibiotics only if indicated","Routine antibiotics for all cases"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes Plan A/B/C fluids by hydration level, ORS + zinc supplementation, and antibiotics only if indicated.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are given only if indicated, not routinely for all."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the treatment of diarrhoea, which is the FIRST priority?","options":["Correction of electrolyte imbalances","Treating dehydration","Starting antibiotics","Giving antiemetics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states first priority is to treat dehydration, then second priority is correction of electrolyte imbalances.  Why A wrong: Electrolyte correction is the second priority, not first.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics are only given if indicated, not the first priority.  Why D wrong: Antiemetics are not listed as the first priority."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the source, dysentery is classified into which two types?","options":["Bacterial dysentery (Shigella) and amoebic dysentery (Entamoeba histolytica)","Viral and fungal dysentery","Acute and chronic dysentery","Osmotic and secretory dysentery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dysentery is classified as bacterial dysentery (caused by Shigella) and amoebic dysentery (caused by Entamoeba histolytica).  Why B wrong: Viral and fungal are not the two types of dysentery given.  Why C wrong: Acute/chronic is a duration classification of diarrhoea, not dysentery types.  Why D wrong: Osmotic/secretory describes diarrhoea mechanisms, not dysentery types."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"For which severity of dehydration is IV fluid therapy reserved, according to the source?","options":["Mild dehydration only","Moderate dehydration only","Only severe dehydration","All degrees of dehydration"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states IV therapy is used only for severe dehydration; moderate dehydration is treated by ORS.  Why A wrong: Mild dehydration is treated at home with fluids/ORS (Plan A).  Why B wrong: Moderate dehydration is treated by ORS (Plan B), not IV.  Why D wrong: IV is not used for all degrees; only severe."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Regarding storage, ORS is stable at room temperature, which means it:","options":["Requires cold-chain storage","Does NOT require cold-chain storage","Must be frozen before use","Must be refrigerated at all times"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ORS is stable at room temperature and therefore does not require cold-chain storage.  Why A wrong: The point explicitly is that cold chain is NOT required.  Why C wrong: Freezing is not required.  Why D wrong: Refrigeration is not required."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Under Plan A (mild dehydration), the amount of ORS solution over 24 hours for a child < 2 years is:","options":["500 ml/24 hours","1000 ml/24 hours","1500 ml/24 hours","2000 ml/24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For < 2 years, Plan A provides 500 ml/24 hours of ORS.  Why B wrong: 1000 ml/24 hours is for the 2 to 10 year group.  Why C wrong: 1500 ml/24 hours is for > 10 years.  Why D wrong: 2000 ml/24 hours is not a Plan A value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Under Plan A, the amount of ORS over 24 hours for a child aged 2 to 10 years is:","options":["500 ml/24 hours","1000 ml/24 hours","1500 ml/24 hours","2500 ml/24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 2 to 10 years, Plan A provides 1000 ml/24 hours.  Why A wrong: 500 ml/24 hours is for < 2 years.  Why C wrong: 1500 ml/24 hours is for > 10 years.  Why D wrong: 2500 ml/24 hours is not a Plan A value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Under Plan A, the amount of ORS over 24 hours for a child > 10 years is:","options":["500 ml/24 hours","1000 ml/24 hours","1500 ml/24 hours","3000 ml/24 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For > 10 years, Plan A provides 1500 ml/24 hours.  Why A wrong: 500 ml/24 hours is for < 2 years.  Why B wrong: 1000 ml/24 hours is for 2 to 10 years.  Why D wrong: 3000 ml/24 hours is not a Plan A value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Per Plan A, after how many hours should any remaining prepared ORS solution be discarded?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After 24 hours, the remaining ORS solution should be discarded (AIIMS, ESIC, CHO).  Why A wrong: 6 hours is not the discard time given.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not the stated discard time.  Why D wrong: 48 hours is too long; discard after 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In Plan B (moderate dehydration), fluid loss from each loose stool should be replaced with what amount of ORS?","options":["5 ml/kg/loose stool","10 to 20 ml/kg/loose stool","30 ml/kg/loose stool","50 ml/kg/loose stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Plan B, fluid loss from stool is replaced at 10 to 20 ml/kg/loose stool.  Why A wrong: 5 ml/kg is not the specified replacement amount.  Why C wrong: 30 ml/kg is the loading dose in IV therapy (Plan C), not stool replacement.  Why D wrong: 50 ml/kg is not the specified amount."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the Plan B ORS chart, the amount of ORS over 24 hours for the 4–11 months age group is:","options":["200–400 ml/24 hours","400–600 ml/24 hours","600–800 ml/24 hours","800–1200 ml/24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For 4–11 months, the Plan B chart gives 400–600 ml/24 hours.  Why A wrong: 200–400 ml/24 hours corresponds to the youngest (< 4 months) group.  Why C wrong: 600–800 ml/24 hours corresponds to the 12–23 months group.  Why D wrong: 800–1200 ml/24 hours corresponds to 2–4 years."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the Plan B ORS chart, the amount of ORS over 24 hours for the 2–4 years age group is:","options":["400–600 ml/24 hours","600–800 ml/24 hours","800–1200 ml/24 hours",">2200 ml/24 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For 2–4 years, the Plan B chart gives 800–1200 ml/24 hours.  Why A wrong: 400–600 ml/24 hours is for 4–11 months.  Why B wrong: 600–800 ml/24 hours is for 12–23 months.  Why D wrong: >2200 ml/24 hours is for >15 years."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the Plan B ORS chart, the amount of ORS over 24 hours for the >15 years age group is:","options":["800–1200 ml/24 hours","1200–2200 ml/24 hours",">2200 ml/24 hours","400–600 ml/24 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: For >15 years, the Plan B chart gives >2200 ml/24 hours.  Why A wrong: 800–1200 ml/24 hours is for 2–4 years.  Why B wrong: 1200–2200 ml/24 hr is for the 5–14 years group.  Why D wrong: 400–600 ml/24 hours is for 4–11 months."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Under Plan C (severe dehydration), which statement about ORS is correct?","options":["ORS is the first-line treatment","ORS is NOT recommended; IV fluid therapy is required","ORS should be given at double the Plan B dose","ORS is given only via nasogastric tube"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In Plan C, ORS is not recommended and IV fluid therapy is required (amount depends on age group).  Why A wrong: ORS is not first-line in severe dehydration; IV therapy is.  Why C wrong: Doubling ORS is not recommended for severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: The source specifies IV therapy, not NG-tube ORS, for Plan C."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The commonly used IV fluid for severe dehydration (Plan C) is:","options":["Normal saline (0.9% NaCl)","Ringer's Lactate (RL) or Hartmann's solution","5% Dextrose only","Half-normal saline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The commonly used IV fluid is RL or Hartmann's solution.  Why A wrong: Normal saline is not the stated common fluid.  Why C wrong: 5% Dextrose alone is not the recommended resuscitation fluid.  Why D wrong: Half-normal saline is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"For a child LESS than 2 years with severe dehydration (Plan C), the total duration of IV therapy is planned for:","options":["3 hours","6 hours","12 hours","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For < 2 years, Plan C IV therapy is planned for 6 hours.  Why A wrong: 3 hours is the plan for children more than 2 years.  Why C wrong: 12 hours is not the stated duration.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"For a child less than 2 years (Plan C), the IV loading dose and maintenance dose are:","options":["Loading 30 ml/kg over 1 hour; maintenance 70 ml/kg over 5 hrs","Loading 70 ml/kg over 1 hour; maintenance 30 ml/kg over 5 hrs","Loading 30 ml/kg over ½ hour; maintenance 70 ml/kg over 2½ hrs","Loading 10 ml/kg over 1 hour; maintenance 100 ml/kg over 5 hrs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For < 2 years, loading dose is 30 ml/kg over 1 hour and maintenance is 70 ml/kg over 5 hrs.  Why B wrong: The loading (30) and maintenance (70) amounts are reversed.  Why C wrong: The ½-hour loading / 2½-hr maintenance schedule is for children > 2 years.  Why D wrong: 10 ml/kg and 100 ml/kg are not the stated doses."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"For a child MORE than 2 years with severe dehydration (Plan C), the total IV therapy duration and dosing schedule are:","options":["6 hours; loading 30 ml/kg over 1 hr, maintenance 70 ml/kg over 5 hrs","3 hours; loading 30 ml/kg over ½ hour, maintenance 70 ml/kg over 2½ hrs","12 hours; loading 70 ml/kg over 1 hr","24 hours; loading 10 ml/kg over ½ hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For > 2 years, therapy is planned for 3 hours, with loading dose 30 ml/kg over ½ hour and maintenance 70 ml/kg over 2½ hrs.  Why A wrong: The 6-hour / 1-hr loading / 5-hr maintenance schedule is for < 2 years.  Why C wrong: 12 hours and 70 ml/kg loading are not the stated values.  Why D wrong: 24 hours and 10 ml/kg loading are not the stated values."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In Plan C, syrup zinc should be given when the child is:","options":["Unconscious","Conscious","Febrile","Vomiting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states syrup zinc should also be given when the child is conscious.  Why A wrong: Giving oral zinc to an unconscious child risks aspiration and is not advised.  Why C wrong: Consciousness (not fever status) is the stated criterion.  Why D wrong: Vomiting is not the stated criterion; consciousness is."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Oral rehydration therapy (ORS) should be used in babies with which severity of dehydration?","options":["Mild to moderate dehydration; it should NOT be given in severe dehydration","Severe dehydration only","All degrees including severe","Only in the absence of any dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ORS therapy can be given in mild to moderate dehydration and should not be given in severe dehydration.  Why B wrong: ORS is not recommended in severe dehydration (IV is required).  Why C wrong: ORS is contraindicated as sole therapy in severe dehydration.  Why D wrong: ORS is used to treat dehydration, not only when there is none."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the source, the amount of sodium chloride (NaCl) in one ORS pouch is:","options":["1.5 gm","2.6 gm","2.9 gm","13.5 gm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The ORS composition lists sodium chloride (NaCl) as 2.6 gm.  Why A wrong: 1.5 gm is potassium chloride (KCl).  Why C wrong: 2.9 gm is trisodium citrate.  Why D wrong: 13.5 gm is glucose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The amount of potassium chloride (KCl) in one ORS pouch is:","options":["1.5 gm","2.6 gm","2.9 gm","13.5 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ORS composition lists potassium chloride (KCl) as 1.5 gm.  Why B wrong: 2.6 gm is sodium chloride.  Why C wrong: 2.9 gm is trisodium citrate.  Why D wrong: 13.5 gm is glucose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The amount of trisodium citrate in one ORS pouch is:","options":["1.5 gm","2.6 gm","2.9 gm","13.5 gm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The ORS composition lists trisodium citrate as 2.9 gm.  Why A wrong: 1.5 gm is potassium chloride.  Why B wrong: 2.6 gm is sodium chloride.  Why D wrong: 13.5 gm is glucose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The amount of glucose in one ORS pouch is:","options":["1.5 gm","2.6 gm","2.9 gm","13.5 gm"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The ORS composition lists glucose as 13.5 gm.  Why A wrong: 1.5 gm is potassium chloride.  Why B wrong: 2.6 gm is sodium chloride.  Why C wrong: 2.9 gm is trisodium citrate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The total weight of one ORS pouch is:","options":["20.5 gm","13.5 gm","2.9 gm","245 gm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The total weight of one ORS pouch is 20.5 gm.  Why B wrong: 13.5 gm is the glucose content only.  Why C wrong: 2.9 gm is the trisodium citrate content only.  Why D wrong: 245 is the total osmotic value (mmol/lt), not weight in grams."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the old ORS formula, which ingredient was used instead of trisodium citrate?","options":["Sodium bicarbonate","Potassium chloride","Calcium gluconate","Magnesium sulphate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The old formula contained sodium bicarbonate instead of trisodium citrate (IGIMS-2018).  Why B wrong: KCl is a component of both formulas, not the replaced ingredient.  Why C wrong: Calcium gluconate is not an ORS constituent here.  Why D wrong: Magnesium sulphate is not an ORS constituent here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The normal osmotic value of body fluids, per the source, is:","options":["245 mmol/lt","270–310 mmol/lt","75 mmol/lt","65 mmol/lt"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal osmotic value of body fluids is 270–310 mmol/lt.  Why A wrong: 245 mmol/lt is the total osmotic value of one ORS pouch (which makes it hypotonic).  Why C wrong: 75 mmol/ltr is the sodium (and chloride) value in ORS.  Why D wrong: 65 mmol/ltr is the glucose value in ORS."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The new WHO ORS was launched globally and in India in which years/months respectively?","options":["WHO in January 2004; India in June 2004","WHO in June 2004; India in January 2004","WHO in 2002; India in 2003","WHO in 2005; India in 2006"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The new ORS was launched by WHO in January 2004 and in India in June 2004.  Why B wrong: The dates are reversed.  Why C wrong: 2002/2003 are not the stated years.  Why D wrong: 2005/2006 are not the stated years."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"One pouch of ORS should be diluted in how much plain water?","options":["500 ml","1 litre","2 litres","250 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One pouch of ORS should be diluted in 1 litre of plain water.  Why A wrong: 500 ml is not the specified dilution volume.  Why C wrong: 2 litres would over-dilute the ORS.  Why D wrong: 250 ml would under-dilute the ORS."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"After preparation, ORS solution should be used within what time, after which the remaining solution should be discarded?","options":["6 hours","12 hours","24 hours","72 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Prepared ORS should be used within 24 hours; after that, the remaining solution should be discarded.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is not the stated shelf-life for prepared ORS.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not the stated time.  Why D wrong: 72 hours is too long; discard after 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Match the ORS osmotic value: the concentration of sodium in ORS is:","options":["75 mmol/ltr","65 mmol/ltr","20 mmol/ltr","10 mmol/ltr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The osmotic value of sodium in ORS is 75 mmol/ltr.  Why B wrong: 65 mmol/ltr is the glucose value.  Why C wrong: 20 mmol/ltr is the potassium value.  Why D wrong: 10 mmol/ltr is the citrate value."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The osmotic value of glucose in ORS is:","options":["75 mmol/ltr","65 mmol/ltr","20 mmol/ltr","10 mmol/ltr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In WHO/UNICEF reduced-osmolarity ORS, glucose (anhydrous) is 75 mmol/L — deliberately equimolar (1:1) with sodium to drive Na-glucose co-transport. [Corrected: the source transposed glucose and chloride.]  Why B wrong: 65 mmol/L is the value for chloride, not glucose.  Why C wrong: 20 mmol/ltr is potassium.  Why D wrong: 10 mmol/ltr is citrate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The osmotic value of chloride in ORS is:","options":["75 mmol/ltr","65 mmol/ltr","20 mmol/ltr","10 mmol/ltr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In WHO/UNICEF reduced-osmolarity ORS, chloride is 65 mmol/L. [Corrected: the source transposed chloride and glucose.]  Why A wrong: 75 mmol/L is the value for sodium and glucose, not chloride.  Why C wrong: 20 mmol/ltr is potassium.  Why D wrong: 10 mmol/ltr is citrate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The osmotic value of potassium in ORS is:","options":["75 mmol/ltr","65 mmol/ltr","20 mmol/ltr","10 mmol/ltr"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The osmotic value of potassium in ORS is 20 mmol/ltr.  Why A wrong: 75 mmol/ltr is sodium/chloride.  Why B wrong: 65 mmol/ltr is glucose.  Why D wrong: 10 mmol/ltr is citrate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The osmotic value of citrate in ORS is:","options":["75 mmol/ltr","65 mmol/ltr","20 mmol/ltr","10 mmol/ltr"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The osmotic value of citrate in ORS is 10 mmol/ltr.  Why A wrong: 75 mmol/ltr is sodium/chloride.  Why B wrong: 65 mmol/ltr is glucose.  Why C wrong: 20 mmol/ltr is potassium."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The total osmotic value of one pouch of ORS is 245 mmol/lt. This makes ORS a solution that is:","options":["Isotonic","Hypertonic","Hypotonic","Colloid"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: With a total osmotic value of 245 mmol/lt (below the body fluid osmolality of 270–310 mmol/lt), ORS is a hypotonic solution (AIIMS, ESIC, CHO).  Why A wrong: ORS is hypotonic, not isotonic, relative to body fluids.  Why B wrong: ORS is below body fluid osmolality, so it is hypotonic, not hypertonic.  Why D wrong: ORS is a crystalloid electrolyte-glucose solution, not a colloid."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In ORS, what is the specific role of glucose?","options":["It facilitates absorption of sodium and hence water","It corrects metabolic acidosis","It replaces potassium losses","It provides antibacterial action"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glucose facilitates absorption of sodium and hence water (sodium-glucose cotransport).  Why B wrong: Correcting acidosis is the role of citrate.  Why C wrong: Replacing potassium loss is the role of sodium/potassium, not glucose.  Why D wrong: Glucose has no antibacterial role in ORS."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In ORS, sodium and potassium serve to:","options":["Correct acidosis","Replace the loss of these essential elements in diarrhoea and vomiting","Facilitate glucose absorption","Increase stool frequency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium and potassium replace the loss of these essential elements in diarrhoea and vomiting.  Why A wrong: Correcting acidosis is the role of citrate.  Why C wrong: Glucose facilitates sodium absorption, not the other way around.  Why D wrong: ORS decreases losses; it does not increase stool frequency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In ORS, the role of citrate is to:","options":["Facilitate sodium absorption","Correct the acidosis that occurs due to diarrhoea and dehydration","Provide the main calorie source","Thicken the solution"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Citrate corrects the acidosis that occurs due to diarrhoea and dehydration.  Why A wrong: Facilitating sodium absorption is the role of glucose.  Why C wrong: Glucose, not citrate, provides calories.  Why D wrong: Citrate does not act as a thickening agent."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are benefits of giving zinc along with ORS to a child with diarrhoea? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Decreases severity and duration of diarrhoea","Produces less watery stools and less frequency of stools","Decreases hospital admission rates and child mortality","Increases the number of loose stools"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Zinc decreases severity and duration of diarrhoea, produces fewer/less-watery stools, and reduces hospital admissions and mortality.  Why D wrong: Zinc reduces, not increases, stool frequency."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Zinc administration during diarrhoea decreases hospital admission rates by what percentage?","options":["3–5%","15–20%","50%","70%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Zinc administration decreases hospital admission rates by 15–20%.  Why A wrong: 3–5% is the reduction in child mortality, not admission rates.  Why C wrong: 50% is not a stated figure.  Why D wrong: 70% is not a stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Zinc administration during diarrhoea decreases child mortality by what percentage?","options":["3–5%","15–20%","30%","50%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Zinc administration decreases child mortality by 3–5%.  Why B wrong: 15–20% is the reduction in hospital admission rates.  Why C wrong: 30% is not a stated figure.  Why D wrong: 50% is not a stated figure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The recommended zinc dose for children between 2–6 months of age is:","options":["10 mg for 14 days","20 mg for 14 days","10 mg for 7 days","20 mg for 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For children between 2–6 months, zinc dose is 10 mg for 14 days.  Why B wrong: 20 mg for 14 days is for children above 6 months.  Why C wrong: The duration is 14 days, not 7.  Why D wrong: The dose/duration combination is incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The recommended zinc dose for children above 6 months of age is:","options":["10 mg for 14 days","20 mg for 14 days","20 mg for 7 days","40 mg for 14 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For children above 6 months, zinc dose is 20 mg for 14 days.  Why A wrong: 10 mg for 14 days is for children 2–6 months.  Why C wrong: The duration is 14 days, not 7.  Why D wrong: 40 mg is not the stated dose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Congenital heart disease is best defined as:","options":["Abnormal structure formation and functioning of the heart","Acquired damage to heart valves after birth","Inflammation of the pericardium","A viral infection of the myocardium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Congenital heart disease is abnormal structure formation and functioning of the heart.  Why B wrong: Acquired valve damage (e.g., rheumatic) is not congenital.  Why C wrong: Pericardial inflammation is pericarditis, not CHD.  Why D wrong: Myocardial viral infection is myocarditis, not a congenital structural defect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of congenital heart disease? (Select all that apply.)","options":["TORCH infection (mainly rubella)","Radiation exposure","Drug abuse, smoking, and alcohol use","Chromosomal abnormalities such as Down syndrome","Adult-onset hypertension"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TORCH infection (mainly rubella), radiation exposure, drug abuse/smoking/alcohol, and chromosomal abnormalities (Down syndrome) are all listed causes.  Why E wrong: Adult-onset hypertension is not a listed congenital cause."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Congenital rubella syndrome consists of which three abnormalities?","options":["Congenital cataract, congenital deafness, and congenital PDA","Cleft lip, cleft palate, and clubfoot","Anencephaly, spina bifida, and hydrocephalus","Cataract, glaucoma, and blindness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Congenital rubella syndrome consists of congenital cataract, congenital deafness, and congenital PDA.  Why B wrong: Cleft lip/palate and clubfoot are not the rubella triad.  Why C wrong: These are neural tube defects, unrelated to rubella triad.  Why D wrong: Only cataract is correct; deafness and PDA are the other two, not glaucoma/blindness."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the Type-I classification, an acyanotic congenital heart disorder is characterized by which of the following? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Low chance of cyanosis","Blood in the systemic circulation is oxygenated","Left-to-right shunt present","Right-to-left shunt present"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Acyanotic CHD has low chance of cyanosis, oxygenated systemic blood, and a left-to-right shunt.  Why D wrong: A right-to-left shunt is a feature of cyanotic CHD, not acyanotic."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of acyanotic congenital heart disorders (Type-I)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["ASD","VSD","PDA","Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists ASD, VSD, and PDA as examples of acyanotic CHD.  Why D wrong: TOF is a cyanotic CHD, not acyanotic."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the Type-I classification, a cyanotic congenital heart disorder is characterized by:","options":["High chance of cyanosis, deoxygenated systemic blood, right-to-left shunt","Low chance of cyanosis, oxygenated systemic blood, left-to-right shunt","Obstruction in normal blood circulation","Absence of any shunt"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cyanotic CHD has a high chance of cyanosis, deoxygenated systemic blood, and a right-to-left shunt.  Why B wrong: This describes acyanotic CHD.  Why C wrong: Obstruction defines the obstructive category.  Why D wrong: A right-to-left shunt is present in cyanotic CHD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of cyanotic congenital heart disorders (Type-I)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)","Transposition of great arteries","VSD","PDA"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: TOF and transposition of great arteries are listed as cyanotic CHD examples.  Why C wrong: VSD is an acyanotic disorder.  Why D wrong: PDA is an acyanotic disorder."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of obstructive congenital heart disorders (Type-I)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Coarctation of aorta","Pulmonary stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","VSD"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Coarctation of aorta, pulmonary stenosis, mitral stenosis, and aortic stenosis are listed obstructive CHD examples.  Why E wrong: VSD is an acyanotic (increased pulmonary flow) disorder, not obstructive."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In the Type-II classification, which conditions fall under \"Increased Pulmonary Blood Flow\"? (Select all that apply.)","options":["ASD","VSD","PDA","Tricuspid atresia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: ASD, VSD, and PDA are examples of increased pulmonary blood flow lesions (left-to-right shunt).  Why D wrong: Tricuspid atresia falls under decreased pulmonary blood flow."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In lesions with increased pulmonary blood flow, left-to-right shunting results in:","options":["Increased blood pressure on the right side of the heart, increased pulmonary blood flow, decreased systemic circulation","Decreased pulmonary blood flow","Right-to-left shunting with cyanosis","Obstruction to systemic outflow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Left-to-right shunting increases right-sided pressure, increases pulmonary blood flow, and decreases systemic circulation.  Why B wrong: Decreased pulmonary flow is the opposite category.  Why C wrong: Right-to-left shunting/cyanosis characterizes decreased pulmonary flow lesions.  Why D wrong: Obstruction to outflow is the third (obstruction) category."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which clinical findings are associated with increased pulmonary blood flow lesions? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Decreased peripheral pulses","Feeding difficulties and exercise intolerance","Oliguria, pale, cool extremities","Tachycardia, restlessness, irritability, and lethargy","Bounding peripheral pulses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased peripheral pulses, feeding difficulties/intolerance, oliguria/pale/cool extremities, and tachycardia/restlessness/irritability/lethargy are all listed clinical findings.  Why E wrong: The source lists decreased peripheral pulses, not bounding pulses, for this category."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of \"Decreased Pulmonary Blood Flow\" lesions (Type-II)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)","Transposition of great vessels","Truncus arteriosus","Tricuspid atresia","PDA"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TOF, transposition of great vessels, truncus arteriosus, and tricuspid atresia are listed decreased pulmonary blood flow examples.  Why E wrong: PDA is an increased pulmonary blood flow lesion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In decreased pulmonary blood flow lesions, right-to-left shunting leads to which typical clinical features? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Hypoxia","Cyanosis","Polycythemia","Increased pulmonary blood flow"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoxia, cyanosis, and polycythemia typically occur due to right-to-left shunting of desaturated blood.  Why D wrong: These lesions have decreased (obstructed) pulmonary blood flow, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are examples of \"Obstruction of Blood Flow\" lesions (Type-II)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Coarctation of aorta","Aortic stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","ASD"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Coarctation of aorta, aortic stenosis, and pulmonic stenosis are listed obstruction-of-blood-flow examples.  Why D wrong: ASD is an increased pulmonary blood flow (acyanotic) lesion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In obstruction-of-blood-flow lesions, infants and children characteristically present with signs of:","options":["Heart failure","Polycythemia","Clubbing","Cyanotic spells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In obstructive lesions, infants and children show signs of heart failure due to obstruction to outflow.  Why B wrong: Polycythemia is a feature of chronic cyanotic (decreased pulmonary flow) lesions.  Why C wrong: Clubbing results from chronic hypoxia (cyanotic lesions), not obstruction per se.  Why D wrong: Cyanotic spells are characteristic of TOF, not obstructive lesions here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the source, the most common congenital heart disorder overall is:","options":["ASD","VSD","PDA","TOF"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common congenital heart disorder is VSD.  Why A wrong: ASD is not stated as the most common overall.  Why C wrong: PDA is not the most common.  Why D wrong: TOF is the most common cyanotic disorder, not the most common overall."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the source, the most common CYANOTIC congenital heart disorder is:","options":["VSD","ASD","TOF","PDA"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cyanotic congenital heart disorder is TOF (AIIMS-2020).  Why A wrong: VSD is acyanotic (and the most common overall).  Why B wrong: ASD is acyanotic.  Why D wrong: PDA is acyanotic."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the source, the most common ACYANOTIC congenital heart disorder is:","options":["VSD","TOF","Transposition of great arteries","Tricuspid atresia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common acyanotic congenital heart disorder is VSD.  Why B wrong: TOF is a cyanotic disorder.  Why C wrong: Transposition of great arteries is cyanotic.  Why D wrong: Tricuspid atresia is a cyanotic/decreased pulmonary flow lesion."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Ventricular septal defect (VSD) is defined as an abnormal opening in the ventricular septum between the:","options":["Right and left ventricles","Right and left atria","Aorta and pulmonary artery","Right atrium and right ventricle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VSD is an abnormal opening in the ventricular septum between the right and left ventricles.  Why B wrong: An opening between the atria is an ASD.  Why C wrong: A connection between the aorta and pulmonary artery is a PDA.  Why D wrong: The defect is between the two ventricles, not the right atrium and ventricle."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which diagnostic investigations are used for VSD (and other CHDs) in the source? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization","Sweat chloride test","Hanging drop test"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Echocardiography and cardiac catheterization are done for diagnostic purposes in VSD/ASD/PDA.  Why C wrong: The sweat chloride test diagnoses cystic fibrosis.  Why D wrong: The hanging drop test is for cholera, not CHD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In VSD, the left-to-right shunt causes which haemodynamic changes?","options":["Pulmonary circulation becomes increased and cardiac output is decreased","Pulmonary circulation decreases and systemic output increases","No change in pulmonary circulation","Only right-sided cardiac output increases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Due to the left-to-right shunt in VSD, pulmonary circulation becomes increased and cardiac output is decreased.  Why B wrong: This is the opposite of what occurs.  Why C wrong: There is a definite increase in pulmonary circulation.  Why D wrong: The described effect is increased pulmonary flow with decreased cardiac output, not isolated right output increase."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A characteristic auscultatory finding in VSD is:","options":["A murmur-like heart sound due to abnormal blood flow between right and left","A machinery-like continuous murmur","A fixed split second heart sound only","Absence of any murmur"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: VSD produces a murmur-like heart sound due to abnormal blood flow between right and left.  Why B wrong: A machinery-like murmur is characteristic of PDA.  Why C wrong: A fixed split S2 is classically associated with ASD.  Why D wrong: A murmur is present in VSD, not absent."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In VSD, persistently higher blood pressure in the right ventricle from left-to-right shunting can cause hypertrophy and the development of:","options":["Pulmonary hypertension","Systemic hypotension only","Aortic aneurysm","Mitral stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Higher right-ventricular pressure from left-to-right shunting causes hypertrophy with development of pulmonary hypertension (AIIMS-2018).  Why B wrong: The described consequence is pulmonary hypertension, not isolated systemic hypotension.  Why C wrong: Aortic aneurysm is not the described complication.  Why D wrong: Mitral stenosis is a separate obstructive lesion, not the stated complication."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which respiratory signs and growth-related findings are associated with VSD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dyspnoea","Tachypnoea","Tachycardia","Respiratory infection and growth retardation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: VSD is associated with dyspnoea, tachypnoea, tachycardia, respiratory infection, and growth retardation.  (All options are correct per the source.)"},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Atrial septal defect (ASD) is defined as an abnormal opening in the atrial septum between the:","options":["Right ventricle and left ventricle","Right atrium and left atrium","Aorta and left atrium","Pulmonary artery and right atrium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASD is an abnormal opening in the atrial septum between the right atrium and left atrium (DSSSB-2019).  Why A wrong: An opening between the ventricles is a VSD.  Why C wrong: An aorta-to-left-atrium connection is not ASD.  Why D wrong: The defect is between the two atria, not the pulmonary artery and right atrium."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In ASD, the abnormal circulation results in:","options":["Decreased cardiac output and increased pulmonary circulation","Increased cardiac output and decreased pulmonary circulation","No effect on cardiac output","Complete reversal of systemic flow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In ASD, cardiac output becomes decreased and pulmonary circulation becomes increased.  Why B wrong: This is the reverse of what occurs.  Why C wrong: There is a definite effect—decreased cardiac output.  Why D wrong: Complete reversal of systemic flow is not described."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with ASD? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Dyspnoea, tachypnoea, and tachycardia","Murmur-like heart sound","Respiratory infection","Growth retardation","Boot-shaped heart on X-ray"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: ASD is associated with dyspnoea/tachypnoea/tachycardia, a murmur-like heart sound, respiratory infection, and growth retardation.  Why E wrong: A boot-shaped heart is characteristic of TOF, not ASD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Type 1 ASD (Ostium primum) is characterized by an abnormal opening located at the:","options":["Lower portion of the septum","Middle of the septum","Upper portion of the atrial septum","Ventricular septum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Type 1 ASD (Ostium primum) is an abnormal opening at the lower portion of the septum.  Why B wrong: The middle of the septum is Type 2 (Ostium secundum).  Why C wrong: The upper portion is Type 3 (Sinus venosus).  Why D wrong: An opening in the ventricular septum is a VSD, not ASD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which type of ASD is the most common, and where is the defect located?","options":["Type 1 (Ostium primum), lower septum","Type 2 (Ostium secundum), middle of the septum","Type 3 (Sinus venosus), upper atrial septum","Type 4, ventricular septum"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Type 2 ASD (Ostium secundum) is at the middle of the septum and is the most common type of ASD.  Why A wrong: Type 1 (Ostium primum) is at the lower septum and is not the most common.  Why C wrong: Type 3 (Sinus venosus) is at the upper septum and is not the most common.  Why D wrong: There is no \"Type 4\" ASD listed; the defect is atrial, not ventricular."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In Type 3 ASD (Sinus venosus), the abnormal opening is located at the upper portion of the atrial septum where which structure enters the right atrium?","options":["Superior vena cava","Pulmonary artery","Coronary sinus","Aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In Type 3 ASD (Sinus venosus), the opening is at the upper atrial septum where the superior vena cava enters the right atrium.  Why B wrong: The pulmonary artery does not enter the right atrium.  Why C wrong: The coronary sinus is not the structure described here.  Why D wrong: The aorta does not enter the right atrium."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is defined as a failure of the fetal ductus arteriosus to close within:","options":["The first 24 hours of life","The first week or 7 days of life","The first month of life","The first year of life"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PDA is failure of the fetal ductus arteriosus to close within the first week or 7 days of life.  Why A wrong: The stated closure window is 7 days, not 24 hours.  Why C wrong: The stated timeframe is one week, not one month.  Why D wrong: The stated timeframe is one week, not one year."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The drug of choice for closing a PDA is Indomethacin. Its mechanism of action is that it:","options":["Is an NSAID that inhibits prostaglandin synthesis","Is a beta-blocker that slows the heart","Is a diuretic that reduces preload","Is an antibiotic that treats infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indomethacin is an NSAID that inhibits the synthesis of prostaglandin, promoting ductal closure.  Why B wrong: Indomethacin is not a beta-blocker.  Why C wrong: Indomethacin is not primarily a diuretic.  Why D wrong: Indomethacin is an NSAID, not an antibiotic."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In PDA, the abnormal direction of blood flow is from the:","options":["Descending aorta to the pulmonary artery","Pulmonary artery to the descending aorta","Right atrium to the left atrium","Left ventricle to the right ventricle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In PDA, blood flows abnormally from the descending aorta to the pulmonary artery (left-to-right shunt).  Why B wrong: The direction is aorta-to-pulmonary artery, not the reverse.  Why C wrong: Right-to-left atrial flow describes ASD.  Why D wrong: Left-to-right ventricular flow describes VSD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A characteristic auscultatory finding in PDA is:","options":["Machinery-like (continuous) sound","Fixed split S2","Absent heart sounds","Opening snap"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PDA classically produces a machinery-like heart sound (along with dyspnoea, tachypnoea, and tachycardia).  Why B wrong: A fixed split S2 is classically associated with ASD.  Why C wrong: Heart sounds are present, not absent.  Why D wrong: An opening snap is associated with mitral stenosis, not PDA."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which pulse abnormalities are characteristically associated with PDA? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Water hammer / Corrigan pulse","Widened pulse pressure","Bounding pulse","Pulsus paradoxus"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: PDA is associated with water hammer/Corrigan pulse, widened pulse pressure, and bounding pulse.  Why D wrong: Pulsus paradoxus (associated with cardiac tamponade) is not listed for PDA."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" note, during gestation the ductus arteriosus wall synthesizes prostaglandin because of a low oxygen saturation level of approximately:","options":["40–50%","60–70%","80–90%","95–100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During gestation, the DA synthesizes prostaglandin due to low oxygen saturation of 60–70%.  Why A wrong: 40–50% is not the stated fetal saturation.  Why C wrong: 80–90% is not the stated fetal saturation.  Why D wrong: 95–100% is the post-birth saturation that stops prostaglandin synthesis."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"After birth, oxygen saturation may suddenly rise to what level, causing prostaglandin synthesis in the ductus arteriosus to stop and the DA to close?","options":["60–70%","80–85%","95–100%","50–60%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: After birth, oxygen saturation may increase to 95–100%, stopping DA prostaglandin synthesis and causing vasoconstriction/closure.  Why A wrong: 60–70% is the fetal saturation during gestation.  Why B wrong: 80–85% is not the stated post-birth saturation.  Why D wrong: 50–60% is not the stated post-birth saturation."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF), which type of shunting is present, and why?","options":["Left-to-right shunting due to a widened aorta","Right-to-left shunting due to narrowing of the pulmonary valve and opening into the ventricular septum","No shunting occurs","Bidirectional shunting due to atrial defect only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TOF has right-to-left shunting because of narrowing of the pulmonary valve and an opening into the ventricular septum.  Why A wrong: TOF is a right-to-left (cyanotic) shunt, not left-to-right.  Why C wrong: A right-to-left shunt is present in TOF.  Why D wrong: TOF is not defined by an isolated atrial defect; it involves pulmonary narrowing and a VSD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which investigations are used to diagnose Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Echocardiography","Cardiac catheterization","Chest X-ray","Sweat chloride test"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: TOF diagnosis is made by echocardiography, cardiac catheterization, and chest X-ray.  Why D wrong: The sweat chloride test diagnoses cystic fibrosis, not TOF."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The classic \"boot-shaped heart\" appearing on the chest X-ray in TOF is due to:","options":["Left atrial enlargement","Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)","Pericardial effusion","Aortic dilatation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The boot-shaped heart appears on chest X-ray due to right ventricular hypertrophy (FAQs, AIIMS, PGI, GMCH).  Why A wrong: Left atrial enlargement does not produce the boot-shaped contour of TOF.  Why C wrong: A pericardial effusion causes a globular (water-bottle) heart, not a boot shape.  Why D wrong: Aortic dilatation alone does not cause the boot-shaped heart of TOF."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"The specific nursing position for an infant during a TOF hypercyanotic (\"tet\") spell is the:","options":["Trendelenburg position","Knee-chest (genupectoral) / squatting position","Prone position","High Fowler's position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The specific intervention is the knee-chest (genupectoral) or squatting position, which compresses the calf muscles and increases blood circulation into the lungs.  Why A wrong: Trendelenburg is not the stated position for a tet spell.  Why C wrong: The prone position is not the described intervention.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's is not the specified position for TOF spells."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Placing a TOF infant in the knee-chest/squatting position helps relieve a cyanotic spell because it:","options":["Compresses the calf muscles, increasing blood circulation into the lungs","Reduces systemic venous return only","Decreases pulmonary blood flow further","Directly closes the ventricular septal defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states this position compresses the calf muscles, which increases blood circulation into the lungs.  Why B wrong: The source describes increased pulmonary circulation, not merely reduced venous return.  Why C wrong: The aim is to increase, not decrease, pulmonary blood flow.  Why D wrong: Positioning does not close the VSD."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Besides positioning, the general nursing management of a TOF infant includes:","options":["Providing oxygen therapy and initiating general nursing care","Withholding all oxygen","Forcing rapid feeding","Encouraging vigorous physical activity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Management includes providing oxygen therapy and initiating general nursing care.  Why B wrong: Oxygen therapy is provided, not withheld.  Why C wrong: TOF infants have poor feeding; rapid forced feeding is inappropriate.  Why D wrong: Vigorous activity can precipitate tet spells and is not advised."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF) is a combination of which 4 disorders? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Pulmonary stenosis (PS)","Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)","Overriding of aorta","Atrial septal defect (ASD)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TOF is a combination of VSD, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and overriding of the aorta (AIIMS, PGI, ESIC).  Why E wrong: ASD is not a component of the TOF tetrad."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A \"tet spell / blue spell\" in a neonate with TOF is best described as:","options":["Sudden bluish discoloration during physical activities like feeding and crying","A permanent pink coloration at rest","A febrile seizure","A localized skin rash"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A tet spell/blue spell is when the neonate suddenly becomes bluish during physical activities like feeding and crying.  Why B wrong: It is transient cyanosis with exertion, not a permanent pink color.  Why C wrong: A tet spell is a hypercyanotic episode, not a febrile seizure.  Why D wrong: It is a generalized cyanotic episode, not a localized rash."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"In TOF, growth retardation and clubbing of the fingers occur due to:","options":["Chronic hypoxia","Acute infection","Excess oxygen delivery","Iron overload"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Growth retardation and clubbing of fingers in TOF are due to chronic hypoxia (AIIMS, PGI, RRB).  Why B wrong: These are chronic hypoxic changes, not from acute infection.  Why C wrong: TOF causes hypoxia (low oxygen), not excess oxygen delivery.  Why D wrong: Iron overload is not the stated cause of clubbing in TOF."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Tachycardia, tachypnoea, and cyanosis","Poor sucking and poor feeding","Tet spell / blue spell","Growth retardation and clubbing of fingers","Bounding water-hammer pulse"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: TOF signs/symptoms include tachycardia/tachypnoea/cyanosis, poor sucking/feeding, tet spells, and growth retardation with clubbing.  Why E wrong: A water-hammer/bounding pulse is characteristic of PDA, not TOF."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A 6-month-old presents with paroxysmal colicky abdominal pain (drawing legs up), bilious vomiting, and a stool that looks like red currant jelly. On palpation there is a sausage-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":["Hirschsprung's disease","Intussusception","Pyloric stenosis","Acute gastroenteritis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Colicky pain with drawn-up legs, bilious vomiting, currant-jelly stool, and a RUQ sausage-shaped mass are classic for intussusception.  Why A wrong: Hirschsprung's disease presents with constipation/failure to pass meconium, not currant-jelly stool with a sausage mass.  Why C wrong: Pyloric stenosis causes projectile non-bilious vomiting and an olive-shaped epigastric mass.  Why D wrong: Acute gastroenteritis causes watery diarrhoea, not a currant-jelly stool with a palpable sausage mass."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A child with severe dehydration (>10% fluid loss) is drowsy with cold extremities and an unrecordable blood pressure. According to the source, which is the MOST appropriate immediate management?","options":["Oral rehydration solution (ORS) alone","Plan C — IV fluid therapy (RL/Hartmann's solution)","Plan A — home fluids","Withhold all fluids and observe"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe dehydration is treated with Plan C, where ORS is not recommended and IV fluid therapy (RL/Hartmann's) is required.  Why A wrong: ORS should not be given as sole therapy in severe dehydration.  Why C wrong: Plan A (home fluids) is for mild dehydration only.  Why D wrong: Withholding fluids in severe dehydration is dangerous and inappropriate."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"A newborn is suspected of having a congenital heart defect. The nurse recalls that a \"machinery-like\" continuous murmur with a bounding, water-hammer pulse most strongly suggests which defect?","options":["Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)","Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A machinery-like murmur with water-hammer/Corrigan (bounding) pulse and widened pulse pressure is characteristic of PDA.  Why A wrong: VSD produces a murmur but not the machinery-like sound with a water-hammer pulse.  Why B wrong: ASD is associated with a murmur but not the machinery-like/water-hammer combination.  Why D wrong: TOF is cyanotic with tet spells and a boot-shaped heart, not a machinery-like murmur with bounding pulse."},{"section":"Batch 009 · Abnormal stoma colours & surgical procedures for aganglionic megacolon…","question":"According to the source, in a child with diarrhoea and moderate dehydration, the appropriate rehydration approach is:","options":["IV fluids only (Plan C)","ORS therapy (Plan B), with zinc supplementation","No treatment needed","Antibiotics as the sole therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate dehydration is treated by ORS (Plan B), and syrup zinc should also be given.  Why A wrong: IV therapy is reserved for severe dehydration (Plan C), not moderate.  Why C wrong: Moderate dehydration requires active rehydration.  Why D wrong: Antibiotics are given only if indicated, not as sole therapy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In Transposition of the Great Arteries (TGA), what anatomical abnormality occurs?","options":["The location of the great arteries becomes interchanged","The tricuspid valve is completely absent","The pulmonary valve is narrowed","The descending aorta becomes narrowed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In TGA the location of the great arteries becomes interchanged, so they arise from the wrong ventricles.  Why B wrong: Absence of the tricuspid valve describes tricuspid atresia, not TGA.  Why C wrong: Narrowing of the pulmonary valve is pulmonary stenosis.  Why D wrong: Narrowing of the descending aorta is coarctation of the aorta."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In Transposition of the Great Arteries, from which ventricle does the aorta arise?","options":["Right ventricle","Left ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In TGA the aorta begins from the right ventricle.  Why B wrong: Normally the aorta arises from the left ventricle; in TGA it arises from the right ventricle.  Why C wrong: The aorta does not arise from an atrium.  Why D wrong: The aorta does not arise from an atrium."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In Transposition of the Great Arteries, from which ventricle does the pulmonary artery begin?","options":["Left ventricle","Right ventricle","Right atrium","Left atrium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In TGA the pulmonary artery begins from the left ventricle (the reverse of normal).  Why B wrong: The aorta (not the pulmonary artery) arises from the right ventricle in TGA.  Why C wrong: The pulmonary artery does not arise from an atrium.  Why D wrong: The pulmonary artery does not arise from an atrium."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In Transposition of the Great Arteries, the systemic circulation is formed by which structures?","options":["Right ventricle and aorta","Left ventricle and pulmonary artery","Left ventricle and aorta","Right ventricle and pulmonary artery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The condition develops two separate circulations; the systemic circulation is by the right ventricle and aorta.  Why B wrong: The left ventricle and pulmonary artery form the pulmonary circulation in TGA.  Why C wrong: Left ventricle + aorta is the normal (not TGA) systemic pathway.  Why D wrong: Right ventricle + pulmonary artery is the normal pulmonary pathway, not TGA."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In Transposition of the Great Arteries, the pulmonary circulation is formed by:","options":["Left ventricle and pulmonary artery","Right ventricle and aorta","Right ventricle and pulmonary artery","Left atrium and aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In TGA the pulmonary circulation is formed by the left ventricle and pulmonary artery.  Why B wrong: Right ventricle + aorta forms the systemic circulation in TGA.  Why C wrong: This is the normal pulmonary pathway, not the TGA configuration.  Why D wrong: The left atrium and aorta do not form the pulmonary circulation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Why does TGA create two completely separate circulations?","options":["Because there is no connection between the systemic and pulmonary circulations","Because the aorta and pulmonary artery are fused","Because the ventricles are absent","Because the atria are enlarged"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The condition develops two separate circulations without any connection between them.  Why B wrong: The arteries are transposed, not fused.  Why C wrong: The ventricles are present but connected to the wrong great arteries.  Why D wrong: Atrial enlargement is not the defining feature; lack of connection between circuits is."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A neonate with Transposition of the Great Arteries cannot survive unless which associated defects are present?","options":["ASD and VSD","Coarctation and PDA","Tricuspid atresia and pulmonary stenosis","Aortic stenosis and mitral stenosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In TGA the neonate cannot survive if ASD and VSD are not present, as these allow mixing of blood between the two separate circulations.  Why B wrong: Coarctation and PDA are not the survival-critical mixing defects noted for TGA in the source.  Why C wrong: These are separate congenital lesions, not the mixing defects required for TGA survival.  Why D wrong: These stenotic lesions do not provide the necessary shunt for mixing."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Coarctation of the aorta is characterized by:","options":["Narrowing (coarcting) of a portion of the descending aorta producing obstruction to circulation","Interchange of the great arteries","Absence of the tricuspid valve","Complete absence of the aortic arch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In coarctation, some portion of the descending aorta becomes coarcted/narrowed, producing obstruction in normal circulation.  Why B wrong: Interchange of the great arteries is TGA.  Why C wrong: Absence of the tricuspid valve is tricuspid atresia.  Why D wrong: The source describes narrowing of a portion, not complete absence, of the aorta."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A child with coarctation of the aorta is most likely to exhibit which peripheral pulse finding?","options":["Bounding radial pulse and weak, thready femoral pulse","Weak radial pulse and bounding femoral pulse","Equal, bounding pulses in all four extremities","Absent pulses in all extremities"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Coarctation causes a bounding radial (upper) pulse and weak, thready femoral (lower) pulse due to obstruction below the stricture.  Why B wrong: This is the reverse of the classic finding.  Why C wrong: Pulses are unequal (upper stronger than lower), not equal.  Why D wrong: Upper extremity pulses are bounding, not absent."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The classic blood pressure finding in coarctation of the aorta (asked in AIIMS, PGI, JIPMER, ESIC) is:","options":["Increased BP in the upper extremities and decreased BP in the lower extremities","Decreased BP in the upper extremities and increased BP in the lower extremities","Equal BP in all four extremities","Undetectable BP in the upper extremities"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Coarctation produces increased blood pressure in the upper extremities and decreased blood pressure in the lower extremities.  Why B wrong: This is the opposite of the classic pattern.  Why C wrong: BP is unequal between upper and lower extremities.  Why D wrong: Upper extremity BP is increased, not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In coarctation of the aorta, the temperature of the extremities typically shows:","options":["Warm upper extremities and cool lower extremities","Cool upper extremities and warm lower extremities","Warm upper and lower extremities equally","Cold extremities everywhere"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Upper extremities are warmed and lower extremities may feel cool because of reduced blood flow below the stricture.  Why B wrong: This reverses the finding; the lower extremities are the cooler ones.  Why C wrong: There is a temperature difference between upper and lower extremities.  Why D wrong: The upper extremities are warm, not cold."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of coarctation of the aorta? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased systemic circulation above the stricture","Increased intracranial pressure","Epistaxis","Weak radial pulse with bounding femoral pulse","Decreased blood pressure in the upper extremities"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists increased systemic circulation above the stricture, increased intracranial pressure, and epistaxis (nosebleeds) as features.  Why D wrong: The radial pulse is bounding and the femoral pulse is weak/thready (reverse of the option).  Why E wrong: Blood pressure is increased (not decreased) in the upper extremities."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"For a child in respiratory distress from a congenital heart disorder, in which position should the nurse place the child to decrease the work of breathing?","options":["Trendelenburg position, elevating the head and upper body","Prone flat position","Supine flat position","Left lateral recumbent position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if respiratory distress is present, place the child in Trendelenburg position, elevating the head and upper body, to decrease the work of breathing.  Why B wrong: Prone flat position is not the recommended distress position here.  Why C wrong: Supine flat does not elevate the head/upper body to ease breathing.  Why D wrong: Left lateral recumbent is not specified for respiratory distress in CHD here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"When auscultating a child with a congenital heart disorder, the nurse listens to breath sounds specifically for:","options":["Crackles, rhonchi, and wheezing","Bowel sounds","Bruits over the carotids","Murmurs only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse auscultates breath sounds for crackles, rhonchi, and wheezing.  Why B wrong: Bowel sounds are abdominal, not breath sounds.  Why C wrong: Carotid bruits are vascular sounds, not the breath sounds specified.  Why D wrong: The source specifies crackles, rhonchi, and wheezing (breath sounds), not murmurs alone."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"How should an infant with a congenital heart disorder be fed?","options":["Small amounts, frequently, with burping during and after feeding","Large amounts on a fixed 4-hour schedule","Only by continuous nasogastric drip","Withheld until surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends feeding small amounts, frequently, and burping during and after feeding to reduce cardiac workload and fatigue.  Why B wrong: Large-volume feeds increase cardiac and respiratory workload.  Why C wrong: Continuous NG drip is not the recommended method here.  Why D wrong: Feeds are not withheld; small frequent feeds are provided."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A child with a congenital heart disorder develops a cyanotic (\"tet\") spell. What is the immediate nursing action for positioning?","options":["Place the child in the knee-chest position","Place the child supine and flat","Place the child in high Fowler's position","Place the child in Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For cyanotic spells the child should immediately be placed in the knee-chest position, which increases systemic vascular resistance and improves pulmonary blood flow.  Why B wrong: Supine flat does not relieve a cyanotic spell.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is not the specified position for cyanotic spells here.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is used for respiratory distress, not the cyanotic-spell response described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"During a cyanotic spell in a child with congenital heart disease, which interventions are indicated? (Select all that apply)","options":["Place in knee-chest position","Administer 100% oxygen","Administer morphine sulphate","Administer IV fluids","Place in Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: For a cyanotic spell the source directs: knee-chest position, administer 100% oxygen, administer morphine sulphate, and administer IV fluids.  Why E wrong: Trendelenburg is the position for respiratory distress, not the cyanotic-spell protocol; knee-chest is used instead."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which drug is administered during a cyanotic spell in a child with congenital heart disease, per the source?","options":["Morphine sulphate","Digoxin","Furosemide","Adenosine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies administering morphine sulphate during a cyanotic spell.  Why B wrong: Digoxin is not the drug listed for the acute cyanotic spell here.  Why C wrong: Furosemide (a diuretic) is not listed for cyanotic-spell management.  Why D wrong: Adenosine is used for arrhythmias, not the cyanotic-spell protocol described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Preparation of a child for surgery for a congenital heart disorder should be based on the child's:","options":["Developmental age","Chronological age only","Weight percentile only","Birth order"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to prepare physically and emotionally for surgery based on the developmental age.  Why B wrong: The source specifies developmental age, not chronological age alone.  Why C wrong: Weight percentile is not the basis for surgical preparation described.  Why D wrong: Birth order is irrelevant to surgical preparation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"After cardiac catheterisation, why is prevention of constipation an important nursing priority?","options":["Straining and Valsalva maneuver increase intra-thoracic pressure, causing tension on the sutures","Constipation directly causes cyanotic spells","It prevents catheter-site infection","It lowers the child's temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states post-operative prevention of constipation avoids straining and the Valsalva maneuver, because these increase intra-thoracic pressure causing tension on sutures.  Why B wrong: Constipation is not stated to cause cyanotic spells.  Why C wrong: Preventing constipation addresses suture tension, not primarily infection.  Why D wrong: Temperature reduction is not the reason given."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which nursing measure is appropriate for the extremity used for the catheter insertion site after cardiac catheterisation?","options":["Maintain immobility of the extremity used for catheter insertion","Encourage active range-of-motion exercises immediately","Apply heat to increase circulation","Elevate the extremity above the head"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifies immobility of the extremity used for the catheter insertion site to prevent bleeding.  Why B wrong: Immediate active ROM could dislodge the clot and cause bleeding.  Why C wrong: Applying heat is not indicated and could increase bleeding.  Why D wrong: Elevating above the head is not the specified measure."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Frequent post-catheterisation assessment includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Vital signs","Pulse oximetry","Observation of the catheter insertion site","Hourly deep-breathing with forceful coughing"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct (D is the exception): The source lists frequent assessment of vital signs, pulse oximetry, and observation of the catheter insertion site — not forceful coughing, which raises intra-thoracic pressure.  Why A wrong: Vital signs are assessed frequently.  Why B wrong: Pulse oximetry is assessed frequently.  Why C wrong: The catheter insertion site is observed frequently."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A spoon-shaped nail (concave nail) is known as koilonychia and is characteristically seen in:","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Cyanotic heart disease","Kawasaki disease","Rheumatic fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Koilonychia (spoon-shaped nail) is seen in iron deficiency anaemia.  Why B wrong: Cyanotic heart disease is classically associated with clubbing, not koilonychia.  Why C wrong: Kawasaki disease is not linked to koilonychia in the source.  Why D wrong: Rheumatic fever is not associated with spoon-shaped nails."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Infection around the nail bed is known as:","options":["Paronychia","Koilonychia","Onychomycosis","Clubbing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Infection around the nail beds is known as paronychia.  Why B wrong: Koilonychia is a spoon-shaped nail deformity, not an infection.  Why C wrong: Onychomycosis is a fungal nail infection, but the source term for periungual infection is paronychia.  Why D wrong: Clubbing is enlargement of the phalanges, not a nail-bed infection."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In clubbing of the fingers, what happens to the normal Lovibond angle (normally <180 degrees)?","options":["The angle increases","The angle stays the same","The angle disappears","The angle becomes negative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In clubbing, enlargement of the phalanges and drumstick appearance occur, and the normally <180 degree Lovibond angle increases. (AIIMS Raipur-2018)  Why B wrong: The angle changes (increases) in clubbing.  Why C wrong: The angle does not disappear; it increases.  Why D wrong: The angle increases, not becomes negative."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Clubbing of the fingers is described as enlargement of the phalanges with what appearance of the nails?","options":["Drumstick appearance","Spoon-shaped appearance","Pitting appearance","Half-and-half appearance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clubbing is enlargement of the phalanges with a drumstick appearance of the nails.  Why B wrong: Spoon-shaped nails describe koilonychia.  Why C wrong: Pitting is not the described clubbing appearance.  Why D wrong: Half-and-half nails are not the described feature of clubbing here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Pulmonary stenosis is best defined as:","options":["Narrowing of the pulmonary valve at the entrance to the pulmonary artery, decreasing blood flow to the lungs","Narrowing of the descending aorta","Absence of the tricuspid valve","Interchange of the great arteries"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulmonary stenosis is narrowing of the pulmonary valve at the entrance to the pulmonary artery, decreasing blood flow to the lungs.  Why B wrong: Narrowing of the descending aorta is coarctation.  Why C wrong: Absence of the tricuspid valve is tricuspid atresia.  Why D wrong: Interchange of the great arteries is TGA."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In pulmonary stenosis, resistance to blood flow through the narrowed valve causes:","options":["Right ventricular hypertrophy and decreased blood flow","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial dilation only","Aortic dilation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The resistance to blood flow in pulmonary stenosis causes right ventricular hypertrophy and decreased blood flow.  Why B wrong: It is the right (not left) ventricle that hypertrophies because it pumps against the stenotic pulmonary valve.  Why C wrong: The source specifies right ventricular hypertrophy, not isolated left atrial dilation.  Why D wrong: Aortic dilation is not the described consequence."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Tricuspid atresia is characterized by:","options":["Absence of the tricuspid valve with no communication from the right atrium to the right ventricle","Narrowing of the pulmonary valve","Narrowing of the aorta","A hole in the ventricular septum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tricuspid atresia is absence of the tricuspid valve, so no communication exists from the right atrium to the right ventricle.  Why B wrong: Narrowing of the pulmonary valve is pulmonary stenosis.  Why C wrong: Narrowing of the aorta is coarctation.  Why D wrong: A septal hole is a septal defect (VSD), not tricuspid atresia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Kawasaki disease is also known by which name?","options":["Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome","Reye syndrome","Shaken baby syndrome","Marfan syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kawasaki disease is also known as mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome, an acute systemic inflammatory illness.  Why B wrong: Reye syndrome is a separate condition linked to aspirin use in viral illness.  Why C wrong: Shaken baby syndrome is a form of child abuse.  Why D wrong: Marfan syndrome is a connective tissue disorder, not Kawasaki disease."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the known cause of Kawasaki disease?","options":["The cause is unknown","Group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus","Folic acid deficiency","Iron deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the causes of Kawasaki disease are unknown.  Why B wrong: Group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus is the cause of rheumatic fever, not Kawasaki disease.  Why C wrong: Folic acid deficiency is the most common cause of neural tube defects.  Why D wrong: Iron deficiency causes koilonychia/anaemia, not Kawasaki disease."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The most serious complication of Kawasaki disease is:","options":["Cardiac involvement with aneurysm","Renal failure","Hepatic cirrhosis","Bone marrow failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most serious complication of Kawasaki disease may be cardiac involvement with aneurysm (classically coronary artery aneurysm).  Why B wrong: Renal failure is not the described serious complication.  Why C wrong: Hepatic cirrhosis is not associated as the serious complication here.  Why D wrong: Bone marrow failure is not the described complication."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs of the ACUTE stage of Kawasaki disease? (Select all that apply)","options":["Fever","Red throat","Swollen hands and rash","Enlargement of cervical lymph node","Desquamation of the skin of the fingers and toes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The acute stage features fever, red throat, swollen hands and rash, and enlargement of the cervical lymph node.  Why E wrong: Desquamation (peeling) of the skin of the fingers and toes is a sub-acute stage finding, not acute."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The cardinal sign of the sub-acute stage of Kawasaki disease is:","options":["Strawberry tongue","Spoon-shaped nails","Bounding radial pulse","Setting-sun eyes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the sub-acute stage, red throat with strawberry tongue is the cardinal sign.  Why B wrong: Spoon-shaped nails (koilonychia) indicate iron deficiency anaemia.  Why C wrong: Bounding radial pulse is a coarctation sign.  Why D wrong: Setting-sun eyes is a sign of hydrocephalus in infants."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are features of the sub-acute stage of Kawasaki disease? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cracking lips and fissures","Strawberry tongue","Desquamation of the skin on the lips of the fingers and toes","Joint pain and cardiac involvement","Enlargement of the cervical lymph node"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The sub-acute stage includes cracking lips and fissures, strawberry tongue, desquamation of the skin of the fingers and toes, and joint pain with cardiac involvement.  Why E wrong: Enlargement of the cervical lymph node is an acute-stage feature."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In the nursing care of a child with Kawasaki disease, which medication is administered for its antipyretic and antiplatelet effect?","options":["Aspirin","Paracetamol","Ibuprofen","Morphine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin is administered as prescribed for its antipyretic and antiplatelet effects in Kawasaki disease.  Why B wrong: Paracetamol is an antipyretic but lacks the antiplatelet effect and is not the drug specified.  Why C wrong: Ibuprofen is not the medication specified for Kawasaki disease here.  Why D wrong: Morphine is used for cyanotic spells, not Kawasaki disease."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"While a child with Kawasaki disease is receiving aspirin, the nurse must especially monitor for:","options":["Aspirin toxicity","Iron overload","Hyperglycaemia","Hypocalcaemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During aspirin therapy in Kawasaki disease, the nurse should assess for aspirin toxicity.  Why B wrong: Iron overload is unrelated to aspirin therapy for Kawasaki disease.  Why C wrong: Hyperglycaemia is not the monitored aspirin complication here.  Why D wrong: Hypocalcaemia is not the specified aspirin-related concern."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which two nursing assessments are emphasized for a child with Kawasaki disease? (Select all that apply)","options":["Monitor vitals, especially temperature","Assess heart sounds and heart rhythm","Measure head circumference daily","Assess for setting-sun eyes","Assess for bulging fontanelles"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care of Kawasaki disease emphasizes monitoring vitals (especially temperature) and assessing heart sounds and heart rhythm because of the cardiac risk.  Why C wrong: Daily head-circumference measurement relates to hydrocephalus/NTD, not Kawasaki disease.  Why D wrong: Setting-sun eyes is a hydrocephalus sign.  Why E wrong: Bulging fontanelles is a hydrocephalus/increased ICP sign, not Kawasaki-specific."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Rheumatic fever is best classified as which type of disease affecting connective tissue?","options":["An inflammatory autoimmune disease","A congenital structural defect","A hereditary metabolic disorder","A neoplastic disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever is an inflammatory autoimmune disease affecting connective tissue of the heart, joints, skin, blood vessels, and CNS.  Why B wrong: It is acquired/autoimmune, not a congenital structural defect.  Why C wrong: It is autoimmune-inflammatory, not a hereditary metabolic disorder.  Why D wrong: It is inflammatory, not neoplastic (cancerous)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Rheumatic fever affects the connective tissue of all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["The lungs (alveoli)","The heart","The joints and skin (subcutaneous tissue)","Blood vessels and CNS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct (A is the exception): The source lists heart, joints, skin (subcutaneous tissue), blood vessels, and CNS — the lungs/alveoli are not listed.  Why B wrong: The heart is affected.  Why C wrong: Joints and skin (subcutaneous tissue) are affected.  Why D wrong: Blood vessels and CNS are affected."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which heart valve is most commonly affected in rheumatic fever?","options":["Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve","Pulmonary valve","Aortic valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mitral valve is most commonly affected in rheumatic fever.  Why B wrong: The tricuspid valve is not the most commonly affected valve.  Why C wrong: The pulmonary valve is not most commonly affected.  Why D wrong: The aortic valve is less commonly affected than the mitral valve."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The most serious complication of rheumatic fever (a frequently asked question) is:","options":["Cardiac valve involvement, especially the mitral valve","Coronary artery aneurysm","Renal failure","Hydrocephalus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most serious complication of rheumatic fever is cardiac valve involvement, especially the mitral valve. (FAQ's)  Why B wrong: Coronary artery aneurysm is the serious complication of Kawasaki disease.  Why C wrong: Renal failure is not the described serious complication of rheumatic fever.  Why D wrong: Hydrocephalus is a CNS/CSF disorder, unrelated to rheumatic fever's serious complication."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the most common cause of rheumatic fever?","options":["Untreated upper respiratory infection with group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus","Staphylococcus aureus skin infection","Viral myocarditis","Folic acid deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause is an untreated upper respiratory infection with group A beta-haemolytic streptococcal infection.  Why B wrong: The source specifies group A beta-haemolytic streptococcus, not staphylococcus.  Why C wrong: Viral myocarditis is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Folic acid deficiency causes NTDs, not rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Rheumatic fever is diagnosed using which criteria?","options":["Jones criteria","Duke criteria","Centor criteria","Ranson criteria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever is diagnosed using the Jones criteria.  Why B wrong: Duke criteria are used for infective endocarditis.  Why C wrong: Centor criteria estimate the probability of strep throat, not diagnose rheumatic fever.  Why D wrong: Ranson criteria assess pancreatitis severity."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"For controlling joint pain and inflammation in rheumatic fever, the nursing care includes:","options":["Massage and alternating hot and cold applications as prescribed","Complete immobilization in a cast","Deep tissue manipulation of inflamed joints only","Ice packs continuously for 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes controlling joint pain and inflammation with massage and alternating hot and cold applications as prescribed.  Why B wrong: Casting/immobilization is not the described intervention.  Why C wrong: The source describes massage plus alternating hot/cold, not deep tissue manipulation alone.  Why D wrong: The source describes alternating hot and cold, not continuous ice for 24 hours."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following laboratory findings are seen in rheumatic fever? (Select all that apply)","options":["Low grade fever","Elevated anti-streptolysin O (ASO) titer","Elevated ESR","Elevated C-reactive protein (CRP)","Decreased anti-streptolysin O titer"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rheumatic fever shows low grade fever, elevated ASO titer, elevated ESR, and elevated CRP.  Why E wrong: The ASO titer is elevated (not decreased) in rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Aschoff bodies (lesions), characteristic of rheumatic fever, are found in which tissues?","options":["Heart, blood vessels, brain, and joints","Liver and spleen only","Kidneys and bladder","Skin and nails only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aschoff bodies or lesions are found in the heart, blood vessels, brain, and joints.  Why B wrong: The source does not localize Aschoff bodies to the liver and spleen.  Why C wrong: Kidneys and bladder are not the listed sites.  Why D wrong: Skin and nails are not the listed Aschoff-body sites."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In the JONES major criteria for rheumatic fever, what does the letter \"O\" represent?","options":["\"O looks like a heart\" = myocarditis (carditis)","Osteomyelitis","Oliguria","Otitis media"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the JONES mnemonic, \"O\" stands for \"O looks like a heart\" = myocarditis (carditis).  Why B wrong: Osteomyelitis is not a Jones major criterion.  Why C wrong: Oliguria is not part of the JONES mnemonic.  Why D wrong: Otitis media is unrelated to the Jones criteria."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following is a MAJOR (JONES) criterion for rheumatic fever?","options":["Subcutaneous nodules","Arthralgia","Fever","Prolonged PR interval"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Subcutaneous nodules (\"N\" in JONES) are a major criterion.  Why B wrong: Arthralgia is a minor criterion.  Why C wrong: Fever is a minor criterion.  Why D wrong: Prolonged PR interval is a minor criterion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In the JONES major criteria, \"E\" represents which skin manifestation of rheumatic fever?","options":["Erythema marginatum","Erythema nodosum","Erythema multiforme","Ecchymosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"E\" in JONES stands for erythema marginatum.  Why B wrong: Erythema nodosum is not the Jones major criterion.  Why C wrong: Erythema multiforme is a different rash, not the Jones criterion.  Why D wrong: Ecchymosis (bruising) is not a Jones criterion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In the JONES major criteria for rheumatic fever, \"S\" stands for:","options":["Sydenham chorea","Splenomegaly","Strawberry tongue","Seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"S\" in the JONES mnemonic stands for Sydenham chorea.  Why B wrong: Splenomegaly is not a Jones criterion.  Why C wrong: Strawberry tongue is a sign of Kawasaki disease.  Why D wrong: Seizures are not a Jones major criterion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are the five MAJOR (JONES) criteria for rheumatic fever? (Select all that apply)","options":["Joint involvement","Myocarditis (carditis)","Subcutaneous nodules","Erythema marginatum","Sydenham chorea"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The JONES major criteria are Joint involvement, \"O\" = myocarditis, subcutaneous Nodules, Erythema marginatum, and Sydenham chorea — all five options are correct.  (All options are correct; there are no incorrect distractors in this SATA item.)"},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are MINOR criteria for rheumatic fever? (Select all that apply)","options":["CRP increased","Arthralgia","Fever","Prolonged PR interval","Leukocytosis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The minor criteria listed are CRP increased, arthralgia, fever, elevated ESR, prolonged PR interval, anamnesis of rheumatism, and leukocytosis — options A–E are all minor criteria.  (All options are correct minor criteria in this SATA item.)"},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which ECG finding is included among the minor criteria for rheumatic fever?","options":["Prolonged PR interval","ST elevation","Widened QRS complex","Shortened QT interval"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A prolonged PR interval is listed as a minor criterion.  Why B wrong: ST elevation is not a Jones minor criterion.  Why C wrong: Widened QRS is not among the listed criteria.  Why D wrong: A shortened QT interval is not a Jones criterion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"According to the modified diagnostic scheme shown, rheumatic fever can be diagnosed with evidence of streptococcal infection (throat culture growing GABHS or elevated ASO titers) PLUS which combination?","options":["Two major criteria, OR one major criterion and two minor criteria","One major criterion only","Three minor criteria only","Two minor criteria only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The diagnosis requires streptococcal evidence plus either two major criteria OR one major criterion and two minor criteria.  Why B wrong: One major criterion alone is insufficient per the scheme.  Why C wrong: Three minor criteria without a major criterion do not satisfy the diagram.  Why D wrong: Two minor criteria alone are insufficient."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which laboratory findings can serve as evidence of a preceding streptococcal infection in diagnosing rheumatic fever? (Select all that apply)","options":["Throat cultures growing GABHS","Elevated anti-streptolysin O titers","Decreased ESR","Elevated head circumference","Positive Halo ring test"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The diagnosis box lists throat cultures growing GABHS or elevated anti-streptolysin O titers as streptococcal evidence.  Why C wrong: ESR is elevated (not decreased) in rheumatic fever.  Why D wrong: Head circumference relates to hydrocephalus, not strep evidence.  Why E wrong: The Halo ring test detects CSF leakage after shunt surgery, unrelated to rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A neural tube defect (NTD) is best described as:","options":["An abnormal opening in the bone covering of the brain and spinal cord","Accumulation of CSF in the cranial cavity","A narrowing of the aorta","An autoimmune inflammation of connective tissue"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An NTD is an abnormal opening in the bone covering of the brain and spinal cord.  Why B wrong: Accumulation of CSF in the cranial cavity is hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: Narrowing of the aorta is coarctation.  Why D wrong: Autoimmune connective tissue inflammation describes rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Normally, the neural tube closes at which gestational age?","options":["3 to 4 weeks","8 to 10 weeks","12 to 14 weeks","20 to 24 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The neural tube normally closes at 3 to 4 weeks of gestational age.  Why B wrong: 8–10 weeks is later than the stated closure period.  Why C wrong: 12–14 weeks is well after neural tube closure.  Why D wrong: 20–24 weeks is far beyond the closure window."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the most common site of neural tube defects?","options":["Lumbosacral region","Cervical region","Thoracic region","Frontal region"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of NTD is the lumbosacral region.  Why B wrong: The cervical region is not the most common site.  Why C wrong: The thoracic region is not the most common site.  Why D wrong: The frontal region is not the most common site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the most common cause of neural tube defects?","options":["Folic acid deficiency","Iron deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Group A streptococcal infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of NTD is folic acid deficiency.  Why B wrong: Iron deficiency causes anaemia/koilonychia, not NTDs as the most common cause.  Why C wrong: Vitamin D deficiency is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Group A streptococcal infection causes rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which diagnostic test is used to detect neural tube defects and may be included along with physical examination, CT scan, and MRI?","options":["MSAFP (maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein) test","Jones criteria","Halo ring test","Anti-streptolysin O titer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of NTD is made by the MSAFP test, physical examination, CT scan, and MRI.  Why B wrong: Jones criteria diagnose rheumatic fever.  Why C wrong: The Halo ring test detects CSF leakage after shunt surgery.  Why D wrong: ASO titer is a rheumatic fever/strep marker."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"An increased MSAFP value indicates which condition?","options":["Neural tube defect / abnormal wall defect","Down syndrome","Rheumatic fever","Kawasaki disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An increased MSAFP value indicates a neural tube defect and abnormal wall defect.  Why B wrong: A decreased (not increased) MSAFP value indicates Down syndrome.  Why C wrong: MSAFP does not diagnose rheumatic fever.  Why D wrong: MSAFP does not diagnose Kawasaki disease."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A decreased MSAFP value is associated with:","options":["Down syndrome","Neural tube defect","Anencephaly","Spina bifida"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A decreased MSAFP value indicates Down syndrome.  Why B wrong: An increased (not decreased) MSAFP indicates a neural tube defect.  Why C wrong: Anencephaly (an NTD) is associated with increased MSAFP.  Why D wrong: Spina bifida (an NTD) is associated with increased MSAFP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Besides folic acid deficiency, which of the following are listed causes/risk factors for neural tube defects? (Select all that apply)","options":["Radiation exposure","Infections","Smoking","Alcohol and drug abuse","Iron overload"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include folic acid deficiency (most common), radiation exposure, infections, smoking, and alcohol/drug abuse.  Why E wrong: Iron overload is not a listed cause of NTDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which is the MOST SEVERE type of neural tube defect?","options":["Craniorachischisis","Spina bifida occulta","Meningocele","Encephalocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Craniorachischisis is the most severe type of neural tube defect, with both the brain and spinal cord remaining open.  Why B wrong: Spina bifida occulta is a mild, often asymptomatic NTD.  Why C wrong: Meningocele carries low neurological risk and is not the most severe.  Why D wrong: Encephalocele is a herniation defect but not described as the most severe."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In craniorachischisis, which structures remain open?","options":["Both the brain and the spinal cord","Only the spinal cord","Only the brain","Only the meninges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In craniorachischisis both the brain and spinal cord remain open, combining anencephaly and spina bifida from the cervical to the lumbar/sacral region.  Why B wrong: Not only the spinal cord — both brain and cord are open.  Why C wrong: Not only the brain — the cord is also open.  Why D wrong: The defect involves brain and cord, not just meninges."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Anencephaly is defined as:","options":["Complete absence of skull bones so the brain is directly exposed to the environment","Herniation of brain tissue between the skull bones","Abnormal opening only in the vertebral column","Absence of the tricuspid valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anencephaly is complete absence of the skull bones, so the brain is directly exposed to the environment.  Why B wrong: Herniation of brain tissue between skull bones is encephalocele.  Why C wrong: An abnormal vertebral opening describes spina bifida.  Why D wrong: Absence of the tricuspid valve is a cardiac defect (tricuspid atresia)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Regarding the prognosis of anencephaly, which statement is correct?","options":["It is the second most dangerous type of NTD; the neonate cannot survive and dies within a few hours","It is a mild, asymptomatic defect","Neonates survive normally without treatment","It only causes leg weakness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anencephaly is the second most dangerous type of NTD; the neonate cannot survive and dies within a few hours.  Why B wrong: It is severe and fatal, not mild/asymptomatic.  Why C wrong: Neonates cannot survive anencephaly.  Why D wrong: It is fatal, not limited to leg weakness."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Encephalocele is defined as:","options":["Protrusion of brain tissue between the skull bones","Complete absence of skull bones","Protrusion of meninges and CSF from the vertebral column","Narrowing of the pulmonary valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Encephalocele is a protrusion of brain tissue between the skull bones.  Why B wrong: Complete absence of skull bones is anencephaly.  Why C wrong: Protrusion of meninges and CSF from the vertebral column is meningocele.  Why D wrong: Narrowing of the pulmonary valve is pulmonary stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The most common site for an encephalocele is the:","options":["Parietal and occipital area","Lumbosacral region","Frontal region","Cervical region"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common site of encephalocele is the parietal and occipital area.  Why B wrong: The lumbosacral region is the common site for spina bifida, not encephalocele.  Why C wrong: The frontal region is not the stated common site.  Why D wrong: The cervical region is not the stated common site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which is the MOST COMMON type of neural tube defect?","options":["Spina bifida","Anencephaly","Craniorachischisis","Encephalocele"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spina bifida is the most common type of neural tube defect.  Why B wrong: Anencephaly is the second most dangerous, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Craniorachischisis is the most severe, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Encephalocele is not the most common NTD."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which portion of the vertebral column is most commonly affected in spina bifida?","options":["L5 to S1","C1 to C2","T1 to T4","T10 to T12"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly affected portion of the vertebral column in spina bifida is L5 to S1.  Why B wrong: C1–C2 (cervical) is not the most commonly affected region.  Why C wrong: T1–T4 (upper thoracic) is not the stated site.  Why D wrong: T10–T12 (lower thoracic) is not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Spina bifida is classified into which two types based on the protrusion of organs?","options":["Spina bifida occulta and spina bifida cystica","Communicating and non-communicating","Spastic and athetoid","Acute and sub-acute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spina bifida is classified into spina bifida occulta and spina bifida cystica.  Why B wrong: Communicating and non-communicating classify hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: Spastic and athetoid classify cerebral palsy.  Why D wrong: Acute and sub-acute are the stages of Kawasaki disease."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In spina bifida occulta, which statement about the spinal cord and meninges is correct?","options":["The spinal cord remains intact and the meninges are not exposed on the skin surface","The spinal cord and meninges protrude through the opening","The meninges and CSF protrude in a transparent sac","The brain tissue protrudes through the skull"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In spina bifida occulta the spinal cord remains intact and the meninges are not exposed on the skin surface.  Why B wrong: Protrusion of spinal cord and meninges describes myelomeningocele.  Why C wrong: Protrusion of meninges and CSF in a transparent sac describes meningocele.  Why D wrong: Brain tissue protruding through the skull is encephalocele."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which skin findings may be present over the site of spina bifida occulta? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tuft of hair","Port wine stain","Dimple in the skin","Sac-like protrusion covered by transparent membrane","Complete absence of skull bones"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Spina bifida occulta may show a tuft of hair, a port wine stain, and a dimple in the skin at the location of the abnormal opening.  Why D wrong: A sac-like protrusion covered by transparent membrane describes spina bifida cystica (meningocele/myelomeningocele), not occulta.  Why E wrong: Absence of skull bones is anencephaly."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Why is the chance of neurological abnormalities LOW in spina bifida occulta?","options":["Because the spinal cord and nerve roots are not affected","Because the spinal cord protrudes through the opening","Because there is complete absence of the vertebral column","Because CSF is excessively produced"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In spina bifida occulta neurological risk is low because the spinal cord and nerve roots are not affected.  Why B wrong: The cord does not protrude in occulta; it remains intact.  Why C wrong: There is a small opening, not complete absence of the vertebral column.  Why D wrong: Excess CSF production relates to hydrocephalus, not occulta neurological risk."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In spina bifida cystica, the protruding organs are covered by:","options":["A transparent membrane","Intact skull bone","Dry, scaly skin","A muscular layer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In spina bifida cystica the protruding organs are covered by a transparent membrane.  Why B wrong: The covering is a transparent membrane, not skull bone.  Why C wrong: The covering is a transparent membrane, not dry scaly skin.  Why D wrong: It is a transparent membrane, not a muscular layer."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Spina bifida cystica is classified into which two types?","options":["Meningocele and meningomyelocele (myelomeningocele)","Communicating and non-communicating","Occulta and aperta","Spastic and atonic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spina bifida cystica is classified into meningocele and meningomyelocele/myelomeningocele.  Why B wrong: Communicating and non-communicating classify hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: Occulta and aperta is not the source's classification here.  Why D wrong: Spastic and atonic are cerebral palsy types."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In a meningocele, which structures protrude through the abnormal opening?","options":["Meninges and CSF, covered by a transparent membrane","Spinal cord, nerve roots, CSF, and meninges","Brain tissue","Only skin and subcutaneous fat"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a meningocele the meninges and CSF protrude from the abnormal opening and are covered by a transparent membrane.  Why B wrong: Protrusion of spinal cord, nerve roots, CSF, and meninges describes myelomeningocele.  Why C wrong: Brain tissue protrusion is encephalocele.  Why D wrong: A meningocele contains meninges and CSF, not just skin and fat."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Why is the chance of neurological abnormalities LOW in a meningocele?","options":["Because the spinal cord and nerve roots are not involved","Because the spinal cord is completely severed","Because the brain is exposed","Because CSF is not produced"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In meningocele, neurological risk is low because the spinal cord and nerve roots are not involved.  Why B wrong: The cord is not severed; it is uninvolved in the protrusion.  Why C wrong: Brain exposure describes anencephaly.  Why D wrong: CSF is still produced; the low risk is due to uninvolved cord/nerve roots."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In meningomyelocele/myelomeningocele, which structures protrude, usually at the lumbosacral region?","options":["Spinal cord, nerve roots, CSF, and meninges","Only meninges and CSF","Only brain tissue","Only skin and hair"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In myelomeningocele the spinal cord, nerve roots, CSF, and meninges protrude, usually at the lumbosacral region.  Why B wrong: Protrusion of only meninges and CSF is meningocele.  Why C wrong: Brain tissue protrusion is encephalocele.  Why D wrong: The defect involves neural tissue, not just skin and hair."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Why is myelomeningocele considered a neurological emergency?","options":["Because the spinal cord and nerve roots are affected, causing neurological abnormalities","Because the spinal cord and nerve roots are not involved","Because it causes only a skin dimple","Because it involves the aortic valve"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In myelomeningocele, neurological abnormalities occur because the spinal cord and nerve roots are affected, making it a neurological emergency.  Why B wrong: The cord and nerve roots ARE involved (that is why it is an emergency).  Why C wrong: A mere skin dimple describes occulta, not the emergency of myelomeningocele.  Why D wrong: This is a spinal defect, not a cardiac (aortic valve) problem."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Myelomeningocele accounts for what percentage of neural tube defects, making it the most common?","options":["90–95%","40–50%","10–20%","60–70%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myelomeningocele is the most common neural tube defect at 90–95%.  Why B wrong: 40–50% understates the stated frequency.  Why C wrong: 10–20% understates the stated frequency.  Why D wrong: 60–70% is not the figure given."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Myelomeningocele is further classified into which two types?","options":["Myelocele (open type) and myelomeningocele (closed type)","Occulta and cystica","Communicating and non-communicating","Spastic and athetoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: It is further classified into myelocele (open type) and myelomeningocele (closed type).  Why B wrong: Occulta and cystica classify spina bifida generally, not this subdivision.  Why C wrong: Communicating/non-communicating classify hydrocephalus.  Why D wrong: Spastic and athetoid classify cerebral palsy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of spina bifida (neural tube defect)? (Select all that apply)","options":["Dimple in the skin and tuft of hairs","Port wine stain and sac-like protrusion","Disturbance in nerve supply of lower extremities and paraplegia","Disturbance in bowel and bladder control","Increased blood pressure in the upper extremities"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: NTD signs include skin dimple/tuft of hair, port wine stain and sac-like protrusion, disturbed nerve supply of the lower extremities with possible paraplegia, and bowel/bladder disturbance (also hydrocephalus, hip and joint deformities).  Why E wrong: Increased BP in the upper extremities is a coarctation of the aorta finding, not an NTD sign."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In addition to bowel/bladder and lower-limb problems, which complications may occur with spina bifida? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hydrocephalus","Hip deformities","Joint deformities","Coronary aneurysm","Strawberry tongue"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: NTD signs/symptoms include hydrocephalus, hip deformities, and joint deformities.  Why D wrong: Coronary aneurysm is a Kawasaki disease complication.  Why E wrong: Strawberry tongue is a Kawasaki disease sign."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"According to the figure of neural tube defects, iniencephaly is described as:","options":["Occipital skull and spine defects with extreme retroflexion of the head","Completely open brain and spinal cord","Open brain with lack of skull vault","Herniation of the meninges through a defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Iniencephaly is described as occipital skull and spine defects with extreme retroflexion of the head.  Why B wrong: Completely open brain and spinal cord describes craniorachischisis.  Why C wrong: Open brain with lack of skull vault describes anencephaly.  Why D wrong: Herniation of meninges through a defect describes meningocele/encephalocele."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In the figure, \"closed spinal dysraphism\" is described as:","options":["Deficiency of at least two vertebral arches, here covered with a lipoma","Open spinal cord with a meningeal cyst","Complete absence of skull vault","Protrusion of meninges filled with CSF"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Closed spinal dysraphism is described as deficiency of at least two vertebral arches, covered with a lipoma.  Why B wrong: Open spinal cord with a meningeal cyst describes myelomeningocele.  Why C wrong: Complete absence of the skull vault describes anencephaly.  Why D wrong: Protrusion of meninges filled with CSF describes meningocele."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"According to the figure, myelomeningocele is depicted as:","options":["Open spinal cord (with a meningeal cyst)","Closed asymptomatic NTD with some vertebrae not completely closed","Open brain and lack of skull vault","Herniation of the meninges and brain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The figure describes myelomeningocele as an open spinal cord with a meningeal cyst.  Why B wrong: Closed asymptomatic NTD with vertebrae not fully closed describes spina bifida occulta.  Why C wrong: Open brain with lack of skull vault describes anencephaly.  Why D wrong: Herniation of meninges and brain describes encephalocele."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A neonate is born with a myelomeningocele sac. What is the priority nursing action to protect the defect (a NORCET/AIIMS/PGI-tested point)?","options":["Place in prone position and cover the defect with sterile gauze or non-adhesive dressing moistened with normal saline","Cover the sac tightly with a dry diaper","Apply direct pressure to the sac","Place the infant supine directly on the sac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The defect is protected by prone positioning and covering with a sterile gauze or non-adhesive dressing moistened with normal saline to maintain moisture (AIIMS, PGI, GMCH, NORCET-5, Prelims-2023).  Why B wrong: A diaper is contraindicated over the defect and dry covering risks drying/rupture.  Why C wrong: Pressure on the sac must be avoided to prevent rupture.  Why D wrong: Supine positioning on the sac risks injuring/rupturing it; prone is preferred."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"To prevent dryness of the myelomeningocele sac, the dressing should be changed how often?","options":["Every 2–4 hours","Every 12 hours","Once daily","Every 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent dryness and maintain moisture, the dressing is changed every 2–4 hours.  Why B wrong: Every 12 hours is too infrequent to maintain moisture.  Why C wrong: Once daily is too infrequent for the moist dressing.  Why D wrong: Every 30 minutes is not the stated interval."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following nursing measures are appropriate for a neonate with an unrepaired myelomeningocele? (Select all that apply)","options":["Inspect the sac for leaks, abrasions, irritation, or signs of infection","Avoid pressure on the sac and do not cover it with a diaper","Measure head circumference daily","Maintain in prone position to prevent injury to the sac","Encourage supine positioning directly on the sac"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Care includes inspecting the sac, avoiding pressure (no diaper), daily head-circumference measurement, and maintaining prone position to protect the sac.  Why E wrong: Placing the infant supine directly on the sac risks injury; prone position is maintained instead."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Why is a diaper usually contraindicated over an unrepaired neural tube defect?","options":["To avoid pressure/contamination of the sac until the defect is repaired","To prevent overheating","To reduce urine output","To promote clubbing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A diaper is contraindicated to avoid pressure on the sac; it should not be covered with a diaper until the defect has been repaired.  Why B wrong: Overheating is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Reducing urine output is not the reason a diaper is avoided.  Why D wrong: Clubbing is a nail finding unrelated to diaper use."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In the postoperative care of a neural tube defect, in which positions are children kept for a few days to prevent tension on the suture line?","options":["Prone or lateral position, avoiding direct pressure on the incision","High Fowler's position","Supine directly on the incision","Knee-chest position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Post-operatively, children are kept prone or in a lateral position for a few days to prevent tension on the suture line, and direct pressure on the incision is avoided.  Why B wrong: High Fowler's is not the specified postoperative position for NTD repair.  Why C wrong: Supine directly on the incision places pressure on the suture line.  Why D wrong: Knee-chest position is used for cyanotic spells, not NTD postoperative care."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In postoperative wound care for a neural tube defect repair, the dressing should be changed:","options":["Daily, or at any moment if wet or soaked with stools","Only once a week","Only if visibly bleeding","Never, to avoid disturbing the site"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The dressing (covered with waterproof adhesive film) should be changed daily or at any moment if wet or soaked with stools.  Why B wrong: Weekly changes are too infrequent and risk infection.  Why C wrong: Changes are also indicated when wet/soiled, not only when bleeding.  Why D wrong: Dressings must be changed to prevent infection."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"After neural tube defect repair, why should head circumference be measured daily?","options":["Because an increase may indicate developing hydrocephalus","To assess for dehydration","To determine feeding volume","To detect coarctation of the aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Head circumference is measured daily because an increase may indicate developing hydrocephalus.  Why B wrong: Head circumference does not primarily assess dehydration.  Why C wrong: Head circumference does not determine feeding volume.  Why D wrong: Coarctation is a cardiac defect unrelated to head circumference."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the most common postoperative complication following neural tube defect repair?","options":["Hydrocephalus","Myocarditis","Pulmonary stenosis","Aspirin toxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocephalus is the most common postoperative complication (also meningitis and encephalitis).  Why B wrong: Myocarditis relates to rheumatic fever, not NTD repair.  Why C wrong: Pulmonary stenosis is a congenital heart defect.  Why D wrong: Aspirin toxicity relates to Kawasaki disease treatment."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"For periconception folic acid supplementation, what daily dose is advised for eligible couples throughout pregnancy?","options":["400 mcg (0.4 mg)","60 mg","4,000 mcg","500 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eligible couples are advised daily 400 mcg (0.4 mg) folic acid supplementation throughout pregnancy, starting at least 4 weeks/1 month before getting pregnant.  Why B wrong: 60 mg is the elemental iron content of the AMB IFA tablet, not the folic acid dose.  Why C wrong: 4,000 mcg is for women with a prior NTD-affected pregnancy who are planning to conceive again.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg is the folic acid content of the AMB IFA tablet, not the recommended periconception supplement dose."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Folic acid supplementation for prevention of NTDs should begin at what time relative to conception?","options":["At least 4 weeks/1 month prior to getting pregnant","Only after the first trimester","At the time of delivery","Only in the third trimester"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Supplementation should start at least 4 weeks/1 month prior to getting pregnant (periconception).  Why B wrong: Starting after the first trimester is too late, since the neural tube closes at 3–4 weeks.  Why C wrong: At delivery is far too late for NTD prevention.  Why D wrong: The third trimester is too late to prevent NTDs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The periconception period is defined as:","options":["The 14 weeks before and 10 weeks after conception","The 10 weeks before and 14 weeks after conception","The first trimester only","The last 4 weeks of pregnancy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The periconception period is defined as the 14 weeks before and 10 weeks after conception.  Why B wrong: This reverses the correct intervals.  Why C wrong: The first trimester alone is not the definition.  Why D wrong: The last 4 weeks of pregnancy is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Under India's Anaemia Mukt Bharat (AMB) program, the IFA tablet provided to reproductive and pregnant women contains how much elemental iron?","options":["60 mg elemental iron","400 mcg elemental iron","500 mg elemental iron","4,000 mcg elemental iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under the AMB program, the IFA tablet contains 60 mg elemental iron and 500 mcg folic acid.  Why B wrong: 400 mcg is the periconception folic acid dose, not the iron content.  Why C wrong: The tablet contains 60 mg (not 500 mg) elemental iron.  Why D wrong: 4,000 mcg is the folic acid dose for women with prior NTD pregnancy, not the iron content."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The IFA tablet under India's AMB program contains how much folic acid?","options":["500 mcg","60 mg","400 mcg","4,000 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The AMB IFA tablet contains 500 mcg folic acid (and 60 mg elemental iron).  Why B wrong: 60 mg is the elemental iron content.  Why C wrong: 400 mcg is the general periconception folic acid recommendation, not the AMB tablet content.  Why D wrong: 4,000 mcg is for women with a prior NTD-affected pregnancy planning to conceive."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"According to CDC recommendations, a woman who has already had an NTD-affected pregnancy and is planning to become pregnant again should consume how much folic acid daily?","options":["4,000 mcg","400 mcg","60 mg","500 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Such women should consume 4,000 mcg of folic acid each day, beginning 1 month before becoming pregnant and through the first 3 months of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: 400 mcg is the standard dose, but higher (4,000 mcg) is advised after a prior NTD pregnancy when planning conception.  Why C wrong: 60 mg is elemental iron in the AMB tablet, not the folic acid dose.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg is the AMB tablet's folic acid content, not the recommended dose here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Per CDC recommendations, a woman who has had an NTD-affected pregnancy but is NOT planning to become pregnant should consume how much folic acid daily?","options":["400 mcg (0.4 mg)","4,000 mcg","60 mg","500 mcg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When not planning to become pregnant, women with a prior NTD-affected pregnancy should consume 400 mcg (0.4 mg) of folic acid each day.  Why B wrong: 4,000 mcg is the higher dose for when they ARE planning to conceive again.  Why C wrong: 60 mg elemental iron is the AMB IFA tablet's iron content.  Why D wrong: 500 mcg is the AMB tablet's folic acid content, not the recommendation here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"For a woman with a prior NTD-affected pregnancy planning to conceive again, the 4,000 mcg folic acid should be taken:","options":["Beginning 1 month before conception and through the first 3 months of pregnancy","Only during the last trimester","Only on the day of conception","For the entire year after delivery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 4,000 mcg dose begins 1 month before becoming pregnant and continues through the first 3 months of pregnancy.  Why B wrong: The last trimester is too late for neural tube protection.  Why C wrong: A single day of supplementation is insufficient.  Why D wrong: The recommendation is preconception and early pregnancy, not the year after delivery."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Cerebral palsy is characterized by impaired movement/loss of voluntary muscle control and posture resulting from an abnormality in which system?","options":["Extra-pyramidal system","Cardiovascular system","Renal system","Gastrointestinal system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebral palsy results from an abnormality in the extra-pyramidal system.  Why B wrong: The cardiovascular system is not the origin of cerebral palsy.  Why C wrong: The renal system is unrelated to cerebral palsy.  Why D wrong: The GI system is not the source of cerebral palsy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Cerebral palsy is best classified as which type of disorder in terms of progression?","options":["A non-progressive disorder","A rapidly progressive disorder","A relapsing-remitting disorder","An acute reversible disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebral palsy is a non-progressive disorder.  Why B wrong: It is non-progressive, not rapidly progressive.  Why C wrong: It is not described as relapsing-remitting.  Why D wrong: It is irreversible, not acute and reversible."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the most common type of cerebral palsy (NIA Jaipur-2023)?","options":["Spastic cerebral palsy","Athetoid cerebral palsy","Ataxic cerebral palsy","Mixed cerebral palsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Spastic cerebral palsy is the most common type (NIA Jaipur-2023).  Why B wrong: Athetoid (dyskinetic) is not the most common type.  Why C wrong: Ataxic/atonic is not the most common type.  Why D wrong: Mixed cerebral palsy is not the most common type."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of cerebral palsy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hypoxia","TORCH infection","Hypoglycaemia","Head trauma and prematurity","Group A streptococcal throat infection"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include hypoxia, TORCH infection, hypoglycaemia, head trauma, and prematurity.  Why E wrong: Group A streptococcal throat infection is the cause of rheumatic fever, not cerebral palsy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Diagnosis of cerebral palsy is made by:","options":["CT scan and MRI","Jones criteria","MSAFP test","Halo ring test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of cerebral palsy is made by CT scan and MRI.  Why B wrong: Jones criteria diagnose rheumatic fever.  Why C wrong: MSAFP test screens for neural tube defects/Down syndrome.  Why D wrong: Halo ring test detects CSF leakage after shunt surgery."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In spastic cerebral palsy, which nerve tracts are damaged?","options":["Paramedial nerve tracts","Extra-paramedial nerve tracts (basal ganglia)","Cerebellar tracts","Peripheral sensory nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In spastic cerebral palsy the paramedial nerve tracts are damaged.  Why B wrong: Extra-paramedial nerve tracts (basal ganglia) damage is athetoid cerebral palsy.  Why C wrong: Cerebellar damage occurs in ataxic/atonic cerebral palsy.  Why D wrong: Peripheral sensory nerve involvement is not the described lesion in spastic CP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In cerebral palsy terminology, \"hypertonia\" refers to:","options":["Severe muscle spasm","Loss of all muscle tone","Involuntary slow movement","Incoordinated movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertonia refers to severe muscle spasm.  Why B wrong: Loss of muscle tone is hypotonia/atonia, not hypertonia.  Why C wrong: Slow involuntary movement describes athetoid CP.  Why D wrong: Incoordinated movement describes ataxic CP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In cerebral palsy, hemiplegia is defined as:","options":["Weakness and poor voluntary control of the hand and leg on the same side of the body","Paralysis of all four limbs","Paralysis of one limb","Paralysis of three limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemiplegia is weakness and poor voluntary (motor) control of the hand and leg on the same side of the body.  Why B wrong: Paralysis of all four limbs is quadriplegia.  Why C wrong: Paralysis of one limb is monoplegia.  Why D wrong: Paralysis of three limbs is triplegia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Match the correct definition: In cerebral palsy, \"diplegia\" refers to:","options":["Paralysis of upper or lower extremities","Paralysis of one limb","Paralysis of three limbs","Paralysis of all four limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diplegia is paralysis of upper or lower extremities.  Why B wrong: Paralysis of one limb is monoplegia.  Why C wrong: Paralysis of three limbs is triplegia.  Why D wrong: Paralysis of all four limbs is quadriplegia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Monoplegia is defined as paralysis of:","options":["One limb","Two lower limbs","Three limbs","Four limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Monoplegia is paralysis of one limb.  Why B wrong: Paralysis of the two lower limbs is paraplegia.  Why C wrong: Paralysis of three limbs is triplegia.  Why D wrong: Paralysis of four limbs is quadriplegia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Triplegia refers to paralysis of:","options":["Three limbs","One limb","Two limbs","Four limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Triplegia is paralysis of three limbs.  Why B wrong: One limb is monoplegia.  Why C wrong: Two limbs (lower) is paraplegia.  Why D wrong: Four limbs is quadriplegia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Quadriplegia is defined as paralysis of:","options":["All four limbs","One limb","Three limbs","Only the lower limbs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Quadriplegia is paralysis of all four limbs.  Why B wrong: One limb is monoplegia.  Why C wrong: Three limbs is triplegia.  Why D wrong: Only the lower limbs is paraplegia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Athetoid cerebral palsy is also known as:","options":["Dyskinetic cerebral palsy","Spastic cerebral palsy","Ataxic cerebral palsy","Mixed cerebral palsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Athetoid cerebral palsy is also known as dyskinetic cerebral palsy.  Why B wrong: Spastic CP is a separate type (paramedial tract damage).  Why C wrong: Ataxic CP involves cerebellar damage, a different type.  Why D wrong: Mixed CP has features of all types, not the athetoid synonym."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In athetoid (dyskinetic) cerebral palsy, which structure is damaged?","options":["Extra-paramedial nerve tract (basal ganglia)","Paramedial nerve tracts","Cerebellum","Spinal cord anterior horn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In athetoid CP, the extra-paramedial nerve tract (basal ganglia) is damaged, causing slow, involuntary movements.  Why B wrong: Paramedial nerve tract damage causes spastic CP.  Why C wrong: Cerebellar damage causes ataxic/atonic CP.  Why D wrong: Anterior horn cell involvement is not the described lesion in athetoid CP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In ataxic/atonic cerebral palsy, which part of the brain is damaged, producing incoordinated muscle movement?","options":["Cerebellum","Basal ganglia","Frontal lobe","Choroid plexus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In ataxic/atonic CP, incoordinated muscle movement occurs and the cerebellum is damaged.  Why B wrong: Basal ganglia damage causes athetoid CP.  Why C wrong: The frontal lobe is not the specified damaged structure.  Why D wrong: The choroid plexus produces CSF and is unrelated to ataxic CP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which type of cerebral palsy presents with symptoms of all the other types combined?","options":["Mixed cerebral palsy","Spastic cerebral palsy","Athetoid cerebral palsy","Ataxic cerebral palsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mixed cerebral palsy features symptoms of all types of cerebral palsy.  Why B wrong: Spastic CP is one specific type.  Why C wrong: Athetoid CP is one specific type.  Why D wrong: Ataxic CP is one specific type."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of cerebral palsy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe muscle spasm","Slow and involuntary muscle movement / incoordinated movement","Opisthotonic posture","Seizure gait (crossed lower leg)","Bounding radial pulse with weak femoral pulse"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CP signs include severe muscle spasm, slow/involuntary and incoordinated movement, opisthotonic posture, and seizure gait (crossed lower leg / scissoring).  Why E wrong: Bounding radial with weak femoral pulse is a coarctation of the aorta finding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In cerebral palsy, \"paraplegia\" refers to paralysis of:","options":["The lower extremities","One extremity","All four extremities","One side of the body"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paraplegia is paralysis of the lower extremities.  Why B wrong: Paralysis of one extremity is monoplegia.  Why C wrong: Paralysis of all four extremities is quadriplegia.  Why D wrong: Paralysis of one side of the body is hemiplegia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In cerebral palsy, \"hemiparesis\" is best defined as:","options":["Muscle weakness or partial paralysis on one side of the body","Complete paralysis of all four limbs","Paralysis of the lower limbs only","Involuntary movements of the face only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hemiparesis is muscle weakness or partial paralysis on one side of the body.  Why B wrong: Complete four-limb paralysis is quadriplegia.  Why C wrong: Lower-limb paralysis is paraplegia.  Why D wrong: Facial-only involuntary movement is not the definition of hemiparesis."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which additional signs are associated with cerebral palsy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mental retardation","Delayed developmental milestones and feeding difficulties","Delayed disappearance of neonatal reflexes","Seizures and visual difficulties (strabismus common)","Strawberry tongue"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CP is associated with mental retardation, delayed milestones/feeding difficulty, delayed disappearance of neonatal reflexes, and seizures/visual difficulties with strabismus being common.  Why E wrong: Strawberry tongue is a sign of Kawasaki disease, not cerebral palsy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which statement about the treatment of cerebral palsy is correct?","options":["It is irreversible and cannot be cured; only symptomatic treatment may be initiated","It can be completely cured with surgery","It resolves spontaneously by adolescence","It is reversible with folic acid supplementation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cerebral palsy is irreversible and cannot be cured; only symptomatic treatment may be initiated.  Why B wrong: Surgery does not cure CP.  Why C wrong: CP does not spontaneously resolve.  Why D wrong: Folic acid prevents NTDs; it does not reverse CP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"When communicating with a child who has cerebral palsy, the nurse should communicate according to the child's:","options":["Developmental age, not chronological age","Chronological age only","Height and weight","Sibling's age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Communicate with the patient according to developmental age and not chronological age.  Why B wrong: Chronological age alone may not match the child's developmental level.  Why C wrong: Height and weight do not determine communication level.  Why D wrong: A sibling's age is irrelevant."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Hydrocephalus is defined as:","options":["Accumulation of CSF within the cranial cavity due to imbalance between absorption and production","Complete absence of the skull bones","Narrowing of the descending aorta","Inflammation of the connective tissue of the heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrocephalus is the accumulation of CSF within the cranial cavity due to an imbalance between absorption and production.  Why B wrong: Absence of skull bones is anencephaly.  Why C wrong: Narrowing of the descending aorta is coarctation.  Why D wrong: Inflammation of cardiac connective tissue is rheumatic fever."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"CSF is produced by which structure?","options":["Choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles","Arachnoid villi","Foramen of Monro","Aqueduct of Sylvius"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSF is produced by the choroid plexus in the lateral ventricles.  Why B wrong: Arachnoid villi absorb (not produce) CSF.  Why C wrong: The foramen of Monro is a passage for CSF flow, not a production site.  Why D wrong: The aqueduct of Sylvius is a conduit, not a production site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"CSF flows from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle through the:","options":["Foramen of Monro","Aqueduct of Sylvius","Foramen of Magendie","Foramen of Luschka"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSF circulates from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle by the foramen of Monro (interventricular foramen).  Why B wrong: The aqueduct of Sylvius connects the third to the fourth ventricle.  Why C wrong: The foramen of Magendie drains the fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space.  Why D wrong: The foramen of Luschka drains the fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"CSF flows from the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle through the:","options":["Aqueduct of Sylvius (cerebral aqueduct)","Foramen of Monro","Foramen of Magendie","Arachnoid villi"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSF circulates from the third to the fourth ventricle by the aqueduct of Sylvius (cerebral aqueduct).  Why B wrong: The foramen of Monro connects the lateral to the third ventricle.  Why C wrong: The foramen of Magendie drains the fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space.  Why D wrong: Arachnoid villi absorb CSF, not connect ventricles."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"CSF flows from the fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space through which foramina?","options":["Foramen of Magendie and foramen of Luschka","Foramen of Monro only","Aqueduct of Sylvius","Foramen ovale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSF circulates from the fourth ventricle to the subarachnoid space by the foramen of Magendie and foramen of Luschka.  Why B wrong: The foramen of Monro connects the lateral and third ventricles.  Why C wrong: The aqueduct of Sylvius connects the third and fourth ventricles.  Why D wrong: The foramen ovale is a cardiac/skull foramen unrelated to CSF drainage here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"CSF is absorbed by which structure in the subarachnoid space?","options":["Arachnoid villi","Choroid plexus","Foramen of Monro","Cerebral aqueduct"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CSF is absorbed by the arachnoid villi in the subarachnoid space.  Why B wrong: The choroid plexus produces CSF; it does not absorb it.  Why C wrong: The foramen of Monro is a CSF passage, not an absorption site.  Why D wrong: The cerebral aqueduct is a conduit, not an absorption site."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In infants and children, approximately how much CSF is secreted per day?","options":["About 500 ml/day (20 ml/hour)","About 720 ml/day","About 100 ml/day","About 4 ml/day"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In infants and children about 20 ml/hour or 500 ml/day of CSF is secreted.  Why B wrong: 720 ml/24 hours is the adult secretion figure.  Why C wrong: 100 ml is within the adult CSF volume range, not the child's daily secretion.  Why D wrong: 4 ml/kg is the child's CSF volume, not the daily secretion."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In infants and children, the CSF volume is approximately:","options":["4 ml/kg","100 to 150 ml","720 ml","30 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In infants and children the CSF volume is 4 ml/kg.  Why B wrong: 100 to 150 ml is the adult CSF volume.  Why C wrong: 720 ml/24 hours is the adult daily secretion.  Why D wrong: 30 ml/hr is the adult secretion rate, not the child's volume."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In adults, the rate of CSF secretion is:","options":["0.5 ml/min (30 ml/hr or 720 ml/24 hours)","20 ml/hour (500 ml/day)","4 ml/kg per hour","100 ml/min"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults, CSF secretion is 0.5 ml/min, or 30 ml/hr, or 720 ml/24 hours.  Why B wrong: 20 ml/hour (500 ml/day) is the infant/child rate.  Why C wrong: 4 ml/kg is the infant/child CSF volume, not the adult secretion rate.  Why D wrong: 100 ml/min is far too high and not stated."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In adults, the total CSF volume is approximately:","options":["100 to 150 ml","4 ml/kg","500 ml","720 ml"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In adults, the CSF volume is 100 to 150 ml.  Why B wrong: 4 ml/kg is the infant/child volume.  Why C wrong: 500 ml/day is the child's daily secretion, not the adult volume.  Why D wrong: 720 ml/24 hours is the adult daily secretion, not the standing volume."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In communicating hydrocephalus, the mechanism is:","options":["No obstruction in CSF flow, but increased production and decreased absorption of CSF","Obstruction in the normal flow of CSF","Complete absence of CSF production","Excessive absorption of CSF"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In communicating hydrocephalus there is no obstruction in the normal flow of CSF, but hydrocephalus occurs due to increased production and decreased absorption of CSF.  Why B wrong: Obstruction in CSF flow defines non-communicating hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: There is no absence of CSF production in communicating hydrocephalus.  Why D wrong: Absorption is decreased, not excessive."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In non-communicating hydrocephalus, the mechanism is:","options":["Obstruction in the normal flow of CSF","Increased production with decreased absorption but no obstruction","Excess absorption of CSF","Absence of the choroid plexus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In non-communicating hydrocephalus, obstruction occurs in the normal flow of CSF.  Why B wrong: Increased production/decreased absorption without obstruction describes communicating hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: Excess absorption would reduce, not cause, hydrocephalus.  Why D wrong: Absence of the choroid plexus is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of hydrocephalus in an INFANT? (Select all that apply)","options":["Increased head circumference and bulging fontanelles","Widely separated sutures and thin skull bones","Prominent and dilated scalp veins","Setting-sun eyes","Headache on awakening"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Infant signs include increased head circumference/bulging fontanelles, widely separated sutures/thin skull bones, prominent dilated scalp veins, and setting-sun eyes.  Why E wrong: Headache on awakening is a sign in the older CHILD, not an infant."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A cracked-pot sound on percussion of the skull in an infant with hydrocephalus is known as:","options":["Macewen's sign","Chvostek's sign","Trousseau's sign","Kernig's sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cracked-pot sound on percussion is called Macewen's sign.  Why B wrong: Chvostek's sign is a facial twitch in hypocalcaemia.  Why C wrong: Trousseau's sign is carpal spasm in hypocalcaemia.  Why D wrong: Kernig's sign is a meningeal irritation sign, not a percussion note."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"\"Setting-sun eyes,\" a sign of hydrocephalus in infants, refers to:","options":["Sclera visible above the iris (the iris appears to be setting)","Bilateral cataracts","Yellow discoloration of the sclera","Absence of the red reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Setting-sun eyes means the sclera is visible on/above the iris, giving the appearance of a setting sun.  Why B wrong: Cataracts are lens opacities, not the setting-sun sign.  Why C wrong: Yellow sclera is jaundice, not setting-sun eyes.  Why D wrong: Absence of the red reflex suggests other eye pathology, not the setting-sun sign."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of hydrocephalus in an older CHILD? (Select all that apply)","options":["Irritability and lethargy","Headache on awakening","Nausea, vomiting, and high shrill cry","Ataxia, seizures, and nystagmus","Widely separated sutures"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In an older child, signs include irritability/lethargy, headache on awakening, nausea/vomiting with high shrill cry, and ataxia/seizures/nystagmus.  Why E wrong: Widely separated sutures is an infant sign (before suture fusion), not a child sign."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which shunting procedure is most commonly used for hydrocephalus?","options":["Ventriculo-peritoneal (VP) shunt","Ventriculo-atrial shunt","Ventriculo-pleural shunt","Lumbo-peritoneal shunt"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ventriculo-peritoneal (VP) shunt is most commonly used, draining CSF into the peritoneal cavity from the lateral ventricles.  Why B wrong: Ventriculo-atrial shunt is an alternative, not the most common.  Why C wrong: Ventriculo-pleural shunt is an alternative, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Lumbo-peritoneal shunt is not listed as the most common here."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In a VP shunt, CSF is drained from the lateral ventricles into which cavity?","options":["Peritoneal cavity","Pleural cavity","Right atrium","Subarachnoid space"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a VP shunt, CSF is drained into the peritoneal cavity from the lateral ventricles.  Why B wrong: Drainage into the pleural cavity is a ventriculo-pleural shunt.  Why C wrong: Drainage into the right atrium is a ventriculo-atrial shunt.  Why D wrong: The subarachnoid space is part of normal CSF circulation, not the VP shunt destination."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"If too much CSF accumulates in the peritoneum from a VP shunt, there may be a chance of:","options":["Pleural effusion","Coronary aneurysm","Strawberry tongue","Koilonychia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if too much CSF accumulates in the peritoneum there may be a chance of pleural effusion.  Why B wrong: Coronary aneurysm is a Kawasaki disease complication.  Why C wrong: Strawberry tongue is a Kawasaki disease sign.  Why D wrong: Koilonychia is a nail finding in iron deficiency anaemia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are alternative shunts for hydrocephalus mentioned in the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Ventriculo-atrium shunt","Ventriculo-pleural shunt","Ventriculo-peritoneal shunt","Aorto-femoral shunt","Porto-caval shunt"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Alternative shunts mentioned are the ventriculo-atrium shunt and the ventriculo-pleural shunt.  Why C wrong: The VP shunt is the most commonly used shunt, not listed as the \"alternative.\"  Why D wrong: Aorto-femoral shunt is a vascular procedure unrelated to CSF diversion.  Why E wrong: Porto-caval shunt is used for portal hypertension, not hydrocephalus."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate PRE-operative nursing care measures for hydrocephalus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Monitor vital signs and maintain hydration","Provide care during seizures","Change the position of the head regularly or use an egg-crate mattress to prevent pressure sores","Elevate the head of the bed more than 30 degrees","Position the child only on the operated side"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-operative care includes monitoring vitals/hydration, providing care during seizures, and changing head position regularly or using an egg-crate mattress to prevent pressure sores over the enlarged head.  Why D wrong: Elevating more than 30 degrees is a post-operative caution, not a pre-operative measure, and is specifically discouraged.  Why E wrong: Positioning on the un-operated side is a post-operative measure; it is not applicable pre-operatively."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"After VP shunt surgery, to prevent pressure on the shunt valve, the child should be positioned (AIIMS Nagpur, PGI-2020):","options":["On the un-operated side or supine","On the operated side","In high Fowler's position","In the knee-chest position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Post-operatively, position the child on the un-operated side or supine to prevent pressure on the shunt valve (AIIMS Nagpur, PGI-2020).  Why B wrong: Lying on the operated side puts pressure on the shunt valve.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is not the specified post-shunt position.  Why D wrong: Knee-chest position is for cardiac cyanotic spells, not shunt care."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"After VP shunt surgery, if signs of increased ICP are present, how should the head of the bed be positioned?","options":["Elevate the head of the bed to a 30-degree angle (low Fowler's position)","Keep the bed completely flat","Elevate to 90 degrees (high Fowler's)","Place in Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If ICP is increased, elevate the head of the bed to 30 degrees or low Fowler's position.  Why B wrong: Keeping flat does not help drain CSF/reduce ICP.  Why C wrong: Elevating to 90 degrees exceeds the safe 30-degree limit and can dangerously drop ICP.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg (head down) would raise ICP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Why should the head of the bed NOT be elevated more than 30 degrees in the postoperative period after shunt surgery?","options":["Because a sudden decrease in ICP may cause brain tissues to collapse","Because it increases CSF production","Because it causes hypotension","Because it obstructs the shunt permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Elevating more than 30 degrees can cause a sudden decrease in ICP, and brain tissues may collapse.  Why B wrong: Positioning does not increase CSF production.  Why C wrong: The stated reason is brain tissue collapse from a rapid ICP drop, not systemic hypotension.  Why D wrong: The concern is rapid ICP change, not permanent shunt obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"After shunt surgery, how often should head circumference and vital signs be measured?","options":["Every 2 hours","Every 8 hours","Once daily","Every 30 minutes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source directs measuring head circumference every 2 hours and vital signs also.  Why B wrong: Every 8 hours is less frequent than specified.  Why C wrong: Once daily is too infrequent post-operatively.  Why D wrong: Every 30 minutes is not the stated frequency."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Signs of shunt malfunction/infection in an INFANT include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Irritability","High shrill cry","Lethargy","Poor feeding","Lack of appetite with headache"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In an infant, shunt malfunction/infection presents with irritability, high shrill cry, lethargy, and poor feeding.  Why E wrong: Headache with lack of appetite describes the TODDLER presentation, not the infant."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In a toddler, shunt malfunction or infection typically presents as:","options":["Headache and lack of appetite","High shrill cry and bulging fontanelle","Alteration in level of consciousness only","Setting-sun eyes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a toddler, shunt malfunction/infection presents with headache and lack of appetite.  Why B wrong: High shrill cry/bulging fontanelle is the infant presentation.  Why C wrong: Alteration in level of consciousness is the presentation in older children.  Why D wrong: Setting-sun eyes is an infant hydrocephalus sign, not the toddler shunt-malfunction sign."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In older children, shunt malfunction or infection typically presents as:","options":["Alteration in the child's level of consciousness","Poor feeding and high shrill cry","Bulging fontanelle","Lack of appetite only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In older children, shunt malfunction/infection presents as an alteration in the child's level of consciousness.  Why B wrong: Poor feeding/high shrill cry is the infant presentation.  Why C wrong: A bulging fontanelle is an infant sign.  Why D wrong: Lack of appetite with headache is the toddler presentation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"After shunt surgery, the surgical site should be assessed for:","options":["CSF drainage, bleeding, and infection","Coronary aneurysm","Strawberry tongue","Bounding pulses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The surgical site should be assessed for CSF drainage, bleeding, and infection.  Why B wrong: Coronary aneurysm relates to Kawasaki disease.  Why C wrong: Strawberry tongue is a Kawasaki sign.  Why D wrong: Bounding pulses relate to coarctation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The Halo ring test is used after shunt surgery to detect:","options":["CSF leakage (a ring-like structure appears if leakage is present)","Iron deficiency","Coarctation of the aorta","Aspirin toxicity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Halo ring test detects CSF leakage; if leakage is present, a ring-like structure appears (the halo sign).  Why B wrong: Iron deficiency is detected by nail changes/blood tests, not the Halo ring test.  Why C wrong: Coarctation is assessed by BP differences, not the Halo ring test.  Why D wrong: Aspirin toxicity relates to Kawasaki disease treatment, not the Halo ring test."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In a choroid plexectomy, which part is removed and why?","options":["Part of the choroid plexus is removed to reduce the amount of CSF being produced","Part of the arachnoid villi is removed to increase CSF absorption","Part of the skull is removed to relieve pressure","Part of the ventricle wall is removed to widen it"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a choroid plexectomy, a part of the choroid plexus is removed to reduce the amount of CSF being produced.  Why B wrong: The procedure removes the choroid plexus (production site), not the arachnoid villi.  Why C wrong: Skull removal describes a craniectomy, not choroid plexectomy.  Why D wrong: The ventricle wall is not the structure removed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Shaken baby syndrome is best described as:","options":["Vigorous shaking of children causing internal brain injury without external signs, a form of child abuse","Accumulation of CSF in the cranial cavity","A congenital heart defect","An autoimmune connective tissue disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shaken baby syndrome is vigorous shaking of children causing damage to internal brain structures without external signs; it is a type of child abuse.  Why B wrong: CSF accumulation is hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: It is a form of abuse/trauma, not a congenital heart defect.  Why D wrong: It is trauma, not an autoimmune connective tissue disease (rheumatic fever)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of shaken baby syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bruising and skull fractures","Tense or bulging fontanel with cerebral haemorrhage","Retinal haemorrhage, seizure, and coma","Strawberry tongue","Bounding radial pulse"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Signs include bruising/skull fractures, tense or bulging fontanel with cerebral haemorrhage, and retinal haemorrhage, seizure, and coma.  Why D wrong: Strawberry tongue is a Kawasaki disease sign.  Why E wrong: Bounding radial pulse is a coarctation finding."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"In a case of child abuse such as shaken baby syndrome, what is the FIRST priority nursing action?","options":["Inform the child helpline or child abuse prevention committee","Discharge the child home immediately","Confront the parents aggressively","Delay all reporting until discharge"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The first priority in a case of child abuse is to inform the child helpline or child abuse prevention committee (after providing first aid for the symptoms).  Why B wrong: Discharging the child home may return them to an unsafe environment.  Why C wrong: Aggressive confrontation is not the recommended first action.  Why D wrong: Reporting should not be delayed; it is the first priority."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the child helpline number in India?","options":["1098","108","100","1091"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The child helpline number in India is 1098.  Why B wrong: 108 is the emergency ambulance number in India.  Why C wrong: 100 is the police number in India.  Why D wrong: 1091 is the women's helpline number, not the child helpline."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the recommended prevention for shaken baby syndrome?","options":["Do not shake vigorously or fling up the infant/child","Keep the infant prone during sleep","Restrict breastfeeding","Elevate the head of the bed above 30 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prevention is to not shake vigorously or fling up the infant or child, because it causes shaken baby syndrome.  Why B wrong: Prone sleeping relates to SIDS risk, not shaken baby syndrome prevention.  Why C wrong: Restricting breastfeeding is not a prevention measure and is not advised.  Why D wrong: Head-of-bed elevation relates to shunt/ICP care, not shaken baby prevention."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) is defined as the unexpected/unexplained sudden death of an infant younger than:","options":["1 year of age","6 months of age","2 years of age","28 days of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SIDS is the unexpected or unexplained sudden death of an infant younger than 1 year of age.  Why B wrong: The cutoff is younger than 1 year, not 6 months.  Why C wrong: The cutoff is younger than 1 year, not 2 years.  Why D wrong: 28 days defines the neonatal period, not the SIDS age cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Regarding SIDS, which statement about sex distribution is correct?","options":["Male children are most commonly affected","Female children are most commonly affected","It affects both sexes equally","It affects only premature infants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SIDS most commonly affects the male child.  Why B wrong: Females are not the most commonly affected group.  Why C wrong: The source specifies male predominance, not equal distribution.  Why D wrong: SIDS is not limited to premature infants."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is known about the exact cause of SIDS?","options":["The exact cause is unknown","It is caused by group A streptococcus","It is caused by folic acid deficiency","It is caused by coarctation of the aorta"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The exact cause of SIDS is unknown.  Why B wrong: Group A streptococcus causes rheumatic fever, not SIDS.  Why C wrong: Folic acid deficiency causes NTDs, not SIDS.  Why D wrong: Coarctation is a congenital heart defect, not the cause of SIDS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which factor is associated with a LOWER risk of SIDS?","options":["Breastfeeding","Prone sleeping position","Soft bedding","Overheating"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Breastfeeding is associated with a lower risk of SIDS.  Why B wrong: Prone sleeping is the most common cause/risk of SIDS.  Why C wrong: Soft bedding increases SIDS risk (limiting it is protective).  Why D wrong: Overheating is a risk factor, not protective."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which sleeping position is identified as the most common cause of SIDS?","options":["Prone position (lying flat on the abdomen)","Supine position (on the back)","Left lateral position","Semi-Fowler's position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prone position (lying flat on the abdomen) is the most common cause of SIDS.  Why B wrong: Supine is the recommended protective position, not the cause.  Why C wrong: The left lateral position is not identified as the most common cause.  Why D wrong: Semi-Fowler's is not identified as the cause of SIDS."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of SIDS? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pulselessness and breathlessness","Urine and stool are passed","Frothy pink saliva drooling from the neonate's mouth","Bounding radial pulse","Strawberry tongue"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: SIDS signs include pulselessness and breathlessness, passage of urine and stool, and frothy pink saliva drooling from the neonate's mouth.  Why D wrong: A bounding radial pulse is a coarctation finding (and SIDS presents with pulselessness).  Why E wrong: Strawberry tongue is a Kawasaki disease sign."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"The primary nursing care for the family in a case of SIDS is to:","options":["Provide psychological support to the family members","Administer aspirin to the family","Initiate folic acid supplementation","Prepare the infant for cardiac catheterisation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care in SIDS focuses on providing psychological support to the family members, since the infant has died suddenly.  Why B wrong: Aspirin therapy relates to Kawasaki disease, not SIDS family care.  Why C wrong: Folic acid supplementation prevents NTDs and is not the SIDS nursing focus.  Why D wrong: Cardiac catheterisation is unrelated to SIDS bereavement care."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which of the following are recommended preventive measures for SIDS? (Select all that apply)","options":["Change the sleeping position to supine","Breastfeeding","Limiting soft bedding","Immunizing the infant and using pacifiers","Placing the infant prone to sleep"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: SIDS prevention includes supine sleeping, breastfeeding, limiting soft bedding, immunizing the infant, and using pacifiers (plus avoiding all risk factors).  Why E wrong: Prone sleeping is contraindicated and is the most common cause of SIDS, not a preventive measure."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"What is the best sleeping position to prevent SIDS (FAQs)?","options":["Supine position (on the back) on a firm surface","Prone position on a soft surface","Left lateral position on a soft mattress","Semi-Fowler's position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The best position to prevent SIDS is supine — positioning on the back on a firm surface for sleep (FAQs).  Why B wrong: Prone on a soft surface is the most common cause of SIDS.  Why C wrong: The left lateral position on a soft mattress is not the recommended best position.  Why D wrong: Semi-Fowler's is not the recommended sleeping position for SIDS prevention."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Which sleeping position is CONTRAINDICATED for infants because it is the most common cause of SIDS?","options":["Prone position","Supine position","Sitting position","Reverse Trendelenburg"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prone position is contraindicated for infants and is the most common cause of SIDS.  Why B wrong: Supine is the recommended, protective position.  Why C wrong: The sitting position is not identified as the contraindicated SIDS position.  Why D wrong: Reverse Trendelenburg is not the contraindicated position discussed."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"Positional plagiocephaly, a complication of prolonged supine positioning, is characterized by:","options":["Flat occiput and hair loss in the occiput region","Bulging fontanelle and setting-sun eyes","Cracked-pot sound on percussion","Strawberry tongue and cracked lips"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Positional plagiocephaly is a complication of prolonged supine positioning, showing flat occiput and hair loss in the occiput region.  Why B wrong: Bulging fontanelle/setting-sun eyes are signs of hydrocephalus.  Why C wrong: The cracked-pot sound (Macewen's sign) is a hydrocephalus finding.  Why D wrong: Strawberry tongue and cracked lips are Kawasaki disease signs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Transposition of the Great Arteries, Coarctation of Aorta…","question":"A nurse teaches parents that to prevent both SIDS and positional plagiocephaly, they should:","options":["Change the position of the infant when under observation","Keep the infant prone at all times","Never move the infant once asleep","Elevate the head of the crib above 30 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises changing the position of the infant when under observation, balancing SIDS prevention (supine sleep) with avoiding prolonged pressure (plagiocephaly).  Why B wrong: Keeping the infant prone increases SIDS risk.  Why C wrong: Never moving the infant increases the risk of positional plagiocephaly.  Why D wrong: Head-of-crib elevation above 30 degrees is not the recommended measure here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In which age group is the prone (abdomen) sleeping position specifically contraindicated because of its association with Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS)?","options":["Infant less than 1 year of age","Toddler 1–3 years of age","Preschooler 3–5 years of age","School-age child 6–12 years of age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the prone position is contraindicated for an infant less than 1 year of age.  Why B wrong: Toddlers are past the peak SIDS-risk window; the contraindication is specified for infants under 1 year.  Why C wrong: Preschoolers are not the group for whom prone positioning is contraindicated for SIDS.  Why D wrong: School-age children are well outside the at-risk age for SIDS."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which sleeping-related practice is listed as an INFANT risk factor for SIDS?","options":["Sleeping on the abdomen (prone) or on soft bedding, pillows, comforters, quilts","Sleeping supine on a firm mattress","Sleeping in a side-lying position on a firm surface","Room-sharing without bed-sharing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists sleeping on the abdomen (prone position) or on soft bedding, pillows, comforters, and quilts as an infant risk factor for SIDS.  Why B wrong: Supine on a firm mattress is the protective/recommended position, not a risk factor.  Why C wrong: Side-lying on a firm surface is not the listed risk factor.  Why D wrong: Room-sharing without bed-sharing is not listed as a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which birth-related characteristics are listed as infant risk factors for SIDS?","options":["Low birth weight and preterm babies / prematurity","Macrosomia and post-term birth","Normal birth weight and term birth","Large-for-gestational-age status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists low birth weight and preterm babies or prematurity as infant risk factors.  Why B wrong: Macrosomia/post-term is not listed as a SIDS risk factor.  Why C wrong: Normal birth weight at term is not a risk factor.  Why D wrong: Large-for-gestational-age is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the source, SIDS shows a greater incidence in which group?","options":["Male child (higher than female)","Female child (higher than male)","Equal in both sexes","Only in twins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states there is a greater incidence in the male child than female (also restated as \"higher percentage of males than females\").  Why B wrong: The reverse is stated; males have the higher incidence.  Why C wrong: The source specifically notes a male predominance, not equal incidence.  Why D wrong: Twin status is not the listed factor."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which combination is listed among the infant risk factors for SIDS?","options":["Low APGAR scores and hypothermia","High APGAR scores and hyperthermia","Normal APGAR with fever","High APGAR with hypoglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists low APGAR scores and hypothermia as infant risk factors.  Why B wrong: High APGAR/hyperthermia is the opposite of the listed factor.  Why C wrong: Normal APGAR with fever is not listed.  Why D wrong: Hypoglycemia is not a listed SIDS risk factor here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which respiratory disorder is specifically named among the infant risk factors for SIDS?","options":["Broncho-pulmonary dysplasia","Asthma","Cystic fibrosis","Pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"respiratory disorders like broncho-pulmonary dysplasia\" as an infant risk factor.  Why B wrong: Asthma is not the named respiratory disorder here.  Why C wrong: Cystic fibrosis is covered separately and not listed as a SIDS factor.  Why D wrong: Pneumonia is not the named risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Environmental exposure to which of the following is listed as an infant risk factor for SIDS?","options":["Environmental tobacco smoke","High-altitude environment","Cold water exposure","Loud noise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists exposure to environmental tobacco smoke as an infant risk factor.  Why B wrong: High altitude is not listed.  Why C wrong: Cold water exposure is not listed.  Why D wrong: Loud noise is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"A history of which family event increases an infant's risk for SIDS?","options":["A sibling who died of SIDS","A sibling with asthma","A parent with hypertension","A grandparent with diabetes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists a \"sibling who died of SIDS\" (a previous child with SIDS) as a risk factor.  Why B wrong: Sibling asthma is not the listed factor.  Why C wrong: Parental hypertension is not listed.  Why D wrong: Grandparental diabetes is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which of the following is a MATERNAL risk factor for SIDS?","options":["Very young age of the mother","Advanced maternal age over 40","Grand multiparity alone","Maternal obesity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"very young women\" among maternal risk factors for SIDS.  Why B wrong: Advanced maternal age is not the listed maternal factor.  Why C wrong: Grand multiparity is not listed.  Why D wrong: Maternal obesity is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which maternal behaviors during pregnancy are listed as SIDS risk factors?","options":["Smoking during pregnancy and drug abuse","Regular prenatal vitamin use","Adequate hydration and rest","Breastfeeding preparation classes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists smoking during pregnancy and abused drugs as maternal risk factors.  Why B wrong: Prenatal vitamins are protective, not a risk factor.  Why C wrong: Hydration and rest are not risk factors.  Why D wrong: Breastfeeding classes are not a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Apart from infant and maternal factors, which \"other\" mechanism is listed as contributing to SIDS?","options":["Sudden pressure on the baby by adults during sleeping","Excessive swaddling in cold weather","Frequent night feedings","Use of a pacifier"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Suddenly pressure on the baby by adults during sleeping\" as an other/contributing factor.  Why B wrong: Swaddling is not the listed \"other\" factor.  Why C wrong: Night feedings are not listed.  Why D wrong: Pacifier use is not listed as a risk factor here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(NORCET-04, 2023) Which of the following children has the LOWEST risk for Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS)?","options":["Child lying on the back","Child with a history of a previous child who had sudden infant death syndrome","Child lying on the abdomen","Child sleeping in side-lying position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The keyed answer is A. Supine (back) sleeping is protective and carries the lowest SIDS risk.  Why B wrong: A prior sibling SIDS death is a recognized risk factor (higher risk).  Why C wrong: Prone (abdomen) sleeping is a major risk factor.  Why D wrong: Side-lying is unstable (infant may roll prone) and carries higher risk than supine."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are listed risk factors for SIDS?","options":["Hypothermia, low birth weight, low APGAR score, prematurity","Higher percentage of males than females","Sleeping on the abdomen (prone) and on a soft mattress","Advanced maternal age (>40 years)","Maternal smoking during pregnancy and drug abuse"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, E): The source's SIDS risk-factor list explicitly includes hypothermia/low birth weight/low APGAR/prematurity, male predominance, prone/soft-mattress sleeping, and maternal smoking/drug abuse.  Why D wrong: The maternal factor is \"very young women,\" not advanced maternal age."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Cystic fibrosis is described as a congenital abnormality due to a defect in which chromosome?","options":["Chromosome number 7","Chromosome number 21","Chromosome number 23","Chromosome number 11"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cystic fibrosis is a congenital abnormality due to the defect in chromosome number 7.  Why B wrong: Chromosome 21 relates to Down syndrome, not cystic fibrosis.  Why C wrong: Chromosome 23 is not stated as the defect.  Why D wrong: Chromosome 11 is not the source-stated chromosome."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the mode of inheritance of cystic fibrosis, and which type of glands does it primarily affect?","options":["Autosomal recessive disorder affecting exocrine / mucus-producing glands","Autosomal dominant disorder affecting endocrine glands","X-linked recessive disorder affecting sweat glands only","Mitochondrial disorder affecting the pancreas only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is an autosomal recessive (\"receive\") disorder that affects exocrine or mucus-producing glands.  Why B wrong: It is recessive, not dominant, and affects exocrine (not endocrine) glands.  Why C wrong: It is autosomal, not X-linked, and affects all exocrine glands, not sweat glands only.  Why D wrong: It is not mitochondrial and affects multiple exocrine glands."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In cystic fibrosis, what happens to the secretions of the affected exocrine glands?","options":["All exocrine gland secretions become thick","All secretions become watery and dilute","Secretions stop completely","Only sweat becomes thick"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in this disorder the exocrine glands are affected and secretions of all exocrine glands become thick.  Why B wrong: Secretions become thick, not watery/dilute.  Why C wrong: Secretions do not stop; they become abnormally thick.  Why D wrong: All exocrine secretions thicken, not sweat alone."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In cystic fibrosis, pancreatic insufficiency occurs in more than what percentage of patients, and what is the resulting management?","options":[">85% — pancreatic enzymes should be supplemented after every meal",">25% — insulin should be given before every meal",">50% — bile salts should be given at bedtime",">10% — no supplementation needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pancreas insufficiency is >85%, and pancreatic enzymes should be supplemented after every meal.  Why B wrong: The figure is >85%, and the supplement is pancreatic enzymes, not insulin.  Why C wrong: The percentage and supplement are incorrect.  Why D wrong: >10% with no supplementation contradicts the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(AIIMS Mangalagiri-2018) Which organ is MOST commonly affected by cystic fibrosis?","options":["Lungs","Liver","Kidney","Heart"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common organ affected by cystic fibrosis is the lungs (AIIMS Mangalagiri-2018).  Why B wrong: The liver is not the most commonly affected organ.  Why C wrong: The kidney is not the source-stated organ.  Why D wrong: The heart is not the most commonly affected organ."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Why is a child with cystic fibrosis prone to respiratory infection?","options":["Stasis of thick mucus in the lungs","Absence of any mucus in the airway","Excess surfactant production","Hyperinflation of alveoli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the patient may be prone to respiratory infection due to stasis of mucus in the lungs.  Why B wrong: There is excess thick mucus, not an absence of mucus.  Why C wrong: Excess surfactant is not the mechanism described.  Why D wrong: Hyperinflation is not the stated cause of infection risk."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In cystic fibrosis, the concentration of which electrolytes increases in the sweat?","options":["Sodium and chloride","Potassium and calcium","Magnesium and phosphate","Bicarbonate and iron"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the concentration of sodium and chloride increases in the sweat.  Why B wrong: Potassium and calcium are not the listed elevated sweat electrolytes.  Why C wrong: Magnesium and phosphate are not stated.  Why D wrong: Bicarbonate and iron are not stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the sweat chloride test, a sweat chloride level above what value is considered positive for cystic fibrosis?","options":["> 60 mEq/L","> 20 mEq/L","> 100 mEq/L","> 40 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if the sweat chloride level increases to > 60 mEq/L, it is considered positive for cystic fibrosis.  Why B wrong: >20 mEq/L is below the stated positive threshold.  Why C wrong: The source threshold is >60, not >100, mEq/L.  Why D wrong: >40 mEq/L is not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Why does a male with cystic fibrosis become sterile?","options":["Secretions in the vas deferens become thick and sperm is not present in the ejaculation","The testes fail to descend","Testosterone production stops completely","The prostate gland is absent from birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the male becomes sterile because the secretion in the vas deferens becomes thick and sperm is not present in the ejaculation.  Why B wrong: Undescended testes is not the stated mechanism.  Why C wrong: Absent testosterone is not described.  Why D wrong: An absent prostate is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Why does a female with cystic fibrosis become infertile?","options":["Cervical mucus becomes thick and obstructs the penetration of sperm","The ovaries fail to produce eggs","The uterus is congenitally absent","Menstruation never begins"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the female becomes infertile because cervical mucus is thick and produces obstruction to sperm penetration.  Why B wrong: Ovarian failure is not the stated mechanism.  Why C wrong: Uterine absence is not described.  Why D wrong: Absence of menstruation is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is head injury defined in the source?","options":["A mechanical force to the skull, scalp and meninges","A metabolic disturbance of brain cells","An infection of the cerebrospinal fluid","A congenital malformation of the skull"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines head injury as a mechanical force to the skull, scalp and meninges.  Why B wrong: A metabolic disturbance is not the definition.  Why C wrong: CSF infection describes meningitis, not head injury.  Why D wrong: A congenital malformation is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"An open head injury occurs when there is:","options":["A fracture or penetration of the skull by an object","Blunt trauma without skull disruption","Only scalp bruising","Isolated concussion without structural damage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states an open head injury occurs when there is a fracture or penetration of the skull by an object.  Why B wrong: Blunt trauma without disruption describes closed head injury.  Why C wrong: Scalp bruising alone is not the definition of open head injury.  Why D wrong: An isolated concussion is not defined as an open injury."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Why is a closed head injury considered MORE serious than an open head injury?","options":["Because of the greater chance of increased ICP in a closed vault","Because there is always visible bleeding","Because the scalp is torn open","Because it never causes brain damage"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states closed head injury (blunt trauma) is more serious than an open head injury because of the greater chance of increased ICP in a closed vault.  Why B wrong: Visible bleeding is more typical of open injury; this is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: A torn scalp describes open injury, not the reason for closed-injury severity.  Why D wrong: Closed head injury can cause serious brain damage."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In infants, closed head injury most commonly occurs due to which mechanism?","options":["Shaken baby syndrome","Motor vehicle collision","Sports injury","Fall from a bicycle"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states closed head injury mostly occurs in infants by shaken baby syndrome.  Why B wrong: Motor vehicle collision is not the mechanism specified for infants here.  Why C wrong: Sports injury is not applicable to infants.  Why D wrong: A bicycle fall is not the specified infant mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the EARLY sign of increased intracranial pressure (ICP)?","options":["Slight change in vitals and altered level of consciousness (LOC)","Bradycardia with fixed dilated pupils","Decerebrate posturing","Absent motor and sensory responses"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists a slight change in vitals and altered level of consciousness (LOC) as the early sign of increased ICP.  Why B wrong: Bradycardia with pupil changes is a late sign.  Why C wrong: Decerebrate posturing is a late finding.  Why D wrong: Absent motor/sensory responses are late signs."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are EARLY signs of increased ICP specifically in an INFANT?","options":["Irritability and high-pitched cry","Bulging fontanel, increased head circumference, and dilated scalp veins","Macewen's sign (cracked-pot sound on percussion of the head)","Setting sun sign (sclera visible above the iris)","Decerebrate posturing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists irritability/high-pitched cry, bulging fontanel/increased head circumference/dilated scalp veins, Macewen's sign, and setting sun sign as early signs in the infant.  Why E wrong: Decerebrate posturing (rigid extension) is a late sign, not an early infant sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Macewen's sign, an early sign of increased ICP in infants, refers to:","options":["A cracked-pot sound on percussion of the head","Sclera visible above the iris","Bulging fontanel with dilated scalp veins","High-pitched cry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Macewen's sign as a cracked-pot sound on percussion of the head.  Why B wrong: Sclera visible above the iris is the setting sun sign.  Why C wrong: Bulging fontanel/dilated scalp veins is a separate listed finding.  Why D wrong: A high-pitched cry is a separate early sign, not Macewen's sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"The \"setting sun sign,\" an early sign of increased ICP in infants, is described as:","options":["Sclera visible above the iris","Cracked-pot sound on percussion","Pinpoint constricted pupils","Yellowing of the sclera"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes the setting sun sign as sclera visible above the iris.  Why B wrong: A cracked-pot sound is Macewen's sign.  Why C wrong: Pinpoint pupils are not the setting sun sign.  Why D wrong: Yellow sclera (jaundice) is unrelated to the setting sun sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which findings are listed as EARLY signs of increased ICP in a CHILD (older than infancy)?","options":["Headache","Nausea and vomiting","Visual disturbance (diplopia)","Seizures","Bulging fontanel"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists headache, nausea, vomiting, visual disturbance (diplopia), and seizures as early signs in the child.  Why E wrong: A bulging fontanel is an infant sign (the fontanel is closed in older children)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are LATE signs of increased ICP?","options":["Decreased level of consciousness (LOC)","Bradycardia","Decreased motor and sensory responses","Alteration in pupil size and reactivity","Irritability and high-pitched cry"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists decreased LOC, bradycardia, decreased motor/sensory responses, and alteration in pupil size/reactivity as late signs.  Why E wrong: Irritability/high-pitched cry is an early infant sign, not a late sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Decorticate posturing (abnormal flexion) is characterized by adduction of the arms at the shoulders with the arms flexed on the chest, and is due to injury in which area of the brain?","options":["Cerebral cortex","Mid brain","Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states decorticate (abnormal flexion) posture, with arm adduction and flexion on the chest, is due to injury in the cerebral cortex.  Why B wrong: Midbrain injury causes decerebrate posturing, not decorticate.  Why C wrong: The cerebellum is not the stated site for decorticate posturing.  Why D wrong: The medulla is not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Decerebrate posturing, showing rigid extension and pronation of the arms and legs, is caused by injury in which area of the brain?","options":["Mid brain","Cerebral cortex","Frontal lobe","Occipital lobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states decerebrate (extension position) — rigid extension and pronation of arms and legs — is caused by injury in the mid brain.  Why B wrong: Cerebral cortex injury causes decorticate posturing.  Why C wrong: The frontal lobe is not the stated site.  Why D wrong: The occipital lobe is not the stated site."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In caring for a child with a head injury, which position should the nurse maintain?","options":["Low Fowler position","Trendelenburg position","Prone position","Complete supine flat position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to monitor LOC and maintain low Fowler position.  Why B wrong: Trendelenburg (head down) would worsen ICP and is not recommended.  Why C wrong: Prone position is not the recommended head-injury position.  Why D wrong: The source specifies low Fowler, not flat supine."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"During the acute phase of a head injury, why are sedating medications withheld?","options":["So that changes in the level of consciousness (LOC) can be assessed","Because they cause respiratory arrest immediately","Because sedatives raise blood glucose","Because they are ineffective in children"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to withhold sedating medications during the acute phase so that a change in LOC can be assessed.  Why B wrong: The reason given is masking of LOC changes, not immediate respiratory arrest.  Why C wrong: Blood glucose effect is not the reason.  Why D wrong: Ineffectiveness is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In a child with a head injury and possible skull fracture, why should the nurse AVOID suctioning through the direct nose (nasal route)?","options":["The catheter could enter the brain through a fracture, risking a secondary infection","It would cause immediate nasal bleeding that cannot be controlled","It stimulates the vagus nerve and stops the heart","It always triggers a seizure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to avoid nasal suctioning because the catheter could enter the brain through a fracture, placing the child at high risk for secondary infection.  Why B wrong: Uncontrollable bleeding is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Vagal cardiac arrest is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Seizure induction is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Drainage from the nose or ear of a head-injured child that tests POSITIVE for glucose most likely indicates:","options":["Leakage of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)","Bacterial sinus infection","Normal nasal secretion","Blood contamination only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that drainage from the nose or ear should be checked for glucose; if positive, it indicates leakage of CSF and the doctor should be informed.  Why B wrong: A positive glucose test indicates CSF, not a sinus infection.  Why C wrong: Normal nasal secretion would not warrant the CSF-leak concern described.  Why D wrong: Blood contamination is not what the glucose test indicates here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"On the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) for infants and children, what is the maximum score obtainable for the EYE OPENING component?","options":["4 (spontaneous eye opening)","5 (spontaneous eye opening)","6 (spontaneous eye opening)","3 (spontaneous eye opening)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the GCS eye-opening component, spontaneous opening scores 4 (highest); to speech 3, to pain 2, no response 1.  Why B wrong: 5 is the maximum for verbal response, not eye opening.  Why C wrong: 6 is the maximum for motor response, not eye opening.  Why D wrong: 3 corresponds to \"to speech,\" not the maximum."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"On the infant GCS, which BEST verbal response earns the maximum verbal score of 5?","options":["Coos and babbles","Irritable","Cries to pain","Moans to pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the infant verbal response, \"coos and babbles\" is the best response and scores 5.  Why B wrong: \"Irritable\" scores 4, not the maximum.  Why C wrong: \"Cries to pain\" scores 3.  Why D wrong: \"Moans to pain\" scores 2."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"On the child GCS, which verbal response corresponds to the maximum verbal score of 5?","options":["Oriented, appropriate","Confused","Inappropriate words","Incomprehensible sounds"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the child verbal response, \"oriented, appropriate\" scores 5 (highest).  Why B wrong: \"Confused\" scores 4.  Why C wrong: \"Inappropriate words\" scores 3.  Why D wrong: \"Incomprehensible sounds\" scores 2."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"On the GCS motor response for a child, what is the maximum score and its corresponding response?","options":["6 — obeys commands","5 — localizes painful stimulus","4 — withdraws in response","3 — flexion in response to pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The motor response maximum is 6 for \"obeys commands\" in a child (moves spontaneously/purposefully = 6 in the infant).  Why B wrong: \"Localizes painful stimulus\" scores 5.  Why C wrong: \"Withdraws in response\" scores 4.  Why D wrong: \"Flexion in response to pain\" scores 3."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"On the infant GCS motor response, which behavior earns the highest motor score of 6?","options":["Moves spontaneously and purposefully","Withdraws to touch","Abnormal flexion posture to pain","Abnormal extension posture to pain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For the infant motor response, \"moves spontaneously and purposefully\" is the best response scoring 6.  Why B wrong: \"Withdraws to touch\" scores 5.  Why C wrong: Abnormal flexion posture scores lower (decorticate-type).  Why D wrong: Abnormal extension posture scores lower (decerebrate-type)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is meningitis defined?","options":["Acute inflammation of the meninges, the layers covering the brain","Inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma","Excessive neuronal discharge in the brain","Degeneration of peripheral nerves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines meningitis as the acute inflammation of the meninges, layers of the brain.  Why B wrong: Inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma is pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Excessive neuronal discharge describes seizure disorder.  Why D wrong: Peripheral nerve degeneration is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Meningitis is described as the most common infection of which system in infants and children?","options":["Central nervous system (CNS)","Respiratory system","Gastrointestinal system","Urinary system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states meningitis is the most common CNS infection in infants and children.  Why B wrong: It is a CNS, not respiratory, infection.  Why C wrong: It is not a GI infection.  Why D wrong: It is not a urinary infection."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the most common type of meningitis in children under 5 years of age?","options":["Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningitis","Fungal meningitis","Tuberculosis meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of meningitis under 5 years of age is bacterial meningitis.  Why B wrong: Viral is not stated as most common under 5 years.  Why C wrong: Fungal meningitis is not the most common type.  Why D wrong: TB meningitis is not the most common under 5 years."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of meningitis?","options":["Direct contact / droplet spread","Fecal-oral route","Blood transfusion only","Vector (mosquito) bite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the mode of transmission of meningitis is direct contact / droplet spread.  Why B wrong: Fecal-oral is not the stated route.  Why C wrong: Blood transfusion is not the stated route.  Why D wrong: Vector-borne transmission is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Community-acquired bacterial meningitis is mainly caused by which organisms, and in what proportions?","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae (50%) and Neisseria meningitidis (15%)","E. coli (50%) and Group B Streptococcus (15%)","Haemophilus influenzae (50%) and Cryptococcus (15%)","Mycobacterium tuberculosis (50%) and enterovirus (15%)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states community-acquired bacterial meningitis is mainly caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae (50%) and Neisseria meningitidis (15%) and others.  Why B wrong: E. coli/GBS proportions are not the stated main causes here.  Why C wrong: H. influenzae/Cryptococcus percentages are not stated.  Why D wrong: TB/enterovirus are not the main community-acquired bacterial causes cited."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Bacterial meningitis is also known by which other names?","options":["Septic or pyogenic meningitis","Aseptic meningitis","Chemical meningitis","Granulomatous meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bacterial meningitis is also known as septic or pyogenic meningitis.  Why B wrong: Aseptic meningitis is the term for viral meningitis.  Why C wrong: Chemical meningitis is not the stated alternate name.  Why D wrong: Granulomatous meningitis is not the stated name."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Bacterial meningitis is transmitted by droplets from which source?","options":["Nasopharyngeal secretions","Contaminated food","Skin-to-skin contact only","Insect bites"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bacterial meningitis is transmitted by droplets from nasopharyngeal secretion.  Why B wrong: Food is not the stated transmission source.  Why C wrong: Skin contact alone is not the stated route.  Why D wrong: Insect bites are not the route."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the diagnostic test for bacterial meningitis?","options":["Lumbar puncture with CSF culture testing","Chest X-ray","Throat swab culture","Sweat chloride test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis of bacterial meningitis is done by lumbar puncture with CSF culture testing.  Why B wrong: Chest X-ray diagnoses pneumonia, not meningitis.  Why C wrong: Throat swab culture diagnoses croup, not meningitis.  Why D wrong: Sweat chloride test diagnoses cystic fibrosis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) In bacterial meningitis, lumbar puncture / CSF findings include which of the following?","options":["Increased WBC of more than 11,000","Cloudy colour with increased ICP pressure","Elevated protein level more than 100 mg/dl","Decreased glucose level below 40 mg/dl","Clear CSF with normal glucose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists WBC >11,000, cloudy colour with increased ICP pressure, protein >100 mg/dl, and glucose <40 mg/dl as bacterial meningitis CSF findings.  Why E wrong: Clear CSF with normal glucose describes viral (aseptic) meningitis, not bacterial."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In bacterial meningitis, the CSF protein level is elevated to more than what value?","options":["More than 100 mg/dl","More than 45 mg/dl","More than 15 mg/dl","More than 60 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states elevated protein level more than 100 mg/dl in bacterial meningitis.  Why B wrong: 45 mg/dl is the upper normal CSF protein, not the elevated bacterial value.  Why C wrong: 15 mg/dl is the lower normal value.  Why D wrong: 60 mg/dl is not the stated cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In bacterial meningitis, the CSF glucose level is decreased to below what value?","options":["Below 40 mg/dl","Below 80 mg/dl","Below 100 mg/dl","Below 50 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states decreased glucose level below 40 mg/dl in bacterial meningitis.  Why B wrong: 80 mg/dl is within/near the normal upper range.  Why C wrong: 100 mg/dl is above normal glucose values.  Why D wrong: 50 mg/dl is at the lower normal boundary, not the stated bacterial cutoff."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Viral meningitis is also called by which name?","options":["Aseptic meningitis","Pyogenic meningitis","Septic meningitis","Tuberculous meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states viral meningitis is also called aseptic meningitis.  Why B wrong: Pyogenic meningitis is bacterial.  Why C wrong: Septic meningitis is bacterial.  Why D wrong: Tuberculous meningitis is a separate type."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which viruses are listed as causes of viral (aseptic) meningitis?","options":["Enterovirus","Mumps","Paramyxovirus","Herpesvirus","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists enterovirus, mumps, paramyxovirus, and herpesvirus as causes of viral meningitis.  Why E wrong: Cryptococcus neoformans is a fungus and the cause of fungal meningitis, not viral."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Tuberculosis meningitis (TBM) is caused by which organism?","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Neisseria meningitidis","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TB meningitis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis bacteria.  Why B wrong: S. pneumoniae causes bacterial meningitis.  Why C wrong: N. meningitidis causes meningococcal meningitis.  Why D wrong: Cryptococcus causes fungal meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the most common complication of tuberculosis meningitis (TBM)?","options":["Hydrocephalus","Deafness","Seizure disorder","Optic atrophy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common complication of TBM is hydrocephalus.  Why B wrong: Deafness is not stated as the most common TBM complication.  Why C wrong: Seizure disorder is not the stated most common complication.  Why D wrong: Optic atrophy is not listed as the most common complication."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"The CSF findings in tuberculosis meningitis include which type of cellular predominance?","options":["Lymphocyte pleocytosis","Neutrophil predominance","Eosinophil predominance","No cells present"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states TBM CSF findings include elevated ICP pressure, low glucose, high protein, and increased WBC level (lymphocyte pleocytosis).  Why B wrong: Neutrophil predominance is more typical of bacterial meningitis, not the TBM finding stated.  Why C wrong: Eosinophil predominance is not stated.  Why D wrong: TBM shows increased WBC (lymphocytes), not an absence of cells."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the most common cause of fungal meningitis?","options":["Cryptococcus neoformans","Candida albicans","Aspergillus fumigatus","Histoplasma capsulatum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of fungal meningitis is Cryptococcus neoformans.  Why B wrong: Candida is not stated as the most common cause.  Why C wrong: Aspergillus is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: Histoplasma is not the stated cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) According to the source, which organisms are the most common causes of bacterial meningitis in NEWBORNS?","options":["Group B Streptococcus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Listeria monocytogenes","E. coli","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): For newborns, the source lists Group B Streptococcus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, L. monocytogenes, and E. coli.  Why E wrong: Cryptococcus neoformans is the fungal meningitis cause, not a newborn bacterial meningitis organism."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In \"teens and young adults,\" which organisms are the most common causes of bacterial meningitis?","options":["Neisseria meningitidis and Streptococcus pneumoniae","E. coli and Listeria monocytogenes","Cryptococcus neoformans and Candida","Mycobacterium tuberculosis alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Neisseria meningitidis and S. pneumoniae for teens and young adults.  Why B wrong: E. coli/Listeria are more associated with newborns/older adults.  Why C wrong: Fungal organisms are not the stated bacterial causes.  Why D wrong: M. tuberculosis alone is not the listed cause for this age group."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"For \"babies and young children,\" which of the following is listed among the most common bacterial meningitis organisms?","options":["Haemophilus influenzae","E. coli","Cryptococcus neoformans","Pseudomonas aeruginosa"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For babies and young children, the source lists Streptococcus pneumoniae, N. meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B Streptococcus, and M. tuberculosis.  Why B wrong: E. coli is listed under newborns, not this group.  Why C wrong: Cryptococcus is a fungal cause, not listed here.  Why D wrong: Pseudomonas is not listed for this age group."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which are the listed most common bacterial meningitis organisms in OLDER ADULTS?","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Neisseria meningitidis","Haemophilus influenzae","Group B Streptococcus","Listeria monocytogenes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): For older adults the source lists S. pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, H. influenzae, group B Streptococcus, and L. monocytogenes.  Why none excluded: All five organisms are explicitly listed for older adults per the CDC source cited."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are signs and symptoms of meningitis IN INFANTS?","options":["Nuchal rigidity or hyperextension of the neck (opisthotonos)","Irritability and high-pitched cry","Fever and difficulty in feeding or anorexia","Bulging or tense fontanels","Photophobia as the earliest sign"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists nuchal rigidity/opisthotonos, irritability/high-pitched cry, fever/feeding difficulty/anorexia, and bulging/tense fontanels for infants.  Why E wrong: Photophobia is listed under children/adolescents/adults, not as the earliest infant sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In meningococcal meningitis, the combination of vomiting, petechiae, purpuric skin rashes, peripheral circulatory collapse, and shock is known as:","options":["Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","Reye's syndrome","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Cushing's triad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source names this presentation Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome.  Why B wrong: Reye's syndrome relates to aspirin use in viral illness, not this.  Why C wrong: Guillain-Barré is an ascending paralysis, not this syndrome.  Why D wrong: Cushing's triad describes late ICP signs, not meningococcal shock."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) In children, adolescents, and adults, which findings are listed for meningitis?","options":["Increased ICP — headache, bradycardia, irritability, and vomiting","Fever, nausea, and vomiting","Deterioration in level of consciousness (LOC)","Irritability, agitation, and photophobia","Bulging fontanels"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists increased ICP signs, fever/nausea/vomiting, deterioration in LOC, and irritability/agitation/photophobia for children, adolescents, and adults.  Why E wrong: Bulging fontanels is an infant sign (fontanels are closed in older children)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"\"Ear drains\" (ear drainage) as a symptom of meningitis is specifically associated with which type?","options":["Pneumococcal meningitis","Meningococcal meningitis","Viral meningitis","Fungal meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ear drains are seen with pneumococcal meningitis.  Why B wrong: Meningococcal meningitis is associated with petechiae/purpura and Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome, not ear drainage per this text.  Why C wrong: Viral meningitis is not associated with ear drainage here.  Why D wrong: Fungal meningitis is not linked to ear drainage in the source."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which two signs are described as the cardinal (hallmark) signs of meningitis?","options":["Positive Kernig's sign and Brudzinski's sign","Positive Babinski and Romberg signs","Positive Chvostek and Trousseau signs","Positive Murphy and McBurney signs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists positive Kernig's sign and Brudzinski's sign as the cardinal signs (hallmark sign of meningitis in the figure).  Why B wrong: Babinski/Romberg are neurological signs unrelated to meningeal irritation cardinal signs here.  Why C wrong: Chvostek/Trousseau indicate hypocalcemia.  Why D wrong: Murphy/McBurney are abdominal signs."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is Kernig's sign defined?","options":["Due to severe stiffness of hamstrings — inability to extend the legs when the thigh is flexed anteriorly at the hip","Neck flexion causing flexion of the hips and knees","Involuntary jerking of the extremities","Downgoing plantar reflex"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Kernig's sign as inability to extend the legs (from hamstring stiffness) when the thigh is flexed anteriorly at the hip.  Why B wrong: Neck flexion causing hip/knee flexion describes Brudzinski's sign.  Why C wrong: Involuntary jerking is not the definition of Kernig's sign.  Why D wrong: A plantar reflex is unrelated to Kernig's sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is Brudzinski's sign defined?","options":["Severe neck stiffness / neck flexion causes adduction and flexion movement of the lower extremities","Inability to extend the legs when the thigh is flexed at the hip","Pain on abrupt release of abdominal pressure","Facial twitch on tapping the cheek"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines Brudzinski's sign as severe neck stiffness or neck flexion causing adduction and flexion movement of the lower extremities.  Why B wrong: That description is Kernig's sign.  Why C wrong: Rebound abdominal tenderness is not Brudzinski's sign.  Why D wrong: A facial twitch (Chvostek) is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the CSF value table, what are the NORMAL CSF values for glucose and protein?","options":["Glucose 50–80 mg/dl; Protein 15–45 mg/dl","Glucose 15–45 mg/dl; Protein 50–80 mg/dl","Glucose 90–180 mg/dl; Protein <8 mg/dl","Glucose <40 mg/dl; Protein >100 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists normal CSF glucose as 50–80 mg/dl and normal protein as 15–45 mg/dl.  Why B wrong: The glucose and protein values are transposed.  Why C wrong: 90–180 mm H2O is the normal opening pressure, and <8 is the cell count, not these values.  Why D wrong: Glucose <40 / protein >100 are the bacterial meningitis values, not normal."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the CSF value table, the NORMAL CSF opening pressure and leukocyte (WBC) count are:","options":["Opening pressure 90–180 mm H2O; WBC <8 cells/mm3","Opening pressure 15–45 mm Hg; WBC >11,000 cells/mm3","Opening pressure 50–80 mm Hg; WBC <40 cells/mm3","Opening pressure 200–300 mm Hg; WBC 100 cells/mm3"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists normal CSF opening pressure as 90–180 mm H2O and normal leukocyte count as <8 cells/mm3.  Why B wrong: These are not the normal values listed.  Why C wrong: 50–80 mg/dl is the normal glucose, not the pressure/WBC.  Why D wrong: These values are not listed as normal."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(ESIC-2024) In BACTERIAL meningitis, the CSF appearance, glucose, and protein are respectively:","options":["Turbid appearance; low glucose; high protein","Clear appearance; normal glucose; normal protein","Clear appearance; low glucose; high protein","Turbid appearance; high glucose; low protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists bacterial meningitis as turbid CSF, high leukocyte count, low glucose, high protein, and high opening pressure (ESIC-2024).  Why B wrong: Clear/normal values describe viral meningitis.  Why C wrong: Bacterial CSF is turbid, not clear.  Why D wrong: Bacterial CSF shows low glucose and high protein, not the reverse."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the CSF value table, VIRAL meningitis characteristically shows which CSF pattern?","options":["Clear appearance, low WBC, normal glucose, normal protein, slightly high opening pressure","Turbid appearance, high WBC, low glucose, high protein","Clear or straw colour, high WBC, low glucose, high protein","Turbid appearance, low WBC, low glucose, high protein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists viral meningitis as clear, low leukocyte count, normal glucose, normal protein, and slightly high opening pressure.  Why B wrong: That pattern (turbid, low glucose, high protein) describes bacterial meningitis.  Why C wrong: Clear/straw with high WBC and low glucose describes TB meningitis.  Why D wrong: Turbid with low glucose/high protein describes fungal meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the CSF value table, which meningitis type shows a \"clear or straw\" CSF appearance with HIGH leukocyte count, LOW glucose, and HIGH protein?","options":["TB meningitis (TBM)","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningitis","Fungal meningitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists TB meningitis (TBM) as clear or straw appearance, high WBC, low glucose, high protein, and high opening pressure.  Why B wrong: Bacterial CSF is turbid, not clear/straw.  Why C wrong: Viral CSF has normal glucose and protein.  Why D wrong: Fungal CSF is turbid with low WBC."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the CSF value table, FUNGAL meningitis shows which appearance and leukocyte count?","options":["Turbid appearance with LOW leukocyte count","Clear appearance with high leukocyte count","Turbid appearance with high leukocyte count","Clear or straw with low leukocyte count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists fungal meningitis as turbid appearance, low leukocyte count, low glucose, high protein, and normal to high opening pressure.  Why B wrong: Fungal CSF is turbid, not clear, and WBC is low.  Why C wrong: Fungal CSF has low, not high, leukocytes.  Why D wrong: Clear/straw with low WBC does not match the fungal row."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What position should be provided for a child with meningitis during nursing care?","options":["Side lying with the head supported in extension","Prone with head turned to the side","High Fowler with the chin on the chest","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states position should be side lying with head supported with extension.  Why B wrong: Prone is not the recommended meningitis position.  Why C wrong: Chin-on-chest (neck flexion) worsens meningeal irritation and is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg is not the recommended position."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"For a child with meningitis, respiratory isolation precautions should be maintained for at least how long after antibiotics are administered?","options":["At least 24 hours","At least 48 hours","At least 72 hours","At least 12 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to provide respiratory isolation precautions for at least 24 hours after antibiotics are administered.  Why B wrong: 48 hours refers to droplet precautions after start of therapy, not respiratory isolation.  Why C wrong: 72 hours is not the stated period.  Why D wrong: 12 hours is not the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"For meningitis, droplet precautions should be provided for at least how long, after which the client may no longer be contagious following the start of antibiotic therapy?","options":["At least 48 hours","At least 24 hours","At least 96 hours","At least 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to provide droplet precautions for at least 48 hours, and usually after 48 hours from the start of antibiotic therapy the client may no longer be contagious.  Why B wrong: 24 hours refers to respiratory isolation, not droplet precautions.  Why C wrong: 96 hours is not stated.  Why D wrong: 7 days is not the stated period."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the drug of choice for meningitis, and what is the second drug of choice?","options":["Penicillin is the drug of choice; ceftriaxone is the second DOC","Ceftriaxone is the drug of choice; penicillin is the second DOC","Vancomycin is the drug of choice; gentamicin is the second DOC","Acyclovir is the drug of choice; penicillin is the second DOC"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For empiric treatment of bacterial meningitis, current guidance (IDSA) is a third-generation cephalosporin — ceftriaxone (or cefotaxime) — as first-line, generally with vancomycin added (± ampicillin if Listeria is a concern in neonates/elderly/immunocompromised). Penicillin G is reserved as a drug of choice only for CONFIRMED penicillin-susceptible N. meningitidis / S. pneumoniae. [Corrected: ceftriaxone is the empiric DOC, not penicillin.]  Why A wrong: Penicillin is no longer empiric first-line due to widespread penicillin-resistant pneumococcus; it is used only for confirmed susceptible organisms.  Why C wrong: Vancomycin is an adjunct (added to a cephalosporin), and gentamicin is not a stand-alone meningitis DOC.  Why D wrong: Acyclovir is antiviral (for HSV encephalitis), not the DOC for bacterial meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(PGI-2018) Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is best described as:","options":["Adrenal gland failure due to bleeding into the adrenal gland, usually caused by severe meningococcal meningitis with peripheral vascular collapse","Failure of the pituitary gland due to postpartum hemorrhage","Thyroid storm secondary to infection","Renal failure from acute glomerulonephritis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is adrenal gland failure due to bleeding into the adrenal gland, usually caused by severe meningococcal meningitis resulting in peripheral vascular collapse; it is the most severe complication of bacterial meningitis (PGI-2018).  Why B wrong: Pituitary failure from postpartum hemorrhage is Sheehan's syndrome.  Why C wrong: Thyroid storm is unrelated.  Why D wrong: Renal failure from glomerulonephritis is unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In meningitis nursing care, the head of the bed should be elevated to what degree, avoiding neck flexion and extreme hip flexion?","options":["30 degrees","45 degrees","15 degrees","90 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to elevate the head of the bed 30 degrees, avoiding neck flexion and extreme hip flexion.  Why B wrong: 45 degrees is not the stated elevation.  Why C wrong: 15 degrees is not the stated elevation.  Why D wrong: 90 degrees is not the stated elevation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is a seizure disorder defined?","options":["Excessive or unorganised neuronal discharges in the brain","Inflammation of the meninges","Mechanical force to the skull","Thick secretions from exocrine glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines seizure disorder as excessive or unorganised neuronal discharges in the brain.  Why B wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis.  Why C wrong: Mechanical force to the skull is head injury.  Why D wrong: Thick exocrine secretions describe cystic fibrosis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Partial seizures are characterized by:","options":["Arising from a specific area or one side of the brain, causing limited symptoms","Involving the entire brain from onset with loss of consciousness","Occurring only during sleep","Always being accompanied by fever"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states partial seizures arise from a specific area in the brain or only one side of the brain and cause limited symptoms.  Why B wrong: Whole-brain involvement describes generalized seizures.  Why C wrong: Sleep-only occurrence is not the defining feature.  Why D wrong: Fever accompaniment describes febrile seizures, not partial seizures."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the most common type of seizure in NEONATES?","options":["Subtle seizure","Febrile seizure","Absence seizure","Tonic-clonic seizure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of seizure in neonates is the subtle seizure.  Why B wrong: Febrile seizure is most common in children, not neonates.  Why C wrong: Absence seizure is not the stated neonatal type.  Why D wrong: Tonic-clonic is not stated as the most common neonatal type."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the most common type of seizure in CHILDREN?","options":["Febrile seizure","Subtle seizure","Myoclonic seizure","Atonic seizure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common type of seizure in children is the febrile seizure.  Why B wrong: Subtle seizure is most common in neonates.  Why C wrong: Myoclonic seizure is not the stated most common childhood type.  Why D wrong: Atonic seizure is not the stated most common type."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the drug of choice for FEBRILE seizures?","options":["Intranasal midazolam and rectal diazepam","Oral phenobarbital only","Intravenous phenytoin","Lorazepam only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for febrile seizures is intranasal midazolam and rectal diazepam.  Why B wrong: Oral phenobarbital is not the stated DOC for febrile seizures.  Why C wrong: IV phenytoin is not the stated febrile-seizure DOC.  Why D wrong: Lorazepam is the DOC for status epilepticus, not febrile seizures."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the drug of choice for status epilepticus?","options":["Lorazepam","Intranasal midazolam","Rectal diazepam","Penicillin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for status epilepticus (\"status epileptics\") is lorazepam.  Why B wrong: Intranasal midazolam is listed for febrile seizures.  Why C wrong: Rectal diazepam is listed for febrile seizures.  Why D wrong: Penicillin is an antibiotic for meningitis, not a seizure drug."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are listed causes of seizures?","options":["Trauma","Tumors","Genetic factors","Circulatory and metabolic disorders","Toxicity and infections"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): The source lists causes including trauma, tumors, genetic factors, circulatory and metabolic disorders, and toxicity and infections.  Why none excluded: All five categories are explicitly listed as seizure causes."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"When assessing a child with seizures, the nurse should ask about the presence of an \"aura,\" which is described as:","options":["A warning sign of impending seizures","The post-seizure confusion period","Involuntary tongue biting","A type of febrile response"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes aura as a warning (\"warming\") sign of impending seizures.  Why B wrong: Post-seizure confusion is the postictal state, not the aura.  Why C wrong: Tongue biting is a seizure event, not the aura warning.  Why D wrong: An aura is not a febrile response."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"If a febrile seizure is suspected, why should the child be protected from shivering?","options":["Shivering increases the metabolic rate, which further raises temperature","Shivering directly triggers another seizure focus","Shivering lowers oxygen saturation to zero","Shivering causes hyperglycemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to protect from shivering because it increases metabolic rate, which then further raises temperature.  Why B wrong: The stated mechanism is metabolic/temperature-related, not a direct seizure focus.  Why C wrong: Oxygen saturation dropping to zero is not the stated reason.  Why D wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"A priority nursing action during any seizure is to:","options":["Protect the child from physical injury","Restrain the child's limbs firmly","Insert a padded tongue blade between the teeth","Give oral fluids to prevent dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to protect the child from physical injury during a seizure.  Why B wrong: Forcible restraint can cause injury and is not advised.  Why C wrong: Inserting objects in the mouth during a seizure is contraindicated.  Why D wrong: Oral fluids during an active seizure risk aspiration."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) described?","options":["A complex of neuro-developmental disorders with alteration in social interaction, verbal impairment, and bizarre behaviour pattern","An acute inflammation of the meninges","A chronic inflammatory disorder of the airway","A defect in chromosome 7 affecting exocrine glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes ASD as a complex of neuro-developmental disorders with alteration in social interaction, verbal impairment, and bizarre behaviour pattern.  Why B wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis.  Why C wrong: Airway inflammation is asthma.  Why D wrong: Chromosome 7 exocrine defect is cystic fibrosis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"The severity of impairment in Autism Spectrum Disorder ranges from:","options":["Mild to severe","Only mild","Only severe","Always profound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states impairment includes mild to severe.  Why B wrong: It is not limited to mild only.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to severe only.  Why D wrong: It is not always profound."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(DSSSB-2019) In the ASD spectrum, \"Autism\" is specifically characterized by impairment in:","options":["Communication","Motor coordination only","Vision","Hearing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Autism is characterized by impairment in communication (DSSSB-2019).  Why B wrong: Motor coordination-only impairment is more aligned with Rett syndrome features.  Why C wrong: Vision impairment is not the stated feature.  Why D wrong: Hearing impairment is not the stated feature."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Asperger syndrome is characterized by:","options":["Alteration in social behaviour and non-verbal communication","Impairment in language and repetitive movements","Complete loss of speech","Impairment in swallowing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Asperger syndrome involves alteration in social behaviour and non-verbal communication.  Why B wrong: Impairment in language/repetitive movements describes Rett syndrome.  Why C wrong: Complete loss of speech is not the stated feature.  Why D wrong: Swallowing impairment is not part of Asperger syndrome here."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Rett syndrome is characterized by impairment in language, coordination, and repetitive movements, with a higher incidence in which group?","options":["Female children","Male children","Newborns of both sexes equally","Adolescent males only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Rett syndrome involves impairment in language, coordination, and repetitive movements, with incidence more in female children.  Why B wrong: Incidence is higher in females, not males.  Why C wrong: It is not equal in both sexes.  Why D wrong: It is not specific to adolescent males."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of Autism Spectrum Disorder?","options":["Social alteration","Communication or verbal alteration","Behaviour alteration","Bulging fontanel","Petechial rash"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The source lists social alteration, communication/verbal alteration, and behaviour alteration as ASD signs and symptoms.  Why D wrong: Bulging fontanel is a meningitis/ICP sign, not an ASD symptom.  Why E wrong: Petechial rash relates to meningococcal meningitis, not ASD."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"A key nursing care measure for a child with Autism Spectrum Disorder is to:","options":["Determine the child's habits, routines, and preferences and maintain consistency as much as possible","Frequently change the daily routine to build flexibility","Encourage crowded, stimulating environments","Avoid any psychological support"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to determine the child's habits, routines, and preferences and maintain consistency as much as possible.  Why B wrong: Frequently changing routines is the opposite of the recommended consistency.  Why C wrong: Crowded/overstimulating environments are not recommended.  Why D wrong: The source states to seek psychological care, not avoid it."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Mental retardation is also known by which other terms?","options":["Intellectual disability or cognitive impairment","Learning enrichment","Attention deficit disorder","Sensory processing disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states mental retardation is also known as intellectual disability or cognitive impairment.  Why B wrong: \"Learning enrichment\" is not a synonym.  Why C wrong: ADHD is a separate condition.  Why D wrong: Sensory processing disorder is not the stated synonym."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"By which formula is the Intelligence Quotient (IQ) measured?","options":["IQ = (MA / CA) × 100","IQ = (CA / MA) × 100","IQ = MA + CA","IQ = (MA × CA) / 100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source gives the formula Intelligence Quotient (IQ) = MA/CA × 100.  Why B wrong: The ratio is MA over CA, not CA over MA.  Why C wrong: IQ is a ratio times 100, not a sum.  Why D wrong: It is a division, not a product divided by 100."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(ESIC-2024) What is the normal / average IQ of a human being?","options":["90–110","50–70","70–90","110–130"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal/average IQ of a human being is 90–110 (ESIC-2024).  Why B wrong: 50–70 is the mild MR range.  Why C wrong: 70–90 is not the stated normal range.  Why D wrong: 110–130 is above the stated average range."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In the IQ formula, what does \"CA\" stand for?","options":["Chronological Age (actual physical age)","Cognitive Ability","Communication Age","Corrected Age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states CA means Chronological Age or actual physical age.  Why B wrong: \"Cognitive Ability\" is not the definition given.  Why C wrong: \"Communication Age\" is not the definition.  Why D wrong: \"Corrected Age\" is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In the IQ formula, what does \"MA\" stand for?","options":["Mental Age, based on growth and development","Motor Ability","Muscular Age","Maternal Age"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MA means Mental Age, which is based on growth and development.  Why B wrong: \"Motor Ability\" is not the definition.  Why C wrong: \"Muscular Age\" is not the definition.  Why D wrong: \"Maternal Age\" is not the definition."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(AAMR-1983) A child with an IQ of 50–70 falls into which classification of mental retardation and educational category?","options":["Mild MR — Educable","Moderate MR — Trainable","Severe MR — Custodial or dependent","Profound MR — Severely handicapped"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the AAMR-1983 table, IQ 50–70 is Mild MR, classified as Educable MR.  Why B wrong: Moderate MR corresponds to IQ 35–49 (Trainable).  Why C wrong: Severe MR corresponds to IQ 20–25 (Custodial/dependent).  Why D wrong: Profound MR corresponds to IQ <20 (Severely handicapped)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(AAMR-1983) Moderate mental retardation corresponds to which IQ level and educational classification?","options":["IQ 35–49 — Trainable MR","IQ 50–70 — Educable MR","IQ 20–25 — Custodial or dependent MR","IQ <20 — Severely handicapped"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, Moderate MR is IQ 35–49, classified as Trainable MR.  Why B wrong: IQ 50–70 is Mild MR (Educable).  Why C wrong: IQ 20–25 is Severe MR (Custodial/dependent).  Why D wrong: IQ <20 is Profound MR (Severely handicapped)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(AAMR-1983) Which classification corresponds to an IQ level of 20–25?","options":["Severe MR — Custodial or dependent MR","Mild MR — Educable MR","Moderate MR — Trainable MR","Profound MR — Severely handicapped"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, IQ 20–25 is Severe MR, classified as Custodial or dependent MR.  Why B wrong: Mild MR is IQ 50–70 (Educable).  Why C wrong: Moderate MR is IQ 35–49 (Trainable).  Why D wrong: Profound MR is IQ <20 (Severely handicapped)."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(AAMR-1983) A child with an IQ below 20 is classified as:","options":["Profound MR — Severely handicapped","Severe MR — Custodial or dependent MR","Moderate MR — Trainable MR","Mild MR — Educable MR"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Per the table, IQ <20 is Profound MR, classified as Severely handicapped.  Why B wrong: Severe MR is IQ 20–25.  Why C wrong: Moderate MR is IQ 35–49.  Why D wrong: Mild MR is IQ 50–70."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is croup syndrome defined?","options":["A disorder of the respiratory system in which multiple parts of the respiratory system are affected","Inflammation of the meninges","A chromosome 7 exocrine gland defect","A neuro-developmental disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines croup syndrome as a disorder of the respiratory system in which multiple parts of the respiratory system are affected.  Why B wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis.  Why C wrong: Chromosome 7 exocrine defect is cystic fibrosis.  Why D wrong: A neuro-developmental disorder describes ASD."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What diagnostic test is used to make the diagnosis of croup syndrome?","options":["Throat swab culture","Lumbar puncture","Sweat chloride test","Chest X-ray only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis of croup is made by throat swab culture.  Why B wrong: Lumbar puncture is for meningitis.  Why C wrong: Sweat chloride test is for cystic fibrosis.  Why D wrong: Chest X-ray is the gold standard for pneumonia, not the stated croup diagnostic."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Acute epiglottitis is defined as inflammation of the epiglottis due to which type of infection?","options":["Bacterial infection","Viral infection","Fungal infection","Allergic reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute epiglottitis is inflammation of the epiglottis due to bacterial infection.  Why B wrong: Viral infection is more associated with laryngotracheobronchitis.  Why C wrong: Fungal infection is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: An allergic reaction describes spasmodic laryngitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What are the causative agents of acute epiglottitis?","options":["Haemophilus influenzae type B (Hib) and beta-haemolytic streptococci","Influenza virus and RSV","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Haemophilus influenzae type B (Hib) and beta-haemolytic streptococci as causative agents of acute epiglottitis.  Why B wrong: Influenza/RSV are causes of laryngotracheobronchitis.  Why C wrong: M. tuberculosis causes TB meningitis, not epiglottitis here.  Why D wrong: Cryptococcus is a fungal meningitis cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Acute epiglottitis is most commonly seen in which age group and season?","options":["3 to 5 years of age, during winter seasons","6 months to 1 year, during summer","Adolescence, during spring","Newborn period, year-round"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute epiglottitis is most commonly seen in the 3-to-5-year age group and during winter seasons.  Why B wrong: 6 months to 1 year in summer is not the stated group/season.  Why C wrong: Adolescence in spring is not stated.  Why D wrong: The newborn period year-round is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which are the listed assessment findings of acute epiglottitis?","options":["High-grade fever","Drooling","Dysphagia","Odynophagia","Dysphonia and aphonia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): The source lists high-grade fever, drooling, dysphagia, odynophagia, dysphonia, and aphonia as assessment findings of acute epiglottitis.  Why none excluded: All the listed findings appear in the epiglottitis assessment."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Spasmodic laryngitis is best described as which type of condition?","options":["An allergic condition with inflammation and sudden muscle spasm of the larynx","A bacterial infection of the epiglottis","A viral infection of the entire lower airway","A congenital chromosomal disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states spasmodic laryngitis is an allergic condition in which inflammation and sudden muscle spasm of the larynx occurs.  Why B wrong: A bacterial epiglottis infection describes acute epiglottitis.  Why C wrong: Viral lower-airway infection describes laryngotracheobronchitis.  Why D wrong: It is not a congenital chromosomal disorder."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which are the listed assessment findings of spasmodic laryngitis?","options":["Stridor sound","Dysphonia","Respiratory distress","Aphonia","Purulent secretions with high fever"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists stridor sound, dysphonia, respiratory distress, and aphonia as spasmodic laryngitis findings.  Why E wrong: Purulent secretions with high fever are features of bacterial tracheitis, not spasmodic laryngitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Bacterial tracheitis is defined as:","options":["Inflammation of the trachea due to bacterial infection","Inflammation of the epiglottis due to allergy","Viral inflammation of the bronchioles","Inflammation of the meninges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines bacterial tracheitis as inflammation of the trachea due to bacterial infection.  Why B wrong: Epiglottis allergy is not the definition.  Why C wrong: Bronchiole inflammation is bronchitis/bronchiolitis.  Why D wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which organisms are listed as causative agents of bacterial tracheitis?","options":["Haemophilus influenzae type B","Beta-haemolytic streptococci","Pseudomonas","RSV (respiratory syncytial virus)","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The source lists Haemophilus influenzae type B, beta-haemolytic streptococci, and pseudomonas (etc.) as causative agents of bacterial tracheitis.  Why D wrong: RSV is a causative agent of laryngotracheobronchitis, not bacterial tracheitis.  Why E wrong: Cryptococcus is a fungal meningitis cause."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which are the listed clinical features of bacterial tracheitis?","options":["Fever","Dyspnoea and respiratory distress","Cyanosis","Croupy cough with purulent secretions","Sclera visible above the iris"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists fever, dyspnoea, respiratory distress, cyanosis, croupy cough, and purulent secretions as clinical features of bacterial tracheitis.  Why E wrong: Sclera visible above the iris is the \"setting sun sign\" of increased ICP, unrelated to bacterial tracheitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which condition is described as the MOST COMMON type of croup syndrome?","options":["Laryngo-tracheo-bronchitis (LTB)","Acute epiglottitis","Spasmodic laryngitis","Bacterial tracheitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states laryngo-tracheo-bronchitis is the most common type of croup syndrome.  Why B wrong: Acute epiglottitis is not stated as the most common type.  Why C wrong: Spasmodic laryngitis is not the most common.  Why D wrong: Bacterial tracheitis is not the most common type."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Laryngo-tracheo-bronchitis (LTB) occurs mostly due to which type of infection?","options":["Viral infections (and sometimes bacterial)","Only bacterial infection","Only fungal infection","Allergic reaction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states LTB occurs mostly from viral infections and sometimes bacterial infection also.  Why B wrong: It is primarily viral, not solely bacterial.  Why C wrong: Fungal-only is not stated.  Why D wrong: Allergic-only describes spasmodic laryngitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which are the listed causative agents of laryngo-tracheo-bronchitis (LTB)?","options":["Influenza and RSV (respiratory syncytial virus)","Haemophilus influenzae type B and pseudomonas","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Streptococcus pneumoniae and Neisseria meningitidis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists influenza and RSV (respiratory syncytial virus) as causative agents of LTB.  Why B wrong: Hib/pseudomonas are causes of bacterial tracheitis.  Why C wrong: M. tuberculosis is a TB meningitis cause.  Why D wrong: S. pneumoniae/N. meningitidis are meningitis organisms."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which are the listed clinical features of laryngo-tracheo-bronchitis (LTB)?","options":["High-grade fever","Nasal flaring and sternal retraction","Dyspnoea, respiratory distress, and cyanosis","Muffled voice, stridor, and aphonia","Petechial skin rash"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists high-grade fever, nasal flaring, sternal retraction, R. stridor, dyspnoea, respiratory distress, cyanosis, muffled voice, stridor, and aphonia as LTB clinical features.  Why E wrong: A petechial skin rash relates to meningococcal meningitis, not LTB."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(NORCET-5 Mains-2023) A 7-year-old child is brought to the ER at midnight with abrupt onset of temperature 104.5°F (40.3°C), difficulty swallowing, drooling, absence of a spontaneous cough, and agitation. These findings are indicative of which condition?","options":["Acute epiglottitis","Acute tracheitis","Acute spasmodic croup","Acute laryngotracheobronchitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The three cardinal features of epiglottitis are drooling, absence of spontaneous cough, and agitation, with rapid onset accompanied by high fever and dysphagia — matching this case (keyed answer C in source = \"Acute epiglottitis\"). Note: the source's option letters differ; the factually correct diagnosis is acute epiglottitis.  Why B wrong: Acute tracheitis features a 2–3 day URI history, croupy cough, stridor, and purulent secretions — not the abrupt cardinal triad.  Why C wrong: Spasmodic croup shows a URI history, croupy cough, stridor, low-grade fever, and slow progression (typically age 3 months–3 years).  Why D wrong: LTB has slow onset with URI history, low-grade fever, stridor, brassy cough, and irritability."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the source, which clinical manifestations characterize acute tracheitis?","options":["A 2–3 day history of URI, croupy cough, stridor, purulent secretions, and high fever","Abrupt drooling, absent cough, and agitation","Slow onset with brassy cough and low-grade fever","Wheezing only during expiration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states acute tracheitis includes a 2–3 day history of URI, croupy cough, stridor, purulent secretions, and high fever.  Why B wrong: Abrupt drooling/absent cough/agitation are the epiglottitis triad.  Why C wrong: Slow onset with brassy cough describes LTB.  Why D wrong: Expiratory wheezing is the cardinal sign of asthma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"According to the source, spasmodic croup most commonly affects which age group?","options":["3 months to 3 years","3 to 5 years","6 to 12 years","Newborn to 1 month"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the age group most affected by spasmodic croup is 3 months to 3 years.  Why B wrong: 3 to 5 years is the peak age for acute epiglottitis.  Why C wrong: 6 to 12 years is not the stated age group.  Why D wrong: The newborn period is not the stated group."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a child with croup syndrome?","options":["Monitor vital signs","Provide semi-Fowler position","Provide oxygen therapy","Provide cool mist tent nebulisation to prevent dryness of the respiratory tract","Keep the child completely flat and supine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists monitoring vital signs, semi-Fowler position, oxygen therapy, and cool mist tent nebulisation as croup nursing care.  Why E wrong: A completely flat supine position is not recommended; semi-Fowler is preferred."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"For a child with severe croup whose condition goes out of control, which medications may be administered?","options":["Epinephrine, or Heliox (a mixture of helium and oxygen) to reduce breathing efforts","Oral iron supplements","Rectal diazepam","Palivizumab vaccine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to administer epinephrine if the condition goes out of control, or administer Heliox (mixer of helium and oxygen), which reduces breathing efforts.  Why B wrong: Oral iron is not a croup treatment.  Why C wrong: Rectal diazepam is for febrile seizures.  Why D wrong: Palivizumab is a preventive for RSV, not croup rescue therapy."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is bronchitis defined in the source?","options":["Inflammation of the bronchioles causing production of thick mucus that occludes the bronchioles","Inflammation of the pulmonary alveoli only","Inflammation of the epiglottis","Inflammation of the meninges"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines bronchitis as inflammation of the bronchioles that causes production of thick mucus that occludes the bronchioles.  Why B wrong: Alveolar inflammation describes pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Epiglottis inflammation is epiglottitis.  Why D wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) is a common cause of which condition?","options":["Bronchiolitis","Meningitis","Epiglottitis","Cystic fibrosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RSV can cause an acute viral infection and is a common cause of bronchiolitis.  Why B wrong: RSV is not a cause of meningitis.  Why C wrong: Epiglottitis is bacterial (Hib), not typically RSV.  Why D wrong: Cystic fibrosis is a genetic disorder, not caused by RSV."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(BHU-2017) How is RSV transmitted?","options":["By direct contact with respiratory secretions (not airborne)","By airborne droplet nuclei only","By the fecal-oral route","By mosquito vectors"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RSV is not transmitted by airborne; it is transmitted by direct contact with respiratory secretions (BHU-2017).  Why B wrong: The source specifically states it is NOT airborne.  Why C wrong: Fecal-oral is not the stated route.  Why D wrong: Vector transmission is not the stated route."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"RSV mostly affects infants of what age, and in which seasons?","options":["6-month-old infants, in winter and spring","Newborns, in summer and autumn","2-year-olds, in monsoon","5-year-olds, year-round"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RSV mostly affects 6-month-old infants in winter and spring season.  Why B wrong: The stated age is 6 months and seasons are winter/spring, not summer/autumn.  Why C wrong: 2-year-olds in monsoon is not stated.  Why D wrong: 5-year-olds year-round is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which measures are listed for the PREVENTION of RSV?","options":["Improve breastfeeding","Frequent hand washing","Administer medications","Increase exposure to crowds","Discontinue all fluids"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The source lists improving breastfeeding, frequent hand washing, and administering medications as RSV prevention.  Why D wrong: Increasing crowd exposure would increase, not prevent, transmission.  Why E wrong: Discontinuing fluids is not a prevention measure."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(NORCET-5, Mains-2023) Which drugs are the drugs of choice to PREVENT RSV, and by which routes are they given?","options":["Palivizumab vaccine via IM route; Ribavirin drug via intranasal route","Palivizumab via oral route; Ribavirin via IV route","Ceftriaxone via IM route; Penicillin via oral route","Epinephrine via subcutaneous route; Heliox via inhalation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the DOC to prevent RSV are (1) Palivizumab vaccine via IM route and (2) Ribavirin drug via intranasal route (NORCET-5, Mains-2023).  Why B wrong: The routes are wrong; Palivizumab is IM and Ribavirin is intranasal.  Why C wrong: Ceftriaxone/penicillin are meningitis drugs.  Why D wrong: Epinephrine/Heliox are croup rescue therapies, not RSV prevention."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of RSV?","options":["Rhinorrhoea, pharyngitis, coughing, sneezing, wheezing","Fever and eye & ear drainage","Signs of air hunger, tachypnoea, retractions, and cyanosis","Drooling with absent cough","Nuchal rigidity"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The source lists rhinorrhoea/pharyngitis/coughing/sneezing/wheezing, fever with eye and ear drainage, and signs of air hunger/tachypnoea/retractions/cyanosis as RSV symptoms.  Why D wrong: Drooling with absent cough is the epiglottitis triad.  Why E wrong: Nuchal rigidity is a meningitis sign."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In the later stage of worsening RSV, the tachypnoea reaches what rate, along with decreased breath sounds and episodes of apnoea?","options":["Tachypnoea > 70 breaths/minute","Tachypnoea > 30 breaths/minute","Tachypnoea > 40 breaths/minute","Tachypnoea > 100 breaths/minute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that in the later stage while condition worsens, tachypnoea is > 70 b/m, with decreased breath sounds, poor air exchange, listlessness, and episodes of apnoea.  Why B wrong: >30 is not the stated worsening rate.  Why C wrong: >40 is not the stated rate.  Why D wrong: >100 is not the stated rate."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"When hospitalizing a child with RSV, what is the appropriate isolation/room arrangement?","options":["Isolate the child, or place the child in a room with another child who also has RSV","Place the child in a room with any respiratory patient","Place the child in the general open ward","No isolation is needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to isolate the child in the hospital or place the child in a room with another child with RSV (cohorting).  Why B wrong: Rooming with any respiratory patient risks cross-infection; cohorting is with RSV-positive children only.  Why C wrong: An open general ward does not provide required isolation.  Why D wrong: The source requires isolation/cohorting."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In RSV nursing care, nurses caring for an RSV-positive child are instructed to:","options":["Follow contact and standard precautions and not care for another child at the same time","Care for RSV and non-RSV children interchangeably","Avoid hand washing to save time","Place the child in Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to follow contact and standard precautions and ensure that nurses caring for an RSV-positive child do not care for another child.  Why B wrong: Interchangeable care risks transmission and is contraindicated.  Why C wrong: Avoiding hand washing violates precautions.  Why D wrong: The source recommends semi-Fowler to Fowler with slight extension, not Trendelenburg."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What position should a child with RSV be placed in, according to nursing care?","options":["Semi-Fowler to Fowler with the neck slightly extended","Prone with the head flat","Left lateral Trendelenburg","Knee-chest position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states to place the child in semi-Fowler to Fowler with slightly extended (neck).  Why B wrong: Prone with head flat is not recommended.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not indicated.  Why D wrong: Knee-chest is not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) According to the source, asthma is a chronic inflammatory disorder of the airway that includes which of the following components?","options":["Spasm of the bronchi and bronchioles","Oedema of the mucus membrane","Increased secretions","Respiratory acidosis from accumulation of carbon dioxide","Alveolar consolidation with pus"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C, D): The source lists spasm of bronchi/bronchioles, oedema of mucus membrane, increased secretions, and respiratory acidosis from CO2 accumulation as components of asthma.  Why E wrong: Alveolar consolidation with pus describes pneumonia/empyema, not asthma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is \"status asthmaticus\" defined, and how is it categorized clinically?","options":["A continuing acute asthma attack lasting more than 30 minutes with respiratory distress; a medical emergency that must be treated immediately or it will be fatal","A mild wheeze that resolves without treatment","A chronic stable phase of asthma","An allergic skin reaction only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states status asthmaticus is a continuing acute asthma attack of more than 30 minutes with respiratory distress; it is a medical emergency requiring immediate treatment, otherwise the condition will be fatal.  Why B wrong: A self-resolving mild wheeze is not status asthmaticus.  Why C wrong: A chronic stable phase is not an acute emergency.  Why D wrong: An allergic skin reaction is not status asthmaticus."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Therapeutic measures for asthma include refraining from allergens and using short-acting/rescue bronchodilators through which delivery devices?","options":["Metered Dose Inhaler (MDI) or nebulizer","Intramuscular injection only","Rectal suppository","Transdermal patch"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states therapeutic measures include refraining from allergens and using short-acting or rescue bronchodilators through a Metered Dose Inhaler (MDI) or nebulizer.  Why B wrong: IM injection is not the stated delivery method.  Why C wrong: Rectal suppository is not stated.  Why D wrong: A transdermal patch is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is the diagnosis of asthma made according to the source?","options":["On the basis of the child's symptoms","By throat swab culture","By lumbar puncture","By sweat chloride test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states diagnosis is done on the basis of the child's symptoms.  Why B wrong: Throat swab culture diagnoses croup.  Why C wrong: Lumbar puncture diagnoses meningitis.  Why D wrong: Sweat chloride test diagnoses cystic fibrosis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of asthma?","options":["Wheezing during expiration","Wheezing during inspiration only","Absent breath sounds","Barking (brassy) cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies dyspnoea with wheezing during expiration as the cardinal sign of asthma.  Why B wrong: The cardinal wheeze is expiratory, per the source.  Why C wrong: Absent breath sounds is not the stated cardinal sign.  Why D wrong: A brassy/barking cough is associated with croup (LTB), not asthma."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In asthma, bronchoconstriction, bronchospasm, and obstruction occur due to which mediator, released by which cells?","options":["Histamine released by mast cells","Insulin released by beta cells","Cortisol released by the adrenal cortex","Surfactant released by type II pneumocytes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bronchoconstriction, bronchospasm, and obstruction are due to histamine which is released by mast cells.  Why B wrong: Insulin from beta cells is unrelated to asthma bronchospasm.  Why C wrong: Cortisol is not the stated mediator of asthmatic bronchospasm.  Why D wrong: Surfactant is not the stated mediator."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) Which are the listed PRIORITY nursing interventions for a child with status asthmaticus?","options":["Assess airway patency and respiratory status","Administer humidified oxygen by nasal cannula or face mask","Administer quick-relief or short-acting bronchodilators","Initiate an IV line","Prepare the child for a chest radiograph and obtain a blood sample for ABG analysis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct (all): The source lists assessing airway patency/respiratory status, administering humidified oxygen, administering quick-relief/short-acting bronchodilators, initiating an IV line, preparing for a chest radiograph, and obtaining a blood sample for ABG analysis.  Why none excluded: All listed actions are stated priority interventions for status asthmaticus."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"How is pneumonia defined?","options":["Inflammation or consolidation of the pulmonary parenchyma (functional unit of lungs) or alveoli or both, caused by virus, bacteria, and aspiration of foreign substances","Inflammation of the bronchioles only","Inflammation of the meninges","A chromosome 7 exocrine defect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines pneumonia as inflammation or consolidation of the pulmonary parenchyma or alveoli or both, caused by virus, bacteria, and aspiration of foreign substances.  Why B wrong: Bronchiole-only inflammation is bronchitis/bronchiolitis.  Why C wrong: Meningeal inflammation is meningitis.  Why D wrong: A chromosome 7 exocrine defect is cystic fibrosis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In pneumonia terminology, \"empyema\" refers to:","options":["Pus collection in the space between the lungs or pleural cavity","Fluid or water collection in the pleural cavity","Gathering of lung tissue filled with liquid","Air trapped in the pleural cavity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines empyema as pus collection in the space between the lungs or pleural cavity.  Why B wrong: Fluid/water collection describes pleural effusion.  Why C wrong: Lung tissue filled with liquid describes consolidation.  Why D wrong: Air trapped in the pleural cavity is pneumothorax, not empyema."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"In pneumonia terminology, \"pleural effusion\" refers to:","options":["Fluid or water collection in the space between the lungs or pleural cavity","Pus collection in the pleural cavity","Hardening of lung tissue","Inflammation of the trachea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines pleural effusion as fluid or water collection in the space between the lungs or pleural cavity.  Why B wrong: Pus collection describes empyema.  Why C wrong: Hardening of lung tissue is part of consolidation/induration.  Why D wrong: Tracheal inflammation is bacterial tracheitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"\"Consolidation,\" described as lung tissue filled with liquid instead of air and marked by induration (swelling & hardening), is described as the cardinal sign of pneumonia in which context?","options":["It is a radiologic cardinal sign of pneumonia","It is a laboratory cardinal sign of pneumonia","It is an auscultatory cardinal sign of pneumonia","It is a genetic marker of pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states consolidation is compressible/gathering lung tissue filled with liquid instead of air, marked by induration, and it is a radiologic cardinal sign of pneumonia.  Why B wrong: It is described as radiologic, not laboratory.  Why C wrong: It is described as radiologic, not auscultatory.  Why D wrong: It is not described as a genetic marker."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) By which of the following is the diagnosis of pneumonia made?","options":["Chest X-ray","Blood culture","Other blood tests","Sweat chloride test","Lumbar puncture"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The source states diagnosis is made by chest X-ray, blood culture, and other blood tests.  Why D wrong: Sweat chloride test diagnoses cystic fibrosis.  Why E wrong: Lumbar puncture diagnoses meningitis."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which test is considered the gold standard / confirmative diagnosis of pneumonia, despite not identifying the causative organism?","options":["Chest X-ray","Blood culture","Sputum Gram stain","Throat swab culture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chest X-ray is considered the gold standard or confirmative diagnosis of pneumonia, but this test does not identify the pathogenic causative organism.  Why B wrong: Blood culture helps identify organisms but is not stated as the gold-standard/confirmative diagnostic here.  Why C wrong: Sputum Gram stain is not the stated gold standard.  Why D wrong: Throat swab culture is the croup diagnostic."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which is the most common type of pneumonia?","options":["Viral pneumonia","Bacterial pneumonia","Fungal pneumonia","Aspiration pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states viral pneumonia is the most common type of pneumonia.  Why B wrong: Bacterial pneumonia is more severe but not stated as most common.  Why C wrong: Fungal pneumonia is not stated as most common.  Why D wrong: Aspiration pneumonia is not stated as most common."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Primary atypical pneumonia is usually caused by which organisms, and typically presents with what cough?","options":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae or Chlamydia pneumoniae; a dry \"hacking\" cough (mostly in fall or winter)","Streptococcus pneumoniae; a productive purulent cough","RSV; a brassy cough","Mycobacterium tuberculosis; a bloody cough"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states primary atypical pneumonia is usually caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae or Chlamydia pneumoniae, occurs mostly in fall or winter, and a dry 'hacking' cough is usually seen.  Why B wrong: S. pneumoniae with productive cough describes typical bacterial pneumonia, not atypical.  Why C wrong: RSV/brassy cough is not the atypical pneumonia description.  Why D wrong: TB with bloody cough is not the stated atypical pneumonia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Which type of pneumonia is described as the more severe type that requires hospitalization?","options":["Bacterial pneumonia","Viral pneumonia","Primary atypical pneumonia","Interstitial pneumonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bacterial pneumonia is a more severe type of pneumonia that requires hospitalization.  Why B wrong: Viral pneumonia is the most common but not described as the more severe type requiring hospitalization.  Why C wrong: Primary atypical pneumonia is not described as the more severe hospitalization-requiring type.  Why D wrong: Interstitial pneumonia is an anatomical class, not the stated severe type."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"\"Community-acquired pneumonia\" is defined as pneumonia caused by:","options":["An infectious agent contracted outside the hospital","An agent acquired after 48 hours of hospital admission","Endotracheal intubation","Immunosuppression only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines community-acquired pneumonia as pneumonia caused by an infectious agent contracted outside the hospital.  Why B wrong: An agent acquired after 48 hours of admission is hospital-acquired pneumonia.  Why C wrong: Endotracheal intubation relates to ventilator-associated pneumonia.  Why D wrong: Immunosuppression relates to opportunistic pneumonia."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"\"Hospital-acquired pneumonia\" is defined as pneumonia that occurs after how long from hospital admission?","options":["After 48 hours of hospital admission","After 12 hours of hospital admission","After 72 hours of hospital admission","Within the first hour of admission"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hospital-acquired pneumonia occurs after 48 hours of hospital admission.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not the stated cutoff.  Why C wrong: 72 hours is the reference used for VAP, not hospital-acquired pneumonia.  Why D wrong: Within the first hour is not the stated definition."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"\"Opportunistic pneumonia\" occurs in which patient population?","options":["Immunocompromised patients","Only healthy young adults","Only in newborns","Only in the elderly with normal immunity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states opportunistic pneumonia occurs in immunocompromised patients.  Why B wrong: Healthy young adults with intact immunity are not the stated population.  Why C wrong: It is not specified as newborn-only.  Why D wrong: It is defined by immunocompromise, not normal-immunity elderly."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"Ventilator-Associated Pneumonia (VAP) refers to pneumonia that occurs more than how long after endotracheal intubation?","options":["More than 48–72 hours after endotracheal intubation","More than 12 hours after intubation","More than 7 days after intubation","Immediately at the time of intubation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states VAP refers to pneumonia occurring more than 48–72 hours after endotracheal intubation.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not the stated timeframe.  Why C wrong: 7 days is not the stated timeframe.  Why D wrong: VAP is not defined as occurring immediately at intubation."},{"section":"Batch 011 · SIDS risk factors, Cystic Fibrosis, Head Injury & Increased ICP…","question":"(Select all that apply) The anatomical classification of pneumonia includes which of the following?","options":["Bronchopneumonia","Lobar pneumonia","Interstitial pneumonia","Meningeal pneumonia","Pleural pneumonia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct (A, B, C): The source states anatomical classification includes bronchopneumonia, lobar pneumonia, and interstitial pneumonia.  Why D wrong: \"Meningeal pneumonia\" is not a listed anatomical class (meninges are not lung tissue).  Why E wrong: \"Pleural pneumonia\" is not one of the three listed anatomical classes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which stage of pneumonia occurs during the first 24 hours and is characterized by vascular dilation, alveolar exudates (intra-alveolar fluid) containing mostly bacteria?","options":["Stage 1 – Congestion","Stage 2 – Red hepatisation","Stage 3 – Grey hepatization","Stage 4 – Resolution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 1 (Congestion) occurs in the first 24 hours with vascular dilation and alveolar exudates containing mostly bacteria; the lungs may look red from increased blood flow and swelling.  Why B wrong: Red hepatisation is stage 2, occurring at 48–72 hours.  Why C wrong: Grey hepatization is stage 3, occurring at 4–6 days.  Why D wrong: Resolution is stage 4, occurring after >6 days."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In stage 1 (congestion) of pneumonia, why are few white blood cells available in the lungs despite many bacteria being present?","options":["Many bacteria are present but few WBCs have yet arrived to fight the infection","WBCs have completely digested all the bacteria","The lung has already reached full recovery","Macrophages have removed all the exudate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states many bacteria are present in the lungs but few white blood cells are available to fight the infection during congestion.  Why B wrong: Bacteria are not yet digested in this earliest stage.  Why C wrong: Recovery is the resolution stage (>6 days), not congestion.  Why D wrong: Macrophage clearance of exudate occurs in later stages."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The stage of pneumonia occurring at 48–72 hours (2–3 days) in which alveolar exudates contain neutrophils, erythrocytes and fibrin, and the affected lung becomes dry, granular, airless and resembles liver is called:","options":["Red hepatisation (consolidation)","Congestion","Grey hepatization","Resolution"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 2, red hepatisation/consolidation (48–72 hours), features exudates of neutrophils, erythrocytes and fibrin, and the lung resembles liver.  Why B wrong: Congestion is stage 1 (first 24 hours).  Why C wrong: Grey hepatization is stage 3 (4–6 days).  Why D wrong: Resolution is stage 4 (>6 days)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"During red hepatisation of pneumonia, the airspaces filled by exudate lead to a gross appearance described as:","options":["Solidification/consolidation of the alveolar parenchyma","Enzymatic digestion of exudates","Vascular dilation with intra-alveolar fluid","Fragmentation of red blood cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In red hepatisation, airspaces filled by exudate lead to a gross appearance of solidification/consolidation of the alveolar parenchyma.  Why B wrong: Enzymatic digestion is the resolution stage.  Why C wrong: Vascular dilation with intra-alveolar fluid is congestion (stage 1).  Why D wrong: Fragmented RBCs characterize grey hepatization (stage 3)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In which stage of pneumonia (4–6 days) do fragmented RBCs, alveolar exudates with neutrophils and fibrin appear, with the lung looking grey or yellow but still with the consistency of liver, and death typically occurring in severe cases?","options":["Grey hepatization (Stage 3)","Congestion (Stage 1)","Red hepatisation (Stage 2)","Resolution (Stage 4)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 3 grey hepatization (4–6 days) has fragmented RBCs, exudates with neutrophils and fibrin, a grey/yellow lung with liver consistency, and death typically in severe cases.  Why B wrong: Congestion is the first 24 hours.  Why C wrong: Red hepatisation is 48–72 hours.  Why D wrong: Resolution is >6 days and is the recovery stage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"During grey hepatization of pneumonia, which type of large white blood cell starts to form as fibrin, hemosiderin and red blood cells break down into a more fluid-like exudate?","options":["Macrophages","Neutrophils","Eosinophils","Basophils"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states macrophages, a type of large white blood cell, start to form during grey hepatization.  Why B wrong: Neutrophils are present but are not described as the large WBC forming here.  Why C/D wrong: Eosinophils and basophils are not mentioned in this stage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The final recovery stage of pneumonia (>6 days), characterized by enzymatic digestion of exudates and return of airways and air sacs to normal function, is called:","options":["Resolution (Stage 4)","Congestion (Stage 1)","Red hepatisation (Stage 2)","Grey hepatization (Stage 3)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 4 resolution (>6 days) is the final recovery stage with enzymatic digestion of exudates; airways and alveoli return to normal function.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are earlier stages (congestion, red hepatisation, grey hepatization) that precede recovery."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the resolution stage of pneumonia, the exudate is cleared by which two mechanisms?","options":["Enzymatic activity/macrophages and the cough mechanism","Antibiotics and dialysis","Vascular dilation and fibrin deposition","Neutrophil migration and eschar formation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The exudate is digested by enzymatic activity and cleared by macrophages or by the cough mechanism.  Why B wrong: Dialysis is unrelated to alveolar clearance.  Why C wrong: Vascular dilation and fibrin deposition are features of earlier stages.  Why D wrong: Eschar formation is a burn concept, not pneumonia resolution."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the pneumonia nursing care points, what type of oxygen should be administered if respiratory distress is present?","options":["Cool oxygen","Heated humidified oxygen","Hyperbaric oxygen","Dry oxygen only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states: assess vital signs and administer cool oxygen if respiratory distress is present.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies cool oxygen, not heated, hyperbaric, or dry-only oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"For infants with pneumonia who have secretions, which device is recommended for suctioning?","options":["Bulb syringe","Yankauer suction","Closed inline catheter","Nasogastric tube"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states suctioning from bulb syringes in infants if secretion is there.  Why B/C/D wrong: These devices are not the ones specified in the source for infant secretions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"How frequently should chest physiotherapy and postural drainage be performed for a child with pneumonia?","options":["Every 4 hours","Every 2 hours","Every 6 hours","Every 8 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page specifies chest physiotherapy and postural drainage every 4 hours.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states every 4 hours, not 2, 6, or 8 hours."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A child has unilateral pneumonia. To split the chest and reduce the discomfort caused by pleural rubbing, the nurse should encourage the child to lie in which position? (AIIMS, PGI, JIPMER, ESIC, GMCH)","options":["On the affected side","On the unaffected side","Prone position","High-Fowler position only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to encourage the child to lie on the affected side (if pneumonia is unilateral) to split the chest and reduce discomfort caused by pleural rubbing.  Why B wrong: Lying on the unaffected side is not what the source recommends for splinting.  Why C/D wrong: Prone or high-Fowler positions are not specified for this purpose."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"When should antibiotic therapy be started in a child with pneumonia?","options":["As soon as possible","Only after culture results return","After 48 hours of observation","Only if fever persists beyond 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states antibiotic therapy should be started as soon as possible.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source calls for prompt initiation, not delay for culture, observation, or prolonged fever."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Tuberculosis is a contagious disease caused by which organism, described as an acid-fast bacillus?","options":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Group-A beta-haemolytic streptococcus","Haemophilus influenzae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: TB is a contagious disease caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, an acid-fast bacillus.  Why B wrong: S. pneumoniae causes pneumonia, not TB.  Why C wrong: Group-A beta-haemolytic strep is linked to glomerulonephritis.  Why D wrong: H. influenzae is not the cause of TB."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"What is the mode of transmission of tuberculosis from an active case? (FAQs)","options":["Droplet/air","Fecal–oral route","Blood transfusion only","Contaminated water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mode of transmission is droplet/air from an active case of TB.  Why B/C/D wrong: TB is not primarily transmitted by fecal–oral route, blood transfusion, or contaminated water per the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which set represents the cardinal signs and symptoms of tuberculosis? (AIIMS, CHO)","options":["Mild fever, cough, weight loss, anorexia and lymphadenopathy","High-grade fever, seizures, and jaundice","Periorbital oedema and smoky brown urine","Blister formation and eschar"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cardinal signs and symptoms of TB are mild fever, cough, weight loss, anorexia and lymphadenopathy.  Why B wrong: Seizures/jaundice are not the cardinal TB features listed.  Why C wrong: Periorbital oedema and smoky urine describe glomerulonephritis.  Why D wrong: Blister/eschar describe burns."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which test is described as the screening test for tuberculosis? (FAQs)","options":["Mantoux test","Sputum culture","Chest X-ray","Polymerase chain reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The screening test of tuberculosis is the Mantoux test.  Why B wrong: Sputum culture is the confirmatory diagnosis, not screening.  Why C wrong: Chest X-ray is supportive/screening but the page names Mantoux as the screening test.  Why D wrong: PCR is a diagnostic method but not named as the screening test here."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which investigation is the confirmatory diagnosis of tuberculosis? (AIIMS, JIPMER, GMCH, DSSSB, PGI)","options":["Sputum culture","Mantoux test","Chest X-ray","Complete blood count"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sputum culture is the confirmative diagnosis of tuberculosis.  Why B wrong: Mantoux is a screening test.  Why C wrong: Chest X-ray is supportive/screening, not confirmatory.  Why D wrong: CBC is nonspecific and not confirmatory for TB."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"When should a sputum specimen for TB culture be obtained?","options":["In the early morning before breakfast","After the evening meal","At midnight","Immediately after exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The culture sputum specimen should be obtained in the early morning before breakfast.  Why B/C/D wrong: These timings are not specified by the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Regarding chest X-ray in tuberculosis, which statement is correct?","options":["It is a supportive or screening diagnosis, not a confirmative diagnosis of TB","It is the confirmatory diagnosis of TB","It replaces the need for sputum culture","It is used only after DOTS is completed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chest X-ray is supportive or screening diagnosis; it is not the confirmative diagnosis of TB.  Why B wrong: Sputum culture, not X-ray, is confirmatory.  Why C wrong: X-ray does not replace sputum culture.  Why D wrong: The source does not restrict X-ray to post-DOTS use."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A ______ months DOTS course may be prescribed to prevent a latent TB infection.","options":["9 months","6 months","12 months","3 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 9-month DOTS course may be prescribed to prevent a latent infection.  Why B/D wrong: The source specifies 9 months, not 6 or 3 months, for latent infection.  Why C wrong: 12 months is for a child co-infected with AIDS/HIV."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A ______ months DOTS course can be prescribed for a child infective with AIDS/HIV positive.","options":["12 months","9 months","6 months","18 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 12-month DOTS course can be prescribed for a child infective with AIDS/HIV positive.  Why B wrong: 9 months is for latent infection prevention.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies 12 months for HIV-positive children, not 6 or 18."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis is also known by which eponymous name?","options":["Koch's bacillus","Döderlein's bacillus","Ducrey's bacillus","Hansen's bacillus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: M. tuberculosis is also known as Koch's bacillus.  Why B/C/D wrong: These names refer to other organisms and are not used for M. tuberculosis in the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the page, M. tuberculosis is a highly aerobic bacterium requiring high oxygen levels; for this reason it mostly affects which part of the lungs?","options":["Upper lobe of the lungs","Lower lobe of the lungs","Right middle lobe only","Pleural cavity only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Being highly aerobic and requiring high oxygen, M. tuberculosis mostly affects the upper lobe of the lungs where oxygen levels are usually high.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the upper lobe, not the lower lobe, middle lobe, or pleural cavity."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Who first discovered Mycobacterium tuberculosis and in what year?","options":["Robert Koch in 1882","Louis Pasteur in 1861","Alexander Fleming in 1928","Wallace in 1882"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states M. tuberculosis was first discovered in 1882 by Robert Koch.  Why B/C wrong: Pasteur and Fleming are associated with other discoveries.  Why D wrong: Wallace is associated with the rule of nine for burns, not TB."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which acid-fast stain is commonly used to identify Mycobacterium tuberculosis?","options":["Ziehl-Neelsen stain","Gram stain","Giemsa stain","India ink"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An acid-fast stain such as Ziehl-Neelsen is commonly used to identify M. tuberculosis.  Why B wrong: Gram stain is not the acid-fast method named here.  Why C wrong: Giemsa is used for blood parasites, not routine acid-fast staining.  Why D wrong: India ink is used for cryptococcus, not M. tuberculosis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Select ALL the most frequently used diagnostic methods for tuberculosis mentioned on the page. (Select all that apply)","options":["Tuberculin skin test","Acid-fast stain","Culture","Polymerase chain reaction","Lumbar puncture"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The most frequently used diagnostic methods for TB are the tuberculin skin test, acid-fast stain, culture, and polymerase chain reaction.  Why E wrong: Lumbar puncture is not listed among the frequently used TB diagnostic methods."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by increased permeability of the glomerular basement membrane to which plasma component?","options":["Plasma albumin","Red blood cells","Platelets","White blood cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nephrotic syndrome involves increased permeability of the glomerular basement membrane to plasma albumin.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies albumin (protein) leakage, not cellular components."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In nephrotic syndrome, leakage of albumin through the glomerular membrane leads directly to which change in serum albumin?","options":["Decreased serum albumin level","Increased serum albumin level","No change in serum albumin","Increased serum sodium only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerular membranes become permeable to protein (especially albumin), which leaks through, so serum albumin level is decreased.  Why B/C wrong: Serum albumin falls; it does not rise or stay unchanged.  Why D wrong: The primary change described is decreased albumin, not sodium."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the pathophysiology of nephrotic syndrome, decreased capillary oncotic pressure and increased hydrostatic pressure result in which condition?","options":["Hypovolemia","Hypertension crisis","Polycythemia","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Capillary oncotic pressure decreases and hydrostatic pressure increases, so a hypovolemia condition occurs.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source describes hypovolemia, not a hypertensive crisis, polycythemia, or hyperkalemia, as the direct result."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In nephrotic syndrome, low serum protein triggers the liver to synthesize proteins and lipids, leading to which condition?","options":["Hyperlipidaemia","Hypoglycemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypochloremia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Due to low serum protein, the liver starts synthesis of proteins and lipids, so hyperlipidaemia occurs.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source links low serum protein to hyperlipidaemia, not to glucose, calcium, or chloride changes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the nephrotic syndrome pathophysiology flow chart, generalized edema occurs because fluid moves from the vascular space to which compartment?","options":["Extracellular fluid","Intracellular fluid","Cerebrospinal fluid","Synovial fluid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart states generalized edema results from fluid moving from the vascular space to the extracellular fluid.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies extracellular fluid, not intracellular, CSF, or synovial fluid."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the nephrotic syndrome flow chart, generalized edema activates which system, leading to sodium retention and further edema?","options":["Renin-angiotensin system","Sympathetic nervous system only","Complement system","Coagulation cascade"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flow chart shows generalized edema → activation of renin-angiotensin system → sodium retention → edema.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the renin-angiotensin system in this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are cardinal signs and symptoms of nephrotic syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Massive proteinuria","Hypoalbuminemia (hypoproteinaemia)","Hyperlipidaemia","Hypovolemia and oedema (periorbital oedema)","Smoky brown coloured urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal signs of nephrotic syndrome are massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia/hypoproteinaemia, hyperlipidaemia, and hypovolemia & oedema (puffiness of face, periorbital oedema).  Why E wrong: Smoky brown urine is a sign of glomerulonephritis, not nephrotic syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Severe generalized oedema seen in nephrotic syndrome, especially on arising, is termed:","options":["Anasarca","Lymphedema","Myxedema","Angioedema"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes generalised oedema (anasarca), ascites and scrotal oedema in males, seen on arising.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different edema types not used by the source for nephrotic syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"What is the typical blood pressure finding in a child with nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Average/normal or slightly decreased","Severely elevated in all cases","Consistently very high","Undetectable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states blood pressure is average/normal or slightly decreased in nephrotic syndrome.  Why B/C wrong: Marked hypertension is characteristic of glomerulonephritis, not nephrotic syndrome per the source.  Why D wrong: BP is not undetectable."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which additional findings are listed for a child with nephrotic syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Weight gain","Oliguria","Lethargy","Anorexia and pallor","Polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists child weight gain, oliguria, lethargy, anorexia and pallor.  Why E wrong: Oliguria (decreased urine), not polyuria, is the listed finding."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In which position should a child with nephrotic syndrome be placed for comfort?","options":["Semi-Fowler to Fowler","Prone","Trendelenburg","Left lateral only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to place the child in semi-Fowler to Fowler for comfort.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies semi-Fowler to Fowler, not prone, Trendelenburg, or left lateral."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are listed as the major goals of therapy in nephrotic syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reduce the excretion of urinary protein","Maintain or restore serum protein level","Reduce oedema","Prevent infection and minimise complications","Increase urinary protein loss"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The four listed major goals are to reduce urinary protein excretion, maintain/restore serum protein, reduce oedema, and prevent infection/minimise complications.  Why E wrong: The goal is to reduce, not increase, urinary protein loss."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"For monitoring a child with nephrotic syndrome, urine should be specifically checked for which parameters?","options":["Specific gravity and protein","Ketones and glucose","Bilirubin and urobilinogen","Nitrites only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to monitor urine for specific gravity and protein.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies specific gravity and protein, not ketones/glucose, bilirubin, or nitrites."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice for steroid therapy in nephrotic syndrome?","options":["Prednisone","Cyclophosphamide","Furosemide","Albumin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To give steroids as per order, prednisone is the drug of choice.  Why B wrong: Cyclophosphamide is the immunosuppressant used when steroids are ineffective.  Why C wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic, not a steroid.  Why D wrong: Albumin is a plasma protein replacement, not a steroid."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"If steroid therapy is not effective in nephrotic syndrome, which immunosuppressant is the drug of choice?","options":["Cyclophosphamide","Prednisone","Methotrexate","Azathioprine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If steroid therapy is not effective, immunosuppressant therapy is started, and cyclophosphamide is the drug of choice.  Why B wrong: Prednisone is the initial steroid, not the immunosuppressant used for steroid failure.  Why C/D wrong: The source names cyclophosphamide, not methotrexate or azathioprine."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In nephrotic syndrome, when is a sodium-restricted diet specifically indicated?","options":["During periods of massive proteinuria","Only after full recovery","Only in the absence of oedema","When serum protein is normal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A sodium-restricted diet is used during periods of massive proteinuria.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source ties sodium restriction to periods of massive proteinuria, not to recovery, absence of oedema, or normal protein."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In nephrotic syndrome, protein restriction in the diet is indicated when:","options":["The condition is worsened (out of control) or renal failure/Azotaemia develops","Proteinuria has resolved","The child is hypoalbuminemic and stable","Oedema is absent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Protein restriction is used if the condition is worsened (out of control) or renal failure or azotaemia develops.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source ties protein restriction to worsening/renal failure/azotaemia, not to resolution, stable hypoalbuminemia, or absence of oedema."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the dietary considerations for nephrotic syndrome, what type of diet is offered when indicated?","options":["Regular diet","Ketogenic diet","High-sodium diet","Clear liquid diet only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to offer a regular diet when indicated.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies a regular diet, not ketogenic, high-sodium, or clear-liquid-only diets."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Glomerulonephritis is a group of kidney disorders characterized by what type of injury in the glomerulus?","options":["Inflammatory injury","Ischemic injury only","Traumatic injury only","Toxic drug injury only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerulonephritis is a group of kidney disorders characterized by inflammatory injury in the glomerulus.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source describes inflammatory (immunological/auto-immune) injury, not primarily ischemic, traumatic, or drug-related."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Glomerulonephritis is usually caused by which type of reactions?","options":["Immunological or auto-immune reactions","Allergic contact reactions","Mechanical obstruction","Congenital malformation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glomerulonephritis is usually caused by immunological or auto-immune reactions.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies immunological/auto-immune reactions, not allergy, obstruction, or congenital cause."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A common cause of glomerulonephritis is a past history of infection with which organism of the pharynx or skin?","options":["Group-A beta-haemolytic streptococcus","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Escherichia coli"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A cause is past infection with group-A beta-haemolytic streptococcus of the pharynx or skin.  Why B wrong: M. tuberculosis causes TB, not post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis.  Why C/D wrong: These organisms are not named as the streptococcal cause of glomerulonephritis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the page, glomerulonephritis often follows a history of pharyngitis or laryngitis by what interval?","options":["Before 2 to 3 weeks","Before 2 to 3 days","Before 6 months","Before 1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is often a history of pharyngitis or laryngitis before 2 to 3 weeks.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies 2 to 3 weeks, not days, months, or a year."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In glomerulonephritis, hypertensive encephalopathy occurs due to nitrogenous waste products such as ammonia that cross which barrier to reach the brain?","options":["Blood-brain barrier (BBB)","Glomerular basement membrane","Placental barrier","Alveolar-capillary membrane"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypertensive encephalopathy is due to nitrogenous waste products such as ammonia which can cross the BBB and reach the brain.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the blood-brain barrier, not these other membranes."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are listed complications of glomerulonephritis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Kidney failure","Hypertensive encephalopathy","Pulmonary oedema and heart failure","Hyperlipidaemia as the primary feature"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Complications listed are kidney failure (loss of glomerulus/GFR function), hypertensive encephalopathy, and pulmonary oedema/heart failure (fluid and waste accumulation from loss of renal function).  Why D wrong: Hyperlipidaemia is a cardinal feature of nephrotic syndrome, not a complication of glomerulonephritis here."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In glomerulonephritis, periorbital or facial oedema is described as more prominent at what time of day?","options":["In the morning","At night","After exercise","After meals"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Periorbital or facial oedema is more prominent in the morning.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the morning."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The urine in a child with glomerulonephritis is classically described as:","options":["Decreased in output and cloudy, smoky brown coloured","Clear and increased in volume","Bright red and frothy","Pale and dilute"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page describes decreased urine output and cloudy, smoky brown coloured urine.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies decreased output with smoky brown urine, not clear/increased, bright red frothy, or pale dilute urine."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In an older child with glomerulonephritis, which symptoms may occur? (Select all that apply)","options":["Headache","Abdominal or flank pain","Dysuria","Hypertension and proteinuria","Polyuria with clear urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Older children may show headache, abdominal/flank pain, dysuria, plus hypertension and proteinuria.  Why E wrong: The source describes decreased output and smoky urine, not polyuria with clear urine."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Azotaemia in glomerulonephritis refers to increased levels of which substances in the blood?","options":["Blood urea, nitrogen and creatinine","Glucose and ketones","Albumin and lipids","Bilirubin and bile salts"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Azotaemia is defined as increased blood urea, nitrogen and creatinine levels in the blood.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies urea/nitrogen/creatinine, not glucose, albumin/lipids, or bilirubin."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In glomerulonephritis, how often should urine output be monitored per the nursing care instructions?","options":["Every hour","Every 4 hours","Every 8 hours","Once per shift"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states to monitor vital signs and strictly the I/O chart, with urine output every hour.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies hourly urine output monitoring."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The best method of assessing fluid status in a child with glomerulonephritis is to weigh the baby: (AIIMS, PGI, ESIC)","options":["Daily at the same time, on the same scale, in the same clothing","Weekly at any convenient time","Only when oedema is visible","After each diuretic dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Daily weighing at the same time, same scale, and same clothing is one of the best methods of assessing fluid status.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source emphasizes daily, standardized weighing, not weekly, oedema-triggered, or post-diuretic weighing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Why should seizure precautions be initiated in a child with glomerulonephritis?","options":["Oedema puts pressure on brain tissues, so the child may be at risk of seizures that occur suddenly","Diuretics always cause seizures","Sodium restriction causes hypernatremic seizures","Steroids directly induce seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Seizure precautions are initiated because oedema puts pressure on brain tissues, so the child may be at risk of seizures that occur suddenly.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source attributes seizure risk to cerebral oedema/pressure, not to diuretics, sodium restriction, or steroids."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A fracture is defined as a breakdown in the continuity of the bone resulting from which causes? (Select all that apply)","options":["Trauma","Twisting","Bone decalcification","Bacterial infection of alveoli"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A fracture is a breakdown in bone continuity from trauma, twisting, or bone decalcification.  Why D wrong: Alveolar infection relates to pneumonia, not fractures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs and symptoms of a fracture? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain or tenderness","Oedema and ecchymosis","Muscle spasm and loss of function","Crepitation","Smoky brown urine"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Fracture signs include pain/tenderness, oedema, ecchymosis, muscle spasm, loss of function, and crepitation.  Why E wrong: Smoky brown urine is a glomerulonephritis sign, not a fracture sign."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which type of fracture is the most common type seen in infants and older children? (FAQ's, AIIMS, PGI, NHM, DSSSB)","options":["Greenstick fracture","Spiral fracture","Comminuted fracture","Open fracture"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Greenstick fracture is the most common type seen in infants and older children.  Why B/C/D wrong: Spiral, comminuted (printed \"communicated\"), and open are other listed types but not the most common in children."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The page lists \"open, communicated, liner and spiral\" fractures as other types. What are the factually correct spellings of \"communicated\" and \"liner\" fractures?","options":["Comminuted and linear","Compound and lunar","Commuted and lateral","Compressed and lined"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source's \"communicated\" is a misprint for comminuted, and \"liner\" is a misprint for linear fractures.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the standard fracture terminology (comminuted = many fragments; linear = crack along bone axis)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the \"5 Ps\" assessment tool for fracture, which sign is specifically noted as a LATE sign?","options":["Pulselessness distal to the fracture site","Pain and tenderness","Pallor","Paresthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulselessness distal to the fracture site is specifically noted as a late sign.  Why B/C/D wrong: Pain, pallor, and paresthesia are among the 5 Ps but are not the ones flagged as late signs."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the 5 Ps assessment for fracture, \"paralysis\" is defined as:","options":["Lack of movement distal to the fracture site","Loss of sensation only","Absence of pulse","Pale skin colour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paralysis is defined as lack of movement distal to the fracture site.  Why B wrong: Loss of sensation relates to paresthesia.  Why C wrong: Absence of pulse is pulselessness.  Why D wrong: Pale skin is pallor."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"List ALL five components of the 5 Ps fracture assessment tool. (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain and tenderness","Pulselessness distal to fracture site","Pallor","Paresthesia distal to fracture site","Paralysis distal to fracture site"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The five Ps are pain and tenderness, pulselessness (late sign), pallor, paresthesia, and paralysis, all assessed distal to the fracture site.  Why any omission wrong: All five options are part of the tool per the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"If a compound (open) fracture exists, what is the appropriate immediate nursing action?","options":["Cover the wound with a sterile dressing","Apply direct heat to the wound","Push the exposed bone back in","Leave the wound open to air"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If a compound fracture exists, cover the wound with a sterile dressing.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unsafe/incorrect; the source specifies a sterile dressing."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A child arrives with an injured extremity from a fracture. Based on the nursing care list, which combination of local measures is recommended?","options":["Elevate the injured extremity and apply ice to the injured area","Lower the extremity and apply heat","Massage the fracture site vigorously","Apply a tourniquet routinely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists elevating the injured extremity and applying ice to the injured area.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the source (which specifies elevation and ice)."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which type of traction is commonly used for stabilization of femur, hip, and acetabular fractures in children?","options":["Buck's traction","Cervical traction","Russell traction","Halo traction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Buck's traction is commonly used for stabilization of femur, hip and acetabular fractures in children.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source names Buck's traction specifically for these fractures."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is best described as:","options":["Abnormal development of the hip joint where the femoral head does not remain permanently in the acetabulum cavity","Inflammation of the glomeruli","A congenital abnormality of the foot","A fracture of the femur"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DDH is an abnormal development of the hip joint; the joint is unstable and the femoral head does not remain permanently in the acetabulum cavity.  Why B wrong: That describes glomerulonephritis.  Why C wrong: A foot abnormality describes club foot.  Why D wrong: A femoral fracture is a different condition."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which type of DDH is the most common, in which the femoral head is partially displaced?","options":["Subluxation type","Complete dislocation type","Fused type","Rotational type"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Subluxation type of DDH is the most common type, in which the femoral head is partially displaced.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source names subluxation (partial displacement) as most common."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The Allis sign in DDH, which shows shortening of the affected extremity when the infant is supine, is also known by which other name? (FAQs, PGI, AIIMS DELHI-2019,20)","options":["Galeazzi's sign","Ortolani sign","Barlow's sign","Klisic sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Allis sign is also known as the Allis or Galeazzi's sign.  Why B/C/D wrong: Ortolani, Barlow, and Klisic are separate DDH signs."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which findings are associated with the Allis sign of DDH? (Select all that apply)","options":["Shortening of affected extremities","Restriction of the limb on the affected side in supine position","Unequal gluteal folds when the infant is supine","Usually seen in newborns and infants","Only seen in adults with varicose veins"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Allis sign features shortening of the affected extremity, restriction of the limb in supine position, unequal gluteal folds when supine, and is usually seen in newborns and infants.  Why E wrong: Varicose veins relate to the Trendelenburg test note, not the Allis sign."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Ortolani sign for DDH, a 'clicking' sensation is heard during abduction. This clicking indicates: (SJH-2018)","options":["A dislocated femoral head moving into the acetabulum","The femoral head moving out of the acetabulum","A fracture of the femoral neck","Normal hip development"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In Ortolani sign, a clicking sensation during abduction indicates a dislocated femoral head moving into the acetabulum.  Why B wrong: The femoral head moving OUT of the acetabulum is the Barlow's sign.  Why C/D wrong: The click is a reduction sign, not a fracture or normal finding."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"During the Ortolani maneuver, the lower extremity is abducted and pressure is applied toward which structure so the femoral head moves inside the acetabulum?","options":["Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter","Iliac crest","Patella"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Ortolani sign, abduction is done and pressure is applied toward the greater trochanter, so the femoral head moves inside the acetabulum.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the greater trochanter."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Barlow's sign for DDH involves adduction of the lower extremity with pressure applied in which direction, causing the femoral head to move out of the acetabulum?","options":["Downward and backward","Upward and forward","Laterally only","Medially only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Barlow's sign involves adduction of the lower extremity with pressure applied downward and backward, so the femoral head moves out of the acetabulum cavity.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies downward and backward."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"With which other DDH maneuver is Barlow's sign usually performed together, and in which age group is it usually seen?","options":["Ortolani sign; in newborns and infants","Trendelenburg sign; in older children","Klisic sign; in bilateral DDH","Allis sign; in adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Barlow's sign is usually seen in newborns and infants and is performed together with the Ortolani sign.  Why B/C/D wrong: Trendelenburg (older children), Klisic (bilateral DDH), and Allis are separate signs not paired with Barlow per the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The Asymmetrical Gluteal Fold sign of DDH (abnormal walking) is usually seen in which age group?","options":["Older children","Newborns only","Neonates in the first 24 hours","Only preterm infants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Asymmetrical Gluteal Fold sign (abnormal walking) is usually seen in older children.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source ties this sign to older children, not neonates or preterm infants."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Trendelenburg sign for DDH, when the child stands vertically on one affected extremity, the level of the hip of the elevated extremity is:","options":["Lower than the other hip","Higher than the other hip","Equal to the other hip","Completely dislocated upward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Trendelenburg sign, standing on one affected extremity causes the hip of the elevated extremity to be lower than the other hip.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source states it is lower, not higher, equal, or dislocated upward."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the note on the page, the Trendelenburg TEST (as opposed to the Trendelenburg sign for DDH) is seen in which condition?","options":["Varicose veins","Nephrotic syndrome","Club foot","Burn injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states the Trendelenburg test is seen in varicose veins.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifically links the Trendelenburg test to varicose veins."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The Klisic sign is usually seen in which form of DDH?","options":["Bilateral DDH","Unilateral DDH only","Subluxation only","Fractured hip"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Klisic sign is usually seen in bilateral DDH.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source ties Klisic sign to bilateral DDH."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"To elicit the Klisic sign, the examiner places the index finger on the anterior superior iliac spine and the middle finger on the greater trochanter. In a NORMAL hip, the imaginary line between these two points should point:","options":["Toward or above the umbilicus","Below the umbilicus","Toward the knee","Toward the opposite hip"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the Klisic sign, the imaginary line should point toward or above the umbilicus normally.  Why B wrong: The line passes below the umbilicus if the hip is bilaterally dislocated.  Why C/D wrong: The source references the umbilicus, not the knee or opposite hip."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Klisic sign, if the hip is bilaterally dislocated, the imaginary line will pass:","options":["Below the umbilicus","Above the umbilicus","Through the xiphoid process","Through the pubic symphysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The line will pass below the umbilicus if the hip is bilaterally dislocated.  Why B wrong: Toward/above the umbilicus is the normal finding.  Why C/D wrong: The source references the umbilicus as the landmark."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The Pavlik harness used in DDH maintains the lower extremities in which position?","options":["Abduction, slight flexion, and external rotation","Adduction, extension, and internal rotation","Full extension with the legs together","Hyperflexion with adduction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Pavlik harness maintains the lower extremities in abduction, slight flexion, and external rotation.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies abduction/slight flexion/external rotation."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"For how long is the Pavlik harness worn continuously in DDH?","options":["3 to 6 months","1 to 2 weeks","12 weeks","20 to 23 hours per day for life"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Pavlik harness is worn continuously for 3 to 6 months.  Why B wrong: 1–2 weeks is the bebax shoe size-change interval (club foot).  Why C wrong: 12 weeks is the hip spica cast duration after open reduction.  Why D wrong: 20–23 hours/day refers to the Milwaukee brace for scoliosis."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which of the following are assistive devices used for DDH? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pavlik harness","Von Rosen's splint","Craig splint","Milwaukee brace","Dennis Browne bar"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Devices used for DDH are the Pavlik harness, Von Rosen's splint, and Craig splint.  Why D wrong: The Milwaukee brace is used for scoliosis/spinal deformities.  Why E wrong: The Dennis Browne bar is used for club foot."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"After open reduction surgery for DDH, a hip spica cast is applied for how long to immobilize the hip joint?","options":["12 weeks","3 to 6 months","1 to 2 weeks","6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: After open reduction surgery, the hip spica cast is applied for 12 weeks to immobilize the hip joint.  Why B wrong: 3–6 months is the Pavlik harness duration.  Why C wrong: 1–2 weeks is the bebax shoe size-change interval.  Why D wrong: The source specifies 12 weeks, not 6."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Surgical management in DDH is generally required for which patients?","options":["Older infants, toddlers, and children","All newborns immediately at birth","Only preterm neonates","Only adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Surgical management is required for older infants or toddlers and children.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies older infants/toddlers/children, not all newborns, preterm neonates, or adults."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A cast for immobilization of a fractured part is made from which material?","options":["Plaster of Paris (POP)","Fiberglass resin only","Silicone","Thermoplastic foam"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cast is made by plaster of Paris (POP) (printed \"plaster of peris\").  Why B/C/D wrong: The source names plaster of Paris; the printed \"peris\" is a misspelling of Paris."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"To prevent skin breakdown under a cast, what should be applied under the margin of the cast?","options":["A cotton pad","Petroleum jelly","A tight elastic band","Adhesive tape directly on skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To prevent skin breakdown, a cotton pad should be applied under the margin of the cast.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies a cotton pad."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"To help a cast dry, which measures are appropriate, and what should be avoided? (Select all that apply)","options":["Expose the cast to air","Open the window and may use a fan","Should NOT use a dryer","Wrap the cast tightly in plastic"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: For drying, the cast should be exposed to air, the window opened and a fan may be used; a dryer should not be used.  Why D wrong: Wrapping tightly in plastic is not recommended and would prevent drying."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A nurse handling a newly applied WET cast should use which technique? (AIIMS, PGI-2020)","options":["Handle the wet cast smoothly with the palm of the hand","Use the fingertips to grip the cast firmly","Use only one finger to move the cast","Handle with the fingertips of the affected extremity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A wet cast should be smoothly handled by the palm of the hand; do not handle the wet cast using the fingertips of the affected extremity.  Why B/C/D wrong: Using fingertips can create pressure indentations; the source specifies palms of the hands."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which procedure can be performed if circulatory impairment occurs under a cast?","options":["Fasciotomy","Escharotomy","Debridement","Skin grafting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A fasciotomy can be done if circulatory impairment occurs.  Why B wrong: Escharotomy is for circumferential burns.  Why C/D wrong: Debridement and grafting are burn-wound procedures, not the response to cast-related circulatory impairment."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which signs indicate a compromised neurovascular status of an extremity in a cast that should be reported to the HCP? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pain and oedema","Pallor, numbness and tingling","Coolness and decreased sensitivity/mobility","Diminished pulse","Warm pink fingers with normal movement"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Neurovascular compromise signs include pain, oedema, pallor, numbness/tingling, coolness, decreased sensitivity or mobility, and diminished pulse; if noted, notify the HCP.  Why E wrong: Warm pink fingers with normal movement is a normal finding, not a warning sign."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Club foot is also known by which medical term?","options":["Talipes equinovarus (TEV)","Talipes calcaneovalgus","Coxa vara","Genu valgum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clubfoot is also known as talipes equinovarus (TEV), in which the foot points downward and inward.  Why B wrong: Talipes calcaneovalgus is a different variant (dorsiflexion + outward).  Why C/D wrong: Coxa vara and genu valgum are other skeletal deformities, not club foot."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In club foot (talipes equinovarus), the foot characteristically points:","options":["Downward and inward","Upward and outward","Downward and outward","Upward and inward"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In clubfoot, the foot points downward and inward.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies downward and inward."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which is the most common device used for management of club foot? (NORCET-2021)","options":["Bebax shoes and Dennis Browne Bar (DBB)/Dennis Browne splint","Pavlik harness","Milwaukee brace","Hip spica cast"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common device used for club foot management is Bebax shoes and the Dennis Browne Bar (DBB) or Dennis Browne splint.  Why B wrong: The Pavlik harness is for DDH.  Why C wrong: The Milwaukee brace is for scoliosis.  Why D wrong: The hip spica cast is used after DDH open reduction."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"How often should the size of the bebax shoes be changed in club foot management?","options":["Every 1–2 weeks","Every 3–6 months","Every 12 weeks","Every 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The size of the bebax shoes should be changed every 1–2 weeks.  Why B wrong: 3–6 months is the Pavlik harness duration.  Why C wrong: 12 weeks is the hip spica cast duration.  Why D wrong: The source specifies every 1–2 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which type of talipes is defined by plantar flexion (toes walking) with the toes lower than the heel?","options":["Talipes equinus","Talipes calcaneus","Talipes valgus","Talipes varus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes equinus is plantar flexion (toes walking) with toes lower than the heel.  Why B wrong: Talipes calcaneus is dorsiflexion (heel walking) with toes higher than the heel.  Why C wrong: Talipes valgus is outward movement/eversion.  Why D wrong: Talipes varus is inward movement/inversion."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Talipes calcaneus is defined as:","options":["Dorsiflexion (heel walking) with toes higher than the heel","Plantar flexion with toes lower than the heel","Inward movement of the foot (inversion)","Outward movement of the foot (eversion)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes calcaneus is dorsiflexion or heel walking, with toes higher than the heel.  Why B wrong: Plantar flexion with lower toes is talipes equinus.  Why C wrong: Inversion is talipes varus.  Why D wrong: Eversion is talipes valgus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The page lists \"Talipes versus\" as inward movement of the foot with the lateral surface touching the ground (inversion). What is the factually correct term?","options":["Talipes varus","Talipes valgus","Talipes versus","Talipes vertus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The printed \"Talipes versus\" is a misspelling; the correct term for inward movement/inversion is talipes varus.  Why B wrong: Talipes valgus is outward movement/eversion.  Why C/D wrong: These are not standard terms; \"versus\"/\"vertus\" are misprints."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Talipes valgus is characterized by:","options":["Outward movement of the foot with the medial surface touching the ground (eversion)","Inward movement with the lateral surface touching the ground","Plantar flexion with toes lower than the heel","Dorsiflexion with toes higher than the heel"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes valgus is outward movement of the foot with the medial surface touching the ground (eversion).  Why B wrong: Inward movement/inversion is talipes varus.  Why C wrong: Plantar flexion is talipes equinus.  Why D wrong: Dorsiflexion is talipes calcaneus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which combination defines Talipes Equinovarus, described as the most common type? (OSSSC-2023)","options":["Plantar flexion + inward movement","Plantar flexion + outward movement","Dorsiflexion + inward movement","Dorsiflexion + outward movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes equinovarus is plantar flexion + inward movement and is the most common type.  Why B wrong: Plantar flexion + outward is talipes equinovalgus.  Why C wrong: Dorsiflexion + inward is talipes calcaneovarus.  Why D wrong: Dorsiflexion + outward is talipes calcaneovalgus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Match the combined talipes type to its definition: Talipes Equinovalgus is:","options":["Plantar flexion + outward movement","Plantar flexion + inward movement","Dorsiflexion + inward movement","Dorsiflexion + outward movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes equinovalgus is plantar flexion + outward movement.  Why B wrong: Plantar flexion + inward is talipes equinovarus.  Why C wrong: Dorsiflexion + inward is talipes calcaneovarus.  Why D wrong: Dorsiflexion + outward is talipes calcaneovalgus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Talipes Calcaneovarus is defined as which combination of movements?","options":["Dorsiflexion + inward movement","Dorsiflexion + outward movement","Plantar flexion + inward movement","Plantar flexion + outward movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes calcaneovarus is dorsiflexion + inward movement.  Why B wrong: Dorsiflexion + outward is talipes calcaneovalgus.  Why C wrong: Plantar flexion + inward is talipes equinovarus.  Why D wrong: Plantar flexion + outward is talipes equinovalgus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Talipes Calcaneovalgus is defined as:","options":["Dorsiflexion + outward movement","Dorsiflexion + inward movement","Plantar flexion + outward movement","Plantar flexion + inward movement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Talipes calcaneovalgus is dorsiflexion + outward movement.  Why B wrong: Dorsiflexion + inward is talipes calcaneovarus.  Why C wrong: Plantar flexion + outward is talipes equinovalgus.  Why D wrong: Plantar flexion + inward is talipes equinovarus."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Lateral deviation of the spine is called:","options":["Scoliosis","Lordosis","Kyphosis","Spondylosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scoliosis is lateral deviation of the spine.  Why B wrong: Lordosis is inward curvature of the lumbar spine.  Why C wrong: Kyphosis is outward curvature of the thoracic spine.  Why D wrong: Spondylosis is not the term for lateral deviation here."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Inward curvature of the lumbar spine, also known as swayback or flatback deformity and commonly seen in pregnancy and obesity, is called: (AIIMS, ESIC, RRB)","options":["Lordosis","Kyphosis","Scoliosis","Ankylosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lordosis is the inward curvature of the lumbar spine, also known as swayback or flatback deformity, commonly seen in pregnancy and obesity.  Why B wrong: Kyphosis is outward curvature of the thoracic spine.  Why C wrong: Scoliosis is lateral deviation.  Why D wrong: Ankylosis is joint fusion, not a spinal curvature."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Outward curvature of the thoracic spine, also known as humpback, roundback, or Dowager's hump disease, is called: (AIIMS, CHO, RUHS)","options":["Kyphosis","Lordosis","Scoliosis","Osteomalacia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kyphosis is the outward curvature of the thoracic spine, also known as humpback/roundback or Dowager's hump disease.  Why B wrong: Lordosis is inward lumbar curvature (swayback).  Why C wrong: Scoliosis is lateral deviation.  Why D wrong: Osteomalacia is bone softening, not a curvature."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In spinal column deformities, the assessment finding of an asymmetrical thorax cavity is known as:","options":["Adam's sign","Ortolani sign","Klisic sign","Galeazzi's sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The assessment finding of asymmetrical thorax cavity is known as \"Adam's sign.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: Ortolani, Klisic, and Galeazzi's signs relate to DDH, not spinal deformity assessment."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"The most common assistive device used to prevent further progression of lateral spinal deviation, worn at least 20 to 23 hours a day, is the:","options":["Milwaukee brace","Pavlik harness","Dennis Browne bar","Craig splint"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Milwaukee brace is the most common device used to prevent progression of lateral deviation, worn at least 20 to 23 hours a day.  Why B wrong: Pavlik harness is for DDH.  Why C wrong: Dennis Browne bar is for club foot.  Why D wrong: Craig splint is for DDH."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the page, a burn injury is defined as damage of the skin and tissues due to:","options":["Heat","Immune reaction","Bacterial infection","Nutritional deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A burn injury is damage of the skin and tissues due to heat.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source defines burns as heat-related tissue damage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to cause, how many types of burn are there, and which are they? (Select all that apply for the types)","options":["Thermal","Chemical","Electrical","Radiation","Inhalation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: There are 5 types of burn according to cause: thermal, chemical, electrical, radiation, and inhalation burn.  Why any omission wrong: All five listed options are the recognized types per the source."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which is the most common type of burn, caused by hot liquids and steam (scald burns)? (ESIC-2016)","options":["Thermal burn","Chemical burn","Electrical burn","Radiation burn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thermal burn is the most common; scald burns are caused by hot liquids and steam.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source identifies thermal (scald) burns as most common."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A burn involving more than what percentage of the TBSA requires IV fluid therapy?","options":["More than 10% of the TBSA","More than 5% of the TBSA","More than 20% of the TBSA","More than 30% of the TBSA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a burn involving more than 10% of the TBSA requires IV fluids therapy.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies more than 10%."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the depth of the burn, which type involves ONLY the epidermis?","options":["Superficial burn","Partial burn","Deep partial burn","Full thickness burn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A superficial burn involves only the epidermis.  Why B wrong: Partial burn involves both epidermis and dermis.  Why C wrong: Deep partial burn involves whole epidermis + whole dermis.  Why D wrong: Full thickness burn involves epidermis + dermis + subcutaneous tissue + muscles + bone."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A superficial partial burn (by depth) involves which structures?","options":["Whole epidermis + some part of the dermis","Only the epidermis","Whole epidermis + whole dermis","Epidermis + dermis + muscle + bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A superficial partial burn involves the whole epidermis + some part of the dermis.  Why B wrong: Only the epidermis is a superficial burn.  Why C wrong: Whole epidermis + whole dermis is a deep partial burn.  Why D wrong: Epidermis + dermis + subcutaneous + muscle + bone is a full thickness burn."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"By depth, a deep partial burn involves:","options":["Whole epidermis + whole dermis","Whole epidermis + some part of the dermis","Only the epidermis","Epidermis + dermis + subcutaneous tissue + muscles + bone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A deep partial burn involves the whole epidermis + whole dermis.  Why B wrong: Whole epidermis + some dermis is a superficial partial burn.  Why C wrong: Only epidermis is a superficial burn.  Why D wrong: That describes a full thickness burn."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"By depth, a full thickness burn involves which structures? (Select all that apply)","options":["Epidermis","Dermis","Subcutaneous tissue","Muscles and bone","Only the superficial epidermis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: A full thickness burn involves epidermis + dermis + subcutaneous tissue + muscles + bone.  Why E wrong: Only superficial epidermis describes a superficial burn, not full thickness."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A 1st degree burn, in which the skin appears pink and red with severe pain, has which of the following characteristics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hospitalization is not required","Heals within 7 days","Skin appears pink and red","Severe pain is present","Nerve endings are damaged and pain is absent"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: A 1st degree burn has pink/red skin, severe pain present, no hospitalization required, and heals within 7 days.  Why E wrong: Absent pain from damaged nerve endings is a feature of 3rd and 4th degree burns, not 1st degree."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A 2nd degree burn (superficial partial burn) is characterized by blister formation that may rupture. Within how many days does it heal? (Delhi AIIMS-2018)","options":["Within 21 days","Within 7 days","Within 48 hours","Within 6 weeks"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A 2nd degree burn heals within 21 days; blister formation occurs and it may be ruptured.  Why B wrong: 7 days is the healing time for a 1st degree burn.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies 21 days."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Regarding hospitalization in a 2nd degree burn, the page states:","options":["Hospitalization may or may not be required","Hospitalization is never required","Hospitalization is always required","The patient must be discharged within 7 days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a 2nd degree burn, hospitalization may be or may not be required.  Why B wrong: \"Never required\" applies more to 1st degree (no hospitalization).  Why C/D wrong: The source states it may or may not be required."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In a 3rd degree burn (deep partial burn), why is pain absent?","options":["Nerve endings are damaged","Blood supply is increased","The skin is only pink and red","Only the epidermis is involved"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a 3rd degree burn, pain is absent because the nerve endings are damaged; tissue appears black and brown.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe superficial burns; the source attributes absent pain to nerve ending damage."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In a 3rd degree burn, eschar formation produces complications in circumferential burns, and which procedure is performed at the bedside without anaesthesia?","options":["Escharotomy","Fasciotomy","Skin grafting","Debridement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 3rd degree burns, escharotomy is performed at the bedside without any anaesthesia (nerve endings are damaged, so no pain).  Why B wrong: Fasciotomy is done for circulatory impairment under a cast per this book.  Why C/D wrong: Grafting and debridement are separate management steps, not the bedside no-anaesthesia procedure described."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In a 4th degree (full thickness) burn, the tissue appears which colours?","options":["Black, brown and white","Pink and red","Yellow and green","Blue and purple"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In a 4th degree full thickness burn, tissue appears black, brown and white; pain is absent as nerve endings are damaged.  Why B wrong: Pink/red is a 1st/2nd degree finding.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies black, brown and white."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"For which degrees of burn may a skin graft be required, according to the page?","options":["3rd and 4th degree burn","1st and 2nd degree burn","1st degree only","2nd degree only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The page states a skin graft may be required for 3rd and 4th degree burns; tissue debridement may also be required in 4th degree.  Why B/C/D wrong: Skin grafting is associated with 3rd and 4th degree burns, not 1st/2nd degree."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"By severity, a MILD burn is defined as:","options":["Less than 10% of TBSA burned, of first and second degree","10–20% of TBSA of 1st and 2nd degree","More than 20% TBSA of 1st and 2nd degree","More than 10% TBSA of 3rd and 4th degree"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A mild burn is less than 10% of total body surface area burned, of first and second degree.  Why B wrong: 10–20% is a moderate burn.  Why C/D wrong: These thresholds describe a severe burn."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"By severity, a MODERATE burn is defined by which criteria? (Select all that apply)","options":["10–20% of TBSA burn of 1st and 2nd degree","2–5% TBSA of 3rd and 4th degree burn","Less than 10% TBSA of 1st and 2nd degree","More than 20% TBSA of 1st and 2nd degree"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: A moderate burn is 10–20% TBSA of 1st and 2nd degree burn, and 2–5% TBSA of 3rd and 4th degree burn.  Why C wrong: <10% is a mild burn.  Why D wrong: >20% is a severe burn."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"By severity, a SEVERE burn includes which of the following criteria? (Select all that apply)","options":["More than 20% TBSA of first- and second-degree burn","More than 10% TBSA of 3rd and 4th degree","Burn of the face and genitalia (always considered severe)","Less than 10% TBSA of 1st and 2nd degree"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: A severe burn is >20% TBSA of first/second degree, >10% TBSA of 3rd/4th degree, and burns of the face and genitalia are always considered severe due to increased chance of complication.  Why D wrong: <10% TBSA of 1st/2nd degree is a mild burn."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which method for estimating TBSA burned was developed by Wallace and is used in children over 12 years of age and adults?","options":["Wallace rule of nine","Lund-Browder chart","Rule of fives","Parkland formula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Wallace rule of nine, developed by Wallace, is used for estimating TBSA in children >12 years of age and adults.  Why B wrong: The Lund-Browder chart is used for infants and children <12 years.  Why C wrong: The rule of fives is proposed for obese patients >80 kg but not currently used.  Why D wrong: The Parkland formula is not mentioned on this page."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which chart is used for estimation of TBSA in infants and children less than 12 years of age?","options":["Lund-Browder chart","Wallace rule of nine","Rule of fives","Rule of nine for adults"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Lund-Browder chart is used for TBSA estimation in infants and children <12 years.  Why B/D wrong: The rule of nine is used for those >12 years and adults.  Why C wrong: The rule of fives is proposed for obese patients, not routinely used."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"For obese patients weighing more than 80 kg, which rule was proposed for TBSA estimation (though currently NOT used)?","options":["Rule of fives","Rule of nine","Lund-Browder chart","Rule of tens"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For obese patients weighing more than 80 kg, a rule of fives is proposed but is currently not used.  Why B/C wrong: The rule of nine and Lund-Browder chart are used clinically for other groups.  Why D wrong: The source specifies a rule of fives, not tens."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"According to the Rule of Nine for burns, what percentage of TBSA is assigned to the head and neck?","options":["9%","18%","1%","4.5%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The head + neck is assigned 9% TBSA in the Rule of Nine.  Why B wrong: 18% applies to the anterior trunk, posterior trunk, and each lower limb.  Why C wrong: 1% is the genitalia.  Why D wrong: 4.5% is each anterior/posterior portion of an upper limb."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Rule of Nine, the anterior trunk is assigned 18%, which is broken down as:","options":["Chest 9% + abdomen 9%","Upper torso 9% + lower back 9%","Anterior limb 4.5% + posterior limb 4.5%","Head 9% + neck 9%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The anterior trunk is 18% (chest 9% + abdomen 9%).  Why B wrong: Upper torso/shoulder 9% + lower back 9% is the posterior trunk.  Why C wrong: 4.5% + 4.5% describes an upper limb.  Why D wrong: Head + neck totals 9%, not 18%."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Rule of Nine, the posterior trunk (18%) is divided into:","options":["Upper torso/shoulder 9% + lower back 9%","Chest 9% + abdomen 9%","Anterior limb 4.5% + posterior limb 4.5%","Genitalia 1% + head 9%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The posterior trunk is 18% (upper torso/shoulder 9% + lower back 9%).  Why B wrong: Chest 9% + abdomen 9% is the anterior trunk.  Why C wrong: 4.5% + 4.5% describes an upper limb.  Why D wrong: This mixes unrelated regions."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Rule of Nine, each upper limb is assigned what percentage of TBSA?","options":["9% (anterior limb 4.5% + posterior limb 4.5%)","18% (anterior 9% + posterior 9%)","1%","4.5% total"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Each upper limb (right or left) is 9% (anterior limb 4.5% + posterior limb 4.5%).  Why B wrong: 18% is assigned to each lower limb, not upper.  Why C wrong: 1% is the genitalia.  Why D wrong: 4.5% is one surface of the limb, not the whole upper limb."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Rule of Nine, each lower limb is assigned what percentage of TBSA?","options":["18% (anterior lower limb 9% + posterior lower limb 9%)","9% (anterior 4.5% + posterior 4.5%)","1%","36%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Each lower limb (left or right) is 18% (anterior lower limb 9% + posterior lower limb 9%).  Why B wrong: 9% is assigned to each upper limb.  Why C wrong: 1% is the genitalia.  Why D wrong: 36% would be both lower limbs combined, not one."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"In the Rule of Nine, what percentage of TBSA is assigned to the genitalia, and what is the total?","options":["Genitalia 1%; total 100%","Genitalia 9%; total 100%","Genitalia 1%; total 99%","Genitalia 4.5%; total 100%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The genitalia is assigned 1% and the total is 100%.  Why B/D wrong: Genitalia is 1%, not 9% or 4.5%.  Why C wrong: The total is 100%, not 99%."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"On the Lund-Browder chart, area A (half of the head) at 1 year of age is assigned what percentage of surface area?","options":["8.5","6.5","5.5","4.5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: On the Lund-Browder chart, area A at 1 year is 8.5.  Why B wrong: 6.5 is area A at 5 years.  Why C wrong: 5.5 is area A at 10 years.  Why D wrong: 4.5 is area A at 15 years."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"On the Lund-Browder chart, how does the surface area of area A (head) change with age from 1 to 15 years?","options":["It decreases: 8.5 (1 yr) → 6.5 (5 yr) → 5.5 (10 yr) → 4.5 (15 yr)","It increases: 4.5 → 5.5 → 6.5 → 8.5","It stays constant at 8.5","It increases then decreases"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Area A (head) decreases with age: 8.5 at 1 year, 6.5 at 5 years, 5.5 at 10 years, and 4.5 at 15 years, reflecting the proportionally larger head in infants.  Why B/C/D wrong: The values decrease progressively with age, as shown on the chart."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"On the Lund-Browder chart, what is the surface area value for area C at 15 years of age?","options":["3.25","2.5","2.75","3.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Area C at 15 years is 3.25 (values for C: 2.5 at 1 yr, 2.75 at 5 yr, 3.0 at 10 yr, 3.25 at 15 yr).  Why B wrong: 2.5 is area C at 1 year.  Why C wrong: 2.75 is area C at 5 years.  Why D wrong: 3.0 is area C at 10 years."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"On the Lund-Browder chart, the surface area value for area B is 3.25 at 1 year and rises to which value at both 10 and 15 years?","options":["4.5","4.0","3.25","5.5"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Area B is 3.25 at 1 year, 4.0 at 5 years, and 4.5 at both 10 and 15 years.  Why B wrong: 4.0 is area B at 5 years.  Why C wrong: 3.25 is area B at 1 year.  Why D wrong: 5.5 is not a listed area B value."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A 3-year-old is admitted with a scald burn from hot water covering an estimated 15% of the body surface. Based on the page, which TBSA estimation tool is most appropriate for this child?","options":["Lund-Browder chart (used for children < 12 years)","Wallace rule of nine (for >12 years and adults)","Rule of fives (for obese patients >80 kg)","No estimation tool is needed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a child under 12 years, the Lund-Browder chart is the appropriate TBSA estimation tool.  Why B wrong: The Wallace rule of nine is for children >12 years and adults.  Why C wrong: The rule of fives is proposed only for obese patients >80 kg and is not currently used.  Why D wrong: A burn of this size (>10% TBSA) needs estimation to guide IV fluid therapy."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A child with a burn has tissue that appears black and brown, and the child reports no pain at the burn site. Based on the burn degree classification, this most likely represents at least a:","options":["3rd degree burn (nerve endings damaged, pain absent)","1st degree burn","Superficial burn","Mild burn of the epidermis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Black/brown tissue with absent pain (damaged nerve endings) matches a 3rd degree burn per the source.  Why B/C/D wrong: 1st degree/superficial/mild burns have pink-red skin with severe pain present, not painless black/brown tissue."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A newborn is examined and shows unequal gluteal folds, a shortened affected extremity, and limb restriction in the supine position. Which sign of DDH do these findings represent?","options":["Allis (Galeazzi's) sign","Ortolani sign","Barlow's sign","Klisic sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shortening of the affected extremity, limb restriction in supine, and unequal gluteal folds when supine define the Allis (Galeazzi's) sign.  Why B wrong: Ortolani sign is a clicking during abduction as the head enters the acetabulum.  Why C wrong: Barlow's sign involves adduction with downward/backward pressure to dislocate the head.  Why D wrong: Klisic sign uses ASIS/greater trochanter landmarks in bilateral DDH."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A child with a fracture suddenly develops pulselessness distal to the fracture site along with pallor, coolness, and paresthesia. Which is the PRIORITY interpretation and action based on the 5 Ps and neurovascular monitoring?","options":["These indicate neurovascular compromise (pulselessness is a late sign); monitor neurovascular status and notify the HCP","These are expected findings requiring no action","Apply a dryer to warm the extremity","Encourage the child to walk on the extremity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pulselessness (a late sign), pallor, coolness, and paresthesia signal neurovascular compromise; the source directs monitoring neurovascular status and notifying the HCP.  Why B wrong: These are warning signs, not normal.  Why C wrong: A dryer is contraindicated (used only in the cast-drying context, where it is also prohibited).  Why D wrong: Weight bearing on a compromised extremity is unsafe."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"A child recovering from a streptococcal throat infection about 2 weeks ago now presents with periorbital oedema (worse in the morning), smoky brown urine, decreased urine output, and hypertension. Which condition is most consistent with these findings?","options":["Glomerulonephritis","Nephrotic syndrome","Tuberculosis","Club foot"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A history of group-A beta-haemolytic streptococcal infection 2–3 weeks prior, morning periorbital oedema, smoky brown urine, oliguria, and hypertension are classic for glomerulonephritis.  Why B wrong: Nephrotic syndrome features massive proteinuria, hypoalbuminemia, hyperlipidaemia, and normal/slightly decreased BP, not smoky urine and hypertension.  Why C wrong: TB presents with mild fever, cough, weight loss, and lymphadenopathy.  Why D wrong: Club foot is a foot deformity, unrelated to these renal findings."},{"section":"Batch 012 · Stages of pneumonia and nursing care…","question":"Which statement correctly contrasts blood pressure findings in nephrotic syndrome versus glomerulonephritis based on the pages?","options":["Nephrotic syndrome: BP average/normal or slightly decreased; Glomerulonephritis: hypertension","Both conditions always cause severe hypertension","Nephrotic syndrome causes hypertension; glomerulonephritis causes hypotension","Both conditions always cause hypotension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The pages state nephrotic syndrome BP is average/normal or slightly decreased, whereas glomerulonephritis features hypertension.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reverse or misstate the source findings."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which of the following is the primary cause of death in the first 24–48 hours after a burn injury?","options":["Septic shock","Hypovolemic shock","Compartment syndrome","Renal failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypovolemic shock is the primary cause of death in the first 24–48 hours after burn (AIIMS Delhi-2018).  Why A wrong: Septic shock/infection is the primary cause of death after the initial period (after 48 hours), not in the first 24–48 hours.  Why C wrong: Compartment syndrome is a complication managed by fasciotomy, not the leading early cause of death.  Why D wrong: Renal failure is a listed complication but not the primary early cause of death."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"After 48 hours following a burn injury, the primary cause of death shifts to:","options":["Hypovolemic shock","Septic shock or infection","Cardiac failure","Contractures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Septic shock or infection is the primary cause of death after the initial period or after 48 hours (ESIC-2019; GMCH-2022).  Why A wrong: Hypovolemic shock dominates in the first 24–48 hours, not after 48 hours.  Why C wrong: Cardiac failure is a general complication but not the identified post-48-hour cause of death.  Why D wrong: Contractures are a late complication, not a cause of death."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Complications of burn injury include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Shock","Infections","Contractures","Cardiac and renal failure","Compartment syndrome"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists complications of burn injury as shock, infections, contractures, cardiac and renal failure, and compartment syndrome — all five apply.  Why A/B/C/D/E wrong: None are wrong; each is an explicitly listed complication of burn injury."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Compartment syndrome occurring due to extensive burn injury is managed by:","options":["Fasciotomy","Escharotomy of the airway","Immediate amputation","Skin grafting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states compartment syndrome occurs due to extensive burn injury and is managed by fasciotomy.  Why B wrong: Airway escharotomy is not the stated management for compartment syndrome.  Why C wrong: Amputation is not the described treatment.  Why D wrong: Grafting addresses wound healing, not the acute compartment pressure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"During the initial or resuscitative phase of burn management, the highest priority is:","options":["Prevention of infection","Fluid management","Nutritional support","Physiotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fluid management is the highest priority in the initial or resuscitative phase.  Why A wrong: Prevention of infection is the priority during the active phase or after 48 hours, not initially.  Why C wrong: Nutrition is important but not the initial resuscitative priority.  Why D wrong: Physiotherapy is rehabilitative, not the acute priority."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The intravenous fluid of choice for burn injury resuscitation is:","options":["Normal saline","5% dextrose","Ringer lactate (RL solution)","Dextran"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IV fluid therapy uses Ringer lactate (RL solution) as the choice of fluid (NORCET-2022).  Why A wrong: Normal saline is not the identified choice of fluid for burns here.  Why B wrong: 5% dextrose is not the resuscitation fluid of choice.  Why D wrong: Dextran is a colloid not specified as the choice."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The amount of fluid required for a burn patient depends on:","options":["Age and gender only","Total Burn Surface Area (TBSA) and the client's weight","Blood pressure alone","Depth of burn only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The amount of fluids depends on Total Burn Surface Area (TBSA) and the client's weight.  Why A wrong: Age/gender alone are not the stated determinants.  Why C wrong: Blood pressure is a monitoring parameter, not the calculation basis.  Why D wrong: Depth alone does not determine fluid volume; TBSA and weight do."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"During the active phase or after 48 hours of a burn, the priority management goal is:","options":["Fluid resuscitation","Prevention of infection and promotion of wound healing","Pain control only","Early ambulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The priority management during the active phase or after 48 hours is prevention of infection and promotion of wound healing (AIIMS, JIPMER, JSSK, IGNOU).  Why A wrong: Fluid resuscitation is the priority in the initial/resuscitative phase, not the active phase.  Why C wrong: Pain control matters but is not the stated priority.  Why D wrong: Ambulation is not the identified priority in this phase."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The drug of choice used topically to prevent infection in burn injury is:","options":["Silver sulfadiazine","Permethrin","Neomycin","Acyclovir"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Silver sulfadiazine is the drug of choice for burn injury, used to prevent infection.  Why B wrong: Permethrin is the drug of choice for scabies, not burns.  Why C wrong: Neomycin is not identified as the burn drug of choice.  Why D wrong: Acyclovir is an antiviral, irrelevant to burn wound infection prophylaxis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The recommended diet for a burn patient to promote wound healing is:","options":["Low calorie, low protein, high fat","High calorie (5000 cal/day), high protein, moderate fat, high vitamin-C","High carbohydrate, low protein, no fat","Protein-restricted, high sodium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Recommended diet is high calorie (5000 cal/day), high protein, moderate fat, and high vitamin-C to promote wound healing.  Why A wrong: Low calorie/low protein opposes the healing demand of burns.  Why C wrong: Protein is needed for healing; a low-protein diet is wrong.  Why D wrong: Protein restriction is contraindicated in burns."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"According to the Davies formula, the protein requirement for a burn patient is calculated as:","options":["(3 gm/kg) + (1 gm/%TBSA)","(1 gm/kg) + (3 gm/%TBSA)","(60 Kcl/kg) + (35 Kcl/%TBSA)","4 ml/kg/%TBSA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Davies formula protein requirement = (3 gm/kg) + (1 gm/%TBSA).  Why B wrong: The multipliers are reversed and incorrect.  Why C wrong: That is the Davies calorie requirement, not protein.  Why D wrong: 4 ml/kg/%TBSA is the Parkland fluid formula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"According to the Davies formula, the calorie requirement for a burn patient is:","options":["(3 gm/kg) + (1 gm/%TBSA)","(60 Kcl/kg) + (35 Kcl/%TBSA)","(35 Kcl/kg) + (60 Kcl/%TBSA)","4 ml/kg/%TBSA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Davies formula calorie requirement = (60 Kcl/kg) + (35 Kcl/%TBSA).  Why A wrong: That is the protein component.  Why C wrong: The two multipliers are transposed.  Why D wrong: That is the Parkland fluid formula, not a calorie formula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A graft taken from another part of the patient's own body is called:","options":["Allograft","Autograft","Xenograft","Isograft"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Autograft is a graft taken from the patient's own body's another part.  Why A wrong: Allograft/homograft is taken from the same species (human to human).  Why C wrong: Xenograft/heterograft is taken from an animal species.  Why D wrong: Isograft/syngeneic graft is from monozygotic/identical twins."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A graft taken from an animal species and applied to a human (animal to human) is termed:","options":["Autograft","Allograft/Homograft","Xenograft/Heterograft","Isograft/Syngeneic graft"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Xenograft/heterograft is a graft taken from animal species (animal to human).  Why A wrong: Autograft is from the patient's own body.  Why B wrong: Allograft/homograft is human to human (same species).  Why D wrong: Isograft is from identical twins."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A graft obtained from a monozygotic (identical) twin is classified as a/an:","options":["Allograft/Homograft","Isograft/Syngeneic graft","Xenograft/Heterograft","Autograft"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Isograft/syngeneic graft is taken from monozygotic and identical twins.  Why A wrong: Allograft/homograft is from the same species generally (human to human), not specifically identical twins.  Why C wrong: Xenograft is from an animal species.  Why D wrong: Autograft is from one's own body."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A graft taken from the same species (human to human) is known as a/an:","options":["Autograft","Allograft/Homograft","Xenograft","Isograft"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Allograft/homograft is a graft taken from the same species (human to human).  Why A wrong: Autograft is from one's own body.  Why C wrong: Xenograft is from an animal.  Why D wrong: Isograft is specifically from identical twins."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The Parkland formula for fluid resuscitation in burns is also known as the:","options":["Baxter formula","Davies formula","Guthrie formula","Beutler formula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Parkland formula is also known as the Baxter formula, the most commonly used method for calculating fluids for burn patients.  Why B wrong: Davies formula is for protein and calorie requirements.  Why C wrong: Guthrie is a blood test for phenylketonuria.  Why D wrong: Beutler test diagnoses galactosemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The Parkland (Baxter) formula for burn fluid resuscitation is:","options":["2 ml/kg/%TBSA","4 ml/kg/%TBSA burn area","3 gm/kg/%TBSA","4 ml/kg only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Parkland formula is 4 ml/kg/%TBSA burn area.  Why A wrong: 2 ml/kg/%TBSA is not the stated formula.  Why C wrong: 3 gm/kg relates to Davies protein, not fluid.  Why D wrong: The %TBSA and weight factors are both required."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the Parkland formula, how is the calculated 24-hour fluid volume administered?","options":["All in the first 8 hours","Half in the first 8 hours and the remaining half over the next 16 hours","Half in the first 16 hours and half in the next 8 hours","Equally divided every 6 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Half of the fluid should be administered in the first 8 hours (to prevent hypovolemic shock) and the remaining half over the next 16 hours.  Why A wrong: Giving all in 8 hours risks fluid overload; the source splits it.  Why C wrong: The time split is reversed.  Why D wrong: The source specifies an 8-hour/16-hour split, not equal 6-hour divisions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Using the Parkland formula for a 60 kg patient with 20% TBSA burn, the total fluid required in 24 hours is:","options":["2400 ml","4800 ml","1200 ml","6000 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 4 × 60 × 20 = 4800 ml fluid, as worked in the source example.  Why A wrong: 2400 ml is half of the total (the first-8-hour portion), not the total.  Why C wrong: 1200 ml is incorrect arithmetic.  Why D wrong: 6000 ml does not match 4 × 60 × 20."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"For the 60 kg, 20% TBSA burn example (total 4800 ml), the infusion rate during the first 8 hours is:","options":["150 ml/hr","300 ml/hr","600 ml/hr","100 ml/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Half (2400 ml) is given in the first 8 hours → 2400/8 = 300 ml/hr.  Why A wrong: 150 ml/hr is the rate for the next 16 hours (2400/16).  Why C wrong: 600 ml/hr would deliver too much fluid.  Why D wrong: 100 ml/hr does not match 2400/8."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"For the same burn example, the infusion rate for the remaining 2400 ml over the next 16 hours is:","options":["300 ml/hr","150 ml/hr","200 ml/hr","75 ml/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 2400 ml over 16 hours = 2400/16 = 150 ml/hr.  Why A wrong: 300 ml/hr is the first-8-hour rate.  Why C wrong: 200 ml/hr does not match 2400/16.  Why D wrong: 75 ml/hr is incorrect arithmetic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"If the burning source is a thermal or flame fire, the first priority action of a rescuer is to:","options":["Switch off the electrical supply","Stop the burning process","Start IV fluids","Apply silver sulfadiazine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stopping the burning process is the first priority/action when the source is thermal or flame fire (AIIMS, JIPMER-2019, OSSSC-2023).  Why A wrong: Switching off the electrical supply is first only when the source is electrical.  Why C wrong: IV fluids come after immediate danger is stopped.  Why D wrong: Topical antimicrobials are later wound care, not first aid."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"When the burning source is electrical, the first priority action of the rescuer is to:","options":["Cover the patient with linen","Switch off the electrical supply","Wash with tap water","Stop the flame"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: If the source is electrical, switching off the electrical supply is the first priority action (WCL-2022).  Why A wrong: Covering with linen is a later step, not first for electrical burns.  Why C wrong: Washing with tap water is for chemical burns.  Why D wrong: Stopping the flame applies to thermal/flame fire."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"First aid for a chemical burn includes:","options":["Applying dry dressing immediately","Washing the chemical burn with tap water","Neutralizing with an acid or base","Covering with linen only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source instructs to wash the chemical burn with tap water if the victim is burned by a chemical.  Why A wrong: A dry dressing does not remove the offending chemical.  Why C wrong: Neutralizing agents are not advised; copious water is used.  Why D wrong: Covering alone does not decontaminate the chemical."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In a hospital or ward fire, the RACE formula is used to prioritize actions. What does RACE stand for, in order?","options":["Rescue, Alarm, Confine, Extinguish","Remove, Alert, Cover, Escape","React, Assess, Call, Evacuate","Rescue, Assess, Confine, Evacuate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: RACE = R Rescue the patient, A Alarm activate, C Confine the fire, E Extinguish the fire (AIIMS, SHJ, ESIC, PGI).  Why B wrong: These words do not match the source's RACE expansion.  Why C wrong: Incorrect expansion.  Why D wrong: \"Assess\" and \"Evacuate\" are not the source's A and E."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which additional first-aid measures are appropriate at the scene of a burn? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cover the patient with linen and lie the patient down on the floor","Remove the patient immediately from the danger sources","Maintain a patent airway","Wash a chemical burn with tap water","Leave the patient standing near the fire"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: A, B, C, and D are all listed first-aid measures for burns.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: These are correct measures, not distractors.  Why E wrong: Leaving the patient near the fire contradicts removing them from danger sources."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Scabies is caused by which organism?","options":["Staphylococcus aureus","Sarcoptes scabiei","Enterobius vermicularis","Candida albicans"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Scabies is caused by Sarcoptes scabiei (the \"itch mite\").  Why A wrong: Staphylococcus aureus causes impetigo.  Why C wrong: Enterobius vermicularis causes pinworm (enterobiasis).  Why D wrong: Candida albicans causes candidiasis/oral thrush."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Scabies is also commonly known as:","options":["Itch mite","Jock itch","Athlete's foot","Ringworm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scabies is also known as the itch mite.  Why B wrong: Jock itch is tinea cruris.  Why C wrong: Athlete's foot is tinea pedis.  Why D wrong: Ringworm is dermatophytosis (fungal)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most commonly affected age group for scabies is:","options":["Neonates","School going children","Elderly adults","Adolescents only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly affected age group for scabies is school going children.  Why A wrong: Neonates are not identified as the most commonly affected group.  Why C wrong: The elderly are not the stated group.  Why D wrong: Adolescents are not specified as the most commonly affected group."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Scabies is transmitted primarily by:","options":["Contaminated food","Close personal contact with an infected person","Mosquito bite","Airborne droplets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Transmission is by close personal contact of an infected person.  Why A wrong: Food is not the route of scabies transmission.  Why C wrong: Mosquitoes do not transmit scabies.  Why D wrong: Scabies is not spread by airborne droplets."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In scabies, after the female parasite enters the skin and deposits her eggs, the eggs become mature within:","options":["1 to 2 days","4 to 6 days","10 to 14 days","24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Within 4 to 6 days the eggs become mature (after the female deposits eggs and then dies).  Why A wrong: 1–2 days is too short per the source.  Why C wrong: 10–14 days is longer than stated.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is not the stated maturation time."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which sign is characteristic of scabies?","options":["Honey coloured crusts","Grayish thread-like burrows with pruritic papules (itching worse at night)","Dry silvery white scaly lesions","White patches in the mouth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Scabies is characterised by grayish/skin-coloured thread-like serpiginous burrows with intensely pruritic papules and itching that is worse at night. [Corrected: the source's \"greenish red burrows\" is an inaccurate descriptor.]  Why A wrong: Honey coloured crusts are seen in impetigo.  Why C wrong: Dry silvery white scaly lesions are the cardinal sign of psoriasis.  Why D wrong: White patches in the mouth indicate oral thrush (candidiasis)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The drug of choice for scabies, applied over the entire body surface area, is:","options":["Permethrin 5% ointment","Silver sulfadiazine","Acyclovir cream","Fluconazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Permethrin 5% ointment is the drug of choice for scabies and should be applied over the entire body surface area.  Why B wrong: Silver sulfadiazine is used for burns.  Why C wrong: Acyclovir is an antiviral.  Why D wrong: Fluconazole is an antifungal."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Lindane ointment for scabies is contraindicated in children younger than 2 years because it can cause:","options":["Hepatotoxicity","Neurological emergencies such as seizures","Renal failure","Skin necrosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lindane is contraindicated in children <2 years because it can cause neurological emergencies like seizures in small children.  Why A wrong: Hepatotoxicity is not the stated reason.  Why C wrong: Renal failure is not the cited risk.  Why D wrong: Skin necrosis is not the described adverse effect."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Impetigo is a highly contagious skin infection most commonly caused by:","options":["Sarcoptes scabiei","Staphylococcus aureus","Candida albicans","Trichophyton"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Impetigo's most common causative organism is Staphylococcus aureus (a bacterial infection).  Why A wrong: Sarcoptes scabiei causes scabies.  Why C wrong: Candida albicans causes candidiasis.  Why D wrong: Trichophyton causes ringworm (fungal)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Impetigo most commonly occurs in which age group and due to what predisposing factor?","options":["Less than 5 years; poor hygiene","School-age children; poor nutrition","Adolescents; hormonal changes","Elderly; immobility"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Impetigo occurs because of poor hygiene and is most common in children less than 5 years.  Why B wrong: The source specifies <5 years and poor hygiene, not school-age/nutrition.  Why C wrong: Adolescents/hormones are not cited.  Why D wrong: The elderly are not the stated group."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The body part most commonly affected in impetigo is the:","options":["Groin and genital region","Face around the mouth region and neck region","Scalp and hair","Palms and soles"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly affected body part in impetigo is the face around the mouth region and the neck region.  Why A wrong: The groin (tinea cruris) is not the impetigo site.  Why C wrong: Scalp/hair is affected in tinea capitis/pediculosis.  Why D wrong: Palms/soles are not the described impetigo site."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The classic lesion of impetigo, formed when thickened vesicle fluid ruptures, is described as:","options":["Greenish red burrows","Honey coloured crusts","Dry silvery scales","Weeping crusting allergic lesions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In impetigo, vesicle fluid becomes thick, vesicles rupture, and leave \"honey coloured crusts\" (NORCET-2022).  Why A wrong: Greenish red burrows are scabies.  Why C wrong: Dry silvery scales are psoriasis.  Why D wrong: Weeping/crusting allergic lesions describe eczema."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which sequence correctly describes impetigo lesion progression?","options":["Vesicles → maculo-papular rashes → crusts","Maculo-papular rashes → vesicles → honey coloured crusts","Burrows → pustules → scars","Scales → plaques → fissures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Initially maculo-papular rashes occur, progress into vesicles, and the thick ruptured fluid leaves honey coloured crusts.  Why A wrong: The order is reversed.  Why C wrong: Burrows/pustules/scars describe a different lesion pattern.  Why D wrong: Scales/plaques/fissures describe psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the nursing care of impetigo, which measure helps remove or soften the crust?","options":["Cold alcohol swabs","Warm saline compresses / \"Burow solution\"","Petroleum jelly occlusion","Iodine painting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Warm saline compresses can be used to remove the crust, and \"Burow solution\" may be prescribed to soften the crust.  Why A wrong: Cold alcohol swabs are not the described method.  Why C wrong: Petroleum jelly occlusion is not stated.  Why D wrong: Iodine painting is not the described crust-softening method."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Burow's solution used in impetigo care is chemically composed of:","options":["5% aluminium subacetate (water and aluminum acetate)","5% silver nitrate","5% aluminium subacetate is wrong; it is 10% acetic acid","Sodium hypochlorite"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Burow's solution is 5% aluminium subacetate, a liquid made with water and aluminum acetate; it relieves itching/stinging and helps stop growth of bacteria and fungus.  Why B wrong: It is not silver nitrate.  Why C wrong: The composition is 5% aluminium subacetate, not 10% acetic acid.  Why D wrong: It is not sodium hypochlorite."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which infection-control measures are appropriate in caring for a child with impetigo? (Select all that apply)","options":["Maintain strict contact precautions","Separate and disinfect equipment, bed sheets, and utensils","Administer antibiotics as ordered","Allow shared towels among siblings"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Strict contact precautions, separate/disinfect equipment and linens, and antibiotics are all part of impetigo care.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct measures.  Why D wrong: Sharing towels would spread this highly contagious infection and contradicts contact precautions."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Psoriasis is best described as:","options":["A contagious bacterial skin infection","A non-infectious autoimmune disorder with excessive keratinisation of the skin layer","A parasitic infestation","A fungal infection of the nails"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Psoriasis is a non-infectious autoimmune disorder in which excessive keratinisation of the skin layer occurs.  Why A wrong: It is non-infectious, not a bacterial infection.  Why C wrong: It is autoimmune, not parasitic.  Why D wrong: Nail fungal infection is tinea unguium."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The cardinal sign of psoriasis is:","options":["Honey coloured crusts","Dry silvery white scaly skin lesions","Greenish red burrows","Weeping crusting lesions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dry silvery white scaly skin lesions are the cardinal sign of psoriasis, along with pruritus (AIIMS Rishikesh-2018).  Why A wrong: Honey coloured crusts are impetigo.  Why C wrong: Greenish red burrows are scabies.  Why D wrong: Weeping crusting lesions are eczema."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Management of psoriasis includes Balneotherapy, which refers to:","options":["Bathing with water containing sulphur silica","Application of silver sulfadiazine","Oral antibiotics","Cellophane tape application"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Balneotherapy is bathing with water containing sulphur silica; management also includes steroids, UV radiation, and immunosuppressant drugs.  Why B wrong: Silver sulfadiazine is a burn drug.  Why C wrong: Antibiotics treat bacterial infection, not autoimmune psoriasis.  Why D wrong: Cellophane tape test is for pinworms."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In psoriasis management, UV radiation is used mainly to:","options":["Increase keratinization","Decrease keratinization of the skin","Kill parasites","Provide analgesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: UV radiation decreases keratinization of the skin in psoriasis.  Why A wrong: The aim is to decrease, not increase, keratinization.  Why C wrong: Psoriasis is autoimmune, not parasitic.  Why D wrong: Analgesia is not the purpose of UV therapy here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Match the \"Fast Review\" characterization: Eczema is characterized as:","options":["Honey coloured crush — bacterial","Greenish red burrow formation — parasite","Weeping & crusting lesions — allergic","Dry silvery white scaly lesions — autoimmune"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fast Review Points list eczema as weeping & crusting lesions — allergic.  Why A wrong: Honey coloured crust — bacterial describes impetigo.  Why B wrong: Greenish red burrow — parasite describes scabies.  Why D wrong: Dry silvery white scaly lesions — autoimmune describes psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the \"Fast Review\" points, Pediculosis is characterized by:","options":["Honey coloured crust","Hair — lice infestation","Greenish red burrows","Dry silvery scales"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fast Review Points list pediculosis as hair — lice infestation.  Why A wrong: Honey coloured crust is impetigo.  Why C wrong: Greenish red burrows are scabies.  Why D wrong: Dry silvery scales are psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the \"Fast Review\" points, Psoriasis is characterized as:","options":["Dry silvery white scaly skin lesions — auto immune","Honey coloured crush — bacterial","Hair — live infestation","Weeping & crusting lesions — allergic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fast Review Points list psoriasis as dry silvery white scaly skin lesions — auto immune.  Why B wrong: That describes impetigo.  Why C wrong: That describes pediculosis.  Why D wrong: That describes eczema."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Phenylketonuria (PKU) is caused by a lack of which enzyme?","options":["Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme","Lactase","Galactokinase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PKU is a lack of phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme.  Why A wrong: GALT deficiency causes galactosemia.  Why C wrong: Lactase deficiency relates to lactose digestion, not PKU.  Why D wrong: Galactokinase deficiency is associated with galactosemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In PKU, the missing enzyme normally converts phenylalanine into:","options":["Galactose","Tyrosine","Glucose","Glycine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phenylalanine hydroxylase enzyme is responsible to change phenylalanine into tyrosine.  Why A wrong: Galactose relates to galactose metabolism, not PKU.  Why C wrong: Glucose is not the product of phenylalanine hydroxylation.  Why D wrong: Glycine is not the conversion product described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In PKU, why does accumulation of phenylalanine harm the infant?","options":["Phenylalanine is an essential amino acid that accumulates in the body and affects normal growth and development of the baby","It converts to excess glucose","It causes iron overload","It destroys the thyroid gland"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Due to lack of the enzyme, phenylalanine (an essential amino acid) accumulates in the body and affects normal growth and development of the baby.  Why B wrong: The problem is amino acid accumulation, not glucose conversion.  Why C wrong: Iron overload is not part of PKU pathophysiology.  Why D wrong: PKU does not act by destroying the thyroid."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with phenylketonuria? (Select all that apply)","options":["Growth failure","Frequent vomiting and irritability","Mental retardation","Urine with a strong, musty odour","Polyuria with sweet-smelling urine only"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: PKU signs include growth failure, frequent vomiting and irritability, mental retardation, and urine with a strong, musty odour (due to phenylacetic acid).  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are listed PKU features.  Why E wrong: The source describes a strong musty odour from phenylacetic acid, not sweet-smelling polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The characteristic musty odour of urine in PKU is due to:","options":["Phenylacetic acid","Ketone bodies","Bilirubin","Uric acid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urine has a strong, musty odour due to phenylacetic acid.  Why B wrong: Ketone bodies are not the cited cause.  Why C wrong: Bilirubin relates to jaundice, not the musty odour.  Why D wrong: Uric acid is not the source of this odour."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The blood test used to diagnose phenylketonuria, performed after protein (breast milk) ingestion within the first 24 hours, is the:","options":["Beutler test","Guthrie blood test","Cellophane tape test","Wangensteen test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Guthrie blood test is used to diagnose PKU; it should be performed after protein ingestion (breast milk) initially within the first 24 hours.  Why A wrong: The Beutler test diagnoses galactosemia.  Why C wrong: The cellophane tape test is for pinworms.  Why D wrong: Wangensteen (& Rice) findings relate to imperforate anus X-ray, not PKU."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The dietary recommendation for an infant with PKU is:","options":["Complete elimination of phenylalanine from the diet","A low phenylalanine diet (20–30 mg/kg), because phenylalanine is essential for brain development","A high-protein diet","A galactose-free diet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phenylalanine should not be fully restricted because it is an essential amino acid needed for brain development; a low phenylalanine diet (20–30 mg/kg) should be offered.  Why A wrong: Complete elimination is wrong because phenylalanine is essential.  Why C wrong: A high-protein diet would raise phenylalanine intake harmfully.  Why D wrong: A galactose-free diet applies to galactosemia, not PKU."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Regarding breastfeeding in an infant with PKU, which statement is correct?","options":["Breastfeeding is absolutely contraindicated","Breastfeeding is not contraindicated because breast milk has low protein","Only formula with high protein is allowed","Breast milk must be fortified with phenylalanine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Breast feeding is not contraindicated because breast milk has low protein.  Why A wrong: The source states it is not contraindicated.  Why C wrong: High-protein formula would increase phenylalanine load.  Why D wrong: Fortifying with phenylalanine is contrary to a low-phenylalanine goal."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In galactosemia, dietary lactose (from dairy products or breast milk) is normally broken down by lactase enzyme into:","options":["Glucose and galactose","Glucose and fructose","Galactose and tyrosine","Sucrose and maltose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lactose in food is usually broken down by lactase enzyme into glucose and galactose.  Why B wrong: Fructose is not the product of lactose breakdown.  Why C wrong: Tyrosine relates to PKU, not lactose digestion.  Why D wrong: Sucrose/maltose are different disaccharides, not lactose products."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Galactosemia is caused by deficiency of which enzymes needed for galactose metabolism?","options":["Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltranserase (GALT) and galactokinase","Lactase and sucrase","Thyroid peroxidase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Galactosemia is caused by deficiency of galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltranserase (GALT) and galactokinase enzyme, needed for galactose metabolism.  Why A wrong: Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency causes PKU.  Why C wrong: Lactase/sucrase deficiencies are not the cause given.  Why D wrong: Thyroid peroxidase relates to thyroid function, not galactosemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of galactosemia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hepatomegaly (enlarged liver) and cirrhosis","Cataracts and kidney failure","Vomiting, seizure, and hypoglycaemia (low blood sugar)","Lethargy, brain damage, and ovarian failure","Hyperglycaemia with polyuria"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Galactosemia signs include hepatomegaly, cirrhosis, kidney failure, cataracts, vomiting, seizure, low blood sugar (hypoglycaemia), lethargy, brain damage, and ovarian failure.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are listed features.  Why E wrong: The source lists hypoglycaemia (low blood sugar), not hyperglycaemia with polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Without treatment, the approximate mortality in infants with galactosemia is:","options":["About 25%","About 50%","About 75%","About 10%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Without treatment, mortality in infants with galactosemia is about 75%.  Why A wrong: 25% understates the source value.  Why B wrong: 50% is not the stated figure.  Why D wrong: 10% is far below the stated 75%."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Galactosemia is an example of which pattern of inheritance?","options":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked dominant","Mitochondrial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Galactosemia is an example of an inherited autosomal recessive disorder (AIIMS, RRB-2019).  Why A wrong: It is recessive, not dominant.  Why C wrong: It is autosomal, not X-linked.  Why D wrong: It is not a mitochondrial inheritance pattern."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Galactosemia is diagnosed by which test?","options":["Guthrie test","Beutler test","Cellophane tape test","Wangensteen test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Galactosemia is diagnosed by the Beutler test.  Why A wrong: The Guthrie test diagnoses PKU.  Why C wrong: The cellophane tape test is for pinworms.  Why D wrong: Wangensteen findings relate to imperforate anus imaging."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Congenital hypothyroidism in children is due to:","options":["Autoimmune destruction of the thyroid","Embryonic failure to develop the thyroid gland","Iodine excess","Pituitary tumour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Congenital hypothyroidism is embryonic failure to develop the thyroid gland.  Why A wrong: Autoimmune destruction describes lymphocytic (Hashimoto) thyroiditis.  Why C wrong: Iodine excess is not the stated cause.  Why D wrong: A pituitary tumour is not the described mechanism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Lymphocytic thyroiditis, the most common type of hypothyroidism in children, is also known as:","options":["Graves' disease","Hashimoto disease","Cretinism","Myxedema coma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Lymphocytic thyroiditis is also known as Hashimoto disease, the most common type, caused by autoimmune reactions.  Why A wrong: Graves' disease is hyperthyroidism, not this condition.  Why C wrong: Cretinism is the syndrome of congenital hypothyroidism, not the name of lymphocytic thyroiditis.  Why D wrong: Myxedema coma is a severe hypothyroid emergency, not this entity."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which are signs/symptoms of congenital hypothyroidism? (Select all that apply)","options":["Jaundice","Feeding difficulties","Growth failure","Excessive sleeping and little crying","Constipation with decreased growth and metabolic rate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: S/S of congenital hypothyroidism include jaundice, feeding difficulties, growth failure, excessive sleeping, little crying, constipation, and decreased growth and metabolic rate.  Why A/B/C/D/E wrong: All are listed features of congenital hypothyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Signs and symptoms of lymphocytic thyroiditis include enlarged thyroid gland, hoarseness, and dysphagia. The dysphagia is due to:","options":["Tracheal compression","Oesophageal atresia","Cleft palate","Laryngomalacia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In lymphocytic thyroiditis, hoarseness and dysphagia occur due to tracheal compression by the enlarged thyroid gland.  Why B wrong: Oesophageal atresia is a separate congenital anomaly.  Why C wrong: Cleft palate is not the described cause.  Why D wrong: Laryngomalacia is not mentioned as the cause here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The management of hypothyroidism in children centers on:","options":["Early detection and replacement of thyroxine hormone","Antithyroid drugs","Radioactive iodine ablation","Iodine restriction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Early detection and replacement of thyroxine hormone are the management.  Why B wrong: Antithyroid drugs treat hyperthyroidism.  Why C wrong: Radioactive iodine ablation is used for hyperthyroidism, not replacement.  Why D wrong: Iodine restriction is not the described management."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Failure to Thrive (FTT) is best defined as:","options":["Insufficient weight gain or insufficient use of calories for growth","Excessive weight gain in infancy","A congenital heart defect","A metabolic bone disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FTT is an insufficient weight gain or insufficient use of calories for growth.  Why B wrong: FTT involves inadequate, not excessive, weight gain.  Why C wrong: It is a growth/nutrition problem, not a cardiac defect.  Why D wrong: It is not defined as a metabolic bone disease."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"FTT is commonly used to describe any child who fails to:","options":["Gain weight or height according to standard medical growth charts","Reach developmental reflexes only","Pass meconium","Achieve puberty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: FTT is used for any child who fails to gain weight or height according to standard medical growth charts.  Why B wrong: It is broader than reflexes; it concerns growth chart parameters.  Why C wrong: Failure to pass meconium relates to imperforate anus/ARM.  Why D wrong: FTT concerns infancy/childhood growth, not puberty attainment."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which are clinical manifestations of Failure to Thrive? (Select all that apply)","options":["Growth failure","Language deficits","Inadequate development of social, motor and adaptive behaviours","Accelerated puberty"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical manifestations include growth failure, language deficits, and inadequate development of social, motor and adaptive behaviours.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct manifestations.  Why D wrong: Accelerated puberty is not a listed manifestation of FTT."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Cessation of breathing is termed:","options":["Apnoea","Bradypnoea","Dyspnoea","Tachypnoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cessation of breathing is called apnoea.  Why B wrong: Bradypnoea is slow breathing, not cessation.  Why C wrong: Dyspnoea is difficult breathing.  Why D wrong: Tachypnoea is rapid breathing."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In infants, apnoea is defined as a cessation of breathing lasting:","options":["At least 20 seconds or more","5 seconds or more","15 seconds or less","60 seconds or more"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apnoea in infants is a duration of at least 20 seconds or more (AIIMS BBSR-2018).  Why B wrong: 5 seconds is too short.  Why C wrong: 15 seconds or less is the value for older children and adults.  Why D wrong: 60 seconds is not the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In older children and adults, apnoea is defined as a breathing cessation lasting:","options":["20 seconds or more","15 seconds or less","30 seconds","45 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apnoea in older children and adults is a duration of 15 seconds or less.  Why A wrong: 20 seconds or more is the infant definition.  Why C wrong: 30 seconds is not the stated value.  Why D wrong: 45 seconds is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Apnoea may occur along with which two findings, and is most commonly seen in which infants?","options":["With bradycardia and cyanosis; most commonly in preterm babies","With tachycardia and pallor; most commonly in term babies","With hypertension and flushing; in post-term babies","With fever and rash; in toddlers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apnoea may occur with bradycardia and cyanosis and is most commonly seen in preterm babies.  Why B wrong: The source states bradycardia (not tachycardia) and cyanosis, and preterm (not term) babies.  Why C wrong: Hypertension/flushing and post-term are not stated.  Why D wrong: Fever/rash in toddlers is unrelated to apnoea of infancy."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common drugs used for Apnoea of Infancy (AOI) are methylxanthines, which include:","options":["Theophylline and caffeine","Morphine and codeine","Diazepam and phenytoin","Salbutamol and ipratropium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common drugs for AOI are methylxanthines (theophylline and caffeine).  Why B wrong: Morphine/codeine are opioids that depress respiration.  Why C wrong: Diazepam/phenytoin are not the AOI drugs of choice.  Why D wrong: Salbutamol/ipratropium are bronchodilators, not the stated methylxanthines."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Vomiting is defined as:","options":["Forceful ejection of stomach content, usually associated with nausea","Painless regurgitation of milk","Passage of loose stools","Difficulty swallowing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vomiting is the forceful ejection of stomach content, usually associated with nausea.  Why B wrong: Painless regurgitation is a different phenomenon.  Why C wrong: Loose stools describe diarrhoea.  Why D wrong: Difficulty swallowing is dysphagia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common cause of vomiting in infants is:","options":["Infections","Pyloric stenosis","Increased ICP","Drug ingestion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of vomiting in infants is infections.  Why B wrong: Pyloric stenosis causes projectile vomiting but is not the most common cause.  Why C wrong: Increased ICP is a possible cause of recurrent/chronic vomiting, not the most common.  Why D wrong: Drug ingestion is an \"other cause,\" not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Intense forceful or projectile vomiting in an infant is characteristically seen in:","options":["Pyloric stenosis","Colic","Enuresis","Impetigo"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intense forceful or projectile vomiting is seen in pyloric stenosis (FAQs).  Why B wrong: Colic causes cramping pain, not projectile vomiting.  Why C wrong: Enuresis is bed wetting.  Why D wrong: Impetigo is a skin infection."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Severe vomiting leads to dehydration, electrolyte depletion, and which acid-base disturbance?","options":["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis due to loss of hydrogen ions, because hydrogen ion is present in the stomach as a form of HCL.  Why B wrong: Loss of acid causes alkalosis, not acidosis.  Why C wrong: The disturbance is metabolic, not respiratory.  Why D wrong: It is metabolic alkalosis, not respiratory alkalosis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"During and after feeding, in which position should an infant prone to vomiting be maintained?","options":["Supine flat position","Side lying or low Fowler's position, and burp the baby","Trendelenburg position","Prone with head flat"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The baby should be maintained in side lying or low Fowler's position and burped during and after feeding.  Why A wrong: Supine flat increases aspiration risk during vomiting.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not recommended here.  Why D wrong: Prone with head flat is not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Colic is described as:","options":["Paroxysmal abdominal cramp-like severe pain","Painless bloody diarrhoea","A skin infection","A metabolic enzyme deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Colic is paroxysmal abdominal cramp-like severe pain.  Why B wrong: Bloody diarrhoea is not colic.  Why C wrong: Colic is not a skin infection.  Why D wrong: Colic is not an enzyme deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common cause of colic is:","options":["Gas accumulation in the stomach and intestine due to mal technique of breast feeding","Bacterial infection of the skin","Autoimmune keratinisation","Enzyme deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common causes are gas accumulation in the stomach and intestine due to mal technique of breast feeding.  Why B wrong: Skin infection is unrelated to colic.  Why C wrong: Autoimmune keratinisation describes psoriasis.  Why D wrong: Enzyme deficiency describes metabolic disorders, not colic."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are seen in an infant with colic? (Select all that apply)","options":["Pulling up the arms and legs onto the abdomen","Red face after crying for a long period","Excessive gas","Painless jaundice"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Colic S/S include pulling up the arms and legs on the abdomen, red face after crying a long period, and excessive gas.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct colic features.  Why D wrong: Painless jaundice is not a colic feature (jaundice appears in hypothyroidism/galactosemia)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"After feeding an infant with colic, positioning on the right side facilitates digestion due to:","options":["The anatomical gravity of the stomach and duodenum","Increased cardiac output","Reduced intracranial pressure","Decreased renal perfusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Positioning on the right side after feeding facilitates digestion due to the anatomical gravity of the stomach and duodenum.  Why B wrong: Cardiac output is not the mechanism.  Why C wrong: ICP is unrelated to this positioning benefit.  Why D wrong: Renal perfusion is not the described rationale."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"If cramping or abdominal distension occurs in an infant with colic, the recommended position is:","options":["Knee-chest position","Supine flat","High Fowler's","Left lateral with legs extended"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Place the baby in the knee-chest position if any cramping or abdominal distension occurs.  Why B wrong: Supine flat is not the recommended relief position.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is not specified for colic cramping.  Why D wrong: Left lateral with legs extended is not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Nursing care for colic includes which measures? (Select all that apply)","options":["Offer smaller, more frequent feedings","Burp frequently during and after feeding","Position on the right side after feeding","Withhold all feeds for 24 hours"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Colic nursing care includes smaller/more frequent feedings, frequent burping during and after feeding, and positioning on the right side after feeding.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct interventions.  Why D wrong: Withholding all feeds for 24 hours is not recommended and risks dehydration/hypoglycaemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Acute Otitis Media (AOM) is an infection of the:","options":["External ear","Middle ear","Inner ear","Mastoid bone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AOM is the infection of the middle ear.  Why A wrong: External ear infection is otitis externa.  Why C wrong: Inner ear involvement is labyrinthitis, not AOM.  Why D wrong: Mastoid infection is mastoiditis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common bacterial causative organism of Acute Otitis Media is:","options":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Respiratory syncytial virus","H. influenzae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common bacterial cause is Streptococcus pneumoniae; H. influenzae is also bacterial, and viral causes include RSV or influenza.  Why B wrong: Staphylococcus aureus is the impetigo organism, not the most common AOM cause.  Why C wrong: RSV is a viral cause, not the most common bacterial cause.  Why D wrong: H. influenzae is a bacterial cause but not the most common one."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which viral organisms can cause Acute Otitis Media? (Select all that apply)","options":["Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)","Influenza","Streptococcus pneumoniae","H. influenzae"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: Viral causes of AOM include RSV or influenza.  Why A/B wrong: Both are correctly identified viral causes.  Why C wrong: Streptococcus pneumoniae is bacterial, not viral.  Why D wrong: H. influenzae is bacterial, not viral."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The cardinal sign of Acute Otitis Media in an infant is:","options":["Pain while moving the head, rubbing the ear or rolling the head side to side, possibly hitting the head against a hard surface","Painless white patches in the mouth","Honey coloured crusts around the ear","Projectile vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign is pain while the infant moves the head and rubs the ear or rolls the head side to side, and may hit the head against a hard surface.  Why B wrong: White patches in the mouth indicate oral thrush.  Why C wrong: Honey coloured crusts are impetigo.  Why D wrong: Projectile vomiting relates to pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A feeling of fullness in the ear, sometimes with loss of hearing, occurs in:","options":["Otitis media with effusion","Pinworm infestation","Ringworm","Enuresis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A feeling of fullness in the ear occurs in otitis media with effusion, sometimes with loss of hearing.  Why B wrong: Pinworm affects the GI tract/perineum, not the ear.  Why C wrong: Ringworm is a fungal skin infection.  Why D wrong: Enuresis is bed wetting."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"When instilling ear drops in a child younger than 3 years of age, the pinna should be pulled:","options":["Upward and backward","Downward and backward","Upward and forward","Straight out"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For <3 years of age, pull the pinna downward and backward (PGI, JIPMER-2020).  Why A wrong: Upward and backward is the technique for >3 years of age.  Why C wrong: Upward and forward is not the described technique.  Why D wrong: Pulling straight out is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"When instilling ear drops in a child older than 3 years of age, the pinna should be pulled:","options":["Downward and backward","Upward and backward","Downward and forward","Upward and forward"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For >3 years of age, pull the pinna upward and backward.  Why A wrong: Downward and backward is for <3 years.  Why C wrong: Downward and forward is not the described technique.  Why D wrong: Upward and forward is not specified."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which surgical procedure, with insertion of tympanotomy tubes, may be done for Acute Otitis Media?","options":["Myringotomy","Orchidopexy","Fasciotomy","Adenoidectomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Myringotomy with insertion of tympanotomy tubes may be done for AOM.  Why B wrong: Orchidopexy corrects cryptorchidism.  Why C wrong: Fasciotomy relieves compartment syndrome in burns.  Why D wrong: Adenoidectomy removes adenoids, not the AOM procedure described."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Pinworm infection is also called:","options":["Enterobiasis","Dermatophytosis","Enuresis","Cryptorchidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pinworm is also called enterobiasis.  Why B wrong: Dermatophytosis is ringworm.  Why C wrong: Enuresis is bed wetting.  Why D wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended testis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Pinworm is an infestation of the GI tract by which organism?","options":["Nematode Enterobius vermicularis","Sarcoptes scabiei","Candida albicans","Trichophyton"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pinworm is infestation of the GI tract by the nematode Enterobius vermicularis.  Why B wrong: Sarcoptes scabiei causes scabies.  Why C wrong: Candida albicans causes candidiasis.  Why D wrong: Trichophyton causes ringworm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In pinworm infestation, the adult female migrates to which site at night to lay eggs?","options":["The anus, where eggs hatch on the perineal skin","The mouth","The middle ear","The bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The adult female migrates to the anus at night and lays eggs, which hatch on the perineal skin.  Why B wrong: Eggs enter via the mouth, but the female lays eggs at the anus at night.  Why C wrong: The middle ear is unrelated to pinworm.  Why D wrong: The bladder is not the egg-laying site."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common route of transmission (re-infestation) of pinworm is:","options":["Fingers-to-anus-to-mouth route","Mosquito bite","Contaminated water only","Airborne droplets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common route of transmission is re-infestation through the fingers to anus to mouth route.  Why B wrong: Mosquitoes do not transmit pinworm.  Why C wrong: Water alone is not the stated main route.  Why D wrong: Airborne droplet spread is not the pinworm route."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with pinworm infestation? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe pruritus of the anal area","Irritability and insomnia","Anorexia and weight loss","Projectile vomiting"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Pinworm S/S include severe pruritus of the anal area, irritability and insomnia, anorexia and weight loss.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct features.  Why D wrong: Projectile vomiting relates to pyloric stenosis, not pinworm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The diagnostic test for pinworms, in which a sample is collected from near the anus in the morning before a bowel movement, is the:","options":["Cellophane tape test","Guthrie test","Beutler test","Cellophane tape test done at night after defecation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Cellophane Tape Test is diagnostic for pinworms; the sample is collected from near the anus in the morning before bowel movement.  Why B wrong: The Guthrie test diagnoses PKU.  Why C wrong: The Beutler test diagnoses galactosemia.  Why D wrong: The sample is collected in the morning before bowel movement, not at night after defecation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The drug of choice for pinworm infestation is:","options":["Albendazole or Mebendazole (once a week single dose)","Permethrin 5% ointment","Silver sulfadiazine","Acetaminophen"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice is Albendazole or Mebendazole (once a week single dose).  Why B wrong: Permethrin is for scabies.  Why C wrong: Silver sulfadiazine is for burns.  Why D wrong: Acetaminophen is used in Reye's syndrome/analgesia, not for pinworm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Ringworm is also known as dermatophytosis and is a fungal infection caused by:","options":["Trichophyton or Epidermophyton group of fungus","Candida albicans","Staphylococcus aureus","Enterobius vermicularis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ringworm (dermatophytosis) is a fungal infection caused by the trichophyton or epidermophyton group of fungus.  Why B wrong: Candida albicans causes candidiasis/thrush.  Why C wrong: Staphylococcus aureus causes impetigo (bacterial).  Why D wrong: Enterobius vermicularis is the pinworm nematode."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Ringworm infection of the scalp, hair, eyebrows, and eyelashes is called:","options":["Tinea capitis","Tinea pedis","Tinea corporis","Tinea cruris"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea capitis is ringworm infection of the scalp, hair, eyebrows and eyelashes.  Why B wrong: Tinea pedis affects the foot.  Why C wrong: Tinea corporis affects the body.  Why D wrong: Tinea cruris affects the groin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Tinea pedis, ringworm infection of the foot, is also known as:","options":["Athlete's foot","Jock itch","Oral thrush","Ring finger"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea pedis is ringworm infection of the foot, also known as athlete's foot (AIIMS BPL-2016).  Why B wrong: Jock itch is tinea cruris.  Why C wrong: Oral thrush is candidiasis.  Why D wrong: \"Ring finger\" is not a term used here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Ringworm infection all over the body is called:","options":["Tinea corporis","Tinea capitis","Tinea unguium","Tinea barbae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea corporis is ringworm infection all over the body.  Why B wrong: Tinea capitis affects the scalp.  Why C wrong: Tinea unguium affects the nails.  Why D wrong: Tinea barbae affects the beard."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Tinea cruris, ringworm infection of the groin area, is also known as:","options":["Jock itch","Athlete's foot","Barber's itch","Oral thrush"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea cruris is ringworm of the groin area, also known as jock itch.  Why B wrong: Athlete's foot is tinea pedis.  Why C wrong: Barber's itch/beard is tinea barbae.  Why D wrong: Oral thrush is candidiasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Ringworm infection of the nails (fingernails and toenails) is called:","options":["Tinea unguium","Tinea pedis","Tinea corporis","Tinea barbae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea unguium is ringworm infection of the nails (fingernails and toenails).  Why B wrong: Tinea pedis affects the foot.  Why C wrong: Tinea corporis affects the body.  Why D wrong: Tinea barbae affects the beard."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Ringworm infection of the beard is termed:","options":["Tinea barbae","Tinea cruris","Tinea capitis","Tinea unguium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tinea barbae is ringworm infection of the beard.  Why B wrong: Tinea cruris affects the groin.  Why C wrong: Tinea capitis affects the scalp/hair.  Why D wrong: Tinea unguium affects the nails."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Candidiasis is a fungal infection caused by which organism, and when it affects the mouth it is called:","options":["Candida albicans; oral thrush","Trichophyton; tinea capitis","Epidermophyton; athlete's foot","Staphylococcus aureus; impetigo"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Candidiasis is caused by Candida albicans; when it affects the mouth it is called oral thrush.  Why B wrong: Trichophyton causes ringworm, not oral thrush.  Why C wrong: Epidermophyton causes tinea, not candidiasis.  Why D wrong: Staphylococcus aureus causes impetigo."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The cardinal sign of oral thrush is:","options":["White patches in the mouth","Honey coloured crusts","Greenish red burrows","Dry silvery scales"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cardinal sign of oral thrush is white patches in the mouth.  Why B wrong: Honey coloured crusts are impetigo.  Why C wrong: Greenish red burrows are scabies.  Why D wrong: Dry silvery scales are psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Antifungal medicines ordered for candidiasis/ringworm-type infections include:","options":["Itraconazol, Fluconazol","Albendazole, Mebendazole","Silver sulfadiazine","Imipramine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antifungal medicines ordered include Itraconazol, Fluconazol, etc.  Why B wrong: Albendazole/Mebendazole are anthelmintics for pinworm.  Why C wrong: Silver sulfadiazine is a burn antimicrobial.  Why D wrong: Imipramine (a TCA) is used for enuresis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Clinical features of candidiasis skin lesions include which of the following?","options":["Red, scaly, itchy or raised patches that may be redder on the outside edges or resemble a ring","Dry silvery scales only","Honey coloured crusts only","Greenish burrows only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most clinical features include red, scaly, itchy or raised patches; patches may be redder on outside edges or resemble a ring.  Why B wrong: Dry silvery scales describe psoriasis.  Why C wrong: Honey coloured crusts describe impetigo.  Why D wrong: Greenish burrows describe scabies."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which measures help prevent the spread of ringworm/fungal skin infection? (Select all that apply)","options":["Keeping the skin dry","Not walking barefoot in public","Not sharing personal items","Not touching lesions and maintaining good hygiene with washing of hands and body","Sharing footwear to reduce cost"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Prevention includes keeping the skin dry, not walking barefoot in public, not sharing personal items, not touching lesions, and good hygiene with hand and body washing.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are correct prevention strategies.  Why E wrong: Sharing footwear would spread the fungal infection, contradicting prevention."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Parents of a child with ringworm should be instructed to:","options":["Maintain proper hygiene","Encourage barefoot walking in public","Share towels and personal items","Rub the lesions frequently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Instruct parents to maintain proper hygiene.  Why B wrong: Barefoot walking in public should be avoided.  Why C wrong: Personal items should not be shared.  Why D wrong: Lesions should not be touched to prevent spread."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Hearing impairment is defined as:","options":["The inability to hear or voice in normal condition","Loss of vision","Cessation of breathing","Bed wetting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hearing impairment is the inability to hear or voice in normal condition.  Why B wrong: Loss of vision is visual impairment.  Why C wrong: Cessation of breathing is apnoea.  Why D wrong: Bed wetting is enuresis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which of the following are causes of hearing impairment? (Select all that apply)","options":["Congenital disorders and ear infections","Prematurity and asphyxia","Maternal infection and bacterial meningitis","Ototoxic drugs","Improved neonatal screening"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include congenital disorders, ear infections, prematurity, asphyxia, maternal infection, bacterial meningitis, and ototoxic drugs.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are listed causes.  Why E wrong: Improved neonatal screening is a preventive/detection measure, not a cause of hearing impairment."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common type of hearing loss, due to inability to transmit voice from the external ear to the internal ear, is:","options":["Conductive hearing loss","Sensorineural hearing loss","Mixed hearing loss","Central hearing loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Conductive hearing loss is due to inability to transmit voice from external ear to internal ear and is the most common type of hearing loss.  Why B wrong: Sensorineural loss is due to defects in the internal ear and auditory nerves.  Why C wrong: Mixed loss is not identified as the most common type here.  Why D wrong: Central hearing loss is not the described most common type."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Sensorineural hearing loss is due to a defect in the internal ear and auditory nerves. Its causes include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Kernicterus","Ototoxic drugs","Excessive noise exposures","Cerumen impaction"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Sensorineural hearing loss causes include kernicterus, ototoxic drugs, and excessive noise exposures.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct listed causes.  Why D wrong: Cerumen (wax) impaction causes conductive loss and is not listed as a sensorineural cause."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Normally, the human ear can hear sound at a frequency range of:","options":["20 to 20,000 Hz","0 to 120 Hz","200 to 2000 Hz","20 to 200 Hz"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Normally, the human ear hears frequency from 20 to 20,000 Hz (ESIC-2016).  Why B wrong: 0 to 120 refers to intensity in dB, not frequency.  Why C wrong: 200 to 2000 Hz understates the range.  Why D wrong: 20 to 200 Hz is far too narrow."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The normal intensity range that the human ear can hear is:","options":["From 0 dB to 120 dB or more","From 20 dB to 200 dB","From 0 dB to 20 dB","From 55 dB to 90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The human ear hears intensity from 0 dB to 120 dB or more (ESIC-2016).  Why B wrong: 20 to 200 dB is not the stated range.  Why C wrong: 0 to 20 dB is too narrow.  Why D wrong: 55 to 90 dB describes hearing-loss classification categories, not the normal hearing range."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Clinical signs of hearing impairment in an infant include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lack of reflexes","Failure to locate sources of sound at 2–3 feet","Inability to understand words by 12 months of age","Ability to mimic speech by 3 months"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical signs in an infant include lack of reflexes, failure to locate sources of sound at 2–3 feet, and inability to understand words by 12 months of age.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are correct infant signs.  Why D wrong: Mimicking speech by 3 months is a normal finding, not a sign of impairment."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Clinical features of hearing impairment in a toddler include:","options":["Use of inappropriate gesture rather than verbalization and a history of frequent respiratory tract infections or otitis media","Normal speech development","Excessive verbal fluency","Projectile vomiting"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Toddler features include use of inappropriate gesture rather than verbalization and a history of frequent respiratory tract infections or otitis media.  Why B wrong: Normal speech development would not indicate impairment.  Why C wrong: Excessive verbal fluency is not a sign of hearing impairment.  Why D wrong: Projectile vomiting relates to pyloric stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which nursing care measures are appropriate for a child with hearing impairment? (Select all that apply)","options":["Use face-to-face communication to facilitate lip reading, with adequate light on the speaker's face","Speak at face level and speak toward the unaffected ear; do not walk or run away while talking","Talk in a normal voice and do not talk loudly, especially if the loss is sensorineural","Use facial or non-verbal communication techniques","Always shout to ensure the child hears"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care includes face-to-face communication with adequate light for lip reading, speaking at face level toward the unaffected ear without walking away, talking in a normal voice (not loudly, especially if sensorineural), and using facial/non-verbal techniques.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are correct interventions.  Why E wrong: Shouting is contraindicated; the source says do not talk loudly, especially if the loss is sensorineural."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"According to the source, a hearing loss greater than what intensity is sometimes considered severe hearing loss?","options":[">85 dB",">25 dB",">55 dB",">120 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sometimes, >85 dB is considered as severe hearing loss.  Why B wrong: >25 dB does not match the stated severe threshold.  Why C wrong: >55 dB is within the moderate-to-severe band, not the >85 dB figure.  Why D wrong: >120 dB exceeds the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the Clark's (audiometric) classification of hearing impairment, the \"Slight\" degree corresponds to which dB range?","options":["16–25 dB","25 to 55 dB","55 to 70 dB","70 to 90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In this audiometric (Clark's) grading, Slight = 16-25 dB. [Corrected attribution: these dB bands are Clark's/audiometric classification, NOT the WHO grades. Current WHO (2021) grades are Normal <20, Mild 20-<35, Moderate 35-<50, Moderately-severe 50-<65, Severe 65-<80, Profound 80-<95, Complete >=95 dB.]  Why B wrong: 25 to 55 dB is \"Mild to moderate.\"  Why C wrong: 55 to 70 dB is \"Moderate to severe.\"  Why D wrong: 70 to 90 dB is \"Severe.\""},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the Clark's (audiometric) classification of hearing impairment, the \"Mild to moderate\" degree corresponds to:","options":["16–25 dB","25 to 55 dB","55 to 70 dB",">90 dB"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mild to moderate = 25 to 55 dB per the WHO table.  Why A wrong: 16–25 dB is \"Slight.\"  Why C wrong: 55 to 70 dB is \"Moderate to severe.\"  Why D wrong: >90 dB is \"Profound.\""},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the Clark's (audiometric) classification of hearing impairment, the \"Moderate to severe\" degree corresponds to:","options":["55 to 70 dB","16–25 dB","70 to 90 dB",">90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate to severe = 55 to 70 dB per the WHO table.  Why B wrong: 16–25 dB is \"Slight.\"  Why C wrong: 70 to 90 dB is \"Severe.\"  Why D wrong: >90 dB is \"Profound.\""},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the Clark's (audiometric) classification of hearing impairment, the \"Severe\" degree corresponds to:","options":["70 to 90 dB","55 to 70 dB","25 to 55 dB",">90 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Severe = 70 to 90 dB per the WHO table.  Why B wrong: 55 to 70 dB is \"Moderate to severe.\"  Why C wrong: 25 to 55 dB is \"Mild to moderate.\"  Why D wrong: >90 dB is \"Profound.\""},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the Clark's (audiometric) classification of hearing impairment, the \"Profound\" degree corresponds to:","options":[">90 dB","70 to 90 dB","55 to 70 dB","16–25 dB"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Profound = >90 dB per the WHO table (NORCET-5 Prelims-2023).  Why B wrong: 70 to 90 dB is \"Severe.\"  Why C wrong: 55 to 70 dB is \"Moderate to severe.\"  Why D wrong: 16–25 dB is \"Slight.\""},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Visual impairment is defined as:","options":["Loss of vision, or the partial or total inability of visual perception","Inability to hear normally","Cessation of breathing","Failure to pass meconium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Visual impairment is loss of vision or the partial or total inability of visual perception.  Why B wrong: Inability to hear is hearing impairment.  Why C wrong: Cessation of breathing is apnoea.  Why D wrong: Failure to pass meconium relates to imperforate anus/ARM."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"According to the source, the leading global cause of vision impairment and blindness is:","options":["Uncorrected refractive errors (36%)","Cataracts (17%)","Glaucoma (2%)","Diabetic retinopathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The leading global causes are uncorrected refractive errors (36%), followed by cataracts (17%) and glaucoma (2%); refractive errors are the leading cause.  Why B wrong: Cataracts (17%) are the second-listed cause globally.  Why C wrong: Glaucoma (2%) is a smaller contributor.  Why D wrong: Diabetic retinopathy is not listed among these global causes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Refractive errors that contribute to visual impairment include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Near-sightedness","Far-sightedness","Presbyopia","Astigmatism","Cataract"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Refractive errors include near-sightedness, far-sightedness, presbyopia, and astigmatism.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All four are listed refractive errors.  Why E wrong: Cataract is a separate cause of impairment, not classified as a refractive error here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In India, the most common cause of blindness is:","options":["Cataract","Glaucoma","Refractive error","Diabetic retinopathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In India, cataract is the most common cause of blindness (FAQs, AIIMS, NHM, DSSSB-2019).  Why B wrong: Glaucoma is not the most common Indian cause per the source.  Why C wrong: Refractive error is the leading global cause but cataract is India's most common cause of blindness.  Why D wrong: Diabetic retinopathy is not the stated most common cause in India."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the WHO Classification of Vision Impairment, Category 0 (visual acuity 6/6 to 6/18) corresponds to:","options":["No impairment","Moderate visual impairment","Severe visual impairment","Blind"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category 0 with acuity 6/6 to 6/18 = No impairment.  Why B wrong: Moderate visual impairment is Category 1 (<6/18 to 6/60).  Why C wrong: Severe visual impairment is Category 2 (<6/60 to 3/60).  Why D wrong: Blind is Category 3 (<3/60)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the WHO Classification of Vision Impairment, Category 1 with visual acuity <6/18 to 6/60 indicates:","options":["No impairment","Moderate visual impairment","Severe visual impairment","Blind"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Category 1 (<6/18 to 6/60) = Moderate visual impairment.  Why A wrong: No impairment is Category 0 (6/6 to 6/18).  Why C wrong: Severe visual impairment is Category 2 (<6/60 to 3/60).  Why D wrong: Blind is Category 3 (<3/60)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the WHO Classification of Vision Impairment, Category 2 with visual acuity <6/60 to 3/60 indicates:","options":["Severe visual impairment","Moderate visual impairment","No impairment","Blind"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category 2 (<6/60 to 3/60) = Severe visual impairment.  Why B wrong: Moderate is Category 1 (<6/18 to 6/60).  Why C wrong: No impairment is Category 0.  Why D wrong: Blind is Category 3 (<3/60)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the WHO Classification of Vision Impairment, Category 3 with visual acuity <3/60 indicates:","options":["Blind","Severe visual impairment","Moderate visual impairment","No impairment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Category 3 (<3/60) = Blind.  Why B wrong: Severe visual impairment is Category 2 (<6/60 to 3/60).  Why C wrong: Moderate is Category 1.  Why D wrong: No impairment is Category 0."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy is the surgical removal of tonsils and adenoids, which are both:","options":["Lymphoid tissue that helps prevent infection or invasion of organisms","Endocrine glands","Parts of the middle ear","Salivary glands"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both tonsils and adenoids are lymphoid tissue that helps prevent infection or invasion of organisms.  Why B wrong: They are lymphoid, not endocrine, tissue.  Why C wrong: They are not parts of the middle ear.  Why D wrong: They are not salivary glands."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common complication in the postoperative period after tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy is:","options":["Hemorrhage and shock","Constipation","Urinary retention","Hypothermia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Both are highly vascular organs, so within the first 24 hours there is a risk of haemorrhage and shock; monitor for haemorrhage during the initial postoperative period (AIIMS, PGI, ESIC).  Why B wrong: Constipation is not the most common complication.  Why C wrong: Urinary retention is not the stated main complication.  Why D wrong: Hypothermia is not identified as the key complication."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The classical sign of haemorrhage after tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy is:","options":["Frequent swallowing","Increased urine output","Constipation","Bradycardia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The classical sign of haemorrhage is frequent swallowing, with bright red blood in vomitus, restlessness, and increased pulse and pallor.  Why B wrong: Increased urine output is not a sign of postoperative haemorrhage here.  Why C wrong: Constipation is unrelated to this haemorrhage sign.  Why D wrong: The source describes increased pulse, not bradycardia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which findings suggest postoperative haemorrhage after tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Frequent swallowing","Bright red blood in vomitus","Restlessness","Increased pulse and pallor","Decreased pulse with flushing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Signs of haemorrhage include frequent swallowing (classical sign), bright red blood in vomitus, restlessness, and increased pulse and pallor (AIIMS, JIPMER, ESIC, RRB, GMCH, PGI).  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are correct signs.  Why E wrong: The source describes increased pulse and pallor, not decreased pulse with flushing."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The recommended position in the postoperative period after tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy is:","options":["Prone position to promote drainage of secretion, with the head of bed elevated to limit oedema","Supine flat with the neck extended","Trendelenburg position","High Fowler's with the neck flexed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prone position promotes drainage of secretion, and the head of bed is elevated to limit oedema.  Why B wrong: Supine flat does not promote secretion drainage as well.  Why C wrong: Trendelenburg is not recommended and could worsen oedema/aspiration risk.  Why D wrong: High Fowler's with neck flexed is not the stated position."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"After tonsillectomy/adenoidectomy, which type of liquids should be offered?","options":["Cool liquids that are not red in colour or thick","Hot liquids","Red-coloured juices","Thick milkshakes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Offer cool liquids that are not in red colour or thick (NORCET-5 Mains-2023). Red colour would be confused with blood; thick liquids and hot liquids can irritate/promote bleeding.  Why B wrong: Hot liquids can promote bleeding and are not advised.  Why C wrong: Red-coloured liquids can be confused with blood.  Why D wrong: Thick liquids are specifically to be avoided."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Anorectal Malformation (ARM) is best defined as:","options":["Abnormal formation of the anal canal and rectum","Abnormal opening of the urethra","Failure of testicular descent","Fatty change in the liver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ARM is abnormal formation of the anal canal and rectum.  Why B wrong: Abnormal urethral opening describes epispadias/hypospadias.  Why C wrong: Failure of testicular descent is cryptorchidism.  Why D wrong: Fatty change in the liver describes Reye's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of Anorectal Malformation? (Select all that apply)","options":["Radiation","Infection","Alcohol","Smoking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of ARM include radiation, infection, alcohol, and smoking.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All four are explicitly listed causes."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In an imperforate anal membrane, the anal opening is covered by a transparent membrane so that:","options":["Meconium does not pass and the membrane becomes greenish","Urine is diverted to the rectum","The testes fail to descend","Ribbon-like stool passes freely"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In imperforate anal membrane, the anal opening is covered by a transparent membrane so meconium does not pass and the membrane becomes greenish.  Why B wrong: Urine diversion describes fistula pathology, not the membrane itself.  Why C wrong: Testicular descent relates to cryptorchidism.  Why D wrong: Ribbon-like stool is seen in anal stenosis, where narrowing (not full obstruction) occurs."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In anal stenosis, the characteristic stool appearance due to narrowing of the anal canal is:","options":["Ribbon-like stool","Rice-water stool","Currant jelly stool","Clay-coloured stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anal stenosis is narrowing of the anal canal, and ribbon-like stool can be seen due to the stenosis.  Why B wrong: Rice-water stool is associated with cholera, not anal stenosis.  Why C wrong: Currant jelly stool suggests intussusception.  Why D wrong: Clay-coloured stool suggests biliary obstruction."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Anal agenesis is defined as:","options":["Failure of the formation of the anal canal, with no anal opening, resulting in intestinal obstruction","Narrowing of the anal canal","A transparent membrane over the anus","Abnormal connection between rectum and bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anal agenesis is failure of the formation of the anal canal; the anal opening is not present, and intestinal obstruction may occur.  Why B wrong: Narrowing describes anal stenosis.  Why C wrong: A transparent membrane describes imperforate anal membrane.  Why D wrong: An abnormal rectum-bladder connection is a recto-vesical fistula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In ARM, stool may be passed from abnormal locations through a fistula. A recto-urethral fistula is an abnormal connection between the:","options":["Rectum and urethra","Rectum and vagina","Rectum and bladder","Rectum and skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A recto-urethral fistula is an abnormal connection between the rectum and urethra.  Why B wrong: The rectum-vagina connection is a recto-vaginal fistula.  Why C wrong: The rectum-bladder connection is a recto-vesical fistula.  Why D wrong: A rectum-skin connection is not the described recto-urethral fistula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A recto-vaginal fistula is an abnormal connection between the:","options":["Rectum and vagina","Rectum and urethra","Rectum and bladder","Bladder and vagina"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A recto-vaginal fistula is an abnormal connection between the rectum and vagina.  Why B wrong: Rectum-urethra is a recto-urethral fistula.  Why C wrong: Rectum-bladder is a recto-vesical fistula.  Why D wrong: Bladder-vagina is a vesicovaginal fistula, not described here."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A recto-vesical fistula is an abnormal connection between the:","options":["Rectum and bladder","Rectum and vagina","Rectum and urethra","Rectum and skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A recto-vesical fistula is an abnormal connection between the rectum and bladder.  Why B wrong: Rectum-vagina is a recto-vaginal fistula.  Why C wrong: Rectum-urethra is a recto-urethral fistula.  Why D wrong: Rectum-skin is not the described recto-vesical fistula."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Rectal agenesis is described as:","options":["The most common type of ARM, with failure in the formation of the rectum and possible intestinal obstruction","Narrowing of the anal canal only","An abnormal urethral opening","Failure of testicular descent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal agenesis is the most common type of ARM, a failure in the formation of the rectum, and intestinal obstruction may occur.  Why B wrong: Narrowing of the anal canal is anal stenosis.  Why C wrong: Abnormal urethral opening is epispadias/hypospadias.  Why D wrong: Failure of testicular descent is cryptorchidism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are seen in rectal agenesis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Meconium not passed","Greenish membrane over the anal opening","Ribbon-like stool","Meconium passing through the urine and vagina","Projectile vomiting of milk"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rectal agenesis S/S include meconium not passed, greenish membrane over the anal opening, ribbon-like stool, and meconium passing through the urine and vagina.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are listed signs.  Why E wrong: Projectile vomiting of milk relates to pyloric stenosis, not rectal agenesis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Diagnosis of rectal agenesis is done by physical examination and imaging, with which classic X-ray findings?","options":["Wangensteen & Rice findings","Beutler findings","Guthrie findings","Currant jelly sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis is done by physical examination and USG & X-Ray (Wangensteen & Rice findings seen on X-ray).  Why B wrong: Beutler is a blood test for galactosemia.  Why C wrong: Guthrie is the blood test for PKU.  Why D wrong: Currant jelly sign relates to intussusception stools, not this X-ray."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a GI disorder that may be caused by:","options":["Stress and emotional factors","Bacterial invasion of the bowel wall","Fungal infection","Failure of anal canal formation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IBS is a GI disorder that may be caused by stress and emotional factors.  Why B wrong: Bacterial invasion is not the stated cause of IBS.  Why C wrong: Fungal infection is not the cause.  Why D wrong: Failure of anal canal formation describes anal agenesis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In IBS, increased intestinal motility can lead to:","options":["Spasm and pain","Complete obstruction","Testicular torsion","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Due to IBS, intestinal motility may increase, which can lead to spasm and pain.  Why B wrong: Complete obstruction is not the described IBS mechanism.  Why C wrong: Testicular torsion is unrelated to IBS.  Why D wrong: Hepatic encephalopathy relates to Reye's syndrome/liver disease."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A characteristic bowel pattern in IBS is:","options":["Alternating constipation and diarrhoea with undigested food and mucus in the stool","Ribbon-like stool only","Currant jelly stool","Absent bowel movements permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IBS shows alternating constipation and diarrhoea with the presence of undigested food and mucus in the stool.  Why B wrong: Ribbon-like stool is characteristic of anal stenosis.  Why C wrong: Currant jelly stool suggests intussusception.  Why D wrong: Permanent absence of bowel movements is not the IBS pattern."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"An important reassurance to give parents and a child regarding IBS is that the problem is:","options":["Self-limiting and intermittent and will resolve","Always progressive and fatal","Contagious","Requiring lifelong surgery"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reassure the parents and child that the problem is self-limiting and intermittent and will resolve.  Why B wrong: The source frames it as self-limiting, not fatal.  Why C wrong: IBS is not described as contagious.  Why D wrong: Lifelong surgery is not indicated for IBS."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Imperforate anus is defined as:","options":["Incomplete development or absence of the anus in its normal position in the perineum","Narrowing of the urethra","Failure of testicular descent","Excessive keratinisation of skin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Imperforate anus is incomplete development or absence of the anus in its normal position in the perineum.  Why B wrong: Urethral narrowing is not imperforate anus.  Why C wrong: Failure of testicular descent is cryptorchidism.  Why D wrong: Excessive keratinisation describes psoriasis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Complete absence of the anus is specifically called:","options":["Anal agenesis","Anal stenosis","Imperforate membrane","Recto-vaginal fistula"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Complete absence of the anus is called anal agenesis.  Why B wrong: Anal stenosis is narrowing, not complete absence.  Why C wrong: Imperforate membrane is a covering membrane, not complete absence.  Why D wrong: A recto-vaginal fistula is an abnormal connection, not absence of the anus."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which are clinical findings of imperforate anus? (Select all that apply)","options":["Failure to pass meconium","Absence or stenosis of the anal and rectal canal","Presence of an anal membrane","External fistula to the perineum","Projectile vomiting"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical findings include failure to pass meconium, absence or stenosis of the anal and rectal canal, presence of an anal membrane, and external fistula to the perineum.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are listed clinical findings.  Why E wrong: Projectile vomiting is not a listed finding of imperforate anus."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The preferred postoperative position for a child after imperforate anus repair is:","options":["Side lying prone position","Supine flat","High Fowler's","Left lateral with hips flexed to 90 degrees"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Postoperatively, the side lying prone position is preferred.  Why B wrong: Supine flat is not the preferred stated position.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is not indicated here.  Why D wrong: Left lateral with hips flexed to 90 degrees is not the stated preferred position."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Reye's syndrome is best described as:","options":["A hepatic encephalopathy in which fatty changes occur in the liver","A fungal skin infection","An autoimmune keratinisation disorder","Failure of testicular descent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reye's syndrome is a hepatic encephalopathy in which fatty changes occur in the liver, and it affects the liver.  Why B wrong: A fungal skin infection describes ringworm/candidiasis.  Why C wrong: Autoimmune keratinisation describes psoriasis.  Why D wrong: Failure of testicular descent is cryptorchidism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The most common risk factor for Reye's syndrome is:","options":["Use of aspirin or acetylsalicylic acid in a patient with a viral infection","Use of acetaminophen","High-protein diet","Ototoxic drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common risk factor is use of aspirin or acetylsalicylic acid in a patient with a viral infection.  Why B wrong: Acetaminophen is the recommended alternative, not the risk factor.  Why C wrong: A high-protein diet is unrelated to Reye's syndrome.  Why D wrong: Ototoxic drugs relate to hearing loss, not Reye's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Aspirin or acetylsalicylate should not be given to children with a febrile illness, varicella, or influenza because it can cause:","options":["Reye's syndrome","Galactosemia","Phenylketonuria","Cryptorchidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Don't give aspirin/acetylsalicylate to children with a febrile illness, varicella, or influenza because aspirin can cause Reye's syndrome.  Why B wrong: Galactosemia is an inherited enzyme deficiency, not aspirin-induced.  Why C wrong: PKU is a genetic enzyme deficiency, not aspirin-induced.  Why D wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended testis, unrelated to aspirin."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The medicine of choice for fever/pain in a child at risk of Reye's syndrome (instead of aspirin) is:","options":["Acetaminophen","Ibuprofen","Acetylsalicylic acid","Diclofenac"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acetaminophen is the choice of medicine for Reye's syndrome (used instead of aspirin).  Why B wrong: Ibuprofen is not the stated choice.  Why C wrong: Acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin) is the very drug to avoid.  Why D wrong: Diclofenac is not mentioned as the choice."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A history of systemic viral illness such as influenza or varicella typically precedes the onset of Reye's syndrome symptoms by:","options":["4 to 7 days","1 to 2 hours","3 to 4 weeks","2 to 3 months"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: There is a history of systemic viral illness like influenza or varicella 4 to 7 days before the onset of symptoms.  Why B wrong: 1–2 hours is too short.  Why C wrong: 3–4 weeks is longer than stated.  Why D wrong: 2–3 months does not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are associated with Reye's syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Lethargy and right upper quadrant pain","Jaundice","Increased SGOT & SGPT and blood ammonia level","Fever, seizures, and neurological dysfunction","White patches in the mouth"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Reye's syndrome S/S include altered hepatic function signs such as lethargy, right upper quadrant pain, jaundice, increased SGOT & SGPT and blood ammonia level, fever, seizures, and neurological dysfunction.  Why A/B/C/D wrong: All are listed features.  Why E wrong: White patches in the mouth indicate oral thrush, not Reye's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Diagnosis of Reye's syndrome is made by which test?","options":["Liver function test (LFT)","Guthrie test","Cellophane tape test","Beutler test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis is made by liver function test (LFT).  Why B wrong: The Guthrie test diagnoses PKU.  Why C wrong: The cellophane tape test diagnoses pinworms.  Why D wrong: The Beutler test diagnoses galactosemia."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In Reye's syndrome, seizures can be prevented by:","options":["Decreasing environmental stimuli","Increasing bright light","Administering aspirin","High-protein feeding"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prevent seizures by decreasing the environmental stimuli.  Why B wrong: Increasing bright light would increase stimulation.  Why C wrong: Aspirin is the causative drug to avoid.  Why D wrong: High-protein feeding is not the seizure-prevention measure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In Reye's syndrome management, the serum ammonia level is decreased by using:","options":["Syrup lactulose or lactulose enema","Aspirin","Silver sulfadiazine","Permethrin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decrease serum ammonia level by using syrup lactulose or lactulose enema.  Why B wrong: Aspirin is contraindicated in Reye's syndrome.  Why C wrong: Silver sulfadiazine is a burn drug.  Why D wrong: Permethrin treats scabies."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"During a viral infection, which class of drugs should always be avoided (to prevent Reye's syndrome)?","options":["Aspirin or salicylate acid-like drugs","Acetaminophen","Antifungals","Anthelmintics"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Always avoid aspirin or salicylate acid-like drugs during a viral infection.  Why B wrong: Acetaminophen is the recommended alternative, not one to avoid.  Why C wrong: Antifungals are unrelated to Reye's syndrome risk.  Why D wrong: Anthelmintics are not implicated in Reye's syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Enuresis is also known as:","options":["Bed wetting","Encopresis","Enterobiasis","Cryptorchidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Enuresis is also called bed wetting.  Why B wrong: Encopresis is the inability to control defecation.  Why C wrong: Enterobiasis is pinworm infection.  Why D wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended testis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Enuresis is defined as the inability to control bladder function even when:","options":["The child's age has reached the stage when control of bladder function is usually expected","The child is under 1 year old","The child is asleep only during the day","The child has a urinary tract infection only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Enuresis is inability to control bladder function even when the child's age has reached the point when control of bladder function is usually expected.  Why B wrong: Bladder control is not expected under 1 year, so this does not define enuresis.  Why C wrong: Daytime-only sleep is not the defining criterion.  Why D wrong: Enuresis is not defined solely by a UTI."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"By approximately what age are most children aware of bladder fullness and able to control voiding?","options":["5 years","2 years","8 years","1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: At 5 years, most children are aware of bladder fullness and are able to control voiding.  Why B wrong: 2 years is earlier than the stated age.  Why C wrong: 8 years is later than stated.  Why D wrong: 1 year is too early for reliable voiding control."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Which TCA-group drug is a drug of choice for enuresis?","options":["Imipramine","Fluconazole","Theophylline","Albendazole"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The TCA group drug imipramine is a drug of choice for enuresis.  Why B wrong: Fluconazole is an antifungal.  Why C wrong: Theophylline is a methylxanthine for apnoea of infancy.  Why D wrong: Albendazole is an anthelmintic for pinworm."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Encopresis is defined as the inability to control:","options":["Defecation","Bladder function","Breathing","Body temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Encopresis is the inability to control defecation.  Why B wrong: Inability to control bladder function is enuresis.  Why C wrong: Inability to control breathing relates to apnoea.  Why D wrong: Temperature control is unrelated to encopresis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Cryptorchidism is defined as:","options":["Failure in the descending of the testes","Abnormal urethral opening","Inability to retract the foreskin","Exposure of the urinary bladder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Failure in the descending of the testes is called cryptorchidism.  Why B wrong: Abnormal urethral opening describes epispadias/hypospadias.  Why C wrong: Inability to retract foreskin describes phimosis/paraphimosis.  Why D wrong: Exposure of the urinary bladder describes exstrophy of the bladder."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"During physical examination of a child with cryptorchidism, the scrotal sac feels:","options":["Empty","Enlarged and firm","Fluid-filled","Tender and red"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: During physical examination, the scrotal sac feels empty in cryptorchidism.  Why B wrong: An enlarged firm scrotum is not the described finding.  Why C wrong: A fluid-filled scrotum suggests hydrocele, not cryptorchidism.  Why D wrong: Tender and red suggests infection/torsion, not the described empty sac."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The surgical procedure of choice to correct cryptorchidism is:","options":["Orchidopexy","Orchidectomy","Circumcision","Fasciotomy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Orchidopexy is the choice of surgery to correct cryptorchidism (surgical management of cryptorchidism).  Why B wrong: Orchidectomy is the surgical management of testicular cancer.  Why C wrong: Circumcision addresses the foreskin (e.g., phimosis).  Why D wrong: Fasciotomy relieves compartment syndrome in burns."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Orchidectomy is the surgical management of:","options":["Testicular cancer","Cryptorchidism","Phimosis","Enuresis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The note states orchidectomy is surgical management of testicular cancer.  Why B wrong: Cryptorchidism is managed by orchidopexy, not orchidectomy.  Why C wrong: Phimosis may be managed by circumcision.  Why D wrong: Enuresis is managed medically (e.g., imipramine)."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"To provide a favourable environment for spermatogenesis, the scrotal temperature normally remains:","options":["3°C below body temperature","Equal to body temperature","3°C above body temperature","10°C below body temperature"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Scrotal temperature always remains 3°C below body temperature to give a favourable environment for spermatogenesis.  Why B wrong: Equal temperature would not provide the cooler environment needed.  Why C wrong: Above body temperature would impair spermatogenesis.  Why D wrong: 10°C below is not the stated value."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Epispadias is defined as an abnormal urethral opening on which surface of the penis?","options":["Dorsal surface (above)","Ventral surface (below)","Lateral surface","Tip only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Epispadias is an abnormal urethral opening on the dorsal surface (above) of the penis.  Why B wrong: The ventral (below) surface opening is hypospadias.  Why C wrong: A lateral opening is not the described condition.  Why D wrong: The tip is the normal position, not epispadias."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Hypospadias is defined as an abnormal urethral opening on which surface of the penis?","options":["Ventral surface (below)","Dorsal surface (above)","Lateral surface","Base of the scrotum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypospadias is an abnormal urethral opening on the ventral surface (below) of the penis.  Why B wrong: The dorsal (above) surface opening is epispadias.  Why C wrong: A lateral opening is not described.  Why D wrong: The base of the scrotum is not the defining location."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"According to the source's mnemonic for hypospadias and epispadias, the prefixes mean:","options":["\"hypo\" means below and \"epi\" means above","\"hypo\" means above and \"epi\" means below","Both mean below","Both mean above"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic is that the prefix \"hypo\" means below and \"epi\" means above.  Why B wrong: The meanings are reversed.  Why C wrong: The two prefixes have opposite meanings, not both below.  Why D wrong: They are opposite, not both above."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Phimosis is defined as:","options":["Failure of the foreskin to retract over the shaft of the penis","Inability to return a retracted foreskin to its anatomical position","An abnormal urethral opening","Failure of testicular descent"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phimosis is failure of the foreskin to retract over the shaft of the penis.  Why B wrong: Inability to return a retracted foreskin is paraphimosis.  Why C wrong: An abnormal urethral opening is epispadias/hypospadias.  Why D wrong: Failure of testicular descent is cryptorchidism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A retracted foreskin is left behind the glans and cannot be pulled forward; the glans is now swollen and painful. This condition and its urgency are:","options":["Paraphimosis — a urologic emergency requiring prompt reduction","Phimosis — a routine finding needing no intervention","Hypospadias — a congenital anomaly for elective repair","Epispadias — corrected only in adulthood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paraphimosis is a retracted foreskin that cannot be returned over the glans, causing venous congestion and swelling; it is a urologic EMERGENCY (risk of glans ischaemia) needing prompt manual reduction.  Why B wrong: Phimosis is a non-retractable foreskin (opposite mechanism) and is usually not emergent.  Why C wrong: Hypospadias is a ventral urethral opening, unrelated to this scenario.  Why D wrong: Epispadias is a dorsal urethral opening, not this scenario."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"If epispadias and hypospadias are present along with phimosis, why should the circumcision procedure be postponed?","options":["Because the foreskin skin can be used for reconstruction of the urethra","Because circumcision cures hypospadias","Because the child is too young for anaesthesia","Because circumcision worsens cryptorchidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: If epispadias and hypospadias are present with phimosis, circumcision should be postponed because the skin can be used for reconstruction of the urethra.  Why B wrong: Circumcision does not cure hypospadias.  Why C wrong: The stated reason is skin preservation for reconstruction, not anaesthesia age.  Why D wrong: The reason concerns urethral reconstruction, not cryptorchidism."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"Exstrophy of the bladder is defined as:","options":["Exposure of the urinary bladder to the environment due to an abnormal opening in the lower abdominal wall","Failure of the foreskin to retract","An abnormal urethral opening on the dorsal penis","Narrowing of the anal canal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Exstrophy of the bladder is exposure of the urinary bladder to the environment due to an abnormal opening in the lower abdominal wall.  Why B wrong: Failure of the foreskin to retract is phimosis.  Why C wrong: A dorsal urethral opening is epispadias.  Why D wrong: Narrowing of the anal canal is anal stenosis."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"In the management of bladder exstrophy, the exposed defect should be covered with:","options":["A non-adhering dressing to prevent dehydration, infection, and injury","A dry gauze taped tightly","An alcohol-soaked pad","Silver sulfadiazine cream"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cover the defect with a non-adhering dressing to prevent dehydration, infection, and injury.  Why B wrong: A tightly taped dry gauze could adhere and injure the exposed bladder.  Why C wrong: An alcohol-soaked pad would irritate and damage the exposed mucosa.  Why D wrong: Silver sulfadiazine is a burn dressing, not the specified management."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"The recommended position for an infant with bladder exstrophy is:","options":["Supine position","Prone position","Knee-chest position","Left lateral position"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Provide the supine position for bladder exstrophy (AIIMS Rai Bareily-2018).  Why B wrong: The prone position is not the stated recommendation and could injure the exposed bladder.  Why C wrong: The knee-chest position is used for colic cramping, not exstrophy.  Why D wrong: The left lateral position is not the specified recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A nurse is teaching parents of a preterm infant on a home apnoea monitor. Which infant finding is consistent with apnoea of infancy and warrants intervention?","options":["A pause in breathing lasting at least 20 seconds, possibly with bradycardia and cyanosis","Periodic breathing pauses of 5 seconds with a normal heart rate and colour","Rapid breathing at 60 breaths/min with pink colour","A brief 15-second pause with no colour change in an adult"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apnoea of infancy is a cessation of breathing of at least 20 seconds or more, and may occur with bradycardia and cyanosis, most commonly in preterm babies.  Why B wrong: A 5-second pause with normal heart rate/colour is below the 20-second infant threshold.  Why C wrong: Rapid breathing with pink colour is not apnoea.  Why D wrong: A 15-second pause applies to older children and adults, not the infant definition."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A child with a burn is prescribed fluids by the Parkland formula and is in the first 8 hours post-burn. Which is the priority reason for giving half the calculated fluid during this period?","options":["To prevent hypovolemic shock","To prevent septic shock","To promote wound healing","To prevent contractures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Half of the fluid should be administered in the first 8 hours to prevent hypovolemic shock, which is the primary cause of death in the first 24–48 hours.  Why B wrong: Septic shock is the concern after 48 hours, not the reason for early front-loaded fluids.  Why C wrong: Wound healing is the priority in the active phase/after 48 hours, not the resuscitative reason.  Why D wrong: Contracture prevention is a later rehabilitative goal, not the reason for early fluids."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A nurse notes a postoperative tonsillectomy child is swallowing frequently and appears restless. What is the priority interpretation?","options":["This is a sign of haemorrhage and requires immediate assessment","This is normal and needs no action","The child is simply hungry","The child needs a red-coloured drink"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frequent swallowing is the classical sign of haemorrhage after tonsillectomy; with restlessness it warrants immediate assessment given the first-24-hour bleeding risk.  Why B wrong: Frequent swallowing is not a normal finding; it signals possible bleeding.  Why C wrong: Hunger does not explain frequent swallowing plus restlessness in this context.  Why D wrong: Red-coloured drinks are specifically avoided as they can mask/mimic blood."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A newborn has not passed meconium, has a greenish membrane over the anal opening, and X-ray shows Wangensteen & Rice findings. Which condition is most likely?","options":["Rectal agenesis (a type of ARM)","Pyloric stenosis","Reye's syndrome","Cryptorchidism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Failure to pass meconium, greenish membrane over the anal opening, and Wangensteen & Rice X-ray findings are diagnostic of rectal agenesis/ARM.  Why B wrong: Pyloric stenosis presents with projectile vomiting, not failure to pass meconium.  Why C wrong: Reye's syndrome is a hepatic encephalopathy, unrelated to meconium passage.  Why D wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended testis, not an anorectal finding."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A mother reports her febrile child with chickenpox was given aspirin. The nurse recognizes the child is at risk for which condition, and which drug should have been used instead?","options":["Reye's syndrome; acetaminophen","Galactosemia; lactulose","Phenylketonuria; imipramine","Scabies; permethrin"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aspirin in a child with varicella/febrile illness risks Reye's syndrome; acetaminophen is the choice of medicine instead.  Why B wrong: Galactosemia is inherited and unrelated to aspirin; lactulose lowers ammonia in Reye's but is not the substitute analgesic.  Why C wrong: PKU is genetic; imipramine is for enuresis.  Why D wrong: Scabies/permethrin is unrelated to aspirin exposure."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A nurse is instilling ear drops in a 2-year-old with acute otitis media. Which action is correct?","options":["Pull the pinna downward and backward","Pull the pinna upward and backward","Pull the pinna straight up","Do not manipulate the pinna at all"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For a child <3 years of age, pull the pinna downward and backward.  Why B wrong: Upward and backward is used for children >3 years of age.  Why C wrong: Pulling straight up is not the described technique.  Why D wrong: The pinna must be positioned to straighten the canal for correct instillation."},{"section":"Batch 013 · Burn injury complications & nursing care, grafts…","question":"A school nurse suspects pinworm in a child with nighttime anal itching and insomnia. Which specimen collection instruction is correct?","options":["Apply cellophane tape near the anus in the morning before a bowel movement","Collect a stool sample at night after a bowel movement","Perform a Guthrie heel-stick blood test","Perform a Beutler blood test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The cellophane tape test collects a sample from near the anus in the morning before bowel movement, because the female lays eggs on the perineal skin at night.  Why B wrong: Collection is done in the morning before bowel movement, not at night after defecation.  Why C wrong: The Guthrie test diagnoses PKU, not pinworm.  Why D wrong: The Beutler test diagnoses galactosemia, not pinworm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In which congenital anomaly of the male urethra is the urethral opening located on the ventral (under) surface of the penis?","options":["Epispadias","Hypospadias","Phimosis","Cryptorchidism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In hypospadias the urethral meatus opens on the ventral/underside of the penis rather than at the tip, as contrasted with the normal figure.  Why A wrong: Epispadias has the opening on the dorsal (upper) surface.  Why C wrong: Phimosis is inability to retract the foreskin, not an abnormal meatal position.  Why D wrong: Cryptorchidism is undescended testis, unrelated to the urethral opening."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A newborn boy has the urethral opening on the dorsal (upper/top) surface of the penis. This defect is termed:","options":["Hypospadias","Chordee","Epispadias","Paraphimosis"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Epispadias is defined by a urethral opening on the dorsal/top surface of the penis.  Why A wrong: Hypospadias places the opening on the ventral surface.  Why B wrong: Chordee is a ventral curvature often seen with hypospadias, not a meatal-position term.  Why D wrong: Paraphimosis is entrapment of retracted foreskin behind the glans."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In a normal male infant, the urethral opening is located at:","options":["The tip of the glans penis","The ventral shaft","The dorsal shaft","The scrotum"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The normal figure shows the urethral opening at the tip of the glans penis.  Why B wrong: A ventral opening indicates hypospadias.  Why C wrong: A dorsal opening indicates epispadias.  Why D wrong: A scrotal opening would be a severe (perineal) hypospadias, not normal anatomy."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Wilms tumor is a malignancy arising from which organ?","options":["Liver","Kidney","Adrenal medulla","Bone marrow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wilms tumor is a tumor of the kidney (nephroblastoma).  Why A wrong: Hepatoblastoma is the liver tumor of childhood.  Why C wrong: Neuroblastoma arises from the adrenal medulla/sympathetic chain.  Why D wrong: Leukemia arises from bone marrow."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Wilms tumor is also known by which other name?","options":["Retinoblastoma","Nephroblastoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma","Osteosarcoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Wilms tumor is synonymous with nephroblastoma.  Why A wrong: Retinoblastoma is a malignant tumor of the retina.  Why C wrong: Rhabdomyosarcoma is a soft-tissue/muscle tumor.  Why D wrong: Osteosarcoma is a malignant bone tumor."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Wilms tumor is described as a capsulated tumor. What is the significance of its capsule?","options":["It prevents any spread of the tumor permanently","Metastasis can occur after rupture of the capsule","The capsule secretes tumor markers","It makes the tumor benign"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states it is a capsulated tumor but metastasis can occur after rupture of the capsule.  Why A wrong: The capsule limits spread only until it ruptures, after which metastasis occurs.  Why C wrong: The capsule is a physical boundary, not a secretory structure.  Why D wrong: Wilms tumor is malignant regardless of the capsule."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are recognized clinical features of Wilms tumor? (Select all that apply)","options":["Abdominal pain","Haematuria","Anaemia","Hypertension","Jaundice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists abdominal pain, haematuria, anaemia and hypertension as the clinical features.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly named clinical features.  Why E wrong: Jaundice is not listed as a feature of Wilms tumor."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which imaging investigations are used to diagnose Wilms tumor?","options":["USG, CT-Scan and MRI","X-ray only","Bone scan only","PET-CT only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis is done by USG, CT-Scan and MRI per the source.  Why B wrong: Plain X-ray alone is not the stated diagnostic method.  Why C wrong: Bone scan is not listed.  Why D wrong: PET-CT alone is not listed as the diagnostic approach."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Why are renal biopsy and abdominal palpation contraindicated in a child with Wilms tumor? (AIIMS, JIPMER)","options":["They cause severe pain","They can rupture the capsule (and cause spread)","They are too expensive","They cause allergic reactions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states renal biopsy and abdominal palpation are contraindicated because they can rupture the capsule.  Why A wrong: Pain is not the reason given.  Why C wrong: Cost is irrelevant to the contraindication.  Why D wrong: Allergy is not the concern; capsule rupture and tumor spread are."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In a preoperative child with Wilms tumor, abdominal girth should be measured at least once a day primarily to detect:","options":["Nutritional status","A surgical emergency due to rupture of the tumor","Fluid overload","Bowel obstruction"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states abdominal girth is measured at least once a day before surgery to detect a surgical emergency due to rupture of the tumour.  Why A wrong: Girth is not used here for nutrition assessment.  Why C wrong: Fluid overload is not the stated purpose.  Why D wrong: Detecting tumor rupture, not bowel obstruction, is the goal."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Marfan syndrome is primarily a disorder of which body system?","options":["Connective tissue of the skeletal system","Cardiac conduction system","Endocrine system","Hematologic system"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes Marfan as a connective system disorder of the skeletal system.  Why B wrong: Although cardiac complications occur, the source classifies it as a connective/skeletal system disorder.  Why C wrong: It is not an endocrine disorder.  Why D wrong: It is not a hematologic disorder."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Marfan syndrome is caused by defects in which gene?","options":["Dystrophin gene","Fibrillin-1 gene","CFTR gene","HBB gene"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Marfan is caused by defects in the fibrillin-1 gene.  Why A wrong: Dystrophin defects cause Duchenne muscular dystrophy.  Why C wrong: CFTR defects cause cystic fibrosis.  Why D wrong: HBB defects cause sickle cell/thalassemia."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are common clinical features of Marfan syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Tall and thin body structure","Slender fingers","Long arms and legs","Curvature of the spine","Short stature"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists tall/thin build, slender fingers, long arms and legs, and spinal curvature.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All four are explicitly named.  Why E wrong: Marfan causes tall stature, not short stature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Eczema is an allergic skin condition also known by which name(s)?","options":["Contact dermatitis or atopic dermatitis","Impetigo","Cellulitis","Tinea corporis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states eczema is an allergic condition also known as contact dermatitis or atopic dermatitis.  Why B wrong: Impetigo is a bacterial skin infection.  Why C wrong: Cellulitis is a bacterial soft-tissue infection.  Why D wrong: Tinea corporis is a fungal (ringworm) infection."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Eczema initially affects which layer of the skin?","options":["Dermis","Epidermis","Subcutaneous fat","Fascia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states eczema initially affects the epidermis layer of the skin.  Why A wrong: The dermis is the deeper layer, not the initially affected one.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous fat is not the initially affected layer.  Why D wrong: Fascia is deep connective tissue, not involved initially."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which signs and symptoms are characteristic of eczema? (Select all that apply)","options":["Erythema","Pruritus","Papular lesions that may progress to vesicles","Weeping and crusting lesions","Painless hard nodules"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists erythema, pruritus, papular lesions progressing to vesicles, and weeping/crusting lesions.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly named signs and symptoms.  Why E wrong: Painless hard nodules are not a feature of eczema."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Specific nursing care for a child with eczema includes:","options":["Applying highly toxic soap and shampoo","Avoiding allergic factors and highly toxic soap/shampoo","Frequent hot-water scrubbing of lesions","Exposing lesions to strong sunlight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends avoiding allergic factors and highly toxic soap, shampoo, etc.  Why A wrong: Toxic soaps/shampoos should be avoided, not applied.  Why C wrong: Hot-water scrubbing worsens eczema.  Why D wrong: Strong sun exposure is not the recommended care."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Pediculosis refers to:","options":["Fungal infection of the nails","Lice infestation on different parts of the body","A viral skin rash","Bacterial folliculitis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines pediculosis as lice infestation on different parts of the body.  Why A wrong: Nail fungus is onychomycosis.  Why C wrong: It is a parasitic (lice) infestation, not viral.  Why D wrong: Folliculitis is bacterial, not lice-related."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the incubation period of nits in pediculosis?","options":["1-3 days","7-10 days","14-21 days","30-45 days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the incubation period of nits is 7-10 days.  Why A wrong: 1-3 days is too short per the source.  Why C wrong: 14-21 days exceeds the stated period.  Why D wrong: 30-45 days is far longer than stated."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In pediculosis, what are \"nits\"?","options":["Adult female lice","White eggs attached to the shaft of the hair","Bite marks on the skin","Skin scales shed by lice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes nits as the minor form of lice — white eggs attached to the shaft of the hair.  Why A wrong: Nits are eggs, not adult lice.  Why C wrong: Bite marks are not nits.  Why D wrong: Nits are eggs, not skin scales."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Pediculosis affecting the scalp is termed:","options":["Pediculosis capitis","Pediculosis corporis","Pediculosis cruris","Pediculosis pubis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pediculosis capitis is present on the scalp.  Why B wrong: Corporis affects the whole body.  Why C wrong: Cruris affects the groin region.  Why D wrong: Pubis is not the term used in the source for the scalp type."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Pediculosis corporis refers to lice infestation of the:","options":["Scalp only","Whole body","Groin region only","Eyelashes"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pediculosis corporis is present on the whole body.  Why A wrong: Scalp-only involvement is capitis.  Why C wrong: Groin-only involvement is cruris.  Why D wrong: Eyelash involvement is not the corporis type."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Pediculosis cruris affects which region of the body?","options":["Scalp","Groin region","Trunk","Face"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states pediculosis cruris is present on the groin region.  Why A wrong: The scalp type is capitis.  Why C wrong: Whole-body/trunk type is corporis.  Why D wrong: The face is not the cruris region."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Specific nursing care for pediculosis includes recommending which medicated shampoo?","options":["Ketoconazole shampoo","Permethrin shampoo","Selenium sulfide shampoo","Coal tar shampoo"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends permethrin shampoo and avoiding contact/sharing of combs and towels.  Why A wrong: Ketoconazole is an antifungal, not for lice.  Why C wrong: Selenium sulfide is used for dandruff/tinea versicolor.  Why D wrong: Coal tar is used for psoriasis/seborrhea, not lice."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"To prevent spread of pediculosis, the child should avoid:","options":["Drinking cold water","Sharing combs and towels and close contact with other persons","Eating spicy food","Sunlight exposure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source advises avoiding contact with other persons and sharing of combs and towels.  Why A wrong: Water intake is unrelated to lice spread.  Why C wrong: Food does not spread lice.  Why D wrong: Sunlight does not spread lice."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which clinical sign, in which a plaque bleeds at pinpoint spots when its scale is scraped off, is characteristic of psoriasis?","options":["Auspitz sign","Nikolsky sign","Koebner phenomenon","Darier sign"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Auspitz sign — pinpoint bleeding after removal of the silvery scale of a psoriatic plaque — is characteristic of psoriasis (a non-infectious autoimmune disorder with excessive keratinisation).  Why B wrong: Nikolsky sign (epidermal shearing with pressure) is seen in pemphigus and SSSS/TEN, not psoriasis.  Why C wrong: The Koebner phenomenon (lesions at sites of trauma) occurs in psoriasis but is not the pinpoint-bleeding scraping sign asked here.  Why D wrong: Darier sign (wheal on stroking) is seen in urticaria pigmentosa/mastocytosis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of psoriasis? (AIIMS Rishikesh-2018)","options":["Honey-coloured crusts","Dry silvery white scaly skin lesions","Target-shaped lesions","Fluid-filled bullae"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source names dry silvery white scaly skin lesions as the cardinal sign, along with pruritus.  Why A wrong: Honey-coloured crusts are seen in impetigo.  Why C wrong: Target lesions occur in erythema multiforme.  Why D wrong: Bullae are seen in bullous disorders, not as the psoriasis cardinal sign."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are included in the management of psoriasis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Steroids","UV radiation to decrease keratinization","Immunosuppressant drugs","Balneotherapy","Broad-spectrum oral antibiotics as first-line"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists steroids, UV radiation (decreases keratinization), immunosuppressant drugs, and balneotherapy.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed management options.  Why E wrong: Psoriasis is non-infectious, so first-line antibiotics are not part of the listed management."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In psoriasis management, balneotherapy refers to:","options":["Application of topical steroids","Bathing in water containing sulphur and silica","Oral immunosuppressant therapy","Phototherapy with UV light"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines balneotherapy as bathing in water containing sulphur silica.  Why A wrong: Steroids are a separate management item.  Why C wrong: Immunosuppressants are a separate management item.  Why D wrong: UV radiation is listed separately from balneotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Down syndrome is also known as:","options":["Trisomy-18","Trisomy-21","Trisomy-13","Monosomy X"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Down syndrome is also known as Trisomy-21.  Why A wrong: Trisomy-18 is Edward's syndrome.  Why C wrong: Trisomy-13 is Patau syndrome.  Why D wrong: Monosomy X is Turner syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Down syndrome results from an abnormality of which chromosome?","options":["Chromosome 18","Chromosome 21","Chromosome 13","Sex chromosome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Down syndrome is an abnormality in chromosome-21.  Why A wrong: Chromosome 18 abnormality is Edward's syndrome.  Why C wrong: Chromosome 13 abnormality is Patau syndrome.  Why D wrong: Down syndrome is autosomal (chromosome 21), not a sex-chromosome disorder."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of Down syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Flattened face with depressed nasal bridge (saddle nose)","Almond-shaped eyes and low-set ears with short neck","Brachycephaly or flat occiput","Small hands and feet with poor muscle tone and short stature","Prominent occiput and micrognathia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists flattened face/depressed nasal bridge, almond-shaped eyes, low-set ears, short neck, brachycephaly/flat occiput, small hands and feet, poor muscle tone and short stature.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed Down syndrome features.  Why E wrong: Prominent occiput and micrognathia are features of Edward's syndrome, not Down syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In Down syndrome, the skull shape is typically described as:","options":["Dolichocephaly (long head)","Brachycephaly or flat occiput","Prominent occiput","Scaphocephaly"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists brachycephaly or flat occiput as a Down syndrome feature.  Why A wrong: Dolichocephaly is not the described shape.  Why C wrong: A prominent occiput is an Edward's syndrome feature.  Why D wrong: Scaphocephaly is not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Edward's syndrome is also known as:","options":["Trisomy-21","Trisomy-18","Trisomy-13","Gonadal dysgenesis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Edward's syndrome is also known as Trisomy-18.  Why A wrong: Trisomy-21 is Down syndrome.  Why C wrong: Trisomy-13 is Patau syndrome.  Why D wrong: Gonadal dysgenesis is Turner syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Edward's syndrome involves an abnormality of which chromosome?","options":["Chromosome 21","Chromosome 18","Chromosome 13","Chromosome 15"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Edward's syndrome is an abnormality in chromosome 18.  Why A wrong: Chromosome 21 is Down syndrome.  Why C wrong: Chromosome 13 is Patau syndrome.  Why D wrong: Chromosome 15 is not the affected chromosome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of Edward's syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Deformed low-set ears","Small jaw (micrognathia)","Prominent occiput","Short survival; if survival occurs, severe mental retardation","Flat occiput and saddle nose"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists deformed low-set ears, micrognathia, prominent occiput, and short survival with severe mental retardation.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed Edward's features.  Why E wrong: Flat occiput and saddle nose are Down syndrome features, not Edward's."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A small jaw seen in Edward's syndrome is medically termed:","options":["Macrognathia","Micrognathia","Prognathism","Retrognathia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source names micrognathia (small jaw) as an Edward's feature.  Why A wrong: Macrognathia is an abnormally large jaw.  Why C wrong: Prognathism is a protruding jaw.  Why D wrong: The source specifically uses \"micrognathia.\""},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Turner syndrome is also known as:","options":["Gonadal dysgenesis","Trisomy-18","Klinefelter variant","Fragile X syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Turner syndrome is also known as Gonadal Dysgenesis.  Why B wrong: Trisomy-18 is Edward's syndrome.  Why C wrong: Klinefelter is a distinct male sex-chromosome disorder.  Why D wrong: Fragile X is a different X-linked disorder."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which sex is affected in Turner syndrome, and what is its karyotype? (PGI-2018)","options":["Only males; 44+XXY","Only females; 44+XO","Both sexes; 46,XX","Only females; 44+XXX"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states only females are affected and the karyotype is 44+XO (one sex chromosome lapsed).  Why A wrong: 44+XXY (Klinefelter) affects only males.  Why C wrong: 46,XX is normal female karyotype.  Why D wrong: The source specifies 44+XO, not 44+XXX."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are signs of Turner syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Webbed or short neck","Short stature (most common)","Developmental failure of secondary sex characteristics","Ovarian agenesis","Gynecomastia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists webbed/short neck, short stature (most common), failure of secondary sex characteristics, and ovarian agenesis.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed Turner features.  Why E wrong: Gynecomastia is a Klinefelter (male) feature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common physical finding in Turner syndrome?","options":["Tall stature","Short stature","Long arms","Gynecomastia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source identifies short stature as the most common feature of Turner syndrome.  Why A wrong: Turner causes short, not tall, stature.  Why C wrong: Long arms are a Klinefelter/Marfan feature, not Turner.  Why D wrong: Gynecomastia is a male (Klinefelter) feature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the karyotype of Klinefelter syndrome?","options":["44+XO","44+XXY","47,XX+21","45,X"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the karyotype of Klinefelter syndrome is 44+XXY (an extra sex chromosome).  Why A wrong: 44+XO is Turner syndrome.  Why C wrong: 47,XX+21 corresponds to Down syndrome.  Why D wrong: 45,X is Turner syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Klinefelter syndrome affects which sex? (AIIMS Rai Bareilly-2018)","options":["Only females","Only males","Both sexes equally","Neither sex (autosomal)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states only males are affected in Klinefelter syndrome.  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome affects only females.  Why C wrong: Klinefelter is specific to males.  Why D wrong: Klinefelter is a sex-chromosome (not autosomal) disorder."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which of the following are signs and symptoms of Klinefelter syndrome? (Select all that apply)","options":["Slightly taller than average with long legs and arms","Small, firm testes and cryptorchidism","Delayed puberty and infertility","Gynecomastia (breast growth) and inadequately developed secondary sex characteristics","Ovarian agenesis"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists tall build with long limbs, small firm testes with cryptorchidism, delayed puberty/infertility, and gynecomastia with poorly developed secondary sex characteristics.  Why A/B/C/D correct: All are explicitly listed Klinefelter features.  Why E wrong: Ovarian agenesis is a Turner (female) feature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In Klinefelter syndrome, breast growth in the male is termed:","options":["Cryptorchidism","Gynecomastia","Hypospadias","Micrognathia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists breast growth (gynecomastia) as a Klinefelter feature.  Why A wrong: Cryptorchidism is an undescended testis.  Why C wrong: Hypospadias is an abnormal urethral opening.  Why D wrong: Micrognathia is a small jaw (Edward's feature)."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Breath-holding spells in a child are characterized by breathing that stops suddenly for approximately:","options":["10 seconds","30 seconds","1 minute","5 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states breath-holding spells is a condition in which a child's breathing stopped suddenly for 1 minute.  Why A wrong: 10 seconds is shorter than stated.  Why B wrong: 30 seconds is shorter than stated.  Why D wrong: 5 minutes exceeds the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Thumb sucking in a young child is best described as:","options":["A normal self-soothing habit up to about 4 years of age","Always pathological and requiring immediate intervention at any age","A sign of intellectual disability","Onychophagia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Digit/thumb sucking is a normal self-comforting behaviour in infancy and early childhood; intervention is generally considered only if it persists beyond ~4 years or affects dentition. [Corrected: the source's term \"onychophagy\" for thumb sucking is wrong — onychophagy/onychophagia means nail biting.]  Why B wrong: It is a normal habit in early childhood, not always pathological.  Why C wrong: It is not a marker of intellectual disability.  Why D wrong: Onychophagia means nail biting, not thumb sucking."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Nail biting is also called:","options":["Bruxism","Onychophagia","Onychomycosis","Onycholysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nail biting is onychophagia (onychophagy is a variant spelling of the same term).  Why A wrong: Bruxism is teeth grinding/clenching, not nail biting.  Why C wrong: Onychomycosis is a fungal nail infection.  Why D wrong: Onycholysis is separation of the nail from its bed."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A toddler who screams, kicks, and throws himself on the ground to get a toy is displaying a temper tantrum. According to the source, this behaviour is:","options":["Always pathological and needs medication","A normal part of development","A sign of autism","A seizure disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes temper tantrum as a prolonged anger reaction in a toddler that is a normal part of development.  Why A wrong: The source states it is normal, not pathological.  Why C wrong: It is not described as a sign of autism.  Why D wrong: A tantrum is a behavioural, not a seizure, event."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Stuttering/stammering, a speech disorder with involuntary repetitions, prolongations, and silent blocks, is also known as:","options":["Dysphagia","Dysphemia","Dyslexia","Dysarthria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The correct medical synonym for stuttering/stammering is dysphemia (also called balbuties). [Corrected: the source's \"dysphonia\" is wrong — dysphonia is a voice/phonation disorder.]  Why A wrong: Dysphagia is difficulty swallowing.  Why C wrong: Dyslexia is a reading disorder.  Why D wrong: Dysarthria is a neuromuscular motor-speech disorder (slurred speech), not stuttering."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"PICA is defined as:","options":["Repeated or chronic ingestion of non-food substances such as clay, charcoal, and soil","Refusal to eat any solid food","Excessive intake of high-calorie food","Fear of swallowing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines PICA as repeated/chronic ingestion of non-food substances such as clay, charcoal, and soil.  Why B wrong: Refusal of solids is not PICA.  Why C wrong: Overeating calorie-rich food is not PICA.  Why D wrong: Fear of swallowing (phagophobia) is not PICA."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A child who chronically eats non-food items (PICA) is at risk of developing which condition? (NHM, MP Staff Nurse-2022)","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Hemophilia","Cystic fibrosis","Rickets"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if a child chronically eats non-food items, iron deficiency anaemia will occur.  Why B wrong: Hemophilia is an inherited bleeding disorder, unrelated to PICA.  Why C wrong: Cystic fibrosis is a genetic disorder.  Why D wrong: Rickets is due to vitamin D deficiency, not PICA."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the drug of choice for Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD)?","options":["Diazepam","Methylphenidate","Haloperidol","Fluoxetine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the drug of choice for ADHD is Methylphenidate.  Why A wrong: Diazepam is a benzodiazepine, not first-line for ADHD.  Why C wrong: Haloperidol is an antipsychotic.  Why D wrong: Fluoxetine is an SSRI antidepressant."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"ADHD is best described as a child psychiatric disorder characterized by which core features?","options":["Inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity","Repetitive stereotyped movements only","Loss of previously acquired milestones","Recurrent seizures"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states ADHD affects normal behaviour and impairs the ability to concentrate, with inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity.  Why B wrong: Stereotyped movements alone describe other disorders.  Why C wrong: Loss of milestones suggests a regressive/degenerative condition.  Why D wrong: Seizures are not the defining feature of ADHD."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which type of anaemia is macrocytic, meaning large-sized RBCs are present in the blood?","options":["Iron deficiency anaemia","Megaloblastic or pernicious anaemia","Sickle cell anaemia","Aplastic anaemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states megaloblastic or pernicious anaemia is macrocytic (large-sized RBCs).  Why A wrong: Iron deficiency anaemia is microcytic.  Why C wrong: Sickle cell anaemia produces sickled, not enlarged, cells.  Why D wrong: Aplastic anaemia is a marrow failure with normocytic cells."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Under the IMNCI/WHO scheme, which finding indicates SOME dehydration in a child with diarrhoea?","options":["Lethargic or unconscious, sunken eyes, skin pinch goes back very slowly (>2 s)","Restless/irritable, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly/thirsty, skin pinch goes back slowly","Not enough signs to classify as some or severe dehydration","Bloody stool with high fever"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Some dehydration\" = two or more of: restless/irritable, sunken eyes, drinks eagerly (thirsty), skin pinch goes back slowly → treat with ORS Plan B.  Why A wrong: Lethargy/unconsciousness with a very slow (>2 s) skin pinch indicates SEVERE dehydration (Plan C), not some.  Why C wrong: That describes \"no dehydration\" (Plan A).  Why D wrong: Bloody stool defines dysentery, not a dehydration category."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common bacterial cause of diarrhoea in children?","options":["E. coli","Rotavirus","Salmonella","Vibrio cholerae"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states E. coli is the most common bacterial cause of diarrhoea in children.  Why B wrong: Rotavirus is viral, not bacterial.  Why C wrong: Salmonella is a cause but not identified as the most common bacterial one.  Why D wrong: Vibrio cholerae causes cholera but is not the most common bacterial cause."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Rice-water stool is characteristically seen in which disease?","options":["Cholera","Dysentery","Typhoid","Rotavirus infection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states rice-water stool is seen in cholera.  Why B wrong: Dysentery produces bloody, mucoid stool.  Why C wrong: Typhoid classically produces pea-soup stool.  Why D wrong: Rotavirus produces watery diarrhoea but rice-water stool is specific to cholera."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common type of cancer in children?","options":["Wilms tumor","Acute lymphocytic leukemia (ALL)","Neuroblastoma","Retinoblastoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common childhood cancer is Acute Lymphocytic Leukemia (ALL).  Why A wrong: Wilms tumor is a common renal tumor but not the most common childhood cancer.  Why C wrong: Neuroblastoma is a common solid tumor but not the most common overall.  Why D wrong: Retinoblastoma is much rarer."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":["Painless cervical and mediastinal lymphadenopathy","Painful generalized lymphadenopathy","Splenic rupture","Massive haematuria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cardinal sign is painless cervical and mediastinal lymphadenopathy.  Why B wrong: The lymphadenopathy is painless, not painful.  Why C wrong: Splenic rupture is not the cardinal sign.  Why D wrong: Haematuria is not a Hodgkin's feature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which cell is the tumor marker associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma?","options":["Reed-Sternberg cells","Auer rods","Downey cells","Sézary cells"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Red Stern Berg Cells,\" which is the misspelling of Reed-Sternberg cells, the hallmark cells of Hodgkin's lymphoma.  Why B wrong: Auer rods are seen in acute myeloid leukemia.  Why C wrong: Downey cells are atypical lymphocytes in infectious mononucleosis.  Why D wrong: Sézary cells are seen in cutaneous T-cell lymphoma."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Cystic fibrosis is inherited in which pattern?","options":["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive disorder.  Why A wrong: It is recessive, not dominant.  Why C wrong: It is autosomal, not X-linked.  Why D wrong: It is not mitochondrially inherited."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"According to the \"Rule of Palm\" for burn assessment, the patient's own palm represents approximately what percentage of total body surface area?","options":["0.5%","1%","5%","9%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Rule of Palm considers the patient's own palm as 1% of the burn area.  Why A wrong: 0.5% is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 5% is not the palm rule value.  Why D wrong: 9% corresponds to the Rule of Nines regions, not the palm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A baby typically attains three times its birth weight by what age?","options":["6 months","1 year","2 years","3 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the baby attains three times its birth weight by one year of age.  Why A wrong: By 6 months the weight roughly doubles, not triples.  Why C wrong: By 2 years it is four times birth weight.  Why D wrong: By 3 years it is five times birth weight."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"By two years of age, a child's weight is approximately how many times the birth weight?","options":["Two times","Three times","Four times","Five times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the baby attains four times its birth weight by two years of age.  Why A wrong: Two times is reached around 5-6 months.  Why B wrong: Three times is reached by 1 year.  Why D wrong: Five times is reached by 3 years."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"By three years of age, a child's weight is approximately how many times the birth weight?","options":["Three times","Four times","Five times","Six times"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the baby attains five times its birth weight by three years of age.  Why A wrong: Three times is by 1 year.  Why B wrong: Four times is by 2 years.  Why D wrong: Six times is not stated."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"At birth, the chest circumference (approximately 31-33 cm) is how much compared to the head circumference?","options":["2-3 cm more than head circumference","2-3 cm less than head circumference","Equal to head circumference","10 cm less than head circumference"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states chest circumference is approximately 31-33 cm, which is 2-3 cm less than head circumference at birth.  Why A wrong: At birth chest circumference is smaller, not larger.  Why C wrong: They are equal at 1 year, not at birth.  Why D wrong: The difference is 2-3 cm, not 10 cm."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"At what age do head circumference (HC) and chest circumference (CC) become equal?","options":["At birth","At 6 months","At 1 year of age","At 2 years of age"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that at 1 year of age HC and CC become equal.  Why A wrong: At birth CC is 2-3 cm less than HC.  Why B wrong: 6 months is not the stated equalization age.  Why D wrong: By 2 years the chest circumference typically exceeds the head circumference."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"If a newborn's head circumference increases by more than 1 cm in two weeks during the first 3 months of life, which condition should be suspected?","options":["Microcephaly","Hydrocephalus","Craniosynostosis","Normal growth"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that an increase of more than 1 cm in two weeks during the first 3 months should raise suspicion of hydrocephalus, which must be investigated.  Why A wrong: Microcephaly is an abnormally small head, the opposite finding.  Why C wrong: Craniosynostosis restricts skull growth, not causing rapid enlargement.  Why D wrong: Such rapid growth is abnormal and warrants investigation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"When does the first tooth typically erupt, and which tooth is it?","options":["At 3 months; upper central incisor","At 6 months; lower central incisor","At 12 months; first molar","At 9 months; canine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the first teeth appear at 6 months, and it is the lower central incisor.  Why A wrong: 3 months and upper incisor are incorrect.  Why C wrong: 12 months and molar are incorrect.  Why D wrong: 9 months and canine are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which bone is most commonly fractured during toddlerhood?","options":["Femur","Radius","Clavicle","Humerus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the radius is the bone commonly fractured during toddlerhood.  Why A wrong: The femur is not identified as the most common in toddlers here.  Why C wrong: The clavicle is the most common fracture at birth, not toddlerhood.  Why D wrong: The humerus is not the stated answer."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the normal haemoglobin level in a newborn?","options":["11-13 gm/dl","14-16 gm/dl","17-20 gm/dl","21-24 gm/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal newborn haemoglobin level is 17 to 20 gm/dl.  Why A wrong: 11-13 gm/dl is an older-child/adult female range.  Why B wrong: 14-16 gm/dl is below the stated newborn range.  Why D wrong: 21-24 gm/dl exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the normal birth weight of an Indian child?","options":["1.5 to 2.0 kg","2.5 to 3.5 kg","4.0 to 4.5 kg","3.5 to 4.0 kg"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the normal birth weight of an Indian child is 2.5 to 3.5 kg.  Why A wrong: 1.5-2.0 kg indicates low birth weight.  Why C wrong: 4.0-4.5 kg indicates macrosomia.  Why D wrong: 3.5-4.0 kg is above the stated normal range."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which is the biomarker used to detect neural tube defects?","options":["MSAFP (Maternal Serum Alpha Feto-Protein)","Beta-hCG","CA-125","CEA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the biomarker of neural tube defects is MSAFP (Maternal Serum Alpha Feto-Protein).  Why B wrong: Beta-hCG is a pregnancy/trophoblastic marker.  Why C wrong: CA-125 is an ovarian cancer marker.  Why D wrong: CEA is a colorectal cancer marker."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which neonatal reflex is elicited by touching the cheek of the baby?","options":["Moro reflex","Rooting reflex","Grasp reflex","Babinski reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the reflex elicited by touching the cheeks is the rooting reflex.  Why A wrong: The Moro reflex is triggered by a sudden loss of support/startle.  Why C wrong: The grasp reflex is elicited by placing an object in the palm.  Why D wrong: The Babinski reflex is elicited by stroking the sole."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"The rooting reflex in an infant normally disappears at what age?","options":["1-2 months","3-4 months","6-8 months","12 months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the rooting reflex disappears at 3-4 months.  Why A wrong: 1-2 months is earlier than stated.  Why C wrong: 6-8 months is later than stated.  Why D wrong: 12 months is far later than stated."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common type of seizure in neonates and infants?","options":["Febrile seizure","Subtle seizure","Absence seizure","Tonic-clonic seizure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common seizure type in neonates and infants is subtle seizures.  Why A wrong: Febrile seizures are most common in toddlers/older children.  Why C wrong: Absence seizures typically occur in school-aged children.  Why D wrong: Tonic-clonic seizures are not the most common neonatal type."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common type of seizure in toddlers and older children?","options":["Subtle seizure","Febrile seizure","Myoclonic seizure","Atonic seizure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common seizure in toddlers/older children is febrile seizure.  Why A wrong: Subtle seizures predominate in neonates/infants.  Why C wrong: Myoclonic seizures are not identified as the most common.  Why D wrong: Atonic seizures are not the most common in this group."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common cause of seizures in neonates?","options":["Hypoxia","Hyperglycemia","Hypertension","Dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of seizure in neonates is hypoxia.  Why B wrong: Hypoglycemia (not hyperglycemia) can cause seizures, but hypoxia is the stated most common cause.  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not the stated most common cause.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is not the stated most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which periconceptional intervention most effectively PREVENTS neural tube defects?","options":["Folic acid supplementation before conception and in early pregnancy","Iron supplementation in the third trimester","Vitamin K at birth","Calcium supplementation during lactation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Periconceptional folic acid (started before conception through the first trimester) is the proven measure to prevent neural tube defects, which most commonly occur in the lumbosacral region.  Why B wrong: Third-trimester iron treats/prevents anaemia; it is too late and unrelated to NTD prevention (neural tube closes by ~week 4).  Why C wrong: Vitamin K at birth prevents haemorrhagic disease of the newborn, not NTDs.  Why D wrong: Calcium in lactation does not prevent NTDs."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common MALIGNANT brain tumor in children?","options":["Astrocytoma","Medulloblastoma","Craniopharyngioma","Ependymoma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant (embryonal, posterior-fossa) brain tumor in children. [Stem clarified to \"malignant\": astrocytoma is the most common childhood CNS tumor overall, so \"malignant\" is required for B to be unambiguous.]  Why A wrong: Astrocytoma (esp. pilocytic) is the most common brain tumor overall in children, but most are low-grade/benign, not the most common malignant tumor.  Why C wrong: Craniopharyngioma is less common and benign.  Why D wrong: Ependymoma is less common."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the cardinal sign of intussusception?","options":["Rice-water stool","Currant jelly-like stool","Ribbon-like stool","Black tarry stool"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the cardinal sign of intussusception is currant jelly-like stool.  Why A wrong: Rice-water stool is seen in cholera.  Why C wrong: Ribbon-like stool is associated with Hirschsprung disease.  Why D wrong: Black tarry stool (melena) indicates upper GI bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which measurement is the index used for assessing nutritional status in children? (AIIMS Patna-2020)","options":["Anthropometry","Spirometry","Audiometry","Tonometry"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states anthropometry is the index for nutritional status in children.  Why B wrong: Spirometry assesses lung function.  Why C wrong: Audiometry assesses hearing.  Why D wrong: Tonometry measures intraocular pressure."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which drug is used to keep the patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) open?","options":["Indomethacin","PGE1 (Prostaglandin E1)","Ibuprofen","Aspirin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states PGE1 (the book names \"Misoprostol\"; the classic agent is Alprostadil/PGE1) is used to keep the ductus arteriosus open. Prostaglandins maintain ductal patency.  Why A wrong: Indomethacin is a prostaglandin inhibitor that closes the PDA.  Why C wrong: Ibuprofen, like indomethacin, closes the PDA.  Why D wrong: Aspirin is not used to maintain ductal patency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which drug is used to treat (close) a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)?","options":["PGE1","Indomethacin (a prostaglandin inhibitor)","Digoxin","Furosemide"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states indomethacin, a prostaglandin inhibitor, is used to treat/close a PDA.  Why A wrong: PGE1 keeps the ductus open, not closed.  Why C wrong: Digoxin is a positive inotrope for heart failure, not for PDA closure.  Why D wrong: Furosemide is a diuretic, not a ductus-closing agent."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common congenital heart disorder in children?","options":["Atrial septal defect (ASD)","Ventricular septal defect (VSD)","Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)","Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common congenital heart disorder in children is VSD.  Why A wrong: ASD is common but not the most common.  Why C wrong: TOF is the most common cyanotic defect, not the overall most common.  Why D wrong: PDA is not the most common congenital heart defect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Projectile vomiting is the classical symptom of which condition (and sometimes of increased ICP)?","options":["Pyloric stenosis","Gastroesophageal reflux","Intussusception","Appendicitis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states projectile vomiting is the classical symptom of pyloric stenosis (and sometimes increased ICP).  Why B wrong: Reflux causes effortless regurgitation, not projectile vomiting.  Why C wrong: Intussusception classically causes currant jelly stool.  Why D wrong: Appendicitis causes RLQ pain, not classic projectile vomiting."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common cause of newborn mortality?","options":["Prematurity","Congenital heart disease","Birth trauma","Sepsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common cause of newborn mortality is prematurity.  Why B wrong: Congenital heart disease is a cause but not the most common.  Why C wrong: Birth trauma is not the stated most common cause.  Why D wrong: Sepsis is a major cause but not identified as the most common here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"For how long can expressed breast milk be stored at room temperature?","options":["Up to 2 hours","Up to 6 hours","Up to 12 hours","Up to 24 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states expressed milk can be stored at room temperature up to 6 hours.  Why A wrong: 2 hours is shorter than the stated limit.  Why C wrong: 12 hours exceeds the stated room-temperature limit.  Why D wrong: 24 hours is the refrigerator limit, not room temperature."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"For how long can expressed breast milk be stored in the refrigerator?","options":["Up to 6 hours","Up to 12 hours","Up to 24 hours","Up to 48 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states expressed milk can be stored in the refrigerator up to 24 hours.  Why A wrong: 6 hours is the room-temperature limit.  Why B wrong: 12 hours is not the stated refrigerator limit.  Why D wrong: 48 hours exceeds the stated refrigerator limit."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"The method used for transportation of vaccine from the site of production to the vaccination site is called:","options":["Cold chain","Herd immunity","Reverse osmosis","Pasteurization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the transportation method for maintaining vaccine potency is the cold chain.  Why B wrong: Herd immunity is a population immunity concept, not transport.  Why C wrong: Reverse osmosis is a water purification method.  Why D wrong: Pasteurization is a heat treatment for milk, not vaccine transport."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Diaper rashes are most commonly caused by which organism?","options":["Candida albicans","Staphylococcus aureus","E. coli","Streptococcus pyogenes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Candida albicans is the most common infectious cause of diaper dermatitis (classically a beefy-red rash with satellite lesions in the skin folds). [Corrected: the source's \"Staphylococcus aureus\" is inaccurate.]  Why B wrong: Staphylococcus aureus can cause bullous impetigo/secondary infection but is not the most common cause of diaper rash.  Why C wrong: E. coli is not the usual diaper-rash organism.  Why D wrong: Streptococcus pyogenes is not the usual cause."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Oral polio vaccine (OPV) is also known as:","options":["Salk vaccine","Sabin vaccine","BCG vaccine","Pentavalent vaccine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oral polio vaccine is also known as Sabin.  Why A wrong: The Salk vaccine is the inactivated (injectable) polio vaccine (IPV).  Why C wrong: BCG is the tuberculosis vaccine.  Why D wrong: Pentavalent vaccine covers DPT-HepB-Hib."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"According to Sigmund Freud's psychosexual development theory, which structures of the personality were defined?","options":["Ego, alter ego, and libido","Id, Ego, and Super ego","Conscious, subconscious, and unconscious only","Sensory, motor, and cognitive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Freud's theory defined the terms Id, Ego, and Super ego.  Why A wrong: \"Alter ego\" and \"libido\" are not the three structures named.  Why C wrong: Those are levels of consciousness, not the personality structures asked.  Why D wrong: These are developmental domains, not Freud's structures."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In Freud's theory, the \"Id\" operates on which principle?","options":["Reality principle","Pleasure principle","Conscience or perfection","Morality principle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Id is the pleasure principle.  Why A wrong: The reality principle governs the Ego.  Why C wrong: Conscience/perfection governs the Super ego.  Why D wrong: Morality relates to the Super ego, not the Id."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In Freud's theory, the \"Ego\" is based on which principle?","options":["Pleasure","Reality","Conscience","Perfection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Ego is based on reality.  Why A wrong: Pleasure is the Id's principle.  Why C wrong: Conscience is the Super ego's basis.  Why D wrong: Perfection is the Super ego's basis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In Freud's theory, the \"Super ego\" is based on:","options":["Pleasure principle","Reality principle","Conscience or perfection","Instinctual drives"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Super ego is based on conscience or perfection.  Why A wrong: Pleasure is the Id's basis.  Why B wrong: Reality is the Ego's basis.  Why D wrong: Instinctual drives are associated with the Id."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Haemophilia affects which individuals, and what is the status of females?","options":["Affects only females; males are carriers","Affects only males; females are only carriers","Affects both sexes equally","Affects only females; males are unaffected"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states haemophilia affects only males, and females are only in the carrier state (X-linked recessive inheritance).  Why A wrong: The sexes are reversed; males are affected.  Why C wrong: It does not affect both sexes equally.  Why D wrong: Males are affected, not unaffected."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A \"boot-shaped heart\" on chest X-ray is characteristic of which congenital cardiac disease?","options":["Ventricular septal defect","Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)","Transposition of great arteries","Coarctation of aorta"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a boot-shaped heart is seen in TOF.  Why A wrong: VSD does not classically produce a boot-shaped heart.  Why C wrong: Transposition classically gives an \"egg-on-a-string\" appearance.  Why D wrong: Coarctation may show rib notching, not a boot-shaped heart."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common symptom of Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF)?","options":["Clubbing of the fingers","Bounding pulses","Bradycardia","Hypertension"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common symptom of TOF is clubbing of the fingers (from chronic cyanosis).  Why B wrong: Bounding pulses are seen in PDA, not typical of TOF.  Why C wrong: Bradycardia is not the classical TOF symptom.  Why D wrong: Hypertension is not a feature of TOF."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Deficiency of growth hormone (GH) leads to which condition?","options":["Gigantism","Dwarfism","Acromegaly","Cretinism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GH deficiency leads to dwarfism.  Why A wrong: Gigantism results from GH excess in children.  Why C wrong: Acromegaly results from GH excess in adults.  Why D wrong: Cretinism results from congenital hypothyroidism."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Hypersecretion of growth hormone (GH) in children is called:","options":["Dwarfism","Gigantism","Acromegaly","Rickets"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GH hypersecretion in children is called gigantism.  Why A wrong: Dwarfism is GH deficiency.  Why C wrong: Acromegaly is GH excess in adults.  Why D wrong: Rickets is vitamin D deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Hypersecretion of growth hormone in adults is called:","options":["Gigantism","Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Osteomalacia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GH hypersecretion in adults is called acromegaly.  Why A wrong: Gigantism is GH excess in children.  Why C wrong: Dwarfism is GH deficiency.  Why D wrong: Osteomalacia is adult vitamin D deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Deficiency of vitamin D in children is called:","options":["Osteomalacia","Rickets","Scurvy","Beriberi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states vitamin D deficiency in children is called rickets.  Why A wrong: Osteomalacia is adult vitamin D deficiency.  Why C wrong: Scurvy is vitamin C deficiency.  Why D wrong: Beriberi is vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Deficiency of vitamin D in adults is called:","options":["Rickets","Osteomalacia","Osteoporosis","Pellagra"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states vitamin D deficiency in adults is called osteomalacia.  Why A wrong: Rickets is the childhood form.  Why C wrong: Osteoporosis is reduced bone density, not specifically vitamin D deficiency.  Why D wrong: Pellagra is niacin (B3) deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What concentration of injectable adrenaline is recommended during CPR in a newborn? (AIIMS Delhi 2019)","options":["1:1000","1:10000","1:100","1:100000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the adrenaline concentration for CPR in a newborn is 1:10000.  Why A wrong: 1:1000 is stated for children and adults per the source.  Why C wrong: 1:100 is far more concentrated than recommended.  Why D wrong: 1:100000 is far more dilute than recommended."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What concentration of injectable adrenaline (epinephrine) is recommended for IV/IO use during CPR (cardiac arrest) in newborns, children and adults?","options":["1:10000","1:1000","1:5000","1:100"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: For CPR / cardiac arrest, the IV/IO adrenaline concentration is 1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL) for ALL ages — newborns, children and adults (AHA ACLS/PALS, NRP). [Corrected: the source's child/adult \"1:1000\" is a dangerous error.]  Why B wrong: 1:1,000 (1 mg/mL) is the IM concentration for anaphylaxis, NOT for CPR.  Why C wrong: 1:5000 is not a standard adrenaline concentration.  Why D wrong: 1:100 is not used."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which scoring system is used to assess a respiratory-distressed newborn?","options":["Apgar score","Downes score","Glasgow Coma Scale","Ballard score"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Downes score is used in a respiratory-distressed newborn.  Why A wrong: The Apgar score assesses immediate newborn transition, not specifically respiratory distress severity.  Why C wrong: The Glasgow Coma Scale assesses consciousness.  Why D wrong: The Ballard score assesses gestational age."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the best and most accurate method for calculating a medication dose for infants and children?","options":["Age","Weight","Height","Body surface area estimate by age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states weight is the best and most accurate method of measuring the medication dose for infants and children.  Why A wrong: Age is less accurate than weight-based dosing.  Why C wrong: Height alone is not the stated best method.  Why D wrong: While BSA is used, the source specifies weight as the best method."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"When assessing vital signs in a newborn \"from least intrusive to more intrusive\" (RPT method), which parameter should be assessed FIRST?","options":["Temperature","Pulse","Respiration rate","Blood pressure"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states you first assess respiration rate and last of all temperature, because if the baby cries the readings may be falsely elevated.  Why A wrong: Temperature is assessed last, as it is most intrusive.  Why B wrong: Pulse is assessed after respiration, not first.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure is not the stated first parameter."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"In assessing newborn vital signs, why is temperature assessed last?","options":["Because the thermometer is expensive","Because if the baby cries, readings may be falsely (inaccurate)","Because temperature is unimportant","Because respiration is difficult to count"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states temperature is assessed last because if the baby cries then readings may be falsely obtained; the least intrusive measures come first.  Why A wrong: Cost is not the reason.  Why C wrong: Temperature is important; the sequence is about accuracy.  Why D wrong: The rationale concerns crying-induced inaccuracy, not difficulty counting respiration."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"According to Erikson's psychosocial theory, what is the developmental task for toddlers?","options":["Trust vs Mistrust","Autonomy vs Shame and Doubt","Initiative vs Guilt","Industry vs Inferiority"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the developmental task for toddlers is autonomy vs shame/doubt.  Why A wrong: Trust vs Mistrust is the infancy stage.  Why C wrong: Initiative vs Guilt is the preschool stage.  Why D wrong: Industry vs Inferiority is the school-age stage."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common complication monitored in preterm infants?","options":["Hyperglycemia","Respiratory distress","Hypertension","Polycythemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common complication monitored in preterm infants is respiratory distress.  Why A wrong: Hyperglycemia is not the stated most common complication (hypoglycemia is more typical).  Why C wrong: Hypertension is not the stated most common preterm complication.  Why D wrong: Anemia, not polycythemia, is common in preterm infants."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A generally diminished or absent Moro reflex in a newborn is most concerning for which condition?","options":["Kernicterus (bilirubin encephalopathy) / CNS depression","Normal finding at birth","Down syndrome only","Hydrocephalus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A bilaterally diminished/absent Moro reflex indicates CNS depression, and in a jaundiced newborn is a classic sign of kernicterus (acute bilirubin encephalopathy). [Stem corrected from the source's \"asymmetric Moro = kernicterus\": an ASYMMETRIC (unilateral) Moro indicates brachial plexus/Erb's palsy or clavicle fracture, whereas kernicterus/CNS depression diminishes the Moro bilaterally.]  Why B wrong: An absent Moro is abnormal; the Moro is normally present at birth.  Why C wrong: Down syndrome may show hypotonia/diminished reflexes but is not the best single answer here.  Why D wrong: Hydrocephalus classically shows the sunset sign, not primarily an absent Moro."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"During newborn CPR, what is the ratio of breaths (ventilations) to chest compressions?","options":["1:5","3:1","2:15","1:1"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the ratio of breathing to chest compression in a newborn during CPR is 3:1 (3 compressions to 1 ventilation is delivered as a 3:1 cycle in neonatal resuscitation).  Why A wrong: 1:5 is not the stated ratio.  Why C wrong: 2:15 is not the neonatal ratio.  Why D wrong: 1:1 is not the stated ratio."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the size of the suction catheter used in neonates for oral and nasal suction?","options":["<10 Fr","12-14 Fr","16-18 Fr","20 Fr"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the suction catheter size for neonatal oral and nasal suction is <10 Fr.  Why B wrong: 12-14 Fr is larger than recommended for neonates.  Why C wrong: 16-18 Fr is an adult-sized catheter.  Why D wrong: 20 Fr is far too large for a neonate."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which is the ideal route for drug administration during neonatal resuscitation?","options":["Intramuscular","Umbilical vein","Subcutaneous","Oral"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the ideal route for drug administration during neonatal resuscitation is the umbilical vein.  Why A wrong: Intramuscular absorption is too slow and unreliable in resuscitation.  Why C wrong: Subcutaneous is not appropriate for emergency drugs.  Why D wrong: The oral route is inappropriate during resuscitation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"The \"sunset sign\" (setting-sun eyes) is seen in which condition?","options":["Kernicterus","Hydrocephalus","Meningitis","Cerebral palsy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the sunset sign is seen in hydrocephalus.  Why A wrong: Kernicterus is associated with an abnormal/asymmetric Moro per the source.  Why C wrong: Meningitis is diagnosed by lumbar puncture, not the sunset sign.  Why D wrong: The sunset sign is specific to raised ICP/hydrocephalus."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common complication in low-birth-weight (LBW) babies?","options":["Hypertension","Respiratory distress","Hyperglycemia","Constipation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common complication in LBW babies is respiratory distress.  Why A wrong: Hypertension is not the stated most common LBW complication.  Why C wrong: Hypoglycemia (not hyperglycemia) is more typical in LBW babies.  Why D wrong: Constipation is not the stated most common complication."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) should be continued until the baby attains what weight?","options":["1.5 kg","2.0 kg","2.5 kg","3.0 kg"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states KMC care should be necessary till the baby attains a weight of 2.5 kg.  Why A wrong: 1.5 kg is below the stated threshold.  Why B wrong: 2.0 kg is below the stated threshold.  Why D wrong: 3.0 kg exceeds the stated threshold."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the total lung capacity of a newborn?","options":["50 ml","150 ml","300 ml","500 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total lung capacity in a newborn is 150 ml.  Why A wrong: 50 ml is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 300 ml exceeds the stated value.  Why D wrong: 500 ml is far above the stated newborn value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What should be the temperature of the delivery room to maintain the warmth of a newborn?","options":["18-20°C","21-24°C","25-28°C","30-33°C"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the delivery room temperature should be 25-28°C to maintain newborn warmth.  Why A wrong: 18-20°C is too cold for a newborn.  Why B wrong: 21-24°C is below the stated range.  Why D wrong: 30-33°C exceeds the stated range."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"How many times per day, and for how long, should a mother initially feed her baby?","options":["4-6 times; 5 minutes each","8-12 times; 10-15 minutes each","2-3 times; 20 minutes each","15-20 times; 5 minutes each"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the mother should feed the baby at least 8-12 times, with each feeding 10-15 minutes long.  Why A wrong: 4-6 times and 5 minutes are below the stated recommendation.  Why C wrong: 2-3 times is too infrequent.  Why D wrong: 15-20 times with 5 minutes is not the stated recommendation."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the best indicator of nutritional status in a child?","options":["Rate of increase of height and weight","Skin colour","Number of teeth","Blood pressure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the best indicator for nutritional status in a child is the rate of increase of height and weight.  Why B wrong: Skin colour is not the best nutritional indicator.  Why C wrong: Number of teeth reflects dental development, not nutrition.  Why D wrong: Blood pressure is not the nutritional indicator."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Bottle-fed babies are more likely to develop which condition?","options":["Jaundice","Diarrhoea","Constipation","Hypothermia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bottle-fed babies are more likely to develop diarrhoea (due to contamination risk).  Why A wrong: Jaundice is not the stated risk of bottle feeding.  Why C wrong: Constipation is not the stated risk.  Why D wrong: Hypothermia is unrelated to feeding method here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Kramer's rule is used to assess which condition?","options":["Dehydration","Jaundice","Malnutrition","Respiratory distress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Kramer's rule is used for jaundice (assessing cephalocaudal progression of neonatal jaundice).  Why A wrong: Dehydration is not assessed by Kramer's rule.  Why C wrong: Malnutrition is assessed by anthropometry.  Why D wrong: Respiratory distress is assessed by Downes/Silverman scores."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the priority need in the care of a child who has pneumonia?","options":["Ambulation","Rest","High-protein diet","Physiotherapy exercises"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the priority need in the care of a child with pneumonia is rest.  Why A wrong: Ambulation increases oxygen demand and is not the priority.  Why C wrong: While nutrition matters, rest is the stated priority.  Why D wrong: Vigorous exercise is not the priority in acute pneumonia."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which lobe is most commonly affected in children with pulmonary atelectasis, and why?","options":["Left upper lobe, due to cardiac compression","Right middle lobe, because its bronchus has a narrow diameter","Left lower lobe, due to gravity","Right upper lobe, due to a horizontal bronchus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the right middle lobe is most commonly affected in pulmonary atelectasis because its bronchus has a narrow diameter.  Why A wrong: The left upper lobe is not the stated site.  Why C wrong: The left lower lobe is not the stated site.  Why D wrong: The right upper lobe is not the stated site; the narrow right middle lobe bronchus is the reason."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the appropriate position for a child in the post-operative period following tonsillectomy? (NORCET-5 Mains-2023)","options":["Supine position","Prone position","High Fowler's position","Trendelenburg position"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the appropriate post-tonsillectomy position is prone, which facilitates drainage of secretions and prevents aspiration.  Why A wrong: Supine risks aspiration of blood/secretions.  Why C wrong: High Fowler's is not the stated position.  Why D wrong: Trendelenburg (head-down) is not appropriate here."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Kawasaki disease is also known as:","options":["Mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Reye's syndrome","Nephrotic syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Kawasaki disease is also known as mucocutaneous lymph node syndrome.  Why B wrong: Guillain-Barré is an acute polyneuropathy.  Why C wrong: Reye's syndrome is encephalopathy with hepatic dysfunction.  Why D wrong: Nephrotic syndrome is a renal disorder."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common side effect of NSAIDs?","options":["Gastrointestinal irritation / bleeding","Haematuria","Hypertension","Weight gain"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common adverse effect of NSAIDs is GI irritation (dyspepsia, gastritis, peptic ulceration/GI bleeding) due to COX-1 inhibition and reduced gastroprotective prostaglandins. [Corrected: the source's \"haematuria\" is not the most common effect.]  Why B wrong: Haematuria/nephrotoxicity can occur but is far less common than GI effects.  Why C wrong: Hypertension/fluid retention is possible but not the most common effect.  Why D wrong: Weight gain is not a characteristic NSAID side effect."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Oxygen toxicity in preterm neonates (affecting the eyes) is known as:","options":["Retrolental fibroplasia","Bronchopulmonary dysplasia","Cataract","Glaucoma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oxygen toxicity in preterm neonates is known as retrolental fibroplasia (retinopathy of prematurity).  Why B wrong: Bronchopulmonary dysplasia is a lung, not eye, complication of oxygen toxicity.  Why C wrong: Cataract is lens opacity, not the stated condition.  Why D wrong: Glaucoma is raised intraocular pressure, not the stated condition."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the most common and confirmative diagnostic test for meningitis?","options":["CT scan","Lumbar puncture","Blood culture","EEG"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common and confirmative diagnosis of meningitis is lumbar puncture (CSF analysis).  Why A wrong: CT may be done before LP but is not confirmatory for meningitis.  Why C wrong: Blood culture supports diagnosis but is not the confirmative test.  Why D wrong: EEG assesses electrical activity, not meningitis diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Deficiency of which vitamin leads to peripheral neuropathy?","options":["Vitamin C","Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Vitamin A","Vitamin K"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states deficiency of pyridoxine (vitamin B6) leads to peripheral neuropathy.  Why A wrong: Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy.  Why C wrong: Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness.  Why D wrong: Vitamin K deficiency causes bleeding."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for milk secretion or production?","options":["Oxytocin","Prolactin","Estrogen","Progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states prolactin is responsible for milk secretion/production.  Why A wrong: Oxytocin is responsible for milk ejection (let-down), not production.  Why C wrong: Estrogen supports breast development but does not produce milk.  Why D wrong: Progesterone inhibits lactation during pregnancy."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Which hormone is responsible for milk ejection or the let-down reflex?","options":["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Growth hormone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states oxytocin is responsible for milk ejection or the let-down reflex.  Why A wrong: Prolactin governs milk production, not ejection.  Why C wrong: Estrogen does not trigger the let-down reflex.  Why D wrong: Growth hormone is unrelated to the let-down reflex."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"A phenotypic male with tall stature, gynaecomastia, small firm testes and infertility (47,XXY) has which condition?","options":["Turner syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome","Down syndrome","Edward syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY; i.e. 44 autosomes + XXY) presents in phenotypic males with tall stature, gynaecomastia, small firm testes, and infertility due to the extra X chromosome. [Reframed from a duplicate karyotype-recall item to a distinct clinical-presentation question.]  Why A wrong: Turner syndrome (45,X) affects phenotypic females (short stature, webbed neck).  Why C wrong: Down syndrome is trisomy 21.  Why D wrong: Edward syndrome is trisomy 18."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the total urine output of a newborn?","options":["100 ml/24 hours or 1 ml/kg/hr","250 ml/24 hours or 2.5 ml/kg/hr","500 ml/24 hours or 5 ml/kg/hr","1000 ml/24 hours or 10 ml/kg/hr"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the total urine output of a newborn is 250 ml/24 hours or 2.5 ml/kg/hr.  Why A wrong: 100 ml/24 hours is below the stated value.  Why C wrong: 500 ml/24 hours exceeds the stated value.  Why D wrong: 1000 ml/24 hours is far above the newborn value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Oliguria in infants is defined as a urine output of less than:","options":["0.5 mL/kg/h","1 mL/kg/h","2 mL/kg/h","400 ml/day"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines oliguria in infants as urine output less than 1 mL/kg/h.  Why A wrong: <0.5 mL/kg/h is the definition for children.  Why C wrong: 2 mL/kg/h is within normal newborn output.  Why D wrong: <400 ml daily is the adult definition."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Oliguria in children is defined as a urine output of less than:","options":["1 mL/kg/h","0.5 mL/kg/h","2 mL/kg/h","400 ml daily"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines oliguria in children as urine output less than 0.5 mL/kg/h.  Why A wrong: <1 mL/kg/h is the infant definition.  Why C wrong: 2 mL/kg/h is normal, not oliguric.  Why D wrong: <400 ml daily is the adult definition."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Oliguria in adults is defined as a urine output of less than:","options":["400 ml daily","1500 ml daily","1 mL/kg/h","0.5 mL/kg/h"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines oliguria in adults as urine output less than 400 ml daily.  Why B wrong: 1500 ml daily is the normal adult output.  Why C wrong: <1 mL/kg/h is the infant definition.  Why D wrong: <0.5 mL/kg/h is the children's definition."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the normal urine output of an adult?","options":["250 ml/24 hrs","1500 ml/24 hrs (or 0.8 ml/kg/hr or 30 ml/hr)","400 ml/24 hrs","3000 ml/24 hrs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states normal adult urine output is 1500 ml/24 hrs, or 0.8 ml/kg/hr, or 30 ml/hr.  Why A wrong: 250 ml/24 hrs is the newborn output.  Why C wrong: <400 ml/24 hrs defines oliguria in adults.  Why D wrong: 3000 ml/24 hrs would indicate polyuria."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the precursor of breast milk (the first fluid secreted after delivery)?","options":["Foremilk","Colostrum","Hindmilk","Transitional milk"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the precursor of breast milk is colostrum.  Why A wrong: Foremilk is the early portion of mature milk during a feed, not the precursor.  Why C wrong: Hindmilk is the fat-rich later portion of a feed.  Why D wrong: Transitional milk follows colostrum, so colostrum is the true precursor."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"The Silverman-Andersen Retraction Score (SAs) is used to assess:","options":["Severity of respiratory distress in newborns and preterm infants without respiratory support","Gestational age","Level of consciousness","Degree of dehydration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Silverman-Andersen Retraction Score assesses severity of respiratory distress in newborns and preterm infants without respiratory support.  Why B wrong: Gestational age is assessed by the Ballard/New Ballard score.  Why C wrong: Consciousness is assessed by the Glasgow Coma Scale.  Why D wrong: Dehydration is assessed clinically, not by the SAs."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the expected pre-ductal SpO2 value in a newborn at 1 minute after birth?","options":["40-50%","60-65%","70-75%","85-95%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-ductal SpO2 at 1 minute is 60-65%.  Why A wrong: 40-50% is below the stated 1-minute value.  Why C wrong: 70-75% is the 3-minute value.  Why D wrong: 85-95% is the 10-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the expected pre-ductal SpO2 value in a newborn at 2 minutes after birth?","options":["60-65%","65-70%","70-75%","80-85%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-ductal SpO2 at 2 minutes is 65-70%.  Why A wrong: 60-65% is the 1-minute value.  Why C wrong: 70-75% is the 3-minute value.  Why D wrong: 80-85% is the 5-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the expected pre-ductal SpO2 value in a newborn at 3 minutes after birth?","options":["65-70%","70-75%","80-85%","85-95%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-ductal SpO2 at 3 minutes is 70-75%.  Why A wrong: 65-70% is the 2-minute value.  Why C wrong: 80-85% is the 5-minute value.  Why D wrong: 85-95% is the 10-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the expected pre-ductal SpO2 value in a newborn at 5 minutes after birth?","options":["70-75%","80-85%","85-95%","95-100%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-ductal SpO2 at 5 minutes is 80-85%.  Why A wrong: 70-75% is the 3-minute value.  Why C wrong: 85-95% is the 10-minute value.  Why D wrong: 95-100% exceeds the stated 5-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"What is the expected pre-ductal SpO2 value in a newborn at 10 minutes after birth?","options":["70-75%","80-85%","85-95%","60-65%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre-ductal SpO2 at 10 minutes is 85-95%.  Why A wrong: 70-75% is the 3-minute value.  Why B wrong: 80-85% is the 5-minute value.  Why D wrong: 60-65% is the 1-minute value."},{"section":"Batch 014 · Hypospadias & epispadias, Wilms tumor, Marfan syndrome, Eczema…","question":"Pre-ductal and post-ductal saturations both refer to which type of saturation?","options":["Venous oxygen saturation","Arterial oxygen saturation (blood from the heart), before and after the ductal orifice in the aorta","Capillary saturation","Mixed venous saturation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the pre- and post-ductal saturations are both arterial saturations (blood from the heart), referring to arterial oxygen saturation in vessels originating from the aorta before and after the ductal orifice.  Why A wrong: They are arterial, not venous, saturations.  Why C wrong: They are arterial saturations, not capillary.  Why D wrong: They are not mixed venous saturations."}];
